8)   Ordinances and Resolutions.
Resolution No. R-100-2022: A Resolution Calling for Federal and State Action on
Gun Safety and Violence (Adoption)
President Rintz indicates that Council will be considering a Resolution calling
for Federal and State action on gun safety and violence. The Village Attorney
has advised Council that there is a State preemption and a recent Supreme Court
ruling that limits the Council’s ability to expand any requirements. President
Rintz defers the discussion to a later date in order to ensure that Village
Attorney Peter Friedman will be in attendance.
Public Comment.
Charl Capria, on behalf of Kaity Brandtor, thanks Council for their time and
consideration reflecting on the devastating events at the Highland Park
Shooting.
Lauren Harper thanks Council for approaching gun related issues and urges
Council to come together to stand for change while also encouraging New Trier
Township High School to engage in conversation of safe gun storage measures.
Speaker name not stated, thanks Council for their service and reflects on the
lack of education behind secure gun storage of gun owners.
Jean Bishop reflects on the beautiful nature of Lake Michigan. Ms. Bishop
shares her story regarding gun violence in the community and thanks Council for
their civic duty.
Natalie Ernest reflects on a letter signed on behalf of the members of the
community regarding matters related to safe gun storage and encourages Council
to take action on gun control matters.
Lizzy Kelly thanks Council for the discussion related to gun violence issues.
Ms. Kelly reflects on the Hubbard Woods school shooting and encourages Council
to consider making changes.
Jackie Raimo expresses her disappointment regarding the State’s preemption and
indicates her interest in working towards gun related safety measures.
Kate McNally thanks Council for their service, commitment, thoughtfulness, and
courage.
Speaker name not stated, reflects on personal experiences related to gun
violence and indicates the importance of presence to make change.
President Rintz commends the value of Winnetka being a safe place and is
disheartened to hear the community question safety in the Village. President
Rintz expresses hope in the State, Federal, and local representatives standing
for change.
Members of Council unanimously express their disappointment in the immense
amount of gun violence related issues at a local and national level and express
their disappointment at the State and Federal levels lack of attention and
effort to work towards resolving gun related issues.
Council deliberates various matters pertaining to gun related issues such as
the process to obtaining a FOID card, manufactures and sellers, negligence
related matters, and implementation measures related to firearm safety.
Council members discuss changes to certain language within the Resolution
presented in order to finalize a more structured and thorough document allowing
Council to move forward with improving public safety regarding gun violence and
ally with local, State, Federal, and private entities.
Trustee Handler motions to introduce, rather than adopt, Resolution No. R-100-
2022, suggesting additional time for the community to review the document and
provide feedback to Council.
Trustee Handler, seconded by Trustee Apatoff, moved to introduce Resolution No.
R-100-2022. By roll call vote, the motion failed. Ayes: Trustees Apatoff and
Handler. Nays: Trustees Cripe, Dalman, Dearborn, Swierk. Absent: None.
Village Manager Bahan confirms the proposed changes suggested by members of the
Council related to the Resolution as follows:
WHEREAS, the American Public Health Association has found that gun violence is
a leading cause of premature death in the United States, killing more than
38,000 people and injuring nearly 85,000 every year; and
WHEREAS, the Village stands in solidarity with other Illinois local governments
and the State of Illinois to promote safe firearms practices and programs, but
recognizes the ineffectiveness inadequacy of local firearms regulations without
comprehensive statewide regulations to protect our communities from gun
violence; and
WHEREAS, assault weapons are military grade weapons, designed and configured
for combat use with the sole purpose of shooting as many people as possible, as
quickly as possible; and
WHEREAS, assault weapons and large-capacity magazines are designed for the sole
purpose of allowing a shooter to fire repeated shots without having to reload;
and
WHEREAS, assault weapons and large-capacity magazines are combat weapons and do
not belong in the hands of civilians; and
WHEREAS, the United States Congress and the Illinois General Assembly must act
to try to stop this threat to our American freedom and take immediate action to
ban assault weapons and large-capacity magazines so that they cannot be used by
civilians on our streets, in our markets, in our houses of worship, in our
schools, special events, parades, and all other community gatherings and
events.
Ban the sale and possession of assault style weapons, as well as high-capacity
magazines and body armor , except as needed by military and law enforcement
bodies.
We call on the United States Congress to repeal the Protection of Lawful
Commerce in Arms Act.  and all other statutes providing immunity to liability
for the manufacturer and sellers of assault weapons for their negligence,
recklessness, or failure to know their customers in the marketing and sale of
firearms.
We call on the State of Illinois to enact stricter enhanced rules for the
issuance of Firearms Owners Identification (“ FOID ”) cards, and and we call on
the State to more rigorously screen applicants for FOID cards and make more
comprehensive use of clear and present danger reporting data in connection with
the review of FOID card applications.
 
President Rintz commends the work of Council on a difficult discussion
Trustee Cripe, seconded by Trustee Dalman, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-100-
2022, as amended. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustee Apatoff,
Cripe, Dalman, Dearborn, Handler, and Swierk. Nays: None. Absent: None.
9)   Old Business. 
None.
10)   New Business.
None.
11)   Appointments:
 
Appoint current Environmental and Forestry Commission member Patrick Hanley to
a full term as Environmental and Forestry Commission Chair – his term will
expire on September 20, 2025.
Trustee Dalman, seconded by Trustee Apatoff, moved to appoint Patrick Hanley to
a full term as Environmental and Forestry Commission Chair. His term will
expire on 9/20/2025. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees
Apatoff, Cripe, Dalman, Dearborn, Handler, and Swierk. Nays: None. Absent:
None.
Appoint Peter Eck to a three-year term on the Environmental and Forestry
Commission – his term will expire on September 20, 2025. Peter is filling a
vacancy from Chuck Dowding’s resignation.
Trustee Dalman, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to appoint Peter Eck to a
three-year term on the Environmental & Forestry Commission. His term will
expire on 9/20/2025. Peter is filling a vacancy from Chuck Dowding’s
resignation. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Apatoff,
Cripe, Dalman, Dearborn, Handler, and Swierk. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Appoint Mark Haller to a full term on the Zoning Board of Appeals. His term
will expire on September 20, 2027.
