Ordinance No. M-03-2023: 1303 – 1311 Scott Subdivision and Variation
(Introduction/Adoption)
Community Development Director David Schoon confirms that the applicants are
requesting a zoning variation and approval of a final plat of subdivision,
increasing the lot size for 1311 Scott and demolishing the existing 1303 Scott
residence.
Kristen Goodfellow, the applicant, addresses Council regarding the home
improvements.
Trustee Dalman, seconded by Trustee Swierk, moved to introduce Ordinance No. M-
03-2023. By voice vote, the motion carried.
Trustee Dalman, seconded by Trustee Swierk, moved to adopt Ordinance No. M-03-
2023. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustee Apatoff, Cripe,
Dalman, Dearborn, Handler, and Swierk. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Resolution No. R-13-2023: Approving a Contract with Independent Mechanical
Industries, Inc. for the Tube Installation and Boiler Casing Replacement in
Boiler #8 (Adoption)
Water & Electric Director Brian Keys informs Council that following the failure
of a “water wall” tube last year, Boiler #8 now requires repairs to its boiler
tubes and the boiler casing.
Staff is suggesting approval of a contract with Independent Mechanical
Industries, Inc. due to previous successfully completed projects by the vendor.
Trustee Dearborn, seconded by Trustee Cripe, moved to adopt Resolution No. R-
13-2023. By roll call vote, the motion carried. Ayes: Trustee Apatoff, Cripe,
Dalman, Dearborn, Handler, and Swierk. Nays: None. Absent: None.
Old Business.
One Winnetka Revised Planned Development Concept Plan Review
 
Community Development Director David Schoon informs members of the public and
Council that the concept plan review provides the applicant an opportunity to
present their ideas to Council and to receive individual feedback.
 
David Schoon reviews the amended proposed planned development concept plan, a
four-story mixed use planned development consisting of residential rental
units, commercial space, and commercial and residential use parking spaces. Mr.
Schoon indicates the zoning code exceptions, variations, special use permits,
and zoning and subdivision relief.
 
John Murphy, of Murphy Development Group, and John Talty, of OKW Architects,
present the proposed changes to the development such as changes to commercial
use, commercial and residential parking changes, increase in residential units,
and commercial use improvements.
 
John Talty discusses proposed plans related to commercial spacing, residential
unit sizes, development scaling, utilization of the development spacing,
proposed building aesthetic and façade, and the proposed development layout.
 
Council discusses the developments scaling, matters related to zoning,
pedestrian safety concerns regarding vehicular traffic, and the overall
aesthetic and façade of the development.
 
Public Comment
John Phelan requests that the Village include an ordinance for monitoring
airborne pollution.
 
Frank Petrin suggests Council members walk the One Winnetka site.
Trustee Apatoff encourages that the developer considers the publics feedback
and concerns regarding the development and expresses his concerns related to
the building scaling and pass through.
Council commends the work of the proposed development, however, suggests that
the developer continue to focus on commercial spacing to promote increased foot
traffic within the Village’s business district.
President Rintz suggests improvements on the Elm and Lincoln corner from an
architectural standpoint and encourages the developer to consider long term
effects of a dark façade given its direct exposure to sunlight.
New Business.
None.
Appointments:
None.
Closed Session: 
None.
Adjournment.
President Rintz asks for call to adjourn. Trustee Dalman, seconded by Trustee
Cripe, moved to adjourn the meeting. By voice vote, the motion carried. The
meeting adjourned at 8:28 PM.
 
   /s/Berina Gradjan, Deputy Village Clerk   
    Recording Secretary
