January 31, 2023
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
SPECIAL STUDY SESSION
January 31, 2023
(Approved: February 21, 2023)
 
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held at Council Chambers on Tuesday, January 31, 2023, at
7:00 PM.
Call to Order.
President Rintz called the meeting to order at 7:03 PM. Manager Bahan called
the roll of the Village Council. Present: Trustees Rob Apatoff, Andy Cripe,
Tina Dalman, Bob Dearborn, Kim Handler, and John Swierk. Absent: None. Also
present: Village Manager Robert Bahan, Assistant Village Manager Kristin
Kazenas, Village Attorney Peter Friedman, Deputy Village Clerk Berina Gradjan,
Community Development Director David Schoon, Assistant Community Development
Director Ann Klaassen, Community Development Building & Code Enforcement
Manager Davorka Kirincic, Engineering Director Jim Bernahl, and approximately
49 persons in the audience.
 
Public Comment.
Ted Wynnychenko addresses Council regarding lakefront matters.
Lakefront Regulations Discussion and Direction – Continued.
President Rintz indicates that Council will continue discussing matters related
to existing zoning, development, and engineering regulations regarding the
lakefront.
Trustee Cripe emphasizes the importance of respecting both the lakefront
homeowners privacy as well as the health, safety, and welfare of the public
acsessing the lakefront.
Trustee Cripe presents information regarding lakefront regulations currently
established in the existing Village Code, enforcement of required permits for
fences, walls, and impermeable surfaces along the lakefront, maintenance duties
protecting the safety of the public, landscaping regulations on private
premises, public nuisance related matters, variance standards, and matters
addressing public safety and public health.
Council commends Trustee Cripe’s informative presentation regarding existing
lakefront regulations.
While Council recongnizes that there is a lack of clarity regarding lakefront
rules, regulations, and enforcement measures in the existing Village Code,
Council consensus is to implement clarification and guidance and continue to be
proactive with constructive conversations related to the lakefront.
Trustee Dalman emphasizes that study sessions are used for educational purposes
and opportunities to express concerns, thoughts, and feedback for topics of
discussion.
Council discusses matters related to permitting processes, zoning code
regulations and improvements, variances, and lakefront standards.
Trustee Dearborn suggests that the Village implement enforcement measures for
permit review and approval while the Village Code is in the process of being
amended. Council indicates the importance of proceeding with constructive
engagement with both lakefront homeowners and the community at large.
Trustee Dalman encourages members of the public to utilize the Village website
for information and subscribe to the E-Newsletter to stay up-to-date with
Village news and current issues.
Village Attorney Peter Friedman indicates that any amendments related to the
Village Code will be brought before Council for review.
Dall Frank commends the work of Village Council and encourages Council to
consider including lakefront homeowners in draft ordinances, provisions, and
direction.
John Edwardson emphasizes the importance of communication between Council and
members of the public and encourages Council to work diligently with the
Illinois Department of Natural Resources and the U.S. Army Corps of Engineers.
Christina Codo commends Council on their work and the informative material
provided for members of the public.
Warren James expresses concerns related to lakefront matters and Park District
involvement.
Jim Rowland encourages Council to survey Winnetka residents on various issues.
Susie Schreiber addresses Council on matters related to the Lake Michigan
Lakewide Management Plan.
David Stevens addresses Council on matters related to lakefront concerns and
issues.
Katie Stevens encourages Council to exercise their jurisdiction and enforcement
related to beach access.
Steve Ginison expresses concerns related to lakefront matters.
Adjournment.
Trustee Cripe, seconded by Trustee Apatoff, moved to adjourn the meeting. By
voice vote, the motion carried. The meeting adjourned at 9:12 PM.
   /s/Berina Gradjan, Deputy Village Clerk   
    Recording Secretary
