January 16, 2024
MINUTES
WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL
STUDY SESSION
(Approved: February 6, 2024)
 
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held at the Council Chambers on Tuesday, January 16, 2024
at 7:00 PM.
1)   Call to Order.
President Rintz called the meeting to order at 7:05 PM. Village Manager Bahan
called the roll of the Village Council. Present: Trustees Kirk Albinson, Rob
Apatoff, Tina Dalman, Kim Handler and Bridget Orsic. Absent: Trustee Bob
Dearborn. Also present: Village Manager Robert Bahan, Assistant Village Manager
Kristin Kazenas, Village Attorney Courtney Willits, Deputy Village Clerk Berina
Gradjan, and approximately 5 persons in the audience.
2)   Public Comment.
i)    Ted Wynnychenko addresses Council on matters related to the Bertling-
Hill-Woodland pathway vacation.
3)   General Village Council Discussion Regarding Policy Matters for Future
Agendas.
President Rintz states that Council will be reviewing current Village projects,
initiatives and discussing suggestions for policy matters for 2024 and beyond.
The discussion provides guidance for Council members, and the community,
regarding Village related agenda items, emerging topics, actionable items, and
aids in providing a better sense of direction with upcoming matters within the
Village.
Village Council reviews the 2024 ongoing projects including stormwater
management; Crow Island stormwater project, Hibbard County Preserve project,
west side stormwater conveyance design, and stormwater communication & grants.
In addition to ongoing stormwater related projects, Council discusses the
advanced metering infrastructure, lead service line replacement, AT&T
undergrounding, building code updates, and IDOT jurisdictional transfers.
Current ongoing Council business consists of the One Winnetka planned
development, Chase Bank planned development, Park District special use
application for Elder & Centennial, Green Bay Trail, and the Boris Café
development site.
Lastly, Council reviews the 2022-2023 Committed Pipeline Projects including the
Electric Plant investment evaluation, IMEA power contract, post office
planning, Hubbard Woods streetscape plan, Indian Hill lot redevelopment, east
side stormwater evaluation and planning, and the Green Bay Road corridor plan
with Kenilworth.
Council discusses matters related to Electric Plant reinvestment, IMEA
contracts, engagement with the lower advisory boards, Union Pacific, various
development and housing, post office site, priority of Village projects and
business, and zoning code updates.
President Rintz emphasizes the importance of performance security as it has
been vital for Council business over the years.
Council commends staff on their exceptional work related to Village projects.
Public Comment.
a)    Ted Wynnychenko addresses Council regarding AT&T undergrounding and
matters related to public access along the lakefront.
4)   Closed Session
for the Purpose of Discussing Specific Personnel, Pending Litigation, and
Review of Closed Session Minutes pursuant to Section 2(c)(1), Section 2(c)(11)
and Section 2(c)(21) of the Open Meetings Act.
Trustee Albinson, seconded by Trustee Dalman, moved to adjourn to Closed
Session for the purpose of discussing Specific Personnel, Pending Litigation,
and Review of Closed Session Minutes pursuant to Section 2(c)(1), Section 2(c)
(11) and Section 2(c)(21) of the Open Meetings Act and to adjourn the Open
Meeting automatically and immediately upon the conclusion of the Closed Session
without the conduct of any further business or comments. By roll call vote, the
motion carried. Ayes: Trustees Albinson, Apatoff, Dalman, Handler, and
Orsic. Nays: None. Absent: Trustee Dearborn.
The Council adjourned into Closed Session at 8:17 p.m.
5)   Adjournment.
The Closed Session meeting ended at 9:27 pm.
 
 
 
/s/ Berina Gradjan, Deputy Village Clerk 
Recording Secretary
 
 
