March 12, 2024
(Approved: April 2, 2024)
(Amended: April 16, 2024)
 
A record of a legally convened meeting of the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, which was held at the Council Chambers on Tuesday, March 12, 2024 at
7:00 PM.
1)   Call to Order.
President Rintz called the meeting to order at 7:01 PM. Village Manager Bahan
called the roll of the Village Council. Present: Trustees Kirk Albinson, Rob
Apatoff, Tina Dalman, Bob Dearborn, Kim Handler and Bridget Orsic. Absent:
None. Also present: Village Manager Robert Bahan, Assistant Village Manager
Kristin Kazenas, Village Attorney Ben Schuster, Deputy Village Clerk Berina
Gradjan, and approximately 3 persons in the audience.
President Rintz confirms that Trustee Apatoff has provided the required notice
that he will be unable to attend the regular meeting due to work obligations.
The Village’s policy provides that Trustee Apatoff may participate remotely
unless 2/3 of the Council objects. With no objections, Trustee Apatoff
participated remotely fully in the meeting.
2)   Public Comment.
i)    Ted Wynnychenko addresses Council on the Village’s involvement and
leadership regarding climate matters.
ii)    Teresa Lucas suggests that any affiliated members of organizations state
their roles during any public commentary.
3)   Discussion.
a)   Post Office Plaza – General Discussion on Next Steps
President Rintz and Council discuss the future development of the Post Office
Plaza. Council discusses the costs and benefits of either utilizing
international state-of-the-art cutting-edge architects to lead the process or
utilizing a well respected but local firm. President Rintz engaged with Jeanne
Gang, the founding partner of Studio Gang, and recognizes the work the Village
would benefit from with her experience. However, President Rintz also
emphasizes the recognition of the involvement of the community with the overall
development of the Post Office site.
Council discusses the importance of utilizing practitioners with developing the
public space, understanding the community and the various challenges of
creating a vision of a complex development while implementing historic elements
that will enhance the community. Council consensus was to continue considering
various architectural firms with regional or national reputation.
Public Comment.
Teresa Lucas commends the engagement with Jeanne Gang, based upon her previous
experience in Glencoe.
b)   Park District Update and Discussion
 
President Rintz, and Trustee Rob Apatoff, attended a meeting with the Park
District on matters related to the Elder/Centennial project to be brought forth
before Village Council for approvals as a whole. The Park District was provided
two options, an intergovernmental agreement with the Village, or go through the
special use permit process which requires the Park District to go through the
Design Review Board, Plan Development Commission, Zoning Board of Appeals, and
the Village Council for final approval.   
Village Attorney Ben Schuster provides information regarding the Village Code;
application for zoning relief, required standards, and public hearings.
Council discusses the potential of two application submissions, Elder and
Centennial, or a consolidated project, implementation of an intergovernmental
agreement on the stormwater pipe, application and hearing process, assigning
liaisons for both the Park District and Village Council regarding
communication, and matters related to stormwater.
Trustee Dearborn suggested that the stormwater co-liaison role, currently being
served by President Rintz and Trustee Apatoff, be expanded to include issues
related to the Winnetka Park District Elder/Centennial lakefront improvement
plan generally. Given the complexity of the project, the reason to formally
expand the scope would be to facilitate efficient communication with the Park
District as appropriate. There were expressions of support, and the village
attorney confirmed Council consensus, but the Council was not taking any final
action or voting in the study session. Manager Bahan noted that Council liaison
assignments were approved by the Council at the start of each new term.
President Rintz concurred and commented that while the expanded liaison
approach could improve communication, an important consideration is if the Park
District decides to pursue the special use process that the Council not usurp
the advisory board’s review and authority during this process.
   Public Comment.
Ted Wynnychenko addresses Council regarding Park District matters are
Centennial Beach, the transfer of land to private ownership, and encourages the
Council and Park District to have a collaborative approach.
4)   Adjournment.
Trustee Dearborn, seconded by Trustee Dalman moved to adjourn the meeting. By
voice vote, the motion carried. The meeting adjourned at 8:21 pm.
 
 
 
 
/s/ Berina Gradjan, Deputy Village Clerk 
Recording Secretary
