ORDINANCE NO. M-518-98 (not adopted)
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (744 TOWER ROAD)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
   Lot 3 and sub lot 1, in Trustees Subdivision of all of that portion of Lot
1, North or Northwesterly of a line drawn perpendicular to the center line of
Sheridan Road (formerly Lake Avenue) and passing through an iron stake which
said stake is in the east line of said Sheridan Road, 478 feet southeasterly
along said east line from the point of intersection of said east line and the
south line of North Avenue, Said Lot 1, being in Lloyd and Robinson's
Subdivision, in Sections 16 and 17, Township 42 North, Range 13, East of the
Third Principal Meridian, in Cook County, Illinois;
commonly known as 744 Tower Road, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the "A-3"
(One-Half Acre) Single-Family District provided in the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance, Chapter 22, Winnetka Village Code, for variations from the setback
requirements of Section 22.07(h) of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, to allow a
north front setback of 42.27 feet instead of the minimum required front setback
of 50 feet, resulting in a variation of 7.73 feet (15.06%), and to allow a
south front setback of 24 feet instead of the minimum required front setback of
50 feet, resulting in a variation of 26 feet (52%), said variations being
requested for the purpose of allowing the construction of a one- and two-story
addition to the existing single family home on the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, on November 3, 1997, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variations and, by the
unanimous vote of the Board's seven members, has reported to the Council
recommending that the two requested setback variations be granted; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and unique hardships associated
with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect
to the Subject Property because of the irregular shape of the Subject Property,
the original placement of the one-story home on the lot in a non-conforming
location, and the irregular shape of the permitted building area that results
from the terminus of a private street at the southwest corner of the Subject
Property and the Tower Road frontage along the north lot line; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if the
requested variation is not granted, in light of the Subject Property’s
lakefront location and the sizes of lots and homes in the immediate vicinity;
and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because the proposed addition is consistent with the design
and scale of the existing home and the scale of homes in the neighborhood,
because the size of the existing home, including the proposed addition, is less
than the maximum building size allowed on the Subject Property, and because the
nonconformity proposed results from the vertical extension of the existing
building, of which only a portion is within the required setbacks; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed addition will not impair an adequate supply of light
and air because of the minimal intrusion of the proposed addition into the
required front setbacks and because of the large size of the Subject Property
and other zoning lots in the area; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed addition; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed addition will not contribute to congestion on the
public streets, as the structure will continue to be used as a single family
residence; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed addition will not otherwise impair the public health,
safety, comfort morals, and welfare of the inhabitants of the Village.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain:
   SECTION 1: That variations are hereby granted with respect to the Subject
Property, commonly known as 744 Tower Road, which is located in the "A-3" (One-
Half Acre) Single-Family Residential District provided in the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance, Chapter 22, Winnetka Village Code, from the setback requirements of
Section 22.07(h), to allow a north front setback of 42.27 feet instead of the
minimum required front setback of 50 feet, resulting in a variation of 7.73
feet (15.06%), and to allow a south front setback of 24 feet instead of the
minimum required front setback of 50 feet, resulting in a variation of 26 feet
(52%), said variations being requested for the purpose of allowing the
construction of a one- and two-story addition to the existing single family
home on the Subject Property; and
   SECTION 2: That the variation hereby granted is conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 3: That this Ordinance shall be in full force and effect from and
after its passage, approval, and posting.
   Passed and approved this ____ day of _________, 1998.
   AYES:
   NAYS:
   ABSENT:
            Signed:
               Village President
Countersigned:
Village Clerk
Introduced:
Posted:
Passed and Approved:
Posted:
ORDINANCE NO. M-519-98 (not adopted)
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (986 ASBURY COURT)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
   Lot 1 in Blietz' Winnetka Subdivision, being a Subdivision of that part of
the East 1/2 of the Southeast 1/4 of the Northwest 1/4 of Section 18, Township
42 North, Range 13 East of the Third Principal Meridian, lying North of a line
lying midway between the centerline of Asbury Avenue and Englewood Lane
extended West of Grove Street and that part of the West 1/2 of the Southeast 1/
4 of the Northwest 1/4 lying North of the centerline of Edgewood Lane extended
West in Section 18, Township 42 North, Range 13, East of the Third Principal
Meridian, in Cook County, Illinois;
commonly known as 986 Asbury Court, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the "A-
3" (One-Half Acre) Single-Family District provided in the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance, Chapter 22, Winnetka Village Code, for a variation from the maximum
building size (Gross Floor Area) limitations of Section 22.07(g) of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, to allow a gross floor area of 6,384.34 square feet,
whereas 6,167.60 square feet are permitted, resulting in a variation of 216.74
square feet (3.5%), said variation being requested for the purpose of allowing
the completion of an exterior balcony on the second floor of the single family
home under construction on the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, on November 3, 1997, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variation and, by the
favorable vote of five of the Board's seven members, has reported to the
Council recommending that the requested variation be granted; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and unique hardships associated
with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect
to the Subject Property in that the home under construction of the Subject
Property was of a conforming size when the permits were approved for its
construction, but was rendered nonconforming by the amendment of the Winnetka
Zoning Ordinance to include attic spaces and floor levels below cantilevered
features in calculating the maximum building size (Gross Floor Area); and
   WHEREAS, although the balcony for which the variation is sought has an area
of 57 square feet, the recalculated maximum building size also now includes
890.63 square feet of attic area, resulting in a nonconformity of 216.74 square
feet; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because the proposed balcony has a small area, is not roofed
and is not visible from the street; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed addition will not impair an adequate supply of light
and air because of the minimal size and open design of the proposed balcony;
and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property will provide an additional means of
egress in the event of a fire; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed addition; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed addition will not contribute to congestion on the
public streets, as the structure will continue to be used as a single family
residence; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed addition will not otherwise impair the public health,
safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the inhabitants of the Village.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain:
   SECTION 1: That a variation is hereby granted with respect to the Subject
Property, commonly known as 986 Asbury Court, which is located in the "A-3"
(One-Half Acre) Single-Family Residential District provided in the Winnetka
Zoning Ordinance, Chapter 22, Winnetka Village Code, for a variation from the
maximum building size (Gross Floor Area) limitations of Section 22.07(g) of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, to allow a gross floor area of 6,384.34 square feet,
whereas 6,167.60 square feet are permitted, resulting in a variation of 216.74
square feet (3.5%), said variation being requested for the purpose of allowing
the completion of an exterior balcony on the second floor of the single family
home under construction on the Subject Property.
   SECTION 2: That the variation hereby granted is conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 3: That this Ordinance shall be in full force and effect from and
after its passage, approval, and posting.
   Passed and approved this ___ day of __________________, 1998.
