ORDINANCE NO. M-521-98
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (1177 ASH STREET)
WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
   The East 10 ft. of Lot 14 and the West 40 ft. of Lot 15 in Block 4, Winnetka
Manor, a Subdivision of the South 45 acres of the West 90 acres of the
Northwest quarter of Section 20, Township 42 North, Range 13, East of the Third
Principal Meridian, in Cook County, Illinois.
Also:
   Lot 15 (except the West 40 ft.) and the West 40 ft. of Lot 16, in Block 4,
Winnetka Manor, a Subdivision of the South 45 acres of the West 90 acres of the
Northwest quarter of Section 20, Township 42 North, Range 13, East of the Third
Principal Meridian, in Cook County, Illinois.
commonly known as 1177 Ash Street, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the “A-1”
(One-Sixth Acre) Single-Family District provided in the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance, Chapter 22, Winnetka Village Code, for a variation from the
provisions of Section 22.05(e) of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, to permit the
subdivision of the 100 foot wide Subject Property into two lots, each having a
width of 50 feet, instead of the required 60 foot minimum lot width required by
Section 22.05(e), resulting in a variation of 10 feet (16.7%) on each proposed
new lot; and
WHEREAS, on March 11, 1998, the Plan Commission considered the owners’
application for preliminary approval of the subdivision of the Subject Property
into two 50-foot wide lots and, by a vote of 8 to 1, has preliminarily approved
the proposed subdivision subject to the owners’ obtaining a zoning variation
from the minimum lot width requirement of the Zoning Ordinance; and
WHEREAS, on April 20, 1998, the Zoning Board of Appeals conducted a public
hearing on the requested variation and, by the unanimous vote of the four
members then present, the Zoning Board of Appeals has reported to the Council
recommending that the requested variation be granted; and
WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and unique circumstances associated
with carrying out the strict application of the minimum lot width requirement
of the Zoning Ordinance with respect to the Subject Property in that with one
exception all of the lots on the same block face and all of the lots abutting
the rear lot lines of those lots, are 50 feet wide, while the Subject Property
has been a 100 foot wide zoning lot under the Zoning Ordinance and thus needs
to be subdivided to restore it to the original lot configuration and lot width
that existed at the time of the original subdivision in 1936; and
WHEREAS, the requested variation is in harmony with the general purpose and
intent of the Zoning Ordinance, as the “A-1” (One-Sixth Acre) Single-Family
District has the smallest lot dimensions of all of the zoning districts and the
variation, if granted, will allow for the construction of two single-family
residents consistent with the distinct purpose stated in Section 22.05(a) of
the Zoning Ordinance, which is intended “to provide for a single-family
residential environment of relatively intense suburban character,” and to
maintain the existing building scale in the neighborhood; and
WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if the 60-foot
minimum lot width requirement is strictly applied to it, as it would result in
the creation of one 60-foot lot and one 40-foot lot, the latter being narrower
than any of the other lots in the area or in requiring the owners to maintain a
100-foot wide lot, although the existing home requires only a 50-foot lot width
and the other lots in the area are 50-feet wide; and
WHEREAS, the requested lot width variation will not alter the essential
character of the neighborhood, as it will create two lots that are the same
size as the other lots in the neighborhood, and will result in redevelopment
consistent with the scale of the neighborhood, as the two new lots will be
subject to the same bulk limitations as the other lots; and
WHEREAS, the requested lot width variation will not impair an adequate supply
of light and air, as the existing residence meets all requirements of the
Zoning Ordinance pertaining to building bulk and location, the new lots will be
the same width as the other lots in the immediate vicinity and any new
development on the proposed new lots would be subject to the same requirements;
and
WHEREAS, the requested lot width variation will not increase the hazard from
fire and other damages to the Subject Property as the existing use and
buildings would not be changed and any new construction would comply with all
applicable building and fire protection codes; and
WHEREAS, the requested variation will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings through the Village, and will add a new tax parcel which can be
developed in the same manner as other properties in the vicinity, thereby
increasing the taxable value of land; and
WHEREAS, the requested variation will not contribute to congestion on public
streets as both the existing residence and any new development will be limited
to use as a single family residence; and
WHEREAS, the requested variation will not otherwise impair the public health,
safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the Village and its citizens; and
WHEREAS, denial of the requested variation is not in the interests of the
public comfort and welfare of the Village and its citizens, since allowing a
100 foot wide lot to remain among the 50 foot wide lots in the area would
enable the current or subsequent owners to build a house of such a size that it
would be out of scale and incompatible with the immediate neighborhood and will
thus have a negative impact on properties in the immediate vicinity.
NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
SECTION 1: That the foregoing recitals are incorporated herein as the findings
of the Council of the Village of Winnetka.
   SECTION 2 : That a variation is hereby granted with respect to the Subject
Property, commonly known as 1177 Ash Street, which is located in the “A-1”
(One-Sixth Acre) Single-Family Residential District provided in the Winnetka
Zoning Ordinance, Chapter 22, Winnetka Village Code, from the lot width
requirements of Section 22.05(e), to allow the 100 foot wide Subject Property
to be subdivided into two lots, each having a width of 50 feet, instead of the
required 60 foot minimum lot width required by Section 22.05(e), resulting in a
variation of 10 feet (16.7%) on each proposed new lot.
   SECTION 3: That the variation hereby granted is conditioned upon the
recording of the executed plat of subdivision as approved by Resolution R-1428-
98.
   SECTION 5: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 1st day of September 1998, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES: Trustees Derning, Lanphier, Meuer, Hilton, Lien, Duhl
   NAYS: None
   ABSENT: None
   APPROVED this 1st day of September, 1998.
               Signed:
               ss/Louise A. Holland
               Village President
