ORDINANCE NO. M-522-98
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (756 FOXDALE)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
   Lot 10 (except the southwesterly 30 ft.) in Block 6 in Winnetka Park Bluffs,
a Subdivision of Blocks 1, 2, 3, and 4 (except Lots 4, 5, and 6 in Block 4)
Blocks 5 and 6, Lots 8, 9, 10, 11, and 12, in Block 7, Lots 3, 6, 7 in Block 9,
Lots 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, and Lot 7 in Block 12 and the North 75 ft. of Block 11 in
Park Addition to Winnetka, a Subdivision in Sections 17, 20 and 21, Township 42
North, Range 13, East of the Third Principal meridian, according to the plate
thereof recorded June 30, 1891, Book 49 of Plats, page 94, in Cook County,
Illinois;
commonly known as 756 Foxdale Avenue, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the
"A-1" (One-Sixth Acre) Single-Family District provided in the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance, Chapter 22, Winnetka Village Code, for a variation from the maximum
building size (Gross Floor Area) limitations of Section 22.05(g) of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, to allow a gross floor area of 2,548.84 square feet,
whereas 2,400.48 square feet are permitted, resulting in a variation of 148.36
square feet (6.18%), said variation being requested for the purpose of allowing
a second story addition to remain without removing the walls that enclose two
porches on the single family home on the subject property; and
   WHEREAS, on March 9, 1998, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals began a public hearing on the requested variation with five of the
Board's seven members present; and
   WHEREAS, the vote of the five members present at the meeting on March 9,
1998, was insufficient to submit a favorable recommendation to the Council,
with only three of the members present voting to recommend that the requested
variation be granted and two of the members present voting to recommend denial;
and
   WHEREAS, at the request of the owners of the Subject Property, the matter
was continued to April 20, 1998, to allow members of the Board who were not
present on March 9 to review and vote on the application pursuant to the first
paragraph of subsection (b) of Section 22.16 of the Zoning Ordinance; and
   WHEREAS, on April 20, 1998, one of the members who was absent on March 9
certified that she had reviewed the entire record and voted in favor of the
application, and the Board has accordingly reported to the Council that it has
recommended that the requested variation be granted, with four members voting
to recommend approval and two members voting against recommending approval; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and unique hardships associated
with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect
to the Subject Property in that the home is a Chicago bungalow style, built in
1915, the slope of the roof in this style of home is such that approximately
400 square feet of gross floor area under the roof is not high enough to be
usable, and raising a portion of the roof by using a dormer results in the
upper ½ story being considered a full story, with the square footage of the
entire upper story being included in the gross floor area; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that
the proposed addition is for the purpose of adding a third bedroom, and the
owners have established that three bedrooms is the minimum standard for single
family homes in the area and not allowing the addition will increase the
likelihood that the home will need to be replaced with a new home that has more
usable square footage, although similar new homes in the area substantially add
to visible bulk; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because the proposed addition is a dormer in the rear slope of
the roof, and does not increase either the footprint of the building or the
visible bulk of the building; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed addition will not impair an adequate supply of light
and air because of the minimal size of the dormer addition, because of its
location at the rear of the home, and because there are no residential
properties abutting the rear yard which is adjacent to the Green Bay Trail and
Metra commuter railroad right-of-way; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property as it will comply with all applicable
building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed addition; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed addition will not contribute to congestion on the
public streets, as the structure will continue to be used as a single family
residence; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed addition will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain:
   SECTION 1: That a variation is hereby granted with respect to the Subject
Property, commonly known as 756 Foxdale Avenue, which is located in the "A-1"
(One-Sixth Acre) Single-Family Residential District provided in the Winnetka
Zoning Ordinance, Chapter 22, Winnetka Village Code, for a variation from the
maximum building size (Gross Floor Area) limitations of Section 22.05(g) of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, to allow a gross floor area of 2,548.84 square feet,
whereas 2,400.48 square feet are permitted, resulting in a variation of 148.36
square feet (6.18%), said variation being requested for the purpose of allowing
a second story addition to remain without removing the walls that enclose two
porches on the single family home on the Subject Property.
   SECTION 2: That the variation hereby granted is conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance and upon the applicants’ having paid all applicable fees
and charges within 30 days after the adoption of this ordinance.
   SECTION 3: That this Ordinance shall be in full force and effect from and
after its passage, approval, and posting.
   Passed and approved this 2nd day of June, 1998.
   AYES: Trustees Derning, Duhl, Lanphier, Lien and Meuer
   NAYS: None
   ABSENT: Trustee Hilton
               Signed:
                  ss/Louise A. Holland
   Village President
