ORDINANCE NO: M-524-98
ORDINANCE ESTABLISHING 930 FISHER LANE AS A DESIGNATED LANDMARK
WHEREAS, the Village of Winnetka desires to identify, designate and preserve
buildings and structures in the Village that are historically, culturally, and
architecturally significant and to encourage the restoration and rehabilitation
of those buildings and structures; and
WHEREAS, pursuant to Sections 11-48.2-1, et seq. of the Illinois Municipal Code
(65 ILCS 5/11-48.2-1, et seq.), the Council of the Village of Winnetka enacted
Chapter 20 of the Winnetka Village Code, entitled “Landmark Preservation,” for
the purposes of preserving, protecting, enhancing, rehabilitating and
regulating buildings, structures, objects, and places of historical, cultural
or architectural importance; and
WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka has determined that it is in
the best interests of the Village and its residents that participation in the
landmark designation process shall be limited to those owners who voluntarily
elect to participate in that process; and
WHEREAS, pursuant to the provisions of Chapter 20 of the Winnetka Village Code
(“Landmark Ordinance”) and notice duly published and sent, the Landmark
Preservation Commission conducted a public hearing on March 16, 1998, to
consider the application for landmark designation submitted by the owner of the
property located at 930 Fisher Lane (permanent real estate index number 05-17-
200-075); and
WHEREAS, the Landmark Preservation Commission applied the Village’s System for
the Evaluation of Landmarks to the information received at the hearing and made
findings regarding the property at 930 Fisher Lane, including the following:
(1)   That it is a significant property, being an understated colonial revival
residence, featuring a center entrance, paired double-hung windows, a shallow
cornice decorated with medallions, and five bays across the front, with the
center three projecting from the main block of the house; and
(2)   That the house is further distinguished by a wood-shingled, flat-topped
hip roof with flared (belcast) eaves and two shed dormers, and wide-reveal wood
drop siding rather than the more typical overlapping clapboard siding, all of
which make the house rare in the context of Winnetka; and
(3)   That although the method of construction of the house is common, the
wide-reveal wood drop siding is unusual; and
(4)   That the residence merits the highest point value for association with a
master architect or builder, because, although the identity of the original
architect has not been confirmed, families who have lived in the house believe
it to have been designed by Boston architect, Charles Platt, a prominent
architect of his day, and the house’s present day appearance is due to
sensitive alterations made to the front of the main house more than fifty years
ago by Herman Lackner, a well respected North Shore architect from Winnetka;
and
(5)   That due to its location in a small neighborhood far away from frequently
traveled streets, the house is conspicuous and familiar in the context of its
neighborhood; and
(6)   That although the design integrity of the exterior of the building was
rated as fair due to the significant changes made to the front of the building,
the sensitive nature of those changes and the more than fifty years that they
have remained in place have led to those changes having an historic importance
of their own; and
WHEREAS, based on its findings, the Landmark Preservation Commission concluded
that the residence located at 930 Fisher Lane meets the criteria of the
Landmark Ordinance and unanimously recommended that it be so designated; and
WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka have considered the
recommendation of the Landmark Preservation Commission and have determined that
it is in the best interest of the Village and its residents to establish the
property located at 930 Fisher Lane as a designated landmark.
NOW THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain:
SECTION 1 : That the property located at 930 Fisher Lane is hereby designated a
landmark under the Landmark Ordinance, in accordance with the findings and
recommendations of the Landmark Preservation Commission.
   SECTION 2 : This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 2nd day of June, 1998, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:      Trustees Derning, Duhl, Lanphier, Lien and Meuer
   NAYES:   None
   ABSENT:   Trustee Hilton
   APPROVED this 2nd day of June, 1998.
   Signed
   ss/Louise A. Holland
   Village President
Countersigned:
Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
