ORDINANCE NO. M-544-98
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (788 CHERRY STREET)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
   The North 65 feet, lying South of the South line of Cherry Street, of the
East ½ of Block 52 (except the West 50 feet thereof) in Winnetka, a Subdivision
in Sections 20 and 21, Township 42 North, Range 13, East of the Third Principal
Meridian;
commonly known as 788 Cherry Street, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the "A-
1" (One-Sixth Acre) Single-Family Residential District provided in the Winnetka
Zoning Ordinance, Chapter 22, Winnetka Village Code, for the following
variations: (a) from the roofed lot coverage limitations of Section 22.05(f) of
the Zoning Ordinance to allow a total roofed lot coverage of 2,386.71 square
feet, whereas 2,226.08 square feet are permitted, resulting in a variation of
160.63 square feet (7.22%); (b) from the side yard setback requirements of
Section 22.05(i) to allow a south side yard setback of 5.92 feet, whereas 6
feet are required, resulting in a variation of 0.08 feet (1.33%); and (c) from
the rear yard setback requirements of Section 22.05(j) to allow a rear yard
setback of 15.12 feet, whereas 20.55 feet are required, resulting in a
variation of 5.43 feet (26.42%), all for the purpose of expanding a first floor
attached garage by 5 feet and adding approximately 12 square feet to the front
porch on the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, on September 14, 1998, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variation and by the
unanimous vote of the six members then present, has reported to the Council
recommending that the requested variation be granted; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and particular hardships
associated with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance
with respect to the Subject Property in that: (a) the Subject Property is a
corner lot; (b) the original construction of the house and attached garage
placed them close to the south and west lot lines in order to meet required
corner setbacks; (c) placing a two-car garage in a conforming location would
increase the impermeable surface on the Subject Property and would interrupt
the building lines because it would require the garage to be detached; and (d)
the additional square footage at the front porch to create a new front stoop is
minimal; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that
the requested variations are for the purpose of allowing an existing 1½ car
garage to be expanded by 5 feet, thereby creating a two-car garage, which is
the standard in this area, and placing a two-car garage in a conforming
location on the Subject Property would negatively affect the Subject Property
by interrupting the building lines and creating an unappealing second building
mass facing the street; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because they are relatively small in size and maintain the
existing building line and scale; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not impair an adequate supply of
light and air because the new front stoop is lower than the building height and
the five-foot extension of the garage is one story high and situated away from
other buildings; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property as it will comply with all applicable
building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed new first floor garage expansion and front stoop
addition will not contribute to congestion on the public streets, as the
structure will continue to be used as a single family residence, and the
extension of the garage will allow an additional car to be parked in an
enclosed space; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed addition will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village.
NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
SECTION 1: That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the findings
of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth herein.
   SECTION 2: That the following variations are hereby granted with respect to
the Subject Property, commonly known as 788 Cherry Street, which is located in
the “A-1” (One-Sixth Acre) Single-Family Residential District provided in the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Chapter 22, Winnetka Village Code: (a) the Subject
Property is a corner lot; (b) the house and attached garage have been situated
close to the south and west lot lines in order to meet required corner
setbacks; (c) placing a two-car garage in a conforming location would increase
the impermeable surface on the Subject Property and would interrupt the
building lines because it would require the garage to be detached; and (d) the
additional square footage at the front porch to create a new front stoop is
minimal, in accordance with the application for variations and the drawings and
plans submitted with the application.
   SECTION 3: That the variation hereby granted is conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed constructing within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage, approval
and posting.
   ADOPTED this 20th day of October, 1998, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES: Trustees Lanphier, Lien, Meuer, Hilton, Derning and Duhl
   NAYS: None
   ABSENT: None
   APPROVED this 20th day of October, 1998.
   Signed:
   ss/Louise A. Holland
   Village President
Countersigned:
ss/Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: October 6, 1998
Posted: October 7, 1998
Passed and Approved: October 20, 1998
Posted: October 21, 1998
ORDINANCE NO. M-545-98
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A SPECIAL USE PERMIT AND VARIATIONS IN THE APPLICATION OF
THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA, COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS TO A
PROPOSED SITE FOR WIRELESS TELECOMMUNICATIONS ANTENNAS AND EQUIPMENT (911 GREEN
BAY ROAD)
   WHEREAS, Harris Bank Winnetka is the trustee for the John J. Gabler Trust
(hereinafter referred to collectively as the “Owner”) which owns the following
described real estate (the “Subject Property”):
   PARCEL ONE: That part of Lot 8 in Block 5 in Jared Gage’s Subdivision of
parts of Sections 8 and 17, all in Township 42 North, Range 13 east of the
Third Principal Meridian, in Cook county, Illinois, lying northeasterly of a
line drawn from a point in the northwesterly line of said Lot, 120.24 feet
northeasterly from the northwesterly corner thereof, to a point in the
southeasterly line of said Lot, 120 feet northeasterly from the southwesterly
corner of said Lot 8.
   PARCEL TWO: The northeasterly 80 feet of Lot 7 (except the northwesterly 4
feet thereof) in Block 5 in Jared Gage’s Subdivision aforesaid, described as
follows: of parts of Sections 8 and 17, all in Township 42 North, Range 13 East
of the Third Principal meridian in Cook County, Illinois.
   PARCEL THREE: That part of Lot 7 in Block 5 in Jared Gage’s Subdivision
aforesaid, described as follows: Beginning at a point on the southeasterly line
of said Lot 7 a distance of 90 feet northeasterly of the southwesterly corner
thereof; thence northwesterly parallel with the southwesterly line of said Lot
7, a distance of 5 feet; thence northerly 50.65 feet to a point on the
southwesterly line of the parallel with the northeasterly line of said Lot 7, a
distance of 46.01 feet to the southeasterly line of said Lot 7, a distance of
30 feet more or less to the point of beginning.
which is commonly known as 911 Green Bay Road, Winnetka, Illinois, and is
located in the C-2 (General Retail) Commercial Zoning District provided in the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Chapter 22, Winnetka Village Code; and
   WHEREAS, SprintCom, Inc. (“SprintCom”) has entered into an agreement with
the Owner of the Subject Property, whereby SprintCom will be allowed to place
certain wireless telecommunications service antennas and equipment on the roof
of the building located on the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the Owner and SprintCom (the
“Applicants”) for a special use permit in accordance with Section 22.13 and
22.12A of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance to permit the installation of wireless
telecommunication service antennas and equipment on the roof of the existing
building on the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, the Applicants are also requesting the following variations: (a)
from the height limitations of Section 22.12A(n)5b of the Zoning Ordinance, to
allow a height of 56.58 feet, which is more than 10 feet above the parapet of
the roof and exceeds the maximum height of 35 feet permitted under Section
22.11(c) of the Zoning Ordinance, resulting in a variation of 21.58 feet
(61.66%); and (b) from the location limitations of Section 22.12A(n)5a of the
Zoning Ordinance to allow placement of antennas and equipment 39.94 feet and
230 feet of two nearby single family residences, whereas a minimum distance of
250 feet is required, resulting in variations of 210.06 feet (84.02%) and 20
feet (8.0%) on the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, on August 20, 1998, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals opened a public hearing on the requested special use and variations
and, upon considering evidence submitted by the Applicants, the Zoning Board of
Appeals continued the hearing to September 14, 1998, to allow for a technical
review of the application pursuant to Section 22.12A(s)2 of the Zoning
Ordinance; and
   WHEREAS, on September 14, 1998, the engineer retained by the Village (the
“Independent Engineer”) to perform the independent technical review submitted
preliminary conclusions that tended to support the application of SprintCom,
but recommended that actual field testing be performed to confirm those
conclusions; and
   WHEREAS, on September 14, 1998, upon receiving the recommendation of the
Independent Engineer, the Zoning Board of Appeals concluded the public hearing,
considered the evidence and by the affirmative vote of four of the six members
then present, with the remaining two members abstaining because they had not
been present for all of the evidence, has reported to the Council recommending
that the requested variations and special use permit be granted, provided that
testing done under the supervision of the Independent Engineer confirm that a
higher priority site is not a viable alternative and that the requested roof-
mounted antennas are an engineering necessity; and
   WHEREAS, on August 19, 1998, on due notice thereof, the Plan Commission met
to consider the application and recommended, by a vote of six to one, to find
that the requested special use is consistent with the Village’s comprehensive
plan; and
   WHEREAS, the data submitted by SprintCom, and confirmed by testing done
under the supervision of the Independent Engineer, establishes that the
proposed rooftop site on the Subject Property is a matter of engineering
necessity, the higher priority sites established by Section 22.12A of the
Zoning Ordinance being too far away from the Hubbard Woods area to provide the
coverage sought under SprintCom’s network design; and
   WHEREAS, the testing done under the supervision of the Independent Engineer
establishes that antennas on the Subject Property and at the height proposed
are a matter of engineering necessity, in order to avoid the 6dB to 10dB (75%
to 90%) loss in signal strength that the tests demonstrate result from placing
antennas on the existing walls of the building, below the tree line; and
   WHEREAS, locating antenna arrays on the western edge of the golf course, at
the Winnetka landfill or Public Works yards at 1390 Willow Road, or on the golf
netting poles at the golf course driving range, is not technically feasible for
SprintCom, as demonstrated by propagation predictions and field testing, and
there is no replacement site available on the smokestack of the Water and
Electric Plant or on the monopole of the Public Safety Building; and
   WHEREAS, there are no other viable locations for an antenna array that will
clear the tree lines in the vicinity of the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, at the request of the Village Council, the Applicants submitted
alternative proposals for mounting the proposed antennas so as to mitigate the
visual impact of the antennas, and the Council have determined that the
proposal identified as “Alternative C” and submitted at the Council meeting of
October 20, 1998, which proposes mounting the antennas on the exterior of a
brick extension of the building parapet is most consistent with both the
existing building and the character of the vicinity; and
   WHEREAS, the Applicants have met the standards for granting special uses for
antenna arrays in the C-2 Zoning District, as those standards are set forth in
Section 22.13(m) of the Zoning Ordinance; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and unique hardships associated
with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect
to the Subject Property in that there are limited suitable locations for WTSF
in the vicinity of the Subject Property, and SprintCom has demonstrated the
necessity of being allowed to install the proposed antennas and related
equipment at the height requested and in the vicinity of the Subject Property
in order to provide continuous service coverage within the context of the
design of its network; and
   WHEREAS, the location of a nonconforming residence located in the center of
the “C-2” (General Retail) Commercial Zoning District less than 30 feet from
the proposed site poses an additional practical difficulty and unique hardship
which would prevent use of the Subject Property as proposed by SprintCom unless
the requested variation is granted; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations are in harmony with the general purpose
and intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, and in particular with the stated
purpose of Section 22.12A in that granting the requested variations will
promote telecommunications service coverage within the Village while
maintaining the integrity and character of the underlying zoning district; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance as it
pertains to WTSF, in that the strict application of the height and distance
limitations of the Zoning Ordinance would preclude the use of the Subject
Property as a site for antennas, although no other suitable sites within the
immediate vicinity have been identified as being available to SprintCom; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because the portion of the Subject Property on which the
antennas will be located is set back from the street and will be installed in a
color and manner that will reduce visual impact to the extend possible; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not impair an adequate supply of
light and air to adjacent property in that the proposed antennas will be set
back to the extent possible from adjacent properties and will not cast
additional shadows on sidewalks and area streets; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property as the proposed installation will comply
with all applicable building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not diminish the taxable value of
land and buildings throughout the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the placement of antennas on the Subject Property as proposed in
Alternative C will not contribute to congestion on the public streets; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the requested variations will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village.
NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
SECTION 1: That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the findings
of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth herein.
   SECTION 2: That a special use permit under Section 22.12A(n) of the Winnetka
Zoning Ordinance is hereby granted with respect to the Subject Property, which
is located in the “C-2” (General Retail) Commercial Zoning District and which
is commonly known as 911 Green Bay Road, to allow the installation of wireless
telecommunications antennas and related structures on and in the building on
the Subject Property, in accordance with the plans dated January 8, 1998, and
other materials submitted with and in support of the application for variations
and special use permit, and as modified by Alternative C , subject to the
conditions and limitations hereinafter set forth.
   SECTION 3: That variations are hereby granted with respect to the Subject
Property, which is located in the “C-2” (General Retail) Commercial Zoning
District, as follows: (a) a variation of the height limitations of Section
22.12A(n)5b of the Zoning Ordinance to allow the tops of the antennas to be at
a height of 56.58 feet, whereas 35 feet are permitted, resulting in a variation
of 21.58 feet (61.66%), and (b) from the location limitations of Section 22.12A
(n)5a of the Zoning Ordinance to allow placement of antennas and equipment at a
distance of approximately 39.94 feet and 230 feet from the single family
residences at 913½ Green Bay Road and 900 Old Green Bay Road, whereas a
distance of 250 feet is required, resulting in variations 210.06 feet (84.02%)
and 20 feet (8.0%) on the Subject Property, said variations being granted to
permit the installation of wireless telecommunications antennas and related
structures on the roof of the building on the Subject Property, in accordance
with the plans dated January 8, 1998 and other materials submitted with and in
support of the application for variations and special use permit, and as
modified by Alternative C, subject to the conditions and limitations
hereinafter set forth.
   SECTION 4: That the variations and special use permit hereby granted are
conditioned upon the commencement of the proposed construction within twelve
(12) months after the effective date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 5: That the variations and special use permit hereby granted are
further conditioned upon the requirement that the proposed antennas be mounted
on the exterior of a vertical extension of the exterior walls of the existing
penthouse parapet, provided that the extension is made of brick of a color and
size to match the brick on the existing building and that all visible portions
of the antennas, supporting arms and cables and wires be painted to match the
brick surfaces against which they are mounted, all as depicted in Alternative
C, and that the tops of the antennas be no higher than the height specified in
the original zoning application.
SECTION 6: That the variations and special use permit hereby granted are
further conditioned upon SprintCom’s paying to the Village 50% of the costs
incurred by the Village in obtaining the third party independent review
required by Section 22.12A(s) of the Zoning Ordinance, the payment to be made
to the Village in full within 30 days after the bill is submitted to SprintCom.
SECTION 7: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage, approval
and posting.
   ADOPTED this 20th day of October, 1998, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES: Trustees Derning, Duhl, Lanphier, Hilton, Lien and Meuer
   NAYS: None
   ABSENT: None
   APPROVED this 20th day of October, 1998.
   Signed:
   ss/Louise A. Holland
   Village President
Countersigned:
ss/Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: October 6, 1998
Posted: October 7, 1998
Passed and Approved: October 20, 1998
Posted: October 21, 1998
ORDINANCE NO. M-546-98
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A SPECIAL USE PERMIT AND VARIATIONS IN THE APPLICATION OF
THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA, COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS TO A
PROPOSED SITE FOR WIRELESS TELECOMMUNICATIONS ANTENNAS AND EQUIPMENT (874 GREEN
BAY ROAD)
   WHEREAS, Tower Realty (the “Owner”) is the owner of the following described
real estate (the “Subject Property”):
   That part of Lot 1 lying southerly and easterly of Green Bay Road (formerly
known as Center Street) in Block 1 in County Clerk’s Division of the southwest
quarter of Section 17, Township 42 North, Range 13 east of the Third Principal
Meridian, in Cook County, Illinois.
which is commonly known as 874 Green Bay Road, Winnetka, Illinois, and is
located in the C-2 (General Retail) Commercial Zoning District provided in the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Chapter 22, Winnetka Village Code; and
   WHEREAS, A T & T Wireless Services, Inc. (“A T & T”) has entered into an
agreement with the Owner of the Subject Property, whereby A T & T will be
allowed to place certain wireless telecommunications service antennas and
equipment on the walls and roof of the building located on the Subject
Property; and
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the Owner and A T & T (the “Applicants”)
for a special use permit in accordance with Section 22.13 and 22.12A of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance to permit the installation of wireless
telecommunication service antennas and equipment on the roof and penthouse of
the existing building on the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, the Applicants are also requesting the following variations: (a)
from the height limitations of Sections 22.11(c) and 22.12A(n)5b of the Zoning
Ordinance, to allow a height of 51 feet, whereas 35 feet are permitted,
resulting in a variation of 16 feet (45.71%); (b) from the side yard setback
requirements of Section 22.11(g) of the Zoning Ordinance to allow a side yard
of 2.33 feet, whereas 3.0 feet are required, resulting in a variation of .67
feet (22.33%); and (c) from the rear yard setback requirements of Section 22.11
(h) of the Zoning Ordinance to allow a rear yard setback of 1.0 foot, whereas
9.9 feet are required, resulting in a variation of 8.9 feet (89.9%) on the
Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, on August 20, 1998, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals opened a public hearing on the requested special use and variations
and, upon considering evidence submitted by the Applicants, the Zoning Board of
Appeals continued the hearing to September 14, 1998, to allow for a technical
review of the application pursuant to Section 22.12A(s)2 of the Zoning
Ordinance; and
   WHEREAS, on September 14, 1998, the engineer retained by the Village (the
“Independent Engineer”) to perform the independent technical review submitted
preliminary conclusions that tended to support the application of A T & T, but
recommended that actual field testing be performed to confirm that a higher
priority location was not feasible from an engineering standpoint; and
   WHEREAS, on September 14, 1998, upon receiving the recommendation of the
Independent Engineer, the Zoning Board of Appeals concluded the public hearing,
considered the evidence and by the affirmative vote of five of the six members
then present, with the remaining member abstaining because he had not been
present for all of the evidence, has reported to the Council recommending that
the requested variations and special use permit be granted, provided that
testing done under the supervision of the Independent Engineer confirm that a
higher priority site is not a viable alternative; and
   WHEREAS, on August 19, 1998, on due notice thereof, the Plan Commission met
to consider the application and recommended, by a vote of six to one, to find
that the requested special use is consistent with the Village’s comprehensive
plan; and
   WHEREAS, the data submitted by A T & T, confirmed by testing done by A T & T
and reviewed by the Independent Engineer, along with testing done under the
supervision of the Independent Engineer, establishes that the proposed rooftop
and penthouse site on the Subject Property are a matter of engineering
necessity, the higher priority sites established by Section 22.12A of the
Zoning Ordinance being too far away from the Hubbard Woods area to provide the
coverage sought under A T & T’s network design; and
   WHEREAS, locating antenna arrays on the western edge of the golf course, at
the Winnetka landfill or Public Works yards at 1390 Willow Road, or on the golf
netting poles at the golf course driving range, is not technically feasible for
A T & T, as demonstrated by A T & T’s propagation predictions and confirmed by
field testing done under the supervision of the Independent Engineer, and there
is no replacement site available on the smokestack of the Water and Electric
Plant or on the monopole of the Public Safety Building; and
   WHEREAS, there are no other viable locations for an antenna array that will
clear the tree lines in the vicinity of the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, the Applicants have met the standards for granting special uses for
antenna arrays in the C-2 Zoning District, as those standards are set forth in
Section 22.13(m) of the Zoning Ordinance; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and unique hardships associated
with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect
to the Subject Property in that there are limited suitable locations for WTSF
in the vicinity of the Subject Property, and A T & T has demonstrated the
necessity of being allowed to install the proposed antennas and related
equipment in the vicinity of the Subject Property in order to provide
continuous service coverage within the context of the design of its network;
and
   WHEREAS, the height and setback provisions applicable to the Subject
Property pose an additional practical difficulty and unique hardship which
would prevent use of the Subject Property as proposed by A T & T unless the
requested variation is granted, because the building is not set back from the
lot lines and A T & T has been unable to obtain permission to place wall
antennas, which would overhang the adjoining property; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations are in harmony with the general purpose
and intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, and in particular with the stated
purpose of Section 22.12A, in that granting the requested variations will
promote telecommunications service coverage within the Village while
maintaining the integrity and character of the underlying zoning district; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance as it
pertains to WTSF, in that the strict application of the height and setback
limitations of the Zoning Ordinance would preclude the use of the Subject
Property as a site for antennas, although no other suitable sites within the
immediate vicinity have been identified as being available to A T & T; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because the portion of the Subject Property on which the
antennas will be located is set back from the street and will be installed in a
color and manner that will reduce their visual impact to the extent possible;
and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not impair an adequate supply of
light and air to adjacent property in that the proposed antennas will be set
back to the extent possible from adjacent properties and are not of sufficient
height or mass to cast shadows on sidewalks and area streets; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property as the proposed installation will comply
with all applicable building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not diminish the taxable value of
land and buildings throughout the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the placement of antennas on the roof of the Subject Property will
not contribute to congestion on the public streets; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the requested variations will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village.
NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
SECTION 1: That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the findings
of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth herein.
   SECTION 2: That a special use permit under Section 22.12A(n) of the Winnetka
Zoning Ordinance is hereby granted with respect to the Subject Property, which
is located in the “C-2” (General Retail) Commercial Zoning District and which
is commonly known as 874 Green Bay Road, to allow the installation of wireless
telecommunications antennas and related structures on the roof of the building
and penthouse on the Subject Property, in accordance with the revised plans
dated July 28, 1998, and other supporting materials submitted with the
application for variations and special use permit, subject to the conditions
and limitations hereinafter set forth.
   SECTION 3: That variations are hereby granted with respect to the Subject
Property, which is located in the “C-2” (General Retail) Commercial Zoning
District, as follows: (a) a variation of the height limitations of Sections
22.11(c) and 22.12A(n)5b of the Zoning Ordinance to allow a height of 51.00
feet, whereas 35 feet are permitted, resulting in a variation of 16.00 feet
(45.71%); (b) from the side yard setback requirements of Section 22.11(g) to
allow a side yard setback of 2.33, whereas 3 feet are required resulting in a
variation of 0.67 feet (22.33%); and (c) from the rear yard setback provisions
of Section 22.11(h) to allow a south rear yard of 1 foot, whereas 9.9 feet are
required, resulting in a variation of 8.9 feet (89.9%) on the Subject Property,
said variations being granted to permit the installation of wireless
telecommunications antennas and related structures on the roof and penthouse of
the building on the Subject Property, in accordance with the revised plans
dated July 28, 1998 and other supporting materials submitted with the
application for variations and special use permit, subject to the conditions
and limitations hereinafter set forth.
   SECTION 4: That the variations and special use permit hereby granted are
conditioned upon the commencement of the proposed construction within twelve
(12) months after the effective date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 5: That the variations and special use permit hereby granted are
further conditioned upon A T & T’s paying to the Village 50% of the costs
incurred by the Village in obtaining the third party independent review
required by Section 22.12A(s) of the Zoning Ordinance, the payment to be made
to the Village in full within 30 days after the bill is submitted to A T & T.
SECTION 6: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage, approval
and posting.
   ADOPTED this 20th day of October, 1998, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES: Trustees Derning, Lanphier, Meuer, Hilton, Lien, Duhl
   NAYS: None
   ABSENT: None
   APPROVED this 20th day of October, 1998.
   Signed:
   ss/Louise A. Holland
   Village President
Countersigned:
ss/Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
