ORDINANCE NO. M-554-98
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (323 Locust Road)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
   Lot 6, except the North 20 feet thereof; and the North 40 feet of Lot 7 in
Alles’ Sunset Subdivision in the Northeast ¼ of the Southwest ¼ of Section 20,
Township 42 North, Range 13 East of the Third Principal Meridian, in Cook
County, Illinois.
commonly known as 323 Locust Road, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the “A-5”
(One-Fourth Acre) Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Chapter 22, Winnetka Village Code, for a variation
from Section 22.05A(h) of the Zoning Ordinance to allow a front yard setback of
39.62 feet, whereas an average front setback of 51.44 feet is required,
resulting in a variation of 11.90 feet (23.13%) for the purpose of adding an
enclosed front entry and roofed porch to the single family residence on the
Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, on October 12, 1998, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variation and by the
unanimous vote of the five members then present, has reported to the Council
recommending that the requested variation be granted, subject to the condition
that the area of the roofed porch not exceed 60 square feet, which is the
maximum area that exterior doorway entrances are allowed to extend into
required front yards under Section 22.15(a)3.a of the Zoning Ordinance; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and unique hardships associated
with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect
to the Subject Property in that: (a) the Subject Property is an irregularly
shaped lot due to its front lot line being on Locust Road, which creates an
angled front yard and front lot line that slope from southwest to northeast;
(b) the residence on the Subject Property is aligned with the property’s side
and rear lot lines, which are perpendicular to each other; (c) although the
Subject Property exceeds the 30 foot front yard setback for the A-5 zoning
district, it is subject to average front setbacks requirements, because most of
the parcels on the blockface also exceed the 30 foot minimum; and (d) because
of the angle of Locust Road, the property immediately to the south of the
Subject Property has a front setback of 74.33 feet, although the residence
there is not set substantially closer to the rear lot line than is the
residence on the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that
the proposed enclosed entry and roofed porch will create a sheltered front
entrance and hall closet in the only reasonable locations for them in the
existing residence, and that a conforming front entrance and porch could not
otherwise be constructed without moving or replacing the entire residence; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because the front entrance and roofed porch are at a reverse
corner of the building and are consistent with the architectural style of the
residence and the building scale of the neighborhood; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not impair an adequate supply of light
and air because the roofed enclosure is a single story located in a reverse
corner of the house; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property as it will comply with all applicable
building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed front entry and roofed porch will not contribute to
congestion on the public streets, as the Subject Property will continue to be
used for single family residential purposes; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the requested variation will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village.
NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1: That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2: That a variation is hereby granted with respect to the Subject
Property, commonly known as 323 Locust Road, which is located in the “A-5”
(One-Fourth Acre) Single Family Residential District provided in the Winnetka
Zoning Ordinance, Chapter 22, Winnetka Village Code, for a variation from
Section 22.05A(h) of the Zoning Ordinance to allow a front setback of 39.62
feet, whereas the required average front yard setback is 51.44 feet, resulting
in a variation of 11.82 feet (22.98%), all for the purpose of constructing an
enclosed front entry and roofed porch on the Subject Property, in accordance
with the application for variation and plans submitted with the application, as
amended to limit the area of the roofed porch to 60 square feet.
   SECTION 3: That the variation hereby granted is subject to the following
conditions: (a) that the proposed construction commence within twelve months
after the effective date of this Ordinance; and (b) that the applications for
building permit limit the size of the roofed porch to an area of 60 square feet
or less.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 17th day of November, 1998, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES: Trustees Derning, Hilton, Lien and Meuer
   NAYS: Trustee Lanphier.
   ABSENT: Trustee Duhl.
   APPROVED this 17th day of November, 1998.
   Signed:
   ss/Louise A. Holland
    Village President
Countersigned:
ss/Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: November 5, 1998
Posted: November 6, 1998
Passed and Approved: November 17, 1998
Posted: November 18, 1998
ORDINANCE NO. M-555-98
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (1045 Pine Street)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owners of the following described
real estate (the “Subject Property”):
   The East 90 Feet of Lot 8 in Block 3 in Winnetka Heights, being a
Subdivision of that part of the South ½ of the Southwest ¼ of Section 17,
Township 42 North, Range 13 East of the Third Principal Meridian, lying East of
and adjoining the East line extended of Rosewood Avenue, formerly Oak Ridge
Avenue, in the Village of Winnetka, Cook County, Illinois.
commonly known as 1045 Pine Street, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the “A-
2” (One-Third Acre) Single Family Residential Zoning District provided in the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Chapter 22, Winnetka Village Code, for a variation
from Section 22.06(g) which will result in a building size of 5,827.85 square
feet, whereas 5,371.51 square feet are permitted, resulting in a variation of
456.34 square feet (8.5%) to allow the construction of a two-foot wide, 49.7
square foot garage addition in order to relocate the doors of an existing two-
car attached garage, and to enlarge a second garage by 133.73 square feet to
convert it into a family room; and
   WHEREAS, on October 12, 1998, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variation and has reported
to the Council recommending that the requested variation be granted, with all
five members of the Zoning Board then present voting in favor of approving the
garage variation, and four of the five members then present voting in favor of
approving the family room and chimney variation; and
   WHEREAS, the applicants have modified their plans and are now requesting
only the 49.7 square foot addition and have withdrawn their request to
construct the 133.73 square foot family room addition; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and unique hardships associated
with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect
to the Subject Property, in that the proposed variation will make the remaining
two-car garage more easily accessible, will allow removal of an asphalt
driveway surface from the current garage entrances at the north side of the
house, and will increase the functionality of the residence, which was built in
1922; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that
the reorientation of the garage doors to provide more direct access from the
street is consistent with standard patterns of ingress and egress, which could
not otherwise be provided on the Subject Property in conformity with existing
zoning limitations without removing and rebuilding substantial portions of the
existing residence; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because the proposed addition is approximately two-feet deep
and is located at the rear of the existing residence; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not impair an adequate supply of light
and air because the two additions are of modest size and are located at the
rear of the existing residence; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property as it will comply with all applicable
building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not contribute to congestion on the
public streets, as the Subject Property will continue to be used for single-
family residential purposes and enclosed off-street parking will continue to be
provided; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed building additions will
otherwise impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village, and the requested variations will reduce the amount
of impermeable surface on the Subject Property by more than 500 square feet.
NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1: That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2: That a variation is hereby granted with respect to the Subject
Property, commonly known as 1045 Pine Street, which is located in the “A-2”
(One-Third Acre) Single Family Residential Zoning District provided in the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Chapter 22, Winnetka Village Code, for a variation
from Section 22.06(g) which will result in a building size of 5,694.12 square
feet, whereas 5,371.51 square feet are permitted, resulting in a variation of
322.61 square feet (6.0%) to allow the construction of a two-foot wide, 49.7
square foot garage , in order to relocate the doors of an existing two-car
attached, all in accordance with the application for variation and plans
submitted with the application, as modified on December 22, 1998.
   SECTION 3: That the variation hereby granted is conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within twelve months after the
effective date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 5th day of January, 1999, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES: Trustees Derning, Duhl, Lanphier, Lien and Meuer
   NAYS: None.
   ABSENT: None. (Trustee Hilton – recused)
   APPROVED this 5th day of January, 1998.
   Signed:
   ss/Louise A. Holland
    Village President
Countersigned:
ss/Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: November 5, 1998
Posted: November 6, 1998
Passed and Approved: January 5, 1999
Posted: January 6, 1999
ORDINANCE NO. M-556-98
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (832 Foxdale Avenue)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
   The Easterly 120 feet of Lot 13 in Block 5 in Winnetka Park Bluffs, being a
Subdivision by William H. Cairnduff of part of Sections 16, 17 and 20, Township
42 North, Range 13 East of the Third Principal Meridian, in the Village of
Winnetka, Cook County, Illinois.
commonly known as 832 Foxdale Avenue, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the
“A-1” (One-Sixth Acre) Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in
the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Chapter 22, Winnetka Village Code, for a
variation from the maximum building size (gross floor area) limits of Section
22.05(g) of the Zoning Ordinance to permit a gross floor area of 2,610.38
square feet, whereas 2,580.0 square feet is permitted, resulting in a variation
of 30.38 square feet (1.18%), for the purpose of permitting a 60.00 square foot
storage shed constructed without a permit to remain on the Subject Property;
and
   WHEREAS, on October 12, 1998, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variation and by the
favorable vote of four of the five members then present, has reported to the
Council recommending that the requested variation be granted; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and unique hardships associated
with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect
to the Subject Property, rather than being related to the occupants; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because the storage shed is located at the rear of the house
and is less than one story high; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not impair an adequate supply of light
and air because the storage shed is located at the rear of the house and is
less than one story high; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property as it will comply with all applicable
building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed storage shed will not contribute to congestion on the
public streets, as the Subject Property will continue to be used for single
family residential purposes; and
WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed storage shed will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village.
NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1:  That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2: That a variation is hereby granted with respect to the Subject
Property, commonly known as 832 Foxdale Avenue, which is located in the “A-1”
(One-Sixth Acre) Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Chapter 22, Winnetka Village Code, for a variation
from the maximum building size (gross floor area) limits of Section 22.05(g) of
the Zoning Ordinance to permit a gross floor area of 2,610.38 square feet,
whereas 2,580.0 square feet is permitted, resulting in a variation of 30.38
square feet (1.18%), for the purpose of permitting a 60.00 square foot storage
shed constructed without a permit to remain on the Subject Property, in
accordance with the application for variation and plans submitted with the
application.
   SECTION 3: That the variations hereby granted are conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within twelve months after the
effective date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 17th day of November, 1998, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES: Trustees Derning, Hilton, Lanphier, Lien and Meuer
   NAYS: None.
   ABSENT: Trustee Duhl.
   APPROVED this 17th day of November, 1998.
