ORDINANCE NO. M-557-98
AN ORDINANCE AUTHORIZING THE VACATION OF A PORTION OF THE RANDOLPH STREET
RIGHT-OF-WAY SOUTH OF ASBURY STREET AND ACCEPTING A PLAT OF CONSOLIDATION
   WHEREAS, pursuant to Section 11-91-1 of the Illinois Municipal Code (65 ILCS
5/11-91-1), the Council of the Village of Winnetka (“Village Council”) are
authorized to vacate any street or alley or part thereof within the corporate
limits of the Village if the corporate authorities determine that the public
interest will be served by the vacation; and
   WHEREAS, on September 3, 1996, the Village Council adopted ordinance number
M-470-96, which vacated the east 21 feet and the west 21 feet of the Randolph
Street right-of-way south of Asbury Avenue and consolidated the two vacated
strips of right-of-way with the properties adjoining them; and
   WHEREAS, pursuant to M-470-96, the 24-foot center portion of the Randolph
Street right-of-way remained as dedicated right-of-way and has been used
primarily as a location for Village utility lines; and
   WHEREAS, the Village Council have considered whether the remaining 24 feet
of the Randolph Street right-of-way south of Asbury Avenue and have determined
that it is in the public interest that it be vacated, provided that sufficient
utility easements are reserved; and
   WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka have determined that the
fair market value of the remaining portion of the Randolph Street right-of-way
is $154,000; and
   WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka have determined that it is
in the best interests of the Village that the entire vacated right-of-way be
consolidated with the property adjoining it to the west, which is commonly
known as 1330 Asbury Avenue, in order to create more consistency in lot sizes
in the vicinity by increasing the width of 1330 Asbury Avenue to approximately
the same dimensions as the lot immediately to the east of the right-of-way; and
   WHEREAS, the owners of the property abutting the west edge of the Randolph
Street right-of-way south of Asbury Avenue, commonly known as 1330 Asbury
Avenue, have agreed to pay the Village the fair market value of the remaining
24-foot portion of the right-of-way to be vacated; and
   WHEREAS, the vacation of the above-described right-of-way as provided herein
will result in the consolidation of the vacated right-of-way with the property
commonly known as 1330 Asbury Avenue, thereby requiring the reconfiguration of
those lots, as depicted in the plat of consolidation entitled “Busscher’s
Consolidation” (the “Plat of Consolidation”), which is attached hereto as
Exhibit A; and
   WHEREAS, the Plat of Consolidation has been reviewed by the Director of
Community Development pursuant to Section 21.04(f) of the Village’s Subdivision
Ordinance, Chapter 21, Winnetka Village Code; and
   WHEREAS, the Director of Community Development has determined that the Plat
of Consolidation meets the requirements of the Subdivision Ordinance and has
recommended its approval, subject to the condition that sufficient easements be
reserved for the Village's water and electric utilities; and
   WHEREAS, the Director of Water and Electric has determined that a 12-foot
easement is required along the east lot line, provided that the existing
underground electric line is no less than one foot from the west line of the
easement; and
   WHEREAS, the President and the Board of Trustees have determined that the
public interest will be served by vacating the remaining Randolph Street right-
of-way, which is legally described as follows:
That portion of Randolph Street lying South of the south line of the Asbury
Avenue right-of-way and lying North of a line drawn from the Southeast corner
of Lot 2 to the Southwest corner of Lot 1 Busscher-Chadwick Subdivision, being
a subdivision in the Southeast Quarter of the Northeast Quarter of Section 18,
Township 42 North, Range 13, East of the Third Principal Meridian, all in Cook
County, Illinois;
as depicted on the Plat of Consolidation; and
   WHEREAS, the corporate authorities of the Village of Winnetka have
determined that it is in the best interests of the public for the vacation of
the designated portions of the right-of-way to be subject to the execution of
an agreement with the owners of the property commonly known as 1330 Asbury
Avenue, substantially in the form as attached hereto as Exhibit B, and to the
performance by the adjoining owners of all of the conditions provided therein.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain:
   SECTION 1 : The foregoing recitals are hereby adopted by the Council as its
findings and are incorporated herein by reference as if fully set forth herein.
   SECTION 2: That the remaining 24 feet of the Randolph Street right-of-way
south of Asbury Avenue depicted on the plat entitled “Busscher’s Consolidation”
and legally described as follows:
That portion of Randolph Street lying South of the south line of the Asbury
Avenue right-of-way and lying North of a line drawn from the Southeast corner
of Lot 2 to the Southwest corner of Lot 1 Busscher-Chadwick Subdivision, being
a subdivision in the Southeast Quarter of the Northeast Quarter of Section 18,
Township 42 North, Range 13, East of the Third Principal Meridian, all in Cook
County, Illinois;
is hereby vacated, subject to the conditions hereinafter set forth.
   SECTION 3 : The vacation of the above-described right-of-way shall be
subject to: (1) the reservation of an easement for Village water and electric
utilities along the east property line of Lot 1 in Busscher’s Consolidation,
which is commonly known as 1330 Asbury Avenue, said easement to extend at least
one foot west of the underground electric lines located in the right-of-way to
be vacated, provided that the easement shall not be less than 12 feet wide, as
measured from the east property line of Lot 1 and as depicted on the Plat of
Consolidation attached hereto as Exhibit A; (2) the execution by the owners of
the property commonly known as 1330 Asbury Avenue (the “Owners”) of an
agreement substantially in the form attached to this ordinance as Exhibit B;
(3) the receipt by the Village of the full payment of One Hundred Fifty-Four
Thousand Dollars ($154,000.00) from the Owners within 30 days after the receipt
of the final, corrected Plat of Consolidation; and (4) the performance by the
Owners of all other obligations required under the agreement attached hereto as
Exhibit B.
   SECTION 4: Provided that the conditions set forth in Section 3 of this
ordinance are met, the vacated Randolph Street right-of-way shall be
consolidated with the property that is commonly known as 1330 Asbury Avenue,
which is legally described as:
Lot 1 of Busscher-Chadwick Subdivision, being a subdivision in the Southeast
Quarter of the Northeast Quarter of Section 18, Township 42, Range 13, East of
the Third Principal Meridian, in Cook County, Illinois.
   SECTION 5 : The corporate authorities of the Village of Winnetka do hereby
authorize the Village President and Village Clerk to execute the agreement for
street vacation, substantially in the form attached hereto as Exhibit B, and to
execute the plat entitled “Busscher’s Consolidation,” and to take all other
steps necessary to effectuate the vacation of the portion of the Randolph
Street right-of-way described herein, and the recording of the plat entitled
Busscher’s Consolidation, provided that all of the conditions set forth in
Section 3 of this ordinance have first been met.
   SECTION 6 : Upon the execution of the agreement and plat, the Village Clerk
of the Village of Winnetka is hereby authorized and directed to cause a
certified copy of this Ordinance and the Busscher’s Consolidation plat to be
recorded in the Office of the Cook County Recorder of Deeds.
   SECTION 7 : This ordinance shall be in full force and effect from and after
its passage, approval, and posting.
   ADOPTED this 17th day of November, 1998, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES: Trustees Derning, Hilton, Lanphier, Lien and Meuer
   NAYS: None.
   ABSENT: Trustee Duhl.
   APPROVED this 17th day of November, 1998.
   Signed:
   ss/Louise A. Holland
    Village President
Countersigned:
ss/Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: November 5, 1998
Posted: November 6, 1998
Passed and Approved: November 17, 1998
Posted: November 18, 1998
ORDINANCE NO: M-558-98
ORDINANCE AUTHORIZING THE SALE OR OTHER DISPOSITION OF SURPLUS ELECTRIC
DISTRIBUTION EQUIPMENT OWNED BY THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA
WHEREAS, the Village of Winnetka owns certain electric distribution
transformers (hereinafter the “Personal Property”), which have been removed
from service in the Village's electric distribution system and are described in
Exhibit A, which exhibit is attached to this ordinance and incorporated herein
by reference; and
WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka has determined that, upon the
installation of new replacement transformers, the transformers listed in
Exhibit A and any other transformers that are replaced or retired from service
during the 1998-1999 fiscal year, will no longer be necessary or useful to the
Village of Winnetka; and
WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka have further determined that
the cost of storing, and the potential exposure to liability from retaining,
the Personal Property exceeds any value to the Village, and that the Personal
Property should be disposed of in the manner hereinafter provided, pursuant to
Section 11-76-4 of the Illinois Municipal Code (65 ILCS 5/11-76-4), so as to
avoid incurring additional costs and liability for storing the Personal
Property and unnecessary costs in disposing of the Personal Property.
NOW THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
SECTION 1 :   Pursuant to Section 11-76-4 of the Illinois Municipal code (65
ILCS 5/11-76-4), the Council of the Village of Winnetka find that the items of
Personal Property listed in the attached Exhibit A (the “Personal Property”),
either have been removed from service and are no longer necessary or useful to
the Village of Winnetka, or are scheduled to be replaced during the 1998-1999
fiscal year and will no longer be necessary or useful to the Village when that
replacement occurs, and that the best interests of the Village of Winnetka will
be served by the sale or other disposition of the Personal Property.
SECTION 2: Pursuant to Section 11-76-4 of the Illinois Municipal Code, the
Village Manager is hereby authorized and directed to determine the value and
dispose of the Personal Property by directing public bids be taken and the sale
of transformers or any other transformers that are replaced or retired from
service during the 1998-1999 fiscal year, to the highest bidder or at the
lowest cost, with or without advertising.
SECTION 4: That this ordinance shall be in full force and effect from and after
its passage, approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 5th day of November, 1998, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES: Trustees Derning, Duhl, Lanphier, Lien and Meuer
   NAYS: None.
   ABSENT: Trustee Hilton.
   APPROVED this 5th day of November, 1998.
   Signed:
   ss/Louise A. Holland
    Village President
Countersigned:
ss/Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced:   October 20, 1998
Posted:   October 21, 1998
Passed and Approved: November 5, 1998
Posted:   November 6, 1998
ORDINANCE NO. M-559-98
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (562-572 Lincoln Avenue)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owners of the following described
real estate (the “Subject Property”):
   Parcel 1:
   Lot 5 (except that part lying west of a line 50 feet northeasterly of and
parallel with easterly line of Chicago and Northwestern Railroad company right-
of-way) in Block 13 in Winnetka Park Bluffs, being a subdivision by William
Cairnduff of parts of Sections 16, 17 and 20, Township 42 North, Range 13, east
of the Third Principal Meridian, in Cook County, Illinois.
   Parcel 2:
   Lot 11 (except westerly 50 feet thereof) in Block 12 in Park Addition to
Winnetka Subdivision by William H. Cairnduff of part of Sections 16, 17 and 20
Township 42 North, Range 13, east of the Third Principal Meridian, in Cook
County, Illinois.
   Parcel 3:
   The south 5 feet of Lot 6 in Lewis D. Webster’s Resubdivision of Block 11
(except the north 75 feet thereof) and of Lots 6, 8, 9 and 10 in Block 12 in
Park Addition to Winnetka aforesaid, all in Cook County, Illinois;
Parcel 4
That Part Of Lots 11 And 12 In Block 12 In Park Addition To Winnetka
Subdivision By William H. Cairnduff, Also Part Of Lot 15 In Block 13 In
Winnetka Park Bluffs, Being A Strip Of Land 10 Feet In Width Measured At Right
Angles To And Lying Southwesterly From A Line 50 Feet Northeasterly Of And
Parallel With The Easterly Line Of The Chicago And Northwestern Railroad
Company Right-Of-Way And Lying South Of The Westerly Extension Of The North
Line Of The South 5 Feet Of Lot 6 In Lewis D. Webster’s Subdivision And North
Of The South Line Of Said Lot 15 In Block 13 In Winnetka Park Bluffs, All In
Section 20, Township 42 North, Range 13 East Of The Third Principal Meridian,
In Cook County, Illinois.
commonly known as 562-572 Lincoln Avenue, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in
the “C-2” (General Retail) Commercial Zoning District provided in the Winnetka
Zoning Ordinance, Chapter 22, Winnetka Village Code, for a variation of 5.5
feet (15.71%) from the maximum building height of 35 feet permitted by Section
22.11(c) of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance to allow the clock tower on the
building proposed for the Subject Property to be 40.50 feet tall; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property has been consolidated into a single parcel as
the result of the fulfillment of all conditions specified in Resolution R-1435-
98, adopted by the Village Council on August 15, 1998, and the Subject Property
is now legally described as:
Lot 1 of the Cummins Winnetka Galleria Consolidation in Sections 16, 17 and 20,
Township 42 North, Range 13, East of the Third Principal Meridian, in the
Village of Winnetka, in Cook County, Illinois; and
   WHEREAS, on May 21, May 28 and August 13, 1998, the Design Review Board
considered the proposed building design, including the clock tower detail, and
voted to approve a certificate of appropriateness; and
   WHEREAS, on October 12, 1998, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variation; and
   WHEREAS, the Zoning Board of Appeals has not made a favorable recommendation
to the Council on the requested variation, two of the five members present on
October 12, 1998, having voted in favor of recommendation, and the three other
members then present having voted against recommendation; and
   WHEREAS, on August 18, 1998, the Village Council adopted ordinance M-535-98,
which granted a variation from the height limitations of Section 22.11(c) to
allow the proposed building to be 3 stories high rather than the maximum 2½
stories allowed in the C-2 Commercial Zoning District, although the overall
building height complied with the 35 foot height limitation; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed building on the Subject Property will replace an aging
two-story commercial building, which is now demolished, with a mixed use
building having commercial uses on the first floor and condominiums on the
second and third floors, with additional commercial space and enclosed parking
in the lower level; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and particular hardships
associated with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance
with respect to the Subject Property in that the clock tower is both a design
element and a functional feature that serves to obscure the elevator’s flat-
roofed mechanical housing from view, the clock tower is set back more than 10
feet from the street frontage of the building at the rear of an entry
courtyard, and the clock tower would be allowed at the proposed height and
proposed location if the façade of the building extended across the entire
front lot line rather than being divided into two sections with the open
courtyard in the center; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that
the clock tower is an essential element of the building design and lowering the
clock tower to a conforming height would either expose the elevator housing or
require a redesigned, unarticulated building façade, which would increase the
visual bulk of the building, and would have a negative impact on both the
character of the neighborhood and the economics of the proposed development of
the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood, because the clock tower is an essential element of the visual
appearance of the proposed building, which is consistent with the Zoning
Ordinance’s height limitations; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed clock tower will not impair an adequate supply of
light and air because it is set back from the street at the rear of an open
courtyard; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property as the proposed building will comply with
all applicable building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because the clock tower is an essential design
element of a proposed building that will replace an existing, deteriorating
commercial building with a new building containing both residential and
commercial units; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not contribute to congestion on the
public streets, as it has no impact on the use of the proposed building; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed building will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village; and
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain:
   SECTION 1: That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2: That a variation is hereby granted with respect to the Subject
Property, commonly known as 562-572 Lincoln Avenue, which is located in the "C-
2" (General Retail) Commercial District provided in the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance, Chapter 22, Winnetka Village Code, for a variation of 5.5 feet
(15.71%) from the building height limitations of Section 22.11(c) of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance to allow a clock tower on the building proposed for
the Subject Property to reach a height of 40.50 feet, all in accordance with
the application for variation and the plans submitted with the application.
   SECTION 3: That the variation hereby granted is conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed constructing within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage, approval
and posting.
   ADOPTED this 1st day of December, 1998, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES: Trustees Derning, Duhl, Hilton, Lanphier, Lien and Meuer
   NAYS: None.
   ABSENT: None.
   APPROVED this 1st day of December, 1998.
   Signed:
   ss/Louise A. Holland
    Village President
Countersigned:
ss/Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: November 17, 1998
Posted: November 18, 1998
Passed and Approved: December 1, 1998
Posted: December 2, 1998
ORDINANCE NO. M-560-98
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (1228 Pine Street)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
   Lot 8 in Edgewood Manor, being Subdivision of West 573.06 Feet of Block 1 in
Edgewood, Being Resubdivision of Blocks 1, 2, 3, 6, 7 and 8 in Winnetka Land
Association Subdivision of North Half of West 90 Acres of Northwest ¼ of
Section 20, Township 42 North, Range 13, East of the Third Principal Meridian,
According to Plat thereof recorded April 9, 1954 as Document No. 15877253, in
Cook County, Illinois,
commonly known as 1228 Pine Street, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the A-3
(One-Half Acre) Zoning District provided in the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance,
Chapter 22, Winnetka Village Code, for a variation from the total side yard
requirements of Section 22.07(i) of the Zoning Ordinance to permit a total side
yard setback of 23.80 feet, whereas 34.38 feet are required, resulting in a
variation of 10.08 feet (29.32%), for the purpose of replacing existing,
nonconforming attached garage with a new family room, mud room, storage space
and garage addition on the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, on November 9, 1998, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variation and by the
unanimous vote of the five members then present, has reported to the Council
recommending that the requested variation be granted; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and unique hardships associated
with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect
to the Subject Property in that: (a) conforming locations for a garage are
limited due to the topography of the Subject Property and the location of the
100-year floodplain boundaries, (b) the proposed construction will correct an
existing nonconformity in the west side yard of the Subject Property, and (c)
the nonconformity in the total side yard requirement results from the 12.3-foot
remaining east side yard; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that
the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance would prohibit the replacement
of the garage and the provision of a family room, both of which are standard
elements of housing in the community; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because the total side yard will be slightly improved, the
west side yard will be brought into compliance with the 12-foot minimum side
yard requirement, and the proposed addition will maintain the existing 1.5
story height of the residence; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not impair an adequate supply of light
and air because the new addition will not increase the height of the building
and the expansion to allow for construction of the garage will be toward the
front of the property, which is not proximate to the residence on the adjacent
property to the west; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property as it will comply with all applicable
building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the addition of a family room, mud room, storage space and garage
will not contribute to congestion on the public streets, as the proposed
addition will not increase or change the occupancy of the single family home on
the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed addition will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village.
NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
SECTION 1: That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the findings
of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth herein.
   SECTION 2: That a variation is hereby granted with respect to the Subject
Property, 1228 Pine Street, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the A-3 (One-
Half Acre) Zoning District provided in the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Chapter
22, Winnetka Village Code, for a variation from the total side yard
requirements of Section 22.07(i) of the Zoning Ordinance to permit a total side
yard setback of 23.80 feet, whereas 34.38 feet are required, resulting in a
variation of 10.08 feet (29.32%), for the purpose of replacing existing,
nonconforming attached garage with a new family room, mud room, storage space
and garage addition on the Subject Property.
   SECTION 3: That the variation hereby granted is conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed constructing within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage, approval
and posting.
   ADOPTED this 15th day of December, 1998, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES: Trustees Derning, Duhl, Hilton, Lanphier, Lien and Meuer
   NAYS: None.
   ABSENT: None.
   APPROVED this 15th day of December, 1998.
   Signed:
   ss/Louise A. Holland
    Village President
Countersigned:
ss/Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: December 1, 1998
Posted: December 2, 1998
Passed and Approved: December 15, 1998
Posted: December 16, 1998
ORDINANCE NO. M-561-98
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (915 Elm Street)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
   The East 83.0 feet of Lot 14, in Block 4 in Provident Mutual Land
Association Subdivision of Blocks 7, 8, 9, 10, 11, 12, 28, 29, 30, 31, 32, 33,
54, 55, 56, 57, 58 and 59 in the Village of Winnetka, Cook County, Illinois,
Being a Subdivision of the West half of the Northeast quarter of section 20,
Township 42 North, Range 13 East of the Third Principal Meridian,
commonly known as 915 Elm Street, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the A-1
(One-Sixth Acre) Zoning District provided in the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance,
Chapter 22, Winnetka Village Code, for a variation from the front setback
requirements of Section 22.05(h) of the Zoning Ordinance to permit a front yard
setback of 15.56 feet instead of the required minimum of 30.00 feet, resulting
in a variation of 14.44 feet (48.13%) to permit structural changes to an
existing nonconforming basement wall to provide a below grade garage on the
Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, on November 9, 1998, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variation and by the
favorable vote of four of the five members then present, has reported to the
Council recommending that the requested variation be granted; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and unique hardships associated
with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect
to the Subject Property in that: (a) the Subject Property, which was created in
1909 before the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance was enacted, is a substandard lot
measuring 83 feet wide by 46 feet deep, (b) the small lot area and short lot
depth results in a diminished buildable area that is substantially wider than
it is long, as a result of which most of the existing residence on the Subject
Property is in a nonconforming location; and (c) there is no conforming
location on the Subject Property in which to place a garage; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that
there is currently not a garage on the Subject Property and a garage is an
essential element of all single family residential properties in the Village;
and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because the garage will be located below grade, within the
existing building footprint; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not impair an adequate supply of light
and air because there will be no vertical or horizontal change to the existing
building; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property as it will comply with all applicable
building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the construction of the below grade garage will not contribute to
congestion on the public streets, in that it will not increase or change the
occupancy of the single family home on the Subject Property and will provide
enclosed off street parking for the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed construction will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village.
NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
SECTION 1: That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the findings
of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth herein.
   SECTION 2: That a variation is hereby granted with respect to the Subject
Property, 915 Elm Street, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the A-1 (One-Sixth
Acre) Zoning District provided in the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Chapter 22,
Winnetka Village Code, for a variation from the front setback requirements of
Section 22.05(h) of the Zoning Ordinance to permit a front yard setback of
15.56 feet instead of the required minimum of 30.00 feet, resulting in a
variation of 14.44 feet (48.13%) to permit structural changes to an existing
nonconforming basement wall to provide a below grade garage on the Subject
Property.
   SECTION 3: That the variation hereby granted is conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed constructing within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage, approval
and posting.
   ADOPTED this 15th day of December, 1998, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES: Trustees Duhl, Hilton, Lanphier, Lien and Meuer
   NAYS: None.
   ABSENT: Trustee Derning (recused).
   APPROVED this 15th day of December, 1998.
   Signed:
   ss/Louise A. Holland
    Village President
Countersigned:
ss/Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
