ORDINANCE NO. M-562-98
AN ORDINANCE AUTHORIZING THE DISPOSITION OF A SCRAP MOTOR VEHICLE OWNED BY THE
VILLAGE OF WINNETKA
WHEREAS, the Village of Winnetka owns the following described motor vehicle
(hereinafter the “Vehicle”):
   Vehicle ID   Inventory No.   Year   Make   Model   Value
   1G1BL52P9TR139575   446   1996   Chev   Car-Caprice    - 0 -
WHEREAS, the Vehicle was severely damaged in an automobile accident and repair
estimates obtained by the Finance Department range from $7,500 to $9,900 for
visible damage; and
   WHEREAS, recent auctions of surplus vehicles conducted by the Northwest
Municipal Conference show the vehicle, which has over 60,000 miles on it, would
be worth no more than approximately $5,000 if repaired and sold at auction; and
   WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka have determined that the
cost of repairing the Vehicle exceeds the value of the Vehicle, that it is no
longer necessary or useful to, or for the best interest of the Village of
Winnetka to retain the Vehicle and that the Vehicle should be sold in the
manner hereinafter provided, pursuant to Section 11-76-4 of the Illinois
Municipal Code (65 ILCS 5/11-76-4).
NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : Pursuant to Section 11-76-4 of the Illinois Municipal Code (65
ILCS 5/11-76-4), the Council of the Village of Winnetka find that the above
described vehicle is of no value and is no longer necessary or useful to the
Village of Winnetka, and that the best interests of the Village of Winnetka
will be served by the sale of the Vehicle as provided herein.
   SECTION 2: Pursuant to Section 11-76-4, the Village Manager is hereby
authorized to direct the sale of the Vehicle for scrap by sealed bids pursuant
to published notice.
   SECTION 3: The Village Manager is further authorized to sell the Vehicle, in
whole or in part, to the highest bidder or bidders, as long as the net amount
of the highest bid or bids exceeds the cost of publishing the notice of sale.
   SECTION 4 Upon payment in full of the auctioned price by the highest bidder
or bidders for the Vehicle, the Village Manager is authorized to convey and
transfer the title and ownership of the Vehicle, in whole or in part, to the
highest bidder or bidders.
   SECTION 5: That this Ordinance shall be in full force and effect from and
after its passage, approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 5th day of January, 1999, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES: Trustees Derning, Duhl, Hilton, Lanphier, Lien and Meuer
   NAYS: None.
   ABSENT: None.
   APPROVED this 5th day of January, 1998.
   Signed:
   ss/Louise A. Holland
    Village President
Countersigned:
ss/Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: December 15, 1998
Posted: December 16, 1998
Passed and Approved: January 5, 1999
Posted: January 6, 1999
ORDINANCE NO: M-563-98 (not adopted)
1999
ORDINANCE NO. M-598-99
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (757 Locust Street)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
The South 100 feet of Lot 6 Block 19 in County Clerk’s Division of the
Southwest ¼ of Section 17, Township 42 North, Range 13, East of the Third
Principal Meridian, in Cook County, Illinois,
commonly known as 757 Locust Street, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
4 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 22 of the
Winnetka Village Code, for variations from the front and corner setback
requirements of Section 22.05A(h) of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, to maintain
the existing nonconforming front setback of 25.83 feet, whereas a 30-foot front
setback is required, resulting in a variation of 4.17 feet (13.9%), and to
maintain the existing nonconforming corner setback of 17.30 feet, whereas a 30-
foot corner setback is required, resulting in a variation of 12.70 feet
(42.33%), for the purpose of changing the pitch of the roof and adding dormers,
thereby increasing the area in the attic of the residence on the Subject
Property; and
   WHEREAS, on November 8, 1999, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variation and, by the
unanimous vote of the five members then present, has reported to the Council
recommending that the requested variation be granted; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and unique hardships associated
with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect
to the Subject Property in that: (a) the Subject Property is a corner lot which
is subject to 30-foot setbacks from its west and south lot lines; (b) the
residence on the Subject Property was constructed in 1908, prior to the
adoption of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance; and (c) the existing front and
corner setbacks, which are legal nonconformities, have remained unchanged since
the building was first constructed, with the sole exception of a 3.8-foot by
6.45-foot front entry vestibule constructed pursuant to a variation granted in
1984; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that
the existing nonconformities cannot be cured without rebuilding the house in
its entirety, the third floor of the residence currently consists of attic
storage space that is otherwise unusable and allowing the requested
modifications to the roof will make the third floor habitable and improve the
overall functionality of the residence; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because the proposed change is to the roof line only, the
resulting building height of 29.5 feet is only three feet more than the
existing building height and will still be less than the 35-foot maximum
allowable height, and the proposed changes will not alter the building
footprint or any of the exterior walls of the building below the roof line; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not impair an adequate supply of
light and air because the sloping roof places most of the new bulk well back
from the required setback lines, the proposed dormers will be set back at least
4 feet from the existing building lines, and the street frontages that define
the front and corner yards provide a permanent, additional expanse of open
space between the proposed construction and buildings on the opposite sides of
the streets; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property, as the proposed work will comply with
all applicable building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not diminish the taxable value of
land and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed change of roof pitch and addition of dormers will not
contribute to congestion on the public streets, as the Subject Property will
continue to be used for single family purposes; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed change of roof pitch and
addition of dormers will otherwise impair the public health, safety, comfort,
morals, and welfare of the inhabitants of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations are in harmony with the general purpose
and intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that they allow the renovation,
restoration and rehabilitation of a structurally sound existing building, while
maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the community and protecting
established trees and landscaping.
NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1: That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2:  That variations are hereby granted to the Subject Property,
commonly known as 757 Locust Street, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
4 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 22 of the
Winnetka Village Code, for variations from the front and corner setback
requirements of Section 22.05A(h) of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, to maintain
the existing nonconforming front setback of 25.83 feet, whereas a 30-foot
setback is required, resulting in a variation of 4.17 feet (13.9%), and to
maintain the existing nonconforming corner setback of 17.30 feet, whereas a 30-
foot corner setback is required, resulting in a variation of 12.70 feet
(42.33%), for the purpose of changing the pitch of the roof and adding dormers,
thereby increasing the area in the attic of the residence on the Subject
Property, in accordance with the plans and specifications submitted with the
application for variation.
   SECTION 3: That the variation hereby granted is subject to the following
conditions: (a) that the proposed construction shall commence within 12 months
after the effective date of this Ordinance; (b) that the eaves extend no more
than 18 inches from the existing building line; and (c) that the dormers facing
Locust Street and Westmoor Road be set back no less than 4 feet from the
existing building line.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 21st day of December, 1999, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES: Trustees Powell, Hilton, Lien, Duhl and Derning
   NAYS: None
   ABSENT: Trustee Aquilino
   APPROVED this 21st day of December, 1999.
   Signed:
   /ss/Louise A. Holland
   Village President
Countersigned:
/ss/ Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: December 7, 1999
Posted: December 8, 1999
Passed and Approved:December 21, 1999
Posted: December 22, 1999
ORDINANCE NO. M-564-99
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (217 Woodland Avenue)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
   Lot 11 (except the East 5 feet thereof), in Remy Park Subdivision of part of
Section 21, Township 42 North, Range 13 East of the Third Principal Meridian,
according to plat thereof, recorded in the Recorders Office of Cook County,
Illinois, on November 18, 1916, as Document Number 5995668, in Book 147 of
Plats, page 46, in Cook County, Illinois,
commonly known as 217 Woodland Avenue, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the
“A-1” (One-Sixth Acre) Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in
the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Chapter 22, Winnetka Village Code, for a
variation from the maximum building size limitations of Section 22.05(g) of the
Zoning Ordinance to permit an increase to the existing, nonconforming building
size, to a total gross floor area of 4,665.85 square feet, whereas 3,744.31
square feet is permitted, resulting in a variation of 921.54 square feet
(24.61%), for the purpose of converting the existing kitchen into a family room
and replacing an existing screened porch with a 72.92-square foot, one-story
addition, which will contain a new kitchen, covered porch and mud room, in
accordance with the plans and documents submitted with the application for
variation, as amended November 9, 1998; and
   WHEREAS, the original application submitted by the owners requested a
variation of 1,284.94 feet (34.32%) from the gross floor area limitations for a
two-story addition, but was withdrawn after the six members of the Zoning Board
of Appeals who were present when the original application was set for public
hearing on September 14, 1998, voted unanimously to recommend denial of the
request; and
   WHEREAS, on January 4, 1999, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the amended variation request, and by the
unanimous vote of the five members then present, has reported to the Council
recommending that the requested variation be granted; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and unique hardships associated
with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect
to the Subject Property in that the Subject Property exceeds the current
permitted gross floor area, because the house on the Subject Property, which
was built in 1926, has an upper level which is not fully usable but is now
considered a full story for zoning purposes and the structure of the existing
house is such that the existing floor plan, cannot be brought up to modern,
functional within the existing gross floor area limitations; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that
the existing porch is unsound and replacing it provides an opportunity to
improve the functionality of the floor plan by creating a family room, while
adding minimal additional bulk to the property; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because the addition is at the rear of the home, adds and will
appear to be a replacement improvement to the existing porch by retaining the
single story profile of the existing porch and expanding it by only 72.92
square feet; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not impair an adequate supply of light
and air because the addition is one story and is a small increase in the
footprint of the residence; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property as it will comply with all applicable
building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the replacement of the existing porch with the construction of a
one-story addition will not contribute to congestion on the public streets, as
the home will continue to be used as a single-family residence; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed addition will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2: That a variation be granted from the maximum building size
limitations of Section 22.05(g) of the Zoning Ordinance to permit an increase
to the existing, nonconforming building size, to a total gross floor area of
4,665.85 square feet, whereas 3,744.31 square feet is permitted, resulting in a
variation of 921.54 square feet (24.61%), for the purpose of converting the
existing kitchen into a family room and replacing an existing screened porch
with a 72.92-square foot, one-story addition, which will contain a new kitchen,
covered porch and mud room, in accordance with the plans and documents
submitted with the application for variation, as amended November 9, 1998.
   SECTION 3: That the variation hereby granted is conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed constructing within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 16th day of February, 1999, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES: Trustees Derning, Duhl, Hilton, Lanphier, Lien and Meuer
   NAYS: None.
   ABSENT: None.
   APPROVED this 16th day of February, 1999.
   Signed:
   ss/Louise A. Holland
    Village President
Countersigned:
ss/Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: February 2, 1999
Posted: February 3, 1999
Passed and Approved: February 16, 1999
