ORDINANCE NO. M-565-99
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (475 Poplar)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
   The South 90.60 feet of the North 187.00 feet of the West 100.00 feet,
excepting therefrom the South 24.00 feet of the North 120.40 feet of the West
75.00 feet thereof, of Block 41, in Winnetka, a Subdivision of the Northeast ¼
of section 20 and the North ½ of Fractional Section 21, Township 42 North,
Range 13 East of the Third Principal Meridian, Recorded August 8, 1873, as
Document Number 119881 in Cook County, Illinois,
commonly known as 475 Poplar, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the “A-5”
(One-Fourth Acre) Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Chapter 22, Winnetka Village Code, for the following
variations from the provisions of Section 22.05A(f), (g) and (i) of the Zoning
Ordinance for the purpose of constructing a one-story addition and attic
expansion: (a) a variation of 299.44 square feet (16.5%) from the intensity of
use of lot (roofed lot coverage) provisions of Section 22.05A (f), to allow a
roofed lot coverage of 2,114.44 square feet, whereas 1,815.0 square feet are
permitted; (b) a variation of 344.79 square feet (11.87%) from the maximum
building size (gross floor area) limitations of Section 22.05A(g), to allow a
gross floor area of 3,248.79 square feet, whereas 2,904.0 square feet are
permitted; and (c) a variation of 0.21 feet (3.5%) from the side yard
requirements of Section 22.05(i) to allow a north side yard of 5.79 feet,
whereas 6.0 feet are required; and
   WHEREAS, on January 4, 1999, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variation and by the
favorable vote of four of the five members then present, has reported to the
Council recommending that the requested variation be granted; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and unique hardships associated
with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect
to the Subject Property in that it is an irregularly shaped parcel, the small
size of the home is out of scale with the immediate neighborhood, the
nonconforming north side yard currently exists and will not be affected by the
proposed addition, and expanding the attic by raising the roof as proposed is
what accounts for the increase in the nonconforming floor area, although it
will improve the appearance, scale and functionality of the home on the Subject
Property; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance in that
the existing home has a roof elevation that is inconsistent with the other
residences along the block face and the home does not have adequate living
space on the first floor; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood but will enhance it by balancing the mass of the existing home
with other homes in the immediate vicinity; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not impair an adequate supply of light
and air because the one-story addition is lower than the existing house and is
farther away from the nearest adjoining property than the existing garage, and
the additional height resulting from raising the roof is offset set by the
improved slope of the roof line; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property as it will comply with all applicable
building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed addition will not contribute to congestion on the
public streets, as the home will continue to be used as a single-family
residence; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed attic expansion and one-
story addition will otherwise impair the public health, safety, comfort,
morals, and welfare of the inhabitants of the Village.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2: That the following variations are hereby granted from Sections
22.05A(f), (g), and (i) of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance for the purpose of
constructing a one-story addition and attic expansion: (a) a variation of
299.44 square feet (16.5%) from the intensity of use of lot (roofed lot
coverage) provisions of Section 22.05A (f), to allow a roofed lot coverage of
2,114.44 square feet, whereas 1,815.0 square feet are permitted; (b) a
variation of 344.79 square feet (11.87%) from the maximum building size (gross
floor area) limitations of Section 22.05A(g), to allow a gross floor area of
3,248.79 square feet, whereas 2,904.0 square feet are permitted; and (c) a
variation of 0.21 feet (3.5%) from the side yard requirements of Section 22.05
(i) to allow a north side yard of 5.79 feet, whereas 6.0 feet are required, all
to be constructed in accordance with the plans and drawings submitted with the
application for variation.
   SECTION 3: That the variation hereby granted is conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed constructing within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 16th day of February, 1999, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES: Trustees Derning, Duhl, Hilton, Lien and Meuer
   NAYS: Trustee Lanphier.
   ABSENT: None.
   APPROVED this 16th day of February, 1999.
   Signed:
   ss/Louise A. Holland
    Village President
Countersigned:
ss/Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: February 2, 1999
Posted: February 3, 1999
Passed and Approved: February 16, 1999
Posted: February 17, 1999
ORDINANCE NO. M-566-99
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (559 Oak)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
   Parcel 1: The South 203 feet of Lot 9 and the West 15 feet of Lot 10 in
County Clark’s Division of Block 21 in Winnetka in Section 21, Township 42
North, Range 13, East of the Third Principal Meridian, in Cook County,
Illinois.
   Parcel 2: That part of Lot 6 in County Clerk’s Division of Block 21 lying
East of the East line of North 53.5 feet of said Lot extended South in a
straight line, being a strip of land 8 feet wide and 53 ½ long in Section 21,
Township 42 North, Range 13, East of the Third Principal Meridian, in Cook
County, Illinois.
commonly known as 559 Oak Street, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the “A-5”
(One-Fourth Acre) Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Chapter 22, Winnetka Village Code, for a variation
from the accessory building height limitations of Section 22.05A(c) of the
Zoning Ordinance to permit an increase to the height of the garage walls, which
will result in an increase in the existing nonconforming building height to
17.0 feet representing a 3.0 foot increase (21.43%) whereas a height of 14.0
feet is allowed for the purpose of reconstructing and modifying the existing
coach house roof on the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, on January 4, 1999, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variation and by the
unanimous vote of the five members then present, has reported to the Council
recommending that the requested variation be granted; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and unique hardships associated
with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect
to the Subject Property in that (a) the Subject Property is a one-half acre lot
in the quarter-acre zoning district; (b) the single family residence on the
Subject Property was built around 1860; (c) the subject coach house was
constructed around 1880; (d) the coach house has an existing nonconforming
accessory building height of 16.25 feet; (e) the proposed change will not
increase the actual height of the building, as the height of the ridge line
will be unchanged and the increase in the measurable building height results
from increasing the height of the walls, which raises the height of the bottom
of the roof; and the only conforming alternative would requiring demolish the
entire structure and replacing it with new construction, which is likely to
alter the historic appearance of the Subject Property as a whole; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that
the existing building has become structurally unsound and replacing it with a
new and smaller conforming building would diminish the historic value of the
Subject Property and decrease its utility; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because the coach house has existed for several decades and
the modifications retain the original design elements and existing materials of
the building; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not impair an adequate supply of light
and air because the ridge height of the building will not be changed; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property as it will comply with all applicable
building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the reconstruction of the coach house will not contribute to
congestion on the public streets, as the property will continue to be used for
a single-family occupancy; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed variation will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1: That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2: That a variation is hereby granted from the accessory building
height limitations of Section 22.05A(c) of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance to
permit an increase to the height of the walls of the existing garage, which
will result in an increase in the existing nonconforming building height to
17.0 feet representing a 3.0 foot increase (21.43%) whereas a height of 14.0
feet is allowed, for the purpose of reconstructing and modifying the existing
coach house roof in accordance with the plans and drawings submitted with the
application for variation.
   SECTION 3: That the variation hereby granted is conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed constructing within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 16th day of February, 1999, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES: Trustees Derning, Duhl, Hilton, Lanphier, Lien and Meuer
   NAYS: None.
   ABSENT: None.
   APPROVED this 16th day of February, 1999.
   Signed:
   ss/Louise A. Holland
    Village President
Countersigned:
ss/Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
