ORDINANCE NO. M-567-99
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (548 Cherry Street)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
   That part of Block 46 in Winnetka in Cook County, Illinois described as
follows: Beginning at a point in the intersection of the South line of Cherry
St. and the West line of Poplar St., thence West along the South line of Cherry
St. a distance 90 ft., thence South on a line drawn parallel with the West line
of Poplar St. a distance of 187 ft., thence East on a line parallel with the
south Line of Cherry St. a distance of 90 ft. to the West line of Poplar St.,
thence North on the West line of Poplar St. 187 ft. to the point of beginning.
commonly known as 548 Cherry Street, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the “A-
5” (One-Fourth Acre) Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Chapter 22, Winnetka Village Code, for variations
from the building height and maximum building size (gross floor area)
provisions of Sections 22.05A(c) and (g) of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, to
allow a building height of three stories rather than the maximum allowable
height of 2-1/2 stories, and to allow a gross floor area of 5,464.8 square feet
whereas 5,180 square feet is permitted, resulting in a variation of 283.8
square feet (5.48%), for the purpose of reconstructing a portion of the rear of
the residence and constructing a three-story addition, a one-story addition and
a wrap-around porch and a 500 square-foot detached garage on the Subject
Property, in accordance with the plans and drawings submitted with the
application for variation; and
   WHEREAS, on January 11, 1999, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variations and by the
unanimous vote of the five members then present, has reported to the Council
recommending that the requested variations be granted; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and unique hardships associated
with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect
to the Subject Property in that the existing 3-story home was built in the
early 1900’s, has a legally non-conforming building height because the floor is
60% of the area of the floor below, the proposed addition would maintain the
existing roof and ridge lines, and lowering the building height to reduce the
floor area on the third floor would hinder natural light and ventilation on the
third floor and present future maintenance difficulties where the roof and wall
would join; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that
the family room addition and kitchen and bedroom improvements will bring the
amenities of the residence into conformity with the amenities that are
typically found in single family residences in the area; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because it is designed to be an extension of the existing
structure that is consistent in height, width and building materials; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not impair an adequate supply of light
and air; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property as it will comply with all applicable
building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed addition will not contribute to congestion on the
public streets, as the home will continue to be used as a single-family
residence; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed addition will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1: That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2: That variations are hereby be granted from the building height
and maximum building size (gross floor area) provisions of Sections 22.05A(c)
and (g) of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, to allow a building height of three
stories rather than the maximum allowable height of 2-1/2 stories, and to allow
a gross floor area of 5,464.8 square feet whereas 5,180 square feet is
permitted, resulting in a variation of 283.8 square feet (5.48%), for the
purpose of reconstructing a portion of the rear of the residence and
constructing a three-story addition, a one-story addition and a wrap-around
porch and a 500 square-foot detached garage on the Subject Property, in
accordance with the plans and drawings submitted with the application for
variation.
   SECTION 3: That the variations hereby granted are conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed constructing within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 16th day of February, 1999, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES: Trustees Derning, Duhl, Hilton, Lanphier, Lien and Meuer
   NAYS: None.
   ABSENT: None.
   APPROVED this 16th day of February, 1999.
   Signed:
   ss/Louise A. Holland
    Village President
Countersigned:
ss/Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: February 2, 1999
Posted: February 3, 1999
Passed and Approved: February 16, 1999
