ORDINANCE NO. M-568-99
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (984 Hill Road)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
   Parcel 1: Lot 1 (except the East 1 foot), Lots 2 to 4, both inclusive and
lot 5 (except the Westerly 15 feet thereof). All in block 1 in Bradford and
Porter’s addition to Kenilworth, a subdivision of the west ½ of the West ½ of
the Northwest ¼ of the Southeast ¼ of Section 29, Township 42 North, Range 13,
East of the Third Principal Meridian, in Cook County, Illinois.
   Parcel 2: The North ½ of the vacated alley South and adjoining Parcel 1, in
Cook County, Illinois,
commonly known as 984 Hill Road, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the “A-3”
(One Acre) Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in the Winnetka
Zoning Ordinance, Chapter 22, Winnetka Village Code, for a variation from the
rear yard requirements of Section 22.07A(j) of the Zoning Ordinance to allow a
rear yard setback of 34.33 feet, instead of the minimum required rear yard set
back of 50 feet, resulting in a variation of 15.67 feet for the purpose of
allowing the construction of a single, shed dormer on the Subject Property in
accordance with the plans submitted with the application; and
   WHEREAS, on August 18, 1998, on due notice thereof, the Village Council
adopted Ordinance M-530-98 which granted a variation of 31.63 feet (63.26%)
from the required minimum of 50 feet to allow a first floor addition to observe
a rear set back of 18.70 feet; and
   WHEREAS, the applicants have since revised the plans to provide for
construction of a shed dormer across the second floor rather than the two
smaller second floor dormers that were included in the plans approved pursuant
to Ordinance M-530-98; and
   WHEREAS, although it extends across a greater length of roof than the two
dormers originally approved, the proposed shed dormer will slightly reduce the
degree of rear yard encroachment from what was originally approved; and
   WHEREAS, on January 11, 1999, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variation and by the
unanimous vote of the five members then present, has reported to the Council
recommending that the requested variation be granted; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and unique hardships associated
with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect
to the Subject Property in that the Subject Property is a wide and shallow lot
that existed before the Subject Property was annexed into the Village in 1989,
the Subject Property has a legal, nonconforming lot area of approximately one-
third acre and the existing house has legally nonconforming front and rear
yards; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that
the 33-foot buildable lot depth resulting from applying the one-acre
regulations to the Subject Property would prohibit any addition, even though
the building size and lot coverage are below the maximum permitted; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood, as it is consistent with the design and scale of the existing
home and the scale of homes in the neighborhood; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not impair an adequate supply of light
and air as the proposed dormer is within the allowable height limitations in
the one-acre zoning district; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property as it will comply with all applicable
building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the second floor dormer addition will not contribute to congestion
on the public streets, as the home will continue to be used as a single-family
residence; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed construction of two second
floor dormers will otherwise impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals,
and welfare of the inhabitants of the Village.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2:  That a variation be granted from the rear yard requirements of
Section 22.07A(j) of the Zoning Ordinance to allow a rear yard setback of 34.33
feet, instead of the minimum required rear yard set back of 50 feet, resulting
in a variation of 15.67 feet for the purpose of allowing the construction of a
single, shed dormer on the Subject Property in accordance with the plans
submitted with the application.
   SECTION 3: That the variation hereby granted is conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed constructing within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 16th day of February, 1999, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES: Trustees Derning, Duhl, Hilton, Lanphier, Lien and Meuer
   NAYS: None.
   ABSENT: None.
   APPROVED this 16th day of February, 1999.
   Signed:
   ss/Louise A. Holland
    Village President
Countersigned:
ss/Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
