ORDINANCE NO. M-569-99
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A SPECIAL USE PERMIT TO ALLOW RENOVATION OF WINNETKA
STATION PARK
   WHEREAS, the Winnetka Park District is the owner of a parcel of real estate
(the “Subject Property”) bounded by Elm Street, Oak Street and Green Bay Road,
which is commonly known as the Winnetka Station Park, and is legally described
as follows:
All of a certain tract of land bounded on the South by Oak Street, on the West
by Linden Street and on the North and East by the right of way of the Chicago
and Northwestern Railway Company, more particularly described as follows:
Lot Twelve in Wilson and Dale’s Subdivision of the North half of Block Twenty-
six in the Village of Winnetka;
Also that part of Block Twenty-six in the Village of Winnetka in Cook County,
Illinois, and of the land East of and adjoining said block described as
follows: Commencing at the North East corner of Oak and Linden Streets and
running thence North on the East line of said Linden Street 187 feet thence
East on a line parallel with the South line of said block to the Westerly line
of the right of way of the Chicago and Northwestern Railway Company, thence
Southerly along said line to the point at which the South line of said Block
Twenty-six, if projected in an Easterly direction, would intersect the Westerly
line of the said right of way of the Chicago and Northwestern Railway Company,
thence West along said line to the place of beginning.
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property is located in the “A-1” (One-Sixth Acre)
Single-Family Residential Zoning District of the Village of Winnetka, as
provided in the Zoning Ordinance of said Village; and
   WHEREAS, Winnetka Station Park is a public park and is therefore a public
use, which uses are permitted as special uses in all zoning districts, subject
to the conditions and requirements pertaining to special uses, as set forth in
Section 22.13 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Chapter 22, Winnetka Village
Code; and
   WHEREAS, the Winnetka Park District has submitted an application to the
Village of Winnetka seeking a special use permit under Section 22.13 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Chapter 22, Winnetka Village Code, to permit the
renovation of the park on the Subject Property with new pathways, benches,
lighting, landscaping and other amenities, including a brick seat wall, a brick
planter and a drinking fountain; and
   WHEREAS, upon due notice, the Design Review Board met on February 24, 1999,
to consider the redesign of the park and by the unanimous vote of the four
members then present, has submitted a comment that a Certificate of
Appropriateness would issue for the design presented; and
   WHEREAS, upon due notice, the Plan Commission met on February 24, 1999 to
consider the application of the Park District, and by the unanimous vote of the
nine members then present, found that the proposed special use is consistent
with the Parks and Open Space System and Elm Street Business District
provisions of Chapters XI and XIX of the Winnetka Comprehensive Plan, and
recommended that the special use be granted, subject to the condition that the
landscaping be completed next year; and
   WHEREAS, upon due notice on March 8, 1999, the Zoning Board of Appeals held
a public hearing on the proposed special use and, by the unanimous vote of the
entire Board, has reported to the Council recommending that the requested
special use be granted; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed renovation of Winnetka Station Park will benefit the
public health, safety, comfort, morals and general welfare of the Village, by
locating pedestrian pathways away from adjacent vehicular thoroughfares, by
improving site maintenance and eliminating potential problems from snow and
salt spill on the grass, and by providing improved lighting and a drinking
fountain, all in accordance with applicable Village Code requirements; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed special use will not be substantially injurious to the
use and enjoyment of other property in the immediate vicinity, nor will it
substantially diminish or impair property values in the immediate vicinity, as
the use is established in the neighborhood, the renovation will not increase
the intensity of the existing use, and the renovation will improve both the
functionality and appearance of the park; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed special use will not impede the normal and orderly
development or improvement of other property in the immediate vicinity for uses
permitted under applicable zoning regulations, as the facility is a long
established use in the neighborhood and the type of use of the Subject Property
will not be changed; and
   WHEREAS, adequate measures have been taken to provide ingress and egress in
a manner which minimizes pedestrian and vehicular traffic congestion, as a
result of the proposed changes to the pedestrian circulation routes through the
park, which will not change any surrounding vehicular traffic patterns; and
   WHEREAS, adequate parking and access roads serve the Subject Property and,
upon construction of the improvements in accordance with the plans, the
addition of electric and water service will improve lighting and provide for
facilities that will permit better maintenance of the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, the special use in all other respects conforms to the applicable
regulations of this and other Village ordinances and codes;
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain:
   SECTION 1:   That the Village Council hereby adopts the foregoing recitals
as their findings, as if fully set forth herein.
   SECTION 2: That a special use permit under Section 22.13 of the Zoning
Ordinance adopted by the Village of Winnetka, Cook County, Illinois, and now in
effect, is granted with respect to the Subject Property, which is located in
the "A-1" (One-Sixth Acre) Single-Family Residential District provided in said
Zoning Ordinance and which is commonly known as Winnetka Station Park, to
permit the renovation of the park located on the Subject Property, said work to
be done in accordance with the application for special use permit and the plans
dated December 1, 1998, which accompanied the application.
   SECTION 3: That the special use permit hereby granted is conditioned upon
the commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the
effective date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: That, pursuant to Section 22.13(g) of the Zoning Ordinance, all
stipulations, conditions and restrictions set forth in this Ordinance as part
of the terms under which the special use is granted, may be modified or revised
from time to time by the Village Council following public notice and hearing,
using the same procedures set forth in the Zoning Ordinance for processing the
original special use application.
   SECTION 5:   That this Ordinance shall be in full force and effect from and
after its passage, approval, and posting.
   ADOPTED this 20th day of April, 1999, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES: Trustees Derning, Duhl, Hilton, Lanphier, Lien and Meuer
   NAYS: None.
   ABSENT: None.
   APPROVED this __ day of __, 1999.
   Signed:
   ss/Louise A. Holland
    Village President
