ORDINANCE NO. M-571-99
AN ORDINANCE AUTHORIZING THE SALE OR OTHER DISPOSITION OF PERSONAL PROPERTY OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA
   WHEREAS, From time to time, the Winnetka Police Department comes into
possession of items of personal property that have been lost, found or stolen,
which personal property includes the property described in the “Police
Recovered Items Other Than Bicycles” shown on Exhibit A and the “Bicycles”
shown on Exhibit B (hereinafter collectively referred to as the “Personal
Property”), attached to this ordinance; and
   WHEREAS, the Personal Property has been in the possession of the Winnetka
Police Department for more than 6 months, during which time the Chief of Police
of the Winnetka Police Department has made reasonable inquiry and efforts to
identify and notify the owners or other persons entitled to possession of the
Personal Property, but has been unsuccessful in those attempts; and
   WHEREAS, pursuant to Section 3 of the Law Enforcement Disposition of
Property Act, 765 ILCS 1030/3, the Chief of Police has transferred the custody
of the Personal Property to the Village of Winnetka; and
   WHEREAS, the Winnetka Fire Department has determined that two cascade air
tanks (hereinafter referred to as the “Air Tanks”), purchased by the Village in
the late 1970’s, are no longer useful to the Fire Department and has
recommended that the Air Tanks be declared surplus personal property and be
donated to the joint training facility (NIPSTA) in Glenview, Illinois; and
   WHEREAS, the Village of Winnetka owns computer equipment, which will be
retired during the fiscal year, April 1, 1999 through March 31, 2000
(hereinafter referred to as “Retired Computer Equipment”), and which the
Finance Director has recommended be disposed of by methods that are consistent
with past disposal methods; and
   WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka have determined that the
Personal Property, Retired Computer Equipment and surplus Air Tanks described
herein (hereinafter referred to collectively as the “Surplus Property”) are no
longer necessary or useful to the Village of Winnetka, that the cost of storing
said Surplus Property exceeds its value to the Village of Winnetka, that it is
not in the best interests of the Village of Winnetka to retain said Surplus
Property and that the Surplus Property should be disposed of in the manner
hereinafter provided, pursuant to Section 11-76-4 of the Illinois Municipal
Code (65 ILCS 5/11-76-4), so as to avoid incurring additional costs for storing
said Surplus Property.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain:
   SECTION 1 : Pursuant to Section 11-76-4 of the Illinois Municipal Code (65
ILCS 5/11-76-4), the Council of the Village of Winnetka find that the above-
described Surplus Property, consisting of the items of Personal Property shown
on the attached Exhibits A and B, the surplus Air Tanks, and the Retired
Computer Equipment, is not necessary or useful to the Village of Winnetka and
that the best interests of the Village of Winnetka will be served by the sale
or other disposition of such Surplus Property.
   SECTION 2 : The Council of the Village of Winnetka further find that, based
on prior experience in disposing of similar items of property, the cost of
conducting a public sale of the Surplus Property, including the costs of
advertising and publishing, and the costs of personnel needed to maintain
security and to conduct the public sale, exceeds the value of such items.
   SECTION 3: That pursuant to said Section 11-76-4, the Village Manager is
hereby authorized and directed to determine the value of and to dispose of the
Personal Property in any manner he deems reasonable under the circumstances.
   SECTION 4 : That pursuant to said Section 11-76-4, the Village Manager is
hereby authorized and directed to determine the value of the surplus Air Tanks
and to donate them to the joint training facility (NIPSTA) in Glenview,
Illinois, subject to such terms and conditions as he deems reasonable under the
circumstances.
   SECTION 5: That pursuant to said Section 11-76-4, the Director of Finance is
hereby authorized and directed to determine the value of and to dispose of
Retired Computer Equipment that is retired during the fiscal year April 1, 1999
through March 31, 2000, in accordance with disposal methods consistent with
past disposal practices.
   SECTION 6: That this Ordinance shall be in full force and effect from and
after its passage, approval, and posting.
   ADOPTED this 4th day of May, 1999, pursuant to the following roll call vote:
   AYES: Trustees Aquilino, Derning, Duhl, Hilton, and Powell.
   NAYS: None.
   ABSENT: Trustee Lien.
   APPROVED this 4th day of May, 1999.
   Signed:
   ss/Louise A. Holland
    Village President
Countersigned:
ss/Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: April 20, 1999
Posted: April 21, 1999
Passed and Approved: May 4, 1999
