ORDINANCE NO. M-573-99
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (1310 Forest Glen)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
   Lot 34, Lot 35, and the West 3 feet of Lot 36, in Forest Glen, a Subdivision
of the South 660 feet of the Southeast ¼ of the Southeast ¼ of the Northeast ¼
of Section 18, Township 42 North, Range 13, East of the Third Principal
Meridian, also the South 660 feet of the East 1 Acre of the Southwest ¼ of the
Southeast ¼ of the Northeast ¼ of said Section 18, (Except therefrom the South
33 feet and except the East 33 feet of the Southeast ¼ of the Southeast ¼ of
the Northeast ¼ of said Section 18, in Cook County, Illinois,
commonly known as 1310 Forest Glen, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
5 Single-Family Residential District (formerly the A-1 One-Sixth Acre Zoning
District) provided in the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Chapter 22, Winnetka
Village Code, for a variation from the maximum building size limitations of
Section 22.05(g) to allow a gross floor area of 4,858.65 square feet on a
13,616 square foot lot that would be created pursuant to a pending
resubdivision request, whereas 4,538.20 square feet would be permitted,
resulting in a variation of 320.45 square feet (7.06%) for the purpose of
constructing a 301.11 square foot one-story addition at the rear of the
residence on the Subject Property in accordance with the plans and
specifications submitted with the application; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property has an existing area of 22,020 square feet,
and the residence for which the one-story addition is proposed is located
entirely on a platted lot that has an area of 11,190 square feet; and
   WHEREAS, the portion of the Subject Property on which the residence is
located permits a maximum building size of 4,062.20 square feet, which would
result in a variation of 1,073.46 square feet (26.43%); and
   WHEREAS, on April 12, 1999, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variation and by the
unanimous vote of the four members then present, has reported to the Council
recommending that the requested variation be granted; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties associated with carrying out the
strict application of the Zoning Ordinance in that the existing kitchen is
small, poorly located and inefficient, and the interior of the house on the
Subject Property cannot be reconfigured to allow for the expansion and
upgrading of the kitchen; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that
the ability of the home to yield a reasonable return is measured by the
livability of the home for the applicant’s family, and the size and location of
the kitchen make it difficult for the family to utilize the kitchen space in a
practical manner; and
   WHEREAS, the plight of the owner is due to unique circumstances in that
granting the variation in conjunction with the proposed resubdivision will
allow expansion of the residence, while reducing the level of nonconformity and
reducing the size of the remaining parcel of the Subject Property from 10,830
square feet to 8,404 square feet, which will, in turn, reduce the maximum
building size that would be permitted on the remainder of the Subject Property;
and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because the proposed addition is one story high, is not
substantially larger than the solarium that it will replace, will be located at
the rear of the residence and is designed to conform with existing
architecture; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not impair an adequate supply of light
and air because the proposed addition is a single-story addition that is lower
than the building to which it will be attached; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property as it will comply with all applicable
building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed addition will not contribute to congestion on the
public streets, as the residence will continue to be used as a single-family
home; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed addition will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2:  That a variation is hereby granted to the Subject Property,
commonly known as 1310 Forest Glen, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
5 Single-Family Residential District (formerly the A-1 One-Sixth Acre Zoning
District) provided in the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Chapter 22 Winnetka
Village Code for variation from the maximum building size limitations of
Section 22.05(g) to allow a gross floor area of 4,858.65 square feet on a
13,616 square foot lot that will be created pursuant to the applicants’ pending
resubdivision request, whereas 4,538.20 square feet will be permitted,
resulting in a variation of 320.45 square feet (7.06%), for the purpose of
constructing a 301.11 square foot, one-story addition at the rear of the
residence on the Subject Property, in accordance with the plans and
specifications submitted with the application.
   SECTION 3: That the variation hereby granted is conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed constructing within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage, approval
and posting.
   ADOPTED this ______ day of ______________________, 1999, pursuant to the
following roll call vote:
