ORDINANCE NO. M-579-99
AN ORDINANCE AUTHORIZING NORTH SHORE GAS COMPANY TO USE PUBLIC RIGHTS-OF-WAY TO
PROVIDE NATURAL GAS SERVICE IN THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA, COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS
   WHEREAS, North Shore Gas Company, an Illinois Corporation (hereinafter
sometimes referred to as "Grantee”), desires to render gas service within the
corporate limits of the Village of Winnetka, Illinois, a municipal corporation
(hereinafter sometimes referred to as the "Municipality"), and
   WHEREAS, Grantee has requested the Village of Winnetka, Illinois, to grant a
new nonexclusive franchise to Grantee, its successors and assigns:
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as
follows:.
   SECTION 1:  The right, permission and authority be and the same are hereby
granted for a term of twenty-five (25) years to North Shore Gas Company, its
successors and assigns, to construct, operate and maintain in, under, along and
across each and all of the public streets, sidewalks, alleys, avenues, bridges,
other public thoroughfares or parkways within the present corporate limits of
the Village of Winnetka, Cook County, Illinois, and in any presently
unincorporated area which may become annexed thereto, a system of pipes, tubing
mains, feeding or service pipes and other devices, apparatus and equipment as
may be necessary or convenient for the construction, operation and maintenance
of a system for the production, distribution, and sale of gas for fuel,
heating, processing and other purposes in and through the Municipality, subject
to the conditions and regulations hereinafter set forth.
   SECTION 2: Immediately upon completion of any work involving excavation,
Grantee shall restore or replace any removed or displaced sod and leave the
surface of the ground in a neatly graded and safe condition. If any sidewalks,
parkways or pavements are disturbed by Grantee, then Grantee shall restore them
within thirty (30) days of such disturbance, to as good condition as before
such sidewalk, parkway or pavement was disturbed by Grantee. Grantee shall
promptly replace, at its own expense, any tree, shrub, or other vegetation
located in a parkway which, as a result of Grantee's construction, operation,
or maintenance of its system, may be so damaged as to require replacement. In
the event it is impractical to replace any such tree, shrub or vegetation
damaged as aforesaid, Grantee shall reimburse the Municipality for the
reasonable replacement cost thereof. In the event that such sidewalk, parkway
or pavement shall become uneven, unsettled or otherwise require repair and said
disturbance was directly caused by work previously performed by the Grantee,
Grantee shall, within forty-five (45) days of receipt of written notice from
the Municipality, cause said sidewalk, parkway or pavement to be repaired or
restored to as good condition as before such sidewalk, parkway or pavement was
disturbed by Grantee. In the event of any failure of Grantee to promptly
repair, restore, or replace any of the areas described above within the said
forty-five (45) day period, or in the case said repair, restoration,
replacement, grading or other work is not reasonably satisfactory to the
Municipality, the Municipality may restore, repair, or replace any sod,
sidewalk, parkway, pavement, tree, shrub, or other vegetation to its prior
condition, the reasonable expense of which work shall be paid by Grantee. In
all cases, reasonable exceptions shall be made to the time in which the repair
or work requirements must be completed because of adverse climatic conditions.
   Grantee shall maintain a $10,000 performance bond with the Municipality to
stand as security for the performance of any repair, restoration or other
obligation under this Section 2 Such performance bond shall be in a form
reasonably acceptable to the Municipality and shall be issued by a surety
licensed to do business in the State of Illinois and having at least an "A"
rating from A. M. Best or comparable rating agency, or otherwise reasonably
acceptable to the Municipality. In the event the amount of such bond is reduced
to reimburse the Municipality or to pay for the costs of work performed by or
for the benefit of the Municipality under this Section 2, Grantee shall restore
the bond to its full value within fifteen (15) days.
   SECTION 3:  Grantee shall not open or encumber more of any street, sidewalk,
alley, avenue, bridge, other public thoroughfare or parkway at any time than
may be reasonably necessary to enable Grantee to construct or maintain its
pipes, tubing, mains, feeding or service pipes and other devices, apparatus and
equipment, nor shall Grantee permit any such street, sidewalk, alley, avenue,
bridge, other public thoroughfare or parkway to remain open or encumbered for a
longer period than shall be reasonably necessary to complete the intended work.
Grantee shall use reasonable care to keep all structures which it shall
construct by virtue of this Ordinance, in a safe condition at all times, and
shall maintain such barriers and danger signals during the construction, repair
or renewal of said structures as are reasonably required to avoid damage to
life, limb and property.
   SECTION 4:  In the event the Municipality makes a change in grade, width or
location of any street, sidewalk, alley, avenue, bridge or other public
thoroughfare or parkway, in such a manner that good engineering practice shall
require Grantee to relocate its pipes, tubing, mains, feeding or service pipes
or other devices, apparatus or equipment, Grantee shall relocate such
facilities at its expense upon reasonable notice from the Municipality. In
those instances where there shall exist applicable orders, rules and
regulations of the Illinois Commerce Commission, said orders, rules and
regulations shall be presumptive evidence of what constitutes "good engineering
practice" as that term is used in the foregoing provision. The Municipality and
the Grantee believe that it is in their mutual interest that each be informed
of the long-term capital improvement programs of the other so that, wherever
practicable, such programs may be coordinated to minimize the cost of
construction and the inconvenience to the public. To this end, representatives
of Grantee and the Village Manager, or his designated representative, shall
confer once per year. The foregoing provision is not intended to limit the
frequency of contact or the extent of cooperation between said representatives,
but rather to assure that there is some regular contact and discussion. It is
recognized that the respective capital improvement programs of the Municipality
and of Grantee are subject to change, particularly in the case of Grantee,
which is required to provide service to customers in accordance with the
orders, rules and regulations of the Illinois Commerce Commission.
   SECTION 5:  Grantee shall obtain from the Village Engineer a permit in
connection with openings to be made in the streets, sidewalks, alleys, avenues,
bridges, or public thoroughfares or parkways for maintenance, repair or renewal
work or for construction or installation of new pipes, tubing, mains, feeding
or service pipes or other devices, apparatus and equipment and Grantee shall
install its tubing, mains, feeding or service pipes and other devices,
apparatus and equipment in such part or parts of said streets, sidewalks,
alleys, avenues, bridges, other public thoroughfares or parkways in the
Municipality as authorized, provided, however, that emergency work may be done
without prior permit when necessary to prevent interruption in service or for
protection of public safety, and permits therefore shall be obtained as soon as
practically convenient thereafter. Any work performed pursuant to this section
shall be subject to the restoration requirements in Section 2 of this
Ordinance. In addition, prior to the installation of gas mains pursuant to this
Ordinance, Grantee shall file with the Village Engineer, for his information
and use, working drawings of Grantee's mains and, if requested, shall file with
said Village Engineer, for his information and use, permanent maps of Grantee's
gas mains, which maps shall be updated annually by Grantee.
   SECTION 6:  For and in consideration of the passage of this Ordinance by the
President and Board of Trustees of the Municipality, and in lieu of franchise
tax, and any other fees or charges of any kind, including permit, inspection or
street opening fees or charges, upon acceptance of this Ordinance and
thereafter for so long as this Ordinance remains in full force and effect and
Grantee has the right pursuant to the authorization of the Illinois Commerce
Commission to serve the Municipality, Grantee shall, upon timely notification
of occupancy by the Municipality, and subject to any orders, rules and
regulations of the Illinois Commerce Commission hereafter adopted, supply
without charge to the Municipality an amount of gas sufficient for the
reasonable, efficient and normal operation of space heating and domestic-type
water heating equipment (and in the case of a fire house, also domestic-type
cooking and clothes drying equipment) in municipal buildings owned and occupied
from time to time by the Municipality predominantly for governmental purposes
or for such part thereof as may from time to time be so occupied, and not for
proprietary or revenue-producing purposes, such buildings being supplied being
located in the incorporated area of the Municipality which lies within
Grantee's service territory. Without limiting the foregoing exclusion, it is
understood that such gas shall not be supplied for schools, parks, swimming
pools or other recreational buildings or facilities or non-free public
libraries. Accordingly, the buildings listed below are represented to qualify
to be served currently pursuant to this Ordinance so long as they are so used:
Police Department   410 Green Bay Road       Account #919276-87701
Fire Department   428 Green Bay Road       Account #919276-87701
Village Hall      510 Green Bay Road       Account #919321-87601
Public Works      1390 Willow Road      Account #932681-87001
         (East Building)
Fire Training Tower   1390 Willow Road      Account #932686-87501
Similarly qualified buildings occupied in the future by the Municipality for
the purposes indicated above, whether in substitution for or in addition to the
foregoing, shall be served pursuant to this Ordinance.
   SECTION 7:  Grantee shall use reasonable care to cause its pipes, tubing,
mains, feeding or service pipes, and other devices, apparatus, and equipment
located within the Municipality to be kept in a safe condition at all times.
Where applicable safety orders, rules and regulations of the Illinois Commerce
Commission exist, said orders, rules and regulations shall be presumptive
evidence in determining a safe condition.
   Grantee undertakes and agrees to defend, indemnify and hold harmless the
Municipality and its officers, officials, employees, agents, attorneys, and
representatives from and against any and all damages, claims, demands, actions,
causes of action, judgments, losses, or liabilities (including reasonable
attorneys' fees) arising out of any negligent acts or omissions of Grantee, its
officers, agents, servants or contractors in connection with the installation,
construction, operation, maintenance or repair of Grantee's facilities, or from
Grantee's performance or failure to perform any of its obligations under this
Ordinance. The Municipality agrees to provide Grantee with prompt written
notice upon becoming aware of any such claim, demand, action or cause of
action. The Municipality shall not offer or enter into a settlement of any such
claim, demand, action or cause of action brought against the Municipality
without the prior written approval of Grantee, provided that Grantee has
acknowledged in writing to the Municipality that it has accepted responsibility
to pay the entire cost, expenses and claims under terms of this indemnity.
   SECTION 8: Grantee shall, at its sole cost and expense, insure against the
liabilities assumed under Section 7 herein by maintaining general public
liability insurance, including contractual liability coverage, against claims
for personal injury, death or property damage arising within the territory for
which this Ordinance is granted. Such insurance shall afford excess protection
in the amount of Five Million Dollars ($5,000,000) beyond the usual and
customary deductible amounts for a public utility of Grantee's size and
character. All policies shall be written in companies customarily used by
public utilities for said purposes. Certificates of insurance naming the
Municipality, its officers, agents and employees as additional insureds shall
be delivered to the Municipality within the time prescribed in Section 12 of
this Ordinance. Said certificates of insurance shall contain a statement of the
insurer that it will give the Municipality written notice not less than ten
(10) days prior to the expiration of any then current policy. Throughout the
term of this Ordinance, Grantee shall maintain and keep in force the minimum
insurance coverages and limits required pursuant to this Ordinance.
   SECTION 9:  If Grantee fails to comply with any of the provisions in this
Ordinance (unless Grantee is otherwise directed by order, rule, or regulation
of any governmental agency having jurisdiction or any court of competent
jurisdiction, or unless compliance by Grantee with such provision is prohibited
or adjudged unlawful by order, rule or regulation of any governmental agency
having jurisdiction or any court of competent jurisdiction), the Municipality
shall give notice to Grantee of such failure. Within forty-five (45) days after
such notice, Grantee shall have the opportunity to take all necessary steps to
come into full compliance with this Ordinance. If Grantee fails to comply, the
Municipality through its President and Board of Trustees may at its option,
then or at any time within one year thereafter, prior to grantee coming into
full compliance with this ordinance, declare the rights and privileges hereby
granted to be terminated, and any costs incurred by the Municipality (including
reasonable attorney's fees) in connection with such termination or any other
enforcement action shall be reimbursed by Grantee, which obligations shall
survive the termination or expiration of this grant.
   SECTION 10:  All provisions of this Ordinance which are obligatory upon, or
which inure to the benefit of Grantee, shall also be obligatory upon, and shall
inure to the benefit of, any and all successors and assigns of Grantee, and the
word "Grantee," wherever appearing in this Ordinance, shall include, and be
taken to mean, not only North Shore Gas Company, but also each and all of its
successors and assigns; provided, however, that such successors and assigns are
approved by the Illinois Commerce Commission. All provisions of this Ordinance
which are obligatory upon, or which inure to the benefit of the Municipality,
shall also be obligatory upon and shall inure to the benefit of, any and all
successors and assigns of the Municipality, and the word "Municipality,”
wherever appearing in this Ordinance, shall include, and shall be taken to
mean, not only the Village of Winnetka, but also each and all of said
successors and assigns.
   SECTION 11:  If Grantee shall at any time during the duration of this
Ordinance obtain a franchise from any other incorporated city of village with a
population of less than 10,000, wherein such franchise (including any
supplemental agreement or side letters of understanding or agreement) is
granted subject to terms that, in the reasonable opinion of the Municipality,
are more protective of the public interest of such other incorporated city or
village than the terms of the Ordinance are to such interests of the
Municipality, then the Municipality may adopt an ordinance in substitution of
this Ordinance. The substitute ordinance shall be substantially identical in
its terms and provisions to the franchise granted by such other incorporated
city or village, whereupon Grantee shall accept such substitute ordinance in
lieu of this Ordinance. For the purposes of this provision the term "franchise"
or "franchise agreement" shall mean the agreement or ordinance adopted by the
Municipality and accepted by the Grantee, complete with amendments, extensions,
side letters or supplemental agreements and any other documents that can
reasonably be construed as being part of or affecting Grantee's agreement with
said Municipality regarding the terms, conditions and expectations for
occupation of the public way of such Municipality.
   Upon at least ten (10) days prior written notice from the Municipality (and
no more frequently than once in any calendar year), the Grantee shall make
available to the Municipality (or to authorized representatives) for
examination at the Grantee's principal office all original executed franchise
agreements together with all amendments, extensions and side letters in effect
during the term of this agreement.
   Upon written request, the Grantee shall provide the Municipality a list of
all franchise agreements executed during the year immediately preceding the
date of request. Such list shall include: name of municipality, municipal
contact, term of agreement, and date executed.
   SECTION 12:  This Ordinance, if accepted by Grantee as hereinafter provided,
shall be in full force and effect on and after its passage and acceptance
thereof by Grantee and delivery of the certificates of insurance hereinabove
required, and shall, from and after the effective date, supersede, cancel and
be in lieu of any and all other existing or prior grants of right, permission
and authority to Grantee or any predecessor companies or assignors of Grantee,
to construct, operate and maintain any system for the production, distribution
and sale of gas for fuel, heating, processing and other purposes within the
Municipality. Within thirty (30) days after the approval of this Ordinance,
Grantee shall file with the Municipality acceptance in writing of all of the
terms and provisions hereof. This agreement shall be binding upon the successor
to the assets or business of the Grantee.
   SECTION 13:  All Ordinances heretofore passed which conflict with this
Ordinance hereby revealed.
   ADOPTED this 15th day of June, 1999, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES: Trustees Aquilino, Derning, Duhl, Hilton, Lien, and Powell.
   NAYS: None.
   ABSENT: None.
   APPROVED this 15th day of June, 1999.
   Signed:
   ss/Louise A. Holland
    Village President
Countersigned:
ss/Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: June 8, 199
Posted: June 9, 1999
Passed and Approved: June 15, 1999
Posted: June 16, 1999
ORDINANCE NO. M-580-99
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (466 POPLAR)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
   The north 30 feet of the east 25 feet of Lot 8 and the north 60 feet of Lots
9 and 10, in Sherlock’s Subdivision of Block 40 in Winnetka, according to the
map of the subdivision of said block recorded April 24, 1871, as document
number 93665, in Book 173, of maps, page 29, in Cook County, Illinois;
commonly known as 466 Poplar Street, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
5 Single-Family Residential District provided in the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance,
Chapter 22, Winnetka Village Code, for a variation from the maximum building
size (Gross Floor Area) limitations of Section 22.05A(g) of the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance, to allow a gross floor area of 3,928.87 square feet, whereas 3,645
square feet are permitted, resulting in a variation of 283.87 square feet
(7.8%), and variations from the side yard and building line articulation
requirements of Sections 22.05A(i) and 22.05A(q) of the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance, to allow a side yard of 5.70 feet, whereas 8.00 feet are required,
resulting in a variation of 2.30 feet (29%), all of said variations being
requested for the purpose of allowing the replacement of an existing family
room in its original size and location; and
   WHEREAS, on September 15, 1998, the Council of the Village of Winnetka
enacted ordinance M-531-98, which granted a gross floor area variation of
283.37 square feet (7.8%) to allow the construction of a 5-foot by 28-foot,
one-story breakfast room addition on the west side of the existing kitchen of
the single-family residence on the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, part of the work specified in the construction documents that
accompanied the requested variation in 1998 included work on the existing
family room, which is in a nonconforming location; and
   WHEREAS, in the course of performing the work on the Subject Property, it
was determined that the existing family room did not have a complete foundation
and therefore required extensive corrective work consisting of replacing the
family room in order to provide a complete and structurally sound foundation;
and
   WHEREAS, the work proposed for the existing family room exceeds the scope of
maintenance that is permitted on legally nonconforming structures; and
   WHEREAS, the alternatives available to the owners of the Subject Property
are either removal of the family room entirely, which would result in the loss
of an essential element of living space and a substantial loss of value to the
property, or to pour partial footings or piers to support the family room,
which is not desirable from a structural standpoint and which would also result
in a loss of value to the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and particular hardships
associated with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance
with respect to the Subject Property in that the requested variations do not
increase the size or location of the building as approved by the Council when
it adopted ordinance number M-531-98, and the requested variations result from
conditions unknown to the applicant at that time; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that
the requested variations are necessary to allow the completion of the work
previously approved by the Council, consisting of the renovation of the
existing kitchen, the creation of a new breakfast room, and more direct access
to the family room from the kitchen, all of which are typical elements of
single family residences in the area; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood and will not impair an adequate supply of light and air
because the proposed family room will merely replace an existing family room of
the same size in the same location; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property as it will comply with all applicable
building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not diminish the taxable value of
land and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed addition; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not contribute to congestion on the
public streets, as the structure will continue to be used as a single family
residence; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the requested variations will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village.
   WHEREAS, July 12, 1999, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of Appeals
conducted a public hearing on the requested variations and by the unanimous
vote of the six members then present, has reported to the Council recommending
that the variations be granted.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain:
   SECTION 1: That the foregoing recitals are incorporated as the findings of
the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth herein.
   SECTION 2: That a variation is hereby granted with respect to the Subject
Property, commonly known as 466 Poplar Street, which is located in the R-
4 Single-Family Residential District provided in the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance,
Chapter 22, Winnetka Village Code, for a variation from the maximum building
size (Gross Floor Area) limitations of Section 22.05A(g) of the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance, to allow a gross floor area of 3,928.87 square feet, whereas 3,645
square feet are permitted, resulting in a variation of 283.87 square feet
(7.8%), and variations from the side yard and building line articulation
requirements of Sections 22.05A(i) and 22.05A(q) of the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance, to allow a side yard of 5.70 feet, whereas 8.00 feet are required,
resulting in a variation of 2.30 feet (29%), all of said variations being
requested for the purpose of allowing the replacement of an existing family
room in its original size and location.
   SECTION 3: That the variations hereby granted are conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: That this Ordinance shall be in full force and effect from and
after its passage, approval, and posting.
   ADOPTED this 13th day of July, 1999, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES: Trustees Aquilino, Derning, Duhl, Hilton, Lien, and Powell.
   NAYS: None.
   ABSENT: None.
   APPROVED this 13th day of July, 1999.
   Signed:
   ss/Louise A. Holland
    Village President
Countersigned:
ss/Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: July 6, 1999
Posted: July 7, 1999
Passed and Approved: July 13, 1999
Posted: July 14, 1999
ORDINANCE NO. M-581-99
AN ORDINANCE providing for the issue of $1,380,000 General Obligation Limited
Bonds, Series 1999, of the village of Winnetka, Cook County, Illinois, and for
the levy of a direct annual tax to pay the principal and interest on said
bonds.
   WHEREAS, the Village of Winnetka, Cook County, Illinois (the “Village”), is
a municipality with less than 500,000 inhabitants; and
   WHEREAS, the Council of the Village (the “Council”) deems it advisable,
necessary and in the best interests of the Village to borrow the sum of
$1,380,000 for the payment of the cost of reconstruction, resurfacing and
rehabilitation of Village streets and for the payment of the expenses incident
thereto (the “Project”); and
   WHEREAS, the Council does hereby find and determine that upon the borrowing
of said sum and the issuance of bonds of the Village in the amount of
$1,380,000, all in accordance with the provisions of the Section 8-5-16 of the
Municipal Code, the aggregate outstanding bonds of the Village issued pursuant
to said Section, including the bonds herein authorized pursuant to said
Section, will not exceed one-half of one per cent of the assessed value of all
of the taxable property located within the Village, and accordingly, the
Council are authorized to issue such bonds without submitting the question of
such issuance to the electors of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, pursuant to and in accordance with the provisions of the Bond Issue
Notification Act of the State of Illinois, a public hearing (the “Hearing”) was
called for the 15th day of June, 1999, concerning the intent of the Council to
sell said bonds; and
   WHEREAS, notice of the Hearing was given by publication at least once not
less than seven (7) nor more than twenty-one (21) days before the date of the
Hearing in a newspaper of general circulation in the Village, and was not
placed in the legal notice or classified advertisement sections of said
newspaper; and
   WHEREAS, notice of the Hearing was also given by posting a copy of the
notice at least 48 hours before the Hearing at the Winnetka Village Hall, which
is the principal office of the Council and is the building at which the Hearing
was held; and
   WHEREAS, the Hearing was held on the 15th day of June, 1999, and at the
Hearing the Council explained the reasons for the proposed bond issue and
permitted persons desiring to be heard an opportunity to present written or
oral testimony within reasonable time limits; and
   WHEREAS, the Hearing was finally adjourned on the 15th day of June, 1999;
and
   WHEREAS, it is necessary and in the best interests of the Village that said
bonds for the Project be issued in the aggregate amount of $1,380,000; and
   WHEREAS, the bonds of such series shall be issued as limited bonds under the
provisions of Section 15.01 of the Local Government Debt Reform Act of the
State of Illinois, as amended (the “Debt Reform Act”), and as such it is not
necessary to submit the proposition of the issuance of the bonds to the voters
of the Village for approval:
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   Section 1.   Incorporation of Preambles. The Council hereby finds that all
of the recitals contained in the preambles to this ordinance are full, true and
correct and does incorporate them into this ordinance by this reference.
   Section 2.   Authorization. It is hereby found and determined that the
Village has been authorized by law to borrow the sum of $1,380,000 upon the
credit of the Village, and as evidence of such indebtedness to issue bonds of
the Village in said amount, for the purpose of paying the cost of the Project;
and that said bonds for Project purposes should be issued at this time in the
aggregate principal amount of $1,380,000.
   Section 3.   Bond Details. In order to raise the sum of $1,380,000 needed at
this time for the purposes aforesaid, there shall be borrowed by, for and on
behalf of the Village the sum of $1,380,000 and in evidence thereof, bonds of
the Village (the “Bonds”) shall be issued in said amount. The Bonds shall each
be designated “General Obligation Limited Bond, Series 1999”, and shall be
dated August 1, 1999, and shall also bear the date of authentication, shall be
in fully registered form, of the denomination of $5,000 and authorized integral
multiples thereof (but no single Bond shall represent installments of principal
maturing on more than one date), and shall become due and payable serially
(subject to the right of prior redemption) on December 1 of each of the years
and in the amounts and bearing interest per annum as follows:
YEAR OF  PRINICIPAL RATE OF  YEAR OF MATURITY PRINCIPAL AMOUNT RATE OF INTEREST
MATURITY AMOUNT     INTEREST
2000     $50,000    4.350%   2008             $ 95,000         4.550%
2001     70,000     4.375    2009             100,000          4.600
2002     75,000     4.375    2010             105,000          4.650
2003     75,000     4.400    2011             110,000          4.700
2004     80,000     4.400    2012             115,000          4.750
2005     85,000     4.450    2013             120,000          4.800
2006     85,000     4.450    2014             125,000          4.850
2007     90,000     4.500
 
   The Bonds shall bear interest from their date or from the most recent
interest payment date to which interest has been paid or duly provided for,
until the principal amount of the Bonds is paid, such interest (computed upon
the basis of a 360-day year of twelve 30-day months) being payable on the first
days of June and December of each year, commencing on June 1, 2000. Interest on
each Bond shall be paid by check or draft of Amalgamated Bank of Chicago,
Chicago, Illinois (the “Bond Registrar”), payable upon presentation in lawful
money of the United States of America, to the person in whose name such Bond is
registered at the close of business on the 15th day of the month next preceding
the interest payment date. The principal of the Bonds shall be payable in
lawful money of the United States of America at the Bond Registrar.
   The seal of the Village shall be affixed to or printed on each of the Bonds
and the Bonds shall be signed by the President of the Village and attested by
the Village Clerk, and in case any officer whose signature shall appear on any
Bond shall cease to be such officer before the delivery of such bond, such
signature shall nevertheless be valid and sufficient for all purposes, the same
as if such officer had remained in office until delivery.
   All Bonds shall have thereon a certificate of authentication substantially
in the form hereinafter set forth duly executed by the Bond Registrar, as
authenticating agent of the Village and showing the date of authentication. No
Bond shall be valid or obligatory for any purpose or be entitled to any
security or benefit under this ordinance unless and until such certificate of
authentication shall have been duly executed by the Bond Registrar by manual
signature, and such certificate of authentication upon any such Bond shall be
conclusive evidence that such Bond has been authenticated and delivered under
this ordinance. The certificate of authentication on any Bond shall be deemed
to have been executed by the Bond Registrar if signed by an authorized officer
of the Bond Registrar, but it shall not be necessary that the same officer sign
the certificate of authentication on all of the Bonds issued hereunder.
   Section 4.   Registration of Bonds; Persons Treated as Owners. (a) General.
The Village shall cause books for the registration and for the transfer of the
Bonds as provided in this ordinance (the “Bond Register”) to be kept at the
principal corporate trust office of the Bond Registrar, which is hereby
constituted and appointed the registrar of the Village for the Bonds. The
Village is authorized to prepare, and the Bond Registrar shall keep custody of,
multiple Bond blanks executed by the Village for use in the transfer and
exchange of Bonds.
   Upon surrender for transfer of any Bond at the principal corporate trust
office of the Bond Registrar, duly endorsed by, or accompanied by a written
instrument or instruments of transfer in form satisfactory to the Bond
Registrar and duly executed by, the registered owner of his attorney duly
authorized in writing, the Village shall execute and the Bond Registrar shall
authenticate, date and deliver in the name of the transferee or to transferees
a new fully registered Bond or Bonds of the same maturity of authorized
denominations, for a like aggregate principal amount. Any fully registered Bond
or Bonds may be exchanged at said office of the Bond Registrar for a like
aggregate principal amount of Bond or Bonds of the same maturity of other
authorized denominations. The execution by the Village of any fully registered
Bond shall constitute full and due authorization of such Bond and the Bond
Registrar shall thereby be authorized to authenticate, date and deliver such
Bond, provided, however, the principal amount of outstanding Bonds of each
maturity authenticated by the Bond Registrar shall not exceed the authorized
principal amount of Bonds for such maturity less previous retirements.
   The Bond Registrar shall not be required to transfer or exchange any Bond
during the period beginning at the close of business on the 15th day of the
month next preceding any interest payment date on such Bond and ending on such
interest payment date, nor to transfer or exchange any Bond after notice
calling such Bond for redemption has been mailed, nor during a period of
fifteen days next preceding mailing of a notice of redemption of any Bonds.
   The person in whose name any Bond shall be registered shall be deemed and
regarded as the absolute owner thereof for all purposes, and payment of the
principal of or interest on any Bond shall be made only to or upon the order of
the registered owner thereof or his legal representative. All such payments
shall be valid and effectual to satisfy and discharge the liability upon such
Bond to the extent of the sum or sums so paid.
   No service charge shall be made for any transfer or exchange of Bonds, but
the Village or the Bond Registrar may require payment of a sum sufficient to
cover any tax or other governmental charge that may be imposed in connection
with any transfer or exchange of Bonds.
   (b)   Global Book-Entry System. The Bonds shall be initially issued in the
form of a separate single fully registered Bond for each of the maturities of
the Bonds determined as described in Section 3 hereof. Upon initial issuance,
the ownership of each such Bond shall be registered in the Bond Register in the
name of Cede & Co., or any successor thereto (“Cede”), as nominee of The
Depository Trust Company, New York, New York, and its successors and assigns
(“DTC”). All of the outstanding Bonds shall be registered in the Bond Register
in the name of Cede, as nominee of DTC, except as hereinafter provided. The
President and Village Clerk of the Village and the Bond Registrar are each
authorized to execute and deliver, on behalf of the Village, such letters to or
agreements with DTC as shall be necessary to effectuate such book-entry system
(any such letter or agreement being referred to herein as the “Representation
Letter”), which Representation Letter may provide for the payment of principal
of or interest on the Bonds by wire transfer.
   With respect to Bonds registered in the Bond Register in the name of Cede,
as nominee of DTC, the Village and the Bond Registrar shall have no
responsibility or obligation to any broker-dealer, bank or other financial
institution for which DTC holds Bonds from time to time as securities
depository (each such broker-dealer, bank or other financial institution being
referred to herein as a “DTC Participant”) or to any person on behalf of whom
such a DTC Participant holds an interest in the Bonds. Without limiting the
immediately preceding sentence, the Village and the Bond Registrar shall have
no responsibility or obligation with respect to (i) the accuracy of the records
of DTC, Cede or any DTC Participant with respect to any ownership interest in
the Bonds, (ii) the delivery to any DTC Participant or any other person, other
than a registered owner of a Bond as shown in the Bond Register, of any notice
with respect to the Bonds, including any notice of redemption, or (iii) the
payment to any DTC Participant or any other person, other than a registered
owner of a Bond as shown in the Bond Register, of any amount with respect to
the principal of or interest on the Bonds. The Village and the Bond Registrar
may treat and consider the person in whose name each Bond is registered in the
Bond Register as the holder and absolute owner of such Bond for the purpose of
payment of principal and interest with respect to such Bond, for the purpose of
registering transfers with respect to such Bond, and for all other purposes
whatsoever. The Bond Registrar shall pay all principal of and interest on the
Bonds only to or upon the order of the respective registered owners of the
Bonds, as shown in the Bond Register, or their respective attorneys duly
authorized in writing, and all such payments shall be valid and effective to
fully satisfy and discharge the Village’s obligations with respect to payment
of the principal of and interest on the Bonds to the extent of the sum or sums
so paid. No person other than a registered owner of a Bond as shown in the Bond
Register, shall receive a Bond evidencing the obligation of the Village to make
payments of principal and interest with respect to any Bond. Upon delivery by
DTC to the Bond Registrar of written notice to the effect that DTC has
determined to substitute a new nominee in place of Cede, and subject to the
provisions in Section 3 hereof with respect to the payment of interest to the
registered owners of Bonds at the close of business on the 15th day of the
month next preceding the applicable interest payment date, the name “Cede” in
this Ordinance shall refer to such new nominee of DTC.
   In the event that (i) the Village determines that DTC is incapable of
discharging its responsibilities described herein and in the Representation
Letter, (ii) the agreement among the Village, the Bond Registrar and DTC
evidenced by the Representation Letter shall be terminated for any reason or
(iii) the Village determines that it is in the best interests of the beneficial
owners of the Bonds that they be able to obtain certificated Bonds, the Village
shall notify DTC and DTC Participants of the availability through DTC of
certificated Bonds and the Bonds shall no longer be restricted to being
registered in the Bond Register in the name of Cede, as nominee of DTC. At that
time, the Village may determine that the Bonds shall be registered in the name
of and deposited with such other depository operating a universal book-entry
system, as may be acceptable to the Village, or such depository’s agent or
designee, and if the Village does not select such alternate universal book-
entry system, then the Bonds may be registered in whatever name or names
registered owners of Bonds transferring or exchanging Bonds shall designate, in
accordance with the provisions of Section 4(a) hereof.
   Notwithstanding any other provisions of this Ordinance to the contrary, so
long as any Bond is registered in the name of Cede, as nominee of DTC, all
payments with respect to principal of and interest on such Bond and all notices
with respect to such Bond shall be made and given, respectively, in the name
provided in the Representation Letter.
   Section 5.   Redemption. Bonds maturing on and after December 1, 2008, shall
be subject to redemption prior to maturity at the option of the Village as a
whole, or in part in integral multiples of $5,000 in any order of maturity as
shall be determined by the Village (less than all of the Bonds of a single
maturity to be selected by the Bond Registrar), on December 1, 2007, and on any
date thereafter, at the price of par plus accrued interest to the redemption
date.
   The Bonds shall be redeemed only in the principal amount of $5,000 and
integral multiples thereof. The Village shall, at least forty-five (45) days
prior to any redemption date (unless a shorter time period shall be
satisfactory to the Bond Registrar) notify the Bond Registrar of such
redemption date, the principal amount of Bonds to be redeemed and the
maturities from which such Bonds are to be selected. For purposes of any
redemption of less than all of the outstanding Bonds of a single maturity, the
particular Bonds or portions of Bonds to be redeemed shall be selected not more
than sixty (60) days prior to the redemption date by the Bond Registrar, from
the outstanding Bonds of such maturity by such method as the Bond Registrar
shall deem fair and appropriate and which may provide for the selection for
redemption of Bonds or portions of Bonds in principal amounts of $5,000 and
integral multiples thereof.
   The Bond Registrar shall promptly notify the Village in writing of the Bonds
or portions of Bonds selected for redemption and, in the case of any Bond
selected for partial redemption, the principal amount thereof to be redeemed.
   Section 6.   Redemption Procedure. Unless waived by any owner of Bonds to be
redeemed, notice of the call for any such redemption shall be given by the Bond
Registrar on behalf of the Village by mailing the redemption notice by
registered or certified mail at least thirty (30) days and not more than sixty
(60) days prior to the date fixed for redemption to the registered owner of the
Bond or Bonds (or portion thereof) to be redeemed at the address shown on the
Bond Register or at such other address as is furnished in writing by such
registered owner to the Bond Registrar.
   All notices of redemption shall state:
      (1)   the redemption date,
      (2)   the redemption price,
      (3)   if less than all outstanding Bonds are to be redeemed, the
identification (and, in the case of partial redemption, the respective
principal amounts) of the Bonds to be redeemed,
      (4)   that on the redemption date the redemption price will become due
and payable upon each such Bond or portion thereof called for redemption, and
that interest thereon shall cease to accrue from and after said date, and
      (5)   the place where such Bonds are to be surrendered for payment of the
redemption price, which place of payment shall be the office of the Bond
Registrar.
   On or prior to any redemption date, the Village shall deposit with the Bond
Registrar an amount of money sufficient to pay the redemption price of all the
Bonds or portions of Bonds which are to be redeemed on that date.
   Notice of redemption having been given as aforesaid, the Bonds or portions
of Bonds so to be redeemed shall, on the redemption date, become due and
payable at the redemption price therein specified, and from and after such date
(unless the Village shall default in the payment of the redemption price) such
Bonds or portions of Bonds (or portion thereof) shall cease to bear interest.
Upon surrender of such Bonds for redemption in accordance with said notice,
such Bonds shall be paid by the Bond Registrar at the redemption price.
Installments of interest due on or prior to the redemption date shall be
payable as herein provided for payment of interest. Upon surrender for any
partial redemption of any Bond, there shall be prepared for the registered
owner a new Bond or Bonds of the same maturity in the amount of the unpaid
principal.
   Failure to give such notice by mail to any registered owner of the Bonds (or
portion thereof) or any defect therein shall not affect the validity of any
proceedings for the redemption of Bonds (or portions hereof).
   If any Bond or portion of a Bond called for redemption shall not be so paid
upon surrender thereof for redemption, the principal shall, until paid, bear
interest from the redemption date at the rate borne by the Bond or portion of
Bond so called for redemption. All Bonds which have been redeemed shall be
cancelled and destroyed by the Bond Registrar and shall not be reissued.
   Section 7.   Form of Bond. The Bonds shall be in substantially the following
form; provided, however, that if the test of the Bond is to be printed in its
entirety on the front side of the Bond, then paragraph [2] and the legend, “See
Reverse Side for Additional Provisions”, shall be omitted and paragraphs [6]
through [12] shall be inserted immediately after paragraph [1]”
