ORDINANCE NO. M-582-99
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (775 Lincoln Avenue)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
   Lots 13, 14 and 15 in Block 8 in Winnetka Park Bluffs, being a subdivision
by William H. Cairnduff of parts of Sections 16, 17 and 20, Township 42 North,
Range 13 East of the Third Meridian, in Cook County, Illinois,
commonly known as 775 Lincoln Avenue, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
4 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance, Chapter 22, Winnetka Village Code, for a variation from the setback
requirements of Section 22.05A(h) to permit a corner setback of 19.0 feet from
the Summit Street property line, whereas 30.0 feet are required, resulting in a
variation of 11.0 feet (36.66%), for the purpose of replacing a one-story
sunroom with a two-story brick addition that will contain a first floor den and
a second floor private master bathroom; and
   WHEREAS, on June 14, 1999, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variation and by the
favorable vote of five of the six members then present, has reported to the
Council recommending that the requested variation be granted; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties associated with carrying out the
strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect to the Subject Property
in that the single-family residence was constructed in 1924, is located nearer
the two street frontages of a square-shaped corner lot, and is currently in a
nonconforming location, with the existing sunroom observing a nonconforming
setback of 23.50 feet along Summit Street, which nonconformity cannot be cured
without either relocating the house or removing the first floor den entirely;
and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that
the home does not have either modern accommodations for a private master bath
or a functional den, and the size and amenities of the house would be reduced
if the front setback were strictly enforced; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because it will be in keeping with the architectural style of
the house and because the three other homes located at the corners of the same
intersection have similar setbacks; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not impair an adequate supply of light
and air because there are no structures proximate to the proposed addition, the
second floor of the addition will be set back from the first floor’s outer wall
and the roof of the addition is lower than the roof over the main section of
the house; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property as it will comply with all applicable
building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings throughout the Village, because the proposed addition is
generally an improvement to the property; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed addition will not contribute to congestion on the
public streets, as the home will continue to be used as a single-family
residence; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed addition will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2:  That a variation from the setback requirements of Section 22.05A
(h) of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Chapter 22, of the Winnetka Village Code,
be granted to permit a corner setback of 19.0 feet from the Summit Street
property line, whereas 30.0 feet are required, resulting in a variation of 11.0
feet (36.66%), for the purpose of replacing a one-story sunroom with a two-
story brick addition, that will contain a first floor den and a second floor
private master bathroom, in accordance with the drawings and specifications
submitted with the May 15, 1999, application for variation.
   SECTION 3: That the variation hereby granted is conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 17th day of August, 1999, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES: Trustees Aquilino, Derning, Hilton, Lien, and Powell.
   NAYS: None.
   ABSENT: President Holland.
   APPROVED this 17th day of August, 1999.
   Signed:
   ss/Michael F. Duhl
    Village President Pro Tem
Countersigned:
ss/Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: August 3, 1999
Posted: August 4, 1999
Passed and Approved: August 17, 1999
Posted: August 18, 1999
ORDINANCE NO. M-583-99
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A SPECIAL USE PERMIT TO ALLOW THE RENOVATION OF NICK
CORWIN PARK
   WHEREAS, the Winnetka Park District is the owner of a parcel of real estate
(the “Subject Property”) that is located at 1550 Edgewood Avenue, is commonly
known as the Nick Corwin Park, and is legally described as follows:
Parcel 1:
Except the east 33 feet thereof, the North Half of the East Half of that part
of the South East Quarter of the North West Quarter of Section 18, Township 42
North, Range 13, East of the Third Principal Meridian, described as follows:
Beginning on the East line of the said North West Quarter at the point of its
intersection with the center line of Prairie Avenue (now Edgewood Lane)
extended West; thence due West to the West line of the South East ¼ of said
North West ¼; thence South to the South West corner of the said South East ¼ of
the said North West ¼; thence East along the South line of the said North West
¼ to the South East corner of said South East ¼ of said North West ¼; thence
North along the East line of the said North West ¼ to the point of beginning.
Parcel 2:
That part of the South East ¼ of the North West ¼ of Section 18, Township 42
North, Range 13 East of the Third Principal Meridian bounded and described as
follows:
Beginning at the point of intersection of the center line of Prairie Avenue
(now Edgewood Lane) extended from the East, with the West line of the East ½ of
the South East ¼ of the North west ¼ of said Section being the center line of
Bluff Street as extended; thence North along said center line of Bluff Street,
as extended, 192.95 feet, more or less, to a point equidistant between the
center line of Prairie Avenue extended, and the center line of Asbury Avenue
extended; thence East, on a line equidistant between the center lines of
Prairie Avenue and Asbury Avenue extended, to the east line of the South East ¼
of the North West ¼; thence South to the North line of Prairie Avenue; thence
West along the North line of Prairie Avenue, 33 feet; thence South to the point
of intersection of the center line of Prairie Avenue extended from the East,
619.62 feet, more or less, to the point of beginning (except the East 33 feet
of that part thereof lying north of the north line of Prairie Avenue (now
Edgewood Lane), extended from the East, and lying South of a line equidistant
between the center line of said Prairie Avenue and center line of Asbury
Avenue, extended from the East, as dedicated by plat recorded October 21, 1953,
as document number 15749740) in Cook County, Illinois.
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property is located in the R-2 Single-Family
Residential Zoning District of the Village of Winnetka, as provided in the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance. Chapter 22 of the Winnetka Village Code; and
   WHEREAS, Nick Corwin Park is a public park consisting of soccer fields, a
playground area and storage shed, and is therefore a public use, permitted as a
special use in all zoning districts, subject to the conditions and requirements
pertaining to special uses, as set forth in Section 22.13 of the Winnetka
Zoning Ordinance; and
   WHEREAS, the Winnetka Park District has submitted an application to the
Village of Winnetka seeking a special use permit pursuant to said Section
22.13, to permit the renovation of the existing playground at Nick Corwin Park
with new playground equipment, play surface, pathways, benches, landscaping,
curbing and drainage; and
   WHEREAS, upon due notice on July 12, 1999, the Zoning Board of Appeals held
a public hearing on the proposed special use and, by the unanimous vote of the
six members then present, has reported to the Council recommending that the
requested special use be granted, subject to the condition that the proposed
renovation does not have a negative impact on either storm drainage or the two
willow trees located within the playground area; and
   WHEREAS, upon due notice, the Design Review Board met on July 15, 1999, to
consider the redesign of the playground and by the unanimous vote of the five
members then present, has submitted a comment that a Certificate of
Appropriateness would issue for the design presented; and
   WHEREAS, upon due notice, the Plan Commission met on August 25, 1999, to
consider the application of the Park District, and by the unanimous vote of the
eight members then present, found that the proposed special use is consistent
with the Winnetka Park District policy listed in the 1979 Statement of
Community Objectives in the Winnetka Comprehensive Plan and with the Winnetka
Parks and Open Space System policies and recommendations of Chapter XI of the
Winnetka Comprehensive Plan, and recommended that the special use be granted;
and
   WHEREAS, the proposed replacement of the playground equipment at Nick Corwin
Park will benefit the public health, safety, comfort, morals and general
welfare of the Village, by improving the safety, accessibility and appearance
of the playground through the installation of new playground equipment, benches
and a brick paver pathway and by improving the storm drainage on the Subject
Property, all in accordance with applicable requirements of the Americans with
Disabilities Act, playground safety standards and the Village Code; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed special use will not be substantially injurious to the
use and enjoyment of other property in the immediate vicinity, nor will it
substantially diminish or impair property values in the immediate vicinity, as
the use is established in the neighborhood, the existing playground is on the
interior portion of the Subject Property, the renovation will not increase the
intensity of the existing use, and the renovation will improve both the
functionality and appearance of the park; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed special use will not impede the normal and orderly
development or improvement of other property in the immediate vicinity for uses
permitted under applicable zoning regulations, as the facility is a long
established use in the neighborhood and the type of use of the Subject Property
will not be changed; and
   WHEREAS, adequate measures have been taken to provide ingress and egress in
a manner which minimizes pedestrian and vehicular traffic congestion, in that
the proposed changes to the playground will not change the pedestrian
circulation routes through the park and will not change any surrounding
vehicular traffic patterns; and
   WHEREAS, adequate parking and access roads serve the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, adequate utilities, drainage and other facilities necessary to the
operation of the special use exists or will be provided, in that there is
presently water service to the existing water fountains and a new storm drain
will connect directly to the Village’s storm sewer system and provide drainage
for the playground area; and
   WHEREAS, the special use in all other respects conforms to the applicable
regulations of this and other Village ordinances and codes;
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain:
   SECTION 1:   That the Village Council hereby adopts the foregoing recitals
as their findings, as if fully set forth herein.
   SECTION 2: That pursuant to Section 22.13 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance,
Chapter 22 of the Winnetka Village Code, a special use permit is hereby granted
with respect to the Subject Property, which is located in the R-2 Single-Family
Residential District provided in said Zoning Ordinance and which is commonly
known as Nick Corwin Park, to permit the renovation of the Subject Property by
installing new playground equipment, play surface, pathways, benches,
landscaping, curbing and drainage in the playground located on the Subject
Property, said work to be done in accordance with the application for special
use permit and the plans dated June 7, 1999, which accompanied the application.
   SECTION 3: That the special use permit hereby granted is subject to the
following conditions: (1) that the proposed construction commence within 12
months after the effective date of this Ordinance; (2) that the storm drain be
installed by hand to protect the willow trees in the playground area; and (3)
that the two existing willow trees be pruned and be protected during
construction; and
   SECTION 4: That, pursuant to Section 22.13(g) of the Zoning Ordinance, all
stipulations, conditions and restrictions set forth in this Ordinance as part
of the terms under which the special use is granted, may be modified or revised
from time to time by the Village Council following public notice and hearing,
using the same procedures set forth in the Zoning Ordinance for processing the
original special use application.
   SECTION 5:   That this Ordinance shall be in full force and effect from and
after its passage, approval, and posting.
   ADOPTED this 7th day of September, 1999, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES: Trustees Aquilino, Derning, Duhl, Hilton, Lien and Powell
   NAYS: None
   ABSENT: None
   APPROVED this 7th day of September, 1999.
   Signed:
