ORDINANCE NO. M-590-99
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (333 Rosewood Avenue)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owners of the following described
real estate (the “Subject Property”):
   Lot 24 in Alles’ Sunset Subdivision of the Northeast Quarter of the
Southwest Quarter of Section 20, Township 42 North, Range 13, East of the Third
Principal Meridian, in Cook County, Illinois;
commonly known as 333 Rosewood Avenue, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the
R-4 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance, Chapter 22 of the Winnetka Village Code, for variations by ordinance
from Sections 22.05A(i) and 22.05A(q) to allow the replacement of a legally
non-conforming attached garage located on the north side of the residence with
a one-story garage, laundry and home office addition, which is proposed to
observe a side yard of 6 feet and a total side yard of 11.98 feet, which would
result in (a) a variation of 2 feet (25%) from the 8-foot side yard required by
Section 22.05A(i); (b) a variation of .32 feet from the total side yard of
12.30 feet required by Section 22.05A(i); and (c) a variation from the
requirement of Section 22.05A(q) that the building line be articulated by 2
feet toward the center of the lot beginning no farther than 30 feet from the
front building line on the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, on July 12, August 3, and September 13, 1999, on due notice
thereof, the Zoning Board of Appeals conducted a public hearing on the
requested variations; and
   WHEREAS, on September 1, 1999, the owners amended their application by
providing a 2-foot articulation of the north building line toward the center of
the property, beginning at a point 30 feet from the front building line, so
that the rear 27 feet, 10 ½ inches of the proposed addition is in a conforming
location; and
   WHEREAS, upon the completion of the hearing on September 13, 1999, on due
notice thereof, the Zoning Board of Appeals, by the favorable vote of the four
members then present, voted to recommend that the Village Council grant the
requested variations; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and unique hardships associated
with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect
to the Subject Property in that the single-family home was constructed on the
Subject Property in 1938, with a 6-foot north side yard and a 6.08 south side
yard and was improved in 1979 with a two-story addition, which was also in a
conforming location; and
   WHEREAS, due to amendments of the Zoning Ordinance since 1938, the south
side yard is measured at 4.89 feet due to the presence of a 7.43-foot long
chimney; and
   WHEREAS, as a result of the enactment of ordinance MC-221-99, the Subject
Property has both an additional nonconformity in the south side yard and a new
nonconformity in the north side yard, because the south side yard, which is the
smaller of the two side yards, does not meet the articulation requirement
established by ordinance MC-221-99, and the north side yard does not meet the
8-foot side yard requirement established by the same ordinance; and
   WHEREAS, although the existing side yards are approximately equal in size,
the Council of the Village of Winnetka find that it is more consistent with the
provisions of the Zoning Ordinance to (a) treat the south side yard as being
the smaller side yard, (b) impose the 8-foot side yard requirement along the
north side, (c) acknowledge the existence of legal nonconformities from the 6-
foot side yard and side yard articulation requirements in the south side, and
(d) treat the application solely as a request for side yard and total side yard
variation due to the 6-foot north side yard proposed for the new addition; and
   WHEREAS, due to the location of the residence, an attached garage cannot be
constructed in a conforming location on either the north or south side of the
residence, and construction of a garage in a conforming location at the rear of
the Subject Property would require the proposed building addition to be placed
closer to the center of the lot and farther behind the house, as a result of
which both the garage and room addition would substantially reduce the open
space in the rear yard, although no other rear yards in the immediate vicinity
have similar structures; and
   WHEREAS, the amended plans are in harmony with the general purpose and
intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance in that the proposed addition, as
revised, places the garage in a location that is consistent with all other
properties in the immediate vicinity, is consistent with the scale of the
existing neighborhood, provides for the renovation of a structurally sound
existing house, and maintains the existing scale and appearance of the
neighborhood; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, because
the proposed addition will allow the reconfiguration and upgrading of the
kitchen, add a first floor laundry area, add a first floor office that can be
converted into a bedroom, and will replace the existing one-car garage with
another one-car garage that has additional storage space, all of which are
typical amenities in single-family residences in the area, and because
constructing the garage in a conforming location will reduce the value of the
Subject Property by reducing both the amount of open space and the
functionality of the Subject Property and the residence; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because the revised design of the proposed addition is
consistent with the design and scale of both the existing home and the other
single-family properties in the immediate vicinity, all of which are
characterized by residences oriented near the front end of the lot, with
attached garages and open rear yards; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not impair an adequate supply of light
and air because the proposed addition is a single story and does not encroach
any closer to the adjoining properties than does the existing garage; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property as it will comply with all applicable
building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not contribute to congestion on the
public streets, as the Subject Property will continue to be used as the site of
a single-family residence, and the use of the home office that is part of the
proposed addition will generally serve one person per day; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed one-story garage, laundry
and home office addition will otherwise impair the public health, safety,
comfort, morals, and welfare of the inhabitants of the Village.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1: That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2: That variations are hereby granted to the Subject Property,
commonly known as 333 Rosewood Avenue, Village of Winnetka, and located in the
R-4 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance, Chapter 22 of the Winnetka Village Code, from the side yard and
total side yard requirements of Section 22.05A(i) of the Zoning Ordinance, in
order to allow the construction of a one-story garage, laundry and home office
addition to observe a 6-foot side yard, whereas 8 feet are required, resulting
in a variation of 2 feet (25%), and to allow the total of the two side yards to
equal 11.98 feet, whereas a total of 12.30 feet are required, resulting in a
variation of .32 feet (3.3%), in accordance with the plans and specifications
submitted with the application, as amended on September 1, 1999.
   SECTION 3: That the variations hereby granted are conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 19th day of October, 1999, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES: Trustees Aquilino, Derning, Hilton, Lien and Powell
   NAYS: None
   ABSENT: Trustee Duhl
   APPROVED this 19th day of October, 1999.
   Signed:
   /ss/Louise A. Holland
   Village President
Countersigned:
/ss/Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: October 5, 1999
Posted: October 6, 1999
Passed and Approved: October 19, 1999
Posted: October 20, 1999
ORDINANCE NO. M-591-99
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (680 Locust Street)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
   Lot 12 in Block 2 in Winnetka Heights, being a Subdivision of that part of
the South ½ of the Southwest ¼ of Section 17, Township 42 North, Range 13, East
of the Third Principal Meridian, Lying East of and adjoining the East Line and
the East Line Extension of Rosewood Avenue (Formerly Oak Ridge Avenue) in
Village of Winnetka, in Cook County, Illinois;
commonly known as 680 Locust Street, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
3 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance, Chapter 22 of the Winnetka Village Code, for a variation by
ordinance from the maximum building size limitations of Section 22.06(g) of the
Zoning Ordinance, to allow a total gross floor area of 5,170.43 square feet,
whereas 4,677.30 square feet are permitted, resulting in a variation of 493.13
square feet (10.54%), for the purpose of replacing an existing concrete stoop
having an area of 31.39 square feet, with a roofed porch having an area of
48.75 square feet; and
   WHEREAS, on September 13, 1999, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variation and by the
favorable vote of the four members then present, has reported to the Village
Council, recommending that the requested variation be granted; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and unique hardships associated
with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect
to the Subject Property in that the single-family residence on the Subject
Property was constructed in 1923 with its front entrance located adjacent to an
interior corner, as a result of which any roofed protection over the entrance
must now be included in the gross floor area of building; and
   WHEREAS, there is no conforming alternative, because there is no place in
the residence to which the front entrance could be moved so as to allow the
construction of a roofed, 3-sided porch, which could be excluded from gross
floor area; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that a
front entrance that allows guests, family and deliveries to be protected from
the weather while awaiting entry is a prevailing element of housing in the
Village; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because the proposed covered entrance adds only 48.75 square
feet to the gross floor area of the existing building, and the design and scale
of the proposed porch and entrance are consistent with the design and scale of
the existing building and scale of the neighborhood; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not impair an adequate supply of light
and air because the Subject Property is a corner lot, the proposed porch is
located on an interior corner of the building and the two exterior sides of the
proposed covered porch face the abutting streets, rather than any other parcels
of single-family property; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property, as the proposed porch will comply with
all applicable building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed covered porch will not contribute to congestion on the
public streets, as the intensity of the existing use of the Subject Property
will not be changed; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed covered porch is in harmony with the general purpose
and intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that the proposal complies with
the district purpose of modifying an existing structure while maintaining the
existing scale and character of the neighborhood; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed covered porch will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1: That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2: A variation is granted to the Subject Property, commonly known as
680 Locust Street, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-3 Single-Family
Zoning District provided in the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Chapter 22 of the
Winnetka Village Code, for a variation from the maximum building size
limitations of Section 22.06(g) of the Zoning Ordinance, to allow a total gross
floor area of 5,070.43 square feet, whereas 4,677.30 square feet are permitted,
resulting in a variation of 493.13 square feet (10.54%), for the purpose of
replacing an existing concrete stoop having an area of 31.39 square feet, with
a roofed porch having an area of 48.75 square feet, in accordance with the
plans and specifications submitted with the application for variation.
   SECTION 3: That the variation hereby granted is conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 19th day of October, 1999, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES: Trustees Derning, Hilton, Aquilino, Powell and Lien
   NAYS: None
   ABSENT: Trustee Duhl
   APPROVED this 19thday of October, 1999.
   Signed:
   /ss/Louise A. Holland
   Village President
Countersigned:
/ss/Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
