ORDINANCE NO. M-592-99
AN ORDINANCE ADOPTING AMENDMENTS TO THE COMPREHENSIVE PLAN OF THE VILLAGE OF
WINNETKA
   WHEREAS, on April 16, 1945, the Village of Winnetka, Cook County, Illinois,
adopted, by ordinance, an Official Comprehensive Plan (hereinafter the
“Official Plan”), which replaced the Village of Winnetka’s first Official
Comprehensive Plan, which was adopted in 1921; and
   WHEREAS, the Official Plan has been amended from time to time, with the last
amendments having been adopted in 1993; and
   WHEREAS, at the direction of the Winnetka Village Council, the Plan
Commission began a process in 1997 to prepare and recommend a new comprehensive
plan; and
   WHEREAS, on August 25, 1999, the Plan Commission submitted its draft
comprehensive plan, entitled “Winnetka 2020 – A Comprehensive Plan for the
Village of Winnetka,” and unanimously recommended its adoption by the Village
Council; and
   WHEREAS, upon the submission of the Plan Commission’s recommended plan and
pursuant to notice duly published in the manner provided by law, the Village
Council conducted public hearings on September 14, 1999, October 12, 1999,
October 21, 1999, November 2, 1999 and November 16, 1999; and
   WHEREAS, at the public hearings, all persons desiring to be heard in support
or opposition to the proposed amendments were afforded the opportunity to
submit their statements, orally, in writing, or both; and
   WHEREAS, the Village Council, after consideration of the recommendation of
the Plan Commission and information derived in the course of the public
hearings, finds it in the best interests of the Village of Winnetka and its
residents that the proposed comprehensive plan, “Winnetka 2020 – A
Comprehensive Plan for the Village of Winnetka” be adopted.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2: That the plan entitled “Winnetka 2020 – A Comprehensive Plan for
the Village of Winnetka,” a copy of which is attached hereto as Exhibit A, is
hereby adopted as the comprehensive plan for the Village of Winnetka, Illinois.
   SECTION 3 : That the comprehensive plan hereby adopted, “Winnetka 2020 – A
Comprehensive Plan for the Village of Winnetka,” (hereinafter, the
“Comprehensive Plan”) shall supercede the previous Official Plan of the Village
of Winnetka, and all amendments thereto, except that the Official Map shall not
be changed.
   SECTION 4 : That the Comprehensive Plan and the Official Map of the Village
of Winnetka shall be placed on file in the office of the Village Clerk and
shall be available for public inspection and for copying, with the cost of
copies of the Comprehensive Plan and/or Official Map to be set by resolution of
the Village Council.
   SECTION 5 : That the Village Clerk is hereby directed to take all steps
necessary to file a notice of adoption of the Comprehensive Plan with the Cook
County Recorder of Deeds, in the manner provided by statute, and that the
Comprehensive Plan adopted pursuant to this Ordinance shall become effective
upon the expiration of ten days after the filing of said notice of adoption.
   SECTION 3: That this Ordinance shall become effective upon its passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 16th day of November 1999, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES: Trustees Derning, Duhl, Hilton, Lien, Powell and Aquilino
   NAYS: None
   ABSENT: None
   APPROVED this 16th day of November, 1999.
   Signed:
   /ss/Louise A. Holland
   Village President
Countersigned:
/ss/Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: November 2, 1999
Posted: November 3, 1999
Passed and Approved: November 16, 1999
Posted: November 17, 1999
ORDINANCE NO. M-593-99
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING AN AMENDMENT TO SPECIAL USE PERMIT TO ALLOW THE EXPANSION
OF OFFICE SPACE AT 552-554 LINCOLN AVENUE
   WHEREAS, on July 8, 1999, the owner of the following described real estate
(the “Subject Property”):
   Lot 7 (except the Westerly 40.0 feet thereof), in Lewis D. Webster’s Re-
subdivision, being a re-subdivision of Lots 4, 5 and 8 in Block 9, Block 11
(except the North 75 feet thereof) and Lots 6, 8, 9 and 10 in Block 12 in Park
Addition to Winnetka, in Section 17, Township 42 North, Range 13, East of the
Third Principal Meridian, in the Village of Winnetka, Cook County, Illinois,
commonly known as 552-554 Lincoln Avenue, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in
the C-2 Commercial Overlay Zoning District provided in the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance, Chapter 22, Winnetka Village Code, filed an application for an
amendment to a special use permit for the purpose of expanding existing office
space on the ground floor by moving the current recessed building entrance to
the front property line on the Subject Property and by moving a portion of the
north (interior) wall of the office space to the center line of an interior
corridor; and
   WHEREAS, Sections 22.11(b)(1)bp and 22.11(b)2a of the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance, Chapter 22 of the Winnetka Village Code, permit office space as a
special use in the C-2 Overlay District, subject to the conditions and
requirements set forth therein and further subject to the conditions and
requirements pertaining to special uses set forth in Section 22.13 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance; and
   WHEREAS, the ground floor of the Subject Property has a central corridor,
has office space running the full length of the ground floor south of the
corridor and, on the north side of the corridor, has a retail sales business at
the front of the building and office space toward the rear; and
   WHEREAS, the office space on the ground floor of the Subject Property exists
pursuant to special use permits issued in 1991 and 1993; and
   WHEREAS, the existing entrance to all of the businesses on the ground floor
is through a recessed entryway to the central corridor; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed reconfiguration of the front entrance by moving it to
the front property line is permitted as of right for the north side of the
building because of the presence of the retail space, but cannot be
accomplished on the south side of the corridor without an amendment to the
special use permit, due to the presence of the office space; and
   WHEREAS, on August 19, 1999, on due notice thereof, the Design Review Board
reviewed plans for the proposed renovation and issued favorable comment,
stating that it would issue a Certificate of Appropriateness for the design as
presented; and
   WHEREAS, on September 22, 1999, on due notice thereof, the Plan Commission,
considered the application as originally filed, including the expansion of the
office space on the south side of the corridor, and, by the unanimous vote of
the nine members then present, recommended that the Village Council deny the
Special Use Permit to allow the expansion of the office area, finding that the
proposal was not consistent with the Comprehensive Plan; and
   WHEREAS, after receiving the negative recommendation from the Plan
Commission, the owners modified the request for special use by withdrawing its
request to move the interior wall of the office space; and
   WHEREAS, on October 11, 1999, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the revised plan, which would extend the
existing storefront only, and, by the unanimous vote of the six members then
present, has reported to the Council recommending that the revised request to
amend the special use permit be granted; and
   WHEREAS, the requested special use will benefit the public health, safety,
comfort, morals and general welfare of the inhabitants of the Village of
Winnetka, by improving the appearance of the Subject Property and providing
more visibility to the current retail use, thereby increasing the likelihood
that the Subject Property will provide additional sales tax revenues to the
Village; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed special use, as modified by the owners, will not be
substantially injurious to the use and enjoyment of other property in the
immediate vicinity, nor will it substantially diminish or impair property
values in the immediate vicinity, since the proposed amended special use is an
improvement to the Subject Property that does not change the essential
character of either the Subject Property or the uses located thereon, which are
well-established at that location; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed special use will not impede the normal and orderly
development or improvement of other property in the immediate vicinity, as the
special use currently exists and the minimal change proposed in the front
building line will make it consistent with the front building line of other
properties in the immediate vicinity; and
   WHEREAS, the modification of the front building line does not require
additional measures for ingress and egress to minimize pedestrian and vehicular
traffic congestion in the public way; and
   WHEREAS, the modification of the front of the building does not require
additional parking facilities, and all other utilities, access roads, drainage
and other facilities required for the operation of the special use are
currently provided to the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, altering the front building line as proposed while maintaining the
full width of the interior corridor, which is commonly known as the “Winnetka
Walk,” will help to encourage, facilitate and enhance the continuity,
concentration and pedestrian nature of commercial shopping in the area; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed special use will not increase the amount of street
frontage for the special use, but will increase the visibility for the retail
use on the Subject Property, and thus provides for a minimum interruption in
the existing and potential continuity and concentration of retail uses of a
comparison shopping nature; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed modification of the entire front building line will
provide for display windows, facades, signage and lighting similar in nature
and compatible with that provided by the existing uses in the area; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed building modification meets the requirements of
Section 22.11(b)2a4 of the Zoning Ordinance, in that the retail portions of the
building will be located adjacent to the sidewalk, will exceed the 20-foot
minimum frontage requirement, will exceed the 400 square foot gross floor area
minimum requirement and will continue to be devoted to active retail
merchandising that maintains typical and customary hours of operation; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed amendment to the special use does not alter the nature
of the existing uses and thus does not significantly diminish the availability
of parking for persons who wish to patronize existing retail businesses in the
immediate vicinity; and
   WHEREAS, the special use, when constructed in accordance with this
Ordinance, will in all other respects conform to the applicable regulations of
the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance and other Village ordinances and codes.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2: That a special use permit under Section 22.11(b)2a and Section
22.13 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Chapter 22, Winnetka Village Code, be
granted with respect to the Subject Property, which is commonly known as 552-
554 Lincoln Avenue and is located in the "C-2" (General Retail) Commercial
Overlay Zoning District, to allow the building storefront on the Subject
Property to be modified by moving it to the front lot line without moving the
interior walls of the office space currently located on the first floor of the
Subject Property, all in accordance with the plans submitted with the
application and, as modified prior to consideration by the Zoning Board of
Appeals.
   SECTION 3: That the special use permit hereby granted is conditioned upon
the commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the
effective date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 7th day of December, 1999, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES: Trustees Aquilino, Derning, Hilton, Lien and Powell
   NAYS: None
   ABSENT: Trustee Duhl
   APPROVED this 7th day of December, 1999.
   Signed:
   /ss/Louise A. Holland
   Village President
Countersigned:
/ss/Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: November 16, 1999
Posted: November 17, 1999
Passed and Approved: December 7, 1999
Posted: December 8, 1999
ORDINANCE NO. M-594-99
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (132 Fuller Lane)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
   Lot 22 in Orth’s Subdivision of part of the Southeast Fractional ¼ of
Fractional Section 21, Township 42 North, Range 13, East of the Third Principal
Meridian, According to the Plat thereof, Recorded September 24, 1921 As
Document 7276951, in Cook County, Illinois,
commonly known as 132 Fuller Lane, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
5 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance, Chapter 22, Winnetka Village Code, for a variation from the roofed
lot coverage limitations of Section 22.05(f) to allow a total roofed lot
coverage of 2,063.68 square feet, whereas 1,950.47 square feet is permitted
resulting in a variation of 113.21 square feet (5.80%), and a variation from
the maximum building size limitations of Section 22.05(g) to allow a total
gross floor area of 3,169.02 square feet, whereas 3,120.75 square feet is
permitted, resulting in a variation of 48.27 square feet (1.55%), all for the
purpose of constructing a two-story addition and a two-car garage on the
Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, on October 11, 1999, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variation and by the
unanimous vote of the six members then present, has reported to the Council
recommending that the requested variation be granted; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations are in harmony with the general purpose
and intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance as stated in Section 22.02, and is
consistent with the district purpose as stated in Section 22.05(a), of assuring
that the modification or replacement of existing structures will not alter the
scale and general character of established neighborhoods; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and unique hardships associated
with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect
to the Subject Property in that the Subject Property has a legally
nonconforming lot size and has an unusually shallow lot depth of approximately
104 feet, both of which are offset by a lot width of nearly 75 feet and a rear
lot line that adjoins the New Trier playing fields, and the only conforming
alternatives for increasing the amount of living space and off-street parking
to contemporary market standards would require either constructing a garage in
the basement of an addition or providing off-street parking in the front yard,
which would be out of character with the existing neighborhood; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that
the home on the Subject Property was built in 1924, the addition and garage
which the owners propose to replace have structural problems and inadequate
heating and electrical systems, and the proposed addition and two-car garage
are necessary to improve the condition of the existing home, to accommodate an
interior layout that will be consistent with current market standards, and to
provide a two-car garage, which is the general standard for off-street parking
in the community; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood, because the proposed garage will be in the rear yard and the
proposed addition will be at the rear of the home, thereby maintaining the
existing scale and presence of the home as viewed from the street; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not impair an adequate supply of
light and air because the addition will maintain the existing sideyards, which
substantially exceed the required minimum, and the space behind the Subject
Property is the open, unobstructed New Trier High School athletic field; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property as they will comply with all applicable
building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed two-story addition will not contribute to congestion
on the public streets, as the structure will continue to be used as a single-
family residence and at least one additional off-street parking space will be
provided by the construction of the proposed two-car garage; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed addition will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2: That variations are hereby granted to the Subject Property,
commonly known as 132 Fuller Lane, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
5 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance, Chapter 22, Winnetka Village Code, for a variation from the roofed
lot coverage limitations of Section 22.05(f) to allow a total roofed lot
coverage of 2,063.68 square feet, whereas 1,950.47 square feet is permitted
resulting in a variation of 113.21 square feet (5.80%), and a variation from
the maximum building size limitations of Section 22.05(g) to allow a total
gross floor area of 3,169.02 square feet, whereas 3,120.75 square feet is
permitted, resulting in a variation of 48.27 square feet (1.55%), all for the
purpose of constructing a two-story addition and a two-car garage on the
Subject Property, in accordance with the plans and specifications submitted
with the application for variation.
   SECTION 3: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 7th day of December, 1999, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES: Trustees Aquilino, Derning, Hilton, Lien and Powell
   NAYS: None
   ABSENT: Trustee Duhl
   APPROVED this 7th day of December, 1999.
   Signed:
   /ss/Louise A. Holland
   Village President
Countersigned:
/ss/Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: November 16, 1999
Posted: November 17, 1999
Passed and Approved: December 7, 1999
