ORDINANCE NO: M-595-99 (zoning application withdrawn)
ORDINANCE NO. M-596-99
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A TEMPORARY SPECIAL USE PERMIT FOR THE PLACEMENT OF TWO
MODULAR OFFICE UNITS AT THE WINNETKA PARK DISTRICT (530 Hibbard Road)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the Winnetka Park District, which is the
owner of the following described real estate (the “Subject Property”):
Winnetka Manor Addition Subdivision in the North ½ of the East ½ Section of
Section 19 Township 42 North, Range 13, East of the Third Principle Meridian in
the Village of Winnetka, Cook County, Illinois.
commonly known as 530 Hibbard Road, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
2 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 22 of the
Winnetka Village Code, for a Special Use Permit in accordance with Section
22.13 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance to allow the temporary placement of two
modular office units, to be used as Park District administrative offices, at
the rear, north side of the A.C. Neilsen Tennis Center on the Subject Property,
in order to provide space for the Park District’s administrative offices for
approximately three years; and
   WHEREAS, on October 21, 1999, the Design Review Board unanimously issued a
favorable comment, noting that the buildings has been placed in a relatively
obscure location with respect to the view from the street; and
   WHEREAS, on October 27, 1999, the Plan Commission considered the proposal
and, by the unanimous vote of the 10 members then present, found the proposal
to be consistent with Parks and Recreation Policy No. 5 of the 1979 Statement
of Community Objectives and with Chapter Eleven of the Comprehensive Plan as
then in effect; and
   WHEREAS, on November 8, 1999, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested temporary special use and
by the unanimous vote of the six members then present, has reported to the
Council recommending that the requested special use be granted; and
   WHEREAS, public uses are permitted as special uses in all zoning districts,
subject to the conditions and requirements pertaining to special uses, as set
forth in Section 22.13 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Chapter 22, Winnetka
Village Code; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed modular office units are necessary to the operation of
the Winnetka Park District and the provision of services to Winnetka residents
in that the units will serve as administrative offices for employees who must
be relocated from offices that are currently at Skokie School; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed temporary modular office units will not be detrimental
to the public health, safety, comfort, morals or general welfare of the
Village, in that they will enable the Winnetka Park District to provide
convenient, attractive and safe administrative offices on Park District
property while the Park District evaluates the permanent location of its
offices; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed modular office units will not be substantially
injurious to the use and enjoyment of other property in the immediate vicinity,
nor will it substantially diminish or impair property values in the immediate
vicinity, as the proposed units will not alter the existing use of the Subject
Property and will be located at the rear, north side of the A.C. Neilsen Tennis
Center, approximately 280 feet away from the Hibbard Road right-of-way and
approximately 345 feet away from the nearest residential property line; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed modular office units will not impede the normal and
orderly development or improvement of other property in the immediate vicinity
for uses permitted under applicable zoning regulations in that the use of the
modular offices is not expected to be necessary for more than three years and
will not alter the existing dimensions or use of the Subject Property by the
Park District; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed modular office units will be occupied by thirteen
employees whose vehicles will not alter the amount of parking on or the ingress
or egress to the Subject Property, as the plan proposes to reserve 13 spaces
from the parking lot and will have no negative impact to vehicular or
pedestrian traffic on the surrounding street system; and
   WHEREAS, the relocation of the Park District’s offices from the Skokie
School will increase the availability of on-street parking in the vicinity of
Elm Street and Glendale where Park District employees and patrons currently
park; and
   WHEREAS, adequate parking, utilities, access roads, drainage and other
facilities necessary to the existing special use already exist on the Subject
Property, and the proposed modular office units will not require any additions
to such facilities, as they will be located immediately adjacent to the A. C.
Neilsen Tennis Center on a surface that is currently paved; and
   WHEREAS, the special use in all other respects conforms to the applicable
regulations of this and other Village ordinances and codes.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2:  That a special use permit under Section 22.13 of the Zoning
Ordinance, Chapter 22 of the Winnetka Village Code, is granted with respect to
the Subject Property, which is located in the R-2 Single-Family Residential
Zoning District provided in said Zoning Ordinance, and is commonly known as 530
Hibbard Road, to permit the Winnetka Park District to place two modular office
units to be used as administrative offices for a period of no more than three
years, all in accordance with the plans accompanying the application for
special use permit.
   SECTION 3: That the special use permit hereby granted is conditioned upon
the commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the
effective date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: That, pursuant to Section 22.13(g) of the Zoning Ordinance, all
stipulations, conditions and restrictions set forth in this Ordinance as part
of the terms under which the special use is granted, may be modified or revised
from time to time by the Village Council following public notice and hearing,
using the same procedures set forth in the Zoning Ordinance or processing the
original special use application.
   SECTION 5 : This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 7th day of December, 1999, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES: Trustees Aquilino, Derning, Hilton, Lien and Powell
   NAYS: None
   ABSENT: Trustee Duhl
   APPROVED this 7th day of December, 1999.
   Signed:
   /ss/Louise A. Holland
   Village President
Countersigned:
/ss/Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: November 16, 1999
Posted: November 17, 1999
Passed and Approved: December 7, 1999
Posted: December 8, 1999
ORDINANCE NO. M-597-99
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (5 Indian Hill Road)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
   Parcel 1:
The East 500 feet of the West 1200 feet of that part of the South 20 acres of
the South West quarter of Section 21, Township 42 North, Range 13, East of the
Third Principal Meridian, described a s follows: Beginning at a point 172.7
feet South of the North West corner of said South 20 acres running thence South
along the West line of said 20 acres, 156.2 feet more or less to the South West
corner of said 20 acres; thence East along the South line of said 20 acres
1470.15 feet more or less to the center of Church Road; thence Northerly along
said center line of Church Road, 158.97 feet; thence West along a line parallel
with the South line of said 20 acres, 1440.57 feet more or less to the place of
beginning;
Parcel 2:
The North 18 feet (measured on the West line) of that part lying East of the
West 1200 feet of that part of the South 20 acres of the South West quarter of
Section 21, Township 42 North, Range 13, East of the Third Principal Meridian,
described as beginning at a point 172.7 feet South of the North West corner of
said 20 acres; thence South along the West line of said 20 acres; 156.2 feet to
the South West corner of said 20 acres; thence East along the South line of
said 20 acres, 1470.15 feet to center of Church Road; thence Northerly along
the center line of said Church Road 158.97 feet; thence West along a line
parallel with the South line of said 20 acres, 1440.57 feet to the place of
beginning, said 18 feet being described as beginning at the intersection of
center line of said Church Road with a line parallel to the South line of said
20 acres, said parallel line intersecting the West line of said South 20 acres
at a point 190.7 feet South of the North West corner of said South 20 acres;
thence West on said parallel line to the East line of the West 1200 feet
aforesaid; thence North and parallel to the West line of said South 20 acres,
18 feet; thence East on a line parallel to the South line of said South 20
acres to the center line of said Church Road, thence Southerly along the center
line of said Church Road to the place of beginning;
Parcel 3:
That part of Lot 3 in Owner’s Subdivision of Lot 1 of Indian Hill Subdivision
No.1 of parts of Sections 21, 28, and 29, Township 42 North, Range 13, East of
the Third Principal Meridian, Plat document 7751931 to wit: That part of said
Lot 3 which lies South of the North line of Section 28 aforesaid and East of
the West 700 feet of said Section 28 excepting from the premises above
described that portion which lies Westerly and Southerly of the following
described straight line: Beginning at the North West corner of premises first
described; thence South Easterly to point of intersection with the south line
of said premises first described with a straight line drawn South and at right
angles from a point in said North line of said Section 28 which said last
mentioned point is 25 feet East of the North West corner of said premises first
described, all in Cook County, Illinois.
commonly known as 5 Indian Hill Road, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
2 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 22 of the
Winnetka Village Code, for a variation from the corner setback requirements of
Section 22.07(h) of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, to make structural
alterations to a portion of the principal building located within the north
corner setback, maintaining the existing nonconforming corner setback of 12.00
feet, whereas 22.69 feet are required, resulting in a variation of 10.69 feet
(47.11%), to permit new window openings in the northwest corner of the
residence on the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, on November 8, 1999, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variation and, by the
unanimous vote of the five members then present, has reported to the Council
recommending that the requested variation be granted; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and unique hardships associated
with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect
to the Subject Property, in that: (a) the residence on the Subject Property was
constructed in 1922, before the adoption of the Zoning Ordinance, in what is
now a nonconforming location near the northwest corner of the Subject Property;
and (b) the Subject Property is an irregularly shaped lot that is bounded on
north, west and south by Indian Hill Road, as a result of which it is subject
to corner yard setbacks on its north and south sides, in addition to the west
front yard setback; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that
the structural changes to allow the new window openings are necessary both to
accommodate new room uses resulting from the reconfiguration of existing
interior space, and to meet the light and ventilation requirements of the
Building Code; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood, because the size and location of the building will not be
changed and the new windows are designed to match the other windows located in
conforming walls of the residence; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not impair an adequate supply of light
and air because neither the size nor the location of the residence on the
Subject Property is being altered, and the new windows will allow an increase
of light and air to the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property. as the new windows and other
improvements will comply with all applicable building and fire protection
codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the requested structural change to accommodate new windows will not
contribute to congestion on the public streets, as the Subject Property will
continue to be used for single-family residential purposes; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the requested variation will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation is in harmony with the general purpose and
intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that it allows the renovation,
restoration and rehabilitation of a structural sound existing building while
maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the community and protecting
established trees and landscaping.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2: That a variation is hereby granted to the Subject Property,
commonly known as 5 Indian Hill Road, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
2 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 22 of the
Winnetka Village Code, from the corner setback requirements of Section 22.07(h)
of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, to make structural alterations to a portion
of the principal building located within the north corner setback, maintaining
the existing nonconforming setback of 12.00 feet, whereas 22.69 feet are
required, resulting in a variation of 10.69 feet (47.11%) from the required
corner setback, to permit new window openings in the northwest corner of the
residence on the Subject Property, in accordance with the plans and
specifications submitted with the application for variation.
   SECTION 3: That the variation hereby granted is conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 21st day of December, 1999, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES: Trustees Lien, Duhl, Derning, Powell and Hilton
   NAYS: None
   ABSENT: Trustee Aquilino
   APPROVED this 21st day of December, 1999.
   Signed:
   /ss/Louise A. Holland
   Village President
Countersigned:
/ss/Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
