ORDINANCE NO. M-2-2000
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (1165 Hamptondale Road)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
Lot 9 in Chatfield’s re-subdivision of that part of the East half of the
Northwest quarter of Southwest quarter of Section 17, Township 42 North, Range
13 East of the Third Principal Meridian described as follows: Beginning at a
point in the West line of the East 5 acres of said Northwest quarter, 383.00
feet South of the North line of said Northwest quarter, (said point of
beginning in the South line of Ashland Avenue); thence West parallel with said
North line of Northwest quarter, (along said South line of Ashland Ave.) 499.44
feet, more or less, to a concrete monument in the West line of the East half of
said Northwest quarter; thence South along said West line of East half of
Northwest quarter 361.96 feet to a point in the Northerly line of Hamptondale
Ave.; thence Northeasterly along the said Northerly line of Hamptondale Ave.
550.70 feet more or less, to a point in said West line of East 5 acres, 510.25
feet South of said North line of Northwest quarter; thence North along said
West line of East 5 acres 127.25 feet to the point of beginning, in Cook
County, Illinois;
commonly known as 1165 Hamptondale Road, Winnetka, Illinois, located in the R-
4 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.16 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for a
variation from the rear yard requirements of Section 17.16.010.J of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, to make structural alterations to a portion of the
roof of the principal building that is located within the required rear yard,
maintaining the existing nonconforming rear yard of 3.00 feet, whereas 16.35
feet are required, to permit a new skylight to be placed in the existing roof
of the residence on the Subject Property 10.00 feet from the north property
line, resulting in a variation of 6.35 feet (38.84%), as more fully described
in the plans and specifications dated December 6, 1999; and
   WHEREAS, on January 10, 2000, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variations and, by the
unanimous vote of the seven members then present, has reported to the Council
recommending that the requested variation be granted; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and unique hardships associated
with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect
to the Subject Property, in that: (a) the Subject Property is an irregularly
shaped lot with both the north and south lot lines angling toward each other as
they approach the east side lot line, which is shorter than the west side lot
line; and (b) the residence on the Subject Property was constructed in 1928 in
what is now a nonconforming location near the northeast corner of the Subject
Property; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that
strict application of the Zoning Ordinance would prohibit structural
modifications such as the proposed skylight, which will provide a source of
natural light in a new, and otherwise windowless master bathroom that was
created by reconfiguring the existing interior space; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood, because the size, height, footprint and location of the
building will not be changed; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not impair an adequate supply of light
and air, because none of the exterior dimensions of the residence on the
Subject Property will be altered; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property, as the new skylight and related
improvements will comply with all applicable building code provisions,
including fire and life safety requirements; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the requested structural change to accommodate the proposed
skylight will not contribute to congestion on public streets, as the Subject
Property will continue to be used for a single family residential purposes; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the requested variation will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation is in harmony with the general purpose and
intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that it allows the renovation,
restoration and rehabilitation of a structurally sound existing building while
maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the community and protecting
established trees and landscaping.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2: That a variation is hereby granted to the Subject Property,
commonly known as 1165 Hamptondale Road, Winnetka, Illinois, located in the R-
4 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.16 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, from the rear
yard requirements of Section 17.16.010.J, to make structural alterations to a
portion of the roof of the building that is located within the required rear
yard, maintaining the existing nonconforming rear yard of 3.00 feet, whereas
16.35 feet are required, to permit a new skylight to be placed in the existing
roof of the residence of the Subject Property 10.00 feet from the north
property line, resulting in a variation of 6.35 feet (38.84%), as more fully
described in the plans and specifications dated December 6, 1999.
   SECTION 3: That the variation hereby granted is conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 15th day of February, 2000, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES: Trustees Aquilino, Derning, Duhl, Hilton, Lien and Powell
   NAYS: None
   ABSENT: None
   APPROVED this 15th day of February, 2000.
   Signed:
   /ss/Louise A. Holland
   Village President
Countersigned:
/ss/Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: February 1, 2000
Posted: February 2, 2000
Passed and Approved: February 15, 2000
Posted: February 16, 2000
ORDINANCE NO. M-3-2000
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (301 Sheridan Road)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
Lots 1 and 2 in Claude W. Morris’ Winnetka Subdivision of Lot 1 in the Re-
subdivision of Blocks 1 to 4 and vacated street known as Belleview Place in
Lake Shore Subdivision of Lot 1 in Nicholas Simon and others Subdivision of
part of the Southeast fractional quarter of Section 21 and fractional Southwest
quarter of Section 22, Township 42 North, Range 13, East of the Third Principal
Meridian, in Cook County, Illinois.
commonly known as 301 Sheridan Road, Winnetka, Illinois, located in the R-
2 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.24 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for a
variation from the setback requirements of Section 17.24.010.H of the Winnetka
Zoning Ordinance, to allow the replacement of a legally nonconforming enclosed
entry, located within the required setback, with a larger entry vestibule and
covered porch, which is proposed to observe a 24.00 foot setback, with no
change to the existing setback of –1.80 feet observed by the residence itself,
whereas 50.00 feet are required, resulting in a variation of 26.00 feet
(52.00%), as more fully described in the plans and specifications dated
December 8, 1999; and
   WHEREAS, on January 10, 2000, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variation and, by the
unanimous vote of the seven members then present, has reported to the Council
recommending that the requested variation be granted; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and unique hardships associated
with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect
to the Subject Property in that: (a) the Subject Property is an irregularly
shaped lot that is bordered on the east by Lake Michigan and on the south by
Elder Lane Park; (b) the southwest corner of the Subject Property is encumbered
by a 20-foot wide private road and utility easement that is 103 feet long and
that is a street for zoning purposes, so that the 50-foot front setback along
the south lot line is measured from the edge of the private road easement; and
(c) the buildable area of the lot is further reduced because the north and
south lot lines are not parallel, a 50-foot setback is required along Lake
Michigan, a 30.18 foot side yard is required along the west lot line and east
of the easement along the south lot line, and a 20.00 foot yard is required
along the north lot line; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that:
(a) the residence was built in 1928 in the western part of the Subject
Property, with most of the residence in what is now a nonconforming location
adjacent to the private road easement; and (b) the buildable area of
approximately 6,000 square feet is less than one-fourth of the lot area and is
of such a peculiar shape that it is impossible either to construct a reasonably
usable residence in a conforming location or to make conforming structural
improvements to the existing residence; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because the proposed exterior modification is minor, is
located within a reverse corner of the house, is no closer to the adjoining
properties than the existing residence, and is designed to be more consistent
with the architectural style of the residence; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not impair an adequate supply of light
and air because the building height will not be changed and the proposed
addition is set back from the west and south building lines; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property, as it will comply with all applicable
building, fire protection and life safety codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvement; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed modification to the entrance will not contribute to
congestion on the public streets, as the Subject Property will continue to be
used for a single family residence; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed modification to the front
entrance will otherwise impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and
welfare of the inhabitants of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation is in harmony with the general purpose and
intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that it allows the renovation,
restoration and rehabilitation of a structurally sound existing building while
maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the community and protecting
established trees and landscaping.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2: That the following variations are hereby granted to the Subject
Property, commonly known as 301 Sheridan Road, Winnetka, Illinois, located in
the R-2 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.24 of
the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for a
variation from the setback requirements of Section 17.24.010.H, to allow the
replacement of a legally nonconforming enclosed entry, located within the
required setback, with a larger entry vestibule and covered porch, which is
proposed to observe a 24.00 foot setback, with no change to the existing
building setback of –1.80 feet observed by the residence itself, whereas 50.00
feet are required, resulting in a variation of 26.00 feet (52.00%), as more
fully described in the plans and specifications dated December 8, 1999.
   SECTION 3: That the variation hereby granted is conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 15th day of February, 2000, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES: Trustees Aquilino, Derning, Duhl, Hilton, Lien and Powell
   NAYS: None
   ABSENT: None
   APPROVED this 15th day of February, 2000.
   Signed:
   /ss/Louise A. Holland
   Village President
Countersigned:
/ss/Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: February 1, 2000
Posted: February 2, 2000
Passed and Approved: February 15, 2000
