ORDINANCE NO. M-4-2000
AN ORDINANCE AUTHORIZING THE SALE OR OTHER DISPOSITION OF SURPLUS ELECTRICAL
TRANSFORMERS OWNED BY THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA
   WHEREAS, the Village of Winnetka owns certain electrical transformers
(hereinafter the “Personal Property”) described in Exhibit A, which exhibit is
attached to this ordinance and incorporated herein by reference; and
   WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka have determined that the
Personal Property listed in Exhibit A have been removed from service on the
Village’s electric distribution system and is no longer necessary or useful to
the Village of Winnetka; and
   WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka have further determined
that, upon the installation of new transformers to replace any other electrical
transformers that are retired during the 2000-2001 fiscal year (hereinafter the
“Additional Retired Transformers”), such Additional Retired Transformers will
no longer be necessary or useful to the Village of Winnetka; and
   WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka have further determined that
the storage costs and potential liability resulting from retaining the Personal
Property and the Additional Retired Transformers exceed the value to the
Village of the Personal Property and the Additional Retired Transformers, and
that the Personal Property and Additional Retired Transformers should be
disposed of pursuant to Section 11-76-4 of the Illinois Municipal Code (65 ILCS
5/11-76-4), so as to avoid incurring unnecessary additional costs and exposure
to liability for storing or disposing of the Personal Property and Additional
Retired Transformers.
   NOW THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 :   Pursuant to Section 11-76-4 of the Illinois Municipal code (65
ILCS 5/11-76-4), the Council of the Village of Winnetka find: (a) that the
items of Personal Property listed in the attached Exhibit A (the “Personal
Property”) are no longer necessary or useful to the Village of Winnetka; (b)
that any electrical transformers that are scheduled to be replaced during the
2000-2001 fiscal year (the “Additional Retired Transformers”) will no longer be
necessary or useful to the Village when that replacement occurs; and (c) that
the best interests of the Village of Winnetka will be served by the sale or
other disposition of the Personal Property and the Additional Retired
Transformers as hereinafter provided.
   SECTION 2: The Council of the Village of Winnetka further find that, based
on prior experience in disposing of similar items of property, the cost of
conducting a public sale of such items of property, including the costs of
advertising and publishing, and the costs of personnel needed to maintain
security and to conduct the public sale exceeds the value of such items.
   SECTION 3: Pursuant to Section 11-76-4 of the Illinois Municipal Code, the
Village Manager is hereby authorized and directed to determine the value and
dispose of the Personal Property and the Additional Retired Transformers in the
following manner: (a) transformers that contain no PCB’s shall be sold to the
highest bidder, with or without advertising; and (b) all transformers that
contain PCB’s or that are PCB contaminated shall be disposed of at the lowest
cost to the Village, said cost being determined with or without advertising,
provided that any person or entity that disposes of or destroys any part of
such transformers shall provide a sworn statement to the Village certifying
that such disposal or destruction complies with all applicable environmental
laws and regulations.
   SECTION 4: That this ordinance shall be in full force and effect from and
after its passage, approval and posting.
   PASSED this 21st day of March, 2000, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:   Trustees Aquilino, Derning, Duhl, Lien and Powell
   NAYES:   None
   ABSENT: None
   APPROVED this 21st day of March, 2000.
   Signed
   /ss/Louise A. Holland
   Village President
Countersigned:
/ss/Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced:   March 7, 2000
Posted:   March 8, 2000
Passed and Approved: March 21, 2000
Posted:   March 22, 2000
ORDINANCE NO. M-5-2000
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (445 Cedar Street)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
   The North 98 feet of the West 93 and one half feet of that part East of the
East line of Cedar Street and South of the South line of Cherry Street of the
North half of the West half of Block 47 in Village of Winnetka, Township 42
North, Range 13 East of the Third Principal Meridian in Cook County, Illinois.
commonly known as 445 Cedar Street, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
4 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.16, of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for a
variation from the rear yard requirements of Section 17.16.010.J of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, to allow an air conditioning unit to be placed 9.00
feet from the rear lot line, although a rear yard of 14.70 feet is required,
resulting in a variation of 5.70 feet (38.78%), and to allow structural
alterations to the roof of the single family residence on the Subject Property,
with the proposed roof observing a rear yard of 8.67 feet, resulting in a
variation of 6.03 feet (41.02%), as more fully described in the plans and
specifications submitted with the application and dated October 8, 1999; and
   WHEREAS, on February 14, 2000, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variations and by the
unanimous vote of the four members then present, has reported to the Council
recommending that the requested variation be granted; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and unique hardships associated
with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect
to the Subject Property in that (a) the Subject Property is a nearly square
corner lot that is improved with a single-family residence that was constructed
in 1922; (b) the residence is now in a nonconforming location near the
southeast corner of the Subject Property, as a result of a legal subdivision in
1941, which reduced the size of the Subject Property by creating a second and
smaller lot from the south portion of the original property; and (c) the rear
yard in which the applicants propose to locate the air conditioner functions as
a side yard, due to the orientation of the residence toward Cedar Street, which
the Zoning Board of Appeals, in a 1942 decision, erroneously designated as the
front yard; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that
strict application of the Zoning Ordinance would prohibit structural
modifications to the roof, which cannot be made fully conforming without
removing a portion of the residence, and would prohibit the addition of a
conforming deck in the east side yard, which is proposed by the applicant but
which cannot be accomplished without relocating the air conditioning unit; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because (a) the structural alteration of the roof will replace
the existing hip roof with a gabled roof that is consistent with both the
character of the neighborhood and the period in which the residence was
originally built, (b) the structural alteration of the roof will reduce the
degree of rear yard nonconformity by one foot and (c) the proposed relocation
of the air conditioning unit to the rear yard will keep the air conditioner in
close proximity to the residence, while placing it in a location that is not
visible from the street and is farther removed from the living space of any
adjacent properties; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not impair an adequate supply of
light and air, and the structural modifications to the roof will reduce the
roof overhang in the southeast corner of the property by approximately one
foot; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property as the new roof and related improvements
will comply with all applicable Building Code provisions, including fire and
life safety requirements; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the requested structural change to the roof and the requested
relocation of the air conditioner will not contribute to the congestion on
public streets, as the Subject Property will continue to be used for single
family residential purposes and the off-street parking on the Subject Property
will not be changed; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the requested variations will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations are in harmony with the general purpose
and intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that (a) they allow the
renovation, restoration and rehabilitation of a structurally sound existing
building while maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the community
and protecting established trees and landscaping, and (b) they reduce the
degree of rear yard nonconformity by reducing the size of the eaves on the
roof.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2: That a variation is hereby granted to the Subject Property,
commonly known as 445 Cedar Street, Winnetka, Illinois, located in the R-
4 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.16, of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for a
variation from the rear yard requirements of Section 17.16.010.J of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, to allow an air conditioning unit to be placed 9.00
feet from the rear lot line, although a rear yard of 14.70 feet is required,
resulting in a variation of 5.70 feet (38.78%), and to allow structural
alterations to the roof of the single family residence on the Subject Property,
with the proposed roof observing a rear yard of 8.67 feet, resulting in a
variation of 6.03 feet (41.02%), as more fully described in the plans and
specifications submitted with the application and dated October 8, 1999.
   SECTION 3: That the variations hereby granted are conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   PASSED this 21st day of March, 2000, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES: Trustees Aquilino, Derning, Duhl, Lien and Powell
   NAYS: None
   ABSENT: None
   APPROVED this 21st day of March, 2000.
