ORDINANCE NO. M-6-2000
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (900 Greenwood Avenue)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
Lots 23 and 24 (except the South 100.0 feet of said lots) in Block 31 in
Chicago North Shore Land Company’s Subdivision, in the Southwest quarter of
Section 8 and the Northwest quarter of Section 17 and the Northeast quarter of
Section 18, in Township 42 North, Range 13 East of the Third Principal
Meridian, in Cook County, Illinois.
commonly known as 900 Greenwood Avenue, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the
R-5 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.12 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for the
following variations: (a) a variation from the intensity of use of lot
limitations of Section 17.12.010.F of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance to permit
the construction of a 23.49 square foot second floor bay window, which will
increase the existing roofed lot coverage from 2,389.59 square feet to 2,413.07
square feet, although 2,346.35 square feet is allowed, resulting in a variation
of 66.72 square feet (2.84%), and (b) a variation from the rear yard
requirements of Section 17.12.010.J of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance to permit
the proposed bay window to be located 7.01 feet from the rear lot line, which
will reduce the rear yard from the legally nonconforming 8.51 feet, although a
rear yard of 14.01 feet is required, resulting in a variation of 7.00 feet
(49.96%), all as more fully described in the plans and specifications submitted
with the application dated January 6, 2000; and
   WHEREAS, on February 14, 2000, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variation and by the
unanimous vote of the four members then present, has reported to the Council
recommending that the requested variation be granted; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and unique hardships associated
with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect
to the Subject Property in that: (a) the Subject Property is a nearly square,
nonconforming lot that is wider than it is deep; (b) the residence on the
Subject Property was constructed in 1922 in what is now a nonconforming
location near the rear of the Subject Property; and (c) the residence on the
Subject Property is one of five log and stucco Swedish farmhouse style
residences that are clustered in the vicinity of the northwest corner of Tower
Road and Greenwood Avenue; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that
(a) the proposed bay window addition will create a second functional bedroom on
the second floor, resulting in a total of three bedrooms, which is the standard
number of bedrooms in smaller homes in the Village, (b) there is no conforming
location for the addition of a third bedroom, and (c) the proposed bay window
will create the additional bedroom in the only viable location for an
additional bedroom; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because the proposed bay window is a minimal addition to the
building, is designed to be consistent with the style of the building, and is
located at the rear of the existing house, so as not to be visible from the
street; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not impair an adequate supply of light
and air because of the small size of the bay window and its minimal intrusion
into the rear yard; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property as the new bay window and related
improvements will comply with all applicable Building Code provisions,
including fire and life safety requirements; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not diminish the taxable value of
land and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed bay window will not contribute to congestion on the
public streets, as the Subject Property will continue to be used for single
family residential purposes and off-street parking on the Subject Property will
not be affected; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the requested variations will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village.
   WHEREAS, the requested variations are in harmony with the general purpose
and intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that they allow the renovation,
restoration and rehabilitation of a uniquely designed, structurally sound
existing building while maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the
community and protecting established trees and landscaping.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1: That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2: That the following variations are hereby granted to the Subject
Property, commonly known as 900 Greenwood Avenue, Winnetka, Illinois, located
in the R-5 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.12
of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code: (a) a
variation from the intensity of use of lot limitations of Section 17.12.010.F
of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, to permit the construction of a 23.49 square
foot second floor bay window, which will increase the existing roofed lot
coverage from 2,389.59 square feet to 2,413.07 square feet, although 2,346.35
square feet is allowed, resulting in a variation of 66.72 square feet (2.84%);
and (b) a variation from the rear yard requirements of Section 17.16.010.J of
the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance to permit the proposed bay window to be located
7.01 feet from the rear lot line, which will reduce the rear yard from the
legally nonconforming 8.51 feet, although a rear yard of 14.01 feet is
required, resulting in a variation of 7.00 feet (49.96%), all as more fully
described in the plans and specifications submitted with the applicant and
dated January 6, 2000.
   SECTION 3: That the variations hereby granted are conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   PASSED this 21st day of March, 2000, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES: Trustees Aquilino, Derning, Duhl, Lien and Powell
   NAYS: None
   ABSENT: None
   APPROVED this 21st day of March, 2000.
   Signed:
   /ss/Louise A. Holland
   Village President
Countersigned:
/ss/Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
