ORDINANCE NO. M-10-2000
AN ORDINANCE AUTHORIZING THE SALE OR OTHER DISPOSITION OF PERSONAL PROPERTY OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA
   WHEREAS, from time to time, the Winnetka Police Department comes into
possession of items of personal property that have been lost, found or stolen,
which personal property includes the coins described in Exhibit A and the
“Bicycles” shown on Exhibit B (hereinafter collectively referred to as the
“Personal Property”), attached to this ordinance; and
   WHEREAS, the Personal Property has been in the possession of the Winnetka
Police Department for more than 6 months, during which time the Chief of Police
of the Winnetka Police Department has made reasonable inquiry and efforts to
identify and notify the owners or other persons entitled to possession of the
Personal Property, but has been unsuccessful in those attempts; and
   WHEREAS, pursuant to Section 3 of the Law Enforcement Disposition of
Property Act, 765 ILCS 1030/3, the Chief of Police has transferred the custody
of the Personal Property to the Village of Winnetka; and
   WHEREAS, the Village of Winnetka owns computer equipment, some of which will
be retired as it is replaced during the fiscal year, April 1, 2000 through
March 31, 2001 (hereinafter referred to as “Retired Computer Equipment”), and
which the Finance Director has recommended be disposed of by methods that are
consistent with past disposal methods; and
   WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka have determined that the
Personal Property and Retired Computer Equipment, described herein (hereinafter
referred to collectively as the “Surplus Property”) are no longer necessary or
useful to the Village of Winnetka, that the cost of storing said Surplus
Property exceeds its value to the Village of Winnetka, that it is not in the
best interests of the Village of Winnetka to retain said Surplus Property and
that the Surplus Property should be disposed of in the manner hereinafter
provided, pursuant to Section 11-76-4 of the Illinois Municipal Code (65 ILCS
5/11-76-4), so as to avoid incurring additional costs for storing said Surplus
Property.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain:
   SECTION 1 : Pursuant to Section 11-76-4 of the Illinois Municipal Code (65
ILCS 5/11-76-4), the Council of the Village of Winnetka find that the above-
described Surplus Property, consisting of the items of Personal Property shown
on the attached Exhibits A and B is not necessary or useful to the Village of
Winnetka and that the best interests of the Village of Winnetka will be served
by the sale or other disposition of such Surplus Property.
   SECTION 2 : The Council of the Village of Winnetka further find that, based
on prior experience in disposing of similar items of property, the cost of
conducting a public sale of the Surplus Property, including the costs of
advertising and publishing, and the costs of personnel needed to maintain
security and to conduct the public sale, exceeds the value of such items.
   SECTION 3: Pursuant to said Section 11-76-4, the Village Manager is hereby
authorized and directed to determine the value of and to dispose of the
Personal Property in any manner he deems reasonable under the circumstances.
   SECTION 4: Pursuant to said Section 11-76-4, the Director of Finance is
hereby authorized and directed to determine the value of and to dispose of
Retired Computer Equipment that is retired during the fiscal year April 1,
2000, through March 31, 2001, using disposal methods that are consistence with
past disposal practices.
   SECTION 5: This Ordinance shall be in full force and effect from and after
its passage, approval, and posting.
   ADOPTED this 2nd day of May, 2000, pursuant to the following roll call vote:
   AYES: Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Lien, Powell, Presser and Woodbury
   NAYS: None
   ABSENT: None
   APPROVED this 2nd day of May, 2000.
   Signed:
   /ss/Louise A. Holland
   Village President
Countersigned:
/ss/Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: April 18, 2000
Posted: April 19, 2000
Passed and Approved: May 2, 2000
Posted: May 3, 2000
ORDINANCE NO. M-11-2000
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (976 Sunset Road)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
Lot 1 in Meadow Farm Subdivision of part of the southwest quarter of the
southeast quarter of Section 20, Township 42 North, Range 13 East of the Third
Principal Meridian, in the Village of Winnetka, Cook County, Illinois.
commonly known as 976 Sunset Road, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in R-
2 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.24 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17, Winnetka Village Code, for a variation
from the front setback limitations of Section 17.24.010.H of the Zoning
Ordinance to allow the existing residence to be improved by adding a second
floor directly above the existing first floor, so that the proposed addition
will observe the existing front setback of 22 feet, although a setback of 50
feet is required, resulting in a variation of 28 feet (56.0%), said addition to
be in accordance with the application for variation and plans accompanying the
application; and
   WHEREAS, on March 13, 2000, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variation, which hearing
was continued to allow the applicants to submit revised plans; and
   WHEREAS, on April 10, 2000, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variation and, by the
unanimous vote of the four members then present, has reported to the Council
recommending that the requested variation be granted; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property is an irregularly shaped flag lot, the north
lot line of which is encumbered by part of a 66-foot wide Metropolitan Water
Reclamation District sewer easement, and the north lot line and northeast
corner of which are encumbered by a private street by virtue of an easement for
ingress and egress that serves three residences; and
   WHEREAS, due to the angle of the north lot line and the circular turnaround
in the northeast corner of the lot, substantial portions of the existing two-
story residence are located within the required 50-foot setback, with the
northeast corner of the garage being located 22 feet from the edge of the
circular turnaround portion of the private street; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and unique hardships associated
with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect
to the Subject Property in that (a) the existing front setback nonconformities
cannot be corrected without removing substantial portions of the building or
reconstructing it in its entirety, (b) it is unlikely that the unimproved,
platted turnaround easement will ever be used because of the way the lots at
the end of the easement have been developed, and (c) adding space to the
residence for comparable uses in a conforming location on the Subject Property
would result in an unusual and impractical interior configuration and would
increase impermeable surface on the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that
adding a conforming second floor addition would require a stepped-back gable
end, which would not be practical from the standpoint of maintenance, use or
function, and bringing the Subject Property into full conformity with all
zoning requirements and limitations would require either the removal of a
significant portion of the front of the house or the replacement of the
building with an entirely new structure; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because the proposed second floor addition will be consistent
with the size, scale and presence of the other residences in the immediate
vicinity, and will maintain the existing location of the residence on the
Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not impair an adequate supply of light
and air because the height of the building will be within the height
limitations of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance and the areas of the residence
that are closest to the nearest neighboring residence observe conforming side
yards; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property, as the proposed addition will comply
with all applicable building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvement; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed second floor addition will not contribute to
congestion on the public streets, as the residence will continue to be used as
a single-family residence; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed second floor addition will
otherwise impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation is in harmony with the general purpose and
intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that it allows the renovation,
restoration and rehabilitation of a structurally sound existing building while
maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the community and protecting
established trees and landscaping.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2:  That a variation is hereby granted with respect to the Subject
Property, commonly known as 976 Sunset Road, Winnetka, Illinois, which is
located in the R-2 Single-Family Residential District provided in Chapter 17.24
of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17, Winnetka Village Code, for a
variation from the front setback limitations of Section 17.24.010.H of the
Zoning Ordinance to allow the existing residence to be improved by adding a
second floor directly above the existing first floor, so that the proposed
addition will observe the existing front setback of 22 feet, although a setback
of 50 feet is required, resulting in a variation of 28 feet (56.0%), said
addition to be in accordance with the application for variation and plans
accompanying the application.
   SECTION 3: That the variation hereby granted is conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within twelve months after the
effective date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 16th day of May, 2000, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES: Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Lien, Powell, Presser and Woodbury
   NAYS: None
   ABSENT: None
   APPROVED this 16tj day of May, 2000.
