ORDINANCE NO. M-13-2000
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (550 OAK STREET)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
Lot 4 (except the South 60.0 feet of the East 12.5 feet thereof, and except the
South 50.0 feet of the West 52.50 feet thereof) and Lot 5 (except the South
60.0 feet thereof) in Sherlock’s Subdivision of Block 40 in the Village of
Winnetka, a Subdivision of the Northeast ¼ of Section 20 and the North ½ of
Fractional Section 21, Township 42 North, Range 13 East of the Third Principal
Meridian in Cook County, Illinois,
commonly known as 550 Oak St., Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
4 Single-Family Residential Zoning District, as provided in the Chapter 17.16
of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17, Winnetka Village Code, for
variations from the setback requirements of Section 17.16.010.H of the Zoning
Ordinance and from the limitations on permitted encroachments into required
yards established in Section 17.68.101.A.1.I of the Zoning Ordinance, to allow
the construction of a fence that will be located 18 inches from the
intersecting street lines of Oak Street and Poplar Street and will reduce
visibility at the southwest corner of those intersecting streets lines by more
than 10%, whereas a setback of twenty-five (25) feet is required, resulting in
a variation of 23.5 feet, all as depicted in the application for variation and
plans accompanying the application; and
   WHEREAS, on May 8, 2000, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of Appeals
conducted a public hearing on the requested variation, at the end of which four
of the five members then present voted to recommend that the Council deny the
variation; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and unique hardships associated
with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect
to the Subject Property in that: (a) the Subject Property is a corner lot
located at the southwest corner of Oak Street and Poplar Street; (b) the north
and east lot lines of the Subject Property are contiguous with the south and
west street lines of Oak Street and Poplar Street; (c) both the Oak and Poplar
Street frontages have deep parkways, with there being a distance of
approximately 19 feet from the back of the curb to the lot line along both
street frontages; (d) the fence that the owners propose to construct is a
traditional design that has been designed to be consistent with the style of
the residence on the Subject Property, which was constructed in 1894; (e)
although the fence will reduce visibility at the southeast corner of Oak and
Poplar Streets by more than 10%, the reduction in visibility is not substantial
due to the height and design of the proposed fence, coupled with the deep
parkways; and (f) the Village Engineer has determined that the fence will have
little, if any, impact on sight distances due to the design of the fence, the
width of the parkways and the stop sign configuration at Oak and Poplar
Streets; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that
constructing the fence in a conforming location would have a negative impact on
the value of the property, because it would require either that the entire
fence be set back 25 feet along the entire length of both street frontages,
that the fence be placed at an angle at the corner of Oak and Poplar to observe
the 25-foot sight triangle, or that a more open fence be constructed, none of
which alternatives would be consisted either with the traditional location of
fences or with the traditional appearance of the house and Subject Property;
and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because the proposed fence has been designed to be consistent
with the architectural style of the residence on the Subject Property, which
was built in 1894 and has an established presence in the immediate vicinity;
and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not impair an adequate supply of
light and air because of the height and design of the proposed fence, and
because it will be placed along the street frontages of the Subject Property;
and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property as the proposed fence will comply with
all applicable building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not diminish the taxable value of
land and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvement; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed fence will not contribute to congestion on the public
streets, as it will not change the size, capacity or use of the residence on
the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation is in harmony with the general purpose and
intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that it contributes to the
enhancement of the appearance of the Subject Property in a manner that
maintains the existing scale and appearance of the community, while protecting
established trees and landscaping; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed alterations and additions
will otherwise impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare
of the inhabitants of the Village.
   WHEREAS, the Council have determined that, in this instance, the public
safety is better served by the proposed fence than by conforming alternatives,
which could include the planting, without a permit or a variance, of a solid
wall of trees, shrubs or other landscaping materials over six feet tall at or
near the property line; and
   WHEREAS, the Council have directed staff to consider possible amendments to
the Zoning Ordinance that would eliminate the inconsistencies that can result
from the strict application of Section 17.68.010.A of the Zoning Ordinance and
the need to meet the Zoning Ordinance’s hardship standards in cases similar to
the one now under consideration.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1: That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2:  That variations are hereby granted with respect to the Subject
Property, commonly known as 550 Oak Street, Winnetka, Illinois, which is
located in the R-4 Single-Family Residential District, as provided in the
Chapter 17.16 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka
Village Code, for variations from the setback requirements of Section
17.16.010.H of the Zoning Ordinance and from the limitations on permitted
encroachments into required yards established in Section 17.68.101.A.1.i of the
Zoning Ordinance, to allow the construction of a fence that will be located 18
inches from the intersecting street lines of Oak Street and Poplar Street and
will reduce visibility at the southwest corner of those intersecting streets
lines by more than 10%, whereas a setback of twenty-five (25) feet is required,
resulting in a variation of 23.5 feet, all as depicted in the application for
variation and plans accompanying the application.
   SECTION 3: That the variations hereby granted are conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within twelve months after the
effective date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 16th day of May, 2000, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES: Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Lien, Powell, Presser and Woodbury
   NAYS: None
   ABSENT: None
   APPROVED this 16th day of May, 2000.
   Signed:
   /ss/Louise A. Holland
   Village President
Countersigned:
/ss/Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: May 9, 2000
Posted: May 10, 2000
Passed and Approved: May 16, 2000
