ORDINANCE NO. M-15-2000
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (1124 Gage Street)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
   Lot 5 in Block 7 in Jared Gage’s Subdivision of part of the Southwest ¼ of
Section 8 and of the Northwest ¼ of Section 17, township 42 North, Range 13,
East of the Third Principal Meridian, in Cook County, Illinois,
commonly known as 1124 Gage Street, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
5 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.12 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for the
following variations: (a) from the building height limitations of Section
17.12.010.C to allow a building height of three stories, whereas a maximum
building height of 2.5 stories is allowed; (b) from the maximum building size
limitations of Section 17.12.010.G to permit a total gross floor area of
4,005.44 square feet, whereas a maximum of 2,988.60 square feet is permitted,
reducing the gross floor area by 435.88 square feet and resulting in a
variation of 1,016.84 square feet (34.02%); and (c) from the setback
requirements of Section 17.12.010.H to permit a front setback of 26.00 feet
along Gage Street, whereas 30.00 feet are required, resulting in a variation of
4.00 feet (13.33%) and a floor area ratio of .5361; and (c) all for the purpose
of constructing an addition that will increase the height of the uppermost
level of the split level residence on the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, the pending application is the renewal of a request for the same
variations that was originally filed in 1999 and received a favorable
recommendation from the Zoning Board of Appeals after the applicants withdrew
their request for the height variation; and
   WHEREAS, following the Council’s introduction of an ordinance that would
have granted the applicants’ original request for the gross floor area and
setback variations, the applicants determined that they could not meet the
height limitations of the Zoning Ordinance and withdrew their application in
its entirety; and
   WHEREAS, the applicants subsequently reapplied for all three variations,
including building height; and
   WHEREAS, on April 10, 2000, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variations and by the
unanimous vote of the 4 members then present, has reported to the Council
recommending that the requested variations be granted; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and unique hardships associated
with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect
to the Subject Property in that: (a) the Subject Property has a slightly
irregular shape, which affects the front setback lines; (b) the Subject
Property is a relatively narrow, 50-foot wide through lot, which must observe
setbacks along both Tower Road and Gage Street, thereby reducing the buildable
area on the lot; (c) the Subject Property is improved with a split-level
single-family residence that was constructed in 1950, before the maximum
building size limitations were enacted; and (d) the Subject Property has
existing nonconformities in both gross floor area and front setback, which
cannot be corrected entirely without removing and rebuilding the house; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that
the requested changes will allow the interior of the home to be reconfigured in
order to provide additional living space in the only viable location for such
an addition; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because the proposed alterations will: (a) remove the
cantilevered second floor area along Gage Street, increasing the setback from
21.00 feet to 26.00 feet, thereby reducing the degree in nonconformity in the
Gage Street front setback; (b) reduce the calculated gross floor area by 435.88
square feet by eliminating most of the existing overhanging porch at the north
side of the house and by removing the enclosures for the existing enclosed,
screened porch on the south side of the residence; and (c) will remain within
the measured vertical height limitations because the height of the new ridge
line will be 33 feet and 10 inches, which is less than the 35-foot maximum
allowed under Section 17.12.010.C of the Zoning Ordinance; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not impair an adequate supply of
light and air because the proposed addition raises the roof and ridge line
approximately five feet, and the proposed building modifications will reduce
the visible bulk of the building in the front yard by cutting back on the
existing overhang and will increase the supply of light and air by opening the
walls of an existing screened porch at the rear of the house; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property because the proposed work will comply
with all applicable building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not diminish the taxable value of
land and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed family room addition will not contribute to congestion
on the public streets, as the residence on the Subject Property will continue
to be a single-family residence; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed addition will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village.
   WHEREAS, the requested variations are in harmony with the general purpose
and intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that they will allow the
renovation, restoration and rehabilitation of a structurally sound existing
building while maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the community
and protecting established trees and landscaping.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1: That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2: That, subject to the conditions set forth in Section 3 of this
ordinance, the following variations are hereby granted to the Subject Property,
commonly known as 1124 Gage Street, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
5 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.12 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for the
following variations: (a) from the building height limitations of Section
17.12.010.C to allow a building height of three stories, whereas a maximum
building height of 2.5 stories is allowed; (b) from the maximum building size
limitations of Section 17.12.010.G to permit a total gross floor area of
4,005.44 square feet, whereas a maximum of 2,988.60 square feet is permitted,
reducing the gross floor area by 435.88 square feet and resulting in a
variation of 1,016.84 square feet (34.02%); and (c) from the setback
requirements of Section 17.12.010.H to permit a front setback of 26.00 feet
along Gage Street, whereas 30.00 feet are required, resulting in a variation of
4.00 feet (13.33%) and a floor area ratio of .5361; and (c) all for the purpose
of constructing an addition that will increase the height of the uppermost
level of the split level residence on the Subject Property, all in accordance
with the plans and specification submitted with the application for variations.
   SECTION 3 :  That the variations hereby granted are conditioned upon: (a)
the commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the
effective date of this Ordinance; and (b) the permanent removal, before any
work begins on the proposed addition, of the existing knee walls and screens
enclosing the roofed porch at the rear of the house, without replacement
thereof, in whole or in part, with any material of any kind.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 20th day of June, 2000, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES: Trustees Brower, Lien, Powell, Presser and Woodbury
   NAYS: None
   ABSENT: Trustee Aquilino
   APPROVED this 20th day of June, 2000.
   Signed:
   /ss/Louise A. Holland
   Village President
Countersigned:
/ss/Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: May 16, 2000
Posted: May 17, 2000
Passed and Approved: June 20, 2000
Posted: June 21, 2000
ORDINANCE NO. M-16-2000
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (518 WINNETKA AVENUE)
   WHEREAS, the property commonly known as 518 Winnetka Avenue (the “Subject
Property”), Winnetka, Illinois, is located in the C-1 Limited Retail Commercial
District provided in Chapter 17.40 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17
of the Winnetka Village Code, and is legally described as follows:
Lots 1 and 2 in Block 2 in Manus’ Indian Hill Subdivision of those parts of the
North ½ of Section 28, Township 42 North, Range 13, East of the Third Principal
Meridian in Cook County, Illinois (excepting therefrom that part of Lot 1
described as follows: that part lying northeasterly of a straight line
extending from a point in the East line of Lot 1, 20 feet South of the
Northeast corner of said Lot to a point on the North line of said Lot, 20 feet
West of the Northeast corner conveyed for roadway purposes);
and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property, which is located at the intersections of
Green Bay Road, Winnetka Ave and Brier Street, was the site of a gasoline and
automobile service station for more than 30 years and was used only as an
illegal parking lot after the service station closed in 1999; and
   WHEREAS, on January 20, 2000, on due notice thereof, the Design Review Board
met to consider a design concept proposed by the owner and contract purchaser
(the “Applicants”) of the Subject Property for the development of the Subject
Property with a mixed-used commercial and residential building that fully
complied with all zoning requirements; and
   WHEREAS, in commenting on the preliminary design concept, the Design Review
Board expressed concern about the massiveness of the building in the context of
the neighborhood, the building’s close proximity to the narrow sidewalks, and
the underground parking; and
   WHEREAS, on March 2, 2000, the Applicants submitted an application for the
following variations: (a) a variation from the maximum building height of 35
feet and 2-½ stories of Section 17.40.010.C of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance,
to allow the construction of a 3-story building; and (b) variations from the
maximum setback of 3 feet, as provided in Section 17.40.010.N of the Winnetka
Zoning Ordinance, to allow the street level of the proposed building to observe
a setback of 9 feet along Winnetka Avenue and Brier Street, the second story to
be set back up to 5 feet and the third floor to be set back 16.50 feet,
resulting in variations of 6 feet, 2 feet and 13.50 feet, respectively; and
   WHEREAS, on March 2, 2000, the Applicants also applied for a special use
permit pursuant to Section 17.40.010.K.11 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, to
allow the off-street parking for the building to be located at the street
level; and
   WHEREAS, on March 16, 2000, and April 6, 2000, on due notice thereof, the
Design Review Board considered the revised proposal submitted by Applicants,
which provided for an arcade along the first floor to increase pedestrian
space, terracing of the upper floors to create building articulation and reduce
visual mass, and 23 parking spaces at street level, all of which addressed the
Design Review Board’s preliminary concerns, but would require height and
setback variations and a special use permit; and
   WHEREAS, the Design Review Board has submitted generally favorable comment
to the Council on the proposed building design, as revised, but has expressed
concern about the removal of trees, parking lot buffering and other landscaping
features; and
   WHEREAS, on April 10, 2000, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals held a public hearing on the requested height variation, setback
variations and special use and, by the unanimous vote of the four members then
present, has reported to the Council recommending that the requested variations
be granted and that the requested special use permit be granted, subject to the
Village’s approval of the Applicants’ traffic study; and
   WHEREAS, on April 26, 2000, on due notice thereof, the Plan Commission
considered the revised proposal and its request for a special use and has
submitted extensive findings to the Village Council, with the 12 members of the
Commission then in attendance unanimously finding that the proposal, as
modified, is consistent with the Winnetka Comprehensive Plan, "Winnetka 2020,"
and recommending that the special use permit be granted; and
   WHEREAS, on June 6, 2000, the Village Council began consideration of the
Applicants’ requests, and introduced this ordinance; and
   WHEREAS, at its June 6, 2000, meeting, the Council expressed concern about:
(a) the narrowness of the sidewalk, particularly along Winnetka Avenue; (b) the
size and configuration of parking spaces; (c) the impact of the proposed arcade
on the streetscape, public safety and the viability of the commercial space,
because the arcade area and the commercial space behind it are not readily
visible from the street; and (d) the proposed landscape buffer and other
landscaping issues; and
   WHEREAS, at the Village Council meeting on June 20, 2000, the Applicants
addressed the four areas of concern previously raised by the Council, and
further discussion of the proposal ensued; and
   WHEREAS, following the Village Council meeting on June 20, 2000, and in
response to the Council’s expressed concerns, the Applicants further revised
the proposal, by removing the arcade and setting the building back 4 feet from
all 3 street frontages, which reduced the amount of parking area and resulted
in the Applicants’ applying for a variation from the required number of parking
spaces; and
   WHEREAS, on July 10, 2000, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals held a public hearing on the requested parking variation and forwarded
the application to the Council without a favorable recommendation, the vote of
the six Zoning Board of Appeals members then present having resulted in a tie;
and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations are in harmony with the general purpose
and intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, as the proposal, as modified,
complies with the stated district purpose of providing neighborhood commercial
services complemented by multi-family residential uses, while preserving the
character of the adjoining single family residential uses; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and particular hardships
associated with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance
with respect to the Subject Property in that: (a) the Subject Property is an
irregularly shaped parcel, with its north lot line abutting Winnetka Avenue,
its east lot line abutting Brier Street and its northeast lot line abutting
Green Bay Road; (b) the Subject Property lies in close proximity to single
family residential uses, with single family residential uses being located
immediately to the south of the Subject Property and a short distance to the
west of the Subject Property, with the residential uses to the west being
buffered by a small office building located on the parcel immediately to the
west of the Subject Property; (c) unlike other parcels in the commercial zoning
districts, the Subject Property has no adjacent on-street parking and the
nearest short-term public parking lot is on the east side of Green Bay Road,
south of Winnetka Avenue; (d) full compliance with setback requirements may
have a negative impact on pedestrian safety due to the narrowness of the
sidewalk adjoining the Winnetka Avenue and Green Bay Road street frontages of
the Subject Property and due to the presence of a traffic signal set in the
sidewalk along Green Bay Road; (e) the Subject Property is adjacent to narrow
public sidewalks and must allow for the safe accommodation of heavy pedestrian
traffic, due to its close proximity to New Trier High School and the Indian
Hill Metra train station; (f) the definition of “half-story” in the Zoning
Ordinance was designed to address single-family residential buildings and, as
applied to the Subject Property, has the unintended consequence of allowing
more building mass than the modified proposal, which is designed as and looks
like a 2-½ story building and does not exceed the lot coverage limitations for
the upper floor, although it is considered to be a 3-story building because the
area with a height of at least 7 feet or more is more than 50% of the area of
the part of the story lying directly below it; and (g) the unusual
configuration of both the Subject Property and the street intersection at which
it is located raise additional issues of traffic safety and ingress and egress;
and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that
requiring strict compliance with the half-story and building setback
requirements would require reducing the overall size of the proposed building
below the amount of floor area that would otherwise be permitted on the Subject
Property, would result in the loss of the balcony and building articulation
details while increasing the visual bulk of the building, or would require that
the number of residential units be reduced, all of which would negatively
impact on the economics of the proposed development of the Subject Property;
and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood, because the visual appearance of the proposed building is
consistent with the Zoning Ordinance’s height limitations of 35 feet and 2-½
stories; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed building will not impair an adequate supply of light
and air because the upper floors are set back from the street, the actual
measured height of the proposed building will comply with the 35-foot maximum
vertical height limitation, the third floor is set back further from the
property boundaries than the conforming third floor originally proposed by the
Applicants, and the removal of the arcade will provide more openness at the
street level; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property as the proposed building will comply with
all applicable building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not diminish the taxable value of
land and buildings in the immediate vicinity or throughout the Village as a
whole, and the taxable value of the Subject Property may be increased because
the proposed building will replace an abandoned gasoline service station and
will provide a visual and economic anchor for the Indian Hill commercial
district; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed building will not contribute to congestion on the
public streets, as the proposed building will provide several off-street
parking spaces to support the commercial space, the proposed use will generate
less traffic than was generated by the gasoline service station and illegal
parking lot that previously operated on the Subject Property, and the proposed
building will have an increased setback at the street level, which will provide
additional sidewalk space for pedestrian traffic; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed exterior parking at ground level will benefit the
public health, safety, comfort and welfare of the inhabitants of the Village by
providing parking that is visible from the street and adjacent to the
commercial use, thereby offsetting the absence of adjacent on-street parking
and conveniently located off-street parking; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed interior and exterior ground level parking will
further benefit the public health, safety, comfort and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village because it can be constructed without a full
foundation, which will allow a smaller area to be excavated and will reduce the
extent to which construction activity and equipment may need to intrude into
the Winnetka Avenue, Green Bay Road and Brier Street rights of way; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed interior and exterior ground level parking will
further enhance the public health, safety, comfort and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village in that it is more likely to preserve the trees
located on the property adjoining the Subject Property to the west, because the
excavation for foundations and footings for the building with parking at grade
level will not be as deep or as wide as that required for underground parking;
and
   WHEREAS, the proposed interior parking at ground level will further benefit
the public health, safety, comfort and welfare of the inhabitants of the
Village because it will allow vehicles exiting the interior parking area to
exit at the west end of the Subject Property away from the Winnetka Avenue-
Green Bay Road-Brier Street intersection, which will also direct exiting
traffic away from the single-family zoning district on Brier Street; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed ground level parking will
otherwise impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed interior and exterior parking at ground level will
help to reduce the amount of parking by commercial patrons on the adjoining
residential street and there is no evidence that the proposed ground level
parking, if constructed in accordance with the conditions set forth in this
ordinance, will be substantially injurious to the use and enjoyment of other
property in the immediate vicinity; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that construction of the proposed ground level
parking subject to the terms and conditions of this ordinance will impede the
normal and orderly development or improvement of other property in the
immediate vicinity; and
   WHEREAS, adequate measures have been taken to provide ingress and egress in
a manner that minimizes pedestrian and vehicular traffic congestion in the
public ways, in that the proposed off-street parking is configured so as to
avoid, to the extent possible, interfering with pedestrian and vehicular
traffic; and
   WHEREAS, adequate utilities, access roads, drainage and other facilities
necessary for the operation of the proposed special use on the Subject Property
will be provided when the proposed ground level parking is constructed in
compliance with applicable Village Code requirements; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed ground level parking, when constructed in accordance
with the terms and conditions of this ordinance, will conform to the applicable
regulations of the Zoning Ordinance and the Winnetka Village Code;
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain:
   SECTION 1: The foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the findings of
the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth herein.
   SECTION 2: Subject to the terms and conditions hereinafter set forth, the
following variations are hereby granted with respect to the Subject Property,
which is commonly known as 518 Winnetka Avenue, Winnetka, Illinois, and is
located in the C-1 Limited Retail Commercial District provided in Chapter 17.40
of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code: (a) a
variation from the maximum building height of 35 feet and 2-½ stories permitted
by Section 17.40.010.C of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, to allow the
construction of a 3-story building; (b) variations from the maximum permitted
setback of 3 feet set forth in Section 17.40.010.N of the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance, to allow the street level of the proposed building to observe a
setback of 4 feet along Winnetka Avenue, Green Bay Road and Brier Street, to
allow the second story to be set back 5 feet and to allow the third floor to be
set back 16.50 feet, resulting in variations of 6 feet, 2 feet and 13.50 feet,
respectively; and (c) a variation from the parking requirements set forth in
Section 17.40.010.K.1 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, to allow 20 parking
spaces rather than the 23 spaces required for this development, all as depicted
in the plans and specifications submitted with the application for the
variations and special use, dated March 2000, and modified for submission to
the Zoning Board of Appeals on July 10, 2000, and as further modified in
accordance with Section 3 of this ordinance.
   SECTION 3: Pursuant to Section 17.40.010.K.11 of the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance, a special use permit is hereby granted to the Subject Property,
which is commonly known as 518 Winnetka Avenue, Winnetka, Illinois, and is
located in the C-2 General Retail Commercial District provided in Chapter 17.40
of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, to
permit 20 parking spaces to be located at the street level, (a) subject to the
condition that the Applicants submit a revised parking plan that addresses, to
the satisfaction and approval of the Village Engineer, the conditions specified
in the Village Engineer’s memo of July 3 2000, and (b) further subject to the
terms and conditions hereinafter set forth.
   SECTION 4: The variations and special use permit hereby granted are
conditioned upon the installation of landscaping and lighting pursuant to final
landscaping and lighting plans that shall meet the following standards:
      A.   The final lighting plan shall be subject to the final approval of
the Village Council after review and comment by the Design Review Board. The
final lighting plan shall be designed so as to safely illuminate the premises
while minimizing the spillage of light to adjoining properties. The final
lighting plan shall include a detailed description of all exterior lighting
fixtures, including the height of the fixtures and the intensity of light they
generate, and a description of all steps taken to mitigate the impact of
lighting on adjoining properties, including proposed hours of operation,
direction of light and any light filtering or baffling devices to be used.
      B.   The final landscaping plan shall be subject to the final approval of
the Village Council after review and comment by the Forestry Commission and the
Design Review Board. The final landscaping plan shall use materials approved by
the Village Forester and shall be designed so as to enhance the appearance of
the proposed building and to provide a visual buffer for the adjoining single
family residential property owners, without interfering with sight lines for
traffic exiting the two parking areas. The final landscaping plan shall also
provide a planting strip up to three feet wide along the Winnetka Avenue and
Brier Street frontages of the Subject Property.
   SECTION 5: The variations and special uses hereby granted are further
subject to the condition that parking in at least four of the exterior parking
spaces be made available for patrons of the commercial space on the Subject
Property between the hours of 8:00 a.m. and 6:00 p.m., Monday through Friday,
with the duration of such parking being limited to periods of up to 90 minutes.
The owner of the Subject Property shall be responsible for enforcing the
foregoing parking limitations, including the posting of appropriate signs. In
the event that title to the Subject Property is to be conveyed to a condominium
association, the conditions set forth in this section shall be included in the
condominium declaration and the condominium association shall be responsible
for complying with said conditions.
   SECTION 6: The variations and special use hereby granted are further
conditioned upon the contract purchaser, as developer of the Subject Property,
signing a development agreement with the Village, as required by subsection K
of Section 15.32.080 of the Winnetka Village Code, within 12 months of the date
of this ordinance.
   SECTION 7: That the variation hereby granted is conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within twelve months after the
effective date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 8: In the event that any provision of this ordinance conflicts with
any other ordinance of the Village, the provisions of this ordinance shall
prevail.
   SECTION 9: That this Ordinance shall be in full force and effect from and
after its passage, approval, and posting.
   Passed and approved this 1st day of August, 2000.
   AYES:      Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Lien, Presser and Woodbury
   NAYS:   None
   ABSENT:   Trustee Powell
               Signed:
                  /ss/Louise A. Holland
                  Village President
Countersigned:
/ss/Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced June 6, 2000
Posted June 7, 2000
Passed and Approved August 1, 2000
Posted August 2, 2000
ORDINANCE NO. M-17-2000
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (595 Orchard Lane)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
Lot 3 (except the North 39.7 feet thereof), in Skinner’s Subdivision of the
East 136 feet of the South 164.7 feet of Block 9 in John C. Garland’s addition
to Winnetka, a Subdivision of the North 120 acres of the Southwest ¼ of Section
21, Township 42 North, Range 13, East of the Third Principal Meridian, in Cook
County, Illinois.
commonly known as 595 Orchard Lane, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
5 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.12 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for the
following variations: (a) from the setback requirements of Section 17.12.010.H
of the Zoning Ordinance to permit a front yard setback of 24.25 feet, whereas a
30-foot setback is required, resulting in a variation of 5.75 feet (19.17%);
and (b) from the side yard requirements of Section 17.12.010.I of the Zoning
Ordinance to permit an east side yard of 1.85 feet, whereas a 6-foot side yard
is required, resulting in a variation of 4.15 feet (69.17%), all for the
purpose of replacing the deteriorating wood-frame foundation under the bay
windows on the east and south sides of the residence on the Subject Property,
and rebuilding the existing chimney and screen porch, both of which encroach in
the east side yard; and
   WHEREAS, on May 8, 2000, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of Appeals
conducted a public hearing on the requested variation and by the unanimous vote
of the five members then present, has reported to the Council recommending that
the requested variations be granted; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and unique hardships associated
with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect
to the Subject Property in that: (a) the single-family residence was
constructed in 1916 before current zoning regulations and, due to the presence
of the bay windows, has a legal nonconforming front setback of 24.25 feet and a
legal nonconforming east side yard of 1.85 feet; (b) there is no conforming
alternative for the proposed work, which is intended to repair minor structural
deficiencies in certain portions of the residence while maintaining the
existing building dimensions and building location; and (c) bringing the
Subject Property into full compliance would require either permanently removing
portions of the residence or demolishing the entire structure and replacing it
with new construction, either of which is likely to alter the historic
appearance of the Subject Property as a whole; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that
the existing wood frame foundation supporting the bay windows, the existing
chimney and the existing screen porch have all become structurally unsound and
removing them to make the east side yard and front yard conform with the Zoning
Ordinance would require major design and structural modifications and would
impose additional costs on the owners, while reducing the value of the Subject
Property because it would reduce the size of the building, decrease its
utility, an alter its appearance; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because the proposed work will maintain the existing building
dimensions and location and will also retain the original building design; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not impair an adequate supply of
light and air because it will maintain the existing building dimensions and the
location of the building on the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property as it will comply with all applicable
building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not diminish the taxable value of
land and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed repairs and reconstruction will not contribute to
congestion on the public streets, as the property will continue to be used for
a single-family occupancy; and
WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed variations will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village.
WHEREAS, the requested variations are in harmony with the general purpose and
intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that it allows the renovation,
restoration and rehabilitation of a structurally sound existing building while
maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the community and protecting
established trees and landscaping.
NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1: That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2: That the following variations are hereby granted to the Subject
Property, which is commonly known as 595 Orchard Lane, Winnetka, Illinois, and
is located in the R-5 Single Family Residential Zoning District provided in
Chapter 17.12 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka
Village Code: (a) from the setback requirements of Section 17.12.010.H of the
Zoning Ordinance to permit a front yard setback of 24.25 feet, whereas a 30-
foot setback is required, resulting in a variation of 5.75 feet (19.17%); and
(b) from the side yard requirements of Section 17.12.010.I of the Zoning
Ordinance to permit an east side yard of 1.85 feet, whereas a 6-foot side yard
is required, resulting in a variation of 4.15 feet (69.17%), all for the
purpose of replacing the deteriorating wood-frame foundation under the bay
windows on the east and south sides of the residence on the Subject Property,
and rebuilding the existing chimney and screen porch on the Subject Property,
which encroach in the east side yard, said work to be done in accordance with
the plans and drawings submitted with the application for variations.
   SECTION 3: That the variations hereby granted is conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed constructing within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 20th day of June, 2000, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES: Trustees Brower, Lien, Powell, Presser and Woodbury
   NAYS: None
   ABSENT: Trustee Aquilino
   APPROVED this 20th day of June, 2000.
   Signed:
   /ss/Louise A. Holland
   Village President
Countersigned:
/ss/ Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: June 6, 2000
Posted: June 7, 2000
Passed and Approved: June 20, 2000
Posted: June 21, 2000
ORDINANCE NO. M-18-2000
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (911 Euclid Avenue)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owners of the following described
real estate (the “Subject Property”):
The South 80 feet of the West 12 feet of the North 65 feet of the South 145
feet of the following tract; taken as a single tract of land to wit: Lot 16
(except the West 179.75 feet thereof and except that part dedicated for Euclid
Avenue) in Clarke’s Subdivision of part of the Northwest ¼ of the Northwest ¼
of Section 17, Township 42 North, Range 13 East of the Third Principal
Meridian, in Cook County, Illinois,
commonly known as 911 Euclid Avenue, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
3 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.20 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for variation
by ordinance from Section 17.20.010.I to allow the construction of a new
single-family residence which is proposed to observe total sideyards of 20.00
feet, a variation of 12.28 feet (38.04%) from the 32.28-foot total sideyard
required for the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, on May 8, 2000, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of Appeals
conduct a hearing on the application, at the end of which a motion was made to
recommend that the Village Council grant the requested variation; and
   WHEREAS, because only 3 of the 5 members of the Zoning Board of Appeals
present at the meeting on May 8, 2000, voted in favor of the motion to
recommend, the application was forward for action by the Village Council,
without a recommendation from the Zoning Board of Appeals; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and unique hardships associated
with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect
to the Subject Property in that: (a) the Subject Property is a flag lot; (b)
the only actual street frontage is at the north end of the “flagpole” portion
of the lot, which is only 12 feet wide; (c) the body of the Subject Property is
a rectangle measuring approximately 80 feet by 224.70 feet, with the narrower
dimension of the lot being parallel to the east and west right-of-way lines of
Euclid Avenue; (d) because of the orientation of the body of the Subject
Property, the west portion of the lot has been designated as the front yard of
the Subject Property; (e) due to the designation of the front yard, the
sideyards are located along the north and south lot lines of the Subject
Property, although the dimensions of the sideyards have been calculated based
on the actual average lot width, which considers both the 12-foot width of the
flag pole and the 224.70-foot east-west dimension of the Subject Property; and
(f) because of the method prescribed by the Zoning Ordinance for the
calculation of the average lot width and the determination of the total
sideyard requirement, the required total sideyard is 32.28 feet; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, because
the reduced dimensions of the buildable area on the Subject Property would
result in orienting the new house toward the sideyard of the property to the
north and would require the removal of several mature trees currently located
in the rear yard of the Subject Property, which would unreasonably reduce the
value of the Subject Property by reducing the utility and appeal of the
proposed new home and by changing the character of the Subject Property from
what is now a wooded lot; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because the design of the proposed new residence is consistent
with the design, scale and orientation of the other single-family properties in
the immediate vicinity along Euclid Avenue, all of which are characterized by
residences oriented near the front end of the lot because the proposed new
residence will observe a total sideyard of 20 feet, which is 25% of the north-
south dimension of the Subject Property, which functions as the lot width, and
because the proposed new residence is situated on the Subject Property so as to
preserve the mature stand of trees in the rear yard; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not impair an adequate supply of light
and air because the proposed sideyards will exceed the minimum individual
sideyard requirements for the zoning district and the proposed total sideyard
requirement of 20 feet would satisfy the total sideyard requirement of 25% of
the lot width, if the lot width used the 80-foot dimension that is consistent
with the functional lot width of the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property as it will comply with all applicable
building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not contribute to congestion on the
public streets, as the Subject Property will continue to be used as the site of
a single-family residence; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the construction of a new single-family
home will otherwise impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and
welfare of the inhabitants of the Village.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2: That a variation is hereby granted to the Subject Property,
commonly known as 911 Euclid Avenue, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
3 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.20 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for a
variation by ordinance from Section 17.20.010.I to allow the construction of a
new single-family residence that will observe a total setback of 20.00 feet, a
decrease of 12.28 feet (38.04%) from the required 32.28-foot total yard setback
on the Subject Property, said construction to be in accordance with the plans
and specifications submitted with the application.
   SECTION 3: That the variation hereby granted is conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 6th day of July, 2000, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES: Trustees Brower, Aquilino, Lien, Woodbury and Powell
   NAYS: None
   ABSENT: Trustee Presser
   APPROVED this 6th day of July, 2000.
   Signed:
   /ss/Louise A. Holland
   Village President
Countersigned:
/ss/Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
