ORDINANCE NO. M-20-2000 (Withdrawn)
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (680-690 Elder Lane)
   WHEREAS, the owner of the following described Parcel 1, who is also the
contract purchaser of the following described Parcel 2 (hereinafter
collectively referred to as the “Subject Property”):
Parcel 1: Lot 1 (except the south 17.56 feet thereof and except the North 58
feet of the East 124.45 feet thereof) and the West 6.55 feet (except the North
58 feet) of Lot 4 in Lydia D. Sutters Subdivision of part of the West ½ of
Block 4 in John C. Garland’s Addition to the Village of Winnetka (including
street) being that part of said Block 4 lying West of a line 436.55 feet East
of and parallel with center line of Forest Avenue in the South West ¼ of
Section 21, Township 42 North, Range 13 East of the Third Principal Meridian,
in Cook County, Illinois,
commonly known as 690 Elder Lane, Winnetka, Illinois, and
Parcel 2: The North 58.00 feet of the West 6.55 feet of Lot 4 and the North
58.00 feet of the East 124.45 feet of lot 1 in Lydia D. Sutter’s Subdivision of
part of the West ½ of Block 4 of John C. Garland’s addition to the Village of
Winnetka (including streets) being that part of said block 4 lying West of line
436.55 East of and parallel with the center line of Forest Avenue, in the
Southwest ¼ of Section 21, Township 42 North, Range 13, East of the Third
Principal Meridian, in Cook County, Illinois,
commonly known as 680 Elder Lane, Winnetka, Illinois, which are adjoining
parcels of property located in the R-5 Single-Family Residential Zoning
District provided in the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17, Winnetka Village
Code, has submitted an application for the following variations: (a) the
building height limitations of Section 17.12.010(C) of the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance, to allow an existing accessory building to maintain a height of 23
feet and 2 stories, which exceeds the accessory building height limitations of
14 feet and 1 story, resulting in a variance of 9 feet (64.3%); and (b) a
variation from the rear yard setback requirements of Section 17.12.010(J) of
the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance to allow the existing single-family residence and
attached wood deck located, respectively, 12.91 feet and 6.25 feet from the
south lot line, both of which are less than the 19.87-foot required rear yard,
resulting in a rear yard encroachment of up to 13.62 feet (68.55%), said
variations arising from the consolidation of Parcels 1 and 2 into the Subject
Property in conjunction with the proposed construction of a three-car garage
and one-story kitchen addition with an open front porch on the Subject
Property; and
   WHEREAS, Parcel 1 is a flag lot having a street frontage of 17.59 feet along
the south right-of-way line of Elder Lane with access to the body of the
flaglot by way of a driveway that is 17.59 feet wide and extends southward from
the south right-of-way line of Elder Lane for a distance of 58 feet; and
   WHEREAS, Parcel 2 has a street frontage of 131 feet along the south right-
of-way line of Elder Lane, and adjoins the east side of the “flagpole” portion
of Parcel 1 and the north side of the body of Parcel 1; and
   WHEREAS, Parcel 1 is improved with a 2½ story brick residence and a frame
garage and Parcel 2 is improved with a brick coach house residence; and
   WHEREAS, the applicants have entered into a contract to purchase Parcel 2
and desire to consolidate it with Parcel 1 as part of a project that proposes
to construct a kitchen addition and new front porch for the brick residence, to
remove the frame garage now on Parcel 1, and to restore the coach house to its
original size and character, for use as a three-car garage with garage doors
that face west, towards the interior of the property, rather than toward the
street; and
   WHEREAS, on April 26, 2000, the Plan Commission considered the proposed
consolidation and recommended approval of the preliminary Plat of Consolidation
subject to (1) approval of zoning variations in order to permit nonconformities
arising from the consolidation, (2) the granting of a utility easement along
the north line of the Subject Property, and (3) the removal of the existing
garage on Parcel 2 and portions of the existing residence on Parcel 1, as
specified on the site development plan, before recording the Plat of
Subdivision; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed garage is existing and currently observes a legally
nonconforming front setback of 4.75 feet rather than the required 30-foot
setback and, upon conversion from its existing use to a three-car garage, will
be nearer the street line than the primary structure; and
   WHEREAS, pursuant to Section 16.12.010(D)(3) of the Subdivision Regulations
of Chapter 16.12 of the Winnetka Village Code, the Plan Commission found that
the proposed consolidation, with the resulting nonconforming location of the
coach house, would not have a materially increased adverse impact; and
   WHEREAS, the reconfiguration of lot lines resulting from the proposed
consolidation will create a nonconformity in the location of a portion of the
house and an existing wood deck, which are located respectively, 12.91 feet and
6.25 feet from the south lot line, and thus encroach up to 13.62 feet into the
required rear yard of 19.87 feet; and
   WHEREAS, the existing height of the coach house now located on Parcel 2 is
23 feet and 2 stories which, upon consolidation, will exceed the 14-foot, one-
story height limitation for accessory buildings; and
   WHEREAS, on June 12, 2000, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested rear yard and building
height variations and has reported to the Council as follows: (a) by the
unanimous vote of the six members then present, recommending that the requested
rear yard variation be granted, and (b) by the favorable vote of 4 of the 6
members then in attendance, recommending that the requested building height
variance be granted; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and unique hardships associated
with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect
to the Subject Property in that: (1) Parcels 1 and 2 were originally a single
lot and the coach house was designed in the same architectural character and
detailing as the main house, which was constructed in 1912; (2) the
nonconformities created by the proposed consolidation result from existing
structures that pre-date the Village’s Zoning Ordinance, and from the prior
subdivision of the Subject Property into Parcels 1 and 2; and (3) the proposed
consolidation will restore the Subject Property to its original configuration
and will remove an undesirable flag lot; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that
the two component parcels are not of sufficient size or orientation to warrant
significant improvement individually, the consolidated Subject Property cannot
be brought into full conformity without removing a portion of the existing
house and substantially reducing the height of the coach house, all of which
would negatively impact the Subject Property without providing an offsetting
public benefit; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because the improvements that are proposed to be made in
conjunction with the consolidation and variations will restore the original
character of the Subject Property as a single lot with an architecturally
similar single-family residence and coach house; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not impair an adequate supply of
light and air because all of the variations result from existing structures and
their heights and locations will not be altered; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property, as all building alterations will comply
with all applicable building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not diminish the taxable value of
land and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not contribute to congestion on the
public streets, as the Subject Property will be restored to a single parcel
with a single principal use, rather than the two single-family residences now
on the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed requested variations will
otherwise impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2: That the following variations are hereby granted to the Subject
Property, commonly known as 680-690 Elder Lane, Winnetka, Illinois, which is
located in the R-5 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17, Winnetka Village Code: (a) a variation
from the building height limitations of Section 17.12.010(C) of the Winnetka
Zoning Ordinance, to allow an existing accessory building to maintain a height
of 23 feet and 2 stories, which exceeds the accessory building height
limitations of 14 feet and 1 story, resulting in a variance of 9 feet (64.3%);
and (b) a variation from the rear yard setback requirements of Section
17.12.010(J) of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance to allow the existing single-
family residence and attached wood deck located, respectively, 12.91 feet and
6.25 feet from the south lot line, both of which are less than the 19.87-foot
required rear yard, resulting in a rear yard encroachment of up to 13.62 feet
(68.55%), said variations arising from the consolidation of Parcels 1 and 2
into the Subject Property in conjunction with the proposed construction of a
three-car garage and one-story kitchen addition with an open front porch on the
Subject Property.
   SECTION 3: That the variations hereby granted are conditioned upon the
completion and approval of the final Plat of Consolidation for the Subject
Property and upon the commencement of the proposed construction, both within 12
months after the effective date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this _________ day of ______________________, 2000, pursuant to the
following roll call vote:
   AYES:       
   NAYS:       
   ABSENT:       
   APPROVED this __________ day of ____________________, 2000.
   Signed:
            
   Village President
Countersigned:
_________________________________________________
Village Clerk
