ORDINANCE NO. M-21-2000
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS FOR THE WINNETKA PUBLIC SCHOOL
NURSERY (1155 OAK STREET)
   WHEREAS, the Board of Education of Winnetka Elementary School District No.
36 is the beneficial owner of the following described real estate (the “Subject
Property”):
Vacated Blocks 9, 10, 11 and 12 (including certain streets and alleys) of The
Winnetka Land Association Subdivision in the Northwest ¼ of Section 20, being
the North ½ of the West 90 Acres of the Northwest ¼ of Section 20, Township 42
North, Range 13 East of the 3rd Principal Meridian, in the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois.
commonly known as 1155 Oak Street, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
4 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance Chapter 17, Winnetka Village Code; and
   WHEREAS, the Board of Education of Winnetka Elementary School District No.
36 (“Board of Education”) is a body corporate and politic of the State of
Illinois and owns and operates the Skokie and Washburne Schools and other
related activities on the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, the Board of Education leases a part of the Subject Property to the
Winnetka Public School Nursery for the operation of a pre-school program that
is licensed by the Department of Children and Family Services (“DCFS”) and is
accredited by the National Association for the Education of Young Children
(“NAEYC”); and
   WHEREAS, public schools are permitted as special uses in the R-4 Single-
Family Residential District, subject to the conditions and requirements
pertaining to special uses, as set forth in Chapter 17.56 of the Winnetka
Zoning Ordinance; and
   WHEREAS, the Winnetka Public School Nursery and the Board of Education have
filed an application for the following variations from the provisions of
Chapter 17.16 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka
Village Code: (a) from the maximum building size provisions of Section
17.16.010(G) to permit a total gross floor area of 212,721.00 square feet, an
increase of 67 square feet over the existing nonconforming gross floor area of
212,654.00 square feet, whereas 124,531.13 square feet are permitted, resulting
in a variation of 88,189.87 square feet (70.80%), and (b) from the maximum
building height provisions of Section 17.16.010(C) to permit an accessory
building to have a height of 8 feet, whereas 7 feet is permitted, resulting in
a variation of one foot (14.29%), all to allow the construction of two
replacement storage sheds to be connected with a 14-foot long climbing wall in
the outdoor play area of the public school nursery building located on the
Subject Property, in accordance with the revised plans accompanying the
application for variation; and,
   WHEREAS, on June 22, 2000, the Design Review Board considered the proposed
improvements and have unanimously determined that a certificate of
appropriateness would issue for the proposed storage sheds; and
   WHEREAS, pursuant to due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of Appeals
conducted a public hearing on the requested variations on July 10, 2000, and by
the unanimous vote of the six members then present, has reported to the Council
recommending that the requested variations be granted; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed replacement of the storage sheds, including the
addition of the climbing wall, on the existing outdoor play area is an
accessory to the Subject Property’s primary use and does not require an
amendment to the special use permit; and
   WHEREAS, there are unique circumstances and practical difficulties
associated with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance
with respect to these premises in that (a) the changes in storage and the
addition of the climbing wall are part of renovations being made to the play
area in order to comply with requirements of DCFS, NAEYC, and the Americans
with Disabilities Act; (b) neither the existing storage sheds nor 7-foot high
storage sheds have sufficient capacity to meet changes in the requirements
contained in DCFS licensing standards and NAEYC accreditation standards; and
(c) all of the storage must be provided within the portion of the Subject
Property leased by the Winnetka Public Nursery School; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not alter the essential character of
the locality because the proposed storage sheds will increase the nonconforming
gross floor area by 67 square feet, which is minimal, and have been designed to
be consistence with the existing sheds that are being replaced; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed construction will not impair an adequate supply of
light and air to adjacent property, as the storage sheds meet all setback
requirements and the one foot of excess height is small in scale compared to
the primary structure on the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the applicable zoning
regulations, as the sole purpose of the property is to provide for the
education of students as required by law; and
   WHEREAS, the hazard from fire and other damages to the property will not be
increased, because the new equipment will have no wood; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the taxable value of land and buildings
throughout the Village will diminish; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed renovation of the playground equipment will not
increase congestion in the public streets; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed playground equipment will not be detrimental to or
endanger the public health, safety, comfort, morals and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village, because the new equipment will provide a safer
playground, which will be in full compliance with current safety standards and
guidelines and will meet accessibility requirements of the Americans with
Disabilities Act.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain:
   SECTION 1: That the following variations are hereby granted with respect to
the Subject Property, which is located in the R-4 Single Family Residential
Zoning District, and which is commonly know as the Skokie School property, 1155
Oak Street: (a) a variation from the maximum building size provisions of
Section 17.16.010(G) of Chapter 17.16 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title
17 of the Winnetka Village Code, to permit (a) a total gross floor area of
212,721.00 square feet, an increase of 67 square feet over the existing
nonconforming gross floor area of 212,654.00 square feet, whereas 124,531.13
square feet are permitted, resulting in a variation of 88,189.87 square feet
(70.80%), and (b) a variation from the maximum building height provisions of
Section 17.16.010(C) of Chapter 17.16 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title
17 of the Winnetka Village Code, to permit an accessory building height of 8
feet, whereas 7 feet is permitted, resulting in a variation of one foot
(14.29%), all to allow the construction of two replacement storage sheds to be
connected with a 14-foot long climbing wall in the outdoor play area of the
public school nursery building located on the Subject Property, in accordance
with the revised plans accompanying the application for variation
   SECTION 2: That the variations hereby granted are conditioned upon the
commencement of commencement of the proposed construction within twelve months
after the effective date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 3: That this Ordinance shall be in full force and effect from and
after its passage, approval, and posting.
   ADOPTED this 5th day of September, 2000, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES: Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Lien, Powell and Woodbury
   NAYS: None
   ABSENT: Trustee Presser
   APPROVED this 5th day of September, 2000.
   Signed:
   /ss/Louise A. Holland
   Village President
Countersigned:
/ss/Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: August 15, 2000
Posted: August 16, 2000
Passed and Approved: September 5, 2000
Posted: September 6, 2000
ORDINANCE NO. M-22-2000
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (475 Ash Street)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
   That part of a tract of land, said tract being all that part of Lot six (6)
of Graves Subdivision in Winnetka as recorded January 31, 1882, in Book of
Plats 16, Page 71, as Document 372637, and part of vacated Fairview Avenue
(formerly Graves Place) vacated by Ordinance recorded May 23, 1918, as Document
6328904, Book 13299, Page 490, described as follows: Beginning at a point in
the South line of said Lot six (6), being the North line of Ash Street and
fifty-seven (57) feet East of the South West corner of said Lot six (6); thence
North along a straight line one hundred eighty-six and five tenths (196.5) feet
to a point in a line midway between the North line of said Ash Street and the
South line of Cherry Street, said point being fifty-seven (57) feet East (as
measured on said midway line) from the West line of said Lot six (6); thence
East of said midway line forty-eight (48) feet to a point one hundred eighty-
six and four tenths (186.4) feet North of the South line of said Lot six (6);
thence North Easterly along a straight line fifty-five and eighty-five
hundredths (55.85) feet to a point in the Easterly line of said Lot six (6),
forty-five (45) feet North Westerly as measured along the Easterly line of said
lot six (6) from the intersection of said midway line extended East with
Easterly line of said Lot six (6); thence North Easterly along said North
Easterly line extended in a straight line thirty and two one hundredths (30.02)
feet to the middle line of said Fairview Avenue as vacated; thence South
Easterly along said middle line and said middle line extended and parallel with
the Easterly line of said Fairview Avenue as vacated one hundred seventy-four
and two tenths (174.2) feet to a point in the Northerly line of Ash Street as
extended from the East. Thence South Westerly along said Northerly line of Ash
Street as extended from the East and said Northerly line of Ash Street, being
the Southerly line of said Lot six (6), two hundred sixty and forty-two
hundredths (260.42) feet to a point of beginning, lying Easterly of a straight
line passing through a point in the Southerly line of said above described
tract and one hundred (100) feet as measured along the Southerly line of said
tract South Westerly from the South East corner of said tract and said straight
line passing through a point in the Northerly line of said above described
tract and seventy-seven (77) feet (as measured along the Northerly line of said
tract) Southwesterly from the North Easterly corner of said tract, all in the
North Half Fractional Section twenty-one (21), Township forty-two (42) North,
Range thirteen (13), East of the Third Principal Meridian, in Cook County,
Illinois.
commonly known as 475 Ash Street, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
4 Single Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.16 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for a
variation from the maximum building size limitations of Section 17.16.010(G) to
allow a total gross floor area of 5,087.38 square feet, whereas 4,921.16 square
feet is permitted, resulting in a variation of 166.22 square feet (3.38%), for
the purpose of constructing a permanent screened porch on the same footprint as
an existing nonconforming, temporary one-story screen porch on the Subject
Property; and
   WHEREAS, on July 10, 2000, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variation and by the
favorable vote and one abstention of the six members then present, has reported
to the Council recommending that the requested variation be granted; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and particular hardships
associated with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance,
which would prohibit the proposed construction of a permanent, enclosed porch
of the same area, height and location of an existing, nonconforming, temporary
screened porch that was constructed in 1990 and is removed each fall and
reinstalled each spring; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that
over the past 10 years, the temporary porch area has been incorporated into the
function of the interior of the home and installing a permanent porch feature
will reduce wear and deterioration of the home’s connecting elements which are
caused by the annual installation and removal of the temporary porch; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because the proposed permanent screened porch, which is
located along the side of the house and behind an attached garage, is not
visible from the street and there is a mature landscaping buffer between the
Subject Property and the adjoining property; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not impair an adequate supply of light
and air because the construction of the proposed permanent screened porch will
maintain the size, height, location and dimensions of the temporary structure
it is intended to replace; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property as it will comply with all applicable
building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the permanent nature of the proposed
improvement; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed permanent screened porch will not contribute to
congestion on the public streets, as the use of the Subject Property will not
be changed; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed permanent screened porch
will otherwise impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare
of the inhabitants of the Village.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2:  That a variation is hereby granted with respect to the Subject
Property, commonly known as 475 Ash Street, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in
the R-4 Single Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.16 of
the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, said
variation being from the maximum building size limitations of Section 17.16.010
(G) to allow a total gross floor area of 5,087.38 square feet, whereas 4,921.16
square feet is permitted, resulting in a variation of 166.22 square feet
(3.38%), for the purpose of constructing a permanent screened porch on the same
footprint as an existing nonconforming, temporary one-story screen porch on the
Subject Property.
   SECTION 3: That the variation hereby granted is conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 5th day of September, 2000, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Lien, Powell and Woodbury
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    Trustee Presser
   APPROVED this 5th day of September, 2000.
   Signed:
   /ss/Louise A. Holland
   Village President
