ORDINANCE NO. M-24-2000
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA, COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (446 Provident)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
Lot 2 in Block 10 in Provident Mutual Land Association Subdivision of Blocks 7
to 12, 28 to 33 and 54 to 59, all inclusive, in the Village of Winnetka, being
a subdivision of the West ½ of the N.E. ¼ of Sec. 20. T. 42 N. – R. 13 E. of
the 3rd P.M. in Cook County, Illinois,
commonly known as 446 Provident, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
5 Single Family Residential District provided in Chapter 17.12 of the Winnetka
Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for the following
variations: (a) a variation from the intensity of use of lot limitations of
Section 17.12.010(F), to allow a roofed lot coverage of 2,051.69 square feet,
whereas 1,909.38 square feet are permitted, resulting in a variation of 146.45
square feet (7.67%); (b) a variation from the side yard requirements of Section
17.12.010(I), to allow a north side yard of 5.26 feet, whereas a side yard of
6.00 feet is required, resulting in a variation of 0.74 feet (12.33%); and (c)
a variation from the building line articulation requirements of Section
17.12.010(Q) to allow the legally nonconforming 42.60-foot long north building
line to be extended vertically, all of said variations being requested for the
purpose of adding a second story to the existing one-story residence on the
Subject Property and constructing a one-story addition at the rear of the
existing residence, in accordance with the drawings and plans submitted with
the application for variations; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and particular hardships
associated with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance
with respect to the Subject Property in that: (a) the one-story residence on
the Subject Property was built in 1916, before the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance
was first enacted; (b) both the north and south building lines of the
residence, as originally built, are 42.60 feet long, which exceeds the maximum
allowable length of unarticulated building line by 2.60 feet; (c) the north
building line is not quite parallel to the north lot line, the northwest corner
of the existing residence being located 5.60 feet away from the north lot line
and the northeast corner of the residence being located 5.26 feet away from the
north lot line; (d) the Subject Property is approximately 47 feet wide and 162
feet long, and the combined effect of the location of the existing house and
the narrowness of the lot limit the ability to add to the house, although
maximum building size regulations would allow nearly 1,600 additional square
feet of gross floor area to be added to the Subject Property, which currently
has a gross floor area of 1,339.69 square feet; (e) the proposed additions are
intended to bring the home up to modern standards by adding three bedrooms and
a bathroom to the second floor and by expanding and remodeling the kitchen on
the first floor; (f) the existing configuration of the house is such that the
only way the kitchen could be expanded within the roofed lot coverage limits
would be to remove the garage, which is an essential accessory to single family
residences in this area; and (g) the only way to make the second floor addition
conform with articulation and side yard requirements would be to set one
portion of the proposed second floor 6 inches back from the existing north
building line, and to set the remaining portion back a full 2 feet, which is
prohibitively expensive and is structurally and aesthetically undesirable; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that
existing kitchen cannot accommodate either an eating area or such basic kitchen
appliances as a refrigerator, and the existing gross floor area is
substantially less than what is allowed on the Subject Property and less than
what exists on comparably sized properties in the area; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood, in that the proposed second floor addition will be consistent
with the style of the existing house and will comply with building height
limitations, and the first floor addition is located entirely behind the house,
so it will not impact the streetscape; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not impair an adequate supply of
light and air, because the additions conform with building height requirements
and the new second floor will observe the same setbacks as the existing one-
story building; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property, as the proposed additions will comply
with all applicable building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not diminish the taxable value of
land and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed additions; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not contribute to congestion in the
public streets, as the structure will continue to be used as a single family
residence; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the requested variations will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village.
   WHEREAS, on July 10, 2000, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variations and by the
unanimous vote of the six members then present, has reported to the Council
recommending that the variations be granted.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain:
   SECTION 1: That the foregoing recitals are incorporated as the findings of
the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth herein.
   SECTION 2: That the following variations are hereby granted with respect to
the Subject Property, commonly known as 446 Provident, which is located in the
R-5 Single Family Residential District provided in Chapter 17.12 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code: (a) a
variation from the intensity of use of lot limitations of Section 17.12.010(F),
to allow a roofed lot coverage of 2,051.69 square feet, whereas 1,909.38 square
feet are permitted, resulting in a variation of 146.45 square feet (7.67%); (b)
a variation from the side yard requirements of Section 17.12.010(I), to allow a
north side yard of 5.26 feet, whereas a side yard of 6.00 feet is required,
resulting in a variation of 0.74 feet (12.33%); and (c) a variation from the
building line articulation requirements of Section 17.12.010(Q) to allow the
existing nonconforming north building line to be extended vertically, all of
said variations being requested for the purpose of allowing the addition of a
second story to the existing one-story residence on the Subject Property, and
constructing a one-story addition at the rear of the existing residence, in
accordance with the drawings and plans submitted with the application for
variations.
   SECTION 3: That the variations hereby granted are conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: That this Ordinance shall be in full force and effect from and
after its passage, approval, and posting.
   ADOPTED this 5th day of September, 2000, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES: Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Lien, Powell and Woodbury
   NAYS: None
   ABSENT: Trustee Presser
   APPROVED this 5th day of September, 2000.
   Signed:
   /ss/Louise A. Holland
   Village President
Countersigned:
/ss/Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: August 15, 2000
Posted: August 16, 2000
Passed and Approved: September 5, 2000
Posted: September 6, 2000
ORDINANCE NO. M-25-2000
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (275 Auburn)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
Lot 24 in Willowdale being a consolidation of all the lots and vacated alleys
in Blocks 3, 4, 7 and 8 (except Lots 1 to 11 in Block 4 and except Lots 10 to
39 in Block 8) together with vacated streets or vacated parts thereof adjoining
said Lots and Blocks in Vernamo, a Subdivision of the Northwest ¼ of Section
20, Township 42 North, Range 13, East of the Third Principal Meridian, and the
East 1/3 of the Northeast ¼ of the Southeast ¼ of the Southwest ¼ of Section
19, Township 42 North, Range 13, East of the Third Principal Meridian, also
that part of vacated Sunset Road in Wynwood Manor, a Subdivision of the North
1/3 of the Southwest ¼ of the southwest ¼ of said Section 20, lying West of the
center line of Auburn Avenue in said Vernamo Subdivision, extending South, in
the Village of Winnetka, Cook County, Illinois,
commonly known as 275 Auburn, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-4 Single
Family Residential District provided in Chapter 17.16 of the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for a variation from the
building line articulation requirements of Section 17.16.010(Q) to allow an
existing 15.85-foot by 9.7-foot one-story addition and a shed dormer to be
replaced with a 16-foot by 18.3-foot two-story addition at the northeast corner
of the existing residence on the Subject Property, in accordance with the
drawings and plans submitted with the application for variations; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and particular hardships
associated with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance
with respect to the Subject Property in that: (a) the residence on the Subject
Property was constructed in 1961, before the aggregate side yard and building
line articulation requirements were added to the Zoning Ordinance; (b) the
interior configuration of the residence is such that the only suitable location
for the proposed addition is at the northeast corner of the building; (c) the
proposed addition will not increase the nonconformity in the existing, legally
nonconforming aggregate side yard, which is approximately 3 feet less than the
required 20 feet; (d) the existing north building line is 37 feet long and does
not require articulation; (e) the proposed north building line would be 45.88
feet long, which exceeds the 40-foot maximum allowable unarticulated length;
(f) in order to fully conform with the articulation requirement, it would be
necessary to remove 7 feet of the existing building and to align the new
construction with it, which would require new foundations to be poured, would
shorten the garage, and would result in a smaller, misaligned addition; (g) the
8.51-foot north side yard proposed for the first floor of the addition is the
same dimension as the current north side yard, and is larger than the minimum
side yards that would be required for a new residence; and (h) the second floor
of the proposed addition will be set back more than 2 feet from the north
building line of the floor below; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that
the proposed variation is being requested to add an accessible bedroom suite
and accessory spaces to the first floor and to add improvements to the master
suite on the second floor, all within the size limitations for the Subject
Property, and the nonconforming building line cannot be corrected without
removing portions of the existing house, which would substantially increase the
cost of the proposed work, and would substantially reduce the utility and value
of the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood, in that the proposed addition will remove a shed dormer, will
be consistent with the style of the existing house, and will be located at the
rear of the house, so it will not substantially impact the streetscape; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not impair an adequate supply of light
and air, because the proposed addition will be lower than the existing roof
line of the house, the first floor addition is not significantly larger than
the portion of the first floor that it will replace, and the second floor will
be set back from the first floor building line; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property, as it will comply with all applicable
building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed addition; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not contribute to congestion in the
public streets, as the structure will continue to be used as a single family
residence; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the requested variation will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village.
   WHEREAS, on July 10, 2000, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variation and by the
unanimous vote of the six members then present, has reported to the Council
recommending that the variations be granted.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain:
   SECTION 1: That the foregoing recitals are incorporated as the findings of
the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth herein.
   SECTION 2: That the following variation is hereby granted with respect to
the Subject Property, commonly known as 275 Auburn, Winnetka, Illinois, and
located in the R-4 Single Family Residential District provided in Chapter 17.16
of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code: a
variation from the building line articulation requirements of Section 17.16.010
(Q) to allow an existing 15.85-foot by 9.7-foot one-story addition and shed
dormer to be replaced with a 16-foot by 18.3-foot two-story addition at the
northeast corner of the existing residence on the Subject Property, in
accordance with the drawings and plans submitted with the application for
variations.
   SECTION 3: That the variations hereby granted are conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: That this Ordinance shall be in full force and effect from and
after its passage, approval, and posting.
   ADOPTED this 5th day of September, 2000, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES: Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Lien, Powell and Woodbury
   NAYS: None
   ABSENT: Trustee Presser
   APPROVED this 5th day of September, 2000.
   Signed:
   /ss/Louise A. Holland
   Village President
