ORDINANCE NO. M-27-2000
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (851 Sheridan Road)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
   Lot 6 and Sub-lot 6 in Trustees Subdivision of all that portion of Lot 1
North or Northwesterly of a line drawn perpendicular to the center line of
Sheridan Road (formerly called Lake Avenue) and passing through an iron stake,
which said stake is in the easterly line of said Sheridan Road, 478 feet
southeasterly along said East line from the point of intersection of said East
line and South line of north Avenue, said Lot 1 being in Lloyd and Robinson’s
Subdivision of Section 16 and 17, Township 42 North, Range 13, East of the
Third Principal Meridian, in Cook County Illinois,
commonly known as 851 Sheridan Road, Winnetka, Illinois and located in the R-
2 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.24 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17, Winnetka Village Code, for a variation
from the side yard requirements of Section 17.24.010(I) of the Zoning Ordinance
to allow the existing, nonconforming screened porch at the southeast corner of
the residence on the Subject Property, to be reconstructed in the same
location, but with a 15-inch increase in height and with new ornamental rooftop
and window railings, which will maintain the nonconforming east side yard of 12
feet, whereas a 17.84-foot side yard is required, resulting in a variation of
5.84 feet (32.74%), said construction to be in accordance with the application
for variation and plans accompanying the application; and
   WHEREAS, on August 3, 2000, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variation and, by the
unanimous vote of the four members then present, has reported to the Council
recommending that the requested variation be granted; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property is located on a private street formed in part
by an easement for ingress and egress that extends along the north lot line and
northeast corner of the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and unique hardships associated
with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect
to the Subject Property in that (a) the Subject Property is a relatively square
lot, being approximately 100 feet wide and 150 feet deep; (b) the residence was
built in 1937, before the current side yard requirements were enacted; (c) the
residence is located approximately in the center of the lot, with a 12-foot
east side yard and an 11.45-foot west side yard; (d) the Subject Property does
not meet the aggregate side yard requirement of 29.84 feet and the entire east
side of the residence encroaches 5.84 feet into the required east side yard;
(e) the screened porch that the owners propose to rebuild forms more than half
of the east building line and is aligned with the east wall of an adjoining
sitting room located in the northeast corner of the residence; (f) the roof of
the existing screened porch is deteriorated and needs to be replaced; and (g)
the proposed 15-inch height increase will bring the interior height of the
enclosed porch into compliance with the building code; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that
(a) bringing the Subject Property into full conformity with all zoning
requirements and limitations would either require the replacement of the
building with an entirely new structure, require the removal of nearly 6 feet
of the entire east side of the house, or would allow the replacement of only a
portion of the enclosed porch, which would reduce the utility of the existing
space or and (b) the proposed additional height, ornamental railings and window
upgrades are minor improvements necessary to allow minor interior improvements,
provide for building code compliance and make the exterior of the addition
consistent with the appearance of the remainder of the residence; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because the proposed construction will improve the overall
appearance of the residence, will maintain its existing size, and will maintain
its scale and presence in the context of the surrounding neighborhood; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not impair an adequate supply of light
and air because the horizontal dimensions of the building will not be altered
and the additional 15 inches in height, plus the rooftop railing, do not
significantly increase the height of the one-story enclosed porch; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property as the proposed addition will comply with
all applicable building, fire protection and life safety codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvement; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed reconstruction and modification of the enclosed porch
will not contribute to congestion on the public streets, as the residence will
continue to be used as a single-family residence; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed construction will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation is in harmony with the general purpose and
intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that it allows the renovation,
restoration and rehabilitation of a structurally sound existing building while
maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the community and protecting
established trees and landscaping.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1: That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2:  That a variation is hereby granted with respect to the Subject
Property, commonly known as 851 Sheridan Road, Winnetka, Illinois, which is
located in the R-2 Single-Family Residential District provided in Chapter 17.24
of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17, Winnetka Village Code, for a
variation from the side yard requirements of Section 17.24.010(I) of the Zoning
Ordinance to allow the existing, nonconforming screened porch at the southeast
corner of the residence on the Subject Property, to be reconstructed in the
same location, but with a 15-inch increase in height and with new ornamental
rooftop and window railings, which will maintain the nonconforming east side
yard of 12 feet, whereas a 17.84-foot side yard is required, resulting in a
variation of 5.84 feet (32.74%), said construction to be in accordance with the
application for variation and plans accompanying the application.
   SECTION 3: That the variation hereby granted is conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within twelve months after the
effective date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 19th day of September, 2000, pursuant to the following roll
call vote:
   AYES: Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Powell, Presser and Woodbury
   NAYS: None
   ABSENT: Trustee Lien
   APPROVED this 19th day of September, 2000.
   Signed:
   /s/Louise A. Holland
   Village President
Countersigned:
/s/Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: September 5, 2000
Posted: September 6, 2000
Passed and Approved: September 19, 2000
Posted: September 20, 2000
ORDINANCE NO. M-28-2000
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (614 Willow Road)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
   The West 62 feet of the East 303 feet of the North 165 feet of Block 9 in
John C. Garland’s Addition to Winnetka in Fractional Section 21, Township 42
North, Range 13, East of the Third Principal Meridian, in Cook County,
Illinois,
commonly known as 614 Willow Road, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
5 Single Family Residential District provided in Chapter 17.12 of the Winnetka
Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for a variation from
the building line articulation requirements of Section 17.12.010(Q) to allow an
existing 16-foot by 20-foot screened porch at the southeast corner of the
residence on the Subject Property to be replaced with a 16.12-foot by 25.64-
foot one-story family room, which will extend the nonconforming building east
building line from 60 53 feet long to 66.03 feet long, said addition to be
constructed in accordance with the drawings and plans submitted with the
application for variation; and
   WHEREAS, on August 3, 2000, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variation and by the
unanimous vote of the four members then present, has reported to the Council
recommending that the variations be granted.
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and particular hardships
associated with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance
with respect to the Subject Property in that: (a) the residence on the Subject
Property was constructed in 1926, before the building line articulation
requirement was added to the Zoning Ordinance; (b) the interior configuration
of the residence is such that the only suitable location for the proposed
addition is at the east portion of the south building line; (c) both the
existing, 60.53-foot east building line and the proposed, 66.03-foot east
building line exceed the 40-foot maximum allowable unarticulated building line,
and the proposed addition increases the nonconformity by 5.64 feet; (d) in
order to fully conform with the articulation requirement, the proposed addition
would have to be 2 feet narrower which would preclude the proposed reuse of the
existing foundation, and would result in a narrower, misaligned addition; and
(e) a basement entrance at the rear of the house makes it impossible to orient
the proposed addition by placing its longer side against the existing south
wall of the residence; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that
the proposed variation is being requested to add a first floor family room
within the size limitations for the Subject Property, a family room is an
essential feature in single-family residences in the Village, and the
nonconforming building line cannot be fully corrected without narrowing the
proposed family room by 2 feet, which would substantially increase the cost of
the proposed work while substantially reducing the family room’s utility and
value to the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood, in that the proposed addition will be consistent with the
style of the existing house, and will be located at the rear of the house, so
it will not substantially impact the streetscape; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not impair an adequate supply of light
and air, because the proposed addition will be lower than the existing roof
line of the house and is not significantly larger than the portion of the
screened porch that it will replace, and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property, as it will comply with all applicable
building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed addition; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not contribute to congestion in the
public streets, as the structure will continue to be used as a single family
residence; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the requested variation will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village.
   WHEREAS, the requested variation is in harmony with the general purpose and
intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that it allows the renovation,
restoration and rehabilitation of a structurally sound existing building while
maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the community and protecting
established trees and landscaping.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain:
   SECTION 1: That the foregoing recitals are incorporated as the findings of
the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth herein.
   SECTION 2: That the following variation is hereby granted with respect to
the Subject Property, commonly known as 614 Willow Road, Winnetka, Illinois,
and located in the R-5 Single Family Residential District provided in Chapter
17.16 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code:
a variation from the building line articulation requirements of Section
17.12.010(Q) to allow an existing 16-foot by 20-foot screened porch at the
southeast corner of the residence on the Subject Property to be replaced with a
16.12-foot by 25.64-foot one-story family room, which will extend the
nonconforming building east building line from 60 53 feet long to 66.03 feet
long, said addition to be constructed in accordance with the drawings and plans
submitted with the application for variation.
   SECTION 3: That the variations hereby granted are conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: That this Ordinance shall be in full force and effect from and
after its passage, approval, and posting.
   ADOPTED this 3rd of October, 2000, pursuant to the following roll call vote:
   AYES: Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Lien, Powell and Presser
   NAYS: Trustee Woodbury
   ABSENT: None
   APPROVED this 3rd day of October, 2000.
   Signed:
   /s/Louise A. Holland
   Village President
Countersigned:
/s/Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: September 19, 2000
Posted: September 20, 2000
Passed and Approved: October 3, 2000
Posted: October 4, 2000
ORDINANCE NO. M-29-2000
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (1183 Scott Avenue)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
Lot 1 in Fischer’s Consolidation of Lot 10 (except the east 2 feet thereof) and
all of Lot 11 in Block 10 in Chicago North Shore Land Company’s Subdivision in
Sections 17 and 18, Township 42 North, Range 13, East of the Third Principal
Meridian, in Cook County Illinois.
commonly known as 1183 Scott Avenue, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
5 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in Title 17 of the
Winnetka Village Code, for the following variations: (a) from the maximum
building size limitations of Section 17.12.010(G), to allow a gross floor area
of 5,446.94 square feet, which exceeds the maximum allowable gross floor area
of 5,304.42 square feet by 142.52 square feet (1.42%); (b) from the side yard
and total side yard requirements of Section 17.12.010(I) to allow a west side
yard of 3.29 feet for the building, as measured from the end of the eaves,
which is 2.71 feet (45.17%) less than the 6.00 foot side yard that is required,
and to allow a west side yard of 4.29 feet for windows located in the
nonconforming west wall, which is 1.71 feet (28.5%) less than the 6.00 foot
side yard that is required; (c) from the total side yard requirements of
Section 17.12.010(I) to allow total side yard of 10.98 feet, which is 8.81 feet
(228.5%) less than the 19.60 feet required for the total of the two side yards;
and (c) from the building line articulation requirement of Section 17.12.010(Q)
to allow an unarticulated west building line of 55.05 feet, which exceeds the
maximum allowable unarticulated length of 40 feet, resulting in a variation of
15.05 feet, all of said variations being for the purpose of constructing a 2½-
story addition, relocating window openings, replacing a flat roof section and
an extending the existing 2-foot deep eaves on the residence on the Subject
Property, as described in the application for variation and the drawings and
specifications submitted with the application; and
   WHEREAS, on August 28, 2000, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variation and by the
unanimous vote of the four members then present, has reported to the Council
recommending that the requested variation be granted; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation is in harmony with the general purpose and
intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that it allows the renovation,
restoration and rehabilitation of a structurally sound existing building while
maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the community and protecting
established trees and landscaping; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and unique hardships associated
with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect
to the Subject Property in that: (a) the residence on the Subject Property was
constructed in 1914, before the first Zoning Ordinance was enacted and before
building articulation side yard requirements were established, (b) the proposed
addition is 411.75 square feet and will be partially offset by the removal of a
270 square foot enclosed porch, (c) the proposed addition will be located
within the reversed corner on the north side of the home, away from the
property lines, (d) the removal of the porch will reduce the length of the
unarticulated west building wall, and (e) the location of the building on the
Subject Property, coupled with the broad eaves of the roof form, make it
impossible to fully comply with the Zoning Ordinance; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that
(a) the existing nonconformities cannot be cured without rebuilding the house
in a conforming location, (b) the additional floor area that is proposed is
necessary to tie the proposed master bedroom and bath and proposed family room
into the existing configuration of the house, and (c) the proposed master
bedroom and bath and proposed family room are standard features of residences
in the community and are necessary to improve the utility of the subject home
and bring it up to modern standards; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood, in that the proposed addition will be consistent with the
style of the existing house, will be located within a reverse corner, and will
not have a significant impact on the streetscape; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not impair an adequate supply of light
and air because the proposed addition and reconfigured roof are located away
from the property lines and do not exceed the Zoning Ordinance’s height
limitations; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property as the proposed work will comply with all
applicable building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed addition and roof replacement will not contribute to
congestion on the public streets, as the building will continue to be used as a
single family residence; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the requested variations will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2: That the following variations from the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance,
Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, are hereby granted to the Subject
Property, commonly known as 1183 Scott Avenue and located in the R-5 Single
Family Residential Zoning: (a) a variation from the maximum building size
limitations of Section 17.12.010(G), to allow a gross floor area of 5,446.94
square feet, which exceeds the maximum allowable gross floor area of 5,304.42
square feet by 142.52 square feet (1.42%); (b) a variation from the side yard
and total side yard requirements of Section 17.12.010(I) to allow a west side
yard of 3.29 feet for the building, as measured from the end of the eaves,
which is 2.71 feet (45.17%) less than the 6.00 foot side yard that is required,
and to allow a west side yard of 4.29 feet for windows located in the
nonconforming west wall, which is 1.71 feet (28.5%) less than the 6.00 foot
side yard that is required; (c) a variation from the total side yard
requirements of Section 17.12.010(I) to allow total side yard of 10.98 feet,
which is 8.81 feet (228.5%) less than the 19.60 feet required for the total of
the two side yards; and (d) a variation from the building line articulation
requirement of Section 17.12.010(Q) to allow an unarticulated west building
line of 55.05 feet, which exceeds the maximum allowable unarticulated length of
40 feet, resulting in a variation of 15.05 feet; all of said variations being
for the purpose of constructing a 2½-story addition, relocating window
openings, replacing a flat roof section and an extending the existing 2-foot
deep eaves on the residence on the Subject Property, as described in the
application for variation and the drawings and specifications submitted with
the application.
   SECTION 3: That the variation hereby granted is conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 17th day of October, 2000, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Lien, Powell, Presser and Woodbury
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    None
   APPROVED this 17th day of October, 2000.
   Signed:
   /s/Louise A. Holland
   Village President
