ORDINANCE NO. M-33-2000
AN ORDINANCE ESTABLISHING 535 CHERRY STREET AS A DESIGNATED LANDMARK
   WHEREAS, the Village of Winnetka desires to identify, designate and preserve
buildings and structures in the Village that are historically, culturally, and
architecturally significant and to encourage the restoration and rehabilitation
of those buildings and structures; and
   WHEREAS, pursuant to Sections 11-48.2-1, et seq. of the Illinois Municipal
Code (65 ILCS 5/11-48.2-1, et seq.), the Council of the Village of Winnetka
enacted Chapter 15.64 of Title 15 of the Winnetka Village Code, entitled
“Landmark Preservation,” for the purposes of preserving, protecting, enhancing,
rehabilitating and regulating buildings, structures, objects, and places of
historical, cultural or architectural importance; and
   WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka have determined that it is
in the best interests of the Village and its residents that participation in
the landmark designation process shall be limited to those owners who
voluntarily elect to participate in that process; and
   WHEREAS, pursuant to the provisions of Chapter 15.64 of the Winnetka Village
Code (the “Landmark Ordinance”) and notice duly published and sent, the
Landmark Preservation Commission conducted a public hearing on July 24, 2000,
to consider the application for landmark designation submitted by the owner of
the property located at 535 Cherry Street (permanent real estate index number
05-21-119-007); and
   WHEREAS, the Landmark Preservation Commission applied the Village’s System
for the Evaluation of Landmarks to the information received at the hearing and
made findings regarding the property at 535 Cherry Street, including the
following:
   (1)   That it is a unique property, being an Italian Renaissance Revival
residence, one of very few constructed on the North Shore, featuring an L-
shaped design with the large part of the “L” across the front facing west, and
the small part of the “L” at the south, a symmetrical façade, brick walls,
full-length first floor windows topped by arches and a simple tiled hipped
roof;
   (2)   That the house is further distinguished by a gabled center entrance
featuring 5 window bays across, a paneled wood door surrounded by an unusual
fresco of cream colored foliage on a salmon background, scroll-shaped corbels
of brown painted wood supporting a wrought iron balcony, a tall arched leaded
glass window with side lights above the balcony, a circle of stained glass
illustrating a brown sailing ship with white sails against a blue sky and green
ocean in the curve of the window arch surrounded by a fresco that echoes the
door surround, and a ceramic white cat perched on the southwest corner of the
roof, all of which make the house rare in the context of Winnetka; and
   (3)   That the method of construction of the house is extremely rare due to
the decorative arched leaded windows, the lavish use of leaded glass in a
variety of patterns, the stained glass ship on the front window, the decorative
ceramic cat on the roof and the unusual exterior frescos; and
   (4)   That the residence merits the Commission’s highest point value for
association with a master architect or builder, having been designed and built
in 1916 by architect Ralph W. Varney as his own residence, which gives it
special local historical significance; and
   (5)   That due to its location in a small neighborhood, the house is
conspicuous and familiar in the context of its neighborhood; and
   (6)   That the design integrity of the exterior of the building was rated as
excellent since no changes have been made to the front of the building since
the house was built; and
   WHEREAS, based on its findings, the Landmark Preservation Commission
concluded that the residence located at 535 Cherry Street meets the criteria of
the Landmark Ordinance and unanimously recommended that it be so designated;
and
   WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka have considered the
recommendation of the Landmark Preservation Commission and have determined that
it is in the best interest of the Village and its residents to establish the
Ralph W. Varney house, located at 535 Cherry Street, as a designated landmark.
   NOW THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain:
   SECTION 1: That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2: That the property located at 535 Cherry Street, known as the
Ralph W. Varney house, is hereby designated a landmark under the Landmark
Ordinance, in accordance with the findings and recommendations of the Landmark
Preservation Commission.
   SECTION 3: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 21st day of November, 2000, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Lien, Powell, Presser and Woodbury
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    None
   APPROVED this 21st day of November, 2000.
   Signed:
   /s/Louise A. Holland
   Village President
Countersigned:
/s/Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
