ORDINANCE NO. M-34-2000
AN ORDINANCE ESTABLISHING 687 CHERRY STREET AS A DESIGNATED LANDMARK
   WHEREAS, the Village of Winnetka desires to identify, designate and preserve
buildings and structures in the Village that are historically, culturally, and
architecturally significant and to encourage the restoration and rehabilitation
of those buildings and structures; and
   WHEREAS, pursuant to Sections 11-48.2-1, et seq. of the Illinois Municipal
Code (65 ILCS 5/11-48.2-1, et seq.), the Council of the Village of Winnetka
enacted Chapter 15.64 of Title 15 of the Winnetka Village Code, entitled
“Landmark Preservation,” for the purposes of preserving, protecting, enhancing,
rehabilitating and regulating buildings, structures, objects, and places of
historical, cultural or architectural importance; and
   WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka has determined that it is in
the best interests of the Village and its residents that participation in the
landmark designation process shall be limited to those owners who voluntarily
elect to participate in that process; and
   WHEREAS, pursuant to the provisions of Chapter 15.64 of the Winnetka Village
Code (the “Landmark Ordinance”) and notice duly published and sent, the
Landmark Preservation Commission conducted a public hearing on September 18,
2000, to consider the application for landmark designation submitted by the
owner of the property located at 687 Cherry Street (permanent real estate index
number 05-21-115-005); and
   WHEREAS, the Landmark Preservation Commission applied the Village’s System
for the Evaluation of Landmarks to the information received at the hearing and
made findings regarding the property at 687 Cherry Street, including the
following:
   (1)   That it is a significant property, being a well-proportioned red brick
colonial revival residence built in 1926, featuring a wood center-entry portico
with doric columns topped by a gabled roof, five bay windows across the front,
double-hung windows on both the first and second floors on the west façade, an
outside staircase on the east façade to be reclad in brick during the
rehabilitation of the home, and a decorative stringcourse of soldier bricks at
the division between the first and second floors, all of which make the house
somewhat rare in the context of Winnetka; and
   (2)   That the residence merits the highest point value for association with
a local historical event, since the house was moved in 1939 from its original
location at 696 Cherry Street because of the relocation of the Chicago
Northwestern Railroad tracks from street level to below grade, one of the most
significant historical events in Winnetka; and
   (3)   That the residence, which was built in 1926, is associated with a
master architect or builder, because the original owner, Sam Steffans, was also
the architect of the residence; and
   (5)   That due to its location on a smaller, less frequently traveled
street, the house is conspicuous or familiar in the context of its
neighborhood; and
   (6)   That the design integrity of the exterior of the building is good
since no changes have been made on the street facing front façade of the
building; and
   WHEREAS, based on its findings, the Landmark Preservation Commission
concluded that the Sam Steffans House located at 687 Cherry Street meets the
criteria of the Landmark Ordinance and unanimously recommended that it be so
designated; and
   WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka have considered the
recommendation of the Landmark Preservation Commission and have determined that
it is in the best interest of the Village and its residents to establish the
property located at 687 Cherry Street as a designated landmark.
   NOW THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain:
   SECTION 1: That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2: That the property located at 687 Cherry Street, known as the Sam
Steffans House, is hereby designated a landmark under the Landmark Ordinance,
in accordance with the findings and recommendations of the Landmark
Preservation Commission.
   SECTION 3: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 21st day of November, 2000, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Lien, Powell, Presser and Woodbury
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    None
   APPROVED this 21st day of November, 2000.
   Signed:
   /s/Louise A. Holland
   Village President
Countersigned:
/s/Douglas G. Williams
