ORDINANCE NO. M-01-2001
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (391 Sheridan Road)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
The Northwesterly 225.0 ft. (except the Westerly 202.6 ft. thereof) and (except
the Southerly 3.0 ft. thereof) of Lot 1 in Lake Grove Subdivision in Winnetka,
being a Subdivision of that part of fractional Northeast quarter of Section 21,
Township 42 North, Range 13, East of the Third Principal Meridian, formerly
known (& vacated) as Blocks 43, 70 and 71 of Village of Winnetka (except the
Northerly 175.0 ft. of Block 43) in Cook County, Illinois. Together with rights
of ingress and egress by means of public easement as disclosed by Document No.
12908867 and Document No. 12911756.
commonly known as 391 Sheridan Road, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
2 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance, Title 17, Winnetka Village Code, for the following variations: (a)
from the front setback requirements of Section 17.24.010(H) to allow an
existing elevated concrete deck and boat ramp to remain in their current
locations, set back 27.50 feet from the water’s edge and extending into Lake
Michigan, whereas a setback of 50 feet is required, resulting in a variation of
22.50 feet (45%); (b) from the minimum side yard requirements of Section
17.24.010(I) to permit an existing storage shed to remain in its current
location, 6.14 feet from the south lot line, and to allow an existing wooden
deck to remain in its current location, 0.00 feet from the south lot line,
whereas a minimum side yard of 12.0 feet is required, resulting in variations
of 100% and 48.8%, respectively; and (c) from the total side yard requirement
of Section 17.24.010(I), to permit the total of the two side yards to be 12.45
feet, whereas the total side yard requirement is 63.64 feet, resulting in a
variation of 80.44%; and
   WHEREAS, on December 11, 2000, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variations and, by the
unanimous vote of the four members then present, has reported to the Council
recommending that the requested variations be granted; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and unique hardships associated
with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect
to the Subject Property in that: (a) the Subject Property is a riparian
property located on a bluff along the shore of Lake Michigan; (b) the boat
house, storage shed, concrete deck and boat ramp are all part of a bluff
stabilization project that was approved by the Army Corps of Engineers and the
State of Illinois; (c) the boat house and storage shed serve as retaining walls
that protect a mature tree and prevent bluff subsidence on both the Subject
Property and on the property immediately to the south; (d) the concrete deck
rests over a perched beach that also stabilizes the bluff by preventing beach
erosion; (e) requiring the structures to be set back further from the water’s
edge would require digging further into the bluff and would leave the immediate
water’s edge unprotected; (f) requiring the structures to be set back further
from the south lot line would jeopardize a mature tree directly above the boat
house and would also destabilize the bluff of the property to the south; and
(g) the setback requirements that apply to through lots are generally designed
to prevent structures from being placed too close to paved streets and thus do
not address the unique need to stabilize the Subject Property’s beach and bluff
and to protect them against erosion from wave and storm action; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that:
(a) the boat house, storage shed, concrete deck and boat ramp are all at the
water’s edge, where they both stabilize the beach and bluff, and provide the
owners with access to the lake for recreational purposes; (b) placing these
structures in a conforming location would de-stabilize the bluff, make access
to the water more difficult, require the removal of mature trees and add
structures to the tableland portion of the Subject Property, all of which would
substantially reduce the value of the Subject Property by interfering with the
owner’s right to make reasonable use of its riparian features; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because: (a) the boat house, storage shed, concrete deck and
boat ramp are at the water’s edge and are consistent with the riparian
character of the Subject Property and the neighborhood; and (b) the structures
do not interfere with the appearance of the Subject Property in the context of
the neighborhood, because only the rooftop deck and the small wooden deck along
the south property line are visible from the top of the bluff or from
neighboring properties; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not impair an adequate supply of
light and air because the structures are all less than one story high and are
located low on the bluff; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property, because the primary materials of all of
the structures is concrete and the structures will be brought into compliance
with all applicable codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not diminish the taxable value of
land and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because the structures protect and enhance the bluff
and beach areas of the property without having a negative impact on neighboring
properties; and
   WHEREAS, the structures will not contribute to congestion on the public
streets, because they do not change the use of the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the structures will otherwise impair the
public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the inhabitants of the
Village.
   WHEREAS, the requested variation is in harmony with the general purpose and
intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that it allows the renovation,
restoration and rehabilitation of a structurally sound existing building while
maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the community and protecting
established trees and landscaping.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : The foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the findings
of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth herein.
   SECTION 2:  The following variations from the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance,
Title 17, of the Winnetka Village Code, are granted to the Subject Property,
which is commonly known as 391 Sheridan Road and is located in the R-2 Single-
Family Residential Zoning District: (a) from the front setback requirements of
Section 17.24.010(H) to allow an existing elevated concrete deck and boat ramp
to remain in their current locations, set back 27.50 feet from the water’s edge
and extending into Lake Michigan, whereas a setback of 50 feet is required,
resulting in a variation of 22.50 feet (45%); (b) from the minimum side yard
requirements of Section 17.24.010(I) to permit an existing storage shed to
remain in its current location, 6.14 feet from the south lot line, and to allow
an existing wooden deck to remain in its current location, 0.00 feet from the
south lot line, whereas a minimum side yard of 12.0 feet is required, resulting
in variations of 100% and 48.8%, respectively; and (c) from the total side yard
requirement of Section 17.24.010(I), to permit the total of the two side yards
to be 12.45 feet, whereas the total side yard requirement is 63.64 feet,
resulting in a variation of 80.44%.
   SECTION 3: The variations hereby granted are conditioned upon bringing all
accessory structures on the Subject Property, including all of the existing
structures along the water’s edge, into compliance with all applicable Village
ordinances within 6 months after the effective date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 6th day of February, 2001, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Brower, Lien, Powell, Presser and Woodbury
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    Trustee Aquilino
   APPROVED this 6th day of February, 2001.
   Signed:
   //s//Louise A. Holland
   Village President
Countersigned:
/s/Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: January 2, 2001
Posted: January 3, 2001
Passed and Approved: February 6, 2001
Posted: February 7, 2001
ORDINANCE NO. M-02-2001
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (714 Park Street)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
That part of Lot 2 in Resubdivision of Block 8 in Park Addition to Winnetka
Plat recorded January 11, 1889 as Document 1049627 in Book 32, Page 33, lying
East of a line at right angles to South line of said Lot 2 at a point 135.00
feet West of Southeast corner of said Lot, in Sections 16 and 17, Township 42
North, Range 13, East of the Third Principal Meridian, in Cook County,
Illinois.
commonly known as 714 Park Lane, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
2 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance, Title 17, Winnetka Village Code, for a variation from the front
setback requirements of Section 17.24.010(H) to allow a front setback of 35
feet, whereas a 50-foot setback is required, resulting in a variation of 15
feet (30.00%), for the purpose of expanding the existing, attached two-car
garage by adding a third garage bay with a second floor above it on the Subject
Property; and
   WHEREAS, on December 11, 2000, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variation and, by the
unanimous vote of the four members then present, has reported to the Council
recommending that the requested variation be granted; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and unique hardships associated
with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect
to the Subject Property in that: (a) the Subject Property is an irregularly-
shaped lot, with a curving front lot line that follows the edge of the
partially improved right-of-way of Park Street; (b) due to the curving lot
line, the 135.00-foot long south lot line is slightly more than one half the
length of the north lot line, which measures 248.30 feet in length; (c) the
residence on the Subject Property is oriented to face the south lot line, with
access by way of a driveway that extends from the southeast corner of the
Subject Property to the garage, located in the southeast corner of the
residence; (d) because of the curve of the Park Street right-of-way, the 50-
foot setback line cuts through the southeast corner of the existing garage; and
(e) the southeast corner of the existing garage observes a front setback of
43.50 feet, which was permitted by variation, due to the presence of a steep
hill on the western end of the Subject Property, a restrictive covenant that
prevents new construction in the northern part of the Subject Property and the
presence of Sheridan Park immediately across the unimproved right-of-way; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that
the proposed garage addition is of minimal size, measuring 10 feet by 22 feet,
and improves the utility and value of the property by allowing a third garage
bay and an in-home office, which are reasonable amenities found in other
single-family homes in the immediate vicinity; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because the placement of the addition will complement the Cape
Cod style of the house, improving the balance of the structure and maintaining
its appearance and location within the context of the immediate neighborhood;
and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not impair an adequate supply of light
and air because the proposed addition meets all height limitations of the
Zoning Ordinance and the encroachment into the unimproved right-of-way is in
the direction of open space consisting of the unimproved Park Street right-of-
way and Sheridan Park; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property, as it will comply with all applicable
building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed garage addition will not contribute to congestion on
the public streets, as the Subject Property will continue to be used for
single-family residential purposes and the additional garage bay will allow an
additional vehicle to be parked inside; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed garage addition will
otherwise impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village.
   WHEREAS, the requested variation is in harmony with the general purpose and
intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that it allows the renovation,
restoration and rehabilitation of a structurally sound existing building while
maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the community and protecting
established trees and landscaping.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : The foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the findings
of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth herein.
   SECTION 2:  A variation from the front setback requirements of Section
17.24.010(H) of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17, Winnetka Village Code
is hereby granted to the Subject Property, commonly known as 714 Park Lane,
Winnetka, Illinois, which is located in the R-2 Single-Family Residential
Zoning District, to allow a front setback of 35 feet, whereas a 50-foot setback
is required, resulting in a variation of 15 feet (30.00%), for the purpose of
expanding the existing attached two-car garage by adding a third garage bay
with a second floor above it, all in accordance with the drawings submitted
with the application for variation.
   SECTION 3: That the variation hereby granted is conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 16th day of January, 2001, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Lien, Powell, Presser and Woodbury
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    None
   APPROVED this 16th day of January, 2001.
   Signed:
   /s/Louise A. Holland
   Village President
Countersigned:
/s/Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: January 2, 2001
Posted: January 3, 2001
Passed and Approved: January 16, 2001
Posted: January 17, 2001
ORDINANCE NO. M-3-2001
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (850 Hill Road)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
All that part of Lot 16 in Indian Hill Subdivision Number 2, being a
subdivision of parts of Section 20 and 29, Township 42 North, Range 13, East of
the Third Principal Meridian, as shown by plat thereof, recorded in the
Recorder’s Office of Cook County, Illinois on December 17, 1915, as document
5772391, in Book 139 of Plats, Page 38, lying north of a line 191.90 feet south
of and parallel with the north line of the northeast ¼ of said Section 29, in
Cook County, Illinois,
commonly known as 850 Hill Road, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
1 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for the following variations:
(a) a variation from the use limitations of Section 17.28.010.B.1.i of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, which prohibit the alteration or expansion of
existing accessory buildings that contain living quarters; (b) a variation from
Section 17.64.010.A.3 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, which prohibits
structural alterations to, and increases in the cubic content of, legally
nonconforming buildings that are used for uses that are also legally
nonconforming; (c) a variation from the height limitations of Section
17.28.010.C of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, which limits the height of
accessory buildings to 14 feet and one story; and (d) a variation from the
setback requirements of Section 17.28.010.H of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance,
which require a 50-foot setback on the Subject Property, to allow a one-story
extension to the existing coachhouse to align with the existing coachhouse and
garden wall, which observe a corner setback of 31.59 feet, resulting in a
variation of 16.98 feet (34.96%); all of said variations being for the purpose
adding a one-story workroom addition to the east end of the coachhouse and
replacing the 3-window dormer-like openings on the north and south sides of
existing roof at the east end of the coachhouse with two single-window, gabled
dormers on the north and south sides of the roof; and
   WHEREAS, on January 8, 2001, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variation and, by the
unanimous vote of the six members then present, has reported to the Council
recommending that the requested variation be granted; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and unique hardships associated
with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect
to the Subject Property in that: (a) the Subject Property is located at the
southwest corner of Hill Road and Indian Hill Road; (b) the house and
coachhouse were built in their existing locations in 1925; (c) the Subject
Property was annexed to the Village in 1989, at which time the setbacks, height
of the coachhouse and use of the coachhouse as a second dwelling unit became
legal nonconformities; (d) the second dwelling unit, located on the second
floor of the coachhouse has been maintained as a legal, nonconforming use due
to the owner’s annual registration of the additional dwelling unit as required
by Section 17.72.030 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance; (e) the coachhouse is
connected to the principal residence on the Subject Property by a roofed
walkway, the north side of which is a brick garden wall that aligns with the
north wall of the coachhouse; and (f) the proposed changes do not alter
existing roof lines, because a part of the existing garden wall will be
incorporated into the north wall of the proposed one-story addition; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that
the proposed one-story workroom addition is a reasonable accessory use for the
Subject Property, there is no suitable location for such an addition to the
principal residence, and the changes proposed to the east portion of the
existing roof of the coachhouse will enhance the Subject Property by creating a
consistent architectural style in all structures on the Subject Property with
the least amount of structural alteration to the nonconforming coachhouse; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because (a) the one-story workroom addition will be tied into
the existing brick garden wall and roofed walkway and will not be visible from
the street ; and (b) the new dormers, which will replace windows currently
built into the roof of the east end of the coachhouse, will match the dormers
on the west end of the coachhouse; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not impair an adequate supply of
light and air because the roof lines of the existing structures on the Subject
Property will not be altered and the one-story workroom addition will be no
closer to the adjoining property line than the south wall of the existing
coachhouse; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property as it will comply with all applicable
building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not diminish the taxable value of
land and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed garage addition and coachhouse roof modifications will
not contribute to congestion on the public streets, as the use of the Subject
Property will not change and the amount of off-street parking will remain the
same, while the amount of habitable space in the coachhouse will be reduced;
and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed garage addition and
coachhouse roof modifications will otherwise impair the public health, safety,
comfort, morals, and welfare of the inhabitants of the Village.
   WHEREAS, the requested variation is in harmony with the general purpose and
intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that it allows the renovation,
restoration and rehabilitation of a structurally sound existing building while
maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the community and protecting
established trees and landscaping.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : The foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the findings
of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth herein.
   SECTION 2:  The following variations are granted to the Subject Property,
commonly known as 850 Hill Road, located in the R-1 Single-Family Residential
Zoning District provided in the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the
Winnetka Village Code: (a) a variation from the use limitations of Section
17.28.010.B.1.i of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, which prohibit the alteration
or expansion of existing accessory buildings that contain living quarters; (b)
a variation from Section 17.64.010.A.3 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, which
prohibits structural alterations to, and increases in the cubic content of,
legally nonconforming buildings that are used for uses that are also legally
nonconforming; (c) a variation from the height limitations of Section
17.28.010.C of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, which limits the height of
accessory buildings to 14 feet and one story; and (d) a variation from the
setback requirements of Section 17.28.010.H of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance,
which require a 50-foot setback on the Subject Property, to allow a one-story
extension to the existing coachhouse to align with the existing coachhouse and
garden wall, which observe a corner setback of 31.59 feet, resulting in a
variation of 16.98 feet (34.96%); all of said variations being for the purpose
adding a one-story workroom addition to the east end of the coachhouse and
replacing the 3-window dormer-like openings on the north and south sides of
existing roof at the east end of the coachhouse with two single-window, gabled
dormers on the north and south sides of the roof, all in accordance with the
plans and specifications submitted with the application for variations.
   SECTION 3: The variations hereby granted are subject to the following
conditions: (a) the proposed construction shall commence within 12 months after
the effective date of this Ordinance; and (b) the owners shall remove one of
the three existing bedrooms and one of the two existing bathrooms from the
coachhouse, as indicated in the plans submitted with the application for
variation.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 20th day of February, 2001, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Lien, Powell, Presser and Woodbury
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    None
   APPROVED this 20th day of February, 2001.
   Signed:
   /s/Louise A. Holland
   Village President
Countersigned:
/s/Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
