ORDINANCE NO. M-4-2001
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (333 Sunset Road)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
Lots 24 and 25 (except the easterly 2 feet of Lot 25) in Remy Park Subdivision
of Part of Section 21, Township 42 North, Range 13, East of the Third Principal
Meridian, according to a plat recorded in the Recorder’s Office of Cook County,
Illinois on November 18, 1916 as document number 5995688, in Book 147 of Plats,
Page 46, in Cook County, Illinois.
commonly known as 333 Sunset Road, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
4 Single-Family Zoning District established by the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance,
Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for a variation from the maximum
building size requirements of Section 17.16.010.G of the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance, to allow a total gross floor area of 6,152.15 square feet whereas a
maximum gross floor area of 5,797.34 square feet is allowed, resulting in a
variation of 354.81 square feet (6.12%), for the purpose of constructing a
second floor master bath addition and making two small additions to the first
floor to reconfigure the rear entrance, add a laundry and mud room, and provide
enclosed, conforming access to the basement of the residence on the Subject
Property; and
   WHEREAS, on January 8, 2001 on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variation and has forwarded
the request to the Village Council without recommendation, the vote of the
Zoning Board of Appeals being two votes in favor of the variation and three
against, although a concurring vote of four members is necessary to recommend;
and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and unique hardships associated
with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect
to the Subject Property in that : (a) the Subject Property is nearly 200 feet
deep and has a detached garage that is located nearly 40 feet from the rear
quarter of the lot, which prevents the applicants from taking advantage of the
400 square foot gross floor area allowance for garages located in the rear
quarter of the lot; (b) the existing garage was built with brick that matches
the residence on the Subject Property, at or near the time the residence was
built in 1925; (c) the location of the garage on the Subject Property is
consistent with the location of accessory structures on neighboring properties,
which results in a large expanse of open space in the rear of the Subject
Property and the three adjoining lots; (d) relocating the garage to the rear
quarter of the lot would require the removal of several mature trees located in
the rear of the Subject Property and, although not all of the trees are
significant enough to be subject to protection under the Village’s Tree
Ordinance, they are sufficient in number to change the character of the rear of
the Subject Property and the adjoining properties if they were removed to
accommodate a relocated garage; (e) the amount of gross floor area that would
be added by the proposed additions is less than the 400 square foot garage
allowance; and (f) although there are conforming alternatives for the location
of the proposed master bathroom and for the smaller first floor additions, they
cannot be accomplished without significant structural changes and a major
reconfiguration of the entire residence, and would yield smaller and less
practical results while substantially increasing the cost of the project; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that
reconfiguring the interior of the residence or relocating the garage would add
substantial costs which would not result in a corresponding increase in value,
and the residence, as currently configured, is functionality obsolete, because
(a) the master bedroom does not have its own bathroom and is not adjacent to
one of the two existing bathrooms on the second floor; (b) the two bathrooms on
the second floor serve five bedrooms, including the master bedroom; (c) the
master bedroom has minimal closet space; (d) the only access to the basement is
by way of either a narrow, interior spiral staircase with a low head clearance,
or a steep, open, exterior stairway; and (e) the existing rear entryway is not
visible from the garage, creating a hazard for persons entering or leaving the
residence if someone is backing out the garage; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because: (a) the proposed additions will match the style and
materials of the existing residence; (b) the second floor addition will be
directly over the existing library; (c) the proposed first floor additions are
of a minimal size; and (d) allowing the proposed additions to be built without
requiring the garage to be relocated will preserve the established open space
at the rear of the Subject Property and the three adjoining lots as it
currently exists; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not impair an adequate supply of light
and air because the proposed additions are well within the required setbacks
and are less than the full height of the existing residence, and the total
gross floor area that will result on the Subject Property will be less than
would be permitted if the garage were relocated to the rear quarter of the
Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property as the proposed additions will comply
with all applicable building and fire protection codes and will provide safer
ingress and egress from the basement and at the rear entrance; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the additions will not contribute to congestion on the public
streets, as the existing use will be unchanged and the number of bedrooms will
not increase; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed additions will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation is in harmony with the general purpose and
intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that it allows the renovation,
restoration and rehabilitation of a structurally sound existing building while
maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the community and protecting
established trees and landscaping.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : The foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the findings
of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth herein.
   SECTION 2: There is hereby granted to the property commonly known as 333
Sunset Road, Winnetka, Illinois, (the “Subject Property”), located in the R-
4 Single-Family Zoning District established by the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance,
Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, a variation from the maximum building
size requirements of Section 17.16.010.G of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, to
allow a total gross floor area of 6,152.15 square feet whereas a maximum gross
floor area of 5,797.34 square feet is allowed, resulting in a variation of
354.81 square feet (6.12%), for the purpose of constructing a second floor
master bath addition and making two small additions to the first floor to
reconfigure the rear entrance, add a laundry and mud room, and provide
enclosed, conforming access to the basement of the residence on the Subject
Property, all in accordance with the plans and specifications submitted with
the application for variation.
   SECTION 3: The variation hereby granted is conditioned upon the commencement
of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective date of this
Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 6th day of March, 2001, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Lien, Presser and Woodbury
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    Trustee Powell
   APPROVED this 6th day of March, 2001.
   Signed:
   /s/Louise A. Holland
   Village President
Countersigned:
/s/Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: February 20, 2001
Posted: February 21, 2001
Passed and Approved: March 6, 2001
