ORDINANCE NO. M-5-2001
AN ORDINANCE AUTHORIZING THE SALE OR OTHER DISPOSITION OF SURPLUS PROPERTY FROM
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA’S PUBLIC WORKS BUILDING AND YARDS
   WHEREAS, the Village of Winnetka (the “Village”) has entered into contracts
for the construction of a new Public Works Facility at the Village Yards
located at 1390 Willow Road, to house the Public Works Department and the Water
and Electric; and
   WHEREAS, part of the construction project involves the demolition of the
existing Public Works Building and disposition of its contents, which includes
certain machinery parts, equipment, appliances, furniture and tools that have
exceeded their useful life (the “Personal Property”), and either cannot be
reused in the new building or will otherwise have no further practical use for
the Village; and
   WHEREAS, it is the opinion of a majority of the corporate authorities of the
Village: (a) that said Personal Property is surplus material that is no longer
necessary or useful to the Village; (b) that the cost of storing the Personal
Property exceeds its value to the Village of Winnetka; (c) that the cost of
conducting a public sale of the Personal Property exceeds its limited value;
(d) that it is, therefore, not in the best interests of the Village to retain
the Personal Property; (e) that the Personal Property should be disposed of in
the manner hereinafter provided, pursuant to Section 11-76-4 of the Illinois
Municipal Code (65 ILCS 5/11-76-4), and (f) that it is in the best interest of
the Village that the Surplus Property be sold or otherwise disposed of to avoid
the costs of unnecessary storage;
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : Pursuant to Section 11-76-4 of the Illinois Municipal Code (65
ILCS 5/11-76-4), the corporate authorities of the Village of Winnetka find that
all of the above-described Personal Property that is removed in the course of
constructing the new Public Works Facility is surplus material that is no
longer necessary or useful to the Village of Winnetka and has no trade-in
value, and that the best interests of the Village of Winnetka will be served by
disposing of said surplus material by sale or by such other means as may be
safe and reasonable.
   SECTION 2 : The Council of the Village of Winnetka further find that, based
on prior experience in disposing of similar items of property, the cost of
conducting a public sale of such items of property, including the costs of
advertising and publishing, and the costs of personnel needed to maintain
security and to conduct the public sale, exceeds the value of such items.
   SECTION 3: Pursuant to said Section 11-76-4, the Village Manager is hereby
authorized and directed to dispose of said surplus material in any manner he
deems reasonable under the circumstances, including but not limited to the sale
of the surplus material with or without advertising, provided that the
disposition and removal of said surplus material from the Village is in
accordance with all applicable environmental and safety standards and
regulations.
   SECTION 4: That this Ordinance shall be in full force and effect from and
after its passage, approval, and posting.
   ADOPTED this 20th day of March, 2001, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Lien, Powell, Presser and Woodbury
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    None
   APPROVED this 20th day of March, 2001.
   Signed:
   /s/Louise A. Holland
   Village President
Countersigned:
/s/Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: March 6, 2001
Posted: March 7, 2001
Passed and Approved: March 20, 2001
Posted: March 21, 2001
ORDINANCE NO. M-6-2001
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (420 Elder Lane)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
Lot 3 (except South 50 feet thereof) and the West 10 feet of Lot 2 (except
South 50 feet thereof) in Block 12 in Lake Shore Subdivision in Village of
Winnetka, being a subdivision of Lot 1, in a subdivision by Nicholas Simon &
others of part of fractional southwest ¼ of Section 22, Township 42 North,
Range 13, East of the Third Principal Meridian, and the 5 acres lying North of
and adjoining said lot, in Cook County, Illinois (in the West ½ of the South
East 1/4 of Section 21, Township 42 North, Range 13),
commonly known as 420 Elder Lane, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
5 Single-Family District provided in Chapter 17.12 of the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for a variation from the rear
yard setback requirements of Section 17.12.010(J) of the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance, to permit the construction of a 16-foot by 20-foor, one-story
detached garage three feet from the west lot line of the Subject Property,
whereas a six-foot setback is required, resulting in a variation of three feet
(50%); and
   WHEREAS, on February 12, 2001, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variation and, by the
unanimous vote of the six members then in attendance, has reported to the
Council recommending that the requested variation be granted; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and unique circumstances
associated with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance
with respect to the Subject Property in that: (a) the Subject Property is a
shallow lot, being only 120 feet deep, so that its rear yard, which is the rear
quarter of the lot, adjoins the side yard of the lot immediately to the west,
which is approximately 170 feet deep; (b) because the rear yard of the Subject
Property adjoins the side yard of the property to the west, the Subject
Property is subject to side yard setbacks all the way to its rear lot line; (c)
the proposed garage will be located in the southwest corner of the Subject
Property, in the same area as a garage that once stood on the Subject Property,
and a driveway to access the garage already exists; (d) placing the proposed
garage in a conforming location in the southwest corner of the Subject Property
would jeopardize the survival of a 250-year old maple tree and a triple-trunk
ash tree in the rear yard; and (e) placing the proposed garage in a conforming
location on the opposite side of the Subject Property would require removing
the existing driveway and putting in a new driveway in the substantial open
space on the east side of the house, which would substantially increase the
cost of the project while reducing the utility of the new garage, because the
existing driveway maximizes access to the house; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that:
(a) a garage is an essential amenity for single family residences in the
Village; (b) placing the proposed garage in a conforming location in the
southwest corner of the Subject Property would cause the loss of two
significant trees, and wold thereby reduce the value of the Subject Property;
and (c) placing the proposed garage in a conforming location in the southeast
corner of the Subject Property would substantially increase the cost of the
project , would reduce the utility and of the proposed garage by placing it in
an inconvenient location, and would substantially interfere with the existing
open space on the Subject Property without adding an offsetting amount of open
space on the west side of the lot; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because the proposed garage is a modest style, will be in the
same area of the lot that a previous garage stood, will maintain the existing
configuration of the Subject Property, and will allow the mature trees on the
Subject Property to be preserved; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not impair an adequate supply of light
and air because the proposed garage will be only one story high and will be set
as far away from the residence on the neighboring property as is possible; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property as the proposed garage will comply with
all applicable building and fire protection; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed garage will not contribute to congestion on the public
streets, as the existing use of the Subject Property will not be changed and
the new garage will provide off-street parking; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the requested variation will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation is in harmony with the general purpose and
intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that it allows the improvement of
the Subject Property while maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the
community and protecting established trees and landscaping.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain:
   SECTION 1: That the foregoing recitals are incorporated as the findings of
the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth herein.
   SECTION 2: That there is hereby granted with respect to the Subject
Property, commonly known as 420 Elder Lane, which is located in the R-5 Single-
Family Residential District provided in Chapter 17.12 of the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, a variation from the rear
yard setback requirements of Section 17.12.010(J) of the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance, to permit the construction of a 16-foot by 20-foot, one-story
detached garage 3 feet from the west lot line of the Subject Property, whereas
a 6-foot setback is required, resulting in a variation of 3 feet (50%), said
garage to be constructed in accordance with the application and plans submitted
with the application for variation.
   SECTION 2: The variation hereby granted is conditioned upon the commencement
of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective date of this
Ordinance.
   SECTION 3: This Ordinance shall be in full force and effect from and after
its passage, approval, and posting.
   ADOPTED this 3rd day of April, 2001, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Powell, Woodbury, Lien, Brower, Presser and Aquilino
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    None
   APPROVED this 3rd day of April, 2001.
   Signed:
   /s/Louise A. Holland
   Village President
Countersigned:
/s/Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: March 20, 2001
Posted: March 21, 2001
Passed and Approved: April 3, 2001
Posted: April 4, 2001
ORDINANCE NO. M-7-2001
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA, COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (1036 Spruce)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
Lot 7 in Block 4 in Groveland addition to addition to Winnetka, being a
subdivision of the East 70 acres of the Northwest quarter of Section 20,
Township 42 North, Range 13, East of the Third Principal Meridian, in Cook
County, Illinois, commonly known as 1036 Spruce Street, Winnetka, Illinois, and
located in the R-5 Single Family Residential District provided in Chapter 17.12
of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for
the following variations: (a) a variation from the side yard setback
requirements of Section 17.12.010(I), to allow a west side yard of 2.68 feet,
whereas a side yard setback of 6.00 feet is required, resulting in a variation
of 3.32 feet (55.33%); and (b) a variation from the building line articulation
requirements of Section 17.12.010(Q), said variations being requested to allow
38 feet and 7¾ inches of the legally nonconforming, unarticulated 47.68-foot
long west building line to be extended vertically, to increase the height of
the upper floor of the residence on the Subject and to add windows to the non-
conforming west wall, as part of a plan to expand and modify the existing
residence by increasing its height from 1½ stories to two stories and adding a
2½-story addition on the east side of the residence, in accordance with the
drawings and plans submitted with the application for variations; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and particular hardships
associated with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance
with respect to the Subject Property in that: (a) the 1½-story residence on the
Subject Property was built in 1906, before the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance was
first enacted and before the building line articulation requirement was added;
(b) the west building line of the residence, as originally built, is 47.68 feet
long, which exceeds the maximum allowable length of unarticulated building line
by 7.68 feet; (c) although the proposed upward extension of the west building
line is less than 40 feet, it is required to be articulated because the length
of the building line is measured at its longest point and the extent of the new
construction exceeds the scope of rehabilitation and thus triggers the
articulation requirement that applies to new construction; (d) the addition of
windows to the first floor of the west wall will not further intrude into the
west side yard; and (e) the size and configuration of the rooms in the existing
upper floor makes it impracticable to articulate the west wall of the addition,
as it would reduce usable space and thus defeat the purpose of extending the
walls upward from their existing location; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that;
(a) neither the location nor the lack of articulation of the existing west wall
can be cured without either removing substantial portions of the residence, or
demolishing it altogether; (b) the other homes in the immediate vicinity are
all at least two stories high and (c) not allowing windows to be added to the
west wall would deprive the dining room of natural light and reduce the value
of the residence; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood, in that the proposed second floor addition will be consistent
with the style of the existing house, is consistent with the height of other
residences in the immediate vicinity and will comply with building height
limitations; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not impair an adequate supply of
light and air, because the additions conform with building height requirements
and the new second floor will observe the same setbacks as the existing 1½-
story building; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property, as the proposed additions will comply
with all applicable building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not diminish the taxable value of
land and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed additions; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not contribute to congestion in the
public streets, as the structure will continue to be used as a single family
residence; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the requested variations will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village.
   WHEREAS, on February 12, 2001, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variations and by the
favorable vote of five of the six members then present, has reported to the
Council recommending that the variations be granted.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain:
   SECTION 1: That the foregoing recitals are incorporated as the findings of
the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth herein.
   SECTION 2: That the following variations are hereby granted with respect to
the Subject Property, commonly known as 1036 Spruce Street, which is located in
the R-5 Single Family Residential District provided in Chapter 17.12 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code: (a) a
variation from the side yard setback requirements of Section 17.12.010(I), to
allow a west side yard of 2.68 feet, whereas a side yard setback of 6.00 feet
is required, resulting in a variation of 3.32 feet (55.33%); and (b) a
variation from the building line articulation requirements of Section 17.12.010
(Q), said variations being requested to allow 38 feet and 7¾ inches of the
legally nonconforming, unarticulated 47.68-foot long west building line to be
extended vertically, to increase the height of the upper floor of the residence
on the Subject and to add windows to the non-conforming west wall, as part of a
plan to expand and modify the existing residence by increasing its height from
1½ stories to two stories and adding a 2½-story addition on the east side of
the residence, in accordance with the drawings and plans submitted with the
application for variations in accordance with the drawings and plans submitted
with the application for variations.
   SECTION 3: That the variations hereby granted are conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: That this Ordinance shall be in full force and effect from and
after its passage, approval, and posting.
   ADOPTED this 3rd day of April, 2001, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES: Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Presser and Woodbury
   NAYS: Trustees Lien and Powell
   ABSENT: None
   APPROVED this 3rd day of April, 2001.
   Signed:
   /s/Louise A. Holland
   Village President
Countersigned:
