ORDINANCE NO. M-14-2001
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A SPECIAL USE PERMIT TO ALLOW THE RENOVATION OF ELDER
LANE PARK
   WHEREAS, the Winnetka Park District is the owner of a parcel of real estate
(the “Subject Property”) located at 225 Sheridan Road, which is commonly known
as the Elder Lane Park and is legally described as follows:
Lots 2, 3 and 4 and the north 30 feet of Lot 5, and the vacated street known as
Bellevue Place, in the re-subdivision of Blocks 1, 2, 3 and 4, and the vacated
street known as Bellevue Place, lying between Blocks 1 and 2 on the east and
Blocks 3 and 4 on the west, in the Lake Shore Subdivision of Lot 1 in Nicholas
Simon and others’ Subdivision of part of the Southeast fractional quarter of
Section 21 and the Southwest fractional quarter of Section 22, Township 42
North, Range 13, East of the Third Principal Meridian, in Cook County,
Illinois.
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property is located adjacent to Lake Michigan in the R-
2 Single-Family Residential Zoning District of the Village of Winnetka, as
provided in Chapter 17.24 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the
Winnetka Village Code; and
   WHEREAS, Elder Lane Park is a public park consisting of a playground area,
bushes, patios, walkways, stairways, fencing, lighting and a parking area at
the top of the bluff, and a beach and beach house along the shore of the lake;
and
WHEREAS, Elder Lane Park is a public use, permitted as a special use in all
zoning districts, subject to the conditions and requirements pertaining to
special uses, as set forth in Chapter 17.56 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance;
and
   WHEREAS, the Winnetka Park District has submitted an application to the
Village of Winnetka seeking a special use permit pursuant to said Chapter
17.56, to permit the renovation of the existing facilities at Elder Lane Park
by providing new playground equipment, picnic tables, benches, patios,
walkways, stairways, fencing, landscaping and lighting, and by replacing the
beach house and by adding a handicap accessible parking space; and
   WHEREAS, upon due notice on April 9, 2001, the Zoning Board of Appeals held
a public hearing on the proposed special use and, by the unanimous vote of the
five members then present, has reported to the Council recommending that the
requested special use be granted; and
   WHEREAS, upon due notice, the Design Review Board met on April 5, 2001, to
consider the redesign of the playground and by the unanimous vote of the five
members then present, has submitted comment on the proposal, stating that the
proposed alterations would be an improvement to the park, that the playground,
lighting and arrangement of the fixtures are excellent additions, and that the
proposed new beach house, particularly the all-stone alternative, is an
improvement over the existing structure; and
   WHEREAS, upon due notice, the Plan Commission met on April 11, 2001, to
consider the application of the Park District and, by the unanimous vote of the
nine members then present, recommended that the special use be granted, based
on findings that the proposed renovations to Elder Lane Park are consistent
with (a) the goals and objectives of Winnetka 2020, the Winnetka Comprehensive
Plan, pertaining to the preservation of open space and recreational
opportunities, the encouragement of landscaping and other efforts to beautify
the Village, the reduction and control of light pollution, the preservation and
enhancement of the bluffs, beaches and Lake Michigan shorelines, and the use of
high quality design and materials for public improvement; and (b) with the
recommendations of Winnetka 2020 pertaining to providing adequate parking,
developing on-site solutions for parking, and minimizing the adverse impact of
recreational uses on adjacent residential neighborhoods, and
   WHEREAS, the proposed renovation of Elder Lane Park will benefit the public
health, safety, comfort, morals and general welfare of the Village, in that:
(a) the safety, accessibility and appearance of the entire park will be
improved by the installation of new playground equipment and safety surfacing;
and (b) the other proposed improvements will bring Elder Lane Park into
compliance with accessibility requirements of the Americans with Disabilities
Act, including providing accessibility to the beach that is not currently
provided; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed special use will not be substantially injurious to the
use and enjoyment of other property in the immediate vicinity, nor will it
substantially diminish or impair property values in the immediate vicinity,
because the park is established in the neighborhood, and the renovation of the
park: (a) will not increase the intensity of the existing use; (b) will enhance
parking lot screening; (c) will not result in the loss of green space; (d) will
improve both the functionality and appearance of the park; and (e) will improve
the appearance of the park because the Park District has modified its plans to
incorporate comments from the neighbors by replacing an unattractive chain link
fence with decorative fencing, using muted colors, and restricting light
placement, levels of illumination and hours of operation; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed special use will not impede the normal and orderly
development or improvement of other property in the immediate vicinity for uses
permitted under applicable zoning regulations, because the park is a long-
established use in the neighborhood and neither the type nor intensity of use
of the Subject Property will be changed; and
   WHEREAS, adequate measures have been taken to provide parking and to provide
ingress and egress in a manner that minimizes pedestrian and vehicular traffic
congestion by maintaining the existing parking area, including the existing
points of ingress and egress, and by adding a handicap accessible parking space
adjacent to the beach house; and
   WHEREAS, adequate access roads, utilities, drainage and other facilities
necessary to the operation of the special use exist, including existing
overhead utilities, which will be placed underground; and
   WHEREAS, the special use in all other respects conforms to the applicable
regulations of this and other Village ordinances and code.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain:
   SECTION 1:   The Village Council hereby adopts the foregoing recitals as
their findings, as if fully set forth herein.
   SECTION 2: Pursuant to Chapter 17.56 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title
17 of the Winnetka Village Code, and subject to the conditions hereinafter set
forth, a special use permit is hereby granted with respect to the Subject
Property, commonly known as Elder Lane Park, which is located in the R-
2 Single-Family Residential District provided in said Zoning Ordinance, to
permit the renovation of the Subject Property by installing new playground
equipment, picnic tables, benches, patios, walkways, stairways, fencing,
lighting and landscaping, by replacing the existing beach house and by adding
handicap accessible parking, said work to be done in accordance with the
application for special use permit and the plans dated October 19, 2000, with
modifications as presented to the Zoning Board of Appeals.
   SECTION 3: The special use permit hereby granted is subject to the following
conditions: (1) the proposed construction shall commence within 12 months after
the effective date of this Ordinance; (2) the proposed practice ball field,
soccer field and volleyball courts depicted in the bluff area of the site plan
shall not be used for regularly scheduled program activities; (3) no permanent
improvements or structures shall be added to allow the use, as such, of the
proposed ball field, soccer field and volleyball courts depicted in the bluff
area of the site plan; and (4) the final landscape, lighting plan and building
materials shall be subject to approval by the Village Council, following review
and recommendations from the Design Review Board and Forestry Commission.
   SECTION 4: Pursuant to Section 17.56.010.G of the Zoning Ordinance, all
stipulations, conditions and restrictions set forth in this Ordinance as part
of the terms under which the special use is granted, may be modified or revised
from time to time by the Village Council following public notice and hearing,
using the same procedures set forth in the Zoning Ordinance for processing the
original special use application.
   SECTION 5:   This Ordinance shall be in full force and effect from and after
its passage, approval, and posting.
   ADOPTED this 5th day of June, 2001, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES: Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser and Woodbury
   NAYS: None
   ABSENT: None
   APPROVED this 5th day of June, 2001.
   Signed:
   /s/Michael F. Duhl
   Village President
Countersigned:
/s/Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: May 15, 2001
Posted: May 16, 2001
Passed and Approved: June 5, 2001
Posted: June 6, 2001
ORDINANCE NO. M-15-2001
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (1005 Pine Tree Lane)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
Lot 17 in Blietz Winnetka Subdivision, being a subdivision of that part of the
East ½ of the southeast ¼ of the Northwest ¼ of Section 18, Township 42 North,
Range 13, East of the Third Principal Meridian, lying North of a line lying
midway between the center line of Asbury Avenue and Edgewood Lane extended West
of Grove Street, and that part of the West ½ of the southeast ¼ of the
Northwest ¼ lying North of the center line of Edgewood Lane extended West in
Section 18, Township 42 North, Range 13, East of the Third Principal Meridian
in Cook County, Illinois,
commonly known as 1005 Pine Tree Lane, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the
R-2 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.24 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for a
variation from the front setback requirements of Section 17.24.010(H) of the
Zoning Ordinance, to permit a new, 2-1/2 story residence to observe a front
setback of 25.0 feet along the north lot line, whereas a 50-foot setback is
required, resulting in a variation of 25 feet (50%); and
   WHEREAS, on May 14, 2001, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of Appeals
conducted a public hearing on the requested variation and by the unanimous vote
of the six members then present, has reported to the Council recommending that
the requested variation be granted, and recommending that the proposed
landscape plan be implemented to provide a buffer along the north property
line; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and unique hardships associated
with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect
to the Subject Property in that: (a) the Subject Property is an irregularly
shaped lot, having a 270.00-foot north lot line, 235.35-foot south lot line,
66.92-foot west lot line, 79.75 east lot line, and a curved lot line at the
southwest corner where the Subject Property abuts the Pine Tree Lane cul-de-
sac; (b) the north lot line of the Subject Property is at the north Village
limits and abuts a 40-foot wide public right-of-way known as Krieger Avenue;
(c) the Subject Property is required to observe 50-foot front setbacks from
both the north lot line (Krieger Avenue) and the cul-de-sac; and (d) the
combined effect of the large setbacks and the irregular shape of the Subject
Property result in an unusually long and narrow buildable area that severely
limits the size and location of what can be built on the Subject Property and
does not allow for the construction of a reasonably sized residence in a
conforming location; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that
allowing the owners to construct a home only in a conforming location (a) will
effectively prevent them from replacing the existing, dilapidated home, which
is also in a nonconforming location and (b) will result in an oddly shaped,
long, narrow home that is inconsistent with housing standards in the Village;
and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because (a) the proposed new residence will be of a character
and scale similar to the other homes in the area , (b) the requested 25-foot
north setback will be greater than the setback observed by the existing home,
and (c) the requested 25-foot north setback is mitigated by the proposed
planting of an arbor vitae buffer along the north lot line; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not impair an adequate supply of light
and air because the new residence will comply with the height and size
limitations of the Zoning Ordinance and because the residences to the north are
further buffered by the 40-foot Krieger Avenue right-of-way; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property, the proposed construction will comply
with all applicable building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not contribute to congestion on the
public streets, as the use of the Subject Property will not change and off-
street parking will be provided; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the requested variation will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation is in harmony with the general purpose and
intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that it maintains the existing
scale and appearance of the community and will allow several large, established
trees on the Subject Property to remain.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2:  There is hereby granted to the Subject Property, commonly known
as 1005 Pine Tree Lane, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-2 Single-
Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.24 of the Winnetka
Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, a variation from the
front setback requirements of Section 17.24.010(H) of the Zoning Ordinance, to
permit a new, 2-1/2 story residence to observe a front setback of 25.0 feet
along the north lot line, whereas a 50-foot setback is required, resulting in a
variation of 25 feet (50%), subject to the conditions hereinafter set forth.
   SECTION 3: That the variation hereby granted is subject to the conditions
(a) that the proposed construction commence within 12 months after the
effective date of this Ordinance; and (b) that a landscape buffer be provided
along the north lot line, as presented to the Zoning Board of Appeals.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 19th day of June, 2001, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser and Woodbury
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    None
   APPROVED this 19th day of June, 2001.
   Signed:
   //s//Michael F. Duhl
   Village President
Countersigned:
//s//Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: June 5, 2001
Posted: June 6, 2001
Passed and Approved: June 19, 2001
Posted: June 20, 2001
ORDINANCE NO. M-16-2001
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (518 WINNETKA AVENUE)
   WHEREAS, the property commonly known as 518 Winnetka Avenue (the “Subject
Property”), Winnetka, Illinois, is located in the C-1 Limited Retail Commercial
District provided in Chapter 17.40 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17
of the Winnetka Village Code, and is legally described as follows:
Lots 1 and 2 in Block 2 in Manus’ Indian Hill Subdivision of those parts of the
North ½ of Section 28, Township 42 North, Range 13, East of the Third Principal
Meridian in Cook County, Illinois (excepting therefrom that part of Lot 1
described as follows: that part lying northeasterly of a straight line
extending from a point in the East line of Lot 1, 20 feet South of the
Northeast corner of said Lot to a point on the North line of said Lot, 20 feet
West of the Northeast corner conveyed for roadway purposes);
and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property, which is located at the intersections of
Green Bay Road, Winnetka Ave and Brier Street, was the site of a gasoline and
automobile service station for more than 30 years and was used only as an
illegal parking lot after the service station closed in 1999; and
   WHEREAS, on August 1, 2000, the Winnetka Village Council adopted Ordinance
M-16-2000, granting the following variations: (a) a variation from the maximum
building height of 35 feet and 2-½ stories permitted by Section 17.40.010.C of
the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, to allow the construction of a building that had
a third floor with an area that was 50% of the area of the floor below; (b)
variations from the maximum permitted setback of 3 feet set forth in Section
17.40.010.N of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, to allow the street level of the
proposed building to observe a setback of 4 feet along Winnetka Avenue, Green
Bay Road and Brier Street, to allow the second story to be set back 5 feet and
to allow the third floor to be set back 16.50 feet, resulting in variations of
6 feet, 2 feet and 13.50 feet, respectively; and (c) a variation from the
parking requirements set forth in Section 17.40.010.K.1 of the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance, to allow 20 parking spaces rather than the 23 spaces required for
this development; and
   WHEREAS, on October 5 and November 16, 2000, the applicant appeared before
the Design Review Board for consideration of designs based on the building
envelope established under Ordinance M-16-2000, but was unable to obtain
favorable comment on the proposed designs; and
   WHEREAS, on December 15, 2000, the applicants submitted an application for a
variation from the maximum building height of 35 feet and 2-½ stories of
Section 17.40.010(C) of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, to allow the
construction of a modified design with a third floor that has an area of 72% of
the area of the floor below; and
   WHEREAS, the December 15, 2000, application to the Zoning Board of Appeals
complied with required setbacks and retained the parking configuration approved
by the Village Council under Ordinance M-16-2000; and
   WHEREAS, on December 21, 2000, the applicant appeared before the Design
Review Board with the revised design concept, which received a more favorable
comment than the previous design efforts, but did not receive final approval;
and
   WHEREAS, on January 8, 2001, the Zoning Board of Appeals held a public
hearing on the requested height variation and by the unanimous vote of the six
members then present, reported to the Council recommending that the requested
variation be denied; and
   WHEREAS, due to the negative recommendation from the Zoning Board of
Appeals, the application was placed on the Council’s agenda for policy
direction on February 1, 2001, at which time the Council expressed concern
about considering another request for zoning relief based on a design concept
that had not been fully considered by the Design Review Board; and
   WHEREAS, pursuant to the directive of the Village Council following the
February 1, 2001, Council meeting, the applicant appeared before the Design
Review Board on March 1, April 5, and May 3, 2001, during which meetings the
applicant and Design Review Board addressed numerous design changes, including
a revision to the upper floor roof configuration from a flat roof to a gable
roof, which reduced the vertical height to a conforming 35 feet; and
   WHEREAS, during the time the applicant’s development has been pending the
Village has been developing design guidelines, which were reviewed by the
Village Council on May 1, 2001, and adopted by the Design Review Board on May
17, 2001; and
   WHEREAS, at its meeting on May 17, 2001, the Design Review Board reviewed
further modifications to the applicant’s proposed building design and issued a
recommendation to approve the Certificate of Appropriateness for the building
design; and
   WHEREAS, the Design Review Board also submitted comments to the Village
Council recommending: (a) that the applicant explore brick mould casings on the
exterior upper level residential windows; and (b) that the size of proposed
light fixtures be studied further; and
   WHEREAS, on May 30, 2001, the Forestry Commission considered the proposed
landscaping plan and the impact of the proposed development on trees near the
south and west property lines, but did not provide any comment to the Council;
and
   WHEREAS, on June 19, 2001, the applicant’s request was placed on the
Council’s agenda for policy direction and, after considering the entire record,
directed the preparation of an ordinance granting the requested height
variation; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation is in harmony with the general purpose and
intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, as the proposal, as modified, complies
with the stated district purpose of providing neighborhood commercial services
complemented by multi-family residential uses, while preserving the character
of the adjoining single family residential uses; and
   WHEREAS, in addition to the practical difficulties and particular hardships
recited in the Council’s findings in Ordinance M-16-2000, the Council finds
that (a) the applicant has demonstrated good faith in attempting to present a
building design that conforms with the Zoning Ordinance and receives favorable
design review; (b) despite the applicant’s good faith efforts, it has been
unable to present a building design that could obtain favorable recommendations
from both the Zoning Board of Appeals and the Design Review Board; (c) the
design that is now before the Council is less massive and more in harmony with
the neighborhood and with the new design guidelines than both the initial,
fully conforming design concept and the design concept approved under Ordinance
M-16-2000; and (d) the building that is now before the Council is smaller in
usable area than the conforming building design initially submitted for review;
and
   WHEREAS, the findings recited in support of the variations granted under
Ordinance M-16-2000 are supported by the full record now before the Council and
the Council therefore incorporates those findings by reference, as if fully set
forth herein.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain:
   SECTION 1: The foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the findings of
the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth herein.
   SECTION 2: Subject to the terms and conditions hereinafter set forth, the
following variation is hereby granted with respect to the Subject Property,
which is commonly known as 518 Winnetka Avenue, Winnetka, Illinois, and is
located in the C-1 Limited Retail Commercial District provided in Chapter 17.40
of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code a
variation from the maximum building height of 2-½ stories permitted by Section
17.40.010.C of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, to allow the construction of a 3-
story building as depicted in the plans and specifications submitted to the
Village Council in the materials entitled “The Belvedere,” bearing a cover
dated June 19, 2001, which plans and specifications: (a) incorporate the plans
and specifications approved by the Council in Ordinance M-16-2000, as modified
for consideration by the Zoning Board of Appeals dated December 15, 2000, and
further modified for consideration by Design Review Board on May 17, 2001; and
(b) are attached to this Ordinance as Exhibit A and are incorporated herein by
reference.
   SECTION 3: The variation granted by this Ordinance is subject to the
condition that the final building design conform to the design and materials
depicted in the attached Exhibit A.
   SECTION 4: The variation granted herein is conditioned upon the installation
of landscaping and lighting pursuant to final landscaping and lighting plans
that shall meet the following standards:
      A.   The final lighting plan shall be subject to the final approval of
the Village Council after review and comment by the Design Review Board. The
final lighting plan shall be designed so as to safely illuminate the premises
while minimizing the spillage of light to adjoining properties. The final
lighting plan shall include a detailed description of all exterior lighting
fixtures, including the height of the fixtures and the intensity of light they
generate, and a description of all steps taken to mitigate the impact of
lighting on adjoining properties, including proposed hours of operation,
direction of light and any light filtering or baffling devices to be used. The
Council reserves the right to exercise its discretion in reviewing the final
lighting plan and to modify the lighting requirement without amending this
ordinance.
      B.   The final landscaping plan shall be subject to the final approval of
the Village Council after review and comment by the Forestry Commission and the
Design Review Board. The final landscaping plan shall use materials approved by
the Village Forester and shall be designed so as to enhance the appearance of
the proposed building and to provide a visual buffer for the adjoining single
family residential property owners, without interfering with sight lines for
traffic exiting the two parking areas. The final landscaping plan shall also
provide a planting strip up to three feet wide along the Winnetka Avenue and
Brier Street frontages of the Subject Property, except that the Council
reserves the right to exercise its discretion in reviewing the final landscape
plan and to modify the planting strip requirement without amending this
ordinance.
   SECTION 5: The variation granted herein is further conditioned upon the
contract purchaser, as developer of the Subject Property, signing a development
agreement with the Village, as required by subsection K of Section 15.32.080 of
the Winnetka Village Code, within 12 months of the date of this ordinance.
   SECTION 6: The variation granted herein is conditioned upon the commencement
of the proposed construction within twelve months after the effective date of
this Ordinance.
   SECTION 7: This ordinance is not intended to supersede or alter the parking
variation and grade level parking special use permit granted pursuant to
Ordinance M-16-2000, which was adopted by the Council on August 1, 2000. This
ordinance is intended to supersede Ordinance M-16-2000, as follows: (a) the
height variation granted herein shall supersede the height variation granted in
Section 2(a) of Ordinance M-16-2000; (b) the lighting and landscaping
conditions of this ordinance shall supersede the lighting and landscaping
provisions set forth in Section 4 of Ordinance M-16-2001; and (c) the
requirement of Section 7 of Ordinance M-16-2000 to begin construction within 12
months after the effective date of that ordinance shall be superseded by
Section 6 of this ordinance.
   SECTION 8: In the event that any provision of this ordinance conflicts with
any ordinance of the Village, the provisions of this ordinance shall prevail.
   SECTION 9: That this Ordinance shall be in full force and effect from and
after its passage, approval, and posting.
   Passed and approved this 17th day of July, 2001.
   AYES:      Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Presser and Woodbury
   NAYS:   Trustee Powell
   ABSENT:   None
               Signed:
                  //s//Michael F. Duhl
                  Village President
Countersigned:
//s//Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
