ORDINANCE NO. M-17-2001
AN ORDINANCE ESTABLISHING 20 FOX LANE AS A DESIGNATED LANDMARK
   WHEREAS, the Village of Winnetka desires to identify, designate and preserve
buildings and structures in the Village that are historically, culturally, and
architecturally significant and to encourage the restoration and rehabilitation
of those buildings and structures; and
   WHEREAS, pursuant to Sections 11-48.2-1, et seq. of the Illinois Municipal
Code (65 ILCS 5/11-48.2-1, et seq.), the Council of the Village of Winnetka
enacted Chapter 15.64 of Title 15 of the Winnetka Village Code, entitled
“Landmark Preservation,” for the purpose of preserving, protecting, enhancing,
rehabilitating and regulating buildings, structures, objects, and places of
historical, cultural or architectural importance; and
   WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka have determined that it is
in the best interests of the Village and its residents that participation in
the landmark designation process shall be limited to those owners who
voluntarily elect to participate in that process; and
   WHEREAS, pursuant to the provisions of Chapter 15.64 of the Winnetka Village
Code (the “Landmark Ordinance”) and notice duly published and sent, the
Landmark Preservation Commission conducted a public hearing on June 18, 2001,
to consider the application for landmark designation submitted by the owner of
the property located at 20 Fox Lane (permanent real estate index number 05-29-
202-035); and
   WHEREAS, the Landmark Preservation Commission applied the Village’s System
for the Evaluation of Landmarks to the information received at the hearing and
made findings regarding the property at 20 Fox Lane, including the following:
   (1)   That it is a unique property, which the Commission found to be
extremely rare, in that it is a Georgian Revival residence that features
nuances of a Georgian country house plan, site plan and elevations, with a
symmetrical façade, white painted masonry walls, and windows, shutters, details
and garden walls all as originally constructed;
   (2)   That the house is further distinguished by: (a) a front façade with a
central projecting three-window bay surmounted by a pediment and flanked by
three-bay wings on either side; (b) a single-story, projecting portico with two
Tuscan columns on either side supporting a well-proportioned classical
entablature; (c) an oval oculus with finely delineated muntins at the center of
the pediment; (d) strong dentil molding, consistent with the Georgian period,
along the pediment and roof cornice; (e) four massive masonry chimneys that
project well above the roof, creating a visual symmetry that frames the
composition; and (f) a servant’s wing with two additional bay windows, that
extends to the east to a small yard where a garden wall conceals the garage,
all of which make the house extremely rare in the context of Winnetka; and
   (3)   That the method of construction of the house is extremely rare due to
the exterior masonry walls with reinforced concrete floors, which is indicative
of the substantial nature of the architectural commission, was limited to very
few houses in Winnetka, and is never seen in today’s construction; and
   (4)   That the residence merits the Commission’s highest point value for
association with a master architect or builder, having been designed and built
in 1936 by architect Stanley Anderson, which gives it special local historical
significance; and
   (5)   That the design integrity of the exterior of the building was rated as
excellent, since the house remains largely unaltered from when it was completed
in 1936, except for the enclosure of a rear porch and the addition of an
ancillary garage, neither of which substantially affects the character of the
house; and
   WHEREAS, based on its findings, the Landmark Preservation Commission
concluded that the residence located at 20 Fox Lane meets the criteria of the
Landmark Ordinance and unanimously recommended that it be so designated; and
   WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka have considered the
recommendation of the Landmark Preservation Commission and have determined that
it is in the best interest of the Village and its residents to establish the
Everett D. Graff house, located at 20 Fox Lane, as a designated landmark.
   NOW THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain:
   SECTION 1: That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2: That the property located at 20 Fox Lane, known as the Everett D.
Graff house, is hereby designated a landmark under the Landmark Ordinance, in
accordance with the findings and recommendations of the Landmark Preservation
Commission.
   SECTION 3: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon its passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 21st day of August, 2001, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser and Woodbury
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    None
   APPROVED this 21st day of August, 2001.
