ORDINANCE NO. M-19-2001
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (1009 OLD GREEN BAY ROAD)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
That part of Lot 1 lying southerly and westerly of the following described
lines, beginning at the intersection of a line 1400.0 feet north of and
parallel with the south line of the northeast fractional quarter of Section 17,
Township 42 North, Range 13, east of the third principal meridian, with the
east line of Green Bay Road, thence east parallel with south line of said
northeast fractional quarter a distance of 60.0 feet thence southeasterly along
a line making an angle with the last described course of 122 degrees, 35
minutes (as measured from west to southeast) a distance of 223.0 feet more or
less to the southerly line of said lot 1, all in the subdivision of Block 9 in
Hubbard Estate Subdivision in the northeast fractional quarter of Section 17,
Township 42 North, Range 13 East of the third principal meridian in Cook
County, Illinois,
commonly known as 1009 Old Green Bay Road, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in
the R-2 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.24 of
the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for the
following variations: (a) a variation from the front setback requirements of
Section 17.24.010(H) of the Zoning Ordinance, to allow a proposed 4-foot by
40.33-foot, one-story addition to the existing single-family residence to
observe a front setback of 46.75 feet instead of the minimum required 50 feet,
resulting in a variation of 3.25 feet (6.50%), and (b) a variation from the
rear yard setback requirements of Section 17.24.010(J) of the Zoning Ordinance
to allow proposed 18-inch by 12.00-foot one-story bay window addition to the
existing single-family residence to reduce the legally nonconforming rear yard
from 16.33 feet to 15.55 feet, a variation of 9.45 feet (37.80%) from the
required minimum rear yard setback of 25 feet; and
   WHEREAS, on July 9, 2001, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of Appeals
conducted a public hearing on the requested variations and has made the
following recommendations to the Council: (a) by the vote of 6 ayes and one
nay, the Zoning Board of Appeals has recommended that the front setback
variation be granted, and (b) by the vote of 6 ayes and one nay, the Zoning
Board of Appeals has recommended that the rear yard setback variation be
denied; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and unique hardships associated
with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect
to the Subject Property in that: (a) the Subject Property is an irregularly
shaped located in the ravines; (b) the Subject Property has a 172-foot front
(west) lot line along Old Green Bay Road and a 223-foot long rear (east) lot
line, that extends southeast from the 60-foot north side lot line at an angle
of 122 degrees, 35 minutes; (c) the Subject Property has a meandering south lot
line formed by the Winnetka ravines; (d) although the area of the Subject
property is 0.63 acres, approximately 0.23 acres of the lot is in the ravines;
(e) the irregular shape of the lot and the presence of the ravines limits the
area that is suitable for building; (f) the existing residence was constructed
in 1987 with its kitchen located on the west side of the house, facing the
front lot line; and (e) the purposes of the proposed addition to the west side
of the residence are to expand and reconfigure the kitchen and adjacent mudroom
area, and to improve access to the finished area of the basement by enclosing
an existing, exterior stairway and removing a nonconforming circular stairway
located in the family room; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that:
(a) the existing kitchen is relatively small in relation to the rest of the
residence and cannot be expanded without encroaching into the front setback;
(b) the proposed addition to the west side of the residence will also improve
the view of the property from the street by eliminating the existing concrete
stairwell; (c) the proposed addition to the west side of the residence will
provide code-compliant access to the finished basement and will allow the
owners to remove the existing, non-compliant circular stairway currently in the
family room; and (d) there are no alternative locations for the improved
stairway or the expanded kitchen, due to the location and configuration of the
existing house; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because the proposed addition will be less than half the
length of the west wall of the residence, will be located approximately in the
center of the west wall, will be consistent with the design of the existing
residence, and will improve the appearance of the building from the street by
eliminating the exposed, concrete stairwell; and
   WHEREAS, the requested front setback variation will not impair an adequate
supply of light and air because the proposed addition will be only one-story
high, will be more than 70 feet from the curb and will be more than 100 feet
from any neighboring residence; and
   WHEREAS, the requested front setback variation will not increase the hazard
from fire and other dangers to the Subject Property, as it will comply with all
applicable building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested front setback variation will not diminish the taxable
value of land and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of
the Subject Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, allowing the proposed one-story addition on the west side of the
existing residence will not contribute to congestion on the public streets, as
the addition will not change the existing single-family use and will not
increase the occupancy of the residence; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed one-story addition on the
west side of the residence will otherwise impair the public health, safety,
comfort, morals, and welfare of the inhabitants of the Village.
   WHEREAS, the requested variation is in harmony with the general purpose and
intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that it allows the renovation,
restoration and rehabilitation of a structurally sound existing building while
maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the community and protecting
established trees and landscaping.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2: The following variation is hereby granted to the Subject
Property, commonly known as 1009 Old Green Bay Road, Winnetka, Illinois, and
located in the R-2 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in
Chapter 17.24 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka
Village Code: a variation from the front setback requirements of Section
17.24.010(H) of the Zoning Ordinance, to allow a proposed 4-foot by 40.33-foot,
one-story addition to the existing single-family residence to observe a front
setback of 46.75 feet instead of the minimum required 50 feet, resulting in a
variation of 3.25 feet (6.50%), said addition to be constructed in accordance
with the plans and elevations submitted with the application on June 8, 2001.
   SECTION 3: That the variation hereby granted is conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 4th day of September, 2001, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser and Woodbury
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    None
   APPROVED this 4th day of September, 2001.
   Signed:
   /s/Michael F. Duhl
   Village President
Countersigned:
/s/Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
