ORDINANCE NO. M-20-2001
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A SPECIAL USE PERMIT AND VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF
THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA, COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (1070
Gage Street and 928-930 Green Bay Road)
   WHEREAS, Harris Trust and Savings Bank as successor Trustee to Harris Bank
Winnetka, as successor Trustee to the First National Bank of Winnetka as
Trustee U/T/A dated August 20, 1976 and known as Trust No. 587 (hereinafter,
“Harris Bank”), is the owner of the following described real estate (the
“Subject Property”):
Lots 1 and 2 of Harris Bank Winnetka Subdivision, being a part of the east half
of the northwest quarter of Section 17, Township 42 North, Range 13 East of the
Third Principal Meridian, in Cook County, Illinois,
commonly known as 1070 Gage Street and 928-930 Green Bay Road, Winnetka,
Illinois, and located in the C-2 General Retail Commercial Zoning District
provided in Chapter 17.44 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the
Winnetka Village Code; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property, as legally described above, was created by a
subdivision approved by the Council of the Village of Winnetka pursuant to its
adoption of Resolution R-30-2001, on August 21, 2001; and
   WHEREAS, prior to the approval of the Harris Bank Winnetka Subdivision, the
Subject Property was legally described as follows:
Lots 1 and 2 (except the Southwesterly 16 feet of each said lots) in Block 6 in
Jared Gage’s subdivision of part of the East half of the Northwest quarter and
part of the West half of the Northwest quarter of fractional Section 17 and
part of the East half of the Southwest quarter of fractional Section 17 and
part of the East half of the Southwest quarter of fractional Section 8,
Township 42 North, Range 13, East of the Third Principal Meridian, in Cook
County, Illinois,
commonly known as 1070 Gage Street and 928-930 Green Bay Road, Winnetka,
Illinois; and
   WHEREAS, Lot 1 of the Subject Property, commonly known as 1070 Gage Street,
is improved with a bank building with a drive-through facility that has its
ingress from Tower Road by way of the public alley that extends north from
Tower Road and lies adjacent to the west lot line of the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, Lot 2 of the Subject Property , commonly known as 923-930 Green Bay
Road, is improved with a one-story commercial building with retail space facing
green Bay Road and a paved area in the rear, which includes on-site parking and
also provides access to the drive-through banking facility on Lot 1; and
   WHEREAS, the Plat of Subdivision dividing the Subject Property into Lots 1
and 2 reserves an easement over a portion of Lot 2 to allow continued access to
the drive-through banking facility on Lot 1; and
   WHEREAS, the bank has existed on the Subject Property for at least 25 years
and exists as a lawful special use pursuant to Section 17.56(K) of the Winnetka
Zoning Ordinance because it existed before the Zoning Ordinance was amended to
designate banks and drive-through facilities as special uses in the C-
2 Commercial Zoning District; and
   WHEREAS, Harris Bank has filed an application for a special use permit
pursuant to Section 17.44.010(B)(2) and Chapter 17.56 of the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance, seeking to permit the alteration of the existing special use by
subdividing the underlying parcels into two new lots of record; and
   WHEREAS, Harris Bank has also applied for a variation from the intensity of
use of lot limitations of Section 17.44.010(E) of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance
to permit an increase in the impermeable coverage of Lot 2 of the Subject
Property, which is the southerly lot resulting from the subdivision approved by
Resolution No. R-30-2001, said variation being requested to allow a total lot
coverage of 5,488.64 square feet, which exceeds the maximum permitted lot
coverage of 4,939.77, resulting in a variation of 548.87 square feet (11.11%);
and
   WHEREAS, on July 9, 2001, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of Appeals
conducted a public hearing on the requested special use and variation and,
following the recusal of one of the seven members then present, the remaining
six members voted unanimously to recommend to the Council that the requested
special use permit and variation be granted; and
   WHEREAS, on July 25, 2001, on due notice thereof, the Plan Commission
reviewed the proposed special use permit and, by the unanimous vote of the nine
members then in attendance, found that the proposed special use permit is
consistent with the Winnetka Comprehensive Plan, Winnetka 2020; and
   WHEREAS, the establishment, maintenance and operation of the special use
will not be detrimental to or endanger the public health, safety, comfort,
morals or general welfare, because no physical change will be made to the
Subject Property and it will continue to operate as it has since 1975, without
change to the location or configuration of the bank and drive-through facility
on Lot 1and the commercial building, parking and partial access to the drive-
through banking facility being provided on Lot 2; and
   WHEREAS, the special use will not be substantially injurious to the use and
enjoyment of other property in the immediate vicinity for uses that are
permitted by right in the C-2 Commercial Zoning District, nor will the special
use substantially diminish or impair property values in the immediate vicinity,
because the bank facilities have been located at this site for at least 25
years without substantial injury to the use and enjoyment of other property in
the neighborhood and because the bank provides useful services to residents and
businesses; and
   WHEREAS, the establishment of the proposed special use will not impede the
normal and orderly development or improvement of other property in the
immediate vicinity for uses permitted by right in the C-2 Commercial Zoning
District, as the bank has been on the Subject Property for at least 25 years,
and the adjacent retail property will be improved by the new owner; and
   WHEREAS, adequate measures have been taken to provide ingress and egress in
a manner that minimizes pedestrian and vehicular traffic congestion in the
public ways, because: (a) ingress and egress for the drive-through banking
facility will continue its existing pattern; (b) pedestrian access to the bank
will continue to be from Gage Street; (c) on-street parking adjacent to the
Subject Property will continue; (d) on-site parking on Lot 2 of the Subject
Property will remain; and (e) appropriate signage will continue to be provided
to direct customers to the bank’s entrance; and
   WHEREAS, adequate parking, utilities, access roads, drainage and other
facilities necessary to the operation of the special use exist, as evidenced
by: (a) the continuing operation of the bank and adjacent businesses on the
Subject Property for at least 25 years; (b) the existence of adjacent and
nearby on-street parking; (c) the continuation of on-site parking on Lot 2; (d)
the provision of utility easements and utility tie-ins in the basement of the
building on Lot 2; (e) the existence of on-site drainage on Lot 1, which
complies with impermeable surface limitations; and (f) the existence of a
loading area on the Subject Property near the rear entrance of the building;
and
   WHEREAS, the special use at the proposed location will encourage, facilitate
and enhance the continuity, concentration and pedestrian nature of the area in
a manner similar to that of retail uses of a comparison shopping nature, in
that the bank facilitates shopping at the retail uses in the area, by providing
walk-up banking and convenient ATM use, and by providing a location for owners,
operators and employees of retail stores in the area to bank; and
   WHEREAS, street frontages providing access to or visibility for the special
use provide for a minimum interruption in the existing and potential continuity
and concentration of retail uses of a comparison shopping nature, in that the
bank is located on the southwest corner of Green Bay Road and Gage Street and
the bank building is an established presence that fits in with the surrounding
area; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed special use will provide for display windows, facades,
signage and lighting similar in nature and compatible with that provided by
retail uses of a comparison shopping nature, in that the bank building is
designed with display windows that are compatible in appearance and size with
nearby retail uses, and the signage and lighting for the building, which have
previously been approved by the Village, will be maintained; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed special use is not subject to subparagraph (a)(4) of
Section 17.44.010(B)(2) of Chapter 17.44, which establishes minimum frontages
for retail uses when a building has or proposes a mix of retail and office
uses, since the bank on Lot 1 of the Subject Property is not a mixed use
building and the building on Lot 2 has retail space on the first floor; and
   WHEREAS, the continued location and operation of the bank and drive-through
will not significantly diminish the availability of parking for district
clientele wishing to patronize existing retail businesses of a comparison
shopping nature, because the drive-through facility allows customers who desire
only to transact business at the bank to perform their banking transactions
without occupying any parking spaces; and
   WHEREAS, the requested special use in all other respects conforms to the
applicable regulations of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance and other Village
ordinances and codes; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and unique hardships associated
with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect
to the Subject Property in that: (a) the Subject Property consists of the
banking facility at 1070 Gage Street and two store fronts in a one-story
building at 928-930 Green Bay Road; (b) the uses on the Subject Property have
existed in the current configuration with no front setbacks for the commercial
buildings on Lot 2 and with pavement extending to the rear of both parcels; (c)
none of the existing conditions of the buildings will change; and (d) removal
of the impermeable surface at the rear of Lot 2 would interfere with ingress to
the drive-through on Lot 1and would result in the loss of parking spaces behind
the commercial buildings on Lot 2; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that
there is no economically viable conforming alternative, as the only way to cure
the nonconformity would be to demolish the building and parking area and to
construct a new building; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because there will be no change to the exterior of the
buildings on the Subject Property and other buildings in the vicinity have
similar storefronts with paved parking surfaces in the rear; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not impair an adequate supply of light
and air because the variation does not require a change to the existing
buildings; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property as it will comply with all applicable
building and fire protection codes and the variation will not require any
change to the existing building; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not contribute to congestion on the
public streets, as the building on Lot 2 will continue to be used as a retail
building and the variation will allow the use to be supported by a small
parking area at the rear of the building, although on-site parking is not
required for first floor retail space; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed variation will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation is in harmony with the general purpose and
intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that it allows an existing retail
building with a parking area in the rear to continue to be used for retail
purposes supported by on-site, off-street parking.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2:  That a special use is hereby granted to the Subject Property,
commonly known as 1070 Gage Street and 928-930 Green Bay Road, Winnetka,
Illinois, and located in the C-2 General Retail Commercial Zoning District
provided in Chapter 17.44 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the
Winnetka Village Code, said special use permit being granted pursuant to
Section 17.44.010(B)(2) and Chapter 17.56 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, to
permit alteration of the existing special use, consisting of a bank and drive-
through facility, by subdividing the underlying property into two separate lots
of record, as provided in the Plat of Subdivision approved pursuant to
resolution R-30-2001.
   SECTION 3: That a variation is hereby granted to the Subject Property,
commonly known as 1070 Gage Street and 928-930 Green Bay Road, Winnetka,
Illinois, and located in the C-2 General Retail Commercial Zoning District
provided in Chapter 17.44 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the
Winnetka Village Code, said variation being from the intensity of use of lot
limitations of Section 17.44.010(E) of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance to allow a
total lot coverage of 5,488.64 square feet on Lot 2 of the Subject Property,
which is the southerly lot resulting from the subdivision approved by
Resolution No. R-30-2001, said impermeable surface exceeding the maximum
permitted lot coverage of 4,939.77, resulting in a variation of 548.87 square
feet (11.11%), to allow the existing paved area at the rear of the commercial
building of Lot 2 to continue to provide on-site parking for Lot 2 and access
to the drive-through banking facility on Lot 1.
   SECTION 4: That the variation and special use hereby granted are conditioned
upon the recording of the Final Plat of Subdivision to the Harris Bank Winnetka
Subdivision within 12 months after the effective date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 5: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 4th day of September, 2001, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Aquilino, Greenough, Powell, Presser and Woodbury
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    Brower (Recused)   
   APPROVED this 4th day of September, 2001.
   Signed:
   /s/Barbara Aquilino
   Village President Pro Tem
Countersigned:
/s/Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: August 21, 2001
Posted: August 22, 2001
Passed and Approved: September 4, 2001
Posted: September 5, 2001
ORDINANCE NO. M-21-2001
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA, COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (503 Orchard Lane)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
The East sixty three feet (63) of the South half of Lot 11 in Garland’s
Subdivision in the Southwest quarter of Section 21, Township 42 North, Range
13, East of the Third Principal Meridian, in Cook County, Illinois,
commonly known as 503 Orchard Lane, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
5 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.16 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for a
variation from the maximum building size limitations of Section 17.16.010(G) of
the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, to allow a gross floor area of 4,360.06 square
feet, although the maximum permitted gross floor area is 3,895.51 square feet,
resulting in a variation of 464.57 square feet (11.93%), for the purpose of
reconstructing and expanding part of the second floor of northern ell of the
single-family residence on the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, on July 9, 2001, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of Appeals
conducted a public hearing on the requested variation and, by the favorable
vote of five of the seven members then present, has reported to the Council
recommending that the requested variation be granted; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and unique hardships associated
with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect
to the Subject Property in that: (a) the single-family residence on the Subject
Property is a center hall Georgian style building, which limits the ability to
modify the interior of the residence without disrupting the floor plan; (b) the
existing gross floor area is a legally nonconforming 4,371.75 square feet, and
the proposed reconstruction of the second floor ell will reduce the degree of
nonconformity while maximizing the interior space; (c) the proposed
reconstruction of the second floor ell will align the interior floor levels and
eliminate two existing shed dormers; and (d) the proposed reconstruction of the
second floor ell will allow for the addition of an elevator to provide full
accessibility for the owners; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that
the purpose of the proposed alteration is to allow continued use of the
residence by allowing full access to the upper floor and the applicants have
demonstrated that there are no reasonably available alternatives to such
access, as alternative solutions would disrupt the layout and reduce the
functionality of the house and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because the proposed reconstruction of the second floor ell is
consistent with the existing architecture, will reduce the calculable gross
floor area, and will eliminate two shed dormers, thereby providing a more
unified appearance for the second floor of the residence; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not impair an adequate supply of light
and air because the proposed expansion of the second floor ell is within the
building height and setback limitations of the Zoning Ordinance and the nearest
building to the area to be reconstructed is a garage in the property to the
west; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property as it will comply with all applicable
building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed reconstruction of the second floor of the northern ell
of the residence will not contribute to congestion on the public streets, as
the building will continue to be used as a single-family residence and the
capacity of the building will not increase; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed expansion of the second
floor ell will otherwise impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and
welfare of the inhabitants of the Village.
   WHEREAS, the requested variation is in harmony with the general purpose and
intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that it allows the renovation,
restoration and rehabilitation of a structurally sound existing building while
maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the community and protecting
established trees and landscaping.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : The foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the findings
of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth herein.
   SECTION 2:  There is hereby granted to the Subject Property, commonly known
as 503 Orchard Lane, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-5 Single-Family
Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.16 of the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, a variation from the maximum
building size limitations of Section 17.16.010(G) of the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance, to allow a gross floor area of 4,360.06 square feet, although the
maximum permitted gross floor area is 3,895.51 square feet, resulting in a
variation of 464.57 square feet (11.93%), for the purpose of reconstructing and
expanding part of the northern ell on the second floor of the single-family
residence on the Subject Property, in accordance with the plans and elevations
submitted with the application on June 6, 2001.
   SECTION 3: The variation hereby granted is conditioned upon the commencement
of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective date of this
Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 4th day of September, 2001, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser and Woodbury
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    None
   APPROVED this 4th day of September, 2001.
   Signed:
   /s/Barbara Aquilino
   Village President Pro Tem
Countersigned:
/s/Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
