ORDINANCE NO. M-24-2001
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (381 Poplar Street)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
That part lying North of a line drawn parallel to and 112.0 feet North of the
North line of Willow Road of the following described property: The west 65.0
feet measured from the east line of Poplar Street of the South ½ of Block 67 in
Winnetka, a Subdivision of the Northeast ¼ of Section 20 and the Northeast
fractional ½ of Section 21, all in Township 42 North, Range 13, East of the
Third Principal Meridian, in Cook County, Illinois.
commonly known as 381 Poplar Street, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
4 Single Family Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.16 of the Winnetka
Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for the following
variations: (a) from the intensity of use of lot limitations of Section
17.16.010.F, to permit a roofed lot coverage of 1,298.37 square feet, which
exceeds the maximum allowable of 1,207.38 square feet, resulting in a variation
of 90.99 square feet (7.54%); (b) from the setback requirements of Section
17.16.010.H, to permit a front yard setback of 15.24 feet instead of the
required 30.00-foot setback, resulting in a variation of 14.76 feet (49.20%);
and (c) from the side yard requirements of Section 17.16.010.I, to permit a
north side yard of 5.07 feet , instead of the required minimum of 6.0 feet,
resulting in a variation of 0.93 feet (15.5%); all to allow the construction of
an addition to the first floor and the replacement of the second floor of the
residence on the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, on June 6, 2000, the Council adopted ordinance M-14-2000, which
granted the identical variations to allow the identical work; and
   WHEREAS, due to delays in getting completed plans and obtaining contractor
bids, the applicants were unable to commence the proposed construction within
12 months of the grant of the variation, as required by ordinance M-14-2000,
and that variation therefore lapsed; and
   WHEREAS, on August 13, 2001, the Zoning Board of Appeals conducted a hearing
on the renewed variation requests and, by the unanimous vote of the four
members then in attendance, have reported to the Council recommending that the
variations be granted; and
   WHEREAS,  the plans submitted with the renewed application include plans for
a garage that would require a further variation if built; and
   WHEREAS, the plans for the garage were formally withdrawn by the applicants
at the April 10, 2000, Zoning Board of Appeals hearing on the first application
and were therefore not included in the variations previously granted by
ordinance M-14-2000; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and unique hardships associated
with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect
to the Subject Property in that: (a) the Subject Property is a shallow,
relatively square, legally nonconforming lot that was created by a metes and
bounds division prior to the Village’s adoption of its Subdivision Ordinance,
and has a total lot area of 4,825.50 square feet; (b) the single-family
residence on the Subject Property was constructed in the 1880’s, prior to the
adoption of the Village’s original Zoning Ordinance and prior to the time that
the Village first began issuing building permits; and (c) the existing
nonconformities in setback, side yard and intensity of use of lot cannot be
cured without removing a significant portion of the residence; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that
the proposed modifications are necessary to retrofit the existing home to
accommodate a more functional, modern layout, including an increase in headroom
in the second floor; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because the proposed changes are consistent with the original
style and historical character of the building and will maintain the existing
scale and presence of the residence within the neighborhood; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not impair an adequate supply of
light and air because the proposed building addition raises the height of the
building within permitted limits and the existing building is located closest
to the north and east lot lines of the Subject Property, which abut the rear
yards of the adjoining parcels; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property as the proposed work will comply with all
applicable building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed building additions will not contribute to congestion
on the public streets, as the building and subject property will continue to be
used for single-family residential purposes; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed building additions will
otherwise impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village.
   WHEREAS, the requested variations are in harmony with the general purpose
and intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that they will allow the
renovation, restoration and rehabilitation of a structurally sound building
that has existed in the current location for more than 100 years, while
maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the community and protecting
established trees and landscaping.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1: The foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the findings of
the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth herein.
   SECTION 2:  The following variations are hereby granted to the Subject
Property, commonly known as 381 Poplar Street, Winnetka, Illinois, and located
in the R-4 Single Family Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.16 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code: (a) a
variation from the intensity of use of lot limitations of Section 17.16.010.F,
to permit a roofed lot coverage of 1,298.37 square feet, which exceeds the
maximum allowable is 1,207.38 square feet, resulting in a variation of 90.99
square feet (7.54%); (b) a variation from the setback requirements of Section
17.16.010.H, to permit a front yard setback of 15.24 feet instead if the
required 30.00-foot front setback, resulting in a variation of 14.76 feet
(49.20%); and (c) a variation from the side yard requirements of Section
17.16.010.I, to permit a north side yard of 5.07 feet instead of the required
minimum of 6.0 feet is required, resulting in a variation of 0.93 feet (15.5%);
all to allow the construction of an addition to the first floor and the
replacement of the second floor of the residence on the Subject Property, in
accordance with the plans and specifications submitted with the application for
variations as amended by the withdrawal of the request for the construction of
the detached garage.
   SECTION 3: That the variations hereby granted are conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 4th day of September, 2001, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES: Trustees Brower, Aquilino, Greenough, Powell, Presser and Woodbury
   NAYS: None
   ABSENT: None
   APPROVED this 4th day of September, 2001.
   Signed:
   /s/Michael F. Duhl
   Village President
Countersigned:
/s/Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: Introduction waived September 4, 2001
Posted: September 5, 2001
Passed and Approved: September 4, 2001
Posted: September 5, 2001
ORDINANCE NO. M-25-2001
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (266 Linden Avenue)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
Parcel 1: Lot 3, the North 10 feet of Lot 4 and the East 6 feet of Lot 10 and
the North 10 feet of the East 6 feet of Lot 9 in Block 4 in Dale’s Third
Addition to Winnetka, a subdivision of the Northeast ¼ (except the Northeast ¼
thereof) of the Southeast ¼ of Section 20, Township 42 North, Range 13, East of
the Third Principal Meridian, in Cook County, Illinois.
Parcel 2: The West 5.87 feet of the East 11.87 feet of the South 31.42 feet of
the North 38.67 feet of Lot 10 in Block 4 in Dale’s Third Addition to Winnetka,
a subdivision of the Northeast ¼ (except the Northeast ¼ thereof) of the
Southeast ¼ of Section 20, Township 42 North, Range 13, East of the Third
Principal Meridian, in Cook County, Illinois,
commonly known as 266 Linden Avenue, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
3 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for a variation from the
maximum building size limitations of Section 17.20.010(G) of the Winnetka
Zoning Ordinance to increase the legal, nonconforming gross floor area by
244.56 square feet from 7,428.02 square feet to 7,672.58 square feet, whereas
6,664.75 square feet are permitted, resulting in a variation of 1,007.83 square
feet (15.12%), for the purpose of (a) constructing a 76.71 square foot one-
story addition to reconfigure and enclose the existing outdoor basement stairs
along the north side of the existing residence on the Subject Property; (b)
expanding the mudroom area by 3.60 square feet to connect the existing entry
with the enclosed stairway; and (c) adding a 164 square foot one-story addition
to a room at the rear of the house in order to create a family room; and
   WHEREAS, on September 10, 2001, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variations and, by the
unanimous vote of the five members then present, has reported to the Council
recommending that the requested stairway and mudroom variations be granted and
that the variation to extend the family room be denied; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and unique hardships associated
with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect
to the Subject Property in that: (a) the existing single-family residence on
the Subject Property was constructed in 1910, prior to the enactment of the
first Winnetka Zoning Ordinance in 1925 and prior to the 1989 Zoning Ordinance
amendments that imposed gross floor area limitations; (b) the interior
configuration of the residence cannot accommodate a family room near the
existing kitchen; and (c) the only suitable location for a family room is a
small room near the center of the rear of the house, which contains a
nonconforming stairway to the basement and second floors; and (d) the enclosure
of the exterior stairway along the north side of the house, which includes a
small extension of the mudroom area, will allow for the removal of the
interior, nonconforming stairs, thereby creating a 14-foot by 16-foot area for
a family room; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that a
family room in close proximity to the kitchen area is a standard feature in
contemporary housing and replacing the existing, nonconforming interior stairs
with the proposed enclosed stairway will improve the utility of the house by
making space available for a family room and providing conforming interior
access to the basement; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation for the stairway enclosure and mudroom
expansion will not alter the essential character of the neighborhood because
the enclosed stairway is of minimal size and has been designed to be consistent
with the existing house; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation for the stairway enclosure and mudroom
expansion will not impair an adequate supply of light and air because the
proposed one-story stair enclosure is lower than the main body of the house and
aligns with the existing side building line along the driveway; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation for the stairway enclosure and mudroom
expansion will not increase the hazard from fire and other dangers to the
Subject Property, as it will comply with all applicable building and fire
protection codes and the new, enclosed, basement stairway will provide safer
ingress and egress from the basement in the event of an emergency; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation for the stairway enclosure and mudroom
expansion will not diminish the taxable value of land and buildings throughout
the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject Property may be increased
because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed stairway enclosure and mudroom expansion will not
contribute to congestion on the public streets, as the structure will continue
to be used as a single-family residence and no additional bedrooms are
proposed; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed stairway enclosure and
mudroom expansion will otherwise impair the public health, safety, comfort,
morals, and welfare of the inhabitants of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation is in harmony with the general purpose and
intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that it allows the renovation,
restoration and rehabilitation of a structurally sound existing building while
maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the community and protecting
established trees and landscaping.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2: That a variation from the maximum building size limitations of
Section 17.20.010(G) of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance is hereby granted to the
Subject Property, commonly known as 266 Linden Avenue, Winnetka, Illinois and
located in the R-3 Single-Family Residential Zoning District, said variation
being granted to increase the legal, nonconforming gross floor area by 80.31
square feet, from 7,428.02 square feet to 7,508.33 square feet, whereas
6,664.75 square feet are permitted, resulting in a variation of 843.58 square
feet (12.65%), for the purpose of constructing a 76.71 square foot one-story
addition to reconfigure and enclose the existing outdoor basement stairs along
the north side of the existing residence on the Subject Property, along with
expanding the mudroom area by 3.60 feet to connect the existing entry with the
enclosed stairway, all in accordance with the plans and elevations submitted
with the application for zoning variation on August 6, 2001.
   SECTION 3: That the variation hereby granted is conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 16th day of October, 2001, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser and Woodbury
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    None
   APPROVED this 16th day of October, 2001.
   Signed:
   /s/Michael F. Duhl
   Village President
Countersigned:
/s/Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: October 2, 2001
Posted: October 3, 2001
Passed and Approved: October 16, 2001
Posted: October 17, 2001
ORDINANCE NO. M-26-2001
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (649 Locust Street)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
The South 102.00 feet of Lot 9 of Block 1 in Nelson’s Subdivision, in the
Southeast quarter of Section 17, Township 42 North, Range 13 East of the Third
Principal Meridian, in Cook County, Illinois,
commonly known as 649 Locust Street, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
3 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for a variation from the side
yard requirements of Section 17.20.010(I) of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance to
allow the height of the existing roof parapet wall which has a legally
nonconforming side yard of 6.14 feet along the south property line, to be
increased by 15 inches, although a side yard setback of 19.4 feet is required,
resulting in a variation of 13.26 feet (68.35%); and
   WHEREAS, on September 10, 2001, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variation and, by the
unanimous vote of the five members then present, has reported to the Council
recommending that the requested variation be granted; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and unique hardships associated
with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect
to the Subject Property in that: (a) the Subject Property has a legally
nonconforming south side yard of 6.14 feet; and (b) the requested variation
seeks to add 15 inches to the height of the existing roof parapet in order to
block the view of the new roof line behind the parapet, which will result from
modifying the pitch of the roof to improve drainage and eliminate the cause of
water damage; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that:
(a) the existing flat roof behind the parapet does not have sufficient pitch to
drain storm water off the roof; (b) the ponding of water on the flat roof
causes water damage to the residence; (c) the vertical extension of the parapet
will allow the installation of a new roof with a greater pitch to be screened
from view from the street; and (d) the existing nonconformity cannot be cured
without removing a large section of the residence; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because: (a) the new parapet will be only 15 inches higher
than the existing parapet: (b) the parapet has been designed to be in balance
with the remainder of the building; (c) increasing the height of the parapet
will allow the parapet both to maintain the building’s appearance from the
street and to continue to perform its function of hiding the roof from view;
and (d) the integral railings designed for the parapet will match the new
parapet currently under construction on other portions of the building, all of
which are in conforming locations; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not impair an adequate supply of light
and air because: (a) the 15 inches in additional height of the parapet is
minimal and is substantially lower than the principal part of the residence;
and (b) there are no other buildings in close proximity to the nonconforming
building wall; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property, because: (a) it will comply with all
applicable building and fire protection codes; and (b) will eliminate the cause
of water damage to the building; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the additional parapet height will not contribute to congestion on
the public streets, as it does not affect the capacity or use of the residence;
and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed vertical extension of the
parapet will otherwise impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and
welfare of the inhabitants of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation is in harmony with the general purpose and
intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that it allows the renovation,
restoration and rehabilitation of a structurally sound existing building, while
maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the community and protecting
established trees and landscaping.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2: There is hereby granted a variation to the Subject Property,
commonly known as 649 Locust Street, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
3 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for a variation from the side
yard requirements of Section 17.20.010(I) of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance to
allow the height of the existing roof parapet wall, which has a side yard of
6.14 feet along the south property line, to be increased by 15 inches, although
a side yard setback of 19.4 feet is required, resulting in a variation of 13.26
feet (68.35%), said vertical extension to be in accordance with the plans and
elevations submitted with the application for zoning variation on August 9,
2001.
   SECTION 3: That the variation hereby granted is conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 16th day of October, 2001, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser and Woodbury
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    None
   APPROVED this 16th day of October, 2001.
   Signed:
   /s/Michael F. Duhl
   Village President
Countersigned:
/s/Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: October 2, 2001
Posted: October 3, 2001
Passed and Approved: October 16, 2001
Posted: October 17, 2001
ORDINANCE NO. M-27-2001
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A SPECIAL USE PERMIT AND A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION
OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA, COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (452-
462 Winnetka Avenue and 82-86 Tempel Court)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
PARCEL 1: That part of Lot 6 lying Easterly of a line drawn parallel to and 45
feet Northeasterly from (measured at right angles to) the Westerly line of said
Lot 6 in Owners Subdivision of Lots 1, 2, 3, 4 and 5 in Block 3 of Tempel’s
Resubdivision of that part of the West half of the Northeast quarter of Section
28, Township 42 North, Range 13 East of the Third Principal Meridian, lying
East of the Chicago and Milwaukee Electric Railway right of way and North of
the Village limits of Kenilworth, Cook County, Illinois, in Book of Plats 98,
Page 9, filed as Document 4133672.
PARCEL 2: Lot 7 and 8 (except the West 45 feet of the North 56.16 feet of Lot 8
and except all that part of Lot 7 and Lot 8 (except the North 56.16 feet of
said Lot 8) lying Westerly of a line drawn parallel to and 45 feet
Northeasterly of the Southwesterly line of Lot 7 (measured at right angles) in
Owners Subdivision of Lots 1, 2, 3, 4 and 5 in Block 3 of Tempel’s
Resubdivision of that part of the West half of the Northeast quarter of Section
28, Township 42 North, Range 13 East of the Third Principal Meridian, lying
East of the Chicago and Milwaukee Electric Railway right of way and North of
the Village limits of Kenilworth, Cook County, Illinois, in Book of Plats 98,
Page 9, filed as Document 4133672.
PARCEL 3: That part of Lot 6, Lot 7 and Lot 8 (except the North 56.16 feet of
said Lot 8) in Owners Subdivision lying Westerly of a line drawn parallel to
and 45 feet Northeasterly of the southwesterly line of Lot 7 (measured at right
angles) and lying Easterly of the following described line: Commencing at a
point in the southerly line of said Lot 6, Distant 10.0 feet Easterly of
(measured at right angles to) the Southwesterly line of said Subdivision,
187.52 feet to a point; thence Southwesterly at right angles to the last
described course, 8.75 feet to a point in a line which is parallel with and
1.25 feet Easterly of, measured at right angles to the Southwesterly line of
said Subdivision; thence Northwesterly along the last described parallel
straight line to an intersection with the West line of said Lot 8, said West
line being also the East line of the Northwest quarter of Section 28, Township
42 North, Range 13 East of the Third Principal Meridian, Owners Subdivision of
Lots 1, 2, 3, 4 and 5 in Block 3 of Tempel’s Resubdivision of that part of the
West half of the Northeast quarter of Section 28, Township 42 North, Range 13
East of the Third Principal Meridian, lying East of the Chicago and Milwaukee
Electric Railway right of way and North of the Village limits of Kenilworth,
Cook County, Illinois, in Book of Plats 98, Page 9, filed as Document 4133672.
PARCEL 4: All that part of the Northwest quarter of Section 28, Township 42
North, Range 13 East of the Third Principal Meridian, lying Easterly of a line
drawn parallel with and 41.25 feet Easterly of the Easterly right of way of the
Chicago Northwestern Railway Company, measured at right angles thereto.
PARCEL 5: The West 45 feet of the North 56.16 feet of Lot 8 in Owner’s
Subdivision (Hereinafter described) that part of Lot 6, Lot 7 and Lot 8 (except
the North 56.16 feet of said Lot 8) in Owner’s Subdivision lying Westerly of a
line drawn parallel to and 45 feet Northeasterly of the Southwesterly line of
Lot 7 (measured at right angles) and lying Easterly of the following described
line: Commencing at a point in the Southerly line of said Lot 6, Distant 10.0
feet Easterly of measured at right angles to the Southwesterly line of said
subdivision; thence Northwesterly on a straight line parallel with and 10.0
feet Easterly of measured at right angles, to the Southwesterly line of said
subdivision, 187.52 feet to a point, thence Southwesterly at right angles to
the last described course, 8.75 feet to a point in a line which is parallel
with and 1.35 feet Easterly of, measured at right angles to the Southwesterly
line of said subdivision, thence Northwesterly along the last described
parallel straight line to an intersection with the West line of said Lot 8,
said West line being also the East line of the Northwest quarter of Section 28,
Township 42 North, Range 13 East of the Third Principal Meridian, in Owners
Subdivision of Lots 1, 2, 3, 4 and 5 in Block 3 of Tempel’s Resubdivision of
that part of the West half of the Northeast quarter of Section 28, Township 42
North, Range 13 East of the Third Principal Meridian, lying East of the Chicago
and Milwaukee Electric Railway right of way and North of the Village limits of
Kenilworth, Cook County, Illinois, in Book of Plats 98, Page 9, filed as
Document 4133672,
commonly known, collectively, as 452-462 Winnetka Avenue and 82-86 Tempel
Court, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the C-1 Commercial Zoning District
provided in the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village
Code, for a special use permit pursuant to Chapter 17.56 of the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance, for the construction of a parking lot at grade level; and
   WHEREAS, the owner of the Subject Property has also submitted an application
for a variation from the landscaping requirements of Section 17.40.010(O) of
the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, to allow a reduced landscape buffer along the
east and west property lines, although a landscape buffer of 5.0 feet is
required, resulting in a variation of 4 feet (80%), to permit the parking area
to be located within one foot of the property line on the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, the special use permit and variation are being sought in
conjunction with the renovation and redevelopment of the existing building on
the Subject Property, which will replace existing retail commercial space on
the first floor with 27 office spaces, will renovate the 25 residential
apartments on the second floor, and will add two new apartments in the attic
space; and
   WHEREAS, the existing building on the subject Property was built in 1920 as
a mixed use building with retail commercial space on the first floor and
offices and apartments on the second floor; and
   WHEREAS, the commercial space on the first floor has been vacant for several
years; and attempts to redevelop the first floor as commercial space have not
been successful; and
   WHEREAS, on August 22, 2001, on due notice thereof, the Plan Commission met
to consider the request for the special use permit and, by the favorable vote
of the nine members then in attendance, has found that the proposed
redevelopment of the Subject Property is consistent with 27 of 30 applicable
provisions of Winnetka 2020, The Winnetka Comprehensive Plan, and has
recommended that the Village Council grant the special use permit; and
   WHEREAS, on September 10, 2001, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the special use and requested variation
and, by the favorable vote of the five members then present, has reported to
the Council recommending that the requested variation be granted; and
   WHEREAS, on September 18, 2001, the Village Council adopted Resolution R-34-
2001, granting a certificate of appropriateness that approved the design and
materials for the exterior building renovations and approved the proposed
landscape plan; and
   WHEREAS, the existing building is currently served by a grade-level, gravel
parking lot that has no drainage facilities; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed changes to the parking lot will add pavement, drainage
and stormwater detention, and will increase the number of parking spaces from
approximately 29 marked spaces to 39 spaces; and
   WHEREAS, although the proposed number of parking spaces does not meet the
zoning ordinance’s requirement to provide 2¼ spaces per residential unit, no
variation is required for the parking because the building was constructed
before 1998 and because the number of residential units requiring parking is
being increased less than 15%; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and unique hardships associated
with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect
to the landscape buffer required for the Subject Property in that: (a) the
Subject Property is an irregularly shaped parcel that abuts the east side of
the Union Pacific Railroad right-of-way embankment south of the Winnetka Avenue
right-of-way; (b) the proposed redevelopment of the Subject Property involves
preserving the existing building, which precludes expanding the size of the
parking area; (c) the proposed reduction in the size of the landscape buffer
will allow the applicant to maximize the number of off-street parking spaces
that can be provided; (d) the on-street parking spaces located along the south
side of Winnetka Avenue adjacent to the Subject Property are short-term parking
spaces; and (e) all the parking spaces along the north side of Winnetka Avenue
across from the Subject Property, all of the spaces along the east side of
Wilson Avenue on the south side of Winnetka Avenue, have been reserved for use
by New Trier High School on school days pursuant to an agreement with the
Village of Winnetka; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, because
providing a full, 5-foot wide landscaped buffer around the perimeter of the
parking lot would reduce the number of available parking spaces and impede
ingress and egress to the spaces, which would deter potential tenants from
renting space in the building; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood, because it will preserve and improve the appearance of the
existing building, which has been a fixture in the neighborhood for
approximately 80 years; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not impair an adequate supply of light
and air because it does not affect the building footprint or building height;
and
   WHEREAS, the requested landscape variation will not increase the hazard from
fire and other dangers to the Subject Property, because it does not affect the
building structure, the renovation of which will comply with all applicable
building and fire protection codes, and because the variation will allow
maximum configuration of the parking, thereby increasing the Subject Property’s
accessibility by emergency vehicles; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the requested variation will not diminish
the taxable value of land and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable
value of the Subject Property may be increased because of the proposed
improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the requested landscape buffer variation will not contribute to
congestion on the public streets, because: (a) it will allow the number of
useable parking spaces on the Subject Property to be increased to 39; and (b)
the proposed office uses will not generate as much traffic as retail commercial
uses in the same space; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the requested landscape buffer variation
will otherwise impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare
of the inhabitants of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation is in harmony with the general purpose and
intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that it provides landscaping on the
Subject Property to the extent it is reasonably possible, while supporting the
renovation, restoration and rehabilitation of a structurally sound existing
building while maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the community
and protecting established trees and landscaping; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the public health, safety, comfort,
morals and welfare of the inhabitants of the Village will be impaired by the
grade level parking lot and
   WHEREAS, the proposed exterior parking at ground level will benefit the
public health, safety, comfort and welfare of the inhabitants of the Village in
that: (a) it will increase the number of off-street parking spaces in an area
where parking demand exceeds supply; (b) it will provide adequate drainage for
the parking area; and (c) it will improve the appearance of the Subject
Property; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed ground level parking will further benefit the public
health, safety, comfort and welfare of the inhabitants of the Village because
it can be constructed without excavating a substantial portion of the Subject
Property, thereby reducing the impact of the construction activity and
equipment on Winnetka Avenue, which is a heavy traffic area near New Trier High
School; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed ground level parking area will further benefit the
public health, safety, comfort and welfare of the inhabitants of the Village,
because it will improve the flow of traffic as it enters and leaves the Subject
Property; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed ground level parking will
otherwise impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the increased number of parking spaces provided by the proposed
ground level parking will help to reduce the demand for parking demand in the
streets and parking lots in the immediate vicinity; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed ground level parking, if
constructed in accordance with the conditions set forth in this ordinance, will
be substantially injurious to the use and enjoyment of other property in the
immediate vicinity; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that construction of the proposed ground level
parking subject to the terms and conditions of this ordinance will impede the
normal and orderly development or improvement of other property in the
immediate vicinity; and
   WHEREAS, adequate measures have been taken to provide ingress and egress in
a manner that minimizes pedestrian and vehicular traffic congestion in the
public ways, in that the proposed off-street parking is configured so as to
avoid, to the extent possible, interfering with pedestrian and vehicular
traffic; and
   WHEREAS, adequate utilities, access roads, drainage and other facilities
necessary for the operation of the proposed special use on the Subject Property
will be provided when the proposed ground level parking is constructed in
compliance with applicable Village Code requirements; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed ground level parking, when constructed in accordance
with the terms and conditions of this ordinance, will conform to the applicable
regulations of the Zoning Ordinance and the Winnetka Village Code.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2: Subject to the terms and conditions hereinafter set forth, there
is hereby granted to the Subject Property, commonly known, collectively, as
452-462 Winnetka Avenue and 82-86 Tempel Court, Winnetka, Illinois, and located
in the C-1 Commercial Zoning District provided in the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, a variation from the
landscaping requirements of Section 17.40.010(O) of the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance, to allow a reduced landscape buffer along the west property lines,
although a landscape buffer of 5.0 feet is required, resulting in a variation
of 4 feet (80%), to permit the parking area to be located within one foot of
the west property line on the Subject Property, so as to maintain the full,
5.0-foot landscape buffer adjacent to the single-family properties to the east,
all in accordance with the plans, materials and elevations submitted with the
application for special use permit, as modified and depicted in the landscape
and parking plans approved by the Village Council in Resolution R-34-2001.
   SECTION 3: Subject to the terms and conditions hereinafter set forth, there
is hereby granted to the Subject Property, commonly known, collectively, as
452-462 Winnetka Avenue and 82-86 Tempel Court, Winnetka, Illinois, and located
in the C-1 Commercial Zoning District provided in the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, a special use permit as
provided in Chapter 17.56 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, to allow the
construction of parking lot at grade level, in accordance with the plans,
materials and elevations submitted with the application for special use permit,
and in accordance with the landscape and parking plans approved in Resolution
R-34-2001.
   SECTION 4: That the variation and special use hereby granted are subject to
the following conditions: (a) the proposed construction shall commence within
12 months after the effective date of this Ordinance; (b) none of the parking
spaces shall be used by or rented to any person who is not either a resident of
the building, a commercial tenant of the building, or an employee of a
commercial tenant of the building; (c) in assigning parking spaces, the owner
and its agents shall give first priority to residential tenants, except that no
more than two parking spaces shall be reserved for any one apartment; and (d)
the owner and its agents shall be responsible for posting all necessary notices
and otherwise providing for the enforcement of the foregoing parking
restrictions.
   SECTION 5 : The stipulations, conditions and restrictions set forth in the
foregoing Section 4 of this Ordinance may be modified or revised from time to
time by the Village Council following public notice and hearing, following the
procedures specified in Section 17.56 of the Winnetka Village Code for
processing special use applications.
   SECTION 6: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 16th day of October, 2001, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser and Woodbury
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    None
   APPROVED this 16th day of October, 2001.
   Signed:
   /s/Michael F. Duhl
   Village President
Countersigned:
/s/Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: October 2, 2001
Posted: October 3, 2001
Passed and Approved: October 16, 2001
Posted: October 17, 2001
ORDINANCE NO. M-28-2001
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A SPECIAL USE PERMIT AND VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF
THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA, COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (411
Linden Avenue)
   WHEREAS, the Winnetka Historical Society (the “Society”) is the owner of the
following described real estate (the “Subject Property”):
Lots 1 in Meleney Subdivision in the south 100 feet of the southwest quarter of
Block 51 in Winnetka, (except streets) in the southeast quarter of the
northeast quarter of Section 20, Township 42 North, Range 13, East of the Third
Principal Meridian in Cook County, Illinois,
commonly known 411 Linden Street, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
5 Single-family Residential Zoning District, provided in Chapter 17.12 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code; and
   WHEREAS, on May 10, 2001, the Society filed with the Village of Winnetka an
application for a special use pursuant to Section 17.12.010(B)(2) and Section
17.56.010 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, to allow the Society to modify and
use the single-family residence on the Subject Property as the Society’s
permanent home; and
   WHEREAS, on May 10, 2001, the Society also filed an application for a
variation from Section 17.12.010(J) of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, to allow
a rear yard setback of 6.11 feet, rather than the required rear yard setback of
16.92 feet, resulting in a variation of 10.81 feet (63.89%), in order to expand
the width of the existing detached garage and attach it to the residence; and
   WHEREAS, on May 10, 2001, the Society also filed an application for a
Certificate of Appropriateness, seeking the Design Resign Board’s preliminary
comment on the conceptual design of the residence as proposed by the Society,
including the proposed garage addition, a sign and lamp post, a rear yard ramp
to provide wheelchair accessibility, and the proposed restoration of a side
porch and tower that had previously existed on the house; and
   WHEREAS, on May 17, 2001, on due notice thereof, the Design Review Board
considered the Society’s conceptual design and, by the concurrence of four of
the five members then in attendance, has forwarded favorable comment to the
Village Council on the building’s design, the fifth member then present having
recused himself because he is the Society’s architect for the project; and
   WHEREAS,  on June 13, 2001, on due notice thereof, the Plan Commission met
to consider whether the proposed special use is consistent with Winnetka 2020,
the Winnetka Comprehensive Plan; and
   WHEREAS, after considering 27 different provisions of Winnetka 2020, the
Plan Commission found that three of the cited provisions were not applicable
and that the Society’s special use request was consistent with 23 of the
remaining 24 provisions; and
   WHEREAS, two of the ten members present at the June 13, 2001, Plan
Commission meeting recused themselves from the consideration of the Society’s
application, due to their roles as advocates for the Society, one of the two
being the Society’s architect for the proposal and the other being the attorney
for the Society; and
   WHEREAS, by the affirmative vote of seven of the remaining members present
at the June 13, 2001, meeting and the abstention of the eighth member then
present, the Plan Commission found the special use consistent with the
Comprehensive Plan and recommended that the special use be granted, subject to
certain conditions pertaining to parking and attempting to minimize the impact
of the proposed use on the neighboring single-family properties; and
   WHEREAS, on June 11, 2001 and June 28, 2001, on due notice thereof, the
Zoning Board of Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested special use
and variation and, by the unanimous vote of the entire Board, has recommended
that the Council grant the request for the special use permit and, by the
affirmative vote of six of the seven members of the Board, has recommend that
the Council grant the requested variation; and
   WHEREAS, the recommendations and findings of the Design Review Board, Plan
Commission and Zoning Board of Appeals were forwarded to the Village Council,
which first considered the Society’s application at the regular Council
meetings on July 17, 2001; and
   WHEREAS, on July 17, 2001, the owners of nine of the 27 lots within 250 feet
of the Subject Property (the “Objectors”) filed signed and acknowledged
objections to the Society’s special use application; and
   WHEREAS, the Society’s application was furthered considered by the Village
Council at the regular Council meeting on August 7, 2001, at which time it
tabled the application until October 16, 2001, to allow the Village staff to
determine whether there were any viable alternative locations for the Society
on property owned by the Village or the Winnetka Park District; and
   WHEREAS, the Council considered the Village staff’s report at the regular
Council meetings on September 18, 2001, and October 2, 2001, and concluded that
it could not locate a suitable alternative site for the Society, whereupon the
Village Council voted to remove the Society’s application from the table and
set it for further consideration at the October 9, 2001, study session; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property is located at the northeast corner of Ash and
Linden Streets and is improved with a single-family residence that was built
around 1859, and a one-car garage that was built subsequent to 1997; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property is a relatively square lot, measuring 99.9
feet by 112.81 feet, and was created pursuant to Resolution R-1384-97, which
created two lots from a parcel that originally had a lot depth of approximately
187.6 feet; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property is a nonconforming lot, having been rendered
nonconforming by the Village Council’s adoption of Ordinance MC-216-99, which
amended the Subdivision Ordinance to prohibit subdivisions that cause a rear
lot line to abut a side lot line, and amended lot depth requirements in the R-
5 zoning district to require a minimum lot depth of 120 feet; and
   WHEREAS, the corner yard of the Subject Property, which abuts the Ash Street
right-of-way, is also nonconforming, having a depth of 23.2 feet instead of the
minimum required corner yard of 30.00 feet; and
   WHEREAS, the front yard of the Subject Property is along Linden Street,
which is identified in the Comprehensive Plan, Winnetka 2020, as a collector
street; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property is included in the East and West Elm Street
Planning Sub-Area, as depicted in both the existing land use map and the land
use plan contained in Winnetka 2020; and
   WHEREAS, Winnetka 2020 identifies the East and West Elm Street Planning Sub-
Area as a single-family neighborhood that is affected by commercial activity;
and
   WHEREAS, the Society has a 500-volume collection of books and other
reference materials as well as a collection of local historical artifacts,
which it places on display in exhibits that change from time to time; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed use of the Subject Property includes the storage of
historical artifacts, the maintenance of the archive and research library,
exhibit display space and staff offices; and
   WHEREAS, one of the primary roles of the Historical Society is to serve as a
repository of local historical documents and artifacts and to serve as a
research and reference resource for the history of the Village of Winnetka; and
   WHEREAS, as stated in the Comprehensive Plan, the Winnetka Historical
Society was founded in 1932 to collect, preserve and present artifacts
representing Winnetka History and to increase public knowledge about the
community; and
   WHEREAS, on January 20, 1987, the Council of the Village of Winnetka adopted
Resolution R-910-87, pursuant to which the Village of Winnetka leased and paid
for the space occupied by the Winnetka Historical Society at Skokie School,
which was occupied by the Society until 1999; and
   WHEREAS, prior to 1987, the Winnetka Historical Society occupied space at
the Winnetka Public Library; and
   WHEREAS, the Winnetka Historical Society has been without permanent space
since it was required to vacate the space leased at the Skokie School and moved
out of the Skokie School in 2000; and
   WHEREAS, the Village of Winnetka continues to appropriate funds for the
Society’s operations; and
   WHEREAS, in addition to being available to the general public, the Society
performs research and provides information to the Village on the historic
significance of properties within the Village in conjunction with the Village’s
building demolition procedures; and
   WHEREAS, based on the longstanding relationship between the Society and the
Village, the Society’s history of being located in public buildings, the
Society’s availability to the Village as a whole, and the formal relation
between the Society and the Village, the Council of the Village of Winnetka
have determined that the Society constitutes a non-essential public use for
purposes of Section 17.56.010(B) of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, both as the
Zoning Ordinance provided at the time the Winnetka Historical Society’s
application was filed, and as the Zoning Ordinance currently provides,
following its amendment by Ordinance MC-10-2001; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed special use is consistent with the Comprehensive
Plan’s community goal of preserving and enhancing those public assets and
architecturally significant structures that create the attractive appearance
and peaceful, single-family residential character of the Village, in that it
will preserve the residence on the Subject Property, which has been part of the
neighborhood for approximately 140 years; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed special use is consistent with the Comprehensive
Plan’s community goal of supporting educational excellence and the enrichment
of Winnetka’s cultural environment, in that it will provide a permanent home
for the Winnetka Historical Society and its collection of historical reference
materials and artifacts, assuring that they will remain available for students
and citizens of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, subject to the terms and conditions hereinafter set forth, the
proposed special use is consistent with the Comprehensive Plan’s community goal
of limiting commercial, institutional and residential development within the
Village to minimize the potentially adverse impacts on adjacent residential
neighborhoods and to prevent the need for significant increases in
infrastructure and other community resources, in that it maintains the
residential appearance of the neighborhood, controls the activities to make
them more closely parallel to single-family residential impacts and does not
require additional infrastructure or community resources; and
   WHEREAS, subject to the terms and conditions hereinafter set forth, the
proposed special use is consistent with the Comprehensive Plan’s community goal
of ensuring that institutional development is appropriate with the character
of, and minimizes the adverse impact on, its surrounding neighborhood; and
   WHEREAS, subject to the terms and conditions hereinafter set forth, the
proposed special use is consistent with the Comprehensive Plan, in that it
furthers the objective of recognizing the critical role of the Village’s
historic architecture in defining Winnetka’s unique character in public,
institutional, commercial and residential areas and encourage its preservation;
and
   WHEREAS, the proposed special use is consistent with the Comprehensive Plan,
in that it furthers the objective of encouraging organizations, schools,
religious institutions, business and citizens in their efforts to beautify the
Village; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed special use is consistent with the Comprehensive Plan,
in that it furthers the objective of preserving the predominant defining
character of Winnetka as a community for owner-occupied high quality single-
family residences, by providing for the preservation of a valuable single-
family residence that has been a fixture in the community for over 140 years
and by allowing the building to revert to single-family use if the Society
moves to another location; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed special use is consistent with the Comprehensive Plan,
in that it furthers the objective of retaining the character of existing
residential neighborhoods, because the proposed garage addition is in keeping
with the scale of the house on the Subject Property and with neighboring
houses, and because the proposed use maintains existing front and side yard
setbacks, has no impact on any trees, and preserves the pedestrian orientation
of the neighborhood; and
   WHEREAS, subject to the terms and conditions hereinafter set forth, the
proposed special use is consistent with the Comprehensive Plan, in that it
fosters the objective of protecting residential neighborhoods and homes from
the encroachment of incompatible land uses and traffic patterns, by preserving
the residential appearance of the property, limiting the activities of the
Society on the Subject Property to functions that have an intensity comparable
to single-family activities and providing for parking off the site and away
from the single-family residential neighborhood; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed special use is consistent with the Comprehensive Plan,
in that it furthers the objective of encouraging the preservation of older
houses that contribute to the character of the neighborhood through additions
and improvements; and
   WHEREAS, subject to the terms and conditions hereinafter set forth, the
proposed special use is consistent with the Comprehensive Plan, in that it
fosters the objective of maintaining the quiet ambience of residential
neighborhoods by restricting the activities of the Society to activities that
have an intensity comparable to single-family uses; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed special use is consistent with the Comprehensive Plan,
in that it furthers the objective of recognizing the critical importance of
educational and community institutions to Village residents; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed special use is consistent with the Comprehensive Plan,
in that it furthers the objective of maintaining an atmosphere in which diverse
cultural and educational organizations may flourish and in which special
activities for residents of all ages may be enhanced, by maintaining the
Society’s collection of reference materials and historical artifacts in a
permanent location where it can be exhibited for students on field trips and
for the general public; and
   WHEREAS, subject to the terms and conditions hereinafter set forth, the
proposed special use is consistent with the Comprehensive Plan, in that it
furthers the objective of engaging in a public process that balances
institutional goals and minimizes adverse impact to the character of the
adjacent residential neighborhood; and
   WHEREAS, subject to the terms and conditions hereinafter set forth, the
proposed special use is consistent with the Comprehensive Plan’s recommendation
to encourage governmental and non-governmental institutions to work with their
constituents, neighbors and the Village to minimize the impact of traffic and
parking on surrounding residential streets and to develop on-site solutions
where appropriate; and
   WHEREAS, subject to the terms and conditions hereinafter set forth, the
proposed special use is consistent with the Comprehensive Plan’s recommendation
to ensure that proposed uses do not have an adverse impact on the residential
character of the surrounding residential neighborhoods; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed special use is consistent with the Comprehensive Plan,
in that it fulfills the recommendation to help the Winnetka Historical Society
to find a permanent home; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed special use and variation are consistent with the
Comprehensive Plan’s recommendation to retain the integrity of front yards by
placing garages to the rear of houses or lots where possible; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed special use is consistent with the Comprehensive
Plan’s recommendation to reduce on-street parking along narrow streets within
residential neighborhoods in that it provides adequate on-site parking for day-
to-day activities and provides adequate off-site parking in existing parking
lots in the vicinity; and
   WHEREAS, by preserving one of the oldest buildings in the Village, the
proposed special use is consistent with the Comprehensive Plan’s recommendation
to recognize the role of the Village’s historic architecture in defining
Winnetka’s unique residential character and to encourage its preservation; and
   WHEREAS, because it preserves one of the oldest buildings in the Village and
provides a permanent home for the Society, the proposed special use is
consistent with the Comprehensive Plan’s recommendation to promote historic
preservation as a contributor to the quality and character of the Village, by
encouraging the study and inventory of existing houses and commercial buildings
that define Village character; and
   WHEREAS, by preserving one of the oldest buildings in the Village and by
providing a permanent location for the Society’s archives and research
materials, the proposed special use is consistent with the Comprehensive Plan’s
recommendation to support the Landmark Commission’s work in public education,
in research, in recommending landmarks and in providing technical assistance
for renovating older properties; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed special use is consistent with the Comprehensive
Plan’s recommendation to incorporate historic preservation as a standard
component of all elements of Village planning; and
   WHEREAS, the establishment, maintenance and operation of the special use
will not be detrimental to or endanger the public health, safety, comfort,
morals or general welfare, in that: (a) the Winnetka Historical Society
provides a valuable public service; (b) providing a permanent location where
the Society’s historical records and artifacts can be stored and made
accessible to the public is a worthy public purpose that furthers the general
welfare of the Village of Winnetka; and (c) the terms and conditions
hereinafter set forth provide adequate safeguards to protect the public health,
safety and comfort from the negative impacts of the non-residential use of the
Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, the special use will not be substantially injurious to the use and
enjoyment of other property in the immediate vicinity for uses that are
permitted by right in the R-5 zoning district, in that: (a) the number of
employees, hours of operation and frequency of events are not substantially
different from single-family activities, which often include such activities as
visits and/or deliveries by persons who do not live on the premises, backyard
gatherings and other similar outdoor activities, and occasional evening parties
attended by larger numbers of people; and (b) the limitations imposed upon the
use by the terms and conditions set forth in Section 4 of this Ordinance,
including the periodic review of the impact of the use, will protect the owners
of property in the immediate vicinity from an unreasonable increase in the
intensity of the use; and
   WHEREAS, the Winnetka Historical Society has presented credible evidence
that the proposed special use will not substantially diminish or impair
property values in the immediate vicinity, in that: (a) the Society presented
the expert testimony and written analysis of a qualified appraiser; (b) the
Society’s expert presented a paired sales analysis, comparing sales of similar
properties with similar buildings of comparable age and amenities, and pairing
parcels in the same zoning classification, with one parcel in each pair being
located close to a commercial, institutional or park use; (c) the paired sales
analysis used by the Society’s expert is a reasonable method of analysis; (d)
the Society’s expert has demonstrated that there are no comparable uses within
the Village; (e) all other institutional uses within the Village are in
institutional buildings and are of substantially greater intensity than the
intensity of use proposed by the Society; (f) the Society’s expert has
substantial experience with the real estate market in the Village of Winnetka
and demonstrated an understanding of both the real estate market and land use
patterns within the Village; and (g) having concluded that existing, more
intense institutional uses do not have a substantial negative impact on
property values within the Village, the Society’s expert has presented a
credible conclusion that the Society’s proposed use of the Subject Property,
which is less intense and which retains the existing residence and residential
configuration of the property, will not have a substantial negative impact on
property values in the immediate vicinity; and
   WHEREAS, the sale of the properties at 410 Linden, which is across the
street from the Subject Property, and at 421 Linden, which is immediately north
of the Subject Property, during the period that the Society’s application was
pending, provides further evidence that the proposed special use will not
substantially diminish or impair property values in the immediate vicinity,
because: (a) both parcels of property were sold after the notices of special
use and variation were issued; (b) there is no evidence that the pending zoning
application had a substantial, negative impact on the sale of either of the two
properties; and (c) the property at 421 Linden was under contract for sale
within a few days after it was first listed,; and
   WHEREAS, the Objectors have failed to present any credible evidence in
support of their contention that the proposed special use will have an
substantial negative impact on property values in that: (a) although the
Objectors presented the expert testimony and written analysis of a qualified
appraiser, the Objectors’ expert did not have any demonstrated experience or
familiarity with the real estate market and land use patterns within the
Village of Winnetka; (b) the Objectors’ expert’s use of a target and control
method to analyze property values was not credible, in that it compared groups
of parcels of property that had dissimilar characteristics, including being
located in different zoning districts, rendering them of no value in measuring
the impact of institutional uses on nearby residential properties; (c) the
Objectors’ expert conceded that there is no comparable use in the Village to
provide a basis for comparison; (e) the stated purpose of the Objectors’
expert’s analysis was not to render an opinion on the impact of the proposed
use on property values in the immediate vicinity, but to present his opinion
that the Society had not met its burden of proof, an issue that is reserved for
the Village Council in this matter; and (f) the Objectors’ expert acknowledged
that he was unable to establish a substantial impact from the proposed special
use; and
   WHEREAS, evidence presented by the Objectors pertaining to the locations of
historical societies and historical museums in other communities, and of the
zoning districts and land use patterns surrounding those uses, is not material
to the Council’s consideration of the Society’s application, due to differences
in building sizes, lots, zoning districts and operations of the various
historical societies and historic museums; and
   WHEREAS, the establishment of the special use will not impede the normal and
orderly development or improvement of other property in the immediate vicinity
for uses permitted by right in the district or districts of concern, in that
the preservation of the single-family residential building, the low intensity
of the proposed use during normal business days, and the imposition of
conditions hereinafter set forth to protect against any negative impacts of the
use, will preserve the single-family character of the immediate neighborhood,
which is fully developed with established land uses; and
   WHEREAS, adequate measures have been taken to provide ingress and egress in
a manner that minimizes pedestrian and vehicular traffic congestion in the
public ways, in that: (a) the proposed use maintains the existing driveway
configuration, which will provide four on-site parking spaces; (b) the existing
driveway is consistent in size and location on the property with other
driveways in the immediate vicinity; (c) the Society having demonstrated that
the intensity of the proposed use is comparable to the intensity of single-
family uses, the additional pedestrian and vehicular traffic caused by the
proposed special use will not add significantly to the existing traffic; (d)
Linden Street is a collector street that is heavily traveled and the amount of
traffic that will be generated by the one to three visitors a day that will
visit the Society will not significantly add to those traffic volumes; and (e)
the combination of on-street parking in the immediate neighborhood and the
availability of off-street parking at the public library and Christian Science
Church, as well as at the Elm Street station and Village Hall parking lot, will
assure that vehicular traffic for larger events will be kept away from the
immediate single-family neighborhood; and
   WHEREAS, adequate parking facilities necessary to the operation of the
special use exist or are to be provided, in that: (a) the Society has presented
evidence that it will be allowed to use the parking lots at the Winnetka Public
Library and Christian Science Church for events that will occur at times when
the library and church are not operating; (b) the Society has demonstrated that
it has arranged for its employees to park off-site at the Christian Science
Church parking lot on weekdays; (c) the four parking spaces on the driveway are
adequate to accommodate daily visitors; (d) parking for evening events and
occasional weekend events is also available on the street in the immediate
neighborhood and at the public parking lots at Village Hall and the Elm Street
train station; and
   WHEREAS, adequate utilities, access roads, drainage and other facilities
necessary to the operation of the proposed special use exist, because both the
Subject Property and the neighborhood are fully developed and the proposed
special use does not require any additional facilities to operate; and
   WHEREAS, when implemented in accordance with the terms and conditions set
forth in Section 4 of this Ordinance, the special use in all other respects
will conform to the applicable regulations of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance and
other Village ordinances and codes; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and particular hardships
associated with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance
with respect to the rear yard variation requested for the proposed expansion of
the existing single car garage and its connection to the residence on the
Subject Property, in that (a) the shallow depth of the lot limits the location
of the garage, placing it in such close proximity to the house that it is
impossible to make any measurable expansion to the size of the garage without
connecting it to the house; (b) an expanded garage could be allowed in the
location proposed, without a variation, if it were not attached to the
residence; (c) there is no conforming location for an expanded garage, because
of the location of the house on the Subject Property, the shallow lot depth and
the nonconforming corner yard; (d) reorienting the garage to face Ash Street
would change the established appearance of the Linden Street block face and
would also require a setback variation, due to the nonconforming corner yard
and the shallow rear yard; and (e) setting the garage back further from the
rear lot line would preclude any measurable expansion of the garage, would
result in the garage’s being attached to a portion of the dining room, and
would alter the established appearance of the Subject Property in the
neighborhood by placing garage doors closer to the front lot line ; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, because:
(a) a two-car garage could be allowed in the location proposed, without a
variation, if it were not attached to the residence; (b) due to the location of
the house on the lot, the footprint of a full two-car garage overlaps part of
the footprint of the house; (c) a two-car garage is a standard feature of the
single-family residential properties in the Village; (d) storage areas that are
enclosed and part of a residence are essential features of single-family
residences within the Village of Winnetka; and (e) the basement of the existing
residence cannot be used for storage because of its low ceiling height and
because of extensive ductwork from the gravity-fed hot air heating system; and
   WHEREAS, the plight of the owner is due to unique circumstances in that: (a)
the Subject Property was one of the last subdivisions to allow an oversized
corner lot to be divided into two relatively square parcels and, although it
was created by a subdivision in 1997, it was rendered nonconforming by a 1999
amendment to the Zoning Ordinance and subdivision regulations; (b) the Subject
Property does not have sufficient depth to allow a detached two-car garage to
be constructed in a conforming location; (c) constructing an attached two-car
garage in a conforming location would require moving the garage closer to the
front of the lot, and would interfere with the side of the house, thereby
changing its historic nature; and (d) the Historical Society has been without a
permanent home for several years and the Village Council has concluded, after
conducting an independent study in which both the Society and Objectors
participated, that there are no viable alternatives for a site for the
Historical Society, because the cost of moving the existing residence to
another location is cost prohibitive and would jeopardize the structural
integrity of the building, there are no other suitable historic buildings in
the commercial or multi-family zoning districts and building a new facility is
cost prohibitive due to land acquisition and construction costs; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because it will preserve and improve the appearance of the
existing residence, which has been a fixture in the neighborhood for
approximately 140 years, it will maintain the appearance of both the Linden
Street and Ash Street block faces, and will maintain the residential appearance
of a single-family residence with a two-car garage at the rear of the property;
and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not impair an adequate supply of light
and air because: (a) although the eave line of the garage will be raised to
align with the eave line of the one-story porch roof of the house, the height
of the garage will continue to be a single story; (b) the expanded garage will
have a hipped roof, so there will be no extended ridge line and the parts of
the roof that face the adjoining properties will slope away from those
properties; (c) the height of the proposed addition is substantially lower than
the height of the existing house on the Subject Property and the house on the
property to the east; and (d) a detached garage of the same dimensions could be
placed in the same location on the Subject Property without a variation; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not increase the hazard from fire and
other damages to the Subject Property, in that the garage expansion and its
attachment to the residence will be constructed in accordance with all
applicable building and fire safety codes and will include climate control and
a sprinkler system; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings throughout the Village, in that the expanded garage will increase
the value and utility of the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not contribute to congestion on the
public streets, in that the expanded garage will not limit the off-street
parking available on the Subject Property and the Society’s use of the
property, which will be supported by the expanded garage, will have a
negligible impact on traffic in the area; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the requested variation will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village and, as previously stated in these recitals, above,
the proposed use of the Subject Property, as modified pursuant to the
variation, will benefit the public welfare; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation is in harmony with the general purpose and
intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that: (a) it fosters the goals and
policies of the Comprehensive Plan of the Village; (b) it maintains the
appearance and intensity of use of a detached, single-family home, which is the
principal land use in the Village; (c) it protects the scale and character of
the existing neighborhood by protecting against excessive bulk on the Subject
Property and by assuring that the use of the Subject Property by the Society
will remain compatible with the uses in the immediate vicinity; (d) it provides
a permanent home for the Winnetka Historical Society with the least possible
impact on the single-family character of the R-5 zoning district and the
immediate neighborhood of the Subject Property; (e) it protects existing public
facilities and utilities from being overloaded due to excess development; (f)
it ensures and facilitates the preservation of sites, areas and structures of
historical, architectural and aesthetic importance; (g) it assures the
development and redevelopment of established neighborhoods in a manner
consistent with their established character by encouraging the renovation,
restoration and rehabilitation of a structurally sound existing house; and (h)
it protects and respects the justifiable reliance of existing residents,
business people and taxpayers on the continuation of existing, established land
use patterns.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : The foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated into this section
as the findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set
forth herein.
   SECTION 2: Subject to the terms and conditions hereinafter set forth, there
is hereby granted to the Subject Property, commonly known as 411 Linden Avenue,
Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-5 Single-family Residential Zoning
District provided in the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Section 17.12 of Title 17
of the Winnetka Village Code, a variation from the rear yard setback
requirements of Section 17.12.010(G) to allow a rear yard setback of 6.11 feet
rather than the required 16.91-foot rear yard, resulting in a variation of
10.81 feet (63.89%), to allow the east wall of the garage to be extended by
10.54 feet and to allow the garage to be attached to the residence on the
Subject Property, all in accordance with the plans, materials and elevations
submitted on May 10, 2001, with the application for special use permit and
variation.
   SECTION 3: Subject to the terms and conditions hereinafter set forth, there
is hereby granted to the Subject Property, commonly known as 411 Linden Avenue,
Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-5 Single-family Residential Zoning
District provided in the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka
Village Code, a special use permit, pursuant to Section 17.12.010(B)(2) and
Section 17.56.010(B) of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, and pursuant to the
provisions of Chapter 17.56 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, to allow the
Subject Property to be used by the Winnetka Historical Society as the location
for the museum’s operations, consisting of historical material archives,
research library, museum exhibit display space, offices and storage, all in
accordance with the plans, materials and elevations submitted on May 10, 2001,
with the application for special use permit and variation.
   SECTION 4: The variation and special use granted herein are subject to the
following conditions:
   A.   The proposed construction shall commence within 12 months after the
effective date of this Ordinance;
   B.   Any exterior security lighting shall be consistent with single-family
residential security lighting in the Village.
   C.   No off-premises signs shall be permitted.
   D.   All exterior lighting and signage shall be subject to approval by the
Village Council following the receipt of recommendations by the Design Review
Board.
   E.   In reviewing signage, exterior lighting or any exterior changes to the
buildings, the Design Review Board shall apply standards applicable to single-
family residential buildings rather than the Design Guidelines previously
approved for commercial applications.
   F.   No additions or modifications shall be made to the Subject Property if
they result in increasing the gross floor area beyond the maximum building size
allowed in the R-5 zoning district.
   G.   Only events sponsored by the Winnetka Historical Society may be held on
the Subject Property and the Winnetka Historical Society shall not enter into
any agreements for any third party to hold an event on the Subject Property. No
private parties shall be allowed on the Subject Property.
   H.   Any outdoor event not in keeping with the single-family residential
character of the neighborhood is strictly prohibited.
   I.   Public hours shall be limited to 64 hours per month, with an average of
approximately 14 hours per week. Monthly board meetings and executive board
meetings may be held on the Subject Property. The annual meeting shall be held
off site.
   J.   There shall be a curfew of 10:30 p.m. for all public functions, except
those held on Friday or Saturday evenings, which shall have a curfew of 11:00
p.m.
   K.   Employees and volunteers of the Winnetka Historical Society shall park
at an off-site parking lot when the Winnetka Historical Society is open to the
public. When the Society is not open to the public, no more than two cars
belonging to staff and/or volunteers shall be parked in the driveway at a time.
   L.   Changes to the configuration of landscaping and the color scheme of the
building shall be subject to review by the Winnetka Landmark Preservation
Commission.
   M.   The driveway shall not be widened, except as necessary to construct a
conforming, accessible parking space.
   N.   The Winnetka Historical Society shall designate a person to be its
liaison with residents whose homes are within 250 feet of the Subject Property
(the “Neighbors”). The Historical Society shall provide the Director of
Community Development and each of the Neighbors with the liaison’s name and
contact information.
   O.   The Winnetka Historical Society shall provide the Neighbors and the
Department of Community Development with a monthly calendar of events.
   P.   No buses shall be allowed to park in the driveway or on the street in
the residential neighborhood. All buses that bring students or other patrons to
the Historical Society shall drop their passengers off at the Subject Property
and proceed to a parking area away from the single family neighborhood
   Q.   The Village Council shall review the use of the Subject Property in
January, 2003, to evaluate whether the conditions imposed in this Ordinance
have provided adequate protection to the immediate neighborhood from the
potential negative impact of the special use.
   R.   The special use permit granted by this ordinance shall not apply to any
other user of the Subject Property. The special use permit granted by this
ordinance shall expire in the event that the Winnetka Historical Society ceases
all use of the Subject Property.
   SECTION 5 : The stipulations, conditions and restrictions set forth in the
foregoing Section 4 of this Ordinance may be modified or revised from time to
time by the Village Council following public notice and hearing, following the
procedures specified in Section 17.56 of the Winnetka Village Code for
processing special use applications.
   SECTION 6: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 20th day of November, 2001, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser and Woodbury
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    None
   APPROVED this 20th day of November, 2001.
   Signed:
   /s/Michael F. Duhl
   Village President
Countersigned:
/s/Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: October 16, 2001
Posted: October 17, 2001
Passed and Approved: November 20, 2001
Posted: November 21, 2001
ORDINANCE NO. M-29-2001
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (950 Green Bay Road)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
Lot 22 in Block 3 of Lakeside Jared Gage’s Subdivision according to the plat
thereof recorded as Document No. 12387 on February 8, 1872, in the east ½ of
the northwest ¼ of Section 17, Township 42 North, Range 13 east of the 3rd
Principal Meridian, Village of Winnetka, Cook County, Illinois, except that
part of said Lot 22 described as follows: beginning at the most westerly corner
of said Lot 22; thence northeasterly along the northwesterly lined of said Lot
22, 16.0 feet; thence southeasterly parallel with the southwesterly line of
said Lot 22, 1.51 feet; thence southerly on a straight line 49.36 feet to a
point on the southeasterly line of said Lot 22, 6.78 feet northeasterly of the
most southerly corner of Lot 22; and thence northwesterly along said
southwesterly line of Lot 22, 50.0 feet to the point of beginning.
commonly known as 950 Green Bay Road, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the C-
2 General Retail Commercial Overlay District provided in the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for the following variations:
(a) from the intensity of use requirements of Section 17.44.010(E) of the
Zoning Ordinance to allow a total lot coverage of 6,928 square feet, whereas
6,235.2 square feet is permitted, resulting in a variation of 692.8 square feet
(11.11%); and (b) from the parking requirements of Section 17.44.010(K)(2) of
the Zoning Ordinance to allow the reduction of off-street parking to four
spaces, whereas 10 spaces are required, resulting in a variation of six spaces
(60%), all for the purpose of constructing a building addition and
reconstructing the parking lot on the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, on October 8, 2001, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variation and, by the
unanimous vote of the four members then present, has reported to the Council
recommending that the requested variations be granted; and
   WHEREAS, on _October 18, 2001, on due notice thereof, the Design Review
Board considered the proposed changes to the front of the building and to the
parking area at the rear of the building and _has commented _favorably on the
building design, although it asked that more thought be given to the design of
the rear of the building; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and unique hardships associated
with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect
to the Subject Property in that: (a) the building was originally constructed in
1954 and expanded in 1966, before the commercial overlay was added to the C-
2 General Retail Zoning District; (b) the building and existing, paved parking
area cover 100% of the lot area; (c) the building was originally constructed as
an office building and both floors have continuously been used for office
purposes; (d) the proposed expansion of the first floor is necessary to convert
the space for retail uses; and (e) although the proposed expansion of the first
floor will result in the loss of 6 parking spaces, the Subject Property is
located in a special service area established to fund the construction of the
Hubbard Woods parking deck and the owner of the Subject Property has thus
contributed to the construction of off-street parking designed to alleviate
some of the parking demands in the Hubbard Woods shopping district; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that:
(a) the Subject Property is in a commercial zoning district and the prevailing
uses of adjoining properties are retail uses; (b) the existing first floor
spaces are smaller than the minimum size needed for marketable retail space;
and (c) due to the structure of the building, it is not possible to consolidate
the existing retail spaces into a single, larger retail space; (d) expanding
the first floor of the building as proposed is the only practical alternative
available for providing marketable retail space on the first floor; (e) the
current configuration of the property, with the 100% impermeable surface, is
consistent with other properties in the area; and (f) providing a landscaped
area at the rear of the building is inconsistent with development in the
immediate vicinity, provides no visual benefit to the general public and
further reduces the amount of available parking; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because the building expansion and modified parking area are
at the rear of the building, are consistent with the rear portions of adjacent
properties and do not affect the streetscape; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not impair an adequate supply of
light and air because the proposed building expansion that results in the
reduction of available parking is located under the existing second floor of
the building; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property as the building expansion resulting in
the loss of parking will comply with all applicable building and fire
protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not diminish the taxable value of
land and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because the proposed improvements will enhance the
marketability of its retail space; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed building expansion and requested variations from the
impermeable surface limitations and off-street parking requirements will not
contribute to congestion on the public streets, because the new retail space on
the first floor does not require off-street parking and employee parking and
additional public parking are available in the Hubbard Woods parking deck; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed requested variations will
otherwise impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village, and the creation of viable retail space is likely
to benefit the Village by generating additional sales tax revenues; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations are in harmony with the general purpose
and intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that they will convert the
street level space in the building to retail space, which is the primary
permitted use in the C-2 zoning district, and will result in a building whose
use and appearance are consistent with the uses and appearance of the buildings
in the immediate vicinity.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2:  The following variations are hereby granted to the Subject
Property, commonly known as 950 Green Bay Road, Winnetka, Illinois, and located
in the C-2 General Retail Commercial Overlay District provided in the Winnetka
Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code: (a) from the intensity
of use requirements of Section 17.44.010(E) of the Zoning Ordinance to allow a
total lot coverage of 6,928 square feet, whereas 6,235.2 square feet is
permitted, resulting in a variation of 692.8 square feet (11.11%); and (b) from
the parking requirements of Section 17.44.010(K)(2) of the Zoning Ordinance to
allow the reduction of off-street parking to four spaces, whereas 10 spaces are
required, resulting in a variation of six spaces (60%), all for the purpose of
constructing a building addition and reconstructing the parking lot on the
Subject Property, all in accordance with the drawings and plans submitted with
the application for variation.
   SECTION 3: That the variations hereby granted are conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 20th day of November, 2001, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser and Woodbury
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    None
   APPROVED this 20th day of November, 2001.
   Signed:
   /s/Michael F. Duhl
   Village President
Countersigned:
/s/Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: November 13, 2001
Posted: November 14, 2001
Passed and Approved: November 20, 2001
Posted: November 21, 2001
ORDINANCE NO. M-30-2001
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (339 Sheridan Road)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
That portion of Lot 2 in “Clay and Zimmerman’s” Resubdivision of Lots 3 and 4,
in Block 1 in Dale’s Addition to Winnetka, being a subdivision of the north
7.50 chains of the southeast fractional quarter of Section 21, Township 42
North, Range 12 East of the Third Principal Meridian; together with a strip of
land lying easterly of and adjoining said Lot 4 extending easterly to the shore
of Lake Michigan, and lying between the northerly and southerly lines (as
extended easterly to the shore of Lake Michigan) of said Lot 4; bounded and
described as follows: Beginning at the northwesterly corner of said Lot 2 and
running thence easterly along the northerly line thereof to a point 10.10 feet
west of the easterly line thereof; thence southerly along a line parallel to
and 10.0 feet westerly of said easterly line to the southerly line of said Lot
2; thence westerly along said southerly line of Lot 2 to a point 10.0 feet east
of the westerly line thereof; thence northwesterly along a straight line to the
point of beginning; according to the plat thereof recorded June 20, 1922 as
Document #T.S.-156703 in Book 7 of plats, pages 46 and 47 therein, in Cook
County, Illinois,
commonly known as 339 Sheridan Road, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
2 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for the following variations:
(a) a variation from the front setback requirements of Section 17.24.010(H) of
the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance to allow a front setback of 34 feet, whereas a
50-foot setback is required, resulting in a variation of 16 feet (32%); and (b)
a variation from the rear yard requirements of Section 17.24.010(J) to allow a
rear yard of 7.50 feet, whereas a 10-foot rear yard is required, resulting in a
variation of 2.5 feet (25%), all for the purpose of constructing a one-story
conservatory addition to the residence on the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, on October 8, 2001, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variations and has been
forwarded to the Council without the favorable recommendation of a majority of
the Zoning Board of Appeals, with three of the four members then present having
voted to recommend approval and the fourth member then present having voted
against the requested variations; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and unique hardships associated
with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect
to the Subject Property in that: (a) the Subject Property is a legally
nonconforming lot, with an area of 8,940 square feet rather than the minimum
24,000 square feet, and a lot depth of 59 feet rather than the required minimum
of 200 feet, although its average lot width is 151.52 feet, which exceeds the
minimum average lot width of 100 feet; (b) application of the 50-foot front
setback and 10-foot rear setback requirements virtually eliminates any
buildable area on the Subject Property, making it impossible to develop it in
any way without a variation in the front and rear setbacks; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that:
(a) the gross floor area of the residence, including the proposed conservatory
addition, is well within the maximum building size limitations applicable to
the Subject Property; (b) the proposed conservatory addition will enhance the
livability of the residence, which was built in 1928 and has a fairly small
living room; and (c) in 1996, the Village Council adopted Ordinance M-473-96,
which granted a variation to the Subject Property for a nearly identical
conservatory addition; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because the proposed addition will be built within the
existing front and rear building lines of the residence and will be within the
limits of the Zoning Ordinance for maximum roofed lot coverage and maximum
building size; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not impair an adequate supply of light
and air because the proposed addition is both lower and narrower than the
portion of the house to which it will be attached; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property, as it will comply with all applicable
building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvement; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed conservatory addition will not contribute to
congestion in the public streets, as the structure will continue to be used as
a single family residence and no additional bedrooms are proposed; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed conservatory addition will
otherwise impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation is in harmony with the general purpose and
intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that it allows the renovation of a
structurally sound existing building while maintaining the existing scale and
appearance of the community and protecting established trees and landscaping.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2:  The following variations are hereby granted to the Subject
Property, commonly known as 339 Sheridan Road, Winnetka, Illinois, and located
in the R-2 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in the Winnetka
Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code: (a) a variation from
the front setback requirements of Section 17.24.010(H) of the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance to allow a front setback of 34 feet, whereas a 50-foot setback is
required, resulting in a variation of 16 feet (32%); and (b) a variation from
the rear yard requirements of Section 17.24.010(J) to allow a rear yard of 7.50
feet, whereas a 10-foot rear yard is required, resulting in a variation of 2.5
feet (25%), all for the purpose of constructing a one-story conservatory
addition to the residence on the Subject Property, in accordance with the plans
and drawings submitted with the application for the variations.
   SECTION 3: That the variation hereby granted is conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 20th day of November, 2001, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser an dWoodbury
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    None
   APPROVED this 20th day of November, 2001.
   Signed:
   /s/Michael F. Duhl
   Village President
Countersigned:
/s/Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: November 13, 2001
Posted: November 14, 2001
Passed and Approved: November 20, 2001
Posted: November 21, 2001   
ORDINANCE NO. M-31-2001
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (300 Woodland Avenue)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
Lots 1, 2 and 3 (except the N. 8 ½ feet) in Block 8 in Lake Shore Subdivision
in Winnetka in Section 21, Twp. 42 N., R 13 E of the 3rd P.M., all in Cook
County, Illinois,
commonly known as 300 Woodland Avenue, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the
R-4 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for the following variations:
(a) a variation from the setback requirements of Section 17.16.010(H) of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, to allow a proposed one-story screen porch to be set
back 17.8 feet from the south lot line along Hawthorne Lane, whereas a corner
setback of 26.68 feet is required, resulting in a variation of 8.8 feet
(33.08%); and (b) a variation from the side yard limitations of Section
17.16.010(I) of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance to allow a proposed one-story,
attached garage to be set back 1.93 feet from the north lot line, whereas 6.0-
foot side yard is required, resulting in a variation of 4.07 feet (67.83%); and
   WHEREAS, on October 8, 2001, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variation and by the
unanimous vote of the four members then present, has reported to the Council
recommending that the requested variation be granted; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and unique hardships associated
with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect
to the Subject Property in that: (a) the Subject Property is located at the
northwest corner of Hawthorne Lane and Woodland Avenue; (b) the residence on
the Subject Property was constructed in 1913, before the enactment of the first
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance; (c) the existing, detached frame garage, which is
located 1.49 feet from the north property line, is dilapidated and not suitable
for vehicle storage; (d) the Subject Property is located across the street from
the Greeley School, making driveway access off Hawthorne Lane less practical
and less safe; and (e) constructing a new garage in a conforming location would
substantially increase the amount of impermeable surface on the Subject
Property, would alter the appearance of the Subject Property from Hawthorne
Lane and would reduce the utility of the garage by moving it farther from the
existing home; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that:
(a) the existing, detached frame garage, which is located 1.49 feet from the
north property line, is dilapidated and not suitable for vehicle storage; (b)
constructing the garage and screened porch in conforming locations would
interfere with large trees located in the rear of the Subject Property,
decreasing the property’s value; and (c) the residence on the Subject Property
has a gross floor area that is substantially less than the limit applicable to
the Subject Property and requiring the garage and screened porch to be built in
conforming locations would prevent the owners from developing their property in
a manner and to an extent comparable with the development of other single-
family properties in the immediate vicinity; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because: (a) the proposed garage will be in the same area as
the existing garage and will be accessed by the same driveway; (b) the proposed
garage and screened porch will be located so as to minimize their impact on
mature trees that are visible from Hawthorne Lane; and (c) the design and
materials of the proposed garage and screened porch are consistent with the
design and materials of the existing residence; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not impair an adequate supply of light
and air because the proposed garage and screened porch are only one-story high,
the proposed garage will be set back further from the north lot line than the
existing garage, and the proposed screened porch faces Hawthorne Lane and does
not impact on any other single family properties; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property, as it will comply with all applicable
building and fire protection codes and the new garage will be farther from the
neighboring frame garage, which is located less than one foot from the north
lot line of the Subject Property, providing a one-hour fire separation between
the two garages; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed screened porch and attached garage will not contribute
to congestion on the public streets, as the garage will improve off-street
parking and the property will continue to be used for single family purposes;
and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed screened porch and attached
garage will otherwise impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and
welfare of the inhabitants of the Village; and
WHEREAS, the requested variation is in harmony with the general purpose and
intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that it allows the renovation,
restoration and rehabilitation of a structurally sound existing building while
maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the community and protecting
established trees and landscaping.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2:  The following variations are granted to the Subject Property,
commonly known as 300 Woodland Avenue, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the
R-4 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code: (a) a variation from the
setback requirements of Section 17.16.010(H) of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance,
to allow a proposed one-story screen porch to be set back 17.8 feet from the
south lot line along Hawthorne Lane, whereas a corner setback of 26.68 feet is
required, resulting in a variation of 8.8 feet (33.08%); and (b) a variation
from the side yard limitations of Section 17.16.010(I) of the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance to allow a proposed one-story, attached garage to be set back 1.93
feet from the north lot line, whereas 6.0-foot side yard is required, resulting
in a variation of 4.07 feet (67.83%), all in accordance with the plans and
drawings submitted with the application for variation.
   SECTION 3: That the variation hereby granted is conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 20th day of November, 2001, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser and Woodbury
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    None
   APPROVED this 20th day of November, 2001.
   Signed:
   /s/Michael F. Duhl
   Village President
Countersigned:
/s/Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
