ORDINANCE NO. M-39-2001
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (333 Sheridan Road)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
Lot 5 in Clay and Zimmerman’s Subdivision of Lots 3 and 4 in Block 1 in Dale’s
Addition to Winnetka, a Subdivision of the North 7.5 chains of the southwest
fractional quarter of fractional Section 21, Township 42 North, Range 13, East
of the Third Principal Meridian, and also a strip of land lying easterly of and
adjoining said Lot 4 and extending easterly to the shore of Lake Michigan and
lying between the northerly and southerly lines extended Easterly to the shore
of Lake Michigan of said Lot 4,
commonly known as 333 Sheridan Road, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
2 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.24 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for the
following variations: (a) a variation from the 50.0 foot front setback required
by Section 17.24.010(H) of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, to allow a proposed
addition to the front of the residence to observe a front setback of 45 feet,
resulting in a variation of 5.0 feet (10%); (b) a variation from the 50.0 foot
front setback required by Section 17.24.010(H) of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance
to allow proposed alterations to an existing portion of the building to observe
a front setback of 28 feet, resulting in a variation of 22 feet (44%); and (c)
a variation from the rear yard requirements of Section 17.24.010(J) to allow a
proposed building addition to observe a rear yard of 4.27 feet, whereas a 12-
foot rear yard is required, resulting in a variation of 7.73 feet (64.42%), all
for the purpose of constructing a one-story addition to the west side and rear
of the existing residence, and increasing window openings in the nonconforming
east building wall; and
   WHEREAS, the owners have submitted revised drawings, dated November 1, 2001,
that modified a bay window at the rear of the building, proposing a rear yard
setback of 6.02 feet, thereby reducing the requested rear yard setback
variation to 5.98 feet (49.83%); and
   WHEREAS, on November 12, 2001, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variations and by the
unanimous vote of the five members then present, has reported to the Council
recommending that the requested variation be granted; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and unique hardships associated
with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect
to the Subject Property in that: (a) the Subject Property is an irregularly
shaped, legally nonconforming lot, with an area of 11,652 square feet rather
than the minimum 24,000 square feet, and a lot depth ranging from 86.40 feet on
the west to 72.80 feet on the east, rather than the required minimum lot depth
of 200 feet, although its average lot width is 132.86 feet, which exceeds the
minimum average lot width of 100 feet; (b) the existing residence, which was
built in 1926, has a one-car garage, a small kitchen and no family room; and
(c) application of the 50-foot front setback and 10-foot rear setback
requirements substantially reduces the buildable area on the Subject Property,
so that most of the existing residence is in a nonconforming location, and
adding to the building and making any structural alterations is impossible
without obtaining variations in the front and rear setbacks; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that:
(a) the gross floor area of the residence, including the proposed addition, is
well within the maximum building size limitations applicable to the Subject
Property; (b) the proposed addition is necessary to provide a two-car garage,
an adequate kitchen and a family room, all of which are standard features in
the real estate market in the Village, and in homes in the immediate vicinity
of the Subject Property; (c) the proposed addition must be located at the rear
of the house in order to tie it into the floor plan and structure of the
existing house; and (d) the window changes proposed for the nonconforming east
walls do not change the location of those walls; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because: (a) the proposed addition is located at the rear of
the house and is within the lot coverage and maximum building size limitations
that apply to the Subject Property; (b) the design of the addition is
consistent with the architecture of the existing building; and (c) the new
windows are consistent with the style of windows at the front of the house; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not impair an adequate supply of
light and air because the proposed addition is one story high, falls well
within all building size limitations, and maintains the existing orientation of
the residence on the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property as the proposed addition will comply with
all applicable building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not diminish the taxable value of
land and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed building addition will not contribute to congestion on
the public streets, as the structure will continue to be used as a single
family residence and the two-car garage will increase off-street parking; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed building addition and window
changes will otherwise impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and
welfare of the inhabitants of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations are in harmony with the general purpose
and intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that they will allow the
renovation, restoration and rehabilitation of a structurally sound existing
building while maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the community
and protecting established trees and landscaping.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : The foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the findings
of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth herein.
   SECTION 2: The following variations are hereby granted to the Subject
Property, commonly known as 333 Sheridan Road, Winnetka, Illinois, and located
in the R-2 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.24
of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code: (a) a
variation from the 50.0 foot front setback required by Section 17.24.010(H) of
the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, to allow a proposed addition to the front of the
residence to observe a front setback of 45 feet, resulting in a variation of
5.0 feet (10%); (b) a variation from the 50.0 foot front setback required by
Section 17.24.010(H) of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance to allow proposed
alterations to an existing portion of the building to observe a front setback
of 28 feet, resulting in a variation of 22 feet (44%); and (c) a variation from
the rear yard requirements of Section 17.24.010(J) to allow a proposed building
addition to observe a rear yard of 6.02 feet, whereas a 12-foot rear yard is
required, resulting in a variation of 5.98 feet (49.83%), all for the purpose
of constructing a one-story addition to a portion of the west side and along
the rear of the existing residence, and altering the window openings in the
nonconforming east building wall, all in accordance with the plans and
elevations submitted with the application for variations, as modified on
November 1, 2001.
   SECTION 3: The variations hereby granted are conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 18th day of December, 2001, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    None
   APPROVED this 18th day of December, 2001.
   Signed:
   /s/Michael F. Duhl
   Village President
Countersigned:
/s/Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
