ORDINANCE NO. M-1-2002
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (975 Private Road)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
Lot 1 in Fenbrook, a subdivision of Lot 3 in the subdivision of Block 11
(except the 1 ½ acres thereof) in Hubbard Estates subdivision of the northeast
¼ of fractional Section 17, also a part of Lot 2 in the subdivision of Lot 1 in
the subdivision of Block 10 in said Hubbard Estates subdivision of the
northeast fractional quarter of Section 17, also part of Lot 2 in the
subdivision of Block 10 in said Hubbard Estates subdivision of the northeast
fractional quarter of Section 17, Township 42 North, Range 13, east of the
third Principal Meridian, in Cook County, Illinois,
commonly known as 975 Private Road, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
2 Single Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.24 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for a
variation from the front setback requirements of Section 17.24.010(H) of the
Zoning Ordinance, to allow the relocation of the single family residence on the
Subject Property, with the relocated building and proposed porte cochere
observing a front setback of zero (0) feet, although a minimum setback of 50
feet is required; and
   WHEREAS, on January 14, 2002, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variation and by the
affirmative vote of five of the six members then present, has reported to the
Council recommending that the variation be granted; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property is improved with a 1½ story single-family
residence that has been identified as the coach house and caretaker’s cottage
of the former Lyons estate, which was designed by architect Howard van Doren
Shaw and built in 1906; and
   WHEREAS, the owners of the Subject Property learned of the architectural
significance of the structure in the course of applying for a demolition permit
in conjunction with their plans to replace the existing residence with a new
home; and
   WHEREAS, rather than demolishing the residence, the owners now propose to
renovate the building and to relocate it on the Subject Property by rotating
the building 180 degrees, so that its front, currently the north elevation,
faces Private Road; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property is encumbered by a 20-foot utility easement
that extends along the entire northwest lot line, which is more than 300 feet
long; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property is also encumbered by a 20-foot roadway
easement that extends along the entire south lot line, which is approximately
240 feet long; and
   WHEREAS, Private Road is partially located within the roadway easement,
although it occupies less than half of the roadway easement; and
   WHEREAS, the 50-foot front setback required by the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance
must be measured from the north edge of the roadway easement; and
   WHEREAS, the existing residence, as currently located, lies along the north
edge of the roadway easement and encroaches into a small portion of the
easement, and thus has a front setback of zero feet; and
   WHEREAS, although the proposed new location of the residence is 15 feet
north of the roadway easement line, the owners also propose to add a porte
cochere to the south elevation, with the porte cochere being set at the
easement line, resulting in a proposed front setback of zero feet; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and unique circumstances
associated with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance
with respect to the Subject Property, in that: (1) the buildable area of the
Subject Property is limited by the irregular shape of the Subject Property and
the roadway and utility easements; (2) rotating the house will orient the true
front of the residence to the street; (3) the requested variation will reduce
the degree of nonconformity in the location of the residence, because the bulk
of the residence currently observes a zero-foot front setback and the relocated
residence will be set back 15 feet, with only the porte cochere being at the
setback line; and (4) the requested variations will allow an architecturally
significant building to be saved, by bringing it up to modern living standards;
and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, because,
due to the reduced buildable area that results from the triangular shape of the
lot, the angle of the legs of the triangles, and the utility and roadway
easements, there are no conforming alternatives that would allow the existing
house to be brought up to modern living standards; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because: (a) it will allow the house to be preserved on the
site on which it was built; (b) it will orient the front of the house to the
street line, consistent with the standard orientation of single-family
residences; (c) the existing berm at the southeast corner of the Subject
Property, which is an established feature of the property, will remain and will
obscure much of the new addition and garage proposed for the Subject Property;
and (d) the new location proposed for the residence will be consistent with its
existing location, which also has a nonconforming front setback; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not impair an adequate supply of light
and air because the size and dimensions of the house will remain within the
limits established by the Zoning Ordinance and will observe required side yard
and rear yard setbacks; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property, as the proposed relocation, renovation
and addition will comply with all applicable building and fire protection
codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed relocation of the house will not contribute to
congestion on the public streets, as it will continue to be used as a single
family residence and the proposed work includes a garage addition; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed relocation of the house will
otherwise impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation is in harmony with the general purpose and
intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that: (a) it allows the renovation,
restoration and rehabilitation of a structurally sound existing building while
maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the community and protecting
established trees and landscaping; and (b) it ensures and facilitates the
preservation of a structure of architectural and aesthetic importance.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : The foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the findings
of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth herein.
   SECTION 2: There is hereby granted to the Subject Property, commonly known
as 975 Private Road, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-2 Single Family
Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.24 of the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, a variation from the front
setback requirements of Section 17.24.010(H) of the Zoning Ordinance, to allow
the relocation of the single family residence on the Subject Property, with the
relocated building observing a front setback of approximately 15 feet and with
a new porte cochere observing a front setback of zero (0) feet, rather than the
minimum required setback of 50 feet, as depicted in the proposed site plans and
conceptual building plan submitted with the application for variation.
   SECTION 3: The variation hereby granted is conditioned upon the commencement
of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective date of this
Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 5th day of February, 2002, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser and Woodbury
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    None
   Signed:
   s/s Michael F. Duhl
    Village President
Countersigned:
s/s Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: January 15, 2002
Posted: January 16, 2002
Passed and Approved: February 5, 2002
Posted: February 6, 2002
ORDINANCE NO. M-2-2002(Not adopted)
ORDINANCE NO. M-3-2002
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (220 Birch Street)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
That part of Lot 1 in Block 6 in Alles’ First Addition to Winnetka, in the
southwest quarter of the southeast quarter of Section 20, Township 42 North,
Range 13 east of the Third Principal Meridian, described as follows: the North
146.60 feet (except the North 33 feet for road) of the West 192.0 feet of the
East 225.0 feet of said southwest quarter of the southeast quarter of Section
20, Township 42 North, Range 13 east of the Third Principal Meridian, in Cook
County, Illinois,
commonly known as 220 Birch Street, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
2 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.24 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for a
variation from the front setback requirements of Section 17.24.010(H) to allow
a front setback of 23.27 feet, whereas 50 feet is required, resulting in a
variation of 26.73 feet (53%), for the purpose of constructing dormer additions
to the upper floor of the residence on the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, on February 11, 2002, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variation and by the
unanimous vote of the five members then present, has reported to the Council
recommending that the requested variation be granted; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and unique hardships associated
with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect
to the Subject Property in that: (a) the Subject Property is a corner lot
located at the southwest corner of Birch and Sunset; (b) the single family
residence on the Subject Property was originally constructed as a coach house
around 1919, before the initial enactment of the Village’s Zoning Ordinance;
(c) the single family residence on the Subject Property is an architecturally
significant French country cottage that is an established presence in the
streetscape; (d)the residence has a legally nonconforming front setback, with
the entire main portion of the residence being located within the required 50-
foot setback and a portion of the residence being located 8.10 feet from the
Birch Street property line; and (e) due to the line of the roof, all five of
the proposed dormers will be set further back from the street than the existing
building lines; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that:
(a) the existing nonconformity cannot be cured without demolishing and
replacing the residence in its entirety; (b) the existing residence has no
basement but, due to the roof form and building height, has a large attic, and
the proposed dormers will allow the existing attic space to be converted into
livable space; and (c) because the main body of the residence is in a
nonconforming location, there is no conforming alternative for providing
additional living space in the residence; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because the footprint of the building will not change, the
dormers will all be set back further from the street than the existing building
lines, and the proposed dormers have been designed to be consistent with the
architectural style of the residence; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not impair an adequate supply of light
and air because the proposed dormers will all be lower than the ridge of the
roof and will be set back further from all property lines than the existing
building; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property as it will comply with all applicable
building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the requested variation will diminish the
taxable value of land and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable
value of the Subject Property may be increased because of the proposed
improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed dormers will not contribute to congestion on the
public streets, as the building will remain a single family residence and there
is no reduction in off-street parking; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed dormers will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village.
   WHEREAS, the requested variation is in harmony with the general purpose and
intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that it allows the renovation,
restoration and rehabilitation of a structurally sound existing building while
maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the community and protecting
established trees and landscaping.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2:  The Subject Property, commonly known as 220 Birch Street,
Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-2 Single-Family Residential Zoning
District provided in Chapter 17.24 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17
of the Winnetka Village Code, is hereby granted a variation from the front
setback requirements of Section 17.24.010(H) to allow a front setback of 23.27
feet, whereas 50 feet is required, resulting in a variation of 26.73 feet
(53%), for the purpose of constructing dormer additions to the upper floor of
the residence on the Subject Property, in accordance with the plans and
elevations submitted with the application for variation.
   SECTION 3: That the variation hereby granted is conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 19th day of March, 2002, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser and Woodbury
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    None
   Signed:
   s/s Michael F. Duhl
    Village President
Countersigned:
s/s Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: March 5, 2002
Posted: March 6, 2002
Passed and Approved: March 19, 2002
