ORDINANCE NO. M-6-2002
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (975 Private Road)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
Lot 1 in Fenbrook, a subdivision of Lot 3 in the subdivision of Block 11
(except the 1 ½ acres thereof) in Hubbard Estates subdivision of the northeast
¼ of fractional Section 17, also a part of Lot 2 in the subdivision of Lot 1 in
the subdivision of Block 10 in said Hubbard Estates subdivision of the
northeast fractional quarter of Section 17, also part of Lot 2 in the
subdivision of Block 10 in said Hubbard Estates subdivision of the northeast
fractional quarter of Section 17, Township 42 North, Range 13, east of the
third Principal Meridian, in Cook County, Illinois,
commonly known as 975 Private Road, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
2 Single-family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.24 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for a
variation from the 18-foot garage door width limitation of Section 17.24.0101
(P) of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, to permit the construction on the Subject
Property of an attached garage addition with three front-facing garage doors
having a total width of 27 feet; and
   WHEREAS, on March 11, 2002, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variation and by the
affirmative vote of five of the six members then present, has reported to the
Council recommending that the variation be granted; and
   WHEREAS, on February 5, 2002, the Council of the Village of Winnetka
(”Village Council”) adopted ordinance M-1-2002, which granted a front setback
variation to allow the relocation of the single family residence on the Subject
Property, with the relocated building and proposed porte cochere observing a
front setback of zero foot; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property is improved with a 1½ story single-family
residence that has been identified as the coach house and caretaker’
   WHEREAS, the owners of the Subject Property learned of the architectural
significance of the structure in the course of applying for a demolition permit
in conjunction with their plans to replace the existing residence with a new
home; and
   WHEREAS, rather than demolishing the residence, the owners have proposed to
renovate the building and to relocate it on the Subject Property by rotating
the building 180 degrees, so that its front, currently the north elevation,
faces Private Road, and by adding a porte cohere at the front of the residence
and constructing an attached garage addition on the east side of the residence;
and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and unique circumstances
associated with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance
with respect to the Subject Property, in that: (1) the buildable area of the
Subject Property is limited by the irregular shape of the Subject Property, the
topography of the lot, which is located in the ravines, and the roadway and
utility easements; and (2) the requested variation is being sought in
conjunction with a plan to relocate and modify the existing house, which plan
was approved by the Village Council’s adoption of Ordinance M-1-2002, will
reduce the degree of nonconformity in the location of the residence, and will
allow an architecturally significant building to be saved, by bringing it up to
modern living standards; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, because,
due to the reduced buildable area that results from the triangular shape of the
lot, the angle of the legs of the triangles, and the utility and roadway
easements, there are no conforming alternatives that would allow the
construction of an adequate detached garage at the rear of the property, and
the location of the garage is determined in part by the relocation of the
existing house on the lot, as previously approved by the Village Council’s
adoption of ordinance M-1-2002; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because: (a) it is being requested as part of a plan that will
allow the architecturally significant house to be preserved on the site on
which it was built; and (b) the existing berm at the southeast corner of the
Subject Property, which is an established feature of the property, will remain
and will obscure much of the new addition and garage proposed for the Subject
Property; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not impair an adequate supply of light
and air because the size and dimensions of the house with attached garage
addition will remain within the limits established by the Zoning Ordinance and
will observe required side yard and rear yard setbacks; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property, as the proposed garage will comply with
all applicable building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because the proposed garage is part of a project that
will relocate and preserve an architecturally significant house; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed attached garage with front-facing doors will not
contribute to congestion on the public streets, as it will provide additional
off-street parking and twill be attached to the existing, but relocated,
single-family residence, which will continue to be used as a single-family
residence; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed relocation of the house will
otherwise impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation is in harmony with the general purpose and
intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that: (a) it is part of a plan that
allows the renovation, restoration and rehabilitation of a structurally sound
existing building while maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the
community and protecting established trees and landscaping; and (b) it ensures
and facilitates the preservation of a structure of architectural and aesthetic
importance.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2: There is hereby granted to the Subject Property, commonly known
as 975 Private Road, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-2 Single-family
Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.24 of the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, a variation from the 18-foot
garage door width limitation of Section 17.24.0101(P) of the Zoning Ordinance,
to permit the construction on the Subject Property of an attached garage
addition with three front-facing garage doors having a total width of 27 feet,
said garage to be constructed in conjunction with the relocation and renovation
of the single-family residence on the Subject Property, as previously approved
by the Village Council’s adoption of Ordinance M-1-2002, all of said work to be
as depicted in the proposed site plans and conceptual building plan submitted
with the application for variation.
   SECTION 3: The variation hereby granted is conditioned upon the commencement
of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective date of this
Ordinance; provided, that nothing herein shall be construed as an extension of
time provided in Section 3 of Ordinance M-1-2002 for the commencement of the
work approved in that ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 2nd day of April, 2002, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser and Woodbury
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    None
   Signed:
   s/s Michael F. Duhl
    Village President
Countersigned:
s/s Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: March 19, 2002
Posted: March 20, 2002
Passed and Approved: April 2, 2002
