ORDINANCE NO. M-7-2002
AN ORDINANCE AUTHORIZING AGREEMENTS TO LEASE THE ELM STREET AND HUBBARD WOODS
COMMUTER TRAIN STATIONS FROM THE UNION PACIFIC RAILROAD
   WHEREAS, the Elm Street Commuter Train Station and the Hubbard Woods
Commuter Train Station (“Train Stations”) are owned and operated by the Union
Pacific Railroad (“UPRR”); and
   WHEREAS, UPRR has offered to make improvements to the Train Stations and
their exterior grounds if the Village of Winnetka (“Village”) will thereafter
assume responsibility for the operation and maintenance of the Train Stations;
and
   WHEREAS, the Village has negotiated terms of two lease agreements copies of
which are attached to this resolution as Exhibits 1 and 2 (“Lease Agreements”),
whereby the UPRR will lease the Train Stations to the Village for a period of
20-years, the UPRR will perform certain repairs and renovations of the Train
Stations and exterior grounds at its expense, the Village will assume the
responsibility for operation and maintenance of the Train Stations and the
Village will have the right to sublet space in the Train Stations and to
collect rent for such subleases; and
   WHEREAS, the Village will not assume any responsibility for the operation of
the METRA commuter trains served by the Train Stations; and
   WHEREAS, the Lease Agreements protect the Village from liability related to
the operation, maintenance and condition of the station platforms, station
elevators, station stairways, exterior passenger areas, and railroad right-of-
way, which will remain the responsibility of the UPRR; and
   WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka (“Village Council”) find and
determine that the proposed Lease Agreements for the Train Stations serve a
public purpose by making improvements to the Train Stations and surrounding
station areas, thereby providing safe and clean passenger waiting areas,
improving the appearance of the Train Stations, which are significant landmarks
in the Elm Street and Hubbard Woods retail shopping districts, and providing a
revenue stream that can be used to defray the Village’s costs of operating and
maintaining the train Stations; and
   WHEREAS, pursuant to the provisions of Section 11-61-3 of Article 11 of the
Illinois Municipal Code, 65 ILCS 5/11-61-3, the Village of Winnetka is
authorized to lease real estate for public purposes pursuant to contracts that
provide for the consideration for the lease to be paid at stated intervals
during a period of time not exceeding 20 years.
NOW THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1: The foregoing recitals are incorporated herein as the findings of
the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth herein.
   SECTION 2: The Council of the Village of Winnetka hereby approve the lease
agreement for the UPRR’s leasing of the Elm Street Commuter Railroad Station to
the Village, substantially in the form attached hereto as Exhibit 1 and
incorporated herein by reference.
   SECTION 3: The Council of the Village of Winnetka hereby approve the lease
agreement for the UPRR’s leasing of the Hubbard Woods Commuter Railroad Station
to the Village, substantially in the form attached hereto as Exhibit 2 and
incorporated herein by reference.
   SECTION 4: The Village President and Village Clerk are hereby authorized and
directed to execute the two Lease Agreements on behalf of the Village.
   SECTION 5: This Ordinance shall be in full force and effect from and after
its passage, approval, and posting in the manner provided by law.
   ADOPTED this 9th day of April, 2002, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, and Woodbury
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    Trustee Presser
   Signed:
   s/s Michael F. Duhl
    Village President
Countersigned:
s/s Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: March 19, 2002
Posted: March 20, 2002
Passed and Approved: April 9, 2002
Posted: April 10, 2002
ORDINANCE NO. M-8-2002
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (1162 Asbury Avenue)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
Lot 6 in Block 18 in Chicago North Shore Land Company’s Subdivision in Section
17 and 18, Township 42 North, Range 13, East of the Third Principal Meridian in
Cook County, Illinois,
commonly known as 1162 Asbury Avenue, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
5 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.12 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for the
following variations from the 6.0-foot side yard setback requirement of Section
17.12.010(I): (a) a variation of 2.66 feet (44.33%) to allow an existing
screened porch located 3.34 feet from the west property line to be fully
enclosed; (b) a variation of 2.66 feet (44.33%) to allow a second floor dormer
above the existing screened porch to be expanded to a point no less than 3.34
feet from the west property line; and (c) a variation of 0.02 feet (00.33%) to
allow a new front porch to be constructed with its west wall observing a
setback of 5.98 feet from the west property line; and
   WHEREAS, on March 11, 2002, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variations and, by the
unanimous vote of the five members then present, has reported to the Council
recommending that the requested variations be granted; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and unique hardships associated
with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect
to the Subject Property in that: (a) the Subject Property is improved with a 2½
-story frame residence that was constructed in 1917, before the enactment of
the Village’s Zoning Ordinance; (b) the nonconforming screened porch and dormer
above it are part of the existing structure, rather than being mere appendages,
and the existing nonconformities cannot be cured without removing a portion of
the existing structure and relocating foundations and footings; (c) the
proposed porch enclosure, dormer expansion and new front porch will allow for a
more clearly defined front entry, provide a more efficient use of interior
space, provide an entrance closet and first floor powder room, and allow for
second floor bathrooms to be upgraded, all of which are consistent with
contemporary housing standards in the vicinity; (d) the porch enclosure and
expansion of the dormer do not increase the building’s footprint; (e) the
00.33% variation requested for the front porch is de minimis; and (f) despite
the expansion of the dormer, most of the dormer will be in a conforming
location and the exterior walls of the dormer will be set back further from the
west property line than the walls of the enclosed porch below; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that:
(a) the existing nonconformities cannot be cured without removing portions of
the existing house and relocating parts of the foundation and footings; and (b)
application of the strict letter of the Zoning Ordinance would prohibit the
foundation repairs and structural improvements that are part of the enclosure
of the screened porch and the expansion of the dormer; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because: (a) enclosing the screened porch does not increase
the bulk of the building; (b) the expansion of the dormer adds minimal bulk;
(c) the proposed front porch improves the appearance of the buildings and
defines the front entrance; (d) the 00.33% variation requested for the front
porch is de minimis; (e) the requested variations are part of a renovation and
restoration of the residence that includes removal of aluminum siding and
restoring the home to its previous shingle style; and (f) the size and location
of the building in relation to neighboring properties and the streetscape will
not change; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not impair an adequate supply of
light and air because: (a) the porch enclosure does not alter the existing
footprint of the building; (b) the proposed front porch faces the street and
therefore does not impair sunlight to adjacent properties; (c) both the
enclosed porch and the dormer are well below the ridge of the principal roof
form of the building; (d) the nonconforming portion of the proposed new front
porch is one story high and, at 0.02 feet, is also of negligible width; and (e)
there are no first floor side windows on the adjacent property that would be
affected by the negligible porch encroachment; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property as the work will comply with all
applicable building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not diminish the taxable value of
land and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed enclosure of the existing porch, expansion of the
existing dormer and addition of the front porch will not contribute to
congestion on the public streets, as the Subject Property will continue to be
used for single family purposes; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed enclosure of the screened
porch, expansion of the existing dormer or addition of the front porch will
otherwise impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village.
   WHEREAS, the requested variations are in harmony with the general purpose
and intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that they allows the
renovation, restoration and rehabilitation of a structurally sound existing
building while maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the community
and protecting established trees and landscaping.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2:  That the following variations from the 6.0-foot side yard
setback requirements of Section 17.12.010(I) are hereby granted to the Subject
Property, commonly known as 1162 Asbury Avenue, Winnetka, Illinois, and located
in the R-5 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.12
of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code: (a) a
variation of 2.66 feet (44.33%) to allow an existing screened porch located
3.34 feet from the west property line to be fully enclosed; (b) a variation of
2.66 feet (44.33%) to allow a second floor dormer above the existing screened
porch to be expanded to a point no less than 3.34 feet from the west property
line; and (c) a variation of 0.02 feet (00.33%) to allow a new front porch to
be constructed with its west wall observing a setback of 5.98 feet from the
west property line, all in accordance with the plans and elevations dated
January 24, 2002 and submitted with the application for variation.
   SECTION 3: That the variations hereby granted are conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 16th day of April, 2002, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser and Woodbury
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    None
   Signed:
   s/s Michael F. Duhl
    Village President
Countersigned:
s/s Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: April 9, 2002
Posted: April 10, 2002
Passed and Approved: April 16, 2002
Posted: April 17, 2002
ORDINANCE NO. M-9-2002
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (222 Essex and 357 Sunset)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
Parcel 1: Lot 22 in Remy Park Subdivision of parts of Section 21, Township 42
North, Range 13, East of the Third Principal Meridian, according to the plat
recorded in the Recorder’s Office of Cook County, Illinois, on November 18,
1916, as Document Number 5995688, in Book 147 of Plats, page 46, in Cook
County, Illinois; and
Parcel 2: Lot 21 in Remy Park Subdivision of parts of Section 21, Township 42
North, Range 13, East of the Third Principal Meridian, according to the plat
recorded in the Recorder’s Office of Cook County, Illinois, on November 18,
1916, as Document Number 5995688, in Book 147 of Plats, page 46, in Cook
County, Illinois.
commonly known, respectively, as 222 Essex Road (Parcel 1) and 357 Sunset Road
(Parcel 2), Winnetka, Illinois, for a variation from Section 17.12.010(H) to
permit a front setback of 17.50 feet from the Essex Road corner lot line,
whereas a 30-foot setback is required, resulting in a variation of 12.50 feet
(41.67%), for the purpose of demolishing the residence on Parcel 2 and
expanding the existing residence on Parcel 1 by approximately 2,130.50 square
feet; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property is located in the R-5 Single-Family
Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.12 of the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, with Parcel 1 being located
at the northwest corner of Essex Road and Sunset Road, and Parcel 2 being
located on the north side of Sunset Road, adjacent to the west lot line of
Parcel 1; and
   WHEREAS, although the existing Parcel 1 has a lot area of 10,719.56 square
feet, which exceeds the minimum corner lot size of 8,900 square feet, it’s
average lot width is less than 60 feet; and
   WHEREAS, although the existing Parcel 2 has a lot area of 9,524.00 square
feet, which exceeds the minimum interior lot size of 8,400 square feet, it has
an actual lot width of less than 60 feet; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property, which results from the owner’s proposed
consolidation of Parcel 1 and Parcel 2 into a single lot, has a lot area of
20,243.56 square feet; and
   WHEREAS, the gross floor area on Parcel 1 is currently 4,033.74 square feet,
which exceeds the maximum gross floor area of 3,958.91 square feet; and
   WHEREAS, the current gross floor area on Parcel 2 is approximately 4,050.0
square feet, which exceeds the maximum gross floor area of 3,719.80 square
feet; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed consolidation and expansion will result in a single
principal building, including an attached garage, with a gross floor area of
6,164.25 square feet, which is substantially less than the 8,083.74 square feet
of gross floor area currently on the Subject Property and less than the
6,293.23 square-foot maximum gross floor area that would be allowed on the
Subject Property, if consolidated; and
   WHEREAS, on February 27, 2002, on due notice thereof, the Plan Commission
considered the owner’s request for preliminary approval of the plat of
consolidation and the eight members then in attendance voted unanimously to
provide favorable comment on the proposed consolidation to the Village Council,
noting that their decision was influenced by (a) the design as presented, which
includes a reduction in impermeable surface, tree preservation, and bulk that
is less than what is allowed, (b) the odd shape of the Subject Property due to
the curved street frontage along Essex Road, and (c) the large lots in the R-
4 Zoning District on the east side of Essex Road; and
   WHEREAS, on March 11, 2002, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variation and, although the
five members then present have unanimously recommended that the requested
variation be granted, the recommendation was not supported by findings
sufficient to sustain a favorable recommendation; and
   WHEREAS, the Council find the factual record prepared by the Zoning Board of
Appeals, to be sufficient to support granting the variation; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and particular hardships
associated with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance
with respect to the Subject Property in that: (a) Parcel 1 is an irregularly
shaped lot that has a curved corner lot line along Essex Road, which reduces
the lot width from 80.80 feet at Sunset Road to 44 feet at the north lot line,
thereby limiting the use of the north end of the lot and forcing the principle
building to be pushed closer to the Essex Road street frontage; (b) there is no
useable rear yard on Parcel 1, because the substantial narrowing of the lot
from south to north precludes using the north end as a rear yard and there is
not sufficient lot width for the western portion of the lot to function as a
rear yard; and (c) the nonconforming front yard results solely from the
proposed consolidation of Parcels 1 and 2, as the principle building on Parcel
1 will remain in its current location; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that
the curved corner lot line along Essex Road reduces the lot width by nearly 50
percent, thereby limiting conforming locations for any building on Parcel 1 and
precluding the development of a reasonably useable back yard area on that
property; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because: (a) the residence will continue to be oriented toward
Essex Road, and the Essex Road blockface will not change; (b) although the
length of the building along Sunset Road will be extended, the height of the
extension along Sunset Road is compatible with the heights of buildings to the
west along Sunset Road; and (c) the building design is articulated along Sunset
Road, giving the impression of two main building masses, consistent with the
current development of Parcels 1 and 2; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not impair an adequate supply of light
and air because it will comply with all building height limitations and with
the setbacks required along the north and west lot lines, which abut other
single-family properties; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property as it will comply with all applicable
building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not contribute to congestion on the
public streets, as the use of the Subject Property will not change and the
existing two-car garage will be replaced with a three-car garage; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed variation will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village.
   WHEREAS, the requested variation is in harmony with the general purpose and
intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that: (a) it allows the renovation,
restoration and rehabilitation of a structurally sound existing building on
Parcel 1; (b) it provides articulation and building height and mass along
Sunset Road that is consistent with the other buildings and lots on the
blockface, thus maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the community;
and (c) it protects established trees and landscaping.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2: That, subject to the terms and conditions hereinafter set forth,
there is hereby granted to the Subject Property, commonly known as 222 Essex
Road and 357 Sunset Road and located in the R-5 Single-Family Residential
Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.12 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance,
Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, a variation from Section 17.12.010(H) to
permit a front setback of 17.50 feet from the Essex Road corner lot line,
whereas a 30-foot setback is required, resulting in a variation of 12.50 feet
(41.67%), for the purpose of demolishing the residence on Parcel 2 and
expanding the existing residence on Parcel 1 by approximately 2,130.5 square
feet, in accordance with the plans and elevations submitted with the
application for variation.
   SECTION 3: That the variation hereby granted is subject to the condition
that, in conjunction with the proposed consolidation, a separate restrictive
covenant be recorded against the consolidated Subject Property. The restrictive
covenant shall be as follows:
      A.   No further buildings or additions shall be built on the Subject
Property.
      B.   No swimming pools shall be built on the Subject Property.
      C.   If the principal building on the Subject Property is demolished, the
consolidated lot shall revert to the existing configuration of Parcel 1 and
Parcel 2, with dimensions as they existed prior to the consolidation.
   SECTION 4: That the variation hereby granted is further subject to the
following conditions:
      A.   The owner shall obtain final approval of the proposed consolidation
of Parcel 1 and Parcel 2 and record the final plat of consolidation within 12
months after the effective date of this Ordinance, provided that nothing herein
shall be construed as granting the owner the right to such final approval.
      B.   The owner shall commence the proposed construction within 12 months
after the effective date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 5:   This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 21st day of May, 2002, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, and Presser
   NAYS:    Trustee Woodbury
   ABSENT:    None
   Signed:
   s/s Michael F. Duhl
    Village President
Countersigned:
s/s Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: April 9, 2002
Posted: April 10, 2002
Passed and Approved: May 21, 2002
Posted: May 22, 2002
ORDINANCE NO. M-10-2002
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (562 Green Bay Road)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
The northwesterly 10 feet of Lot 8 and the southeasterly 25 feet of Lot 9, the
southeasterly line of which part of said Lot 8 is 10 feet southeasterly from
and parallel with the northwesterly line of said lot 8, the northwesterly line
of which part of said Lot 9 is 25 feet northwesterly from and parallel with the
southeasterly line of said Lot 9 in Dale and Others Subdivision of Block 14 in
Winnetka Subdivision of Section 20, Township 42 North, Range 13, East of the
Third Principal Meridian in Cook County, Illinois,
commonly known as 562 Green Bay Road, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the C-
2 General Retail Commercial Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.44 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for a
variation from Section 17.44.010(K) to allow the existing language school to
use second floor space for classrooms without providing the required two on-
site parking spaces on the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, on March 11, 2002, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variation and by the
unanimous vote of the five members then present, has reported to the Council
recommending that the requested variation be granted; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and unique hardships associated
with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect
to the Subject Property in that: (a) the Subject Property is improved with a
two-story building that was constructed in 1927 as a dry cleaners and has been
remodeled numerous times and occupied by several different commercial tenants;
(b) the owner of the Subject Property operates the French Institute of the
North Shore on the ground floor; (c) the second floor is occupied by one
residential apartment unit, which the owner proposes to use as additional
classroom space; (d) the building on the Subject Property occupies a majority
of the lot area and no parking is currently provided on site; (e) the only open
space on the Subject Property is an area at the west side of the building that
measures 11.9 feet by 19.05 feet and is accessible via an alley behind the
building; (f) the open area at the rear of the building is partially occupied
by a spiral metal staircase that provides a second means of egress from the
second floor; which is necessary for emergency purposes; (g) the spiral
staircase renders the space at the rear of the building unusable for parking;
(h) no parking is currently required for the existing dwelling unit on the
second floor, which was in existence prior to the amendment to the Zoning
Ordinance that required two spaces per 1,000 square feet of commercial or
office floor area above the ground floor, or 2¼ spaces per dwelling unit; and
(i) pursuant to Section 17.44.010(K)10 of the Zoning Ordinance, required
parking may be provided off-site in order to consolidate the required parking
for more than one lot; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that
the building on the Subject Property was constructed before any off-street
parking requirements were enacted and on-site parking cannot be provided
without eliminating the exterior stairs; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because: (a) the requested variation does not affect the
appearance of the building; (b) the owner has represented that the expansion
into the second floor will allow more comfortable classroom sizes without
increasing staff or enrollment; and (c) the two-car parking requirement that
would be shifted off-site is equal to the amount of off-site parking now being
used by the current residents of the second floor who own two cars; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not impair an adequate supply of light
and air because the requested variation will not change the configuration of
the building in any way; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property because it does not change the existing
building in any way and the spiral staircase that provides emergency egress
from the second floor will remain in place; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not contribute to congestion on the
public streets, because the two parking spaces that will not be provided on
site are equivalent to the number of parking spaces currently used by the
tenants of the second floor who own two vehicles that are parked off site; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the requested variation will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village.
   WHEREAS, the requested variation is in harmony with the general purpose and
intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that it maintains the scale and
character of the existing neighborhood, it protects and respects the
justifiable reliance of existing residents, business people and taxpayers on
the continuation of existing, established land use patterns, and it otherwise
promotes the public health, safety, comfort, morals and welfare by making it
possible for the French Institute of the North Shore to continue to operate on
the Subject Property, which has been substantially improved by the owner.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2:  That a variation from the parking requirements of Section
17.44.010(K) of Chapter 17.44 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the
Winnetka Village Code, is hereby granted to the property commonly known as 562
Green Bay Road, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the C-2 General Retail
Commercial Zoning District, to allow the existing language school on the
Subject Property to use the second floor space for classrooms without providing
two on-site parking spaces on the Subject Property.
   SECTION 3: That the variation hereby granted is conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 16th day of April, 2002, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser and Woodbury
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    None
   Signed:
   s/s Michael F. Duhl
    Village President
Countersigned:
s/s Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: April 9, 2002
Posted: April 10, 2002
Passed and Approved: April 16, 2002
Posted: April 17, 2002
ORDINANCE NO. M-11-2002
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA, COOK, COUNTY, ILLINOIS FOR THE CROW ISLAND SCHOOL
(1112 Willow Road) AND FOR THE CROW ISLAND WOODS (1140 Willow Road)
   WHEREAS, the Board of Education of Winnetka Elementary School District No.
36 is the beneficial owner of the following described real estate which is
commonly known as Crow Island Elementary School, 1112 Willow Road, Winnetka,
Illinois (“Parcel 1”):
Parcel 1: That part of vacated Glendale Avenue and of the east 13.0 feet of Lot
37 to 47, inclusive, in Block 1 in Vernamo, being a subdivision of the
northwest quarter of the southwest quarter of Section 20, Township 42 North,
Range 13, East of the Third Principal Meridian, and the east quarter of
northeast quarter of the southeast quarter of Section 19, Township 42 North,
Range 13, East of the Third Principal Meridian, described as follows: beginning
at a point in the east line of said northwest quarter of the southwest quarter
of said Section 20, 222.33 ft. south of the northeast corner thereof, thence
south along said east line, 250.0 ft., thence west at right angles to said east
line 46.0 ft., thence north parallel to the east line of said northwest quarter
of the southwest quarter of said Section 20, 250.0 ft., thence east 46.0 ft.,
to the place of beginning, in Cook County, Illinois.
Also, Lots 48 to 70, both inclusive, and the east 17.0 ft. of vacated Glendale
Avenue, lying west of and adjoining Lots 61 to 70, both inclusive, in Alles’
Sunset Subdivision of the northeast quarter of the southwest quarter of Section
20, Township 42 North, Range 13, East of the Third Principal Meridian, in Cook
County, Illinois; and
   WHEREAS, the Winnetka Park District is the owner of the following described
real estate which is commonly known as Crow Island Woods, 1140 Willow Road,
Winnetka, Illinois (“Parcel 2”):
Parcel 2: Block 1 (excepting therefrom the east 13.0 feet of Lots 37 to 47
inclusive), and Blocks 2, 9 and 10 in Vernamo Subdivision of the northwest
quarter of the southwest quarter of Section 20, Township 42 North, Range 13,
East of the Third Principal Meridian, also the vacated public streets and
alleys or parts thereof in said Vernamo Subdivision, as recorded October 30,
1891, in book 54 of plats, page 13, as document number 1560706, to-wit: all of
vacated Mt. Pleasant street from the east line of Euclid Avenue (formerly
Larson St.) to the west line extended of Glendale Avenue (formerly Bjornson
St); all of vacated Berkeley Avenue (formerly Peterson St.) from the south line
extended of Willow Road to the previously vacated Sunset Road at the south end
of Block 9; the west 33 feet of vacated Glendale Avenue (excepting therefrom
that portion of Glendale Avenue lying adjacent to Lots 37 to 47 in Block 1);
and all of the vacated alleys lying within Blocks 1, 2 and 9 in the Village of
Winnetka, Cook County, Illinois., in the Village of Winnetka, Cook County,
Illinois,
   WHEREAS, Parcel 1 and Parcel 2 (collectively, the “Subject Property”) are
located in the R-2 Single-Family Residential District provided in Chapter 17.24
of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code; and,
   WHEREAS,   the Board of Education of Winnetka Elementary School District No.
36 is a body corporate and politic of the State of Illinois and operates a
public school known as the Crow Island Elementary School on Parcel 1, which is
improved with the Crow Island School building and accessory structures; and
   WHEREAS, the Winnetka Park District is a body corporate and politic of the
State of Illinois and operates and maintains a public park known as Crow Island
Woods on Parcel 2; and
   WHEREAS, the accessory structures at the Crow Island Elementary School
include several playground areas, including one that extends across the lot
line from Parcel 1 into Parcel 2; and
   WHEREAS, public schools are permitted as special uses in the R-2 Single-
Family Residential District, subject to the conditions and requirements
pertaining to special uses, as set forth in Section 17.56 of the Winnetka
Zoning Ordinance; and
   WHEREAS,   the Winnetka Park District and Board of Education of Winnetka
Elementary School District No. 36 (“applicants”) have filed a joint application
for a special use permit pursuant to Sections 17.24.010(B)2.b and Section
17.56.010 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, to allow the site plan for the
Subject Property to be revised as part of the upgrading of the playground areas
on the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, the applicants have also filed a joint application for the
following variations: (a) a variation from the front setback provisions
permitted by Section 17.24.010(H) of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, to allow a
front setback of 12.5 feet from the Willow Road property line of Parcel 2,
whereas a 50.0-foot setback is required, resulting in a variation of 37.5 feet
(75.00%); and (b) a variation from Section 17.24.010(I) of the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance to allow a 0.00-foot side yard along the west lot line of Parcel 1
and the east lot line of Parcel 2, whereas a 12-foot side yard is required,
resulting in a variation of 12 feet (100%), to permit play equipment to cross
the lot line between the Park District and School District property; said
variations being requested to allow the replacement and renovation of the
existing northwest and southwest playgrounds located on the Subject Property;
and
   WHEREAS, pursuant to due notice thereof, on November 15, 2001, the Design
Review Board issued favorable comment for the proposed renovation of the
northwest playground; and
   WHEREAS, on February 27, 2002, pursuant to due notice thereof, the eight
members of the Plan Commission then present voted unanimously to find the
special use permit application for playground renovations consistent with the
Winnetka Comprehensive Plan; and
   WHEREAS, pursuant to due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of Appeals held a
public hearing on March 11, 2002 to consider the special use permit and
variation requests for the northwest southwest playgrounds, and by the
unanimous vote of the five members then present, the Zoning Board of Appeals
recommended that the requested variations and special use permit be granted;
and
   WHEREAS, the proposed replacement of the playground equipment in the
northwest and southwest playgrounds on the Subject Property will benefit the
public health, safety, comfort, morals and general welfare of the Village, by
improving the safety, accessibility and appearance of the playgrounds by
installing new playground equipment, providing safe use zones and improving the
storm drainage on the Subject Property, all in accordance with applicable
requirements of the Americans with Disabilities Act, playground safety
standards and the Village Code; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed special use will not be substantially injurious to the
use and enjoyment of other property in the immediate vicinity, nor will it
substantially diminish or impair property values in the immediate vicinity, as
the playgrounds have been located on the Subject Property for nearly 50 years,
the renovation will neither increase the intensity of the existing use nor
alter its nature, and the renovation will improve both the functionality and
appearance of the park; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed special use will not impede the normal and orderly
development or improvement of other property in the immediate vicinity for uses
permitted under applicable zoning regulations, as the playgrounds are
established uses in their existing locations on the Subject Property, the use
of the Subject Property will not be changed, and the playground areas are not
in close proximity to any single-family residences; and
   WHEREAS, adequate measures have been taken to provide ingress and egress in
a manner that minimizes pedestrian and vehicular traffic congestion, in that
the existing pedestrian circulation routes through the park, points of access
to the Subject Property and parking will not be changed, and there will be no
changes to vehicular access; and
   WHEREAS, adequate parking and access roads serve the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, adequate utilities, drainage and other facilities necessary to the
operation of the special use exists or will be provided, with new storm drain
lines being installed to improve drainage for the playground area and make it
usable all year; and
   WHEREAS, the special use in all other respects conforms to the applicable
regulations of this and other Village ordinances and codes;
   WHEREAS, the plight of the applicants is unique in that the play equipment
is a necessary accessory to the principal uses of the Subject Property in
meeting the statutory obligations of the applicants to provide educational and
recreational facilities; and
   WHEREAS, the strict application of the front setback requirements create
practical difficulties and particular hardships for the applicants in that
placing the equipment in a conforming location: (a) would require the equipment
to be placed in existing open grass areas that are needed as open play areas;
(b) would divide the open play areas into two smaller areas; (c) would create a
potential safety hazard because children would need to cross through play
equipment play pits to reach new open play areas along Willow Road; (d) would
locate some of the equipment further away from the school; (e) would create
additional hazards by placing equipment closer to trees; and (f) would place
the equipment in a location that is more visible to neighbors; and
   WHEREAS, the variation, if granted, will not alter the essential character
of the locality, in that the school and park are established uses in the
neighborhood, and the proposed improvements will replace play equipment
currently located in the same area of the Subject Property, which is hidden by
vegetation and protected by fencing along Willow Road; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the applicable zoning
regulations, as the sole purpose of the property is to provide educational and
recreational facilities as required by law; and
   WHEREAS, an adequate supply of light and air to adjacent property will not
be impaired, as the tallest decks of the new equipment will be the same height
as the tallest decks of the existing equipment; and
   WHEREAS, the hazard from fire and other damages to the property will not be
increased, because the new equipment will be in an open site, and the new
wooden equipment and playground surface meet safety standards and will not
ignite as easily as the existing mulch playground surface could; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the taxable value of land and buildings
throughout the Village will diminish; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed renovation of the playground equipment will not
increase congestion in the public streets; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed playground equipment will not be detrimental to or
endanger the public health, safety, comfort, morals and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village, because the upgraded playgrounds will be in full
compliance with current safety standards and guidelines, will meet
accessibility requirements of the Americans with Disabilities Act, will provide
additional visual screening for the neighboring properties and will improve
stormwater drainage on the Subject Property.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain:
   SECTION 1: That the Village Council hereby adopts by reference the foregoing
recitals as its findings as if those recitals were fully set forth herein.
   SECTION 2: That, pursuant to Sections 17.24.010(B)2b and 17.56.010 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, a special use
permit is hereby granted with respect to the Subject Property, which is located
in the R-2 Single-Family Residential Zoning District and is commonly known as
the Crow Island Elementary School and the Crow Island Woods Park, to permit the
renovation of the Subject Property by installing new playground equipment, play
surfaces and drainage in the northwest and southwest playgrounds located on the
Subject Property, said work to be done in accordance with plans that
accompanied the application for special use permit, dated October 23, 2001.
   SECTION 3:   That the following variations are hereby granted to the Subject
Property, which is located in the R-2 Single-Family Residential District
provided in Chapter 17.24 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the
Winnetka Village Code, and which is commonly known as the Crow Island
Elementary School and the Crow Island Woods Park: (a) a variation from the
front setback provisions permitted by Section 17.24.010(H) of the Winnetka
Zoning Ordinance, to allow a front setback of 12.5 feet from the Willow Road
property line of Parcel 2, whereas a 50.0-foot setback is required, resulting
in a variation of 37.5 feet (75.00%); and (b) a variation from Section
17.24.010(I) of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance to allow a 0.00-foot side yard
along the west lot line of Parcel 1 and the east lot line of Parcel 2, whereas
a 12-foot side yard is required, resulting in a variation of 12 feet (100%), to
permit play equipment to cross the lot line between the Park District and
School District property; said variations being granted to allow the
replacement playground equipment and renovation of the existing northwest and
southwest playgrounds located on the Subject Property, said work to be done in
accordance with the plans that accompanied the application for variation, dated
October 23, 2001.
   SECTION 4: That the special use and variations hereby granted are subject to
the following conditions:
      A.   That the applicants obtain final design review approval of the plans
for the northwest and southwest playground areas, in accordance with procedures
established by the Village Code; and
      B.   That the applicants begin the proposed construction and
implementation of the playground improvements within twelve (12) months after
the effective date of this ordinance.
   SECTION 5: That, pursuant to Section 17.56.010(G) of the Zoning Ordinance,
all stipulations, conditions and restrictions set forth in this Ordinance as
part of the terms under which the special use is granted, may be modified or
revised from time to time by the Village Council following public notice and
hearing, using the same procedures set forth in the Zoning Ordinance for
processing the original special use application.
   SECTION 6: That this Ordinance shall be in full force and effect from and
after its passage, approval, and posting.
   ADOPTED this 16th day of April, 2002, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    None
   Signed:
   s/s Michael F. Duhl
    Village President
Countersigned:
s/s Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: April 9, 2002
Posted: April 10, 2002
Passed and Approved: April 16, 2002
Posted: April 17, 2002
ORDINANCE NO. M-12-2002
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A SPECIAL USE PERMIT TO ALLOW FOR CONSTRUCTION OF
BASEBALL/SOFTBALL DUGOUTS AT NEW TRIER HIGH SCHOOL’S DUKE CHILDS ATHLETIC FIELD
   WHEREAS, New Trier Township High School District No. 203 (“New Trier”) is
the owner of the following described real estate ("Subject Property"):
Lot 3 in Open Lands Subdivision, being a subdivision of the southeast quarter
of the northeast quarter, excepting therefrom the southerly 50 feet thereof and
the easterly 50 feet thereof, also excepting therefrom Lots 35 and 36 in Block
1 in the Resubdivision of Blocks 8 to 12, inclusive, in the Winnetka Manor
Addition, in Section 19, Township 42 North, Range 13 East of the Third
Principal Meridian in the Village of Winnetka, Cook County, Illinois,
which is commonly known as Duke Childs Field, 1321 Willow Road (northwest
corner of Willow and Hibbard Roads), Winnetka, Illinois, in the R-2 Single-
Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.24 of the Winnetka
Zoning Ordinance, Title 17, Winnetka Village Code; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property was originally owned by the Village of
Winnetka and was conveyed to New Trier Township High School District, subject
to recorded covenants that restrict the use of the Subject Property to that of
an athletic field and sports facility, with associated parking and a then-
present storage building, with all construction of improvements to be subject
to approval by the Village; and
   WHEREAS, New Trier has submitted a request for a special use permit under
Section 17.56 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, to permit the replacement and
expansion of four existing dugouts; and
   WHEREAS, on February 21, 2002, the Design Review Board considered New
Trier’s request and issued favorable comment on the project presented, subject
to the following recommendations: (a) that the applicant substitute a brick
material for the split-face concrete masonry units, due to the location’s
visibility as an entry gateway to the Village, and (b) that climbing vines be
planted and maintained to soften the appearance and visibility of the dugouts;
and
   WHEREAS, at its meeting of February 27, 2002, the eight members of the Plan
Commission then present unanimously found the proposed special use to be
consistent with the Comprehensive Plan and further recommended that special
attention be given to the height of the structures, the final color selections,
the roof material and color and the inclusion of ivy as wall covering because
of the significant visual impact of the dugouts; and
   WHEREAS, pursuant to due notice, the Zoning Board of Appeals conducted a
public hearing on March 11, 2002, and, by the favorable vote of four of the
five members then present, has recommended that the requested special use
permit be granted; and
   WHEREAS, public high schools are permitted as special uses in the R-2 Zoning
District pursuant to Section 17.24.010(B)2b of the Zoning Ordinance, and the
Council of the Village of Winnetka find and determine that the baseball field
and dugouts are necessary and reasonable facilities that are part of the total
educational operation of the New Trier Public High School; and
   WHEREAS, nonessential public uses are permitted as special uses in all
zoning districts, subject to the conditions and requirements pertaining to
special uses, as set forth in Section 17.56.010(B) of the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance, Title 17, Winnetka Village Code and the Council of the Village of
Winnetka find and determine that the baseball fields and dugouts also
constitute a nonessential public use as defined in Section 17.56.010(B) of the
Zoning Ordinance; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed changes to the dugouts are substantially similar to
changes previously approved for the Subject Property pursuant to Ordinance M-
510-97, except that the current application does not seek to add any new
dugouts; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed, improved special use will benefit the public health,
safety, comfort, morals and general welfare of the Village, by enhancing the
safe use of the athletic field by student athletes and by creating a more
attractive and easily maintained athletic field; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed, improved special use will not be substantially
injurious to the use and enjoyment of other property in the immediate vicinity,
nor will it substantially diminish or impair property values in the immediate
vicinity, as the existing baseball and softball diamonds are an established use
in the neighborhood and the proposed improvements will enhance the aesthetic
qualities of the existing facilities and allow for improved maintenance; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed special use will not impede the normal and orderly
development or improvement of other property in the immediate vicinity for uses
permitted under applicable zoning regulations, as the existing baseball and
softball diamonds are an established use in the neighborhood and the proposed
improvements are designed to enhance the use and appearance of the Subject
Property; and
   WHEREAS, adequate measures have been taken to provide ingress and egress in
a manner that minimizes pedestrian and vehicular traffic congestion in the
public ways, since the proposal does not change the nature or intensity of the
use of the athletic fields and thus will have no measurable effect on traffic
in the area; and
   WHEREAS, adequate parking, access roads, utilities, drainage and other
facilities necessary to the operation of the existing special use exist or will
be provided on the Subject Property, as the nature and intensity of the use of
the athletic fields will not be increased and the existing parking lot has
recently been paved and upgraded; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed special use consisting of the existing use of the
fields and the existing and proposed improvements, will continue to be in
conformity with the applicable regulations of the Zoning Ordinance and the
Winnetka Village Code;
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain:
   SECTION 1:   That the Village Council hereby adopts the foregoing recitals
as their findings.
   SECTION 2: That, pursuant to Sections 17.24.010(B)2b and 17.56.010 of the
Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, a special use permit
is hereby granted to the Subject Property, which is located in the R-2 Single-
Family Residential District, is commonly known as Duke Chills Field at the
northwest corner of Hibbard and Willow Roads, and is owned by the New Trier
Township High School District No. 203, to allow the replacement of four
existing dugouts, said work to be done in accordance with the revised site
plans, elevations and specifications submitted with the application for special
use, subject to the following terms and conditions:
      A.   That the height of the front of the dugouts, which face the ball
diamonds, shall be reduced to 10-feet, 4-inches at the front, and the height of
the back of the dugouts shall be reduced to 8-feet, 4-inches;
      B.   That the final landscaping plan shall be subject to approval by the
Village Council, with the Village Council reserving the right and authority to
determine whether and to what extent landscaping shall be provided around the
dugouts until the dugouts have been constructed; and
      C.   That any graphics or signage to be placed on the dugouts or the
Subject Property shall be subject to approval by the Village Council, following
review by and recommendation from the Design Review Board, with the Village
Council reserving the right to determine the consistency of any proposed
graphics and signage with both the Village’s Design Guidelines and the
restrictive covenants that apply to the Subject Property; and
      D.   That the proposed improvements, including landscaping, graphics and
signage, shall be completed within 12 months after the effective date of this
Ordinance
   SECTION 3: That, pursuant to Section 17.56.010(G) of the Zoning Ordinance,
all stipulations, conditions and restrictions set forth in this Ordinance as
part of the terms under which the special use is granted, may be modified or
revised from time to time by the Village Council following public notice and
hearing, using the same procedures set forth in the Zoning Ordinance for
processing the original special use application.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 16th day of April, 2002, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury
   NAYS:    Trustee Greenough
   ABSENT:    None
   Signed:
   s/s Michael F. Duhl
    Village President
Countersigned:
s/s Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: April 9, 2002
Posted: April 10, 2002
Passed and Approved: April 16, 2002
Posted: April 17, 2002
ORDINANCE NO. M-13-2002
AN ORDINANCE AUTHORIZING THE SALE OR OTHER DISPOSITION OF PERSONAL PROPERTY OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA
   WHEREAS, from time to time, the Winnetka Police Department comes into
possession of items of personal property that have been lost, found or stolen,
which personal property includes the “Bicycles” described in Exhibit A,
(“Personal Property”), attached to this ordinance; and
   WHEREAS, the Personal Property has been in the possession of the Winnetka
Police Department for more than 6 months, during which time the Chief of Police
of the Winnetka Police Department has made reasonable inquiry and efforts to
identify and notify the owners or other persons entitled to possession of the
Personal Property, but has been unsuccessful in those attempts; and
   WHEREAS, pursuant to Section 3 of the Law Enforcement Disposition of
Property Act, 765 ILCS 1030/3, the Chief of Police has transferred the custody
of the Personal Property to the Village of Winnetka; and
   WHEREAS, the Village of Winnetka owns computer equipment, some of which will
be retired as it is replaced during the fiscal year, April 1, 2002 through
March 31, 2003 (hereinafter referred to as “Retired Computer Equipment”), and
which the Finance Director has recommended be disposed of by methods that are
consistent with past disposal methods; and
   WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka have determined that the
Personal Property and Retired Computer Equipment described herein (hereinafter
referred to collectively as the “Surplus Property”) are no longer necessary or
useful to the Village of Winnetka, that the cost of storing said Surplus
Property exceeds its value to the Village of Winnetka, that it is not in the
best interests of the Village of Winnetka to retain said Surplus Property and
that the Surplus Property should be disposed of in the manner hereinafter
provided, pursuant to Section 11-76-4 of the Illinois Municipal Code (65 ILCS
5/11-76-4), so as to avoid incurring additional costs for storing said Surplus
Property.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain:
   SECTION 1 : Pursuant to Section 11-76-4 of the Illinois Municipal Code (65
ILCS 5/11-76-4), the Council of the Village of Winnetka find that the above-
described Surplus Property, consisting of the items of Personal Property shown
on the attached Exhibits A and the Retired Computer Equipment described in the
foregoing recitals, are not necessary or useful to the Village of Winnetka and
that the best interests of the Village of Winnetka will be served by the sale
or other disposition of such Surplus Property.
   SECTION 2 : The Council of the Village of Winnetka further find that, based
on prior experience in disposing of similar items of property, the cost of
conducting a public sale of the Surplus Property, including the costs of
advertising and publishing, and the costs of personnel needed to maintain
security and to conduct the public sale, exceeds the value of such items.
   SECTION 3: Pursuant to said Section 11-76-4, the Village Manager is hereby
authorized and directed to determine the value of and to dispose of the
Personal Property in any manner he deems reasonable under the circumstances,
with or without public notice.
   SECTION 4: Pursuant to said Section 11-76-4, the Director of Finance is
hereby authorized and directed to determine the value of and to dispose of
Retired Computer Equipment that is retired during the fiscal year April 1,
2002, through March 31, 2003, using disposal methods that are consistent with
past disposal practices.
   SECTION 5: This Ordinance shall be in full force and effect from and after
its passage, approval, and posting.
   ADOPTED this 16th day of April, 2002, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    None
   Signed:
   s/s Michael F. Duhl
    Village President
Countersigned:
s/s Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
