ORDINANCE NO. M-14-2002
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (1234 Westmoor)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
Lot 1 in Ballard-Senior Subdivision of part of the North 9.0 acres of the West
25.0 acres of the South half of the Southwest quarter of Section 17, Township
42 North, Range 13 East of the Third Principal Meridian, Cook County, Illinois,
commonly known as 1234 Westmoor, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
2 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.24 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for a
variation from the corner setback requirements of Section 17.24.010(H) to
permit a proposed new, 2-story residence to observe a corner setback of 14.90
feet from the edge of the access easement located along the east lot line,
rather than the required corner setback of 27.95 feet, resulting in a variation
of 13 feet (46.51%); and
   WHEREAS, on April 2, 2002, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variation and unanimously
recommended to the Council that the requested variation be granted; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and particular hardships
associated with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance
with respect to the Subject Property in that: (a) the Subject Property is an
interior lot lying along the south edge of Westmoor Road, east of Hibbard Road;
(b) the east 15 feet of the Subject Property is encumbered by a driveway
easement; (c) the driveway easement on the Subject Property adjoins a 15-foot
driveway easement on the property immediately to the east of the Subject
Property and the two easements combine to provide access to the two encumbered
properties and to two parcels directly south of them; (d) because the driveway
easement can serve more than four parcels, it is considered a private street
under the Zoning Ordinance; (e) both the existing building on the Subject
Property and the proposed replacement residence have direct access to Westmoor
Road from the north property line, rather than from the driveway easement; (f)
the property directly to the east of the Subject Property also has access
directly to Westmoor Road from its north lot line, rather than from the
driveway easement; and (g) only a portion of the driveway easement is improved
and the presence of mature trees preclude the widening of the paved area; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that
requiring the new residence to be set back 27.95 feet from the west edge of the
driveway easement will shift the building mass to the west and south areas of
the lot, will (a) alter the Subject Property’s appearance from Westmoor Road,
(b) reduce the available rear yard area, and (c) artificially create the
impression of a street or alley in the blockface along the south side of
Westmoor Road, all without providing any offsetting benefit to the public or
the adjoining property owners; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because the proposed new home has been designed so that its
footprint and location on the Subject Property are consistent with the home
that it will replace, and because the proposed new home will maintain the
existing access directly off of Westmoor Road; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not impair an adequate supply of light
and air because the proposed residence will conform with building height
limitations, will have a north front yard, west side yard and south rear yard
that exceed the requirements of the Zoning Ordinance and will have a setback
along the east lot line that will be greater than the current east setback; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property as the proposed new home will comply with
all applicable building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the requested variation will diminish the
taxable value of land and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable
value of the Subject Property may be increased because of the proposed new
residence; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed new residence will not contribute to congestion on the
public streets, as it will replace a single-family residence of comparable size
and the proposed new construction will continue to provide off-street parking;
and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that allowing the proposed new residence to
observe a reduced east corner setback will otherwise impair the public health,
safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the inhabitants of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation is in harmony with the general purpose and
intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that it maintains the existing
scale and appearance of the community and protects established trees and
landscaping by constructing a new residence that has been designed to have a
comparable footprint, location on the lot and point of access to Westmoor Road
that is comparable to those that currently exist on the Subject Property.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2:  That the Subject Property, commonly known as 1234 Westmoor Road,
is hereby granted a variation from the corner setback requirements of Section
17.24.010(H) to permit a proposed new, 2-story residence to observe a corner
setback of 14.90 feet from the edge of the access easement located along the
east lot line, rather than the required corner setback of 27.95 feet, resulting
in a variation of 13 feet (46.51%), all in accordance with the plans and
elevations submitted with the application for variation.
   SECTION 3: That the variation hereby granted is conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 21st day of May, 2002, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    None
   Signed:
   s/s Michael F. Duhl
    Village President
Countersigned:
s/s Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: Waived
Passed and Approved: May 21, 2002
Posted: May 22, 2002
ORDINANCE NO. M-15-2002
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (714 Park Lane)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
That part of Lot 2 in Resubdivision of Block 8 in Park Addition to Winnetka
Plat recorded January 11, 1889 as Document 1049627 in Book 32, Page 33, lying
East of a line at right angles to South line of said Lot 2 at a point 135.00
feet West of Southeast corner of said Lot, in Sections 16 and 17, Township 42
North, Range 13, East of the Third Principal Meridian, in Cook County,
Illinois.
commonly known as 714 Park Lane, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
2 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance, Title 17, Winnetka Village Code, for the following variations: (a) a
variation from the front setback requirements of Section 17.24.010(H) to allow
a proposed two-story addition at the front of the existing residence to observe
a front setback of 30 feet whereas a 50-foot setback is required, resulting in
a variation of 20 feet (40%); and (b) a variation from the garage door length
limitations of Section 17.24.010(P), to allow the north garage door to be 16
feet wide and to allow the total width of all garage doors to be 25 feet,
rather than the 9-foot width limitation for individual doors and the total door
width limitation of 18 feet or 50% of the front building line, resulting in
variations of 7 feet for both the individual door and the total door width
(77.3%); and
   WHEREAS, on April 8, 2002, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variations and, by the
affirmative vote of five of the members then present, has reported to the
Council recommending that the requested variation be granted; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and particular hardships
associated with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance
with respect to the Subject Property in that: (a) the Subject Property is an
irregularly-shaped lot, with a curving front lot line that follows the edge of
the partially improved right-of-way of Park Street; (b) due to the curving lot
line, the 135.00-foot long south lot line is slightly more than one half the
length of the north lot line, which measures 248.30 feet in length; (c) the
residence on the Subject Property is oriented to face the south lot line, with
access by way of a driveway that extends from the southeast corner of the
Subject Property to the garage, which is located in the southeast corner of the
residence; (d) because of the curve of the Park Street right-of-way, the 50-
foot setback line cuts through the southeast corner of the existing garage; and
(e) the southeast corner of the existing garage observes a front setback of
43.50 feet, which was permitted by variation, due to the presence of a steep
hill on the western end of the Subject Property, a restrictive covenant that
prevents new construction in the northern part of the Subject Property and the
presence of Sheridan Park immediately across the unimproved right-of-way; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that
the proposed garage addition is of modest size, is not as high as the addition
previously approved by the Council’s adoption of Ordinance M-2-2001, and
improves the utility and value of the property by allowing a third garage bay
and an in-home office, which are reasonable amenities found in other single-
family homes in the immediate vicinity; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because the size and location placement of the addition will
complement the style of the house and maintain its appearance and location
within the context of the immediate neighborhood; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not impair an adequate supply of
light and air because the proposed addition meets all height limitations of the
Zoning Ordinance and the encroachment into the unimproved right-of-way is in
the direction of open space consisting of the unimproved Park Street right-of-
way and Sheridan Park; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property, as it will comply with all applicable
building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the requested variations will diminish
the taxable value of land and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable
value of the Subject Property may be increased because of the proposed
improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed addition will not contribute to congestion on the
public streets, as the Subject Property will continue to be used for single-
family residential purposes and the additional garage bay will allow an
additional vehicle to be parked inside; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed addition will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village.
   WHEREAS, the requested variations are in harmony with the general purpose
and intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that they allow the renovation,
restoration and rehabilitation of a structurally sound existing building while
maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the community and protecting
established trees and landscaping.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : The foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the findings
of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth herein.
   SECTION 2:  The following variations are hereby granted to the Subject
Property, commonly known as 714 Park Lane, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in
the R-2 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in the Winnetka
Zoning Ordinance, Title 17, Winnetka Village Code: (a) a variation from the
front setback requirements of Section 17.24.010(H) to allow a proposed two-
story addition at the front of the existing residence to observe a front
setback of 30 feet whereas a 50-foot setback is required, resulting in a
variation of 20 feet (40%); and (b) a variation from the garage door length
limitations of Section 17.24.010(P), to allow the north garage door to be 16
feet wide and to allow the total width of all garage doors to be 25 feet,
rather than the 9-foot width limitation for individual doors and the total door
width limitation of 18 feet or 50% of the front building line, resulting in
variations of 7 feet for both the individual door and the total door width
(77.3%), all in accordance with the drawings submitted with the application for
variation.
   SECTION 3: That the variations hereby granted are conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 21st day of May, 2002, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    None
   Signed:
   s/s Michael F. Duhl
    Village President
Countersigned:
s/s Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: Waived
Passed and Approved: May 21, 2002
Posted: May 22, 2002
ORDINANCE NO. M-16-2002
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A SPECIAL USE PERMIT AND VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF
THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA, COOK, COUNTY, ILLINOIS FOR THE
CROW ISLAND SCHOOL (1112 Willow Road)
   WHEREAS, the Board of Education of Winnetka Elementary School District No.
36 is the beneficial owner of the following described real estate (the “Subject
Property”), which is commonly known as Crow Island Elementary School, 1112
Willow Road, Winnetka, Illinois:
That part of vacated Glendale Avenue and of the east 13.0 feet of Lot 37 to 47,
inclusive, in Block 1 in Vernamo, being a subdivision of the northwest quarter
of the southwest quarter of Section 20, Township 42 North, Range 13, East of
the Third Principal Meridian, and the east quarter of northeast quarter of the
southeast quarter of Section 19, Township 42 North, Range 13, East of the Third
Principal Meridian, described as follows: beginning at a point in the east line
of said northwest quarter of the southwest quarter of said Section 20, 222.33
ft. south of the northeast corner thereof, thence south along said east line,
250.0 ft., thence west at right angles to said east line 46.0 ft., thence north
parallel to the east line of said northwest quarter of the southwest quarter of
said Section 20, 250.0 ft., thence east 46.0 ft., to the place of beginning, in
Cook County, Illinois.
Also, Lots 48 to 70, both inclusive, and the east 17.0 ft. of vacated Glendale
Avenue, lying west of and adjoining Lots 61 to 70, both inclusive, in Alles’
Sunset Subdivision of the northeast quarter of the southwest quarter of Section
20, Township 42 North, Range 13, East of the Third Principal Meridian, in Cook
County, Illinois; and
WHEREAS, the Subject Property is located in the R-2 Single-Family Residential
District provided in Chapter 17.24 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17
of the Winnetka Village Code; and,
   WHEREAS,   the Board of Education of Winnetka Elementary School District No.
36 is a body corporate and politic of the State of Illinois and operates a
public school known as the Crow Island Elementary School, which is improved
with the Crow Island School building and accessory structures; and
   WHEREAS, the accessory structures at the Crow Island Elementary School
include several playground areas; and
   WHEREAS, public schools are permitted as special uses in the R-2 Single-
Family Residential District, subject to the conditions and requirements
pertaining to special uses, as set forth in Section 17.56 of the Winnetka
Zoning Ordinance; and
   WHEREAS, the Board of Education of Winnetka Elementary School District No.
36 (“applicant”) has filed an application for a special use permit pursuant to
Sections 17.24.010(B)2.b and Section 17.56.010 of the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance, to allow the site plan for the Subject Property to be revised as
part of the upgrading of the playground areas on the Subject Property,
including the playground area south of the school and along the east property
line (the “Playground”); and
   WHEREAS, the applicant has also filed an application for a variation from
the front setback provisions permitted by Section 17.24.010(H) of the Winnetka
Zoning Ordinance, to allow a front setback of 39.5 feet from the Glendale
Avenue property line, whereas a 50.0-foot setback is required, resulting in a
variation of 10.5 feet (21%), to allow the replacement and renovation of the
existing Playground; and
   WHEREAS, pursuant to due notice thereof, on April 18, 2002, the Design
Review Board issued favorable comment for the proposed renovation of the
Playground and its equipment; and
   WHEREAS, on February 27, 2002, pursuant to due notice thereof, the eight
members of the Plan Commission then present voted unanimously to find the
special use permit application for renovations to the Playground consistent
with the Winnetka Comprehensive Plan; and
   WHEREAS, pursuant to due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of Appeals held a
public hearing on May 13, 2002 to consider the special use permit and variation
request for the Playground renovation and, by the unanimous vote of the 5
members then present, has recommended that the requested variation and special
use permit be granted; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed replacement of the equipment in the Playground will
benefit the public health, safety, comfort, morals and general welfare of the
Village, by improving the safety, accessibility and appearance of the
playgrounds by installing new playground equipment, providing safe use zones
and improving the storm drainage on the Subject Property, all in accordance
with applicable requirements of the Americans with Disabilities Act, playground
safety standards and the Village Code; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed special use will not be substantially injurious to the
use and enjoyment of other property in the immediate vicinity, nor will it
substantially diminish or impair property values in the immediate vicinity, as
the Playground has been located on the Subject Property for nearly 50 years,
the Playground renovation will neither increase the intensity of the existing
use nor alter its nature, and the renovation will improve both the
functionality and appearance of the park; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed special use will not impede the normal and orderly
development or improvement of other property in the immediate vicinity for uses
permitted under applicable zoning regulations, as the Playground is an
established use in its existing location on the Subject Property, the use of
the Subject Property will not be changed, and the Playground area is not in
close proximity to any single-family residences; and
   WHEREAS, adequate measures have been taken to provide ingress and egress in
a manner that minimizes pedestrian and vehicular traffic congestion, in that
points of access to the Subject Property and parking will not be changed, and
there will be no changes to vehicular access; and
   WHEREAS, adequate parking and access roads serve the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, adequate utilities, drainage and other facilities necessary to the
operation of the special use exist or will be provided; and
   WHEREAS, the special use in all other respects conforms to the applicable
regulations of this and other Village ordinances and codes;
   WHEREAS, the plight of the applicant is unique in that the play equipment is
a necessary accessory to the principal uses of the Subject Property in meeting
the statutory obligations of the applicant to provide educational and
recreational facilities; and
   WHEREAS, the strict application of the front setback requirements create
practical difficulties and particular hardships for the applicant in that
placing the equipment in a conforming location: (a) would require the equipment
to be placed in existing open grass areas that are needed as open play areas
and (b) would require the south parking lot to be relocated; and
   WHEREAS, the variation, if granted, will not alter the essential character
of the locality, in that the school is an established use in the neighborhood,
and the proposed improvements will replace or upgrade play equipment currently
located in the Playground, which is also an established use in its existing
location; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the applicable zoning
regulations, as the sole purpose of the property is to provide educational and
recreational facilities as required by law; and
   WHEREAS, an adequate supply of light and air to adjacent property will not
be impaired, as the existing jungle gym will remain and a proposed fence
enclosure will not be as tall as the equipment; and
   WHEREAS, the hazard from fire and other damages to the property will not be
increased, because the equipment will remain in an open site, and the new
equipment and playground surface will meet safety standards; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the taxable value of land and buildings
throughout the Village will diminish; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed renovation of the Playground equipment will not
increase congestion in the public streets because the Playground is an existing
accessory to the Crow Island School, which is also an existing use; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed Playground equipment will not be detrimental to or
endanger the public health, safety, comfort, morals and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village, because the upgraded Playground will be in full
compliance with current safety standards and guidelines, and will meet
accessibility requirements of the Americans with Disabilities Act.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain:
   SECTION 1: That the Village Council hereby adopts by reference the foregoing
recitals as its findings as if those recitals were fully set forth herein.
   SECTION 2: That, pursuant to Sections 17.24.010(B)2b and 17.56.010 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, a special use
permit is hereby granted with respect to the Subject Property, which is located
in the R-2 Single-Family Residential Zoning District and is commonly known as
the Crow Island Elementary School, to permit the renovation of the Subject
Property by installing new playground equipment in the playground located south
of the School and along the east property line of the Subject Property (the
“Playground”), said work to be done in accordance with the plans, dated March
19, 2002, that accompanied the application for special use permit.
   SECTION 3: That a variation is hereby granted to the Subject Property, which
is located in the R-2 Single-Family Residential District provided in Chapter
17.24 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code,
and which is commonly known as the Crow Island Elementary School from the front
setback provisions permitted by Section 17.24.010(H) of the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance, to allow a front setback of 39.5 feet from the Glendale Avenue
property line, whereas a 50.0-foot setback is required, resulting in a
variation of 10.5 feet (21.00%) to allow the replacement playground equipment
and renovation of the existing playground located south of the school and along
the east property line of the Subject Property (the “Playground”), said work to
be done in accordance with the plans that accompanied the application for
variation, dated March 19, 2002.
   SECTION 4: That the special use and variations hereby granted are subject to
the following conditions:
      A.   That the applicants obtain final design review approval of the plans
for the Playground area, in accordance with procedures established by the
Village Code; and
      B.   That the applicants begin the proposed construction and
implementation of the Playground improvements within twelve (12) months after
the effective date of this ordinance.
   SECTION 5: That, pursuant to Section 17.56.010(G) of the Zoning Ordinance,
all stipulations, conditions and restrictions set forth in this Ordinance as
part of the terms under which the special use is granted, may be modified or
revised from time to time by the Village Council following public notice and
hearing, using the same procedures set forth in the Zoning Ordinance for
processing the original special use application.
   SECTION 6: That this Ordinance shall be in full force and effect from and
after its passage, approval, and posting.
   ADOPTED this 18th day of June, 2002, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    Trustee Greenough
   Signed:
   s/s Michael F. Duhl
    Village President
Countersigned:
s/s Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: June 4, 2002
Posted: June 5, 2002
Passed and Approved: June 18, 2002
Posted: June 19, 2002
ORDINANCE NO. M-17-2002
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (1468 Scott)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
Lot 1 in Block 29 in Chicago North Shore Land Company’s Subdivision in the
south half of the northeast quarter of Section 18, Township 42 North, Range 13,
East of the Third Principal Meridian, in Cook County, Illinois,
commonly known as 1468 Scott Avenue, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
5 Single Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.12 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for the
following variations: (a) a variation from the intensity of use of lot
provisions of Section 17.12.010(F) to allow a total roofed lot coverage of
1,816.09 square feet, which exceeds the maximum allowable roofed lot coverage
of 1,730.68 square feet, resulting in a variation of 35.35 square feet (4.94%);
(b) a variation from the maximum building size limitations of Section 17.12.010
(G) to allow the existing, legally nonconforming gross floor area to be
increased by 3.52 square feet to 3,467.89 square feet, which exceeds the
maximum allowable gross floor area of 2,796.08 square feet, resulting in a
variation of 698.81 square feet (25.24%), and (c) a variation from the front
setback requirements of Section 17.12.010(H), to allow a corner setback of 9.8
feet from the east property line along Greenwood Avenue, which is less than the
required setback of 11.42 feet, resulting in a variation of 1.62 feet (14.19%),
said variations being requested to permit the reconstruction of an existing
open front porch at the front entrance along Greenwood Avenue, the addition of
two bay windows on the first floor along Greenwood Avenue, the addition of two
gables on the second floor above the proposed bay windows, and the addition of
a shallow balcony on the second floor at the south end of the residence, all in
accordance with the plans and elevations submitted with the application for
variation; and
   WHEREAS, on May 13, 2002, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of Appeals
conducted a public hearing on the requested variations and by the unanimous
vote of the five members then present, has reported to the Council recommending
that the requested variations be granted; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and unique hardships associated
with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect
to the Subject Property in that: (a) the Subject Property is a legal,
nonconforming lot located at the southwest corner of Scott Street and Greenwood
Avenue; (b) the residence on the Subject Property was constructed before the
gross floor area limitations were first imposed in 1989; (c) the narrow lot
width and the corner setback requirement limit the buildable area of the
property; (d) although the Subject Property currently conforms with the corner
setback requirements along Greenwood Avenue, the existing porch extends into
the required setback; (e) the proposed reconstruction of the front porch will
maintain its existing size and location; (f) the small balcony proposed for the
south elevation consists primarily of adding a railing outside of French doors
that open in and the square footage it adds is negligible; and (g) the
nonconforming size and location of the existing building on a nonconforming lot
precludes any exterior improvements to the residence; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that
(a) the nonconforming size of the residence and the reduced buildable area
resulting from the narrow lot width and additional setback requirement along
Greenwood Avenue preclude any exterior modifications to the existing building,
(b) the proposed new porch will replace the existing, deteriorating front
porch; and (c) the proposed 0.10% increase in gross floor area and 1.88%
increase in roofed lot coverage resulting from the proposed modifications are
de minimis; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because the residence will not be substantially increased in
size, and the changes will improve its appearance on the streetscape; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not impair an adequate supply of
light and air because the addition of the balcony rail is negligible in size
and the proposed bay windows are located on the first floor and face Greenwood
Avenue; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property as they will comply with all applicable
building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not diminish the taxable value of
land and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed porch reconstruction and the additions of bay windows,
gables and a balcony rail will not contribute to congestion on the public
street, as off-street parking is already provided on the Subject Property,
which will continue to be used as a single-family residence and; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed building improvements will
otherwise impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village.
   WHEREAS, the requested variations are in harmony with the general purpose
and intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that they allow the renovation,
restoration and rehabilitation of a structurally sound existing building while
maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the community and protecting
established trees and landscaping.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2: The following variations are hereby granted to the Subject
Property, commonly known as 1468 Scott Avenue, Winnetka, Illinois, and located
in the R-5 Single Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.12
of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code: (a) a
variation from the intensity of use of lot provisions of Section 17.12.010(F)
to allow a total roofed lot coverage of 1,816.09 square feet, which exceeds the
maximum allowable roofed lot coverage of 1,730.68 square feet, resulting in a
variation of 35.35 square feet (4.94%); (b) a variation from the maximum
building size limitations of Section 17.12.010(G) to allow the existing,
legally nonconforming gross floor area to be increased by 3.52 square feet to
3,467.89 square feet, which exceeds the maximum allowable gross floor area of
2,796.08 square feet, resulting in a variation of 698.81 square feet (25.24%);
and (c) a variation from the front setback requirements of Section 17.12.010
(H), to allow a corner setback of 9.8 feet from the east property line along
Greenwood Avenue, which is less than the required setback of 11.42 feet,
resulting in a variation of 1.62 feet (14.19%); said variations being requested
to permit the reconstruction of an existing open front porch at the front
entrance along Greenwood Avenue, the addition of two bay windows on the first
floor along Greenwood Avenue, the addition of two gables on the second floor
above the proposed bay windows, and the addition of a shallow balcony on the
second floor at the south end of the residence, all in accordance with the
plans and elevations submitted with the application for variation.
   SECTION 3: That the variation hereby granted is conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 18th day of June, 2002, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Powell, Presser, and Woodbury
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    Trustee Greenough
   Signed:
   s/s Michael F. Duhl
    Village President
Countersigned:
s/s Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
