ORDINANCE NO. M-20-2002 (Zoning App Withdrawn)
ORDINANCE NO. M-21-2002
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (470 Chestnut)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
The south 40 feet of the north 80 feet of Winnetka Subdivision, lying west of
Chestnut Street, in the east ½ of the northeast ¼ of Section 20, Township 42
North, Range 13, East of the Third Principal Meridian, in Cook County,
Illinois;
commonly known as 470 Chestnut, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
5 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.12 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for a
variation from the building line articulation requirement of Section 17.30.090
(formerly 17.12.010(Q)) of the Zoning Ordinance, to allow a 16-foot, one-story
addition at the rear of the existing home to align with the existing building
walls, resulting in an unarticulated north building wall that is 52.9 feet
long, than providing 18 inches of articulation for the rear 22.9 feet of the
north building wall; and
   WHEREAS, on June 10, 2002, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variation and, by the
unanimous vote of the five members then present, has reported to the Council
recommending that the requested variation be granted; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and unique hardships associated
with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect
to the Subject Property in that: (a) the Subject Property is a narrow zoning
lot, having a legal, nonconforming width of 40 feet; (b) the existing residence
on the Subject Property was built in 1922, before the enactment of the original
Zoning Ordinance; (c) the proposed one-story addition is designed to align with
the existing north building wall; (d) the proposed addition will add a family
room, mud room and new rear entrance; (e) the alignment of the north wall is
necessary to align the new family room with the adjacent dining room; and (f)
due to the location of the rear of the existing north building line 7.14 feet
away from the north lot line, complying with the articulation requirement would
result in an 8.64-foot north side yard for the 16-foot addition, which would
exceed the 8.0-foot side yard that articulated portions of walls on new single-
family residences are required to observe, thereby imposing a more stringent
requirement on the Subject Property, although the Zoning Ordinance provides for
a less stringent articulation requirement for buildings constructed before
April 20, 1999; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that
strict application of the articulation requirement to the proposed addition
would result in a 9’10” wide family room that will not be fully integrated with
the exterior of the building and that could not be furnished for family room
use without conflicting with the internal flow of the residence; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because the one-story addition is located at the rear of the
existing residence and will not be visible from the street and the exterior
appearance of the addition, with its deck and the rear entrance to the house,
is typical of single-family residences; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not impair an adequate supply of light
and air because the proposed addition is only one story high and the expanded
footprint of the residence will be consistent with the footprints of the
residences on the neighboring properties; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property as it will comply with all applicable
building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed addition will not contribute to congestion on the
public streets, as the Subject Property will remain a single-family residential
property with off-street parking; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed addition will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village.
   WHEREAS, the requested variation is in harmony with the general purpose and
intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that it allows the renovation,
restoration and rehabilitation of a structurally sound existing building while
maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the community and protecting
established trees and landscaping.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2:  That there is hereby granted to the Subject Property, commonly
known as 470 Chestnut, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-5 Single-Family
Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.12 of the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, a variation from the building
line articulation requirement of Section 17.30.090 (formerly 17.12.010(Q)) of
the Zoning Ordinance, to allow a 16-foot, one-story addition at the rear of the
existing home to align with the existing building walls, resulting in an
unarticulated north building wall that is 52.9 feet long, rather than providing
18 inches of articulation for the rear 22.9 feet of the north building wall,
said addition to be in accordance with the plans and elevations submitted with
the application for variation dated March 14, 2002.
   SECTION 3: That the variation hereby granted is conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 16th day of July, 2002, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, and Presser
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    Trustee Woodbury
   Signed:
   s/s Michael F. Duhl
    Village President
Countersigned:
s/s Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: July 2, 2002
Posted: July 3, 2002
Passed and Approved: July 16, 2002
Posted: July 17, 2002
ORDINANCE NO. M-22-2002
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A SPECIAL USE PERMIT FOR THE CONSTRUCTION OF AN
ADMINISTRATION BUILDING FOR THE WINNETKA PARK DISTRICT (540 Hibbard)
   WHEREAS, the Winnetka Park District is the owner of the following described
real estate (the “Subject Property”):
The north ½ of the east ½ of the northeast ¼ of Section 19 (except the easterly
33 feet thereof), in Township 42 North, Range 13, East of the Third Principle
Meridian in the Village of Winnetka, Cook County, Illinois;
commonly known as 540 Hibbard, Winnetka, Illinois, which is located in the R-
2 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code; and
   WHEREAS, on December 7, 1999, the Council adopted Ordinance M-596-99,
granting the Winnetka Park District a temporary special use permit to allow
modular office units to be placed in approximately the same location on the
Subject Property to provide temporary office space pending the development of
plans for permanent office space; and
   WHEREAS, the Winnetka Park District has now applied for a special use permit
under Section 17.56.010 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance to allow the
construction of a permanent one-story addition for administrative offices along
the north side of the existing A. C. Nielsen Tennis Center building on the
Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, on June 10, 2002, pursuant to due notice thereof, the Zoning Board
of Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested special use and, by the
unanimous vote of the five members of the Board, has reported to the Council,
recommending that the requested special use be granted; and
   WHEREAS, on April 18, 2002, on due notice thereof, the Design Review Board
considered the proposed building plans and issued favorable comment on the
proposed design, but also recommended that the Park District review the use of
split face masonry units at the base of the north building elevation and
consider using a higher quality material; and
   WHEREAS, on May 16, 2002, one due notice thereof, the Design Review Board
considered the landscaping and lighting plans for the proposed building
addition project and again issued favorable comment on the proposed design, but
also suggested that the Park District consider eliminating a narrow strip of
grass in favor of expanding a planting bed up to the sidewalk; and
   WHEREAS, on May 22, 2002, pursuant to due notice thereof, the Plan
Commission considered the special use application and, by the unanimous vote of
the nine members then present, found that the proposed special use is
consistent with the Comprehensive Plan, Winnetka 2020; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed special use is consistent with the Comprehensive
Plan’s goal to preserve or expand the quantity, quality and distribution of
open space and recreational opportunities, and to protect the Village’s natural
features and environmental resources; and
   WHEREAS, the Council accept the findings of the Winnetka Plan Commission
that the proposed special use is consistent with the Winnetka Comprehensive
Plan’s Village Character and Appearance Objectives 6, 7, 11 and 13; and
   WHEREAS, the Council accept the findings of the Winnetka Plan Commission
that the proposed special use is consistent with the Winnetka Comprehensive
Plan’s Village Parks, Open Space, Recreation and Environment Objects 5, 6 and
9; and
   WHEREAS, the Council accept the findings of the Winnetka Plan Commission
that the proposed special use is consistent with the recommendation stated in
Section 4.3.6 of the Comprehensive Plan to ensure proposals do not have an
adverse impact on the residential character of the surrounding residential
neighborhoods; and
   WHEREAS, the Council accept the findings of the Winnetka Plan Commission
that the proposed special use is consistent with the recommendation stated in
Section 4.3.6 of the Comprehensive Plan to cooperate with the Winnetka Park
District in achieving the District’s goal of providing Village residents with
high quality recreational programs and open space; and
   WHEREAS, the Council accept the findings of the Winnetka Plan Commission
that the proposed special use is consistent with the recommendation stated in
Section 4.3.6 of the Comprehensive Plan to work with the Park District to
minimize the impact of existing programs on adjacent neighborhoods; and
   WHEREAS, the Council accept the findings of the Winnetka Plan Commission
that the proposed special use is consistent with the recommendation stated in
Section 4.3.6 of the Comprehensive Plan to coordinate planning for any new
facilities and programs to balance recreational needs of the community with the
residential character of the surrounding neighborhood; and
   WHEREAS, the Council accept the findings of the Winnetka Plan Commission
that the proposed special use is consistent with the recommendation stated in
Section 4.3.6 of the Comprehensive Plan to ensure that street and parking
infrastructure are adequate and that other ancillary effects such as artificial
lighting, noise and water run-off are held to acceptable levels; and
   WHEREAS, the Council find that the existing Winnetka Park District
facilities and operations on the Subject Property, as well as the proposed
administrative office addition, constitute a non-essential public use under
Section 17.56.010(B) of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed administrative office addition is necessary to the
operation of the Winnetka Park District and the provision of services to
Winnetka residents in that the addition will serve as administrative offices
for employees who were previously at Skokie School and are currently housed in
the temporary, modular units permitted pursuant to Ordinance M-596-99; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed administrative office addition will not be detrimental
to the public health, safety, comfort, morals or general welfare of the
Village, in that they will enable the Winnetka Park District to provide
convenient, attractive and safe administrative offices on Park District
property; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed administrative office addition will not be
substantially injurious to the use and enjoyment of other property in the
immediate vicinity, nor will it substantially diminish or impair property
values in the immediate vicinity, as the proposed addition will not alter the
existing use of the Subject Property and will be located along the north side
of the A. C. Nielsen Tennis Center, set well back from Hibbard Road and located
approximately 345 feet away from the nearest residential property line; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed administrative office addition will not impede the
normal and orderly development or improvement of other property in the
immediate vicinity for uses permitted under applicable zoning regulations in
that the size of the addition is relatively small in comparison with the
established Park District facilities on the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed administrative office addition will not alter the
amount of parking on or the ingress or egress to the Subject Property, nor will
it have a negative impact to vehicular or pedestrian traffic on the surrounding
street system, as the employees will continue to use the existing parking lot
and the number of employees is substantially less than the number of people who
use the recreational facilities on the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, adequate parking, utilities, access roads, drainage and other
facilities necessary to the existing special use already exist on the Subject
Property, and the proposed office addition will not require any additions to
such facilities, as they will be attached to the A. C. Nielsen Tennis Center;
and
   WHEREAS, the special use in all other respects conforms to the applicable
regulations of this and other Village ordinances and codes.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2:  That, pursuant to Section 17.56.010 of the Zoning Ordinance,
Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, a special use permit is granted with
respect to the Subject Property, which is located in the R-2 Single-Family
Residential Zoning District provided in said Zoning Ordinance, and is commonly
known as 540 Hibbard Road, to permit the Winnetka Park District to construct a
one-story addition to the north side of the A. C. Nielsen Tennis Center
building, thereby providing a permanent location for the administrative offices
of the Winnetka Park District, all in accordance with the plans and elevations
submitted with the application for special use permit.
   SECTION 3: That the special use permit is hereby granted subject to the
following conditions: (a) that the mobile offices be removed upon completion
and occupancy of the new permanent administrative offices, and (b) that the
proposed construction commence within 12 months after the effective date of
this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: That, pursuant to Section 17.56.010(G) of the Zoning Ordinance,
all stipulations, conditions and restrictions set forth in this Ordinance as
part of the terms under which the special use is granted, may be modified or
revised from time to time by the Village Council following public notice and
hearing, using the same procedures set forth in the Zoning Ordinance or
processing the original special use application.
   SECTION 5: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 16th day of July, 2002, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, and Presser
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    Trustee Woodbury
   Signed:
   s/s Michael F. Duhl
    Village President
Countersigned:
s/s Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
