ORDINANCE NO. M-23-2002
AN ORDINANCE ESTABLISHING 411 LINDEN AVENUE AS A DESIGNATED LANDMARK
   WHEREAS, the Village of Winnetka desires to identify, designate and preserve
buildings and structures in the Village that are historically, culturally, and
architecturally significant and to encourage the restoration and rehabilitation
of those buildings and structures; and
   WHEREAS, pursuant to Sections 11-48.2-1, et seq. of the Illinois Municipal
Code (65 ILCS 5/11-48.2-1, et seq.), the Council of the Village of Winnetka
enacted Chapter 15.64 of Title 15 of the Winnetka Village Code, entitled
“Landmark Preservation,” for the purposes of preserving, protecting, enhancing,
rehabilitating and regulating buildings, structures, objects, and places of
historical, cultural or architectural importance; and
   WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka have determined that it is
in the best interests of the Village and its residents that participation in
the landmark designation process shall be limited to those owners who
voluntarily elect to participate in that process; and
   WHEREAS, pursuant to the provisions of Chapter 15.64 of the Winnetka Village
Code (the “Landmark Ordinance”) and notice duly published and sent, the
Landmark Preservation Commission conducted a public hearing on June 3, 2002, to
consider the application of the Winnetka Historical Society for landmark
designation of the property located at 411 Linden Avenue (permanent real estate
index number 5-20-223-015-0000); and
   WHEREAS, the Landmark Preservation Commission applied the Village’s System
for the Evaluation of Landmarks to the information received at the hearing and
made findings regarding the property at 411 Linden Avenue, including the
following:
      (1)   the residence was constructed in 1858-59, making it one of the few
remaining pre-Civil War structures extant in the Village of Winnetka; and
      (2)   the Subject Property was originally subdivided by Charles E. Peck,
who is known as the “founder of Winnetka,” and whose wife, Sarah, is credited
with naming the Village of Winnetka; and
      (3)   Charles E. Peck originally owned the Subject Property in
partnership with Walter S. Gurnee, president of what was then the new Chicago
and Milwaukee Railroad; and
      (4)   photographs of the residence from the late-19th century establish
that the exterior of the residence is largely unaltered, with the exception of
a 2 ½-story tower that burned and was subsequently demolished, and the
extension of a bay window; and
      (5)   the condition of the house still boasts such details as original
exterior clapboard, the original front door, original window mullions and
treatment, original carpenter Gothic under eave detailing and other unique
architectural details; and
      (6)   the structure possesses numerous construction techniques that
ceased to be used long ago, such as continuous wall framing extending between
the basement and the second floor, and thus provides an insight into the
earliest framing techniques; and
      (7)   in addition to being associated with Charles Peck and Walter S.
Gurnee, the property is associated with the Solymon Densmore family, whose
family played an integral to the history of Winnetka, having built the “Fanning
Mill” factory on the southeast corner of Cherry and Linden Streets, and whose
daughter, Fidella Densmore, was a well known opera singer in the late 19th
century; and
      (8)   the Subject Property is a significant and identifiable structure
within its immediate neighborhood with a secondary identification to the
Village as a result of its ownership by the Winnetka Historical Society; and
      (9)   original materials, details, exterior ornament and interior
finishes that appear in photographs from the late 19th century still remain in
the structure; and
      (10)   the Illinois Historic Preservation Agency has informed the
Winnetka Historical Society that the property is considered eligible for
listing on the National Register of Historic Places; and
   WHEREAS, based on its findings, the Landmark Preservation Commission has
concluded that the residence located at 411 Linden Avenue is unique and meets
the criteria of the Landmark Ordinance and has unanimously recommended that it
be so designated; and
   WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka have considered the
recommendation of the Landmark Preservation Commission and have determined that
it is in the best interest of the Village and its residents to establish the
house located at 411 Linden Avenue as a designated landmark.
   NOW THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain:
   SECTION 1: That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2: That the property located at 411 Linden Avenue is hereby
designated a landmark under the Landmark Ordinance, in accordance with the
findings and recommendations of the Landmark Preservation Commission.
   SECTION 3: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 16th day of July, 2002, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, and Presser
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    Trustee Woodbury
   Signed:
   s/s Michael F. Duhl
    Village President
Countersigned:
s/s Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
