ORDINANCE NO. M-29-2002
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (500 Maple)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
The east 160 feet (as measured along the north and south line thereof) of Lot 5
in Prouty Homestead Subdivision of all of Blocks 24 and 25, lying northeasterly
of the right of way of the Chicago and Milwaukee Electric Railroad (except the
north 211 feet of said Block 24) also the west 3 feet of the north 211 feet of
said Block 24, all in Winnetka, a Subdivision by Charles E. Peck of the
northeast ¼ of Section 20, and fractional north ½ of fractional Section 21, all
in Township 42 North, Range 13 East of the Third Principal Meridian, in Cook
County, Illinois,
commonly known as 500 Maple Street, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
4 Single-Family Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.16 of the Winnetka
Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for the following
variations from the yard and setback requirements of Chapter 17.30 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, all for the purpose of constructing a detached
garage on the Subject Property: (a) a variation from the corner setback
requirements of Section 17.30.050B to allow the proposed detached garage to be
located 17.58 feet from the south (Oak Street) lot line, instead of the
required minimum 30-foot corner setback, resulting in a variation of 12.42 feet
(41.4%); b) a variation from the required yard and setback requirements for
garages set forth in Section 17.30.110D and Section 17.30.110E, to allow the
proposed garage to be located 17.58 feet from the south (Oak Street) property
line, which is less than the minimum allowable setback of 20 feet, resulting in
a variation of 2.24 feet (12.1%); and
   WHEREAS, on August 12, 2002, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variation and by the
unanimous vote of the six members then present, has reported to the Council
recommending that the requested variation be granted; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and unique hardships associated
with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect
to the Subject Property in that: (a) the Subject Property is a corner lot
located at the northwest corner of Maple and Oak Streets; (b) the Subject
Property is 14 feet higher at the rear property line than at the front (Maple
Street) lot line, with a rear yard that slopes steeply toward the house; (c)
because of the steep slope in the rear yard, constructing a new garage in a
conforming location would require placing the garage close to the center of the
yard, which would exacerbate storm drainage and flooding problems in the
principal building, which are caused, in part, by the location of the existing
garage at the center of the property directly behind the principal building;
(d) because of the steep slope in the rear yard, locating the new garage in a
conforming location near the northwest corner of the Subject Property would
make the garage significantly higher than the existing residence and would make
it more visible to the adjoining properties; (e) constructing the garage in a
conforming location at the northwest corner of the Subject Property would also
require the removal of several large trees along the west lot line and would
increase the amount of impermeable surface on the Subject Property, further
exacerbating stormwater runoff problems; (f) constructing the garage in the
location of the existing garage would place it too close to the principal
building to maintain adequate fire distances; and (g) constructing the garage
in the location of the existing garage would continue to necessitate backing
out of the garage and onto Oak Street, which is a busy street with limited
sight lines due to the steep slope from west to east; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that:
(a) the existing one-car garage is located 30 inches behind the house, blocking
light and air to several principal rooms on the ground floor and causing water
damage to the residence by directing runoff towards the house, (b) the existing
one-car garage is located in an area that limits the utility of the rear yard,
and (c) the size, location and deteriorating condition of the existing one-car
garage are not consistent with typical garages in the vicinity, which are
generally two-car garages that are located so as to increase useable open space
in the rear yard and to allow vehicles to pull out into the street in a forward
position, and (d) the proposed location of the new garage will reduce its
visibility from the street and from adjoining properties, will increase open
space in the rear yard and will provide sufficient room to exit the garage,
make a three point turn and pull out into Oak Street in a forward position; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because the proposed garage is proposed to be designed and
located on the Subject Property so as to appear to be an out-building, which
will make it compatible with the character of the Subject Property, while
minimizing its visibility from the street and preserving several established
trees on the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not impair an adequate supply of
light and air because the proposed location of the new garage will eliminate
the current obstruction of light and air to the first floor of the residence on
the Subject Property and the new garage will be located lower than the nearest
single family residence, on the adjoining property to the west; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property, as the proposed garage will comply with
all applicable building and fire protection codes, the distance between the
garage and the residence on the Subject Property will be increased, and the
distances between the garage and residences on adjoining properties will be
greater than required minimums; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not diminish the taxable value of
land and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed garage; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed construction will not contribute to congestion on the
public streets, as the proposed garage and turnaround will increase the amount
of off-street parking, and will turn the garage doors 90 degrees from the Oak
Street frontage, providing room to exit the garage, make a three point turn and
pull out onto Oak Street in a forward position; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed garage will otherwise impair
the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the inhabitants of
the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations are in harmony with the general purpose
and intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that it allows the replacement
of a structurally deteriorating accessory building while maintaining the
existing scale and appearance of the community and protecting established trees
and landscaping.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2: That the following variations from the yard and setback
requirements of Chapter 17.30 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance are hereby
granted to the Subject Property, commonly known as 500 Maple Street, Winnetka,
Illinois, and located in the R-4 Single-Family Zoning District provided in
Chapter 17.16 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka
Village Code, all for the purpose of constructing a detached garage and
turnaround on the Subject Property in accordance with the plans and elevations
submitted with the application for variations: (a) a variation from the corner
setback requirements of Section 17.30.050B to allow the proposed detached
garage to be located 17.58 feet from the south (Oak Street) lot line, instead
of the required minimum 30-foot corner setback, resulting in a variation of
12.42 feet (41.4%); b) a variation from the required yard and setback
requirements for garages set forth in Section 17.30.110D and Section
17.30.110E, to allow the proposed garage to be located 17.58 feet from the
south (Oak Street) property line, along Oak Street, which is less than the
minimum allowable setback of 20 feet, resulting in a variation of 2.24 feet
(12.1%).
   SECTION 3: That the variations hereby granted are conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 1st day of October, 2002, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser and Woodbury
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    Trustee Aquilino
   APPROVED this 1st day of October, 2002.
   Signed:
   /s/Michael F. Duhl
   Village President
Countersigned:
/s/Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: September 17, 2002
Posted: September 18, 2002
Passed and Approved: October 1, 2002
Posted: October 2, 2002
ORDINANCE NO. M-30-2002
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (1310 Scott Avenue)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
The east ½ of Lot 5 in Block 13 in Lloyd’s Subdivision of Block 1 to 5 in
Taylor’s Second Addition to Taylorsport, being a Subdivision of part of the
northeast ¼ of Section 18, and part of the northwest ¼ of Section 17, also the
east 24 acres of the north 48 acres of the northeast ¼ of Section 18, Township
42 North, Range 13 East of the Third Principal Meridian, in Cook County,
Illinois,
commonly known as 1310 Scott Avenue, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
5 Single Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.12 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for a
variation from the maximum building size (gross floor area) limitations of
Section 17.30.040B for the purpose of constructing a 42-square foot mudroom
addition to the residence on the Subject Property, which will increase the
legally nonconforming gross floor area to 5,325.46 square feet, which exceeds
the maximum of 3,030.50 square feet, resulting in a variation of 2,294.96
square feet (75.73%); and
   WHEREAS, on August 12, 2002, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variation and by the
unanimous vote of the six members then present, has reported to the Council
recommending that the requested variation be granted; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and unique hardships associated
with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect
to the Subject Property in that (a) due to amendments to the Zoning Ordinance
since the residence on the Subject Property was constructed in 1996, the gross
floor area of the attic is now included in determining the gross floor area of
the Subject Property; (b) due to amendments to the Zoning Ordinance since the
residence on the Subject Property was constructed in 1996, the gross floor area
of the basement is now included in determining the gross floor area of the
Subject Property because the sloping lot increases the amount of basement that
is above grade; (c) the 42-square foot mudroom could have been constructed in
1996 without a variation, because the gross floor area was below the then
applicable limit; and (d) the size of the requested variation is de minimis; as
it amounts to less than 0.8% of the total gross floor area on the Subject
Property and constitutes less than 1.4% of the allowable gross floor area; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because the mudroom is located at the rear of the residence in
an area that is now part of a rear deck; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not impair an adequate supply of light
and air because: the proposed mudroom is a single story, measures 6 feet by 7
feet, and is more than 30 feet away from the property to the east and 120 feet
from the property to the west; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property as it will comply with all applicable
building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed construction will not contribute to congestion on the
public streets, as the use and capacity of the residence will not be increased;
and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed construction will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation is in harmony with the general purpose and
intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that it allows the renovation of a
structurally sound existing building while maintaining the existing scale and
appearance of the community and protecting established trees and landscaping.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2:  That the Subject Property, commonly known as 1310 Scott Avenue,
Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-5 Single Family Residential Zoning
District provided in Chapter 17.12 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17
of the Winnetka Village Code, is hereby granted a variation from the maximum
building size (gross floor area) limitations of Section 17.30.040B for the
purpose of constructing a 42-square foot mudroom addition to the residence on
the Subject Property, which will increase the legally nonconforming gross floor
area to 5,325.46 square feet, which exceeds the maximum of 3,030.50 square
feet, resulting in a variation of 2,294.96 square feet (75.73%), said mudroom
to be constructed in accordance with the plans and elevations submitted with
the application for variation.
   SECTION 3: That the variation hereby granted is conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 15th day of October, 2002, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Aquilino, Greenough, Powell and Presser
   NAYS:    Trustees Brower and Woodbury
   ABSENT:    President Duhl
   APPROVED this 15th day of October, 2002.
   Signed:
   /s/Barbara Aquilino
   Village President Pro Tem
Countersigned:
/s/Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: September 17, 2002
Posted: September 18, 2002
Passed and Approved: October 15, 2002
Posted: October 16, 2002
ORDINANCE NO. M-31-2002
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (1274 Tower Road)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
Lot 1 and the east ½ of Lot 2 (except the west 10 feet and except the south 130
feet of said Lots 1 and 2) in Bell’s Subdivision of the north ½ of the
northeast ¼ of the southeast ¼ of Section 18, Township 42 North, Range 13 East
of the Third Principal Meridian, in Cook County, Illinois,
commonly known as 1274 Tower Road, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
2 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.24 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for a
variation from the front setback requirements of Section 17.30.050A to allow a
proposed one-story family room and mudroom addition at the southeast corner of
the existing residence to be located 13.47 from the east (Hibbard Road) lot
line, which is less than the required 50-foot front setback, resulting in a
variation of 36.53 feet (73.06%); and
   WHEREAS, on August 12, 2002 on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variation and by the
affirmative vote of five of the six members then present, has reported to the
Council recommending that the requested variation be granted; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and particular hardships
associated with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance
with respect to the Subject Property in that: (a) the Subject Property is
located at the southwest corner of Hibbard Road and Road and is required to
observe 50-foot setbacks along both street frontages; (b) the residence on the
Subject Property was constructed in 1911, before the Zoning Ordinance was first
enacted; (c) the house is situated in the east area of the Subject Property and
is surrounded by established, mature trees; (d) the proposed addition will
align with the existing east building line; and (e) there is no viable
conforming location for the proposed family room and mudroom addition because
of the interior layout of the house, the topography of the lot and the
existence of mature trees; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that a
family room and mudroom are typical features of contemporary residences in the
Village and the family room and mudroom are typically located adjacent to the
kitchen and dining areas; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because the addition will align with the existing east wall,
will be one-story high, and will be shielded from view by the existing trees
and wall along the east property line; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not impair an adequate supply of light
and air because: (a) the proposed addition will be lower than applicable height
limits; (b) the proposed addition will be shielded from view by the existing
trees and wall along the east property line; and (c) the residence opposite the
proposed addition is located on the east side of Hibbard Road and does not fall
within the shadow lines of the proposed addition; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property as it will comply with all applicable
building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed addition will not contribute to congestion on the
public streets, as it will not alter the use or capacity of the existing
residence; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed construction will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation is in harmony with the general purpose and
intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that it allows the renovation,
restoration and rehabilitation of a structurally sound existing building while
maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the community and protecting
established trees and landscaping.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2: That the Subject Property, commonly known as 1274 Tower Road,
Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-2 Single-Family Residential Zoning
District provided in Chapter 17.24 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17
of the Winnetka Village Code, is hereby granted a variation from the front
setback requirements of Section 17.30.050A to allow a proposed one-story family
room and mudroom addition at the southeast corner of the existing residence to
be located 13.47 from the east (Hibbard Road) lot line, which is less than the
required 50-foot front setback, resulting in a variation of 36.53 feet
(73.06%), said addition to be construction in accordance with the plans and
elevations submitted with the application for variation.
   SECTION 3: That the variation hereby granted is conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 1st day of October, 2002, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser and Woodbury
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    Trustee Aquilino
