ORDINANCE NO. M-32-2002
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (1230 Westmoor)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
Lot 4 in Ballard-Senior Subdivision of part of the North 9.00 acres of the West
25.00 acres of the South half of the Southwest quarter of Section 17, Township
42 North, Range 13 East of the Third Principal Meridian, in Cook County,
Illinois
commonly known as 1230 Westmoor, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
2 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.24 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for the
following variations: (a) variations from the front setback requirements of
Section 17.30.050A of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, to allow an open front
porch to observe a front setback of 23 feet, whereas a front setback of 50 feet
is required, resulting in a variation of 27 feet (54%), and to allow an
enclosed one and two-story addition at the northeast corner of the existing
residence to observe a front setback of 44 feet, whereas a front setback of 50
feet is required, resulting in a variation of 6 feet (12%); (b) a variation
from Section 17.30.060A of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, to allow a 1½-story
addition at the rear of the existing residence to observe a west side yard
setback of 11.5 feet, whereas a side yard setback of 20.64 feet is required to
comply with the total side yard requirement of 32.84 feet, resulting in a
variation of 9.14 feet (44.28%); and (c) a variation from Section 17.30.110A to
allow a front-facing garage door to be 13 feet wide, which exceeds the 9-ffot
maximum, resulting in a variation of 4 feet; and
   WHEREAS, the on March 12, 2001, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variation and by the
unanimous vote of the five members then present, has reported to the Council
recommending that the requested variation be granted; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations are identical to the variations previously
granted to the Subject Property pursuant to Ordinance M-12-2001, which was
adopted by the Village Council on May 1, 2001; and
   WHEREAS, the owners of the Subject Property have established that they were
unable to commence the work authorized by Ordinance M-12-2001 within 12 months
after the effective date of that ordinance; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and unique hardships associated
with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect
to the Subject Property in that: (a) the Subject Property is an irregularly
shaped interior lot that has no street frontages and is accessed by a private
easement that extends from the south line of Westmoor Road through the
properties directly to the north of the Subject Property; (b) the northwest
corner of the Subject Property is encumbered by a rectangular private road
easement; (c) the north lot line of the Subject Property is considered the
front lot line, from which the 50-foot front yard setback must be measured; (d)
the 50-foot front yard setback is also measured from the ends of the private
road easement; (e) due to the private road easement at the northwest corner and
the interior corner of the inverted L-shape of the Subject Property, the
buildable area of the Subject Property has an irregular shape; (f) the one-
story brick residence on the Subject Property was constructed in 1951, before
the existing zoning classification, with its increased front and side yard
setbacks, was established; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that:
(a) the additional bedrooms, the creation of a first floor master suite, the
extension of the family room and the construction of a new front porch are
modest improvements to the existing residence, which was originally constructed
as a one-bedroom home; (b) due to the existing configuration of the residence,
the proposed additions cannot be built in a conforming location; and (c)
bringing the residence into full conformity with the Zoning Ordinance would
require that it be rebuilt in its entirety; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because (a) the additions will result in a residence that is
primarily 1½ stories high, with a two-story section near the northeast corner,
which is in conformity with the 2½-story height limitation of the R-2 zoning
district and is consistent with the one-story residences to the north, west and
south of the Subject Property, and with the 2-story and 3½-story residences to
the east, northeast and northwest of the Subject Property; and (b) the proposed
additions will be consistent with the style of the existing residence and the
residences in the area, and will approve the appearance of the front building
line; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not impair an adequate supply of
light and air because the proposed additions are well below the height
limitations of the zoning district and the size of the additions is modest in
relation to the existing residence; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property as it will comply with all applicable
building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not diminish the taxable value of
land and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed building additions will not contribute to congestion
on the public streets, as the use of the Subject Property will not be changed;
and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed additions will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village.
   WHEREAS, the requested variations are in harmony with the general purpose
and intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that it allows the renovation,
restoration and rehabilitation of a structurally sound existing building while
maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the community and protecting
established trees and landscaping.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : The foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the findings
of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth herein.
   SECTION 2:  The following variations are hereby granted to the property,
commonly known as 1230 Westmoor, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
2 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.24 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code: (a)
variations from the front setback requirements of Section 17.30.050A of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, to allow an open front porch to observe a front
setback of 23 feet, whereas a front setback of 50 feet is required, resulting
in a variation of 27 feet (54%), and to allow an enclosed one and two-story
addition at the northeast corner of the existing residence to observe a front
setback of 44 feet, whereas a front setback of 50 feet is required, resulting
in a variation of 6 feet (12%), (b) a variation from Section 17.30.060A of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, to allow a 1½-story addition at the rear of the
existing residence to observe a west side yard setback of 11.5 feet, whereas a
side yard setback of 20.64 feet is required to comply with the total side yard
requirement of 32.84 feet, resulting in a variation of 9.14 feet (44.28%), and
(c) a variation from Section 17.30.110A to allow a front-facing garage door to
be 13 feet wide, which exceeds the 9-ffot maximum, resulting in a variation of
4 feet, the proposed addition to be constructed in accordance with the plans
and specifications submitted with the application for variations.
   SECTION 3: The variations granted herein are conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 1st day of October, 2002, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser and Woodbury
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    Trustee Aquilino
   Signed:
   /s/Michael F. Duhl
   Village President
Countersigned:
/s/Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: September 17, 2002
Posted: September 18, 2002
Passed and Approved: October 1, 2002
Posted: October 2, 2002
ORDINANCE NO. M-33-2002
AN ORDINANCE ESTABLISHING 902 GREENWOOD AVENUE AS A DESIGNATED LANDMARK
   WHEREAS, the Village of Winnetka desires to identify, designate and preserve
buildings and structures in the Village that are historically, culturally, and
architecturally significant and to encourage the restoration and rehabilitation
of those buildings and structures; and
   WHEREAS, pursuant to Sections 11-48.2-1, et seq. of the Illinois Municipal
Code (65 ILCS 5/11-48.2-1, et seq.), the Council of the Village of Winnetka
enacted Chapter 15.64 of Title 15 of the Winnetka Village Code, entitled
“Landmark Preservation,” for the purposes of preserving, protecting, enhancing,
rehabilitating and regulating buildings, structures, objects, and places of
historical, cultural or architectural importance; and
   WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka have determined that it is
in the best interests of the Village and its residents that participation in
the landmark designation process shall be limited to those owners who
voluntarily elect to participate in that process; and
   WHEREAS, pursuant to the provisions of Chapter 15.64 of the Winnetka Village
Code (the “Landmark Ordinance”) and notice duly published and sent, the
Landmark Preservation Commission conducted a public hearing on June 3, 2002, to
consider the application of the property owners for landmark designation of the
property located at 902 Greenwood Avenue permanent real estate index number 05-
18-224-020-0000 (“Subject Property”); and
   WHEREAS, the Landmark Preservation Commission applied the Village’s System
for the Evaluation of Landmarks to the information received at the hearing and
made findings regarding the property at 902 Greenwood Avenue, including the
following:
      (1)   The architectural type is extremely rare, in that: (a) the
residence is one of a collection of five neighboring residences constructed in
the 1920s by builder, Andrew Paulson, in the Swedish farmhouse style and
resembles Swedish vernacular architecture; (b) the exterior of the residence is
composed of half-timber, stucco, rough-hewn clapboard and faux stone; and (c)
the home has irregular window shapes (triangle, circle and trapezoid), dormers,
a beggar’s log above the front door, hand-hewn timbering and farm-style window
shutters;
      (2)   The method of construction is extremely rare, in that: (a) the
house was originally built with a sod roof, which subsequently was replaced
with asphalt shingles and which the owners propose to replace with a wood
board-and-batten system; (b) the house has irregular windows and such interior
details as yellow pine floors, plaster walls covered with the architect’s
murals, a fireplace resembling a sugar bowl, a balcony off the living room,
floor joists of bow tie construction; and (c) the house's wood trim, doors and
millwork are carved in idiosyncratic shapes;
      (3)   The Subject Property is associated with a person of national
significance, in that it was built by Andrew Paulson, a recognized muralist who
contributed to the Chicago World Fair’s Colombian Exposition in 1892 and whose
importance as an artist is nationally recognized;
      (4)   The Subject Property is a symbol of the neighborhood and a
conspicuous and familiar structure in the context of the entire Village,
because its unusual character makes it an important contributing member of the
group of five Swedish farmhouse style residences in the immediate vicinity; and
      (5)   The Subject Property received the highest rating for originality
and design integrity because only the sod roof has been changed and the
original windows and exterior materials are intact.
   WHEREAS, based on its findings, the Landmark Preservation Commission has
concluded that the residence located at 902 Greenwood Avenue is unique and
meets the criteria of the Landmark Ordinance and has unanimously recommended
that it be so designated; and
   WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka have considered the
recommendation of the Landmark Preservation Commission and have determined that
it is in the best interest of the Village and its residents to establish the
house located at 902 Greenwood Avenue as a designated landmark.
   NOW THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain:
   SECTION 1: That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2: That the property located at 902 Greenwood Avenue, permanent real
estate index number 05-18-224-020-0000, is hereby designated a landmark under
the Landmark Ordinance, in accordance with the findings and recommendations of
the Landmark Preservation Commission.
   SECTION 3: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 15th day of October, 2002, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser and Woodbury
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    President Duhl
   Signed:
   s/s Barbara Aquilino
    Acting Village President
Countersigned:
s/s Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: October 1, 2002
Posted: October 2, 2002
Passed and Approved: October 15, 2002
Posted: October 16, 2002
ORDINANCE NO. M-34-2002
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A SPECIAL USE PERMIT TO ALLOW THE SCHMIDT BURNHAM LOG
HOUSE TO BE RELOCATED TO CROW ISLAND WOODS PARK (1140 Willow Road)
   WHEREAS, the Winnetka Park District (“Park District”) is the owner of the
following described real estate (the “Subject Property”):
Block 1 (excepting therefrom the east 13.0 feet of Lots 37 to 47 inclusive),
and Blocks 2, 9 and 10 in Vernamo Subdivision of the northwest quarter of the
southwest quarter of Section 20, Township 42 North, Range 13, East of the Third
Principal Meridian, also the vacated public streets and alleys or parts thereof
in said Vernamo Subdivision, as recorded October 30, 1891, in book 54 of plats,
page 13, as document number 1560706, to-wit: all of vacated Mt. Pleasant street
from the east line of Euclid Avenue (formerly Larson St.) to the west line
extended of Glendale Avenue (formerly Bjornson St); all of vacated Berkeley
Avenue (formerly Peterson St.) from the south line extended of Willow Road to
the previously vacated Sunset Road at the south end of Block 9; the west 33
feet of vacated Glendale Avenue (excepting therefrom that portion of Glendale
Avenue lying adjacent to Lots 37 to 47 in Block 1); and all of the vacated
alleys lying within Blocks 1, 2 and 9 in the Village of Winnetka, Cook County,
Illinois., in the Village of Winnetka, Cook County, Illinois,
commonly known as Crow Island Woods Park, 1140 Willow Road, Winnetka, Illinois,
and located in the R-2 Single-family Residential Zoning District, provided in
Chapter 17.24 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka
Village Code; and
   WHEREAS, the Winnetka Historical Society (“Historical Society”) is the
lessee of the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, a building commonly known as the Schmidt-Burnham Log House was
donated to the Historical Society which will become its owner, subject to the
condition that it be moved from its current location at 1407 Tower Road; and
   WHEREAS, the Log House is a 32-foot by 26-foot single family residence that
was built in 1837 and moved to its current location on Tower Road from Indian
Hill in 1917; and
   WHEREAS, the Log House is the longest continuously occupied home in Cook
County and was continuously occupied by the Burnham family from 1917 until the
death of the last surviving owner, Ann Hibbard Burnham Smith, in 2001; and
   WHEREAS, the Historical Society approached the Park District, the Village of
Winnetka and Winnetka Elementary School District No. 36 ("School District") in
an effort to identify possible locations for the Log House; and
   WHEREAS, after evaluating the Crow Island Woods Park, Lloyd Park, Sheridan
Park and Nick Corwin Park as possible locations for the Log House, the Park
District and Historical Society concluded that Crow Island Woods Park was the
most suitable location due to its proximity to the school and its having the
least impact on neighboring properties; and
   WHEREAS, the Park District has agreed to lease a portion of Crow Island
Woods Park to the Historical Society as a location for the Log House and the
Park District and Historical Society have accordingly filed a joint application
for a special use permit to allow the Log House to be moved from 1407 Tower
Road to Crow Island Woods Park; and
   WHEREAS, on August 28, 2002, on due notice thereof, the Plan Commission met
to consider whether the proposed special use is consistent with Winnetka 2020,
the Winnetka Comprehensive Plan; and
   WHEREAS, two of the ten members present at the August 28, 2002, Plan
Commission meeting recused themselves from the consideration of the Society’s
application, due to their roles as advocates for the Society, one of the two
being the Society’s architect for the proposal and the other being the attorney
for the Society; and
   WHEREAS, after considering 23 different provisions of Winnetka 2020, the
Plan Commission found that the joint special use request was consistent with
all 23 of the applicable provisions; and
   WHEREAS, by the affirmative vote of seven of the members remaining present
at the August 28, 2002, meeting and the abstention of the eighth member then
present, the Plan Commission found the proposed special use consistent with the
Comprehensive Plan and recommended that the special use be granted; and
   WHEREAS, on September 12, 2002, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested special use and, by the
unanimous vote of the six members then present, has recommended that the
Council grant the request for the special use permit; and
   WHEREAS, the recommendations and findings of the Plan Commission and Zoning
Board of Appeals were forwarded to the Village Council, which first considered
the Society’s application at the regular Council meeting on October 1, 2002;
and
   WHEREAS, the Village has not received written objections to the special use
application from any owners of property located within 250 feet of the Subject
Property; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property is a 17.8 acre parcel of land that located
immediately west of Crow Island School and east of Euclid Avenue, from the
south right-of-way line of Willow Road to Sunset Road (extended); and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property is a rectangular open area, with several large
grassy areas and mature trees along Willow Road and adjacent to Crow Island
School; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property is improved with a picnic shelter that is
located in the south portion of Crow Island Woods Park and is accessed from a
gravel road that extends south from Willow Road adjacent to Euclid Avenue; and
   WHEREAS, Maps 2, 3, 6 of the Winnetka Comprehensive Plan, Winnetka 2020,
depict the Subject Property as "Park/Open Space"; and
   WHEREAS, the Historical Society and Park District propose to move the house
to Crow Island Woods Park by way of tower Road, Forest Way Drive and Willow
Road; and
   WHEREAS, the Historical Society and Park District propose to locate the Log
House in a wooded portion of the school property, 189 feet south of the Willow
Road right-of-way, approximately 162 feet from Crow Island School and 50 feet
due west-of the southwest corner of the Crow Island Woods School west
playground playground; and
   WHEREAS, the Historical Society and Park District propose to provide
additional pedestrian access by laying a crushed limestone footpath from Willow
Road to the Log House; and
   WHEREAS, the Historical Society proposes to use the Log House as a museum
that is proposed to have limited operation, as follows: (a) the museum will be
open to the public two days a week, by appointment only, between approximately
9:30 a.m. and 2:30 p.m., from April 15 to October 15, and (b) the museum will
also be open one weekend day per month, from April 15 to October 15; and
   WHEREAS, the Historical Society proposes that the museum will also be open
for school-sponsored activities and occasional special events; and
   WHEREAS, the Historical Society and the Park District propose to request
permission from the State of Illinois to add a drop-off area at the south edge
of the Willow Road right-of-way to facilitate access for visitors arriving by
vehicle; and
   WHEREAS, the School District has expressed its support for the proposed
relocation and use of the Log House, because the Log House affords the School
District the opportunity to expand on its nationally recognized "Pioneer Room"
experience for third graders; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed special use is consistent with one of the primary
roles of the Historical Society to serve as a research and reference resource
for the history of the Village of Winnetka; and
   WHEREAS, as stated in the Comprehensive Plan, the Winnetka Historical
Society was founded in 1932 to collect, preserve and present artifacts
representing Winnetka History and to increase public knowledge about the
community; and
   WHEREAS, on January 20, 1987, the Council of the Village of Winnetka adopted
Resolution R-910-87, pursuant to which the Village of Winnetka leased and paid
for the space occupied by the Winnetka Historical Society at Skokie School,
which was occupied by the Society until 1999; and
   WHEREAS, prior to 1987, the Winnetka Historical Society occupied space at
the Winnetka Public Library; and
   WHEREAS, the Village of Winnetka continues to appropriate funds for the
Society’s operations; and
   WHEREAS, in addition to being available to the general public, the Society
performs research and provides information to the Village on the historic
significance of properties within the Village in conjunction with the Village’s
building demolition procedures; and
   WHEREAS, based on the longstanding relationship between the Society and the
Village, the Society’s history of being located in public buildings, the
Society’s availability to the Village as a whole, and the formal relation
between the Society and the Village, the Council of the Village of Winnetka
have determined that the Society constitutes a non-essential public use for
purposes of Section 17.56.010B of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed special use is consistent with the Comprehensive
Plan’s community goal of preserving and enhancing those public assets and
architecturally significant structures that create the attractive appearance
and peaceful, single-family residential character of the Village, in that; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed special use is consistent with the Comprehensive
Plan’s community goal of supporting educational excellence and the enrichment
of Winnetka’s cultural environment, in that; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed special use is consistent with the Comprehensive
Plan’s community goal of limiting commercial, institutional and residential
development within the Village to minimize the potentially adverse impacts on
adjacent residential neighborhoods and to prevent the need for significant
increases in infrastructure and other community resources, in that it maintains
the residential appearance of the neighborhood, controls the activities to make
them more closely parallel to single-family residential impacts and does not
require additional infrastructure or community resources; and
   WHEREAS, subject to the terms and conditions hereinafter set forth, the
proposed special use is consistent with the Comprehensive Plan’s community goal
of ensuring that institutional development is appropriate with the character
of, and minimizes the adverse impact on, its surrounding neighborhood; and
   WHEREAS, subject to the terms and conditions hereinafter set forth, the
proposed special use is consistent with the Comprehensive Plan, in that it
furthers the objective of recognizing the critical role of the Village’
   WHEREAS, the proposed special use is consistent with the Comprehensive Plan,
in that it furthers the objective of encouraging organizations, schools,
religious institutions, business and citizens in their efforts to beautify the
Village; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed special use is consistent with the Comprehensive Plan,
in that it furthers the objective of retaining the character of existing
residential neighborhoods, because the proposed relocation will allow the
redevelopment of 1407 Tower Road with a residence that has contemporary single-
family residential amenities, while preserving one of Winnetka's oldest houses
and the longest continually occupied dwelling in Cook County; and
   WHEREAS, subject to the terms and conditions hereinafter set forth, the
proposed special use is consistent with the Comprehensive Plan, in that it
fosters the objective of protecting residential neighborhoods and homes from
the encroachment of incompatible land uses and traffic patterns, by preserving
the wooded appearance of the Subject Property, limiting the activities of the
Society on the Subject Property, and providing for access without increasing
parking demand within the adjoining residential neighborhood; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed special use is consistent with the Comprehensive Plan,
in that it furthers the objective of encouraging the preservation of older
houses that contribute to the character of the neighborhood through additions
and improvements; and
   WHEREAS, subject to the terms and conditions hereinafter set forth, the
proposed special use is consistent with the Comprehensive Plan, in that it
fosters the objective of maintaining the quiet ambience of residential
neighborhoods by restricting the activities on the Subject Property to hours
that do not coincide with periods of peak traffic in the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed special use is consistent with the Comprehensive Plan,
in that it furthers the objective of recognizing the critical importance of
educational and community institutions to Village residents; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed special use is consistent with the Comprehensive Plan,
in that it furthers the objective of maintaining an atmosphere in which diverse
cultural and educational organizations may flourish and in which special
activities for residents of all ages may be enhanced; and
   WHEREAS, subject to the terms and conditions hereinafter set forth, the
proposed special use is consistent with the Comprehensive Plan, in that it
furthers the objective of engaging in a public process that balances
institutional goals and minimizes adverse impact to the character of the
adjacent residential neighborhood; and
   WHEREAS, subject to the terms and conditions hereinafter set forth, the
proposed special use is consistent with the Comprehensive Plan’s recommendation
to encourage governmental and non-governmental institutions to work with their
constituents, neighbors and the Village to minimize the impact of traffic and
parking on surrounding residential streets and to develop on-site solutions
where appropriate; and
   WHEREAS, subject to the terms and conditions hereinafter set forth, the
proposed special use is consistent with the Comprehensive Plan’s recommendation
to ensure that proposed uses do not have an adverse impact on the residential
character of the surrounding residential neighborhoods; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed special use is consistent with the Comprehensive
Plan’s recommendation to reduce on-street parking along narrow streets within
residential neighborhoods in that it provides adequate on-site parking for day-
to-day activities; and
   WHEREAS, by preserving one of the oldest buildings in the Village, the
proposed special use is consistent with the Comprehensive Plan’s recommendation
to recognize the role of the Village’s historic architecture in defining
Winnetka’s unique residential character and to encourage its preservation; and
   WHEREAS, because it preserves one of the oldest buildings in the Village,
the proposed special use is consistent with the Comprehensive Plan’s
recommendation to promote historic preservation as a contributor to the quality
and character of the Village, by encouraging the study and inventory of
existing houses and commercial buildings that define Village character; and
   WHEREAS, by preserving one of the oldest buildings in the Village, the
proposed special use is consistent with the Comprehensive Plan’s recommendation
to support the Landmark Commission’s work in public education, in research, in
recommending landmarks and in providing technical assistance for renovating
older properties; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed special use is consistent with the Comprehensive
Plan’s recommendation to incorporate historic preservation as a standard
component of all elements of Village planning; and
   WHEREAS, the establishment, maintenance and operation of the special use
will not be detrimental to or endanger the public health, safety, comfort,
morals or general welfare, in that: (a) the Winnetka Historical Society
provides a valuable public service; (b) relocating the Log House to an open,
wooded site that is easily accessible to students and members of the general
public is a worthy public purpose that furthers the general welfare of the
Village of Winnetka; and (c) the terms and conditions hereinafter set forth
provide adequate safeguards to protect the public health, safety and comfort
from possible negative impacts of the addition of the Log House museum to Crow
Island Woods Park; and
   WHEREAS, the special use will not be substantially injurious to the use and
enjoyment of other property in the immediate vicinity for uses that are
permitted by right in the R-2 zoning district, in that: (a) the number of
employees, hours of operation and frequency of events are minimal; (b) the
limitations imposed upon the use by the terms and conditions set forth in
Section 4 of this Ordinance, including the periodic review of the impact of the
use, will protect the owners of property in the immediate vicinity from an
unreasonable increase in the intensity of the use; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed special use will
substantially diminish or impair property values in the immediate vicinity; and
   WHEREAS, the establishment of the special use will not impede the normal and
orderly development or improvement of other property in the immediate vicinity
for uses permitted by right in the district or districts of concern, in that
the preservation of the Log House, the low intensity of the proposed use during
normal business days, and the imposition of conditions hereinafter set forth to
protect against any negative impacts of the use, will preserve the character of
both the Crow Island Woods Park and the immediate neighborhood, which is fully
developed with established land uses; and
   WHEREAS, adequate measures have been taken to provide ingress and egress in
a manner that minimizes pedestrian and vehicular traffic congestion in the
public ways and adequate parking facilities necessary to the operation of the
Log House museum exist, in that: (a) the proposed use does not alter the
existing driveway and parking areas in Crow Island Woods Park; and (b) the
additional pedestrian path will increase accessibility from the public
sidewalks; and
   WHEREAS, adequate utilities, access roads, drainage and other facilities
necessary to the operation of the proposed special use will be provided, as the
Historical Society's plans include providing utilities for the Log House by
connecting to the existing utility lines that serve the immediate area; and
   WHEREAS, when implemented in accordance with the terms and conditions set
forth in Section 3 of this Ordinance, the special use in all other respects
will conform to the applicable regulations of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance and
other Village ordinances and codes; and
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : The foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated into this section
as the findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set
forth herein.
   SECTION 2: Subject to the terms and conditions hereinafter set forth, there
is hereby granted to the Subject Property, commonly known as Crow Island Woods
Park, 1140 Willow Road, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-2 Single-
family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.24 of the Winnetka
Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, a special use permit,
pursuant to Section 17.24.010B.2 and Section 17.56.010B of the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance, and pursuant to the provisions of Chapter 17.56 of the Winnetka
Zoning Ordinance, to allow the Subject Property to allow the Log House
currently located at 1407 Tower Road to be moved to the Subject Property and
used as a historical museum, all in accordance with the plans, materials and
elevations submitted with the application for special use permit.
   SECTION 3: The special use granted herein is subject to the following
conditions:
      A.   The proposed relocation of the Log House shall commence within 12
months after the effective date of this Ordinance;
      B.   The Park District and the Society shall work with the Village to
assure that both the timing and the route for the relocation of the Log House
will not unduly interfere with normal traffic patterns or utility service, and
will not unnecessarily damage trees and vegetation.
      C.   The Log House shall not be used for any events that are not
sponsored or co-sponsored by the Winnetka Historical Society.
   SECTION 4 : The stipulations, conditions and restrictions set forth in the
foregoing Section 3 of this Ordinance may be modified or revised from time to
time by the Village Council following public notice and hearing, following the
procedures specified in Section 17.56 of the Winnetka Village Code for
processing special use applications.
   SECTION 5: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 15th day of October, 2002, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser and Woodbury
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    President Duhl
   Signed:
   s/s Barbara Aquilino
    Acting Village President
