ORDINANCE NO. M-35-2002
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (1447 Edgewood)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
Lot 15 in Block 21 in the Chicago North Shore Land Company’s Subdivision, in
Sections 17 and 18, Township 42 North, Range 13, East of the Third Principal
Meridian, in Cook County, Illinois. (In the West ½ of the Northeast ¼ of
Section 18),
commonly known as 1447 Edgewood Lane, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
5 Single Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.08 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for the
following variations: (a) a variation from the maximum building size
limitations of Section 17.30.040 to allow a gross floor area of 3,283.4 square
feet, which exceeds the maximum allowable gross floor area of 3,220 square feet
by 63.4 square feet (1.9%); and (b) a variation from the intensity of use of
lot provisions of Section 17.30.030, to allow a roofed lot coverage of 2,073.62
square feet, which exceeds the maximum roofed lot coverage of 2,021 square feet
by 61.2 square feet, all for the purpose of constructing a two-story addition
to the residence on the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, on October 14, 2002, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variation and by the
unanimous vote of the four members then present, has reported to the Council
recommending that the requested variation be granted; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and unique hardships associated
with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect
to the Subject Property in that: (a) the Subject Property is improved with a
single family residence and detached garage that were built in 1922, before the
enactment of the Zoning Ordinance; (b) the existing home and garage are
established features in the neighborhood and are in good structural condition;
(c) the existing home has a small, galley-style kitchen on the first floor, but
lacks family gathering space in the vicinity of the kitchen; (d) the second
floor of the existing home has a single, small bathroom, two bedrooms and a
small room that is used as an office; (e) the applicants propose to remove a
small enclosed first floor porch and second floor sunroom at the rear of the
house and to construct a two-story addition to create a new kitchen, family
room and powder room on the first floor, and a master suite and a larger third
bedroom on the second floor; (f) the front of the existing garage is located
approximately 45 feet from the rear lot line, with all but 4.65 feet of the
garage being located in the rear quarter of the lot, as a result of which the
Subject Property does not qualify for the 400-square foot gross floor area
bonus for garages located in the rear quarter of a lot; (g) requiring the
removal and relocation of the garage, to qualify for the gross floor area
bonus, would impose an unreasonable burden on the applicant; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that:
(a) the kitchen improvements, family room and master bedroom suite additions
are necessary to bring the residence up to contemporary living standards; (b)
reducing the size of the addition to bring it within the gross floor area and
roofed lot coverage limitations of the Zoning Ordinance would reduce the size
and functionality of the two new bedrooms on the second floor; and (c) it is
not reasonably possible to reduce the size of the addition or to reconfigure
the addition to bring it into full conformity with the Zoning Ordinance; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because: (a) the proposed addition will match the style and
materials of the existing residence; (b) the proposed addition is at the rear
of the existing residence and will not affect the streetscape; and (c) allowing
the proposed addition to be built without requiring the garage to be relocated
will preserve the established open space at the rear of the Subject Property;
and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not impair an adequate supply of
light and air because the proposed addition is well within the required setback
and the total gross floor area that will result on the Subject Property will be
less than would be permitted if the garage were located entirely in the rear
quarter of the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property as the addition will comply with all
applicable building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not diminish the taxable value of
land and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed addition will not contribute to congestion on the
public streets, as the existing use will be unchanged; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed addition will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations are in harmony with the general purpose
and intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that it allows the renovation,
restoration and rehabilitation of a structurally sound existing building while
maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the community and protecting
established trees and landscaping.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2: That the following variations are hereby granted to the Subject
Property, commonly known as 1447 Edgewood Lane, Winnetka, Illinois, and located
in the R-5 Single Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.08
of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code: (a) a
variation from the maximum building size limitations of Section 17.30.040 to
allow a gross floor area of 3,283.4 square feet which exceeds the maximum
allowable gross floor area of 3,220 square feet by 63.4 square feet (1.9%); and
(b) a variation from the intensity of use of lot provisions of Section
17.30.030, to allow a roofed lot coverage of 2,073.62 square feet, which
exceeds the maximum roofed lot coverage of 2,021 square feet by 61.2 square
feet, all for the purpose of constructing a two-story addition to the residence
on the Subject Property, in accordance with the plans and elevations submitted
with the application for variation.
   SECTION 3: That the variations hereby granted are conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting. November 2002, pursuant to the following roll call vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Brower, Greenough, Powell, and Presser
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    Trustees Aquilino and Woodbury
   Signed:
   s/s Michael F. Duhl
    Village President
Countersigned:
s/s Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: November 5, 2002
Posted: November 6, 2002
Passed and Approved: November 19, 2002
Posted: November 20, 2002
ORDINANCE NO. M-36-2002
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (205 Forest)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
The North 52 feet of the South 166 feet of Lot 1 and the North 52 feet of the
South 166 feet of the West 10 feet of Lot 2 in Block 3 in John C. Garland’s
Addition to Winnetka, being a subdivision of the North 120 acres of the
Southwest ¼ of Section 21, Township 42 North, Range 13, East of the Third
Principal Meridian, in Cook County, Illinois.
commonly known as 205 Forest Street, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
4 Single Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.08 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for a
variation from the rear yard setback requirements of Section 17.30.070 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, to allow a new, detached, two-car garage to be
located 2.38 feet from the rear lot line, although a 6.00 rear yard setback is
required, resulting in a variation of 3.62 feet (60.3%); and
   WHEREAS, on October 14, 2002, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variation and, by the
unanimous vote of the five members then present, has reported to the Council
recommending that the requested variation be granted; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and unique hardships associated
with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect
to the Subject Property in that: (a) the Subject Property is located on the
east side of Forest Street, south of Garland Avenue; (b) the rear yard of the
Subject Property abuts the side yard of the property to the east, which faces
Garland Avenue; (c) because of the rear-yard-to-side-yard configuration of the
Subject Property, the rear yard setback of the Subject Property is required to
equal the minimum side yard setback for the adjoining property; (d) the
existing one-car garage observes a rear yard setback of 2.38 feet; (e) the
existing driveway is along the north lot line and the south aide yard is not
large enough to allow the driveway to be relocated there; and (f) because of
mature trees in the yard, placing the garage in a conforming location would
require paving more of the rear yard to provide access for two cars; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that:
(a) the existing garage is dilapidated and needs to be replaced; (b) the
existing garage is barely large enough for a single vehicle; (c) a two-car
garage is a standard amenity on properties in the vicinity; and (d) moving the
garage to the west or south to meet the full rear yard setback requirement
would jeopardize mature trees in the yard, increase the amount of pavement in
the rear yard, and place the garage in an unconventional location, all of which
would tend to have a negative impact on the value of the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because the proposed garage will be located in the same corner
of the Subject Property as the garage it will replace, and the garage is
designed to complement the style of the existing home; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not impair an adequate supply of light
and air because of the substantial distance between the proposed garage and the
home on the property to the east, which is located close to the east lot line
of the 120-foot wide parcel; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property because the proposed garage will comply
with all applicable building and fire protection codes and will be located a
substantial distance from the principal buildings on neighboring properties;
and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed detached garage will not contribute to congestion on
the public streets, as it will provide additional off-street parking for the
Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed detached garage will
otherwise impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation is in harmony with the general purpose and
intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that it allows the replacement of a
dilapidated structure with one that is of greater utility, while maintaining
the existing scale and appearance of the community and protecting established
trees and landscaping.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 :   That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2:   That there is hereby granted to the Subject Property, commonly
known as 205 Forest Street, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-4 Single
Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.08 of the Winnetka
Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, a variation from the
rear yard setback requirements of Section 17.30.070 of the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance, to allow a new, detached, two-car garage to be located 2.38 feet
from the rear lot line, although a 6.00 rear yard setback is required,
resulting in a variation of 3.62 feet (60.3%), all in accordance with the plans
and elevations submitted with the application for variation.
   SECTION 3: That the variation hereby granted is conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 19th day of November, 2002, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Brower, Greenough, Powell and Presser
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    Trustees Aquilino and Woodbury
   Signed:
   s/s Michael F. Duhl
    Village President
Countersigned:
s/s Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: November 5, 2002
Posted: November 6, 2002
Passed and Approved: November 19, 2002
Posted: November 20, 2002
ORDINANCE NO. M-37-2002
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (675 Garland)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
Lot 3 (except the East 130.0 feet thereof) also the East 2.0 feet of the South
17.56 feet of Lot 1 in the Lydia D. Sutters Subdivision of that part of Block 4
lying West of a line 436.55 feet east of and parallel with the center line of
Forest Street in John C. Garlands Addition to Winnetka in the Southwest ¼ of
Section 21, Township 42 North, Range 13, East of the Third Principal Meridian,
in the Village of Winnetka, Cook County, Illinois,
commonly known as 675 Garland Avenue, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
5 Single Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.08 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for the
following variations: (a) a variation from the maximum building size
limitations of Section 17.30.040 of the Zoning Ordinance, to allow a gross
floor area of 4,029.92 square feet, which exceeds the gross floor area
limitation of 3,763.80 square feet by 266.12 square feet (7%); and (b) a
variation from the intensity of use of lot limitations of Section 17.30.030 of
the Zoning Ordinance, to allow a roofed lot coverage of 2,463.97 square feet,
which exceeds the roofed lot coverage limitation of 2,435.98 square feet by
27.99 square feet (1.2%), all for the purpose of constructing a 195.25 square
foot, one-story addition to the residence on the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, on October 14, 2002, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variation and by the
unanimous vote of the five members then present, has reported to the Council
recommending that the requested variation be granted; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and unique hardships associated
with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect
to the Subject Property in that: (a) the residence is a structurally sound
brick Georgian home that was constructed in 1928; (b) the interior
configuration of the home does not allow for a family room; (c) the proposed
family room addition has been designed as a mirror image of the kitchen area at
the opposite end of the rear of the home; and (d) the proposed addition will
improve both the functionality of the home and the appearance of its rear
elevation; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that:
(a) a family room near the kitchen is a standard features of contemporary
housing within the Village; (b) the 11-foot by 15-foot footprint of the
proposed one-story addition will align with the side and rear walls of the
existing home; (c) it is not possible to have a family room of usable size
without exceeding the maximum building size limitations; and (d) reducing the
size of the addition to bring it more into conformity with the Zoning Ordinance
will result in an addition that does not align with existing walls and will not
be fully integrated into the existing structure, thereby reducing its utility
and value; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because the addition is at the rear of the home and is
designed to fit fully integrate its exterior walls with the exiting building
exterior; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not impair an adequate supply of
light and air because the proposed addition is a single story high and complies
with all setback requirements; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property as the proposed addition will comply with
all applicable building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not diminish the taxable value of
land and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed addition; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed one-story addition will not contribute to congestion
on the public streets, as the use of the Subject Property will not change; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed one-story addition will
otherwise impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations are in harmony with the general purpose
and intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that it allows the renovation,
restoration and rehabilitation of a structurally sound existing building while
maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the community and protecting
established trees and landscaping.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 :   That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2:   That the following variations are hereby granted to the Subject
Property, commonly known as 675 Garland Avenue, Winnetka, Illinois, and located
in the R-5 Single Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.08
of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code: (a) a
variation from the maximum building size limitations of Section 17.30.040 of
the Zoning Ordinance, to allow a gross floor area of 4,029.92 square feet,
which exceeds the gross floor area limitation of 3,763.80 square feet by 266.12
square feet (7%); and (b) a variation from the intensity of use of lot
limitations of Section 17.30.030 of the Zoning Ordinance, to allow a roofed lot
coverage of 2,463.97 square feet, which exceeds the roofed lot coverage
limitation of 2,435.98 square feet by 27.99 square feet (1.2%), all for the
purpose of constructing a 195.25 square foot, one-story addition to the
residence on the Subject Property, in accordance with the plans and elevations
submitted with the application for variation.
   SECTION 3:   That the variations hereby granted are conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4:   This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 19th day of November, 2002, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Brower, Greenough, Powell and Presser a
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    Trustees Aquilino and Woodbury
   Signed:
   s/s Michael F. Duhl
    Village President
Countersigned:
s/s Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
