ORDINANCE NO. M-5-2003
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A SPECIAL USE AND VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE
ZONING ORDINANCE OF THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (151 Fuller
Lane)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
Lot 11 in Orth’s Subdivision of part of the South East fractional one-quarter
(1/4) of fractional Section 21, Township 42 North, Range 13, East of the Third
Principal Meridian, in Cook County, Illinois,
commonly known as 151 Fuller Lane, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
Single Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.12 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for a
variation from the maximum building size limitations of Section 17.30.040 of
the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance to permit the construction of a 91.5 square-foot,
one-story addition that will result in a gross floor area of 3,660.58 square
feet, which exceeds the maximum allowable gross floor area of 3,069.75 square
feet, resulting in a variation of 590.83 square feet (19.25%); and
   WHEREAS, on December 9, 2002, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variation and by the
unanimous vote of the five members then present, has reported to the Council
recommending that the requested variation be granted; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and unique hardships associated
with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect
to the Subject Property in that: (a) the residence on the Subject Property was
built in 1923, before the enactment of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance and long
before the imposition of gross floor area limitations; (b) the existing
residence has a legally nonconforming gross floor area of 3,569.08 square feet;
(c) the proposed addition will be 6.10 feet wide and 15 feet long and will
connect two wings of the structure at the rear of the home; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that
(a) the additional 91.5 square feet of gross floor area will be at the rear of
the house and will not be visible either from the street or from the properties
on either side of the Subject property; (b) the proposed addition is of modest
size; and (c) the proposed addition will allow for the expansion of the
kitchen, bringing the functionality of the home up to contemporary standards;
and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood or impair an adequate supply of light and air because the
proposed addition is one-story high and will be set between two wings at the
rear of the house, where it will not be visible either from the street or from
the properties on either side of the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property as the addition will comply with all
applicable building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not contribute to congestion on the
public streets, as the use and capacity of the building will not be changed;
and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed one-story addition will
otherwise impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village.
   WHEREAS, the requested variation is in harmony with the general purpose and
intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that it allows the renovation,
restoration and rehabilitation of a structurally sound existing building while
maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the community and protecting
established trees and landscaping.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2: The following variation is hereby granted to the Subject
Property, commonly known as 151 Fuller Lane, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in
the R- Single Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.12 of
the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code: a
variation from the maximum building size limitations of Section 17.30.040 of
the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance to permit the construction of a 91.5 square-foot,
one-story addition that will result in a gross floor area of 3,660.58 square
feet, which exceeds the maximum allowable gross floor area of 3,069.75 square
feet, resulting in a variation of 590.83 square feet (19.25%), said
construction to be in accordance with the plans and elevations submitted with
the application for variation.
   SECTION 3: That the variation hereby granted is conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 21st day of January, 2003, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser and Woodbury
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    President Duhl
   Signed:
   s/s Barbara Aquilino
    Village President Pro Tem
Countersigned:
s/s Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: January 7, 2003
Posted: January 8, 2003
Passed and Approved: January 21, 2003
Posted: January 22, 2003
ORDINANCE NO. M-6-2003
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A SPECIAL USE AND VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE
ZONING ORDINANCE OF THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (740 Sheridan
Road)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
That part of Lot 3 in the Resubdivision of Block 8 in Park Addition to
Winnetka, as recorded under Doc. No. 1049627, described as follows: Beginning
at the Northeast corner of said Lot 3 and running thence West along the North
line of said Lot 3, 198.21 feet; thence South at right angles to the North line
of said Lot 3, 124.73 feet; thence Northeasterly along a line which, if
extended, would intersect the northeasterly line of said Lot 3 at a point 80.46
feet southeasterly from the Northeast corner of said Lot 3, for a distance of
78.36 feet to a point 170.51 feet, Southwesterly from the Northeasterly line of
said Lot 3; thence north parallel to the line at right angles to the North line
of Lot 3, 178.27 feet to a point in the Northeasterly line of said Lot 3,
103.07 feet Southeasterly from the Northeast corner of said Lot 3 and thence
Northwesterly along the Northeasterly line of Lot 3, 103.07 feet to the
Northeast corner thereof and the point of beginning.
Also: all that part of the South 25.0 feet, as measured at right angles to the
south line of Lot 4 in the Resubdivision of Block 8 in Park Addition to
Winnetka as recorded under Doc. No. 1049627, lying East of a line drawn North
at right angles to the south line of said Lot 4 from a point in said South
line, 223.0 feet West of the Southeast corner of said Lot 4, in Sections 16 and
17, Township 42 North, Range 13, East of the Third Principal Meridian, in the
Village of Winnetka, Cook County, Illinois.
commonly known as 740 Sheridan Road, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
2 Single Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.24 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for a
variation from the rear yard setback requirements of Section 17.30.070 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, to permit the construction of a two-story addition
that will have a rear yard setback of 8.73 feet, instead of the required
minimum of 25 feet, a variation of 16.27 feet (65.08%); and
   WHEREAS, on December 9, 2002, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variation and by the
unanimous vote of the five members then present, has reported to the Council
recommending that the requested variation be granted; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and unique hardships associated
with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect
to the Subject Property in that: (a) the Subject Property is in irregularly
shaped lot; (b) the single family residence on the Subject Property was built
in 1939 and is set toward the rear of the lot; (c) the proposed addition will
replace the existing attached garage that is located at the rear of the
residence with a slightly larger building that will contain a garage on the
first floor and a bedroom, bath and media room on the second floor; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that
(a) the location of the addition is determined by the location of the attached
garage that it is intended to replace; (b) although the existing garage is
23.67 feet deep, it is only 18.58 feet wide, making it too small to accommodate
two vehicles; and (c) because the garage doors face the side lot line, the only
way to increase the width of the garage is to extend the building line farther
back, thereby reducing the rear yard setback; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because (a) the proposed addition adds minimal visual bulk and
will be located at the rear of the building, where the attached garage it will
replace is now located, and (b) the additional encroachment into the rear yard
setback is minimal; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not impair an adequate supply of light
and air because the additional encroachment into the required rear yard is
minimal and the addition will comply with applicable height limitations; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property as the proposed addition comply with all
applicable building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed addition will not contribute to congestion on the
public streets, as the new garage will increase the amount of off-street
parking; and garage and room addition will not otherwise impair the public
health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the inhabitants of the Village.
   WHEREAS, the requested variation is in harmony with the general purpose and
intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that it allows the renovation,
restoration and rehabilitation of a structurally sound existing building while
maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the community and protecting
established trees and landscaping.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2: The following variation is hereby granted to the subject
Property, commonly known as 740 Sheridan Road, Winnetka, Illinois, and located
in the R-2 Single Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.24
of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code: a
variation from the rear yard setback requirements of Section 17.30.070 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, to permit the construction of a two-story addition
that will have a rear yard setback of 8.73 feet, instead of the required
minimum of 25 feet, a variation of 16.27 feet (65.08%), said construction to be
in accordance with the plans and specifications submitted with the application
for variation.
   SECTION 3: That the variation hereby granted is conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 21st day of January, 2003, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Aquilino, Greenough, Powell, Presser and Woodbury
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    President Duhl and Trustee Brower (recused)
   Signed:
   s/s Barbara Aquilino
    Village President Pro Tem
Countersigned:
s/s Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: January 7, 2003
Posted: January 8, 2003
Passed and Approved: January 21, 2003
Posted: January 22, 2003
ORDINANCE NO. M-7-2003
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (1140 Chatfield)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
Lot 13 in Block 8 in County Clerk’s Division of the Southwest ¼ of Section 17,
Township 42 North, Range 13, East of the Third Principal Meridian, in Cook
County, Illinois,
commonly known as 1140 Chatfield Road, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the
R-4 Single Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.16 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for the
following variations: (a) a variation from the maximum building size
limitations of Section 17.30.040 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance to allow a
gross floor area of 4,545.44 square feet, which exceeds the maximum allowable
gross floor area of 4,362.73 square feet, resulting in a variation request of
182.71 square feet (4.19%); (b) a variation from the front setback requirements
of Section 17.30.050 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance to allow a front setback
of 28.07 feet along Gordon Terrace, although a 30-foot setback is required,
resulting in a variation request of 1.9 feet (6.43%); (c) a variation from the
corner setback requirement of Section 17.30.050 of the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance, to allow a corner setback of 5.58 feet along Hamptondale Avenue,
rather than the required 19.3-foot corner setback, resulting in a variation
request of 13.72 feet (71.09%); and (d) a variation from the rear yard setback
requirements of Section 17.30.070 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, to allow a
setback of 11.96 feet along the west property line, rather than the required
19.8-foot setback, resulting in a variation request of 7.84 feet (39.59%), all
of said variations being to allow the construction of additions to the north
and south sides of the residence and the connection of the existing detached
garage to the residence; and
   WHEREAS, on January 13, 2003, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variations and by the
unanimous vote of the four members then present, has reported to the Council
recommending that the requested variations be granted; and
   WHEREAS, the owner is requesting variations to allow the construction of the
following building additions, which will add a total of approximately 599.31
square feet of gross floor area: (a) a new front entry, with a gross floor area
of 104 square feet, to be located on the north side of the house in a
conforming location; (b) a one-story, 86-square foot addition to connect the
existing garage to the home, in order to resolve problems caused by level
changes and stairs in the short distance between the existing house and garage;
(c) the replacement of a deteriorated, slab-on-grade screened porch at the
southeast corner of the residence with an addition that will extend along the
south side of the house to allow for the construction of a great room,
breakfast room and expanded kitchen; and (d) a 115-square foot addition at the
southeast corner of the second floor, to expand an existing bedroom; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and unique hardships associated
with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect
to the Subject Property in that: (a) the Subject Property is an irregular,
trapezoidal-shaped lot bounded on the north by Chatfield Road, on the east by
Gordon Terrace and on the south by Hamptondale Avenue; (b) because of the three
street frontages, front yard setbacks are imposed along three sides of the lot;
(c) although the 65.21-foot Gordon Terrace lot line is considered the front lot
line under the definitions in the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, that the Subject
Property is the only lot on that blockface and has been developed so that the
residence faces longer blockface along Chatfield Road; and (d) the oldest
permit found for work on the Subject Property was for a detached garage
constructed in 1929, and it appears that the residence itself was constructed
in its present location before the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance was first adopted;
and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that:
(a) the increased setback requirements on three sides of the Subject Property,
combined with the 65.21-foot dimension of the Gordon Terrace frontage,
substantially reduces the buildable area of the Subject Property; (b) although
the Subject Property has a conforming setback along Chatfield Road, the
setbacks along Gordon Terrace and Hamptondale Avenue are both nonconforming;
(c) of the proposed additions, only 109 square feet of the addition along the
south side of the house will be located in a nonconforming location; and (d)
the locations and sizes of the additions are dictated by the interior layout
and structural limitations of the existing residence; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because the variations will allow minor alternations to the
exterior appearance of the building, improve all views of the residence from
the street and provide more consistency in the residence’s design elements; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not impair an adequate supply of
light and air because: (a) the garage connection is a one-story addition on the
interior of the lot between two existing buildings; (b) the new front entry is
also a one-story addition, and is set within the required setback; and (c) the
additions at the south end of the house conform with the Zoning Ordinance’s
height limitations and face Hamptondale Avenue; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property as they will comply with all applicable
building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not diminish the taxable value of
land and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not contribute to congestion on the
public streets, as the Subject Property will continue to be used as a single
family residence and off street parking will continue to be provided; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the requested variations will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations are in harmony with the general purpose
and intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that they allow the renovation,
restoration and rehabilitation of a structurally sound existing building while
maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the community and protecting
established trees and landscaping.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2: That the following variations are hereby granted to the Subject
Property, commonly known as 1140 Chatfield Road, Winnetka, Illinois, and
located in the R-4 Single Family Residential Zoning District provided in
Chapter 17.16 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka
Village Code: (a) a variation from the maximum building size limitations of
Section 17.30.040 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance to allow a gross floor area
of 4,545.44 square feet, which exceeds the maximum allowable gross floor area
of 4,362.73 square feet, resulting in a variation of 182.71 square feet
(4.19%); (b) a variation from the front setback requirements of Section
17.30.050 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance to allow a front setback of 28.07
feet along Gordon Terrace, although a 30-foot setback is required, resulting in
a variation of 1.9 feet (6.43%); (c) a variation from the corner setback
requirement of Section 17.30.050 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, to allow a
corner setback of 5.58 feet along Hamptondale Avenue, rather than the required
19.3-foot corner setback, resulting in a variation of 13.72 feet (71.09%); and
(d) a variation from the rear yard setback requirements of Section 17.30.070 of
the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, to allow a setback of 11.96 feet along the west
property line, rather than the required 19.8-foot setback, resulting in a
variation of 7.84 feet (39.59%), all of said variations being to allow the
construction of additions to the north and south sides of the residence and the
connection of the existing detached garage to the residence, all as depicted in
the plans and elevations dated 12-12-02.
   SECTION 3: That the variations hereby granted are conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 4th day of March, 2003, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, and Presser
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    Trustees Powell and Woodbury
   Signed:
   s/s Michael F. Duhl
    Village President
Countersigned:
s/s Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: February 18, 2003
Posted: February 19, 2003
Passed and Approved: March 4, 2003
Posted: March 5, 2003
ORDINANCE NO. M-8-2003
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A SPECIAL USE PERMIT AND VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF
THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA, COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (310
Green Bay Road)
   WHEREAS, the North Shore Country Day School is the owner of the following
described real estate (the “Subject Property”):
Block 5,6,7 and 8 and vacated Diller Street (except from said Block 8, that
part conveyed to Village of Winnetka, by Document #9200542, and except street)
all in John C. Garland’s Addition to Winnetka, in the Southwest Quarter of
Section 21, Township 42 North, Range 13, East of the Third Principal Meridian
in the Village of Winnetka, Cook County, Illinois.
commonly known 310 Green Bay Road, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
5 Single Family Residential Zoning District, provided in Chapter 17.12 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code; and
   WHEREAS, on November 14, 2002, the North Shore Country Day School (the
"Applicant") filed an application for a special use permit pursuant to Section
17.12.020(C)(2) of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, to allow the expansion and
reconfiguration of its existing facilities, consisting of (a) the addition of
19.323 square feet of floor area in campus buildings by adding a second story
to the existing library and (b) the construction of a 1,300 square-foot lobby,
stairway and elevator addition along the side of the building facing Elder
Lane; and
   WHEREAS, on November 14, 2002, the Applicant also filed an application for a
variation from Section 17.30.040 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance to allow a
maximum building size of 206,593 square feet, which exceeds the maximum
allowable building size of 144,365 square feet, resulting in a variation
request of 62, 228 square feet (43.1%); and
   WHEREAS, on December 18, 2002, on due notice thereof, the Plan Commission
met to consider the proposed building addition and related changes to the
existing special use and, by the unanimous vote of the 11 members then in
attendance, found the special use, as modified by the proposed building and
site plans, to be consistent with Winnetka 2020, the Winnetka Comprehensive
Plan; and
   WHEREAS, on December 9, 2002, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested special use and variations
and, by the unanimous vote of the five members then present, has recommended
that the requested variation and special use be granted; and
   WHEREAS, on December 19, 2002, the Design Review Board reviewed the proposed
building and site alterations and issued favorable comment; and
   WHEREAS, North Shore Country Day School is a private school that has been
located on the Subject Property since 1919, prior to the enactment of the
Village’s Zoning Ordinance; and
   WHEREAS, North Shore Country Day School has served grades 1 through 12 since
its inception; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property has five street frontages: Willow Road on the
north; Green Bay Road and Church Road, which form an angled east lot line that
extends due southeast; Elder Lane along the south; and Forest Street along the
west; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property is a 16 acre campus improved with buildings
containing classrooms for lower grades, middle grades and upper grades, an arts
center, a cafeteria, a library, a gymnasium, an auditorium, athletic fields,
and parking and student drop-off areas; and
   WHEREAS, most of the buildings on the campus are located at the south end of
the Subject Property, with the largest concentration at the southwest corner,
along Elder Lane and Forest Street; and
   WHEREAS, the School has an enrollment of approximately 450 students, with
approximately one third of the students being residents of Winnetka and
approximately two thirds residing within the boundaries of the New Trier High
School District; and
   WHEREAS, private schools having a curriculum equivalent to a public
elementary or public high school are permitted special uses in the R-5 Single
Family Residential Zoning District, pursuant to Section 17.12.020(C) of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance; and
   WHEREAS, pursuant to Section 17.56(K) of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, the
North Shore Country Day School is a lawful special use; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed addition will add a second floor above the existing,
one-story library building; and
   WHEREAS, the existing science facilities are 25 years old and do not have
sufficient space and equipment to meet the needs of the hands-on, discovery-
based approach to science education that is the result of advances in
technology and teaching methods over the last quarter of a century; and
   WHEREAS, the new lobby and entrance proposed for the south side of the
building will have an area of approximately 1300 square feet and will allow the
lower school and middle school offices to be located near the main entrance
areas, thereby improving internal flow, accessibility and building
accessibility; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed school expansion is consistent with the goal of the
Winnetka Comprehensive Plan, Winnetka 2020, of supporting educational
excellence and the enrichment of Winnetka’s religious and cultural environment;
and.
   WHEREAS, the proposed school expansion is consistent with the goal of the
Winnetka Comprehensive Plan, Winnetka 2020, of limiting commercial,
institutional and residential development within the Village to minimize the
potentially adverse impacts on adjacent residential neighborhoods and to
prevent the need for significant increases in the Village’s infrastructure and
other community resources; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed school expansion is consistent with the goal of the
Winnetka Comprehensive Plan, Winnetka 2020, of ensuring that commercial,
institutional, and residential development is appropriate to the character of
and minimizes the adverse impact on its surrounding neighborhood; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed school expansion is consistent with the objective of
the Winnetka Comprehensive Plan, Winnetka 2020, of protecting residential
neighborhoods and homes from the encroachment of incompatible land uses and
traffic patterns; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed school expansion is consistent with the objective of
the Winnetka Comprehensive Plan, Winnetka 2020, of maintaining the quiet
ambience of residential neighborhoods; and 
   WHEREAS, the proposed school expansion is consistent with the objective of
the Winnetka Comprehensive Plan, Winnetka 2020, of recognizing the critical
importance of educational, religious and other community institutions to
Village residents; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed school expansion is consistent with the objective of
the Winnetka Comprehensive Plan, Winnetka 2020, of maintaining an atmosphere in
which diverse cultural, educational and religious organizations may flourish;
and
   WHEREAS, subject to the terms and conditions of this ordinance, the proposed
school expansion is consistent with the objective of the Winnetka Comprehensive
Plan, Winnetka 2020, of engaging in a public process that balances
institutional goals and minimizes adverse impacts on the character of the
adjacent residential neighborhood; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed school expansion is consistent with the objective of
the Winnetka Comprehensive Plan, Winnetka 2020, of recognizing that standards
of educational excellence may change with time, thus necessitating changes in
physical resources; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed school expansion is consistent with the objectives of
the Winnetka Comprehensive Plan, Winnetka 2020, of ensuring safe and attractive
access to educational and community institutions and of pursuing improvements
that address public safety as well as traffic, congestion and parking; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed school expansion is consistent with the objective of
the Winnetka Comprehensive Plan, Winnetka 2020, of encouraging the preservation
of open space inside and outside the Village; and
   WHEREAS, subject to the terms and conditions of this ordinance, the proposed
school expansion is consistent with the recommendation of the Winnetka
Comprehensive Plan, Winnetka 2020, of encouraging governmental and non-
governmental institutions to work with their constituents, neighbors and the
Village to minimize the impact of traffic and parking on surrounding
residential streets and to develop on-site solutions where appropriate; and
   WHEREAS, subject to the terms and conditions of this ordinance, the proposed
school expansion is consistent with the objective of the Winnetka Comprehensive
Plan, Winnetka 2020, of ensuring that proposals don’t have an adverse impact on
the residential character of the surrounding residential neighborhoods; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed expansion of the existing special use will neither
endanger nor be detrimental to the public health, safety, comfort, morals or
general welfare, in that: (a) the building expansion and renovations will bring
it into full compliance with the Americans with Disabilities Act, (b) the new
lobby entrance at the south of the building will improve building security, (c)
the second floor addition is intended to meet the needs of the School for an
up-to-date science learning facility to accommodate its existing enrollment;
and (d) the building expansion will address security issues raised in a
facilities evaluation conducted by the School as required by the Independent
Schools Association of the Central States; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed expansion of the existing
special use will either substantially diminish or impair property values in the
immediate vicinity or be substantially injurious to the use and enjoyment of
land in the immediate vicinity for uses permitted by right in that zoning
district, in that (a) the neighborhood around the School has developed, without
impediment, in the presence of the School, and (b) the proposed building
expansion lies almost entirely within the existing building footprint and is
intended to accommodate the existing enrollment; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed expansion of the existing special use will not impede
the normal and orderly development and improvement of other property in the
immediate vicinity for uses permitted by right in the zoning district, in that
the School has been located in the neighborhood for over eighty years, during
which time the neighborhood has steadily and fully developed; and
   WHEREAS, adequate measures have been or will be taken to provide ingress and
egress in a manner that minimizes pedestrian and vehicular traffic congestion
in the public ways, in that (a) the proposed building expansion is limited
almost entirely to the existing building footprint, (b) the proposed building
expansion does not encroach into or otherwise interfere with existing parking
areas, and (c) the proposed building expansion does not alter the established
patterns of ingress and egress; and
   WHEREAS, adequate parking, utilities, access roads, drainage and other
facilities necessary for the operation of the expanded special use either exist
or will be provided, in that (a) the proposed expansion is intended to
accommodate the existing enrollment and staff, and (b) existing parking,
utilities, established access drives, drainage and other infrastructure for the
existing use will not be altered; and
   WHEREAS,  subject to the terms and conditions of this ordinance, the special
use, as expanded by the proposed building expansion, conforms in all other
respects to the applicable zoning regulations and other applicable Village
ordinances and Codes; and
   WHEREAS, the property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted to be
used only under the conditions allowed by the zoning regulations, in that (a)
the Subject Property has been established as the site of a school facility
since 1919, and (b) the purpose of the proposed addition is to upgrade and
update science facilities and to improve building security, both of which will
help to maintain the school’s academic reputation and assure long term
viability of the School’s academic programs and physical plant; and
   WHEREAS, the plight of the applicant is due to unique circumstances, in that
(a) the irregular shape and topography of the Subject Property have lead to the
development of the property with most of its buildings clustered at the
southwest corner, (b) the maximum building size limitations of the R-5 Zoning
District are based on single-family residential standards, and (c) strict
application of the maximum building size limitations would prevent the
construction and expansion of a school building, notwithstanding the express
statement of the Zoning Ordinance that makes schools permitted uses in the R-
5 Zoning District; and
   WHEREAS, the variation, if granted, will not alter the essential character
of the neighborhood, in that (a) the School has been an established fixture in
the neighborhood since 1919, (b) the proposed science facility addition will
add a second floor to the existing building, and (c) the proposed science
addition has been designed to be obscured to the extent possible from adjoining
properties, thereby maintaining the established appearance of the facilities;
and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not impair an adequate supply of light
and air because (a) the second floor addition is nestled among the existing
two-story buildings, (b) both additions are set well back from the existing
building lines that face Elder Lane and Forest Street, and (c) the proposed
height of the additions will be no greater than the surrounding two-story
campus buildings; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property because the building additions (a) will
comply with all applicable building and fire protection codes and (b) will
include the installation of a fire suppression system; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings throughout the Village, because the proposed additions (a) will
not have a significant impact on the appearance of the School’s campus in the
neighborhood, (b) will not generate additional traffic and (c) will improve the
condition and appearance of the Subject Property while not interfering with the
use of other properties in the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed building additions will not increase congestion in the
public streets because (a) the proposed building additions are designed to
accommodate the existing enrollment, and (b) neither the parking areas nor
campus access patterns will be changed; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed building additions will
otherwise impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, subject to the terms and conditions of this ordinance, the
requested variation is in harmony with the general purpose and intent of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that the building additions that require the
variation will: (a) foster the goals and policies of the Comprehensive Plan,
(b) protect the scale and character of existing neighborhood from the
encroachment of incompatible uses and structures having excessive bulk or
inadequate open space, (c) assure that the buildings and structures are
compatible with the character of the zoning district, (d) conform with building
height and setback requirements, (e) preserve the existing open space on the
Subject Property, (f) assure the development and redevelopment of the
established neighborhood in a manner consistent with its established character
by assuring that the building addition maintains its existing scale and
appearance in the neighborhood and protects established trees and landscaping,
and (g) protect the justifiable reliance of the Village’s taxpayers, in
general, and the residents in the immediate vicinity of the Subject Property,
in particular, on the continuation of existing, established land use patterns.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : The foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated into this section
as the findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set
forth herein.
   SECTION 2: Pursuant to Section 17.12.020(C)(2) of the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance and subject to the terms and conditions hereinafter set forth, a
special use permit is hereby granted to the Subject Property, commonly known
North Shore Country Day School, located at 310 Green Bay Road Winnetka,
Illinois, and located in the R-5 Single-Family Residential Zoning District
provided in the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, to allow the expansion and
reconfiguration of its existing facilities, consisting of (a) the addition of
19,323 square feet of floor area in campus buildings by adding a second story
to the existing library and (b) the construction of a 1,300 square-foot lobby,
stairway and elevator addition along the side of the building facing Elder
Lane, as depicted in the plans and elevations submitted with the application.
   SECTION 3: Subject to the terms and conditions hereinafter set forth, a
variation from Section 17.30.040 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance is hereby
granted to the Subject Property, commonly known North Shore Country Day School,
located at 310 Green Bay Road Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
5 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance, to allow a maximum building size of 206,593 square feet, which
exceeds the maximum allowable building size of 144,365 square feet, resulting
in a variation of 62, 228 square feet (43.1%), all to allow (a) the addition of
19,323 square feet of floor area in campus buildings by adding a second story
to the existing library and (b) the construction of a 1,300 square-foot lobby,
stairway and elevator addition along the side of the building facing Elder
Lane, as depicted in the plans and elevations submitted with the application.
   SECTION 4: The special use and variations granted herein are subject to the
following conditions:
      A.   The proposed construction shall commence within 12 months after the
effective date of this Ordinance.
      B.   Prior to the issuance of any building permits, the North Shore
Country Day School shall enter into a development agreement with the Village
that shall contain provisions regarding: (1) construction traffic and staging
areas; (2) parking for construction workers; (3) parking and traffic patterns
for staff parking and student drop-off and pick-up during the construction
period; (4) ingress and egress for fires and other emergencies that may occur
while the building is under construction; and (5) any of the topics set forth
in Section 15.32.080.K of the Winnetka Village Code that apply to the
circumstances of the applicant’s project.
      C.   Final design approval of building, landscaping, lighting and signage
      D.   The North Shore Country Day School shall extend its best efforts to
communicate with residents whose homes are within 250 feet of the Subject
Property (the Neighbors), in a good-faith effort to develop a plan for managing
and, to the extent reasonably possible, reducing the impact of school-related
traffic along Elder Lane and Forest Street. The School shall report the results
of such efforts for consideration by the Council at the same time that the
final development agreement is presented to the Council for its consideration.
The Council reserves the right, in the exercise of its discretion, to consider
and impose requirements pertaining to taking final action on the development
agreement.
   SECTION 5 : The stipulations, conditions and restrictions set forth in the
foregoing Section 4 of this Ordinance may be modified or revised from time to
time by the Village Council following public notice and hearing, following the
procedures specified in Section 17.56 of the Winnetka Village Code for
processing special use applications.
   SECTION 6: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 18th day of February, 2003, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Aquilino, Greenough, Powell, Presser and Woodbury
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    Trustee Brower (recused)
   Signed:
   s/s Michael F. Duhl
    Village President
Countersigned:
s/s Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: February 4, 2003
Posted: February 5, 2003
Passed and Approved: February 18, 2003
Posted: February 19, 2003
ORDINANCE NO. M-9-2003
AN ORDINANCE ESTABLISHING 94 MARY STREET AS A DESIGNATED LANDMARK
   WHEREAS, the Village of Winnetka desires to identify, designate and preserve
buildings and structures in the Village that are historically, culturally, and
architecturally significant and to encourage the restoration and rehabilitation
of those buildings and structures; and
   WHEREAS, pursuant to Sections 11-48.2-1, et seq. of the Illinois Municipal
Code (65 ILCS 5/11-48.2-1, et seq.), the Council of the Village of Winnetka
enacted Chapter 15.64 of Title 15 of the Winnetka Village Code, entitled
“Landmark Preservation,” for the purposes of preserving, protecting, enhancing,
rehabilitating and regulating buildings, structures, objects, and places of
historical, cultural or architectural importance; and
   WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka have determined that it is
in the best interest of the Village and its residents that participation in the
landmark designation process shall be limited to those owners who voluntarily
elect to participate in that process; and
   WHEREAS, pursuant to the provisions of Chapter 15.64 of the Winnetka Village
Code (the “Landmark Ordinance”) and notice duly published and sent, the
Landmark Preservation Commission conducted a public hearing on September 16,
2002, to consider the application of the property owners for landmark
designation of the property located at 94 Mary Street permanent real estate
index number 05-08-400-037-000 (“Subject Property”); and
   WHEREAS, the Landmark Preservation Commission applied the Village’s System
for the Evaluation of Landmarks to the information received at the hearing and
made findings regarding the property at 94 Mary Street, as hereinafter set
forth; and
   WHEREAS, the Landmark Preservation Commission found that the architectural
type is extremely rare, in that: (a) the residence is one of four residences
remaining in Winnetka designed by architect, Howard Van Doren Shaw in the
English arts and crafts country house style with the interior arranged as an
enfilade of rooms around a common gallery; (b) the residence was built in 1909
and its exterior is composed of red brick with limestone elements, a slate
roof, projecting chimneys and windows, a front façade with a recessed loggia on
the first and second floors, and wooden sleeping porches at the rear of the
home where a 100-foot terrace with wrought iron balustrade faces the garden and
Lake Michigan; (c) the exterior also incorporates many details including
limestone fruit motifs which are also repeated throughout the interior of the
house and two large “H’s” representing the owner’s initials at the front entry;
(d) the residence retains ornamental wooden detailing, including (i) ornamental
wooden railings found at the second floor loggia, on the roof of the servants’
entrance, and at both sleeping porches, (ii) an ornamental wooden widow’s walk
balustrade at the third floor, and (iii) original wooden dentil moldings under
the eaves along the perimeter of the house; and
   WHEREAS, the Landmark Preservation Commission further found that the method
of construction is extremely rare, in that: (a) it is a red brick masonry
structure with limestone detailing, slate roof, limestone balustrade and
wrought iron railings; (b) other than the north porch windows, all of the
double hung sash windows are original; and (c) the exterior and interior retain
the finest materials available at the time the house was constructed; and
   WHEREAS, the Landmark Preservation Commission further found that the Subject
Property is associated with architect, Howard Van Doren Shaw, and landscape
architect, Jens Jensen, both outstanding contributors to early 20th century
architecture with reputations of national significance; and
   WHEREAS, the Landmark Preservation Commission further found that the Subject
Property is not easily visible from the street, but is a symbol of the Village
as a whole in that historic documentation illustrates how this house
contributed to Winnetka’s reputation as a desirable location for great country
houses and encouraged the types of residences that followed; and
   WHEREAS, the Landmark Preservation Commission further found that the Subject
Property should be rated as excellent for originality and design integrity
because, with the exception of certain portions of the sleeping porch, every
visible exterior feature is either original or restored to original condition;
and
   WHEREAS, based on its findings, the Landmark Preservation Commission has
concluded that the residence located at 94 Mary Street is a unique property due
to (a) its construction; (b) its architect and landscape architect; (c) the
faithfulness to its original condition; (d) its appearance on the site and to a
lesser extent in its neighborhood;
   WHEREAS, the Landmark Preservation Commission further found that the Subject
Property meets the criteria of the Landmark Ordinance and has unanimously
recommended that it be so designated; and
   WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka have considered the
recommendation of the Landmark Preservation Commission and have determined that
it is in the best interest of the Village and its residents to establish the
house located at 94 Mary Street as a designated landmark.
   NOW THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain:
   SECTION 1: That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2: That the property located at 94 Mary Street, permanent real
estate index number 05-08-400-037-000, is hereby designated a landmark under
the Landmark Ordinance, in accordance with the findings and recommendations of
the Landmark Preservation Commission.
   SECTION 3: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 4th day of March, 2003, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, and Presser
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    Trustees Powell and Woodbury
   Signed:
   s/s Michael F. Duhl
    Village President
Countersigned:
s/s Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: February 18, 2003
Posted: February 19, 2003
Passed and Approved: March 4, 2003
Posted: March 5, 2003
ORDINANCE NO. M-10-2003
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A SPECIAL USE PERMIT AND VARIATION FOR THE CONSTRUCTION
OF A GARAGE BUILDING FOR THE WINNETKA PARK DISTRICT (600 Hibbard)
   WHEREAS, the Winnetka Park District is the owner of the following described
real estate (the “Subject Property”):
The north ½ of the east ½ of the northeast ¼ of Section 19 (except the easterly
33 feet thereof), in Township 42 North, Range 13, East of the Third Principle
Meridian in the Village of Winnetka, Cook County, Illinois,
which is located in the R-2 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided
in Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code; and
   WHEREAS, on July 16, 2002, the Council adopted Ordinance M-22-2002, granting
the Winnetka Park District a special use permit to allow the construction of a
one-story addition for administrative offices along the north side of the
existing A.C. Nielsen Tennis Center building on the Subject Property at 540
Hibbard Road; and
   WHEREAS, the Winnetka Park District has now applied for a special use permit
under Section 17.56.010 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, to allow the
construction of a one-story garage building at the Skokie Playfield Service
Center located at the north end of the Subject Property and commonly known as
600 Hibbard Road; and
   WHEREAS, the Winnetka Park District has also applied for a variation from
the building height limitations of Section 17.30.080 of the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance, to allow the proposed garage structure to have a height of 21 feet,
which exceeds the maximum allowable height of 15 feet, resulting in a variation
request of 6 feet (40%); and
   WHEREAS, on July 22, 2002, pursuant to due notice thereof, the Zoning Board
of Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested special use and
variation and, by the unanimous vote of the six members of the Board then
present, has reported to the Council, recommending that the requested special
use and variation be granted; and
   WHEREAS, on June 20, 2002, on due notice thereof, the Design Review Board
considered the proposed building plans and issued generally favorable comment
on the proposed design, but also suggested that the Park District explore the
possibilities of breaking up the roof line, the opening of the gas bay or the
use of trees; and
   WHEREAS, on July 24, 2002, pursuant to due notice thereof, the Plan
Commission considered the special use application and seven of the eight
members then present, found that the proposed special use is consistent with
the Comprehensive Plan, Winnetka 2020; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed special use is consistent with the Comprehensive
Plan’s goal to preserve or expand the quantity, quality and distribution of
open space and recreational opportunities, and to protect the Village’s natural
features and environmental resources; and
   WHEREAS, the Council accept the findings of the Winnetka Plan Commission
that the proposed special use is consistent with the Winnetka Comprehensive
Plan’s Village Character and Appearance Objectives 6, 7, and 13; and
   WHEREAS, the Council accept the findings of the Winnetka Plan Commission
that the proposed special use is consistent with the Winnetka Comprehensive
Plan’s Village Parks, Open Space, Recreation and Environment Objectives 5, 6
and 9; and
   WHEREAS, the Council accept the findings of the Winnetka Plan Commission
that the proposed special use is consistent with the recommendation stated in
Section 4.3.6 of the Comprehensive Plan to ensure that proposals do not have an
adverse impact on the residential character of the surrounding residential
neighborhoods; and
   WHEREAS, the Council accept the findings of the Winnetka Plan Commission
that the proposed special use is consistent with the recommendation stated in
Section 4.3.6 of the Comprehensive Plan to cooperate with the Winnetka Park
District in achieving the District’s goal of providing Village residents with
high quality recreational programs and open space; and
   WHEREAS, the Council accept the findings of the Winnetka Plan Commission
that the proposed special use is consistent with the recommendation stated in
Section 4.3.6 of the Comprehensive Plan to work with the Park District to
minimize the impact of existing programs on adjacent neighborhoods; and
   WHEREAS, the Council accept the findings of the Winnetka Plan Commission
that the proposed special use is consistent with the recommendation stated in
Section 4.3.6 of the Comprehensive Plan to coordinate planning for any new
facilities and programs to balance recreational needs of the community with the
residential character of the surrounding neighborhood; and
   WHEREAS, the Council accept the findings of the Winnetka Plan Commission
that the proposed special use is consistent with the recommendation stated in
Section 4.3.6 of the Comprehensive Plan to ensure that street and parking
infrastructure are adequate and that other ancillary effects such as artificial
lighting, noise and water run-off are held to acceptable levels; and
   WHEREAS, the Council find that the existing Winnetka Park District
facilities and operations on the Subject Property, including the proposed
garage building, constitute a non-essential public use under Section 17.56.010
(B) of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed garage building is necessary to the operation of the
Winnetka Park District and the provision of services to Winnetka residents in
that it will replace the existing north and south garages and storage shed
located on the west side of the service center on the Subject Property,
providing enclosed storage for fertilizer, garage space for vehicles and
mowers, oil storage, ladder storage, a separate room for ball field maintenance
equipment and a rinsate pad that complies with State of Illinois Department of
Agriculture standards for lawn care containment; and
   WHEREAS, at the direction of the Village Council, and pursuant to the
request of neighboring property owners, the Park District has evaluated other
sites to determine whether either the existing service center can be moved to
another location in its entirety or the functions of the service center can be
divided among several other locations; and
   WHEREAS, the Park District staff has concluded that the overall concept
impacts the fewest residents, maintains a cost-reducing sharing of functions
between golf, park and ballfield staff, does not require rebuilding current
facilities in a new location and saves Park District funds by only replacing
two decaying and inadequate buildings rather than the entire facility; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed garage building will not be detrimental to the public
health, safety, comfort, morals or general welfare of the Village, in that the
new structure will consolidate the storage and maintenance areas for equipment
and supplies used principally in the adjoining golf course and playfields,
while conforming with applicable agricultural and environmental standards; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed garage building will not be substantially injurious to
the use and enjoyment of other property in the immediate vicinity, nor will it
substantially diminish or impair property values in the immediate vicinity, as
the garage building will replace three deteriorated, substandard buildings in
the service area of the Subject Property, thereby improving the appearance of
the service area portion of the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed garage building will not impede the normal and orderly
development or improvement of other property in the immediate vicinity for uses
permitted under applicable zoning regulations in that the service area of the
Subject Property is an established use, having first been constructed in the
1930’s, with further construction and expansions in 1962 and in the 1980’s,
without any negative impact on development of the adjacent single-family
residential area; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed garage building will not alter the amount of parking
on or the ingress or egress to the Subject Property, nor will it have a
negative impact to vehicular or pedestrian traffic on the surrounding street
system, as the employees will continue to use the existing parking areas; and
   WHEREAS, adequate parking, utilities, access roads, drainage and other
facilities necessary to the existing special use already exist on the Subject
Property; and
   WHEREAS, when constructed pursuant to the terms and conditions set forth in
this ordinance, the special use in all other respects conforms to the
applicable regulations of this and other Village ordinances and codes.
   WHEREAS, the plight of the applicant is unique in that (a) the proposed
garage building is a necessary accessory to the principal uses of the Subject
Property in meeting the Park District’s statutory obligation to provide
recreational facilities; (b) in maintaining it parks, ballfields and golf
course, the Park District is also required to comply with regulations of the
Illinois Department of Agriculture that do not apply to the general public
within the Village; and (c) although the proposed garage building is considered
an accessory building to the principal use of the Park District’s property, the
size of the Subject Property, the number of different activities on the Subject
Property and the separation of the service buildings from the other Park
District buildings located at 540 Hibbard Road makes the service area a
discrete portion of the Subject Property with buildings that are necessarily
larger than typical garages on single family residential properties; and
   WHEREAS, the strict application of the building height limitations create
practical difficulties and particular hardships for the Park District in that
the additional height is necessary (a) to accommodate all of the equipment and
vehicles that will be stored in the proposed garage; (b) to make the new garage
building’s appearance consistent with the appearance of the maintenance
building that will remain between the new garage building and Hibbard Road; and
(c) building more numerous, smaller buildings would increase both construction
costs and operational costs by dividing maintenance operations that are now
consolidated in a single location; and
   WHEREAS, the variation, if granted, will not alter the essential character
of the locality, in that: (a) the Subject Property and its service area at 600
Hibbard Road are established uses in the neighborhood, having been located
there since the 1930’s; (b) the proposed garage building will replace two
deteriorating buildings located on the west side of the service center and the
existing storage shed, greenhouse and maintenance building that are located
between the proposed new garage and Hibbard Road to the east, will remain; (c)
the part of the building facing the single family residential properties width
will be only 32 feet wide, and most of the bulk of the building will extend
southward, away from the single family residential properties; (d) the proposed
garage building will be set back 50 feet from the north property line, which is
a significantly greater distance than the setbacks that would be required if
the service area of the Subject Property were developed with a single family
residence; and (e) the proposed building height of 21 feet is substantially
lower than the 33-foot building height that would be allowed if the service
area of the Subject Property were developed with a single family residence
under the R-2 Zoning District regulations; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the applicable zoning
regulations, as the sole purpose of the Subject Property is to provide
recreational facilities as required by law; and
   WHEREAS, an adequate supply of light and air to adjacent property will not
be impaired, as the proposed garage building will have a larger setback and
lower height than would be permitted if the service area were developed with a
single family residence under the R-2 Zoning District regulations; and
   WHEREAS, the hazard from fire and other damages to the property will not be
increased, because the new garage will comply with applicable building and life
safety codes, and the new rinsate area will comply with Illinois Department of
Agriculture requirements; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the requested height variation will
diminish the taxable value of land and buildings throughout the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not increase congestion in the public
streets, as the proposed garage building will allow the continued, consolidated
storage and servicing of Park District vehicles and equipment on Park District
property; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not be detrimental to or endanger the
public health, safety, comfort, morals and welfare of the inhabitants of the
Village, because it will allow the Park District to replace deteriorated
buildings on the Subject Property with a single building that will impact the
fewest residents of the Village and maintain a cost-reducing sharing of
functions between the golf, park and ballfield staffs without requiring the
unnecessary relocation or rebuilding of other structures, while enabling the
Park District to upgrade its landscaping operations to meet the requirements of
the Illinois Department of Agriculture.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2:   That the Subject Property, which is located in the R-2 Single-
Family Residential District provided in Chapter 17.24 of the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, and which is commonly known
as the Winnetka Park District, is hereby granted a special use permit to allow
the construction of a one-story garage building at the north end of the Subject
Property in that part of the Subject Property commonly known as the Winnetka
Park District Service Center at 600 Hibbard Road
   SECTION 3:   That the Subject Property, which is located in the R-2 Single-
Family Residential District provided in Chapter 17.24 of the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, and which is commonly known
as the Winnetka Park District, is hereby granted the following variation for
that part of the Subject Property commonly known as the Winnetka Park District
Service Center at 600 Hibbard Road: a variation from the building height
limitations of Section 17.30.080 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, to allow the
proposed garage structure to have a height of 21 feet, which exceeds the
maximum allowable height of 15 feet, resulting in a variation request of 6 feet
(40%). Said variation is granted to allow the replacement of the existing
storage and service buildings located in the Service Center at the north end of
the Subject Property in accordance with the plans that accompanied the
application for variation and special use.
   SECTION 4: That the special use and variation hereby granted are subject to
the conditions that: (a) there shall be no parking along the service road or
adjacent to the fences along the north side of the service road; and (b) the
proposed construction shall commence within 12 months after the effective date
of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 5: That variation and special use hereby granted shall have no
effect on the special use granted pursuant to Ordinance M-22-2002.
   SECTION 6:   That, pursuant to Section 17.56.010(G) of the Zoning Ordinance,
all stipulations, conditions and restrictions set forth in this Ordinance as
part of the terms under which the special use is granted, may be modified or
revised from time to time by the Village Council following public notice and
hearing, using the same procedures set forth in the Zoning Ordinance for
processing the original special use application.
   SECTION 7:   This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 4th day of March, 2003, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    President Duhl, Trustees Aquilino, Greenough and Presser
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    Trustees Powell and Woodbury. Trustee Brower recused
   Signed:
   s/s Michael F. Duhl
    Village President
Countersigned:
s/s Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: November 5, 2003
Posted: November 6, 2003
Passed and Approved: March 4, 2003
Posted: March 5, 2003
ORDINANCE NO. M-11-2003
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (1110 Elm Street)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
The West 50 feet of Lot 10 in Block 6 in Groveland Addition to Winnetka, being
a subdivision in the Northwest Quarter (1/4) of Section 20, Township 42 North,
Range 13, East of the Third Principal Meridian, in Cook County, Illinois,
commonly known as 1110 Elm Street, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
5 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.12 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for the
following variations: (a) a variation from the maximum building size
limitations of Section 17.30.040 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance to allow a
gross floor area of 3,596.96 square feet, which exceeds the maximum allowable
gross floor area of 3,530.30 square feet by 66.66 square feet (1.89%); and (b)
a variation from the corner setback requirements of Section 17.30.050 to allow
an attached garage to observe a corner setback of 4.93 feet, rather than the
required minimum corner setback of 14 feet, resulting in a variation of 9.07
feet (64.78%), said variations being for the purpose of constructing a 33-foot
by 17.69-foot two-story room addition on the Subject Property and expanding the
width of the attached garage by 6.6 feet; and
   WHEREAS, on February 10, 2003, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variations and, by the
unanimous vote of the five members then present, has reported to the Council
recommending that the requested variations be granted; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and unique hardships associated
with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect
to the Subject Property in that: (a) the Subject Property is a corner lot,
located at the southeast corner of Elm Street and Glendale Avenue; (b) the
house and garage were built in their existing locations in 1909 and 1920,
respectively, prior to the enactment of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance; (c) the
house and garage are nonconforming structures with corner yard setbacks of 4.90
feet and 4.93 feet; (d) the two-story addition is in a conforming location; (e)
the owners propose to expand the width of the garage by 6.6 feet, from 17.40
feet wide to 24 feet wide; (f) the expanded garage will observe the same 4.93-
foot corner yard setback that the smaller, existing attached garage now
observes; (g) both the maximum building size and corner yard setback variations
are attributable to the garage expansion, which will add approximately 126.75
square feet of gross floor area; and (h) constructing a garage of the desired
size in a conforming location could be accomplished by building a new garage at
the rear of the lot and converting the existing attached garage into living
space, although it would result in substantially more bulk on the Subject
Property and would substantially increase the cost of the improvements proposed
by the owners; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that:
(a) the existing attached garage is 17.40 feet wide by 19.20 feet deep and
cannot accommodate two cars; (b) the proposed expansion of the garage to 24
feet wide by 19.21 feet deep will make the size of the garage consistent with
contemporary standards for two-car garages; and (c) the size of the proposed
two-car garage is consistent with the typical size of garages in the vicinity;
and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because the expansion of the garage is minimal and has been
designed to be consistent with the style of the existing house and with the
proposed two-story addition for a kitchen, family room and master bedroom
suite; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not impair an adequate supply of
light and air because the 6.6-foot expansion of the one-story garage faces
Glendale Avenue, and does not cast a shadow on any other property and does not
extend as far into the rear yard as the proposed, conforming two-story room
addition; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property because the proposed renovations will
include the removal of knob and tube wiring and will otherwise comply with all
applicable and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not diminish the taxable value of
land and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed two-story room addition and one-story garage expansion
will not contribute to congestion on the public streets, as the expanded garage
will be able to accommodate two cars; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed garage expansion and two-
story room addition will otherwise impair the public health, safety, comfort,
morals, and welfare of the inhabitants of the Village.
   WHEREAS, the requested variations are in harmony with the general purpose
and intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that they will allow the
renovation, restoration and rehabilitation of a structurally sound existing
building while maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the community
and protecting established trees and landscaping.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2:  The following variations are hereby granted to the Subject
Property, commonly known as 1110 Elm Street, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in
the R-5 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.12 of
the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code: (a) a
variation from the maximum building size limitations of Section 17.30.040 of
the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance to allow a gross floor area of 3,596.96 square
feet, which exceeds the maximum allowable gross floor area of 3,530.30 square
feet by 66.66 square feet (1.89%); and (b) a variation from the corner setback
requirements of Section 17.30.050 to allow an attached garage to observe a
corner setback of 4.93 feet, rather than the required minimum corner setback of
14 feet, resulting in a variation of 9.07 feet (64.78%), said variations being
for the purpose of constructing a 33-foot by 17.69-foot two-story room addition
on the Subject Property and expanding the width of the attached garage by 6.6
feet, all in accordance with the plans and elevations submitted with the
application for variation.
   SECTION 3: That the variations hereby granted are conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 1st day of March, 2003, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser and Woodbury
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    None
   Signed:
   s/s Michael F. Duhl
    Village President
Countersigned:
s/s Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
