ORDINANCE NO. M-12-2003
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (1213 Ash Street)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
Lot 23 in Block 3 in Winnetka Manor, a Subdivision of the South 45 acres of the
West 90 acres of the Northwest ¼ of Section 20, Township 42 North, Range 13,
East of the Third Principal Meridian, in Cook County, Illinois,
commonly known as 1213 Ash Street, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
5 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.12 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for the
following variations: (a) a variation from the building line articulation
requirements of Section 17.30.090 to allow a 21.93-foot addition to the
existing 44.1-foot long east wall to be articulated by only 4 inches, resulting
in a 66.03-foot long building wall with only 4 inches of articulation, rather
than the 18 inches of articulation required within 30 feet of the front corner
of the building; and (b) a variation from Section 17.64.010(A)5, which
prohibits structural changes to nonconforming buildings to allow the
enlargement of a kitchen window located in the nonconforming east wall; and
   WHEREAS, on February 10, 2003, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variations and, by the
unanimous vote of the five members then present, has reported to the Council
recommending that the requested variations be granted; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and unique hardships associated
with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect
to the Subject Property in that: (a) the Subject Property is an interior lot
located on the north side of Ash Street; (b) the single family residence on the
Subject Property was not built squared to the lot lines, as a result of which
the northeast corner of the building has a side yard setback of 5.83 feet,
while the southeast corner of the building has a side yard setback of 5.92
feet; (c) the house was built in 1951 before the articulation requirements were
added to the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance; (d) except for the required
articulation, the east wall of the proposed addition will have a conforming
side yard setback; (e) articulating the 21.93-foot long east wall of the
proposed room addition by more than 4 inches will prevent the interior walls of
the addition from aligning with the interior wall of the kitchen to which it
will be attached; (f) articulating the east wall of the 12.36-foot wide
proposed room addition by the required 18 inches would either make the addition
unreasonably narrow or require a corresponding shift of the west wall of the
addition, which would conflict with the existing bay window of the dining room
located in the south wall at the center of the rear of the residence; (g) the
existing nonconformity in the east building wall is 0.17 inches, or 2.8% of the
required 6-foot setback; (h) the proposed window enlargement will increase the
size of the kitchen window in the east wall of the kitchen from 4 feet by 3.5
feet to 6 feet by 3.5 feet; (i) the west building wall of the house on the
property immediately to the east of the Subject Property is not articulated,
although it is both longer and higher than the east wall dimensions proposed by
the applicants for the Subject Property; (j) the one-story height of the
proposed addition will provide vertical articulation on the east wall of
residence; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that:
(a) the existing nonconformity in the east side yard setback cannot be cured
without requiring the removal of the existing building; (b) the enlarged window
will provide additional daylight to the kitchen; (c) the one-story addition
will allow the expansion of the small, narrow kitchen and the addition of a
family room; (d) family room additions are typically adjacent to the kitchen
and the proposed family room cannot be relocated to another, conforming part of
the property without reconfiguring the interior of the existing residence; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because the one-story addition at the rear of the building
will not be visible from the street, and the expanded house will not extend as
far into the rear yard as the houses on either of the adjoining parcels of
property; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not impair an adequate supply of
light and air because the proposed addition is a single story, which is lower
than the residence on the property to the east, and the enlarged window will
provide additional light and air for the kitchen in the home on the Subject
Property without intruding into the light or air of the neighboring property;
and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property as the proposed work will comply with all
applicable building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not diminish the taxable value of
land and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed room addition and enlarged kitchen window will not
contribute to congestion on the public streets, as the use of the house will
not be changed; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed room addition and enlarged
kitchen window will otherwise impair the public health, safety, comfort,
morals, and welfare of the inhabitants of the Village.
   WHEREAS, the requested variations are in harmony with the general purpose
and intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that they will allow the
renovation, restoration and rehabilitation of a structurally sound existing
building while maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the community
and protecting established trees and landscaping.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2: That the following variations are hereby granted to the Subject
Property, commonly known as 1213 Ash Street, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in
the R-5 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.12 of
the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code: (a) a
variation from the building line articulation requirements of Section 17.30.090
to allow a 21.93-foot addition to the existing 44.1-foot long east wall to be
articulated by only 4 inches, resulting in a 66.03-foot long building wall with
only 4 inches of articulation, rather than the 18 inches of articulation
required within 30 feet of the front corner of the building; and (b) a
variation from Section 17.64.010(A)5, which prohibits structural changes to
nonconforming buildings to allow the enlargement of a kitchen window located in
the nonconforming east wall, said variations being in accordance with the plans
and elevations submitted with the application for variation.
   SECTION 3: That the variations hereby granted are conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 18th day of March, 2003, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser and Woodbury
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    None
   Signed:
   s/s Michael F. Duhl
    Village President
Countersigned:
s/s Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: March 4, 2003
Posted: March 5, 2003
Passed and Approved: March 18, 2003
Posted: March 19, 2003
ORDINANCE NO. M-13-2003
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (898 Elm Street)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
   Lot 6 (except the East 33 feet thereof) and the East 17 feet of Lot 7 in
Block 5, in Provident Mutual Land Association Subdivision of Blocks 7 to 12
inclusive, 28 to 33 inclusive and 54 to 59 inclusive in the Village of
Winnetka, a Subdivision of the West Half of the Northeast ¼ of Section 20,
Township 42 North, Range 13, East of the Third Principal Meridian, in Cook
County, Illinois,
commonly known as 898 Elm Street, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
5 single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.12 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for the
following variations: (a) a variation from the intensity of use of lot
limitations of Section 17.30.030 to allow a maximum roofed lot coverage of
1,851.57 square feet, which exceeds the maximum allowable roofed lot coverage
of 1,595.57 feet (16.08%); and (b) a variation from the side yard setback
requirements of Section 17.30.060, to allow a west side yard of 5.82 feet,
rather than the required 8.0 feet, resulting in a variation of 2.18 feet
(27.25%), both variations to allow the construction of a one-story family room
addition at the rear of the one-story residence; and
   WHEREAS, on February 10, 2003, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variations and, by the
unanimous vote of the five members then present, has reported to the Council
recommending that the requested variation be granted; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and unique hardships associated
with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect
to the Subject Property in that: (a) the Subject Property is a nonconforming
lot, having a width of approximately 34 feet, rather than the 60-foot average
lot width required in the R-5 zoning district; (b) the Subject Property is the
middle lot of three lots of approximately equal width that were carved by metes
and bounds from two 50-foot wide platted lots in the early 1900’s; (c) the home
on the Subject Property was constructed in 1916, prior to the initial enactment
of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance; (d) the proposed family room addition
measures 16.35 feet by 14 feet and will replace an existing wood deck at the
rear of the house; (e) the proposed one-story addition will adjoin the south
wall of the kitchen located at the south end of the house, and will have walls
that align with the existing kitchen’s walls; and (f) due to the narrow width
of both the lot and the house, and further due to the interior configuration of
the house, there is no conforming location for a family room addition; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that:
(a) the existing house is relatively small, with a gross floor area of 1,252.72
square feet, which is 56.3% of the gross floor area that is allowed on the
Subject Property; (b) the existing house lacks a family room, which is a
standard feature of contemporary homes in the Village; and (c) the existing
roofed lot coverage exceeds the maximum allowable roofed lot coverage, which
would preclude the construction of any addition unless a variation is granted;
and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because the one-story family room addition is at the rear of
the house and maintains the view of the building from the street, thus
preserving the appearance of the Subject Property in relation to the houses on
the two adjoining parcels, which were also built around the time the three
parcels were created; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not impair an adequate supply of
light and air because of the one-story height and modest size of the family
room addition; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property as they will comply with all applicable
building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not diminish the taxable value of
land and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed family room addition will not contribute to congestion
on the public streets, as the use of the Subject Property will not change; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed family room addition will
otherwise impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village.
   WHEREAS, the requested variations are in harmony with the general purpose
and intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that they will allow the
renovation, restoration and rehabilitation of a structurally sound existing
building while maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the community
and protecting established trees and landscaping.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2: That the following variations are hereby granted to the Subject
Property, commonly known as 898 Elm Street, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in
the R-5 single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.12 of
the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code: (a) a
variation from the intensity of use of lot limitations of Section 17.30.030 to
allow a maximum roofed lot coverage of 1,851.57 square feet, which exceeds the
maximum allowable roofed lot coverage of 1,595.57 feet (16.08%); and (b) a
variation from the side yard setback requirements of Section 17.30.060, to
allow a west side yard of 5.82 feet, rather than the required 8.0 feet,
resulting in a variation of 2.18 feet (27.25%), both variations to allow the
construction of a one-story family room addition at the rear of the one-story
residence, all in accordance with the plans and elevations submitted with the
application.
   SECTION 3: That the variations hereby granted are conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 18th day of March, 2003, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser and Woodbury
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    None
   Signed:
   s/s Michael F. Duhl
    Village President
Countersigned:
s/s Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: March 4, 2003
Posted: March 5, 2003
Passed and Approved: March 18, 2003
Posted: March 19, 2003
ORDINANCE NO. M-14-2003
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A ZONING VARIATION AND SPECIAL USE PERMIT TO ALLOW THE
RENOVATION OF TOWER ROAD PARK (901 Sheridan Road)
   WHEREAS, the Winnetka Park District is the owner of the following described
real estate (“Parcel 1”):
That part of Lot C lying Northwesterly of a line parallel and 200 ft.
Southeasterly from the Northwesterly line of said Lot C in the Resubdivision of
Lots 3, 4 and 5 in the Subdivision of Blocks 15 and 16 in the Hubbard Estates
Subdivision in the Northeast Fractional ¼ of Section 17, Township 42 North,
Range 13 East of the Third Principal Meridian, in Cook County, Illinois.
   WHEREAS, pursuant to a lease dated 1942, the Winnetka Park District, is the
lessee of the following described real estate, which is owned by the Village of
Winnetka (“Parcel 2”); and
All that part of Lots 1, 2 and 3 of Chandler’s Subdivision, being a subdivision
in Block 15 of Hubbard’s Estate Subdivision in the Northeast Fractional Quarter
of Section 17, Township 42 North, Range 13 East of the Third Principal
Meridian, in Cook County, Illinois, as shown upon the plat thereof recorded in
the Recorder’s Office of said Cook County as Document Number 5849038, and all
accretions thereto, lying northerly and easterly of the following described
line:
Beginning at a point in the Easterly line of Sheridan Road, (being the westerly
line of said Chandler’s Subdivision) 30 feet southerly from (measured at right
angles to) the Northerly line of said Chandler’s Subdivision; running thence
Northeasterly in a straight line to a point 36 feet Southeasterly from
(measured at right angles to) the said Northerly line of said Chandler’s
Subdivision at a point 180 feet (as measured along said Northerly line of said
Chandler’s Subdivision) from the Easterly line of Sheridan Road; thence
Northeasterly in a straight line to a point 51 feet from (measured at right
angles to) the said Northerly line of said Chandler’s Subdivision at a point
255 feet (as measured along said Northerly line of said Chandler’s Subdivision)
from the Easterly line of Sheridan Road; thence Northeasterly in a straight
line to a point 55 feet from (measured at right angles to) the said northerly
line of said Chandler’s Subdivision at a point 357.5 feet (as measured along
said Northerly line of said Chandler’s Subdivision) from the Easterly line of
Sheridan Road; thence Southeasterly in a straight line to a point on the
Northerly line of said Lot 2 of said Chandler’s Subdivision 370 feet (as
measured along the said Northerly line of said Lot 2 from the Easterly line of
Sheridan Road; thence Northeasterly along the said Northerly line of said Lot 2
to a point in the said Northerly line of said Lot 2 545 feet (as measured along
said Northerly line of said Lot 2) from the Easterly line of Sheridan Road;
thence Easterly in a straight line to a point in the Northerly line of said Lot
3 of said Chandler’s Subdivision, 770 feet from the intersection of the
Northerly line of said Lot 3 with the North line of Tower Road; thence
Southerly in a straight line bearing South 36 degrees 30 minutes East, 62 feet;
thence Northeasterly in a straight line bearing North 53 degrees 30 minutes
East, 225 feet (more or less) to the waters edge.
   WHEREAS, Parcels 1 and 2 are contiguous parcels of property and are commonly
known, jointly, as Tower Road Park, 901 Sheridan Road, Winnetka, Illinois
(“Subject Property”); and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property is located in the R-2 Single-Family
Residential District provided in Chapter 17.24 of the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code; and,
   WHEREAS, Tower Road Park is a public park and beach that include
landscaping, walkways, stairways, fencing, lighting and a parking area at the
top of the bluff, and a playground, boardwalk and beach along the shore of Lake
Michigan; and
   WHEREAS, Tower Road Park is a non-essential public use, permitted as a
special use in all zoning districts, subject to the conditions and requirements
pertaining to special uses, as set forth in Chapter 17.56 of the Winnetka
Zoning Ordinance; and
   WHEREAS, the Winnetka Park District has submitted an application to the
Village of Winnetka seeking a special use permit pursuant to said Chapter
17.56, to permit the renovation of the existing playground at Tower Road Park
on the southwest section of the beach, by adding a tot playground to the east
of the existing boardwalk, and a playground for older children to the west of
the boardwalk, closer to the beach house; and
   WHEREAS, the Winnetka Park District has also filed an application for a
variation from the side yard setback provisions of Section 17.30.060 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, to allow playground equipment in the area that is
bisected by the lot line between Parcel 1 and Parcel 2 without observing a side
yard setback, resulting in a variation of 100%; and
   WHEREAS, pursuant to due notice thereof, on February 20, 2003, the Design
Review Board issued favorable comment for the proposed renovation of the
playground area; and
   WHEREAS, on February 26, 2003, pursuant to due notice thereof, the members
of the Plan Commission then present voted unanimously to find the special use
permit application for playground renovations consistent with the Winnetka
Comprehensive Plan; and
   WHEREAS, the Zoning Board of Appeals held a public hearing on March 10,
2003, and, by the unanimous vote of the six members then present recommended
that the variation be granted; and
   WHEREAS, pursuant to a corrected notice, the ZBA held a second public
hearing on the Park District’s application on April 14, 2003, and, by the
unanimous vote of the six members then present, has recommended that the
variation be granted and, by the favorable vote of five of the six members then
present, has recommended that the special use permit be granted; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed new playgrounds and playground equipment on the
Subject Property will benefit the public health, safety, comfort, morals and
general welfare of the Village, by improving the safety, accessibility and
appearance of the playgrounds by installing new playground equipment, in
accordance with applicable requirements of the Americans with Disabilities Act
and national playground safety standards; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed special use will not be substantially injurious to the
use and enjoyment of other property in the immediate vicinity, nor will it
substantially diminish or impair property values in the immediate vicinity,
because the park is established in the neighborhood, and the renovation of the
park: (a) will not change the existing use; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed special use will not impede the normal and orderly
development or improvement of other property in the immediate vicinity for uses
permitted under applicable zoning regulations, because the park is a long-
established use in the neighborhood and neither the type nor intensity of use
of the Subject Property will be changed; and
   WHEREAS, adequate measures have been taken to provide ingress and egress in
a manner that minimizes pedestrian and vehicular traffic congestion, in that
the existing pedestrian circulation routes through the park, points of access
to the Subject Property and parking will not be changed, and there will be no
changes to vehicular access; and
   WHEREAS, adequate parking, access roads, utilities, drainage and other
facilities necessary to the operation of the special use exist and no
additional drainage is necessary as the site is primarily beach sand; and
   WHEREAS, the special use in all other respects conforms to the applicable
regulations of this and other Village ordinances and codes;
   WHEREAS, the plight of the applicant is unique in that the play equipment is
a necessary accessory to the principal uses of the Subject Property in meeting
the statutory obligation of the Winnetka Park District to provide recreational
facilities; and
   WHEREAS, the strict application of the side yard setback requirements
creates practical difficulties and particular hardships for the applicant in
that placing the equipment in a conforming location: (a) would require the
center of the Subject Property to remain open; and (b) would reduce the size of
the playground area; and
   WHEREAS, the variation, if granted, will not alter the essential character
of the locality, in that the park and its playground are established uses in
the neighborhood, and the proposed improvements will replace play equipment
currently located in the same general area of the Subject Property, which is on
the beach and is not visible from Sheridan Road; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the applicable zoning
regulations, as the sole purpose of the property is to provide recreational
facilities as required by law; and
   WHEREAS, an adequate supply of light and air to adjacent property will not
be impaired, as there are no other residential properties nearby and the
playground equipment is located on the beach; and
   WHEREAS, the hazard from fire and other damages to the property will not be
increased, because the new equipment will be in an open site, and the new
equipment and playground surfaces will meet all applicable life safety codes
and recreational equipment safety standards; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the taxable value of land and buildings
throughout the Village will diminish; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed renovation of the playground equipment will not
increase congestion in the public streets, as the use of the Subject Property
will not change; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed new playground equipment will not be detrimental to or
endanger the public health, safety, comfort, morals and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village, because the upgraded playground areas will be in
full compliance with current safety standards and guidelines, will meet
accessibility requirements of the Americans with Disabilities Act, and will
provide additional seating for parents, caregivers and children.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain:
   SECTION 1: That the Village Council hereby adopts by reference the foregoing
recitals as its findings as if those recitals were fully set forth herein.
   SECTION 2: That, pursuant to Section 17.56.010 of the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, a special use permit is
hereby granted with respect to the Subject Property, which comprises Parcel 1
and Parcel 2, described above, is located in the R-2 Single-Family Residential
Zoning District and is commonly known as the Tower Road Park, 901 Sheridan
Road, to permit renovation of the Subject Property by installing new playground
equipment in the playground located on the Subject Property, said work to be
done in accordance with plans that accompanied the application for special use
permit, dated January 3, 2003.
   SECTION 3: That the following variation is hereby granted to the Subject
Property, which comprises Parcel 1 and Parcel 2, described above, is located in
the R-2 Single-Family Residential District provided in Chapter 17.24 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, and which is
commonly known as the Tower Road Park: (a) a variation from the side yard
setback requirements of Section 17.30.060 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance to
allow a 0.00-foot side yard along the lot line between Parcel 1 and Parcel 2,
whereas a 12-foot side yard is required, resulting in a variation of 12 feet
(100%), to permit play equipment to cross the lot line between Parcel 1 and
Parcel 2; said variation being granted to allow the replacement of playground
equipment and installation of two playgrounds located on the beach of the
Subject Property, said work to be done in accordance with the plans that
accompanied the application for variation, dated January 3, 2003.
   SECTION 4: That the special use and variations hereby granted are subject to
the condition that the applicants begin the proposed construction and
implementation of the playground improvements within twelve (12) months after
the effective date of this ordinance.
   SECTION 5: That, pursuant to Section 17.56.010(G) of the Zoning Ordinance,
all stipulations, conditions and restrictions set forth in this Ordinance as
part of the terms under which the special use is granted, may be modified or
revised from time to time by the Village Council following public notice and
hearing, using the same procedures set forth in the Zoning Ordinance for
processing the original special use application.
   SECTION 6: That this Ordinance shall be in full force and effect from and
after its passage, approval, and posting.
   ADOPTED this 3rd day of June, 2003, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Abell, Brower, Presser, Webster, and Woodbury
   NAYS:    Trustee Greenough
   ABSENT:    None
   Signed:
   s/s Michael F. Duhl
    Village President
Countersigned:
s/s Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: March 18, 2003
Posted: March 19, 2003
Passed and Approved: June 3, 2003
Posted: June 4, 2003
ORDINANCE NO. M-15-2003
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (380 Locust Road)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
The East Fifty (50) feet of the South Half (1/2) of Block Twelve (12) in
Groveland Addition to Winnetka being a Subdivision of the East Seventy (70)
acres of the Northwest Quarter (1/4) of Section 20, Township 42 North, Range
13, East of the Third Principal Meridian, in Cook County, Illinois,
commonly known as 380 Locust Road, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
5 Single Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.12 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for the
following variations: (a) a variation from the maximum building size
limitations of Section 17.30.040 to permit a new single family residence with
attached garage to have a gross floor area of 4,935.95 square feet, rather than
the maximum allowable gross floor area of 3,360.91 square feet, resulting in a
variation request of 1,575.04 square feet (46.86%); (b) a variation from the
front setback requirements of Section 17.30.050 to allow the new resident to
observe a front setback of 33.21 feet, rather than the required 43.84-foot
front setback, resulting in a variation request of 10.63 feet (24.25%); and (c)
a variation from the limitations on the width of attached garages established
by Section 17.30.110(B), to allow an attached garage to make up 69.17% of the
front building line, which exceeds the maximum of 50%; and
   WHEREAS, on February 10, 2003, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variation and by the
unanimous vote of the five members then present, has reported to the Council
recommending that the requested variation be denied; and
   WHEREAS, as a result of applicant’s meetings with staff and subsequent
discussions with the Village Council, the applicant has modified his plans to
lower the elevation of the top of the first floor and to increase the front
yard setback along Willow Road by 3 feet; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and unique hardships associated
with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect
to the Subject Property in that: (a) the Subject Property is a corner lot
located at the northwest corner of Willow Road and Locust Street; (b) the
Subject Property is located in the floodplain, and has an average existing
grade of 624.65 feet, which is lower than the base flood elevation of 625.5
feet; (c) the applicants propose to build a new home that is oriented toward
Locust Street and that has an attached garage at the south side of the house,
along Willow Road; (d) in order to meet the requirements of the Winnetka
Floodplain Ordinance, the applicants are required to provide compensatory
storage for stormwater run-off on the Subject Property; (e) the front yard of
the Subject Property, along Willow Road, cannot be used for compensatory
storage, because excavating for the compensatory storage would jeopardize the
41-inch elm tree and the 32-inch elm tree located in the front yard; (f)
because the only remaining area with sufficient space for the compensatory
storage is the rear yard, it is not possible to construct a detached garage in
the rear yard at the northwest corner of the property, as originally proposed
by the applicants; (g) in order to provide compensatory storage in the rear
yard, it is necessary for the footprint of the house to be shifted to the south
on the lot, resulting in a nonconforming front setback from Willow Road; (h)
although the house is oriented to face Locust Street, the front lot line lies
along Willow Road, as a result of which the attached garage forms 69.17% of the
front building line; (i) it is not possible to build the house and a detached
garage in a conforming location on the Subject Property, due to the need to
provide compensatory storage for stormwater run-off; (j) in order to meet the
floodplain requirement that the top of the foundation be more than one foot
above the base flood elevation, the distance from the average existing grade to
the top of the first floor is 3.77 feet, which exceeds the 2.5 foot distance
that would exclude the basement from gross floor area calculations; and (k) the
1,624.33 square feet of basement area that is counted towards gross floor area
exceeds the 1,575.04 square foot gross floor area variation that the applicants
are requesting; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, because
building a home that complies with the Village’s tree protection requirements,
Floodplain Ordinance and Zoning Ordinance would require the construction of a
home without a garage and without a basement, both of which are basic features
on lots of comparable size of all properties in the R-5 Single Family Zoning
District; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because the new single family home, when built according to
the conditions set forth in this ordinance, will have typical exterior features
and will preserve the two significant trees that are an essential part of the
Willow Road streetscape; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not impair an adequate supply of
light and air because the Subject Property is located at a corner and the
finished height of the building will conform with the Zoning Ordinance’s height
limitations; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property as the new residence will comply with all
applicable building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not diminish the taxable value of
land and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not contribute to congestion on the
public streets, as the Subject Property will continue to be used for single
family residential purposes; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed new residence with attached
garage will otherwise impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and
welfare of the inhabitants of the Village.
   WHEREAS, the requested variations are in harmony with the general purpose
and intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that the requested variations
will allow for the redevelopment of the Subject Property in compliance with the
Floodplain Ordinance while protecting established trees and landscaping and
maintaining the Willow Road streetscape.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2:  That, subject to the conditions set forth in Section 3 of this
ordinance, the following variations are granted to the Subject Property,
commonly known as 380 Locust Road, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
5 Single Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.12 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code: (a) a
variation from the maximum building size limitations of Section 17.30.040 to
permit a new single family residence with attached garage to have a gross floor
area of 4,935.95 square feet, rather than the maximum allowable gross floor
area of 3,360.91 square feet, resulting in a variation request of 1,575.04
square feet (46.86%); (b) a variation from the front setback requirements of
Section 17.30.050 to allow the new residence to observe a front setback of
33.21 feet, rather than the required 43.84-foot front setback, resulting in a
variation request of 10.63 feet (24.25%); and (c) a variation from the
limitations on the width of attached garages established by Section 17.30.110
(B), to allow an attached garage to make up 69.17% of the front building line,
which exceeds the maximum of 50%, all of said variations to be in accordance
with the plans and elevations submitted with the application for variation, as
modified by the conditions set forth in Section 3 of this ordinance.
   SECTION 3: That the variations hereby granted are subject to the following
conditions:
      A.   The top of the foundation shall be set at an elevation of 627.17
feet and the top of the first floor shall be set at an elevation of 628.42
feet, as depicted in the drawing entitled Foundation Section 2, prepared by J.
Kalas Architect and dated 03-10-03;
      B.   The entire building and attached garage shall be shifted 3 feet to
the north, resulting in a front setback of 36.21 feet from Willow Road; and
      C.   The proposed construction shall commence within 12 months after the
effective date of this ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 1st day of April, 2003, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell and Presser
   NAYS:    Trustee Woodbury
   ABSENT:    None
   Signed:
   s/s Michael F. Duhl
    Village President
Countersigned:
s/s Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: March 18, 2003
Posted: March 19, 2003
Passed and Approved: April 1, 2003
Posted: April 2, 2003
ORDINANCE NO. M-16-2003
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (440 Elder Lane)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
Lot 6 in Block 12 in Lake Shore Subdivision in the Village of Winnetka, being a
Subdivision of Lot 1 in a Subdivision by Nicholas Simon and Others of Part of
Fractional Southeast ¼ of Section 21 and of Fractional Southwest ¼ of Section
22, Township 42 North, Range 13 East of the Third Principal Meridian, and the
five acres lying north of an adjoining said Lot 1, in Cook County, Illinois,
commonly known as 440 Elder Lane, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
5 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.12 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for the
following variations: (a) a variation from the intensity of use of lot
limitations of Section 17.30.030 of the Zoning Ordinance, to allow the
construction of two, two-story additions, resulting in a roofed lot coverage of
2,668.5 square feet, which exceeds the maximum allowable roofed lot coverage of
2,125 square feet by 543.5 square feet (25.58%); (b) a variation from the
intensity of use of lot limitations of Section 17.30.030 of the Zoning
Ordinance, to allow the area of the lot covered by impermeable surfaces to be
4,482.02 square feet, which exceeds the maximum allowable impermeable surface
area of 4,250 square feet by 232.02 square feet (5.46%); (c) a variation form
the side yard setback requirements of Section 17.30.060 of the Zoning
Ordinance, to allow an east side yard setback of 2.45 feet, rather than the
required minimum side yard of 6.0 feet, resulting in a variation of 3.55 feet
(59.17%); and (d) a variation from the limitations on alterations to
nonconforming buildings established by Section 17.64.010(A)(5) of the Zoning
Ordinance, to allow the replacement and relocation of a kitchen window in the
nonconforming east building wall, all of said variations being requested to
allow the construction of a mud room, closet and powder room at the rear
entrance of the home along the west side of the building, with an exercise room
on the second floor above it, and to expand the first floor family room and
second floor master bedroom area located at the rear of the home; and
   WHEREAS, on March 10, 2003, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variations and by the
favorable vote of the six members then present, has reported to the Council
recommending that the requested variation be granted; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties associated with carrying out the
strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect to the Subject Property
in that: (a) the existing residence was built in its current location in 1914,
before the enactment of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance; (b) the residence is a
two-story, stucco building with 3.67-foot wide eaves that exceed the 18-inch
eave depth that the Zoning Ordinance allows to be excluded when determining
roofed lot coverage and setback dimensions; (c) the total amount of lot
coverage constituted by the area of eaves greater than 18 inches equals 369
square feet; (d) the previously constructed, detached garage has a roofed lot
coverage of 633 square feet, resulting from the expansion of the original one-
car garage by adding a two-car garage to the front of it in order to protect a
substantial oak tree next to the original garage; (e) the proposed additions
are modest in size, with the two-story addition along the west side of the
building adding 182.1 square feet of floor area and the two-story, 10.76-foot
deep addition at the rear of the building adding approximately 500 square feet
of floor area; (f) the resulting gross floor area will remain well within the
limitations set by the Zoning Ordinance; and (g) the variation requested for
the kitchen window does not alter the setback and will merely allow an existing
window to be shifted along the same plane and replaced with a window of
approximately the same size; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that
the existing nonconformities cannot be cured without eliminating the eaves and
removing substantial portions of the garage, which would: (a) dramatically
alter the appearance of the residence itself; (b) dramatically alter the
appearance of the Subject Property when viewed from the street and from all
adjoining properties, (c) reduce the utility of the garage and cause a camper
now stored in the garage to be stored outdoors on the Subject Property; (d)
require landscaping and additional landscape maintenance in the area behind the
garage, which is otherwise unusable; and (e) substantially increase the cost of
the project while diminishing the value of the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because: (a) the additions will maintain the established roof
and eave lines of the building; (b) the two-story family room and master
bedroom addition is located at the rear of the house and is not visible from
the street; and (c) the mud room and powder room addition at the west side of
the house is partially obstructed by the front entrance, which is also located
along the west side of the building; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not impair an adequate supply of
light and air because: (a) both additions will conform with applicable height
limitations; (b) the additional encroachment into required side yards is de
minimis; and (c) the relocated kitchen window will maintain light and air for
the kitchen on the Subject Property, while providing additional privacy for
both the Subject Property and the adjacent property, which now have windows
directly opposite each other; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property as the additions will comply with all
applicable building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not diminish the taxable value of
land and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed building additions will not contribute to congestion
on the public streets, as the Subject Property will continue to be used as a
single family residence; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed building additions will
otherwise impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation is in harmony with the general purpose and
intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that it allows the renovation,
restoration and rehabilitation of a structurally sound existing building while
maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the community and protecting
established trees and landscaping.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2:  That the following variations are hereby granted to the Subject
Property, commonly known as 440 Elder Lane, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in
theR-5 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.12 of
the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code: (a) a
variation from the intensity of use of lot limitations of Section 17.30.030 of
the Zoning Ordinance, to allow the construction of two, two-story additions,
resulting in a roofed lot coverage of 2,668.5 square feet, which exceeds the
maximum allowable roofed lot coverage of 2,125 square feet by 543.5 square feet
(25.58%); (b) a variation from the intensity of use of lot limitations of
Section 17.30.030 of the Zoning Ordinance, to allow the area of the lot covered
by impermeable surfaces to be 4,482.02 square feet, which exceeds the maximum
allowable impermeable surface area of 4,250 square feet by 232.02 square feet
(5.46%); (c) a variation form the side yard setback requirements of Section
17.30.060 of the Zoning Ordinance, to allow an east side yard setback of 2.45
feet, rather than the required minimum side yard of 6.0 feet, resulting in a
variation of 3.55 feet (59.17%); and (d) a variation from the limitations on
alterations to nonconforming buildings established by Section 17.64.010(A)(5)
of the Zoning Ordinance, to allow the replacement and relocation of a kitchen
window in the nonconforming east building wall, all of said variations being
requested to allow the construction of a mud room, closet and powder room at
the rear entrance of the home along the west side of the building, with an
exercise room on the second floor above it, and to expand the first floor
family room and second floor master bedroom area located at the rear of the
home, with all of said variations being constructed in accordance with the
plans and elevations submitted with the application for variations.
   SECTION 3: That the variation hereby granted is conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 15th day of April, 2003, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Presser and Woodbury
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    Trustee Powell
   Signed:
   s/s Michael F. Duhl
    Village President
Countersigned:
s/s Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: April 1, 2003
Posted: April 2, 2003
Passed and Approved: April 15, 2003
Posted: April 16, 2003
ORDINANCE NO. M-17-2003
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (139 DeWindt Road)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
That part of Lots “F” and “A” in Daughaday’s Resubdivision of Lots 8 to 14,
both inclusive, in Daughaday Acres, being a subdivision in the Southwest ¼ of
Section 20, Township 42 North, Range 13 East of the Third Principal Meridian,
according to the plat thereof recorded April 5, 1929 as Document No. 10330520,
in Cook County, Illinois, bounded and described as follows: Commencing on the
Westerly line of said Lot “F” at a point 164 feet South of, measured at right
angles to, the North line of Lot “E” in said Daughaday’s Resubdivision; running
thence East on a line drawn parallel to and 164 feet South of, measured at
right angles to, the North line and said North line extended of said Lot “E”; a
distance of 213.25 feet to the Easterly line of said Lot “F” (being also the
Westerly line of said Lot “A”); thence North, across said Lot “A”, along a line
at right angles to said North line extended of said Lot “E”, a distance of
163.94 feet to the Northeast corner of said Lot “F”; thence along the
Northerly, Northwesterly, and Westerly line of said Lot “F” to the Point of
Beginning, situated in the Village of Winnetka, County of Cook, in the State of
Illinois,
commonly known as 139 DeWindt Road, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
2 Single Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.24 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for the
following variations: (a) a variation from the setback requirements of Section
17.30.050 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance to permit the construction of a two-
story addition that will result in a front setback of 0.71 feet along the east
side of the Subject Property, whereas a minimum setback of 47 feet is required,
a variation of 46.29 feet (98.49%); (b) a variation from the garage
requirements of Section 17.30.110 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, to allow
the existing, attached two-car garage to be expanded into a three-car garage
with three front-facing garage doors, whereas a maximum of two are permitted,
and to allow the expanded garage to be 34.58 feet wide, which exceeds the
allowable maximum of 22 feet, a variation of 12.58 feet (57.18%); and
   WHEREAS, on March 10, 2003, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variations and by the
unanimous vote of the six members then present, has reported to the Council
recommending that the requested variations be granted; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and unique hardships associated
with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect
to the Subject Property in that: (a) the Subject Property has three street
frontages, with both the north and west lot lines being formed by DeWindt Road;
(b) the east part of the lot is occupied by substantial portions of an
unimproved 25-foot wide platted private street, which requires the east setback
to be measured from the edge of the platted private street; (c) the platted,
unimproved street is not used to access any of the four properties that it
abuts, with the Subject Property, the parcel immediately to the south of the
Subject Property and the parcel immediately to the east of the Subject Property
all being accessed directly from DeWindt Road, and the parcel at the southeast
terminus of the unimproved street being a flag lot with access directly from
DeWindt Road, through the "flagpole" portion of the property, which lies
adjacent to the east lot line of the property to the east of the Subject
Property; (d) because of the location of the house on the Subject Property, the
interior configuration of the house, and the larger setbacks imposed along the
three street frontages of the Subject Property, the Subject Property has a
significantly reduced buildable area, making it impossible to construct an
addition to the existing residence within a conforming location; (e) because
the private street in the east portion of the Subject Property is unimproved,
the east lot line of the Subject Property functions as a side lot line although
it is considered a front or corner lot line under the Zoning Ordinance; and (f)
the garage doors will face the east lot line and the proposed garage addition
will have a setback of 31.44 feet from the east lot line; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that
(a) the north, west and south setbacks meet or exceed the requirements of the
Zoning Ordinance; (b) the proposed addition is in the area occupied by the
existing, attached garage; (c) the proposed addition is to the east side of the
house, in the part of the Subject Property that functions as a side yard and
appears to be a side yard when viewed from the street; (d) there is no
conforming location for the proposed addition; and (e) the resulting roofed lot
coverage and gross floor area will remain well within the limitations imposed
by the Zoning Ordinance; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because: (a) the proposed addition is consistent with the
style of the existing building and with the size and scale of other residences
in the immediate vicinity; and (b) the proposed addition will maintain the
existing orientation of the house and garage as well as the existing setbacks
along the two DeWindt Road street frontages; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not impair an adequate supply of
light and air because: (a) the expanded residence will comply with building
height, roofed lot coverage and gross floor area limitations; and (b) the east
building line of the expanded residence will be 31.44 feet from the east lot
line; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property, as the proposed addition will comply
with all applicable building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not diminish the taxable value of
land and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed addition and garage expansion will not contribute to
congestion on the public streets, as the building will remain a single family
residence and the expanded garage will provide additional off-street parking;
and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed building addition and garage
expansion will otherwise impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and
welfare of the inhabitants of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations are in harmony with the general purpose
and intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that they allow the renovation,
restoration and rehabilitation of a structurally sound existing building while
maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the community and protecting
established trees and landscaping.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2: That the following variations are hereby granted to the Subject
Property, commonly known as 139 DeWindt Road, Winnetka, Illinois, and located
in the R-2 Single Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.24
of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code: (a) a
variation from the setback requirements of Section 17.30.050 of the Winnetka
Zoning Ordinance to permit the construction of a two-story addition that will
result in a front setback of 0.71 feet along the east side of the Subject
Property, whereas a minimum setback of 47 feet is required, resulting in a
variation of 46.29 feet (98.49%), and (b) a variation from the garage
requirements of Section 17.30.110 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, to allow
the existing, attached two-car garage to be expanded, into a three-car garage
with three front-facing garage doors, whereas a maximum of two are permitted,
and to allow the expanded garage to be 34.58 feet wide, which exceeds the
allowable maximum of 22 feet, a variation of 12.58 feet (57.18%), all of which
shall be constructed in accordance with the February 10, 2003, plans and
elevations that were submitted with the application for variations.
   SECTION 3: That the variations hereby granted are conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 15th day of April, 2003, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Presser and Woodbury
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    Trustee Powell
   Signed:
   s/s Michael F. Duhl
    Village President
Countersigned:
s/s Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: April 1, 2003
Posted: April 2, 2003
Passed and Approved: April 15, 2003
Posted: April 16, 2003
ORDINANCE NO. M-18-2003
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (159 Bertling)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
Lot 8 in Trier Center Neighborhood Subdivision being a Subdivision of Lot 7 in
Circuit Court Partition in the Southeast ¼ of Section 21, Township 42 North,
Range 13, East of the Third Principal Meridian, in Cook County, Illinois,
commonly known as 159 Bertling, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
5 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.12 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for a
variation from the front setback requirements of Section 17.30.50 of the Zoning
Ordinance, to allow a new single-family residence to observe a front setback of
17.67 feet, rather than the 36.9-foot required front setback, resulting in a
variation of 19.23 feet (52.11%); and
   WHEREAS, on April 14, 2003, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variation and by the
unanimous vote of the six members then present, has reported to the Council
recommending that the requested variation be granted; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and unique hardships associated
with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect
to the Subject Property in that: (a) the Subject Property is adjacent to a
dedicated, but unimproved, cul de sac at the intersection of Hill Road and
Bertling Lane; (b) the curved line of the cul de sac forms the northwest corner
of the Subject Property; (c) the front building line is parallel to the west
lot line and, as measured from that west lot line, the front setback exceeds
the minimum required setback; (d) the front building line is consistent with
the front building lines of the adjacent properties, the house that now exists
on the Subject Property and the other properties on the block face; (e) due to
the curved lot line at the northwest corner of the property, the required front
setback area is curved and extends into the footprint of the proposed house;
and (f) building a house in strict conformity with the front setback
requirement would not be consistent with the block face, as developed, in that
it either would result in an irregularly shaped, curved northwest building
line, or would require the house to be set substantially farther back in the
lot; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that
building a house in a conforming location on the Subject Property would
substantially reduce the size of the house, substantially reduce the rear yard,
jeopardize the viability of several large trees in the rear yard and/or result
in an irregularly shaped, curved northwest building line, any or all of which
would substantially reduce the value of the Subject Property without providing
a measurable public benefit; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because the size and location of the new house on the Subject
Property will be consistent with the size and location of the house that it
will replace and the proposed front setback will be consistent with the
established block face; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not impair an adequate supply of light
and air because the proposed new house complies with applicable height,
building size and side yard setback requirements; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property in that the new house will comply with
all applicable building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the new house; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not contribute to congestion on the
public streets, as the Subject Property will continue to be used for a single
family residence with off-street parking; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the requested variation will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village.
   WHEREAS, the requested variation is in harmony with the general purpose and
intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that it allows the replacement of
an existing house while maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the
block face and protecting established trees and landscaping.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2: That there is hereby granted to the Subject Property, commonly
known as 159 Bertling, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-5 Single-Family
Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.12 of the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, a variation from the front
setback requirements of Section 17.30.50 of the Zoning Ordinance, to allow a
new single-family residence to observe a front setback of 17.67 feet, rather
than the 36.9-foot required front setback, resulting in a variation of 19.23
feet (52.11%) which shall be constructed in accordance with the plans and
elevations that were submitted with the application for variation.
   SECTION 3: That the variation hereby granted is conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 20th day of May, 2003, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Abell, Brower, Greenough, Presser, Webster and Woodbury
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    None
   Signed:
   s/s Michael F. Duhl
    Village President
Countersigned:
s/s Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: May 6, 2003
Posted: May 7, 2003
Passed and Approved: May 20, 2003
Posted: May 21, 2003
ORDINANCE NO. M-19-2003
AN ORDINANCE AUTHORIZING THE DISPOSITION OF CERTAIN PERSONAL PROPERTY OWNED BY
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA
   WHEREAS, the Village of Winnetka owns the following described items of
personal property (hereinafter the “Personal Property”) and has established a
minimum value for each such item of personal property as hereinafter described:
 
Vehicle ID        Inventory No. Year Make      Model   Minimum Value
1FTEF25Y8KLA02157 84            1989 Ford      F250    $1,200.00
1G1BL52P5RR197255 99            1994 Chevrolet Caprice $1,000.00
1G1BL52P8TR129488 448           1996 Chevrolet Caprice $2,500.00
1G1BL52P8RR197430 67            1994 Chevrolet Caprice $1,000.00
 
   WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka have determined that it is
no longer useful to, or in the best interests of, the Village of Winnetka to
retain said Personal Property and that said Personal Property should be sold in
the manner hereinafter provided, pursuant to Section 11-76-4 of the Illinois
Municipal Code (65 ILCS 5/11-76-4); and
   WHEREAS, the Village Manager of the Village of Winnetka has authorized the
Northwest Municipal Conference to advertise and obtain bids for the sale of the
items of Personal Property at an auction to be conducted by the Northwest
Municipal Conference at 11:00 a.m., Saturday, May 31, 2003 at the Arlington
Heights Public Works Facility, 222 N. Ridge Avenue, Arlington Heights,
Illinois, with the acceptance of any bids being subject to the approval of the
corporate authorities pursuant to a duly enacted ordinance.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain:
   SECTION 1: Pursuant to Section 11-76-4 of the Illinois Municipal Code 65
(ILCS 5/11-76-4), the Council the Village of Winnetka find that the above-
described Personal Property is no longer necessary or useful to the Village of
Winnetka and that the best interests of the Village of Winnetka will be served
by the sale of said Personal Property.
   SECTION 2: Pursuant to said Section 11-76-4, the Village Manager is hereby
authorized to direct the sale of the Personal Property at an auction to be
conducted by the Northwest Municipal Conference (the “NWMC”) at 11:00 a.m.,
Saturday, May 31, 2003 at the Arlington Heights Public Works Facility, 222 N.
Ridge, Arlington Heights, Illinois,.
   SECTION 3: The Village Manager is further authorized to direct the NWMC to
advertise the sale of the Personal Property through area newspapers, direct
mailings, and other channels as the NWMC deems appropriate prior to the date of
the auction.
   SECTION 4: The Village Manager is further authorized to direct the NWMC to
enter into an agreement for the sale of the Personal Property, whereby the
Personal Property shall be sold at the auction to the highest bidder or
bidders, according to the terms set forth in the Specifications for the Sale of
Vehicles and Equipment at Northwest Municipal Conference Auctions, which
specifications are attached hereto and made a part of this ordinance.
   SECTION 5: No bid shall be accepted for the sale of any item of the Personal
Property which is less than the minimum value of said item of personal property
as set forth in the preamble to this ordinance, unless the Village Manager, or
his designee, so authorizes at the time of the auction, and unless the highest
bid received for such item is less than the minimum value set forth in this
ordinance.
   SECTION 6: Upon payment in full of the auctioned price by the highest bidder
or bidders for any item of the Personal Property, the Village Manager is
authorized to direct the NWMC to convey and transfer the title and ownership of
said item of Personal Property to the highest bidder or bidders.
   SECTION 7: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 20th day of May, 2003, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Abell, Brower, Greenough, Presser, Webster and Woodbury
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    None
   Signed:
   s/s Michael F. Duhl
    Village President
Countersigned:
s/s Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: Waived
Passed and Approved: May 20, 2003
Posted: May 21, 2003
ORDINANCE NO. M-20-2003
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (824 Humboldt Avenue)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
Lot 18 in Websters Subdivision of Lots 1 to 5 inclusive in Block 13 in Park
Addition to Winnetka in Section 17, Township 42 North, Range 13, East of the
Third Principal Meridian, in Cook County, Illinois,
commonly known as 824 Humboldt Avenue, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the
R-5 Single Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.12 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for the
following variations from the garage setback requirements of Section 17.30.110
of the Zoning Ordinance, for the purpose of constructing a detached, two-car
garage in the southwest corner of the Subject Property: (a) a variation of 1.5
feet (75%) from the 2.0-foot rear yard setback requirement, to allow a rear
yard setback of 0.5 feet; and (b) a variation of 0.5 feet (25%) from the 2.0-
foot side yard setback requirement, to allow a side yard setback of 1.5. feet
from the west lot line; and
   WHEREAS, on April 14, 2003, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variation with six members
present and, by a vote of five to one, has reported to the Council recommending
against the requested variations; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and particular hardships
associated with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance
with respect to the Subject Property in that: (a) the Subject Property has a
legal, nonconforming lot width of approximately 50 feet; (b) the Subject
Property is roughly rectangular in shape, with a slightly curved front lot line
that results in an east lot line that is 176.25 feet long and a west lot line
that is 166.00 feet long; (c) the new garage is proposed in conjunction with
the construction of a new home on the Subject Property; (d) an attached garage
would occupy nearly two-thirds of the 36-foot maximum allowable width of the
house and would require front-facing garage doors; (e) there are two mature oak
trees near the center of the rear yard, with diameters of 29 inches and 21
inches, both of which would be jeopardized by constructing the garage in a
conforming location anywhere in the rear of the Subject Property; and (f) the
proposed location provides the greatest protection possible for the two large
oaks without negatively impacting trees on adjoining properties; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that:
(a) a two-car garage is a standard feature of single family residential
properties in the immediate vicinity; and (b) strict compliance with the Zoning
Ordinance would require either removal of at least one of the trees, or
construction of an attached garage that would dominate the front of the house,
or the omission of a garage entirely, any of which would have a substantial
impact on the value of the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because the detached garage in the proposed location will
preserve the two mature oak trees and will be in a location consistent with the
location of garages on the adjoining properties, thereby preserving the
appearance of the block face; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not impair an adequate supply of
light and air because the proposed garage will comply with applicable height
limitations and because, as an accessory building, the garage will not be
located in proximity to the houses on the adjoining properties; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property as the new garage will comply with all
applicable building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not diminish the taxable value of
land and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not contribute to congestion on the
public streets, as the proposed location of the garage will maximize off-street
parking on the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the construction of the garage in the
proposed location will otherwise impair the public health, safety, comfort,
morals, and welfare of the inhabitants of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations are in harmony with the general purpose
and intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that they will allow the
redevelopment of the Subject Property while maintaining the existing scale and
appearance of the community and protecting established trees and landscaping.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2: That the following variations from the garage setback
requirements of Section 17.30.110 of the Zoning Ordinance are hereby granted to
the Subject Property, commonly known as 824 Humboldt Avenue, Winnetka,
Illinois, and located in the R-5 Single Family Residential Zoning District
provided in Chapter 17.12 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the
Winnetka Village Code: (a) a variation of 1.5 feet (75%) from the 2.0-foot rear
yard setback requirement, to allow a rear yard setback of 0.5 feet; and (b) a
variation of 0.5 feet (25%) from the 2.0-foot side yard setback requirement, to
allow a side yard setback of 1.5. feet from the west lot line; variations being
for the purpose of constructing a detached, two-car garage in the southwest
corner of the Subject Property in accordance with the plans and elevations
submitted with the application for variations.
   SECTION 3: That the variations hereby granted is conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 20th day of May, 2003, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Abell, Brower, Greenough, Presser, Webster and Woodbury
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    None
   Signed:
   s/s Michael F. Duhl
    Village President
Countersigned:
s/s Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: Waived
Passed and Approved: May 20, 2003
Posted: May 21, 2003
ORDINANCE NO. M-21-2003
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (620 Ash Street)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
   The West 100 feet of the Northwest Quarter (except streets) of Block 65 in
Original Part of Winnetka, being Charles E. Pecks Subdivision of the Northeast
Quarter of Section 20 and North Fractional Half of Fractional Section 21,
Township 42 North, Range 13, East of the Third Principal Meridian, in Cook
County, Illinois,
commonly known as 620 Ash Street, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
4 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.16 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for the
following variations from the 30-foot corner yard setback provisions of Section
17.30.050: (a) a 6.0-foot (20%) variation to allow a 2-story addition to be
located 24 feet from the corner lot line; (b) a 10.06-foot (33.5%) variation to
allow deck on the west side of the new addition to be located 19.94 feet from
the corner lot line; and (c) a 12.06-foot (40.2%) variation to allow window
wells on the west side of the existing building to be located 17.94 feet from
the west lot line; and
   WHEREAS, on May 12, 2003, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of Appeals
conducted a public hearing on the requested variations and, by the unanimous
vote of the six members then present, has reported to the Council recommending
that the variations requested for the addition, including the window wells and
deck, be granted; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and unique hardships associated
with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect
to the Subject Property in that: (a) the Subject Property is located at the
southeast corner of Cedar and Ash Streets; (b) the house on the Subject
Property was built in 1911, prior to the enactment of the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance; (c) the existing building has a legally nonconforming corner setback
of 11.2 feet along Cedar Street; (d) the applicants propose to remove the
breakfast nook that extends into the required corner yard and to add a two-
story addition with an expanded kitchen, reconfigured stairway and new
breakfast area, family room and mud room on the first floor, and with an
additional bathroom, bedroom and laundry area on the second floor; (e) the
existing kitchen and breakfast nook are in the southwest corner of the house
and there is no other practical location for the proposed addition; (f) because
of the location of the interior stairway, it is not possible to shift the
proposed addition farther to the east; (g) the proposed deck is located
adjacent to the new breakfast area, which adjoins the kitchen; and (h) the
proposed new window well will be in the area now occupied by the breakfast nook
that will be removed; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that:
(a) the existing kitchen and breakfast nook are small in relation to the other
principal rooms on the first floor; (b) it is not possible to expand the
kitchen and breakfast areas or add a family room in a location that both
conforms with the corner yard setback requirements and maintains the internal
flow of the rooms; (c) the proposed deck represents a minimal extension from
the proposed new building wall and is a typical amenity in homes of comparable
size in the vicinity; and (d) the proposed window wells are consistent with
existing window wells in the home and are needed to provide additional light
and air for the basement of the residence; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because the proposed addition: (a) will reduce the degree of
nonconformity in the corner yard setback; (b) has been designed to be used
consistent with the exterior appearance of the existing home; (c) will contain
architectural detail to minimize the appearance of visual bulk, and (d) will
maintain the existing configuration of the Subject Property, with the principal
residence at the front and a detached garage in the rear yard; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not impair an adequate supply of
light and air because the proposed improvements will meet all building size and
height limitations of the Zoning Ordinance and will be located at the rear of
the residence, along the Cedar Street frontage, away from nearby residences;
and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property, as the proposed construction will comply
with all applicable building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not diminish the taxable value of
land and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed construction will not contribute to congestion on the
public streets, as the property will continue to be used for single family
residential purposes; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed building addition, deck and
window wells will otherwise impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals,
and welfare of the inhabitants of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations are in harmony with the general purpose
and intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that they allow the renovation,
restoration and rehabilitation of a structurally sound existing building while
maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the community and protecting
established trees and landscaping.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2:  That the following variations from the 30-foot corner yard
setback provisions of Section 17.30.050 are hereby granted to the Subject
Property, commonly known as 620 Ash Street, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in
the R-4 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.16 of
the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code: (a) a
6.0-foot (20%) variation to allow a 2-story addition to be located 24 feet from
the corner lot line; (b) a 10.06-foot (33.5%) variation to allow deck on the
west side of the new addition to be located 19.94 feet from the corner lot
line; and (c) a 12.06-foot (40.2% variation to allow window wells on the west
side of the existing building to be located 17.94 feet from the west lot line,
all of said variations to be as depicted in the plans and elevations submitted
with the application for variations.
   SECTION 3: That the variations hereby granted are conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 17th day of June, 2003, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Abell, Brower, Presser and Webster
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    Trustees Greenough and Woodbury
   Signed:
   s/s Michael F. Duhl
    Village President
Countersigned:
s/s Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: June 3, 2003
Posted: June 4, 2003
Passed and Approved: June 17, 2003
Posted: June 18, 2003
ORDINANCE NO. M-22-2003
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A SPECIAL USE PERMIT AND VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF
THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA, COOK, COUNTY, ILLINOIS FOR THE
CROW ISLAND SCHOOL (1112 Willow Road)
   WHEREAS, the Board of Education of Winnetka Elementary School District No.
36 is the beneficial owner of the following described real estate (the “Subject
Property”), which is commonly known as Crow Island Elementary School, 1112
Willow Road, Winnetka, Illinois:
That part of vacated Glendale Avenue and of the east 13.0 feet of Lot 37 to 47,
inclusive, in Block 1 in Vernamo, being a subdivision of the northwest quarter
of the southwest quarter of Section 20, Township 42 North, Range 13, East of
the Third Principal Meridian, and the east quarter of northeast quarter of the
southeast quarter of Section 19, Township 42 North, Range 13, East of the Third
Principal Meridian, described as follows: beginning at a point in the east line
of said northwest quarter of the southwest quarter of said Section 20, 222.33
ft. south of the northeast corner thereof, thence south along said east line,
250.0 ft., thence west at right angles to said east line 46.0 ft., thence north
parallel to the east line of said northwest quarter of the southwest quarter of
said Section 20, 250.0 ft., thence east 46.0 ft., to the place of beginning, in
Cook County, Illinois.
Also, Lots 48 to 70, both inclusive, and the east 17.0 ft. of vacated Glendale
Avenue, lying west of and adjoining Lots 61 to 70, both inclusive, in Alles’
Sunset Subdivision of the northeast quarter of the southwest quarter of Section
20, Township 42 North, Range 13, East of the Third Principal Meridian, in Cook
County, Illinois; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property is located in the R-2 Single-Family
Residential District provided in Chapter 17.24 of the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code; and,
   WHEREAS, the Board of Education of Winnetka Elementary School District No.
36 is a body corporate and politic of the State of Illinois and operates a
public school known as the Crow Island Elementary School, which is improved
with the Crow Island School building and accessory structures; and
   WHEREAS, the accessory structures at the Crow Island Elementary School
include several playground areas; and
   WHEREAS, public schools are permitted as special uses in the R-2 Single-
Family Residential District, subject to the conditions and requirements
pertaining to special uses, as set forth in Section 17.56 of the Winnetka
Zoning Ordinance; and
   WHEREAS, the Board of Education of Winnetka Elementary School District No.
36 (“applicant”) has filed an application for a special use permit pursuant to
Sections 17.24.010(B)2.b and Section 17.56.010 of the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance, to allow the site plan for the Subject Property to be revised as
part of the upgrading of the playground areas on the Subject Property,
including the kindergarten playground area at the northeast corner of the
school property along the Glendale Avenue property line (the “Kindergarten
Playground”); and
   WHEREAS, the applicant has also filed an application for a variation from
the corner setback requirements of Section 17.30.050 of the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance to permit the play equipment to observe a corner setback of 6.42 feet
from the Glendale Avenue property line, whereas a corner setback of 50 feet is
required, resulting in a variation of 43.58 feet (87.16%); and
   WHEREAS, the current proposal is the third in a series of applications for
improvements to the several playgrounds at Crow Island School; and
   WHEREAS, special use permits and variations were granted for the first grade
playground and the second to fourth grade playground, pursuant to ordinances M-
11-2002; and
   WHEREAS, on February 27, 2002, pursuant to due notice thereof, the Plan
Commission considered the proposed improvements to the Crow Island School
playgrounds and the eight members then present voted unanimously to find the
proposed playground renovations, including the renovations to the Kindergarten
Playground, consistent with the Winnetka Comprehensive Plan; and
   WHEREAS, pursuant to due notice thereof, on April 17, 2003, the Design
Review Board considered the proposed improvements and issued favorable comment
for the proposed renovation of the Kindergarten Playground and its equipment;
and
   WHEREAS, pursuant to due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of Appeals held a
public hearing on May 12, 2003, to consider the special use permit and
variation request for the Kindergarten Playground renovation and, by the
unanimous vote of the five members then present, has recommended that the
requested variation and special use permit be granted; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed replacement of the equipment in the Kindergarten
Playground will benefit the public health, safety, comfort, morals and general
welfare of the Village, by improving the safety, accessibility and appearance
of the playground by installing new playground equipment, providing safe use
zones, reducing impermeable surface and improving the storm drainage on the
Subject Property, all in accordance with applicable requirements of the
Americans with Disabilities Act, playground safety standards and the Village
Code; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed special use will not be substantially injurious to the
use and enjoyment of other property in the immediate vicinity, nor will it
substantially diminish or impair property values in the immediate vicinity, as
the Kindergarten Playground is a firmly established component of the school and
playground use of the Subject Property, which has been in place for over 50
years, the Kindergarten Playground renovation will neither increase the
intensity of the existing use nor alter its nature, and the renovations will
improve both the functionality and appearance of the playground; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed special use will not impede the normal and orderly
development or improvement of other property in the immediate vicinity for uses
permitted under applicable zoning regulations, as the Kindergarten Playground
is an established use in its existing location on the Subject Property, the use
of the Subject Property will not be changed, and the Kindergarten Playground
area is not in close proximity to any single-family residences; and
   WHEREAS, adequate measures have been taken to provide ingress and egress in
a manner that minimizes pedestrian and vehicular traffic congestion, in that
points of access to the Subject Property and parking will not be changed, and
there will be no changes to vehicular access; and
   WHEREAS, adequate parking and access roads serve the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, adequate utilities, drainage and other facilities necessary to the
operation of the special use exist or will be provided; and
   WHEREAS, the special use in all other respects conforms to the applicable
regulations of this and other Village ordinances and codes;
   WHEREAS, the plight of the applicant is unique in that the play equipment is
a necessary accessory to the principal use of the Subject Property in meeting
the statutory obligations of the applicant to provide educational and
recreational facilities; and
   WHEREAS, the strict application of the corner setback requirements create
practical difficulties and particular hardships for the applicant because the
equipment cannot be placed in a conforming location due to the limited amount
of space available in the Kindergarten Playground and the playground’s
proximity to the school building and paved access drives; and
   WHEREAS, the variation, if granted, will not alter the essential character
of the locality, in that the school and its playgrounds are established uses in
the neighborhood, and the proposed improvements will replace or upgrade play
equipment currently located in the Kindergarten Playground, which is an
established use in its existing location; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the applicable zoning
regulations, as the sole purpose of the property is to provide educational and
recreational facilities as required by law; and
   WHEREAS, an adequate supply of light and air to adjacent property will not
be impaired, as the new equipment will be in the same area as the existing
equipment, the tallest component will be five feet high and the storage shed
will be 11 feet high at its peak; and
   WHEREAS, the hazard from fire and other damages to the property will not be
increased, because the equipment will remain in an open site, and the new
equipment and playground surface will meet safety standards; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the taxable value of land and buildings
throughout the Village will diminish; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed renovation of the Kindergarten Playground equipment
will not increase congestion in the public streets because the Kindergarten
Playground is an existing accessory to the Crow Island School, which is also an
existing use; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed Kindergarten Playground equipment will not be
detrimental to or endanger the public health, safety, comfort, morals and
welfare of the inhabitants of the Village, because the upgraded Kindergarten
Playground will be in full compliance with current safety standards and
guidelines, and will meet accessibility requirements of the Americans with
Disabilities Act.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain:
   SECTION 1: That the Village Council hereby adopts by reference the foregoing
recitals as its findings as if those recitals were fully set forth herein.
   SECTION 2: That, pursuant to Sections 17.24.010(B)2b and 17.56.010 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, a special use
permit is hereby granted with respect to the Subject Property, which is located
in the R-2 Single-Family Residential Zoning District and is commonly known as
the Crow Island Elementary School, to permit the renovation of the Subject
Property by installing new playground equipment in the playground located at
the northeast corner of the Subject Property along the Glendale property line
of the Subject Property, said work to be done in accordance with the March 2003
Lakota Group plans, that accompanied the application for special use permit.
   SECTION 3: That a variation is hereby granted to the Subject Property, which
is located in the R-2 Single-Family Residential District provided in Chapter
17.24 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code,
and which is commonly known as the Crow Island Elementary School from the
corner setback provisions permitted by Section 17.30.050 of the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance, to allow a corner setback of 6.42 feet from the Glendale Avenue
property line, whereas a 50.0-foot setback is required, resulting in a
variation of 43.58 feet (87.16%), said variation being for the purpose of
permitting the replacement of playground equipment and renovation of the
existing Kindergarten Playground located at the northeast corner of the Subject
Property along the Glendale Avenue property line of the Subject Property, said
work to be done in accordance with the March 2003 Lakota Group plans that
accompanied the application for variation.
   SECTION 4: That the special use and variations hereby granted are subject to
the following conditions:
      A.   That the applicants obtain final design review approval of the plans
for the Kindergarten Playground area, in accordance with procedures established
by the Village Code; and
      B.   That the applicants begin the proposed construction and
implementation of the Kindergarten Playground improvements within twelve (12)
months after the effective date of this ordinance.
   SECTION 5: That, pursuant to Section 17.56.010(G) of the Zoning Ordinance,
all stipulations, conditions and restrictions set forth in this Ordinance as
part of the terms under which the special use is granted, may be modified or
revised from time to time by the Village Council following public notice and
hearing, using the same procedures set forth in the Zoning Ordinance for
processing the original special use application.
   SECTION 6: That this Ordinance shall be in full force and effect from and
after its passage, approval, and posting.
   ADOPTED this 3rd day of June, 2003, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Abell, Brower, Greenough, Presser, Webster and Woodbury
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    None
   Signed:
   s/s Michael F. Duhl
    Village President
Countersigned:
s/s Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: Waived
Passed and Approved: June 3, 2003
Posted: June 4, 2003
ORDINANCE NO. M-23-2003
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A SPECIAL USE PERMIT AND VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF
THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA, COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (Sacred
Heart School - 1095 Gage Street)
   WHEREAS, the Catholic Bishop of Chicago, a corporation sole (the
“Archdiocese of Chicago”), is the owner of the following described real estate
(the “Subject Property”):
Lots 15, 16, 17, 18, 19, and 20 except that part of said Lot 20 lying easterly
of the westerly line of the 16 foot alley in Block 3 of Jared Gage’s
Subdivision according to the plat thereof recorded as Document No. 12387 on
February 8, 1872, in the East ½ of the Northwest ¼ of Section 17, township 42
North, Range 13 East of the Third Principal Meridian, Village of Winnetka, Cook
County, Illinois,
commonly known as 1095 Gage Street, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the B-
1 Multi-Family Residential Zoning District, provided in Chapter 17.32 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code; and
   WHEREAS, Sacred Heart Church, which is part of the Archdiocese of Chicago,
operates Sacred Heart School, a Catholic elementary school, on the Subject
Property; and
   WHEREAS, Sacred Heart School consists of a school building, playground and
paved parking area, all of which have been located on the Subject Property
since 1951; and
   WHEREAS, on February 3, 2003, Sacred Heart Church (“Applicant”) filed an
application for a special use permit pursuant to Section 17.32.010.B.2c of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, to allow the construction of a two-story addition to
the west side of the school building, adding seven classrooms, an elevator and
other improvements that will bring the school into compliance with the
Americans with Disabilities Act; and
   WHEREAS, on February 3, 2003, the Applicant also filed an application for
the following variations from Chapter 17.32 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance:
(a) a variation from the intensity of use of lot provisions of Section
17.32.010.F to allow an impermeable surface area of 42,238.78 square feet,
which exceeds the maximum of 35,030.40 square feet, resulting in a variation of
7,208.38 square feet (20.58%); and (b) a variation from the principal roof form
requirements of Section 17.32.010.O, to allow both the addition and temporary,
mobile classrooms to have a flat roof rather than a required pitch of between
35º and 60º; and
   WHEREAS, on April23, 2003, on due notice thereof, the Plan Commission met to
consider the proposed building addition and related changes to the existing
special use and, by the unanimous vote of the nine members then present, has
recommended that the special use permit be granted, finding that it is
consistent with Winnetka 2020, the Winnetka Comprehensive Plan; and
   WHEREAS, on May 12 2003, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of Appeals
conducted a public hearing on the requested special use and variations and, by
the unanimous vote of the six members then present, has recommended that the
requested variation and special use be granted; and
   WHEREAS, on March 20, 2003, and April 17, 2003, the Design Review Board
reviewed the proposed building addition and site alterations and has issued
favorable comment; and
   WHEREAS, Section 17.32.020.B2.c of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance allows
educational institutions, such as Sacred Heart School, as special uses in the
B-1 Multi-Family Residential Districts, subject to meeting the conditions set
forth in Chapters 17.32 and 17.56 of the Zoning Ordinance; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property is located on the north side of Gage Street,
with its north and west property lines being adjacent to single family
residential uses in the R-5 Single-Family Residential Zoning District, and its
east property line being adjacent to a public alley that adjoins the commercial
properties on the west side of Green Bay Road north of Gage Street; and
   WHEREAS, Burr Avenue terminates at Gage Street opposite the easterly portion
of the Subject Property, with the Subject Property being located opposite
single family residential uses in the R-5 Single-Family Residential Zoning
District on the west side of Burr Avenue and south side of Gage Street, and
being located opposite the Sacred Heart Church property that lies along the
south side of Gage Street on the east side of Burr Avenue; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed building addition for Sacred Heart School is part of a
plan of Applicant to expand its facilities by adding to the school building and
by constructing a new parish center on Applicant’s property on the east side of
Burr Avenue between Gage Street and Tower Road; and
   WHEREAS, all of the properties that form the Sacred Heart Church campus are
located in the B-1 Multi-Family residential District; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property is improved with school building that includes
a cafeteria/gymnasium/auditorium, paved parking area and two playgrounds; and
   WHEREAS, although most of the school building is two stories high, with some
lower level rooms, the west wing of the school, which contains two classrooms,
is a single story high and has no lower level; and
   WHEREAS, there are approximately 1,200 families in the parish; and
   WHEREAS, the Applicant’s primary educational programs go through the 8th
grade and include the parish school, Sacred Heart School, with approximately
275 students, and a religious education program, with an enrollment of
approximately 560 public school students; and
   WHEREAS, the religious education program uses the same classrooms as the
parochial school but, due to a lack of classroom space, meets in both the
school and the parish center and divides the program into a Sunday morning
session for lower and middle grades and Wednesday evening sessions for upper
grades; and
   WHEREAS, in the 50 years since the school was built, educational programming
has changed, and there is insufficient space to meet current educational
standards and programming needs; and
   WHEREAS, due to a lack of space, (a) the library is now used as a classroom
and a lower corridor functions as the library, (b) music and art are combined
in a single room and (c) the school band meets in the boys locker room; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed building addition will replace the one-story west wing
of the school with a two-story addition with a full lower level; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed improvements also include the addition of a ramp and
an elevator to bring the building into compliance with the Americans with
Disabilities Act; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed school addition is consistent with the goal of the
Winnetka Comprehensive Plan, Winnetka 2020, of supporting educational
excellence and the enrichment of Winnetka’s religious and cultural environment;
and
   WHEREAS, the proposed school addition, which will have substantially the
same footprint as the existing school building, is consistent with the goal of
the Winnetka Comprehensive Plan, Winnetka 2020, of limiting commercial,
institutional and residential development within the Village to minimize the
potentially adverse impacts on adjacent residential neighborhoods and to
prevent the need for significant increases in the Village’s infrastructure and
other community resources; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed school addition is consistent with the goal of the
Winnetka Comprehensive Plan, Winnetka 2020, of ensuring that commercial,
institutional, and residential development is appropriate to the character of
and minimizes the adverse impact on its surrounding neighborhood; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed school addition is consistent with the objective of
the Winnetka Comprehensive Plan, Winnetka 2020, of protecting residential
neighborhoods and homes from the encroachment of incompatible land uses and
traffic patterns; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed school addition is consistent with the objective of
the Winnetka Comprehensive Plan, Winnetka 2020, of recognizing the critical
importance of educational, religious and other community institutions to
Village residents; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed school addition is consistent with the objective of
the Winnetka Comprehensive Plan, Winnetka 2020, of maintaining an atmosphere in
which diverse cultural, educational and religious organizations may flourish;
and
   WHEREAS, the Applicant has met with neighbors in an attempt to minimize the
impact of the proposed special use on the immediate vicinity, in furtherance of
the objective of the Winnetka Comprehensive Plan, Winnetka 2020, of engaging in
a public process that balances institutional goals and minimizes adverse
impacts on the character of the adjacent residential neighborhood; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed school addition is consistent with the objective of
the Winnetka Comprehensive Plan, Winnetka 2020, of recognizing that standards
of educational excellence may change with time, thus necessitating changes in
physical resources; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed school addition is consistent with the objectives of
the Winnetka Comprehensive Plan, Winnetka 2020, of ensuring safe and attractive
access to educational and community institutions and of pursuing improvements
that address public safety as well as traffic, congestion and parking; and
   WHEREAS, because the expanded school will maintain substantially the same
footprint as the existing school building, the proposed school addition is
consistent with the objective of the Winnetka Comprehensive Plan, Winnetka
2020, of encouraging the preservation of open space inside and outside the
Village; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed school addition is consistent with the recommendation
of the Winnetka Comprehensive Plan, Winnetka 2020, of encouraging governmental
and non-governmental institutions to work with their constituents, neighbors
and the Village to minimize the impact of traffic and parking on surrounding
residential streets and to develop on-site solutions where appropriate; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed school addition is consistent with the objective of
the Winnetka Comprehensive Plan, Winnetka 2020, of ensuring that proposals do
not have an adverse impact on the residential character of the surrounding
residential neighborhoods; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed expansion of the existing special use will neither
endanger nor be detrimental to the public health, safety, comfort, morals or
general welfare, in that: (a) the building expansion and renovations will bring
it into full compliance with the Americans with Disabilities Act; (b) the
addition is intended to meet the needs of the Applicant for adequate
educational space for its existing parochial school and religious education
programs; (c) the school improvements will include full sprinkler systems; and
(d) the building expansion will remove obstacles to student traffic in the
building by providing rooms for educational activities that are currently
conducted in building corridors; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed expansion of the existing
special use will either substantially diminish or impair property values in the
immediate vicinity or be substantially injurious to the use and enjoyment of
land in the immediate vicinity for uses permitted by right in that zoning
district, in that: (a) the neighborhood around the school has developed,
without impediment, in the presence of the school; and (b) the proposed
building addition lies almost entirely within the existing building footprint;
and
   WHEREAS, the proposed expansion of the existing special use will not impede
the normal and orderly development and improvement of other property in the
immediate vicinity for uses permitted by right in the zoning district, as the
School has been located in the neighborhood for over 50 years, during which
time the adjacent neighborhood has steadily and fully developed; and
   WHEREAS, adequate measures have been taken to provide ingress and egress in
a manner that minimizes pedestrian and vehicular traffic congestion in the
public ways, in that: (a) the proposed building expansion is limited almost
entirely to the existing building footprint; (b) the proposed building
expansion does not encroach into or otherwise interfere with existing parking
areas; (c) the proposed building expansion does not alter the established
patterns of ingress and egress; (d) the proposed building expansion is not
expected to increase school enrollment; and (e) the school has implemented an
effective method of controlling school-generated traffic during times peak
student drop-off and pick-up times; and
   WHEREAS, adequate parking, utilities, access roads, drainage and other
facilities necessary for the operation of the expanded special use either exist
or will be provided, in that: (a) the proposed expansion is intended to
accommodate the existing enrollment and staff; and (b) existing parking,
utilities, established access drives, drainage and other infrastructure for the
existing use will not be altered; and
   WHEREAS,  subject to the terms and conditions of this ordinance, the special
use, as expanded by the proposed building addition, conforms in all other
respects to the applicable zoning regulations and other applicable Village
ordinances and Codes; and
   WHEREAS, the property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted to be
used only under the conditions allowed by the zoning regulations, in that: (a)
the Subject Property has been established as the site of a school facility
since 1919; (b) the Village of Winnetka is a mature community and no
alternative sites of sufficient size are available for the Applicant’s
facilities; and (c) the Applicant’s educational ministry, which is primarily
achieved through its parish school and religious education program, will be
substantially impaired without the proposed building addition; and
   WHEREAS, strict application of the Zoning Ordinance creates practical
difficulties and particular hardships because: (a) there is no contiguous land
available to expand the school; (b) the variation is being requested from
zoning limitations based on multi-family residential standards that do not
apply to educational uses; (c) the proposed addition has been designed to be
the same height and have the same roof form as the main portion of the existing
building; and (d) strict application of the Zoning Ordinance to the Subject
Property would preclude any expansion to the building; and
   WHEREAS, the plight of the Applicant is due to unique circumstances,
because, in addition to the practical difficulties and particular hardships
described above, the Applicant’s use of the Subject Property is for the
furtherance of its educational ministry rather than the private comfort,
convenience or monetary benefits that typically attach to residential and
commercial uses of property; and
   WHEREAS, the variations, if granted, will not alter the essential character
of the neighborhood, in that: (a) the school has been an established fixture in
the neighborhood for over 50 years; (b) the proposed building addition reflects
only a minor expansion of the building footprint, with most of the impermeable
surface resulting from the reconfiguration of the front entrance to meet
accessibility requirements; and (c) the proposed building addition has been
designed to be the same height as the main portion of the building, with
matching building materials and a matching roof line, thereby maintaining, to
the extent possible, the established appearance of the facilities; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not impair an adequate supply of
light and air because the second floor addition complies with applicable
building height and setback limitations; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property because the building additions will
comply with all applicable building and fire protection codes and will include
the installation of a fire suppression system; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not diminish the taxable value of
land and buildings throughout the Village, because the proposed building
addition: (a) will not have a significant impact on the appearance of the
School’s campus in the neighborhood; (b) will not generate additional traffic;
and (c) will improve the condition and appearance of the Subject Property while
not interfering with the use of other properties in the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed building addition will not increase congestion in the
public streets because: (a) the proposed building addition is designed to
accommodate the existing enrollment; (b) neither the parking areas nor campus
access patterns will be changed; and (c) the Applicant has implemented an
effective method of controlling school-generated traffic during peak student
drop-off and pick-up times; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed building additions will
otherwise impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1: The foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated into this section
as the findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set
forth herein.
   SECTION 2: Pursuant to Section 17.32.010.B.2.c of the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance and subject to the terms and conditions hereinafter set forth, a
special use permit is hereby granted to the Subject Property, commonly known as
Sacred Heart School, 1095 Gage Street, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the
B-1 Multi-Family Residential Zoning District provided in the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance, to allow the construction of a two-story addition to the west side
of the school building, adding seven classrooms and an elevator and other
improvements that will bring the school into compliance with the Americans with
Disabilities Act as depicted in the plans and elevations submitted with the
application.
   SECTION 3: Subject to the terms and conditions hereinafter set forth, the
following variations from Chapter 17.32 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance are
hereby granted to the Subject Property, commonly known as Sacred Heart School,
1095 Gage Street, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the B-1 Multi-Family
Residential Zoning District provided in the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, to allow
the construction of a two-story addition with a footprint of 1,130.10 square
feet and a floor area of 4,204.98 square feet: (a) a variation from the
intensity of use of lot provisions of Section 17.32.010.F to allow an
impermeable surface area of 42,238.78 square feet, which exceeds the maximum of
35,030.40 square feet, resulting in a variation of 7,208.38 square feet
(20.58%); and (b) a variation from the principal roof form requirements of
Section 17.32.010.O, to allow the addition and proposed temporary, mobile
classrooms to have a flat roofs rather than a required pitch of between 35º and
60º, all as depicted in the plans and elevations submitted with the
application.
   SECTION 4: The special use and variations granted herein are subject to the
following conditions:
      A.   Prior to the issuance of any building permits, the Applicant shall
enter into a development agreement with the Village that shall contain
provisions regarding: (1) construction traffic and staging areas; (2) parking
for construction workers; (3) parking and traffic patterns for staff parking
and student drop-off and pick-up during the construction period; (4) ingress
and egress for fires and other emergencies that may occur while the building is
under construction; and (5) any of the topics set forth in Section 15.32.080.K
of the Winnetka Village Code that apply to the circumstances of the applicant’s
project. If construction of the building addition is scheduled to take place
during the same time periods as the construction of the new parish center that
is the subject of Ordinance M-24-2003, the development agreement requirements
of both projects may be included in a single development agreement that will
address both the school addition and the new parish center.
      B.   Final design approval of building, landscaping, lighting and
signage.
      C.   The proposed construction shall commence within 12 months after the
effective date of this Ordinance; and
      D.   The mobile classrooms shall be removed after the completed addition
is occupied.
   SECTION 5 : The stipulations, conditions and restrictions set forth in the
foregoing Section 4 of this Ordinance may be modified or revised from time to
time by the Village Council following public notice and hearing, following the
procedures specified in Section 17.56 of the Winnetka Village Code for
processing special use applications.
   SECTION 6: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   PASSED this 17th day of June, 2003, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Abell, Brower, Presser and Webster
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    Trustees Greenough and Woodbury
   Signed:
   s/s Michael F. Duhl
    Village President
Countersigned:
s/s Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: June 3, 2003
Posted: June 4, 2003
Passed and Approved: June 17, 2003
Posted: June 18, 2003
ORDINANCE NO. M-24-2003
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A SPECIAL USE AND VARIATIONS IN THE APPLICATION OF THE
ZONING ORDINANCE OF THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA, COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS, TO ALLOW
THE CONSTRUCTION OF A CHURCH PARISH CENTER (1077 Tower Road/919 Burr Avenue)
   WHEREAS, the Catholic Bishop of Chicago, a corporation sole (the
“Archdiocese of Chicago”), is the owner of the following parcels of real
estate:
PARCEL 1:
Lots 8, 9, 16, 17, 18, 19, and 20 in Block 6 of Jared Gages' Subdivision of the
east half of the North West Quarter, also part of the west half of the North
West Quarter of Fractional Section 17, Township 42 North, Range 13, also part
of the east half of the South West Quarter of Section 8, Township 42 North,
Range 13, East of the Third Principal Meridian, in Cook County, Illinois,
PARCEL 2:
Lots 3and 4 in Block 6 of Jared Gages' Subdivision of the east half of the
North West Quarter, also part of the west half of the North West Quarter of
Fractional Section 17, Township 42 North, Range 13, also part of the east half
of the South West Quarter of Section 8, Township 42 North, Range 13, East of
the Third Principal Meridian, in Cook County, Illinois,
said parcels being commonly known, respectively, as 1077 Tower Road, and 919
Burr Avenue, Winnetka, Illinois; and
   WHEREAS, Parcel 1 is located in the B-1 Multi-Family Residential District
provided in Chapter 17.32 of the Zoning Ordinance, and is the site of Sacred
Heart Church, which is part of the Archdiocese of Chicago; and
   WHEREAS, Parcel 1 contains the principal buildings of Sacred Heart Church
(“Applicant”), which consist of the church at the northeast corner of Tower
Road and Burr Avenue, a parish center that was formerly a convent, a rectory, a
two-car garage, a storage shed and a paved parking lot; and
   WHEREAS, Parcel 2, which is located adjacent to the north lot line of Parcel
1, was acquired by the Applicant in 2001 and is now improved only with a
garage, the single family residence that was once located there having been
demolished; and
   WHEREAS, the Applicant also operates an elementary school, known as Sacred
Heart School, which is located on a parcel of land on the north side of Gage
Street, opposite and to the west of Parcel 2; and
   WHEREAS, the Village has adopted Ordinance MC-8-2003, granting the
Applicant’s request for a map amendment, thereby changing the zoning
classification for Parcel 2 from R-5 Single Family Residential to B-1 Multi-
Family Residential; and
   WHEREAS, Parcel 1 and Parcel 2 (hereinafter referred to jointly as “Subject
Property”) jointly constitute a single zoning lot as defined in Section
17.040.030 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance; and
   WHEREAS, Sacred Heart Church was built at the northeast corner of Tower Road
and Burr Avenue in 1925; and
   WHEREAS, the church rectory adjoins the north end of the church building and
is located directly south of Parcel 2, facing Burr Avenue; and
   WHEREAS, the convent that currently functions as the parish center was
constructed along Tower Road to the east of the church in the early 1950’s, and
is connected to the church by a small gathering space constructed pursuant to a
special use permit that was granted in 1994; and
   WHEREAS, the Applicant has applied for a special use permit under Section
17.32.010.B.2.c and Chapter 17.56 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of
the Winnetka Village Code, to allow the construction of a new, 11,578-square
foot parish center with expanded parking on the Subject Property near the
southeast corner of Burr Avenue and Gage Street, in the part of the Subject
Property that was formerly occupied by a single family residence; and
   WHEREAS, the Applicant has also applied for the following variations from
the application of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance to the Subject Property, to
allow the construction of the new parish center: (a) a variation from the
intensity of use of lot limitations of Section 17.32.010.F, to increase the
impermeable surface by 12,768.95 square feet from the existing impermeable
surface area of 40,834.44 square feet to a total of 53,603.39 square feet,
which exceeds the maximum allowable impermeable surface of 50,144.40 square
feet by 3,458.99 square feet (6.9%); (b) a variation from the front setback
requirements of Section 17.32.010.H to allow a front setback of 17.5 feet along
both the Gage Street frontage and the Burr Avenue frontage, rather than the
required front setback of 25 feet, resulting in a variation of 7.5 feet (30%)
along both street frontages; (c) a variation from the side yard setback
requirements of Section 17.32.010.I to allow the expanded parking lot to be set
back 4.0 feet from the east lot line adjacent to the alley, rather than
observing the required side yard of 12 feet, resulting in a variation of 8.0
feet (66.67%); (d) a variation from the exterior wall provisions of Section
17.32.010.M to allow the north, west and south building walls to have
unarticulated lengths of 63 feet, 74 feet and 69 feet, respectively, each of
which exceeds the maximum unarticulated distance of 50 feet; and (e) a
variation from the principal roof form requirements of Section 17.32.010.O, to
allow the proposed parish center to have a roof with a pitch that is less than
the required minimum pitch of 35º; and
   WHEREAS, uses of a charitable and eleemosynary nature, including churches,
are permitted as special uses in the B-1 Multi-Family Residential District,
subject to the conditions and requirements pertaining to special uses, as set
Chapter 17.56 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance; and,
   WHEREAS, on April 23, 2003, on due notice thereof, the Plan Commission met
to consider the proposed parish center building and related changes to the
existing special use and, by the unanimous vote of the nine members then
present, has recommended that the special use permit be granted, finding that
it is consistent with Winnetka 2020, the Winnetka Comprehensive Plan; and
   WHEREAS, on May 12, 2003, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of Appeals
conducted a public hearing on the requested special use and variations and, by
the favorable vote of five of the six members then present, has recommended
that the building wall articulation variation be granted and, by the unanimous
vote of the six members then present, has recommended that the special use and
all of the other requested variations be granted; and
   WHEREAS, on March 20, 2003, and April 17, 2003, the Design Review Board
reviewed the proposed parish center building, site alterations and landscape
plan, and has issued favorable comment to the Village Council; and
   WHEREAS, approximately 1200 families are members of Sacred Heart parish; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed parish center is a multi-purpose building with a
circular core that will contain a large, dividable area on the main floor that
will provide a gathering space that can accommodate approximately 200 people at
tables and 300 people in an auditorium seating configuration; and
   WHEREAS, the lower level of the circular core of the proposed parish center
will contain a choir rehearsal room and a youth area, with adjacent storage;
and
   WHEREAS, the main entrance of the proposed parish center will be oriented
toward the northwest corner of the property, facing the intersection of Burr
Avenue and Gage Street, and also facing the main entrance of Sacred Heart
School on the north side of Gage Street; and
   WHEREAS, the perimeter of the proposed parish center will have straight
walls that are parallel to the property lines of Parcel 2 and will create a
lobby and hallway area with a kitchen, stairway, elevator, restrooms, small
library and additional storage and work areas on the perimeters of the main
floor and the lower level; and
   WHEREAS, the Applicant has numerous activities and ministries in addition to
scheduled Masses, including a religious education program for approximately 560
students, a high school youth group, adult choir, adult education and prayer
and study groups, that do not have sufficient space to meet; and
   WHEREAS, the primary location for the Applicant’s social gatherings,
activities and ministries is in the current parish center, which was formerly a
convent and was connected to the church building in 1994 to provide a small
gathering space; and
   WHEREAS, the current parish center houses parish offices, small classrooms
on the second floor formed by modifying convent sleeping rooms, and a
conference and meeting room on the first floor that were formerly the convent’s
dining room and sitting room; and
   WHEREAS, the Applicant does not have sufficient space to accommodate
meetings and materials storage for all of its existing activities and
ministries and the lack of space imposes scheduling and operational limitations
due to conflicting demands for the limited space in the current parish center
and school; and
   WHEREAS, the Applicant has presented evidence of a need for additional space
to accommodate its current program needs; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed special use is consistent with the goal of the
Comprehensive Plan, Winnetka 2020, to “support educational excellence and the
enrichment of Winnetka’s religious and cultural environment;” and
   WHEREAS, the proposed parish center has been designed to be consistent with
the goal of the Comprehensive Plan, Winnetka 2020, to “limit commercial,
institutional and residential development within the Village to minimize the
potentially adverse impacts on adjacent residential neighborhoods and to
prevent the need for significant increases in infrastructure (streets, parking,
utilities, sewers) and other community resources (schools, parks, recreational
facilities, etc.);” and
   WHEREAS, the proposed parish center has been designed to be consistent with
the goal of the Comprehensive Plan, Winnetka 2020, to ensure that commercial,
institutional, and residential development is appropriate to the character of
and minimizes the adverse impact on its surrounding neighborhood; and
   WHEREAS, the Applicant has met with neighbors in an attempt to minimize the
impact of the proposed special use on the immediate vicinity, in furtherance of
the objectives of the Comprehensive Plan, Winnetka 2020, to protect residential
neighborhoods and homes from the encroachment of incompatible land uses and
traffic patterns, and to engage in a public process that balances institutional
goals and minimizes adverse impacts on the character of the adjacent
residential neighborhood; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed special use is consistent with the objective of the
Comprehensive Plan, Winnetka 2020, to recognize the critical importance of
educational, religious and other community institutions to Village residents;
and
   WHEREAS, the proposed special use is consistent with the objective of the
Comprehensive Plan, Winnetka 2020, to maintain an atmosphere in which diverse
cultural, educational and religious organizations may flourish and in which
special activities for residents of all ages may be enhanced; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed special use is consistent with the objective of the
Comprehensive Plan, Winnetka 2020, to ensure safe and attractive access to
educational and community institutions, and to pursue improvements that address
public safety as well as traffic congestion and parking; and
   WHEREAS, although construction of the proposed parish center will result in
the loss of several mature trees, the Applicant has continued to consult with
the Village Forester on tree replacement and landscape issues, consistent with
the objective of the Comprehensive Plan, Winnetka 2020, to encourage new tree
planting on public and private properties to the greatest extent possible; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed special use is consistent with the recommendation of
the Comprehensive Plan, Winnetka 2020, to ensure that proposals do not have an
adverse impact on the residential character of the surrounding residential
neighborhoods; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed parish center will benefit the public health, safety,
comfort, morals and general welfare of the Village, by providing a fully
accessible building with flexible space to accommodate a variety of the
Applicant’s ministries and activities, and will provide a place for the
Applicant’s congregation to gather in large numbers; and,
   WHEREAS, the Applicant has submitted evidence that it has a current need,
without projecting further growth, to have the ability to gather large groups
of parishioners at the same time and to provide both multi-purpose space and
dedicated space for choir rehearsals, youth activities and material storage;
and
   WHEREAS, the Applicant has submitted evidence of an existing need to provide
fully accessible, adequately sized facilities to allow its activities and
programs to take place on the existing parish campus; and
   WHEREAS, there are no other locations within the immediate proximity of the
Church that would allow it to conveniently, safely and economically conduct
meetings, operate existing ministries and hold social functions off the Subject
Property; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed expansion of the Applicant’s facilities, through the
construction of the proposed parish center and an addition to Sacred Heart
School, will provide gathering space, classrooms and multi-purpose areas, all
of which will allow the Applicant to conduct all of the activities of its
ministry on its existing campus; and
   WHEREAS, the Applicant has established that conducting its activities in a
single location with new and expanded facilities is necessary to meet its
program needs and to assure the long-term viability of the Sacred Heart Church,
which has been a part of the Winnetka community for over 100 years; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed parish center will not endanger or be detrimental to
the public health, safety, comfort, morals and welfare of the inhabitants of
the Village, because the proposed parish center will provide space needed by
the Applicant for religious education, adult and youth ministry, and parish
social activities and social outreach ministries; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed addition, if constructed in
accordance with the conditions set forth in this ordinance, will substantially
diminish or impair property values in the immediate vicinity or will be
substantially injurious to the use and enjoyment of other property in the
immediate vicinity, in that the Sacred Heart Church campus has existed on the
Subject Property and the School Property on the north side of Gage Street for
over 50 years, as the surrounding single-family neighborhood has developed; and
   WHEREAS, the Sacred Heart Church is an established use located at the edge
of an established single-family residential area, and there is no evidence that
construction of the proposed addition subject to the terms and conditions of
this ordinance will impede the normal and orderly development or improvement of
other property in the immediate vicinity; and
   WHEREAS, adequate measures have been taken to provide ingress and egress in
a manner which minimizes pedestrian and vehicular traffic congestion in the
public ways, in that the minor reconfiguration of the existing parking area
will widen the access to the alley and increase visibility for cars entering
and leaving the parking lot; and
   WHEREAS, adequate utilities, access roads, drainage and other facilities
necessary for the operation of the special use on the Subject Property exist
due to the presence of the other church buildings on the Subject Property and,
to the extent that they do not, they will be provided when the proposed parish
center is constructed in compliance with applicable Village Code requirements;
and
   WHEREAS, it is not practicable to require the Church to provide additional
on-site parking, because (a) the parking area along Tower Road cannot be
expanded, (b) expanding the parking area into the north end of the Subject
Property near the proposed parish center would require changing the footprint,
height and/or location of the parish center, increasing the amount of
impermeable surface, reducing the amount of green space and further reducing
the number of trees, (c) the proposed special use is not expected to increase
the number of parishioners, and (d0 peak parking demands occur on Sunday
mornings when parking is available on the streets in the immediate vicinity and
in the Village parking lot at Tower and Green Bay Roads; and
   WHEREAS, the special use, when constructed in accordance with the terms and
conditions of this ordinance, will conform to the applicable regulations of the
Zoning Ordinance and the Winnetka Village Code;
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and particular hardships in
carrying out the strict letter of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance as it applies
to the Subject Property in that: (a) the 1.92 acre Subject Property is bounded
on the north by Gage Street, on the south by Tower Road, on the west by Burr
Avenue and on the east by a public alley that separates the Subject Property
from the commercial properties on the west side of Green Bay Road; (b) the
Village of Winnetka is a mature community and no alternative sites of
sufficient size are available for the Applicant’s facilities; (c) although the
Subject Property is four-sided, it is nearly triangular in shape and the
narrow, northern portion is the only open area available for the proposed
parish center; (d) the additional front setbacks required along Burr Avenue and
Gage Street for the proposed parish center further restricting the amount of
buildable area for the proposed parish center; and (e) the Applicant’s
ministry, such as worship, religious education, meetings, social outreach
ministries, youth activities and parish social activities, will be
substantially burdened without the proposed parish center; and
   WHEREAS, unless the requested relief is granted, the property in question
cannot yield a reasonable return to the Applicant, in that the area for the
proposed addition would be rendered useless to the Applicant for its ministry;
and
   WHEREAS, the plight of the Applicant is due to unique circumstances, because
of the practical difficulties and particular hardships described above, and
because the Applicant’s use of the Subject Property is for the furtherance of
its religious ministry rather than the private comfort, convenience or monetary
benefits that typically attach to residential and commercial uses of property;
and
   WHEREAS, the variations, if granted, will not alter the essential character
of the locality, in that: (a) the proposed parish center will be part of the
established parish campus; and (b) the proposed parish center has been designed
to be compatible with the architecture and materials of the existing church,
school and other parish buildings, while maintaining a visual scale that is
compatible with the single-family residential uses on the west side of Burr
Avenue; and
   WHEREAS, an adequate supply of light and air to adjacent property will not
be impaired, because the Subject Property is bounded on its north, west and
south sides by public streets, and on the east by a public alley, the new
parish center will be a substantial distance from the nearest single-family
residence, the new parish center will conform with applicable height
limitations, and the tallest part of the new building will be near the center
of the property, away from the streets; and
   WHEREAS, the hazard from fire and other damages to the property will not be
increased, since the addition will be constructed in accordance with all
applicable building codes, and life safety codes; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed parish center is generally an improvement to the
Subject Property and there is no evidence that the taxable value of land and
buildings throughout the Village will diminish as a result of the proposed
addition; and
   WHEREAS, congestion in the public streets will not increase because the
parish center is being constructed to meet an existing need, rather than
projected growth, the weekend Mass schedule will not change, and the heaviest
traffic volumes will continue to occur during off-peak times.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain:
   SECTION 1:   That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated into this
section as the findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully
set forth herein.
   SECTION 2:   That, pursuant to Section 17.32.010.B.2.c and Chapter 17.56 of
the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, and subject to the terms and conditions
hereinafter set forth, a special use permit is hereby granted to the Subject
Property, commonly known as Sacred Heart Parish, 1077 Tower Road and 919 Burr
Avenue, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the B-1 Multi-Family Residential
Zoning District provided in the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, to allow the
construction of a new, 11,578-square foot parish center with expanded parking
on the Subject Property, near the southeast corner of Burr Avenue and Gage
Street.
   SECTION 3: That, subject to the terms and conditions hereinafter set forth,
the following variations are hereby granted to the Subject Property, commonly
known as Sacred Heart Parish, 1077 Tower Road and 919 Burr Avenue, Winnetka,
Illinois, and located in the B-1 Multi-Family Residential Zoning District
provided in the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, to allow the construction of a new,
11,578-square foot parish center with expanded parking on the Subject Property
near the southeast corner of Burr Avenue and Gage Street: (a) a variation from
the intensity of use of lot limitations of Section 17.32.010.F, to increase the
impermeable surface 12,768.95 square feet from the existing impermeable surface
area of 40,834.44 square feet to a total of 53,603.39 square feet, which
exceeds the maximum allowable impermeable surface of 50,144.40 square feet by
3,458.99 square feet (6.9%); (b) a variation from the setback requirements of
Section 17.32.010.H to allow a front setback of 17.5 feet along both the Gage
Street frontage and the Burr Avenue frontage, rather than the required front
setback of 25 feet, resulting in a variation of 7.5 feet (30%) along both
street frontages; (c) a variation from the side yard requirements of Section
17.32.010.I to allow the expanded parking lot to be set back 4.0 feet from the
east lot line along the alley, rather than observing the required side yard of
12 feet, resulting in a variation of 8.0 feet (66.67%); (d) a variation from
the exterior wall provisions of Section 17.32.010.M to allow the north, west
and south building walls to have unarticulated lengths of 63 feet, 74 feet and
69 feet, respectively, each of which exceeds the maximum unarticulated distance
of 50 feet; and (e) a variation from the principal roof form requirements of
Section 17.32.010.O, to allow the proposed parish center to have a flat roof
rather than a pitch of between 35º and 60º.
   SECTION 4: That the variations and special use permit hereby granted are
subject to the following conditions:
      A.   Prior to the issuance of any building permits, the Applicant shall
enter into a development agreement with the Village that shall contain
provisions regarding: (1) construction traffic and staging areas; (2) parking
for construction workers; (3) parking and traffic patterns for staff parking
and student drop-off and pick-up during the construction period; (4) ingress
and egress for fires and other emergencies that may occur while the building is
under construction; and (5) any of the topics set forth in Section 15.32.080.K
of the Winnetka Village Code that apply to the circumstances of the Applicant’s
project. If construction of the parish center addition is scheduled to take
place during the same time periods as the construction of the school building
addition that is the subject of Ordinance M-23-2003, the development agreement
requirements for both projects may be included in a single development
agreement that will address both the school addition and the new parish center.
      B.   The garage on Parcel 2 (commonly known as 919 Burr Avenue) shall be
removed upon completion of construction.
      C.   The proposed construction shall commence within 12 months after the
effective date of this Ordinance.
      D.   The building and other improvements shall be as depicted in the
plans and elevations that accompanied the application for special use and
variations, with building elevations and design materials as revised and
approved by the Design Review Board.
   SECTION 5 : The stipulations, conditions and restrictions set forth in the
foregoing Section 4 of this Ordinance may be modified or revised from time to
time by the Village Council following public notice and hearing, following the
procedures specified in Section 17.56 of the Winnetka Village Code for
processing special use applications.
   SECTION 6: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 17th day of June, 2003, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Abell, Brower, Presser and Webster
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    Trustees Greenough and Woodbury
   Signed:
   s/s Michael F. Duhl
    Village President
Countersigned:
s/s Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: June 3, 2003
Posted: June 4, 2003
Passed and Approved: June 17, 2003
Posted: June 18, 2003
ORDINANCE NO. M-25-2003
AN ORDINANCE AUTHORIZING THE SALE OR OTHER DISPOSITION OF PERSONAL PROPERTY OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA
   WHEREAS, from time to time, the Winnetka Police Department comes into
possession of items of personal property that have been lost, found or stolen,
which personal property includes the “Bicycles” described in Exhibit A and the
“Miscellaneous Property” described in Exhibit B (hereinafter collectively
referred to as the “Personal Property”), attached to this ordinance; and
   WHEREAS, the Personal Property has been in the possession of the Winnetka
Police Department for more than 6 months, during which time the Chief of Police
of the Winnetka Police Department has made reasonable inquiry and efforts to
identify and notify the owners or other persons entitled to possession of the
Personal Property, but has been unsuccessful in those attempts; and
   WHEREAS, pursuant to Section 3 of the Law Enforcement Disposition of
Property Act, 765 ILCS 1030/3, the Chief of Police has transferred the custody
of the Personal Property to the Village of Winnetka; and
   WHEREAS, the Village of Winnetka owns computer equipment, some of which will
be retired as it is replaced during the fiscal year, April 1, 2003 through
March 31, 2004 (hereinafter referred to as “Retired Computer Equipment”), and
which the Finance Director has recommended be disposed of by methods that are
consistent with past disposal methods; and
   WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka have determined that the
Personal Property and Retired Computer Equipment described herein (hereinafter
referred to collectively as the “Surplus Property”) are no longer necessary or
useful to the Village of Winnetka, that the cost of storing said Surplus
Property exceeds its value to the Village of Winnetka, that it is not in the
best interests of the Village of Winnetka to retain said Surplus Property and
that the Surplus Property should be disposed of in the manner hereinafter
provided, pursuant to Section 11-76-4 of the Illinois Municipal Code (65 ILCS
5/11-76-4), so as to avoid incurring additional costs for storing said Surplus
Property.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain:
   SECTION 1 : Pursuant to Section 11-76-4 of the Illinois Municipal Code (65
ILCS 5/11-76-4), the Council of the Village of Winnetka find that the above-
described Surplus Property, consisting of the items of Bicycles described in
the attached Exhibits A, the Miscellaneous Property described in the attached
Exhibit B, and the Retired Computer Equipment described in the foregoing
recitals, are not necessary or useful to the Village of Winnetka and that the
best interests of the Village of Winnetka will be served by the sale or other
disposition of such Surplus Property.
   SECTION 2 : The Council of the Village of Winnetka further find that, based
on prior experience in disposing of similar items of property, the cost of
conducting a public sale of the Surplus Property, including the costs of
advertising and publishing, and the costs of personnel needed to maintain
security and to conduct the public sale, exceeds the value of such items.
   SECTION 3: Pursuant to said Section 11-76-4, the Village Manager is hereby
authorized and directed to determine the value of and to dispose of the
Personal Property in any manner he deems reasonable under the circumstances,
with or without public notice.
   SECTION 4: Pursuant to said Section 11-76-4, the Director of Finance is
hereby authorized and directed to determine the value of and to dispose of
Retired Computer Equipment that is retired during the fiscal year April 1,
2003, through March 31, 2004, using disposal methods that are consistent with
past disposal practices.
   SECTION 5: This Ordinance shall be in full force and effect from and after
its passage, approval, and posting.
   ADOPTED this 1st day of July, 2003, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Abell, Brower, Presser, Webster and Woodbury
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    Trustee Greenough
   Signed:
   s/s Michael F. Duhl
    Village President
Countersigned:
s/s Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: June 17, 2003
Posted: June 18, 2003
Passed and Approved: July 1, 2003
Posted: July 2, 2003
