ORDINANCE NO. M-28-2003
AN ORDINANCE ESTABLISHING 739 ELM STREET AS A DESIGNATED LANDMARK
   WHEREAS, the Village of Winnetka desires to identify, designate and preserve
buildings and structures in the Village that are historically, culturally, and
architecturally significant and to encourage the restoration and rehabilitation
of those buildings and structures; and
   WHEREAS, pursuant to Sections 11-48.2-1, et seq. of the Illinois Municipal
Code (65 ILCS 5/11-48.2-1, et seq.), the Council of the Village of Winnetka
enacted Chapter 15.64 of Title 15 of the Winnetka Village Code, entitled
“Landmark Preservation,” for the purposes of preserving, protecting, enhancing,
rehabilitating and regulating buildings, structures, objects, and places of
historical, cultural or architectural importance; and
   WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka have determined that it is
in the best interests of the Village and its residents that participation in
the landmark designation process shall be limited to those owners who
voluntarily elect to participate in that process; and
   WHEREAS, pursuant to the provisions of Chapter 15.64 of the Winnetka Village
Code (the “Landmark Ordinance”) and notice duly published and sent, the
Landmark Preservation Commission conducted a public hearing on April 7, 2003,
to consider the application of the Winnetka Historical Society for landmark
designation of the property located at 739 Elm Street (permanent real estate
index number 5-20-204-012); and
   WHEREAS, the Landmark Preservation Commission applied the Village’s System
for the Evaluation of Landmarks to the information received at the hearing and
made findings regarding the property at 739 Elm Street, including the
following:
      (1)   the 2½-story commercial building on the property which has been
used to house the offices of New Trier Township since 1972, was constructed in
1922 as the Winnetka State Bank building by architect Clifford Shopbell, is in
the three-story Tudor revival style with a three-bay façade and is a superior
example of commercial structures of the 1920s; and
      (2)   the building is a masonry structure of superior materials and
details and is constructed of red brick, limestone quoins, entablatures,
capstones, and a slate roof, with a copper gutter, scupper and downspouts
system; and
      (3)   the building’s association with an historical event and/or persons
is of the state, county or local level, as the original bank was organized by
several early Winnetka residents, Henry R. Hale, Carlton Prouty, Leonard Roche
and John Leonard, and since 1974, has been known to the community’s residents
as the New Trier Township offices; and
      (4)   the building’s association with architect Clifford Shopbell is at
the National level, as Mr. Shopbell earned considerable recognition in the
Midwest, was closely associated with the Carnegie Foundation and the evolution
of the architecture for the Carnegie Libraries which association is reflected
in the care and quality of the 739 Elm Street building, and designed 21
structures in Evansville, Indiana that are on the National Register of Historic
Places; and
      (5)   the building’s design has been altered only in that: (1) the
original windows were replaced in 1974 with bronze-tinted and bronze-anodized
aluminum frames, which, if restored, would return the structure to its original
conditions; and (2) the original central entry way door was successfully
relocated to the eastern bay, probably by re-using the original limestone; and
      (6)   the site of the property is considered to be original, as
illustrated by historic photographs of the 739 Elm Street property as well as
the Eckart Hardware store building immediately to the east and the commercial
building immediately to the west; and
      (7)   the structure of the building is considered to be in exceptional
condition and has retained its original appearance with the exception of the
modification of the windows in 1974 and the relocation of the entry way.
   WHEREAS, based on its findings, the Landmark Preservation Commission has
concluded that the residence located at 739 Elm Street is unique and meets the
criteria of the Landmark Ordinance and has unanimously recommended that it be
so designated; and
   WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka have considered the
recommendation of the Landmark Preservation Commission and have determined that
it is in the best interest of the Village and its residents to establish the
building located at 739 Elm Street as a designated landmark.
   NOW THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain:
   SECTION 1: That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2: That the property located at 739 Elm Street is hereby designated
a landmark under the Landmark Ordinance, in accordance with the findings and
recommendations of the Landmark Preservation Commission.
   SECTION 3: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 15th day of July, 2003, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Abell, Brower, Greenough, Presser and Webster
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    Trustee Woodbury
   Signed:
   s/s Michael F. Duhl
    Village President
Countersigned:
s/s Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
