ORDINANCE NO. M- 29-2003
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (693 Sheridan Road)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
That part of the Northwesterly 69 feet of Lot 6 and the Southeasterly 38 feet
of Lot 7 lying Southwesterly of center line of easement recorded in May 13,
1941 in Book 339 of Plats, Page 7 as Document 12679092 in W.H.Garland’s
Subdivision of Blocks 2,3 and 4 in J. Garland’s Subdivision of the Southeast ¼
of Section 17 and the southwest ¼ of Section 16, Township 42, Range 13, East of
the Third Principal Meridian, in Cook County, Illinois,
commonly known as 693 Sheridan Road, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
2 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided for in Chapter 17.24 of
the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for a
variation from the setback requirements of Section 17.30.050 to allow a corner
yard setback of 12.09 feet along the east lot line, whereas a setback of 28.95
feet is required, resulting in a variation of 16.86 feet (58.24%), for the
purpose of constructing a new single-family residence on the Subject Property;
and
   WHEREAS, on June 9, 2003, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of Appeals
conducted a public hearing on the requested variation and by the unanimous vote
of the six members then present, has reported to the Council recommending that
the requested variation be denied; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and unique hardships associated
with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect
to the Subject Property in that: (a) the Subject Property is an irregularly
shaped lot located on the east side of Sheridan Road; (b) the south and east
lot lines are formed by a private road that serves at least three parcels of
property; (c) the Subject Property is considered to be a corner lot with three
street frontages because the private road constitutes a street for zoning
purposes; (d) the applicants propose to construct a new home on the Subject
Property in approximately the same location as the existing home, which has a
nonconforming south corner yard; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that:
(a) the proposed residence cannot be shifted 16 feet to the north, because the
proposed north side yard is 16.33 feet; and (b) reducing the width of the
building will result in extending the footprint further to the east, requiring
relocation of the proposed garage and driveway into an area of the lot that
contains several substantial oak trees, which would reduce the value of the
Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because the proposed new construction will be in approximately
the same location and of approximately the same width as the residence that it
will replace, and the existing, mature trees located in required yards on the
property will be preserved; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not impair an adequate supply of light
and air because the new single-family residence will be of a conforming height
and will be located more than 60 feet from the residence to the south; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property, as the new home will comply with all
applicable building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the new single-family residence will not contribute to congestion
on the public streets, as the redevelopment of the property will provide the
required off-street parking; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed new single-family residence
will otherwise impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare
of the inhabitants of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation is in harmony with the general purpose and
intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that it allows the construction of
a new residence while maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the
community and protecting established trees and landscaping.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2:  There is hereby granted to the Subject Property, commonly known
as 693 Sheridan Road, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-2 Single-Family
Residential Zoning District provided for in Chapter 17.24 of the Winnetka
Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, a variation from the
setback requirements of Section 17.30.050 to allow a corner yard setback of
12.09 feet along the east lot line, whereas a setback of 28.95 feet is
required, resulting in a variation of 16.86 feet (58.24%), for the purpose of
constructing a new single-family residence on the Subject Property, in
accordance with the site plans and elevations submitted with the application
for building permit.
   SECTION 3: That the variation hereby granted is conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 19th day of August, 2003, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Brower, Greenough, Presser, Webster and Woodbury
   NAYS:    Trustee Abell
   ABSENT:    None
   Signed:
   s/s Michael F. Duhl
    Village President
Countersigned:
s/s Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: August 5, 2003
Posted: August 6, 2003
Passed and Approved: August 19, 2003
