ORDINANCE NO. M-30-2003
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (714 Park Lane)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
That part of Lot 2 in Resubdivision of Block 8 in Park Addition to Winnetka,
recorded as Document # 1049827, lying east of a line at right angles to the
south line of said Lot 2 at a point 135 feet west of southeast corner of said
Lot 2 in Winnetka, in Cook County, Illinois (in Section 16, Township 42 North,
Range 13, east of the Third Principal Meridian),
commonly known as 714 Park Lane, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
2 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided for in Chapter 17.24 of
the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for a
variation from the front setback requirements of Section 17.30.050 to allow a
front setback of 11.33 feet, whereas a 50-foot setback is required, resulting
in a variation of 38.57 feet (77.34%), for the purpose of constructing an in-
ground swimming pool on the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, on July 14, 2003, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variation and by a
favorable vote of 5 to 2 of the seven members then present, has reported to the
Council recommending that the requested variation be granted; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and unique hardships associated
with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect
to the Subject Property in that: (a) the Subject Property is an irregularly-
shaped lot, with a curving east lot line that follows the edge of the partially
improved right-of-way of Park Street; (b) due to the curving lot line, the
135.00-foot long south lot line is slightly more than one half the length of
the north lot line, which measures 248.30 feet in length; (c) the residence on
the Subject Property is oriented to face the south lot line, with access by way
of a driveway at the southeast corner of the Subject Property to a garage that
is located in the southeast corner of the residence; (d) because of the curve
of the Park Street right-of-way, the 50-foot setback line cuts through the
southeast corner of the existing residence and garage and substantially reduces
the buildable area in the northeast corner of the Subject Property; (e) the
buildable area behind the house is further reduced by the presence of a steep
hill on the western end of the Subject Property and by a restrictive covenant
that prevents new construction in the northern part of the Subject Property;
(f) constructing a swimming pool in a conforming location would disturb the
natural topography of the Subject Property; and (g) constructing a swimming
pool in a conforming location would have a negative impact on the use and
enjoyment of the property directly to the north of the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that,
although the Subject Property has substantial open space and is more than 7,000
square feet larger than the minimum lot size required in the R-2 Zoning
District, the strict application of the front setback requirement either would
effectively preclude construction of a swimming pool of a functional size or
would cause the pool to be constructed in an area of the Subject Property that
would conflict with the Subject Property’s topography and/or restrictive
covenant, which would reduce the value of the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because the swimming pool will be screened by landscaping and
will not be visible either from the residence on the property or from Sheridan
Road; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not impair an adequate supply of light
and air because the swimming pool and surrounding deck will be at grade level;
and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property as the proposed swimming pool will comply
with all applicable building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the construction of a swimming pool will not contribute to
congestion on the public streets, as the principal use of the Subject Property
will not be changed; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed swimming pool will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation is in harmony with the general purpose and
intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that it allows the improvement of
developed property by adding amenities that enhance and maintain the exiting
building while maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the community
and protecting established trees and landscaping.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2:  There is hereby granted to the Subject Property, commonly known
as 714 Park Lane, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-2 Single-Family
Residential Zoning District provided for in Chapter 17.24 of the Winnetka
Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, a variation from the
front setback requirements of Section 17.30.050 to allow a front setback of
11.33 feet, whereas a 50-foot setback is required, resulting in a variation of
38.57 feet (77.34%), for the purpose of constructing an in-ground swimming pool
on the Subject Property, in accordance with the site and landscaping plans
submitted with the application for variation.
   SECTION 3: That the variation hereby granted is conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 19th day of August, 2003, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Abell, Brower, Greenough, Presser, Webster and Woodbury
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    None
   Signed:
   s/s Michael F. Duhl
    Village President
Countersigned:
s/s Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: August 5, 2003
Posted: August 6, 2003
Passed and Approved: August 19, 2003
Posted: August 20, 2003
ORDINANCE NO. M-31-2003
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (1127 Sheridan Road)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
Parcel 1: Lot 3 in McIlvaine’s Subdivision of Lot 2 in the Re-Subdivision of
Campbell’s Subdivision of Block 1 in Hubbard Estate Subdivision in the
Northeast Fractional ¼ of Section 17, Township 42 North, Range 13, East of the
Third Principal Meridian, reference being had to plat of said McIlvaine’s
Subdivision recorded March 5, 1909 as Document No. 4337612, in Cook County,
Illinois.
Parcel 2: That part of the Northeast Fractional ¼ of Section 17, Township 42
North, Range 13, East of the Third Principal Meridian, described as follows:
Beginning at the point of intersection of the North and Northwest lines of
Hubbard Estate Subdivision; said point being two thousand six hundred ten and
three tenths feet north of the south line and seven hundred twenty nine and
seven tenths feet east of the west line of said Northeast Fractional ¼ Section;
thence southwesterly in a straight line 297 2/10ths feet to a point in the
easterly line of Sheridan Road said point being two thousand five hundred five
feet north of the south line and four hundred fifty feet east of the west line
of said ¼ Section; thence northwesterly along the east line of said Sheridan
Road twenty-six feet more or less to a point two thousand five hundred twenty
seven and eighty four one hundredths feet north of the south line and four
hundred thirty eight and eight tenths feet east of the line west line of said
quarter section; thence northeasterly in a straight line three hundred two and
seventy five one hundredths feet more or less to the place of beginning, all in
Cook County, Illinois,
commonly known as 1127 Sheridan Road, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
2 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.24 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for a
variation from Section 17.30.110 to allow an accessory structure height of 29
feet, whereas a maximum of 18 feet is permitted, resulting in a variation of 11
feet (61.11%), for the purpose of constructing a two-story detached garage on
the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, on July 14, 2003, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variation and by the
unanimous vote of the seven members then present, has reported to the Council
recommending that the requested variation be granted; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and unique hardships associated
with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect
to the Subject Property in that: (a) the Subject Property is an irregularly
shaped lot located in the ravines along the east side of Sheridan Road north of
Tower Road; (b) although the Subject Property is more than 10,000 square feet
larger than the minimum required lot size, the topography limits the buildable
area of the lot; (c) the single-family residence on the Subject Property is
located on the tableland at the top of the bluff; (d) access to the top of the
bluff is by way of a long, sloping driveway located generally along the south
lot line; (e) the Subject Property has access to Lake Michigan at the bottom of
the bluff; (f) the proposed two-story garage will replace a garage that
currently sits on the tableland at the top of the bluff; (g) due to the
topography of the Subject Property, most of the south side of the garage will
be exposed for the full height of the garage, which will have a height of 29
feet as measured from the lowest adjacent natural grade; (h) the two-story
garage will allow for storage of a boat at the bottom of the bluff, closer to
the edge of the water; (i) the proposed two-story garage will allow the
impermeable surface of the property to be reduced by occupying less lot area
and reducing the length of the driveway; (j) the lower level of the two-story
garage will not be visible from the north, east or west, with only the top
story and roof being visible from those directions at the top of the bluff; and
(k) although building a comparably sized garage with a conforming height would
substantially increase the amount of bulk on the top of the bluff; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that:
(a) building a comparably sized garage with a conforming height would
substantially reduce useable open space on the tableland of the Subject
Property; (b) there is no suitable location on the table land for a comparably
sized, conforming garage; and (c) the proposed two-story garage will increase
on-site storage, provide more easily accessible storage for a boat, reduce the
length of the driveway and the amount of impermeable surface on the Subject
Property, all of which will enable the owner to preserve useable open space at
the top of the bluff and to make reasonable use of the Subject Property’s
access to Lake Michigan; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because: (a) the red brick and stone design of the proposed
garage is consistent with the architectural style and period of the house on
the Subject Property; and (b) the design and scale of the proposed garage
maintains open space and reduces the amount of visual bulk at the top of the
bluff; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not impair an adequate supply of light
and air because the lower story of the proposed garage will be set into the
bluff and the top story will have what would otherwise be a conforming height
of 18 feet from the grade at the top of the bluff; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property, as the new garage will comply with all
applicable building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed detached garage will not contribute to congestion on
the public streets, as the Subject Property will continue to be used for
single-family residential purposes and the new garage will continue to provide
off-street parking for the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed detached garage will
otherwise impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation is in harmony with the general purpose and
intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that it allows the construction of
a garage and exterior improvements to an existing single-family property while
maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the community and protecting
established trees and landscaping.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2:  There is hereby granted to the Subject Property, commonly known
as 1127 Sheridan Road, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-2 Single-Family
Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.24 of the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, a variation from Section
17.30.110 to allow an accessory structure height of 29 feet, whereas a maximum
of 18 feet is permitted, resulting in a variation of 11 feet (61.11%), for the
purpose of constructing a two-story detached garage on the Subject Property, in
accordance with the plans and elevations submitted with the application for
variation.
   SECTION 3: That the variation hereby granted is conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 19th day of August, 2003, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Abell, Brower, Greenough, Presser, Webster and Woodbury
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    None
   Signed:
   s/s Michael F. Duhl
    Village President
Countersigned:
s/s Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: August 5, 2003
Posted: August 6, 2003
Passed and Approved: August 19, 2003
Posted: August 20, 2003
ORDINANCE NO. M-32-2003
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (680 Elder Lane)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
The North 58 feet of the West 6.55 feet of Lot 4 and the North 58 feet of the
East 124.45 feet of Lot 1 in Lydia D. Sutters Subdivision of part of the West ½
of Block 4 in John C. Garlands Addition to Village of Winnetka, (including
streets), being that part of said Block 4 lying West of a lined 436.55 feet
East and parallel with center line of Forest Avenue in the Southwest ¼ of
Section 21, Township 42 North, Range 13, East of the Third Principal Meridian,
in Cook County, Illinois,
commonly known as 680 Elder Lane, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
5 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.12 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for
variations from: (a) the maximum building size requirements of Section
17.30.040 to permit a gross floor area of 3,220.21 square feet, whereas a
maximum of 3,039,20 square feet is permitted, resulting in a variation of
181.01 square feet (5.6%); (b) the front setback provisions of Section
17.30.050 to allow a front setback of 4.75 feet, whereas a minimum of 31.92
square feet is required, resulting in a variation of 27.17 feet (85.12%); and
(c) the setback requirements of Section 17.30.060 to allow a side yard setback
of 8.08 feet, whereas a minimum of 12 feet is required, resulting in a
variation of 3.92 feet (32.67%), all for the purpose of constructing a two and
one half-story addition to the residence on the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, on August 11, 2003, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variation and by the
unanimous vote of the five members then present, has reported to the Council
recommending that the requested variation be granted; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and particular hardships
associated with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance
with respect to the Subject Property in that: (a) the Subject Property is a
non-conforming lot, having a non-conforming lot area as well as a non-
conforming lot depth of 58 feet; (b) the shallow lot depth, coupled with an
unusually large lot width of 131 feet, results in a short, wide buildable lot
area that is located in the rear half of the Subject Property; (c) the existing
residence is a legal non-conforming structure located near the northeast corner
of the lot; and (d) substantial portions of the existing residence encroach
into both the front yard setback and the east side yard setback; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that:
(a) the proposed front setback variation is being requested to allow structural
changes to the existing, non-conforming front dormer, although the changes will
not increase the encroachment into the front yard setback; (b) the variations
are also being requested to allow the existing residence, which is structurally
sound, to be brought up to contemporary living standards by providing an eat-in
area in the kitchen, adding a mud room and a study on the first floor, and
adding a second floor master suite; (c) the variations required for the
proposed two-story addition will also provide a small amount of attic storage,
to compensate for the absence of a basement that could provide additional
storage; (d) the existing non-conformities in the residence cannot be cured
without demolishing the residence and constructing a new building; and (e) it
is not possible to build a comparable replacement home in a conforming location
on the Subject Property, because the buildable area of the Subject Property has
a depth of only 18 feet and is located in the rear half of the Subject
Property; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because: (a) the structural changes to the front dormer will
not encroach further into the front yard setback; (b) the variation in the side
yard setback will allow the existing non-conforming east wall of the building
to be extended to the south without encroaching further into the east side
yard; (c) the bulk of the addition is located near the rear of the house and is
minimally visible from the street; and (d) the addition has been designed to
enhance the appearance of the existing residence, while maintaining its
orientation on the Subject Property and within the streetscape; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not impair an adequate supply of light
and air because the proposed addition complies with the building height
limitations of the Zoning Ordinance and is located away from the residence
located on the adjacent lot to the east, which is set back further in the lot
and located closer to its own east lot line; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property as the work will comply with all
applicable building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not diminish the taxable value of
land and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed addition will not contribute to congestion on the
public streets, as: the Subject Property for single family residential purposes
will not change; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed addition will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations are in harmony with the general purpose
and intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that it allows the renovation,
restoration and rehabilitation of a structurally sound existing building while
maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the community and protecting
established trees and landscaping.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2: That the following variations are hereby granted to the Subject
Property, commonly known as 680 Elder Lane, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in
the R-5 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.12 of
the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code: (a) a
variation from the maximum building size requirements of Section 17.30.040 to
permit a gross floor area of 3,220.21 square feet, whereas a maximum of
3,039,20 square feet is permitted, resulting in a variation of 181.01 square
feet (5.6%); (b) a variation from the front setback provisions of Section
17.30.050 to allow a front setback of 4.75 feet, whereas a minimum of 31.92
square feet is required, resulting in a variation of 27.17 feet (85.12%); and
(c) a variation from the setback requirements of Section 17.30.060 to allow a
side yard setback of 8.08 feet, whereas a minimum of 12 feet is required,
resulting in a variation of 3.92 feet (32.67%), all for the purpose of
constructing a two and one half-story addition to the residence on the Subject
Property, in accordance with the plans and elevations submitted with the
application for variation, with modifications to building height as reflected
in drawings date-stamped July 31, 2003.
   SECTION 3: That the variations hereby granted are conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 16th day of September, 2003, pursuant to the following roll
call vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Abell, Brower, Greenough, Webster and Woodbury
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    Trustee Presser
   Signed:
   s/s Michael F. Duhl
    Village President
Countersigned:
s/s Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: September 2, 2003
Posted: September 3, 2003
Passed and Approved: September 16, 2003
Posted: September 17, 2003
ORDINANCE NO. M-33-2003
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (155 Thorntree Lane)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
The North 58 feet of the West 6.55 feet of Lot 4 and the North 58 feet of the
East 124.45 feet of Lot 1 in Lydia D. Sutters Subdivision of part of the West ½
of Block 4 in John C. Garlands Addition to Village of Winnetka, (including
streets), being that part of said Block 4 lying West of a lined 436.55 feet
East and parallel with center line of Forest Avenue in the Southwest ¼ of
Section 21, Township 42 North, Range 13, East of the Third Principal Meridian,
in Cook County, Illinois,
commonly known as 155 Thorntree Lane, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
2 Single Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.24 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for a
variation from the setback provisions of Section 17.30.050 to allow a front
setback of 35 feet, whereas a 50-foot setback is required, resulting in a
variation of 15 feet (30%), for the purpose of constructing an attached garage
on the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, on August 11, 2003, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variation and, by the
unanimous vote of the five members then present, has reported to the Council
recommending that the requested variation be granted; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and particular hardships
associated with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance
with respect to the Subject Property in that: (a) the Subject Property is an
irregularly-shaped flag lot; (b) the single family residence on the Subject
Property was constructed in 1920, prior to the enactment of the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance; (c) the “flag pole” portion of the Subject Property is located at
its northwest corner; (d) the existing detached garage, which the owners
propose to replace with an attached garage, is located near the northwest
corner of the Subject Property, adjacent to the “flag pole” portion of the
Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that:
(a) most of the existing garage is in a conforming location; (b) the proposed
attached garage that will replace the existing detached garage will also lie
primarily in a conforming location; (c) because of the angle of the north lot
line and resulting front setback line, it is not possible to construct a
replacement garage in a conforming location in the northwest corner of the
Subject Property without removing or reconfiguring portions of the residence;
and (d) because the residence is oriented to face the east lot line,
constructing a new garage in a conforming location would require placing it on
the east side of the Subject Property, in the area that functions as the front
yard of the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because the attached garage will be in the same area of the
Subject Property as the detached garage that it will replace, and the new,
attached garage will be integrated into the structure and architectural design
of the existing residence; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not impair an adequate supply of light
and air because the proposed garage addition complies with the height
limitations of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, will bring the west side yard
setback into conformity with the Zoning Ordinance and will encroach no more
than six more feet into the north, front yard setback than the existing
detached garage; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property as the work will comply with all
applicable building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed attached garage will not contribute to congestion on
the public streets, as the use of the Subject Property will not change and the
new garage will provide additional off-street parking; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed attached garage will
otherwise impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation is in harmony with the general purpose and
intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that it allows the renovation,
restoration and rehabilitation of a structurally sound existing building while
maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the community and protecting
established trees and landscaping.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2: That the Subject Property, commonly known as 155 Thorntree Lane,
Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-2 Single Family Residential Zoning
District provided in Chapter 17.24 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17
of the Winnetka Village Code, is hereby granted a variation from the setback
provisions of Section 17.30.050 to allow a front setback of 35 feet, whereas a
50-foot setback is required, resulting in a variation of 15 feet (30%), for the
purpose of constructing an attached garage on the Subject Property, in
accordance with the plans and elevations submitted with the application for
variation.
   SECTION 3: That the variation hereby granted is conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 16th day of SEptember, 2003, pursuant to the following roll
call vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Abell, Brower, Greenough, Webster and Woodbury
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    Trustee Presser
   Signed:
   s/s Michael F. Duhl
    Village President
Countersigned:
s/s Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: September 2, 2003
Posted: September 3, 2003
Passed and Approved: September 16, 2003
Posted: September 17, 2003
ORDINANCE NO. M-34-2003
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (780 Foxdale)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
Lot 4 (except the Westerly 30 feet of said lot) in Block 6 in Winnetka Park
Bluffs, a subdivision by William H. Cairnduff of part of Sections 16, 17 and
20, Township 42 North, Range 13, East of the Third Principal Meridian, in the
Village of Winnetka, in Cook County, Illinois,
commonly known as 780 Foxdale, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
5 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.12 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for a
variation from the maximum building size provisions of Section 17.30.040 to
allow a gross floor area of 2,571.34 square feet, whereas a maximum of 2,400
square feet is permitted, resulting in a variation of 171.34 square feet
(7.14%), for the purpose of constructing an two-story addition, garage and open
entry porch to the residence on the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, on August 11, 2003, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variation and by the
unanimous vote of the five members then present, has reported to the Council
recommending that the requested variation be granted; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and particular hardships
associated with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance
with respect to the Subject Property in that: (a) the Subject Property has a
non-conforming lot area of 6,000 square feet; (b) the Subject Property is
located on the west side of Foxdale and adjoins 20 feet of undeveloped green
space; (c) the adjoining green space was formerly part of the Subject Property,
but was taken in an eminent domain proceeding in conjunction with the placing
of the North Shore Railroad right-of-way below grade; (d) the existing house on
the Subject Property requires significant improvement in order to bring it up
to the standards of comparable properties in the neighborhood; (e) due to the
small lot size and the configuration of the existing house, the permitted floor
area is not sufficient to construct a modest addition; (f) the proposed
addition is adjacent to the dining room of the existing house and includes an
open entry porch along the south side of the house; (g) because of the
orientation of the proposed addition, the new entry porch sits against two
building walls and therefore contributes 173.3 square feet towards gross floor
area; and (g) the requested gross floor area variation is less than the total
area of the new entry porch; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that:
(a) the existing gross floor area of the house is substantially less than the
standard on comparable properties in the neighborhood; (b) the position of the
existing house, front entrance and driveway on the Subject Property preclude
constructing a porch with three open sides, which would be exempt from
contributing to gross floor area; and (c) eliminating the need for a variation
by eliminating the porch would reduce the benefits of the addition to the
house, would negatively impact ingress and egress from the house and addition,
and would negatively affect the appearance of the house from the street, thus
reducing the value of the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because the proposed addition is at the rear of the house and
the scale of the house with the addition remains in keeping with the scale of
other homes in the immediate vicinity; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not impair an adequate supply of light
and air because the side porch remains open and conforms with side yard setback
requirements; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property as it will comply with all applicable
building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the two-story addition will not contribute to congestion on the
public streets, as the use of the Subject Property will not change; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed construction will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation is in harmony with the general purpose and
intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that it allows the renovation,
restoration and rehabilitation of a structurally sound existing building while
maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the community and protecting
established trees and landscaping.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2: That the Subject Property, commonly known as 780 Foxdale,
Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-5 Single-Family Residential Zoning
District provided in Chapter 17.12 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17
of the Winnetka Village Code, is hereby granted a variation from the maximum
building size provisions of Section 17.30.040 to allow a gross floor area of
2,571.34 square feet, whereas a maximum of 2,400 square feet is permitted,
resulting in a variation of 171.34 square feet (7.14%), for the purpose of
constructing an two-story addition, garage and open entry porch to the
residence on the Subject Property; in accordance with the plans and elevations
submitted with the application for variation.
   SECTION 3: That the variation hereby granted is conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 16th day of September, 2003, pursuant to the following roll
call vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Abell, Brower, Greenough, Webster and Woodbury
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    Trustee Presser
   Signed:
   s/s Michael F. Duhl
    Village President
Countersigned:
s/s Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: September 2, 2003
Posted: September 3, 2003
Passed and Approved: September 16, 2003
Posted: September 17, 2003
ORDINANCE NO. M-35-2003
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (727 Hill Road)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
The West ½ of Lots 5 and 8 in M.K. Meyer’s resubdivision of Lots 10 and 11 in
Block 2 in Garland’s Addition to Winnetka, in Section 21, Township 42 North,
Range 13, East of the Third Principal Meridian, in Cook County, Illinois,
commonly known as 727 Hill Road, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
5 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.12 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for a
variation from the maximum building size provisions of Section 17.30.040 to
allow a gross floor area of 3,108.17 square feet, whereas a maximum of 2,927.81
square feet is permitted, resulting in a variation of 180.36 square feet
(6.16%), for the purpose of constructing three additions to the residence on
the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, on August 11, 2003, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variation and by the
unanimous vote of the five members then present, has reported to the Council
recommending that the requested variation be granted; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and particular hardships
associated with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance
with respect to the Subject Property in that: (a) the Subject Property is a
corner lot located at the northeast corner of Hill Road and Ridge Avenue; (b)
the Subject Property is improved with a structurally sound single family
residence that was built in 1928; (c) because of its age, the house has small
rooms and nonconforming stairways, and lacks such typical modern features as a
mudroom, a family room and an eat-in kitchen that can also accommodate modern
appliances; (d) the interior layout of the house, which consists of five
different levels, not including the basement, limit the size and location for
any expansion of the living space; (e) the stairs from the first floor to the
second floor are narrow with winders at both ends, and the stairs to the
basement are narrow and steep; (f) the owners propose a mudroom addition with
81.37 feet of gross floor area; (g) the owners also propose an addition of
111.00 square feet at the rear of the second floor, and a 355.56 square foot
addition with a footprint of 177.78 square feet on the east side of the house,
which will allow expansion and reconfiguration of a bedroom and bath, creation
of a family room, expansion and modernization of the kitchen, and the
reconfiguration of the stairs to bring them into full compliance with
applicable codes; and (h) due to the height of the first floor above grade,
approximately 126 square feet of the basement contributes to gross floor area;
and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that:
(a) the stairways are hazardous and cannot be corrected without the two-story
addition on the east side of the house; and (b) the other two proposed
additions are modest in size and are necessary to provide the existing house
with features comparable to other homes in the vicinity; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because the additions are modest in size, are designed to be
architecturally integrated into the existing house, and are located along the
north and east sides of the house, a substantial distance from the street; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not impair an adequate supply of light
and air because the additions will comply with the height and setback
requirements of the Zoning Ordinance; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property as it will comply with all applicable
building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed additions will not contribute to congestion on the
public streets, as the home will continue to be used as a single-family
residence; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed construction will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation is in harmony with the general purpose and
intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that it allows the renovation,
restoration and rehabilitation of a structurally sound existing building while
maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the community and protecting
established trees and landscaping.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1: That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2: That the Subject Property, commonly known as 727 Hill Road,
Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-5 Single-Family Residential Zoning
District provided in Chapter 17.12 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17
of the Winnetka Village Code, is hereby granted a variation from the maximum
building size provisions of Section 17.30.040 to allow a gross floor area of
3,108.17 square feet, whereas a maximum of 2,927.81 square feet is permitted,
resulting in a variation of 180.36 square feet (6.16%), for the purpose of
constructing three additions to the residence on the Subject Property, all in
accordance with the plans and elevations submitted with the application for
variation.
   SECTION 3: That the variation hereby granted is conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
