ORDINANCE NO. M-36-2003
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (515 Meadow Road)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
Lot 12 in Block 2 in Manus Indian Hills Subdivision of those parts of the North
½ of Section 28, Township 42 North, Range 13, East of the Third Principal
Meridian, described as follows: beginning at a point in the West line of the
Northeast ¼ of Section 28, 5.75 chains South thence East 4.98 chains to the
West line of right of way of Chicago and Northwestern Railroad; thence
Northwesterly along said right of way line to the place of beginning otherwise
known as Lot 6 in Assessors Subdivision (unrecorded); also that part of the
North ½ of the North ½ of the Northwest ¼ of said Section 28 lying between
Ridge Road and said right of way of Chicago and Northwestern Railroad, in Cook
County, Illinois;
commonly known as 515 Meadow Road, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
5 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.12 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for the
following variations: (a) a variation from the intensity of use of lot
limitations of Section 17.30.030 to allow a roofed lot coverage of 1,863.64
square feet, which exceeds the maximum of 1,613.94 square feet, resulting in a
variation of 249.7 square feet (15.47%); (b) a variation from the maximum
building size limitations of Section 17.30.040 to allow a gross floor area of
2,810.15 square feet, which exceeds the maximum of 2,582.3 square feet,
resulting in a variation of 227.85 square feet (8.82%); (c) a variation from
the corner yard setback requirements of Section 17.30.050 to allow a corner
yard setback of 7.11 feet, which is less than the minimum requirement of 10.66
feet, resulting in a variation of 3.55 feet (33.3%); (d) a variation from the
limitations on garage door widths of Section 17.30.110, to allow a single
garage door to be 16 feet wide, which exceeds the maximum of 9.0 feet,
resulting in a variation of 7.0 feet (77.78%), all of said variations being to
allow a 5.83-foot by 20.16-foot expansion of an existing attached garage on the
Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, on September 22, 2003, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variations and, by the
favorable vote of four of the seven members then present, has reported to the
Council recommending that the front setback and garage door width variations be
granted and, by the favorable vote of four of the seven members then present,
has reported to the Council recommending that the variations for roofed lot
coverage and gross floor area be granted; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and particular hardships
associated with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance
with respect to the Subject Property in that: (a) the Subject Property is a
corner lot located at the northwest corner of Brier Street and Meadow Road; (b)
the Subject Property has a legally non-conforming lot width of 41.65 feet; (c)
the existing residence was built in 1935, and has a legally non-conforming
corner yard setback of 6.07 feet, rather than the 10.66-foot minimum setback
that applies to the Subject Property; (d) the requested building expansion
would lengthen the building by 5.83 feet, with the expanded area being set back
approximately one foot more than the residence to which it is attached; (e)
although it is possible to construct a garage in a conforming location in the
rear quarter of the lot, doing so would allow more bulk on the Subject Property
because the resulting gross floor area bonus would exceed the gross floor area
variation being requested; and (f) although a garage could be built in a
conforming location in the rear quarter of the lot, it would increase
impermeable surface on the Subject Property, and the modest mudroom and kitchen
expansion that are proposed would still require a roofed lot coverage
variation, and the resulting roofed lot coverage variation would likely be
greater than the variation that is being requested; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that:
(a) the proposed addition will yield a mud room and a more functional kitchen,
both of which are standard features in contemporary housing in the Village; (b)
due to the interior configuration of the residence, the proposed mud room and
enlarged kitchen are located in the only suitable place in the residence; (c)
expanding the kitchen and creating a mud room by extending into the existing
garage, would result in a one-car garage, although two-car garages predominate
in the Village; and (d) constructing a mud room and expanding the kitchen by
extending into the existing garage would reduce on-site parking to a single
car, because the reduced corner setback does not leave sufficient room for
parking on the driveway apron without parking across the sidewalk; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because the proposed extension of the residence is 5.83 feet
wide, with the space being added between the house and the garage, so that the
appearance of the Subject Property from the street and from the adjoining
properties to the north and west will not be noticeably changed; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not impair an adequate supply of
light and air because the proposed addition is on story high and is set away
from neighboring properties; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property as the work will comply with all
applicable building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not diminish the taxable value of
land and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed addition will not contribute to congestion on the
public streets, as the Subject Property for single family residential purposes
will not change; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed addition will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations are in harmony with the general purpose
and intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that it allows the renovation,
restoration and rehabilitation of a structurally sound existing building while
maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the community and protecting
established trees and landscaping.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2: That the following variations are hereby granted to the Subject
Property, commonly known as 515 Meadow Road, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in
the R-5 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.12 of
the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code: (a) a
variation from the intensity of use of lot limitations of Section 17.30.030 to
allow a roofed lot coverage of 1,863.64 square feet, which exceeds the maximum
of 1,613.94 square feet, resulting in a variation of 249.7 square feet
(15.47%); (b) a variation from the maximum building size limitations of Section
17.30.040 to allow a gross floor area of 2,810.15 square feet, which exceeds
the maximum of 2,582.3 square feet, resulting in a variation of 227.85 square
feet, (8.82%); (c) a variation from the corner yard setback requirements of
Section 17.30.050 to allow a corner yard setback of 7.11 feet, which is less
than the minimum requirement of 10.66 feet, resulting in a variation of 3.55
feet (33.3%); (d) a variation from the limitations on garage door widths of
Section 17.30.110, to allow a single garage door to be 16 feet wide, which
exceeds the maximum of 9.0 feet, resulting in a variation of 7.0 feet (77.78%),
all of said variations being to allow a 5.83-foot by 20.16-foot expansion of an
existing attached garage on the Subject Property, all in accordance with the
plans and elevations submitted with the application for the variation.
   SECTION 3: That the variations hereby granted are conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 21st day of October, 2003, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Abell, Brower, Greenough, Presser, Webster and Woodbury
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    None
   Signed:
   s/s Michael F. Duhl
    Village President
Countersigned:
s/s Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: October 7, 2003
Posted: October 8, 2003
Passed and Approved: October 21, 2003
