ORDINANCE NO. M-38-2003
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (845 Prospect Avenue)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
Lot 2 in O’Brien-Huggard Subdivision in the Southeast ¼ of Fractional Section
17, Township 42 North, Range 13, East of the Third Principal Meridian, in Cook
County, Illinois,
commonly known as 845 Prospect Avenue, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the
R-3 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.20 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for a
variation from the side yard setback requirements of Section 17.30.060 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, to permit a proposed one-story addition at the
southeast corner of the residence on the Subject Property to have a side yard
setback of 5.83 feet, whereas a minimum of 12 feet is required, a variation of
6.17 feet (51.42%); and
   WHEREAS, on September 22, 2003, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variation and, by the
unanimous vote of its seven members, has reported to the Council recommending
that the requested variation be granted; and
   WHEREAS, on October 7, 2003, the Village Council remanded the applicant’s
request to the Zoning Board of Appeals for further hearing and for more
detailed findings as to the existence of practical difficulties and particular
hardships related to the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance to the
Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, on November 11, 2003, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted an additional public hearing on the requested variation and,
by the favorable vote of four of the six members then in attendance, has
reported to the Council recommending that the requested variation be granted;
and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties associated with carrying out the
strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect to the Subject Property
in that: (a) the Subject Property was formed by the O’Brien Huggard
Subdivision, which was approved by resolution R-28-2002, adopted by the Village
Council on December 3, 2002; (b) the O’Brien Huggard Subdivision resubdivided
three adjoining parcels into two parcels; (c) prior to the resubdivision, the
property commonly known as 845 Prospect was a nonconforming lot, with a 50-foot
lot width and a lot area of 7,500 square feet that were more typical of the R-
5 Zoning District; (d) the house on the Subject Property was originally sized
and configured to fit on the smaller lot, with a south side yard of 5.83 feet;
(e) due to the narrowness of the original lot, the kitchen in the house is long
and narrow and located at the southwest corner of the Subject Property; (f) the
O’Brien-Huggard Subdivision added 57 feet of lot width to the north of the
original property, resulting in a 107-foot lot width and a 16,050-square foot
lot area, both of which exceed the minimum requirements of the R-3 Zoning
District; (g) on April 1, 2003, the Village Council enacted ordinance MC-5-
2003, which amended side yard setback requirements by requiring larger side
yards on wider lots, regardless of the Zoning District in which the property is
located; (h) the amendments enacted by ordinance MC-5-2003 rendered the south
side yard nonconforming; and (i) the requested variation is being sought to
allow the kitchen and breakfast area to be expanded by enclosing a deck that
adjoins the existing kitchen; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that:
(a) the kitchen expansion is part of a renovation and enlargement of the
original house that will bring the house into scale with the size of homes
typically built on the larger lots in the R-3 Zoning District; (b) the proposed
kitchen expansion will allow the enlargement and reconfiguration of the kitchen
to make it consistent with the size and type of kitchens found in homes of
comparable size on comparable larger lots; and (c) not allowing the kitchen
expansion would have a negative impact on the value of the Subject Property, as
it would render the kitchen substandard in relation to homes of comparable size
on lots of comparable size; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because the one-story kitchen addition will be constructed by
extending the south building wall and will have the same footprint as the
existing deck, which is located at the southwest corner of the house and is not
visible from the street; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not impair an adequate supply of light
and air because the proposed one-story kitchen addition will comply with the
height requirements of the Zoning Ordinance and will be substantially lower
than the remainder of the house; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property as the addition will comply with all
applicable building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed addition will not contribute to congestion on the
public streets, as the home will continue to be used as a single-family
residence; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed construction will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation is in harmony with the general purpose and
intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that it allows the renovation,
restoration and rehabilitation of a structurally sound existing building while
maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the community and protecting
established trees and landscaping.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1: That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2: That the Subject Property, commonly known as 845 Prospect Avenue,
Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-3 Single-Family Residential Zoning
District provided in Chapter 17.12 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17
of the Winnetka Village Code, is hereby granted a variation from the side yard
setback requirements of Section 17.30.060 of the Zoning Ordinance, to permit a
proposed one-story addition at the southeast corner of the residence on the
Subject Property to have a side yard setback of 5.83 feet, whereas a minimum of
12 feet is required, a variation of 6.17 feet (51.42%), all in accordance with
the plans and elevations submitted with the application for variation.
   SECTION 3: That the variation hereby granted is conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 2nd day of December, 2003, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Abell, Brower, Presser and Webster
   NAYS:    Trustees Greenough and Woodbury
   ABSENT:    None
   Signed:
   s/s Michael F. Duhl
    Village President
Countersigned:
s/s Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: October 7, 2003
Posted: October 8, 2003
Passed and Approved: December 2, 2003
Posted: December 3, 2003
ORDINANCE NO. M-39-2003
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (1004 Elm)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
Lot 1 in Block 5 in “Groveland Addition to Winnetka”, being a subdivision of
the East 70.0 acres in the Northwest Quarter of Section 20, Township 42 North,
Range 13, East of the Third Principal Meridian, in Cook County, Illinois,
commonly known as 1004 Elm Street, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
5 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.12 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for a
variation from the front yard setback requirements of Section 17.30.050 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, to permit the reconstruction of a front porch with
the same dimensions as the original porch, maintaining the nonconforming front
setback of 24.07 feet, whereas a minimum of 32.94 feet is required, a variation
of 8.97 feet (26.93%); and
   WHEREAS, on September 22, 2003, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variation and, by the
unanimous vote of its seven members, has reported to the Council recommending
that the requested variation be granted; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and particular hardships
associated with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance
with respect to the Subject Property in that: (a) the existing home is at least
70 years old and has a deteriorating front porch; (b) the existing front porch
is in a legally nonconforming location; (c) the new porch is necessary to
assure the safety and viability of the residence; and (d) it is necessary for
the new porch to be of the same dimensions and in the same location as the
porch it will replace, in order to maintain the appearance of the residence and
to preserve existing ingress and egress; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that:
(a) allowing the porch to continue to deteriorate jeopardizes the safety and
viability of the residence; and (b) requiring the new porch to observe the
minimum required setback would result in an unusable porch with a depth of less
than two feet, which would have a significant negative impact on the
appearance, utility and value of the residence; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood, because the new porch will be the same size and in the same
location as the porch it will replace; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not impair an adequate supply of light
and air, because the additions will comply with the height requirements of the
Zoning Ordinance and will not encroach into the front yard setback any further
than the existing porch; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property, as the construction will comply with all
applicable building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed porch replacement will not contribute to congestion on
the public streets, as the home will continue to be used as a single-family
residence; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed construction will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation is in harmony with the general purpose and
intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that it allows the renovation,
restoration and rehabilitation of a structurally sound existing building while
maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the community and protecting
established trees and landscaping.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1: That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2: That the Subject Property, commonly known as 1004 Elm Street,
Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-5 Single-Family Residential Zoning
District provided in Chapter 17.12 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17
of the Winnetka Village Code, is hereby granted a variation from front setback
requirements of Section 17.30.050 of the Zoning Ordinance, to permit the
reconstruction of a front porch with the same dimensions as the original porch,
maintaining the nonconforming front setback of 24.07 feet, whereas a minimum of
32.94 feet is required, a variation of 8.97 feet (26.93%), said porch to be
constructed in accordance with the plans and elevations submitted with the
application for variation.
   SECTION 3: That the variation hereby granted is conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 7th day of October, 2003, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Brower, Greenough, Presser, Webster and Woodbury
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    President Duhl, Trustee Abell
   Signed:
   s/s Michael F. Duhl
    Village President
Countersigned:
s/s Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
