ORDINANCE NO. M-40-2003
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (854 El Dorado)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
The Southwest ½ of Lot 1 and the Southwest ½ of Lot 2 (Except the South 3.2
feet of Lot 2) in Block 8 in Winnetka Park Bluffs Subdivision by William H.
Cairnduff of Part of Sections 16, 17 and 20, Township 42 North, Range 13, East
of the Third Principal Meridian, in Cook County, Illinois,
commonly known as 854 Eldorado Street, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the
R-4 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.16 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for the
following variations: (a) a variation from the intensity of use of lot
limitations of Section 17.30.030 of the Zoning Ordinance, to allow a roofed lot
coverage of 1,893.86 square feet, which exceeds the maximum roofed lot coverage
of 1,815.94 square feet, a variation of 77.92 square feet (4.29%); (b) a
variation from the maximum building size limitations of Section 17.30.040 of
the Zoning Ordinance, to allow a gross floor area of 3,261.23 square feet,
which exceeds the maximum permitted building size of 2,905.5 square feet, a
variation of 355.73 square feet (12.24%); (c) a variation from the front yard
setback requirements of Section 17.30.050 of the Zoning Ordinance, to allow the
attached garage to observe a front setback of 29.82 feet, whereas a minimum of
30 feet is required, a variation of 0.18 feet (0.6%); (d) a variation from the
minimum side yard setback requirements of Section 17.30.060 of the Zoning
Ordinance, to allow the expanded garage to observe a side yard setback of 5
feet, rather than the required 7.5 feet, a variation of 2.5 feet (33.33%); (e)
a variation from the 18.75-foot combined side yard requirement of Section
17.030.060 of the Zoning Ordinance, to allow the sum of the two side yards to
be 15.41 feet, a variation of 3.34 feet (17.81%); and (f) a variation from the
garage door width limitations of Section 17.30.110 of the Zoning Ordinance, to
allow for a single garage door to be 16 feet wide, which exceeds the maximum of
9 feet by 7 feet (77.78%); all for the purpose of expanding the attached garage
and adding a second floor above it, and constructing a 60-square foot open
front porch for the single family residence on the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, on September 22, 2003, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variations and, by the
favorable vote of 6 of the 7 members then in attendance, has reported to the
Council recommending in favor of the variations for the front and side yard
setbacks and garage doors, and has further reported that it does not recommend
that the roofed lot coverage and maximum building size variations be granted,
four of the Board’s seven members having voted against a favorable
recommendation; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and particular hardships
associated with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance
with respect to the Subject Property in that: (a) the Subject Property is a
legally non-conforming lot, having a lot area of 7,263.75 square feet, a
conforming lot width of 75 feet and a non-conforming lot depth of 96.8 feet;
(b) the Subject Property is an interior lot that was created from the rear 75
feet of two lots that were originally platted with their front lot lines on
Prospect Avenue; (c) the Subject Property and the other parcel created by the
lateral, metes and bounds subdivision have their front lot lines along Eldorado
Street; (d) because of the orientation of the Subject Property, its rear yard
adjoins the side yard of the property to the south and its side yard adjoins
the rear yard of the property to the west; (e) the single family residence on
the Subject Property, which was constructed in 1947, has an attached garage on
the west side of the building, in close proximity to a garage located in the
rear yard of the property to the west; and (f) the garage in the rear yard of
the property to the west is located closer to the Eldorado street frontage than
the garage on the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that:
(a) the existing garage is not large enough to hold two cars, although two-car
garages are the predominant garage size in the vicinity; (b) the requested
increased encroachment into the west side yard is necessary to allow the garage
to be expanded; (c) it is not possible to construct a two-car garage in a
conforming location due to the presence of a 36-inch tree at the rear lot line
as well as the increased setback requirement imposed along the rear lot line
because it adjoins the side yard of the property to the south; (d) the proposed
second floor addition is for the construction of a master bedroom and bath,
which are typical amenities of single family residences in the vicinity; (e)
the proposed 16-foot wide garage door is necessary because the proposed 18-foot
wide garage will not be wide enough to accommodate two doors, and increasing
the width of the garage would further reduce the requested 5-foot west side
yard; and (f) the proposed front porch, designed as a portico that is ten feet
wide and six feet deep, will improve both the appearance and utility of the
front entrance; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because the proposed additions are consistent with the
architectural style of the existing building, improve the appearance from the
street, set the garage doors more than ten feet back from the front face of the
house, and maintain a west side yard that is comparable to the setback observed
by the garage on the property immediately to the west; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not impair an adequate supply of
light and air because the proposed improvements comply with the Zoning
Ordinance’s height limitations, maintain a side yard setback that is comparable
to the setback observed by the garage on the adjoining property to the west,
and will not cast shadows into any houses on the adjoining properties; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property, as the proposed construction will comply
with all applicable building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not diminish the taxable value of
land and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed construction will not contribute to congestion on the
public streets, as the property will continue to be used for single family
residential purposes and the expanded garage will provide one additional off-
street parking space; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed garage expansion, second
floor addition and front porch will otherwise impair the public health, safety,
comfort, morals, and welfare of the inhabitants of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations are in harmony with the general purpose
and intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that they allow the renovation,
restoration and rehabilitation of a structurally sound existing building while
maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the community and protecting
established trees and landscaping.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2:  That the following variations are hereby granted to the Subject
Property, commonly known as 854 Eldorado Street, located in the R-4 Single-
Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.16 of the Winnetka
Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code: (a) a variation from
the intensity of use of lot limitations of Section 17.30.030 of the Zoning
Ordinance, to allow a roofed lot coverage of 1,893.86 square feet, which
exceeds the maximum roofed lot coverage of 1,815.94 square feet, a variation of
77.92 square feet (4.29%); (b) a variation from the maximum building size
limitations of Section 17.30.040 of the Zoning Ordinance, to allow a gross
floor area of 3,261.23 square feet, which exceeds the maximum permitted
building size of 2,905.5 square feet, a variation of 355.73 square feet
(12.24%); (c) a variation from the front yard setback requirements of Section
17.30.050 of the Zoning Ordinance, to allow the attached garage to observe a
front setback of 29.82 feet, whereas a minimum of 30 feet is required, a
variation of 0.18 feet (0.6%); (d) a variation from the minimum side yard
setback requirements of Section 17.30.060 of the Zoning Ordinance, to allow the
expanded garage to observe a side yard setback of 5 feet, rather than the
required 7.5 feet, a variation of 2.5 feet (33.33%); (e) a variation from the
18.75-foot combined side yard requirement of Section 17.030.060 of the Zoning
Ordinance, to allow the sum of the two side yards to be 15.41 feet, a variation
of 3.34 feet (17.81%); and (f) a variation from the garage door width
limitations of Section 17.30.110 of the Zoning Ordinance, to allow for a single
garage door to be 16 feet wide, which exceeds the maximum of 9 feet by 7 feet
(77.78%); all for the purpose of expanding the attached garage and adding a
second floor above it, and constructing a 60-square foot open front porch for
the single family residence on the Subject Property; and all in accordance with
the plans and elevations submitted with the application for variations.
   SECTION 3: That the variations hereby granted are conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 18th day of November, 2003, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Abell, Brower, Greenough, Presser, and Woodbury
   NAYS:    Trustee Webster
   ABSENT:    None
   Signed:
   s/s Michael F. Duhl
    Village President
Countersigned:
s/s Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: November 4, 2003
Posted: November 5, 2003
Passed and Approved: November 18, 2003
Posted: November 19, 2003
ORDINANCE NO. M-41-2003
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (875 Gordon Terrace)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
Lot 3 in Block 3 in County Clerk’s Division in the Southwest ¼ of Section 17,
Township 42 North, Range 13, East of the Third Principal Meridian, in Cook
County, Illinois,
commonly known as 875 Gordon Terrace, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
4 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.16 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for the
following variations: (a) a variation from the corner yard setback requirements
of Section 17.30.050 of the Zoning Ordinance, to allow the garage addition to
observe a front setback of 25.83 feet, which exceeds the minimum required front
yard setback of 29.09 feet, a variation of 3.26 feet (11.21%); (b) a variation
from the side yard setback requirements of Section 17.30.060 of the Zoning
Ordinance, to allow an east side yard of 4.52 feet rather than the required
9.7-foot side yard, resulting in a variation of 5.18 feet (53.4%); (c) a
variation from the rear yard setback requirements of Section 17.30.070 of the
Zoning Ordinance, to allow the proposed attached garage to observe a rear yard
setback of 6.0 feet, rather than the required minimum rear yard setback of 22.5
feet, resulting in a variation of 16.5 feet (73.33%); (d) a variation from the
garage door width limitations of Section 17.30.110 of the Zoning Ordinance to
allow a single garage door to be 16 feet wide, which exceeds the 9-foot maximum
allowable width by 7 feet (77.78%); and (e) a variation from the garage width
limitations of Section 17.30.110 of the Zoning Ordinance, to allow for the
attached garage, which forms a part of the front building line, to be 25.25
feet wide, which exceeds the permitted maximum of 22 feet by 3.25 feet (14.8%);
all to allow the construction of a two and one-half story addition and attached
garage for the existing single family residence; and
   WHEREAS, on September 22, 2003, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variations and, by the
unanimous vote of the 7 members then present, has reported to the Council
recommending that all of the variations requested be denied;
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and particular hardships
associated with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance
with respect to the Subject Property in that: (a) the Subject Property is a
corner lot located at the southeast corner of Tower Road and Gordon Terrace;
(b) the Subject Property is improved for the single family residence that was
constructed in 1878 and is located in the southeast corner of the Subject
Property; (c) the Subject Property is characterized by a grove of mature Oak
trees; (d) the existing garage is located at the south end of the subject
property and is accessed by a driveway from Gordon Terrace; (e) the existing
frame garage is not large enough to accommodate two vehicles; (f) the existing
garage has a conforming rear yard setback that will be rendered non-conforming
when it is expanded and connected to the new addition proposed for the front of
the house, which faces Gordon Terrace; (g) the proposed attached garage will be
less than a half-foot closer to the south lot line than the existing detached
garage; (h) the variation from the corner yard setback results from expanding
the depth of the garage towards the west lot line and from the attachment of
the garage to the proposed addition at the front of the house; (i) the depth of
the garage cannot be extended to the east because of the presence of a 31-inch
diameter tree; and (j) the variation in the east side yard is being requested
to allow the construction of a 3-foot by 12-foot deck along the east side of
the house, which would reduce the legally non-conforming east side yard from
5.65 feet to 4.52 feet; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that:
(a) the existing frame garage was built in 1951 and is in need of replacement;
(b) the existing garage is smaller than contemporary two-car garages that are
typical accessories for single family residential property in the vicinity and
(c) it is not possible to construct a new garage in a conforming location
without removing the 31-inch diameter tree, which would have a negative impact
on the Subject Property, as well as the neighborhood as a whole; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because the front of the proposed garage will be closer to the
street than the front of the house, maintaining the existing relationship
between the house and garage, and the proposed garage and second floor addition
preserve all of the substantial trees on the Subject Property, which are a
distinguishing feature of the Subject Property and the immediate neighborhood;
and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not impair an adequate supply of
light and air because the non-conforming garage is further from the rear lot
line than would be required for a detached garage, the corner yard setback
variation does not affect any other properties and the proposed deck for the
east side of the house will not have a roof and has a footprint of only 36
square feet; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property, as the proposed construction will comply
with all applicable building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not diminish the taxable value of
land and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed construction will not contribute to congestion on the
public streets, as the property will continue to be used for single family
residential purposes and the expanded garage will provide off-street parking
for two cars; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed addition will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations are in harmony with the general purpose
and intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that they allow the renovation,
restoration and rehabilitation of a structurally sound existing building while
maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the community and protecting
established trees and landscaping.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2:  That the following variations are hereby granted to the Subject
Property, commonly known as 875 Gordon Terrace, and located in the R-4 Single-
Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.16 of the Winnetka
Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code: (a) a variation from
the corner yard setback requirements of Section 17.30.050 of the Zoning
Ordinance, to allow the garage addition to observe a front setback of 25.83
feet, which exceeds the minimum required front yard setback of 29.09 feet, a
variation of 3.26 feet (11.21%); (b) a variation from the side yard setback
requirements of Section 17.30.060 of the Zoning Ordinance, to allow an east
side yard of 4.52 feet rather than the required 9.7-foot side yard, resulting
in a variation of 5.18 feet (53.4%); (c) a variation from the rear yard setback
requirements of Section 17.30.070 of the Zoning Ordinance, to allow the
proposed attached garage to observe a rear yard setback of 6 feet, rather than
the required minimum rear yard setback of 22.5 feet, resulting in a variation
of 16.5 feet (73.33%); (d) a variation from the garage door width limitations
of Section 17.30.110 of the Zoning Ordinance to allow a single garage door to
be 16 feet wide, which exceeds the 9-foot maximum allowable width by 7 feet
(77.78%); and (e) a variation from the garage width limitations of Section
17.30.110 of the Zoning Ordinance, to allow for the attached garage, which
forms a part of the front building line, to be 25.25 feet wide, which exceeds
the permitted maximum of 22 feet by 3.25 feet (14.8%); all to allow the
construction of a two and one-half story addition and attached garage for the
existing single family residence, and all in accordance with the plans and
elevations submitted with the application for variations.
   SECTION 3: That the variations hereby granted are conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 18th day of November, 2003, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Abell, Brower, Greenough, Presser, Webster and Woodbury
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    None
   Signed:
   s/s Michael F. Duhl
    Village President
Countersigned:
s/s Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: November 4, 2003
Posted: November 5, 2003
Passed and Approved: November 18, 2003
Posted: November 19, 2003
ORDINANCE NO. M-42-2003(Tabled and renumbered M-6-2004)
ORDINANCE NO. M-43-2003
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (1422 Edgewood)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
Lot 5 in Block 20 in Chicago North Shore Land Company’s Subdivision in Sections
17 and 18, Township 42 North, Range 13 east of the Third Principal Meridian, in
Cook County, Illinois.
commonly known as 1422 Edgewood Lane, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
5 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.16 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for a
variation from Section 17.30.060 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance to permit the
expansion of an existing second floor dormer that would have a side yard
setback of 2.75 feet, whereas a setback of 6 feet is required, a variation of
3.25 feet (54.17%) and to allow for the replacement of windows in a wall that
is in a nonconforming side yard; and
   WHEREAS, on October 13, 2003, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variations and, by the
unanimous vote of its seven members, has reported to the Council recommending
that the requested variations be granted; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and particular hardships
associated with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance
with respect to the Subject Property in that: (a) the Subject Property is a
long, narrow lot with a non-conforming 50-foot lot width; (b) the lot area of
the Subject Property is 10,000 square feet, which exceeds the required minimum
for the R-5 Zoning District; (c) the Subject Property is improved with a single
family residence that was built in 1917, prior to the enactment of the Winnetka
Zoning Ordinance; and (d) the Subject Property has a legal, non-conforming east
side yard of 2.75 feet; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that:
(a) the variations are being requested so an existing dormer can be extended to
allow for the expansion of an existing bedroom by converting a closet into
bedroom space; (b) the dormer extension is necessary as part of a
reconfiguration of the second floor to allow access to a new master bedroom
suite, which is a standard amenity in single family residential homes in the
vicinity; (c) the variation will also allow windows in the existing dormer and
in a wall in the non-conforming side yard to be replaced; and (d) the window
replacements are improvements that are part of an extensive renovation of the
existing residence that could not be completed if the variations are not
granted; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because the proposed dormer extension is on the east side of
the house, will extend toward the rear of the house and will provide a
continuous and consistent detail on the east roof line, while the proposed
changes in the windows will provide consistency of the style and location of
windows throughout the residence; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not impair an adequate supply of light
and air because the proposed dormer extension is consistent with the location
of the existing dormer and conforms with building height limitations, while the
window replacements will not change the plane of the non-conforming wall; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property, as the proposed construction will comply
with all applicable building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed construction will not contribute to congestion on the
public streets, as the property will continue to be used for single family
residential purposes; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the requested variation will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation are in harmony with the general purpose and
intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that they allow the renovation,
restoration and rehabilitation of a structurally sound existing building while
maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the community and protecting
established trees and landscaping.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2: That the Subject Property, commonly known as 1422 Edgewood Lane,
Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-5 Single-Family Residential District
provided in Chapter 17.16 of the Winnetka Village Code, Title 17 of the
Winnetka Village Code, is hereby granted a variation from Section 17.30.060 of
the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance to permit the expansion of an existing second
floor dormer that would have a side yard setback of 2.75 feet, whereas a
setback of 6 feet is required, a variation of 3.25 feet (54.17%) and to allow
for the replacement of windows in a wall that is in a nonconforming side yard,
all in accordance with the plans and elevations submitted with the application
for variations.
   SECTION 3: That the variations hereby granted are conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 18th day of November, 2003, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Abell, Brower, Greenough, Presser, Webster and Woodbury
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    None
   Signed:
   s/s Michael F. Duhl
    Village President
Countersigned:
s/s Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: November 4, 2003
Posted: November 5, 2003
Passed and Approved: November 18, 2003
Posted: November 19, 2003
ORDINANCE NO. M-44-2003
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (220 Birch)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
That part of Lot 1 in Block 6 in Alles’ First Addition to Winnetka, in the
southwest quarter of the southeast quarter of Section 20, Township 42 North,
Range 13 east of the Third Principal Meridian, described as follows: the North
146.60 feet (except the North 33 feet for road) of the West 192.0 feet of the
East 225.0 feet of said southwest quarter of the southeast quarter of Section
20, Township 42 North, Range 13 east of the Third Principal Meridian, in Cook
County, Illinois
commonly known as 220 Birch Street, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
2 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.24 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for a
variation of 35.32 feet (53 %) from the 50-foot front setback requirement of
Section 17.30.010(H) of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance to permit an existing
covered porch that observes a front setback of 14.68 feet from the Birch Street
property line to be enclosed and converted into a mudroom; and
   WHEREAS, on October 13, 2003, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variation and, by the
unanimous vote of its seven members, has reported to the Council recommending
that the requested variation requested be granted; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and particular hardships
associated with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance
with respect to the Subject Property in that: (a) the Subject Property is a
corner lot located at the southwest corner of Birch Street and Sunset Road; (b)
the single family residence on the Subject Property was originally constructed
as a coach house around 1919, before the initial enactment of the Village’s
Zoning Ordinance; (c) the coach house was converted into a single family
residence in 1949; (d) the single family residence on the Subject Property is
an architecturally significant French country cottage that is an established
presence in the streetscape; (e) the residence has a legally nonconforming
front setback, with the entire main portion of the residence being located
within the required 50-foot setback and the northernmost portion of the
residence is located 8.10 feet from the Birch Street property line, which
creates an “L” shape with the main body of the house; and (f) the porch that
the applicants seek to enclose observes a front setback of 14.68 feet because
it is located along the main body of the house against the interior corner of
the “L”; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that:
(a) the existing front yard nonconformity cannot be cured without demolishing
and replacing the residence in its entirety; (b) a mudroom is a common feature
in residences in the R-2 Single Family Residential Zoning District; (c) the
mudroom that will be created by enclosing the porch will be located in the
typical location for mudrooms, adjacent to the existing kitchen, utility room
and building entrances; and (d) because of the layout of the residence and the
location of the main body of the house in a nonconforming location, there is no
conforming alternative for providing a mudroom in the residence; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because neither the building footprint nor the height of the
porch roof will be changed; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not impair an adequate supply of light
and air because the proposed mudroom will maintain the height and dimensions of
the porch that the applicants seek to enclose; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property, as the proposed construction will comply
with all applicable building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed construction will not contribute to congestion on the
public streets, as the porch enclosure will not alter the use of the Subject
Property, which will continue to be used for single family residential
purposes; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed addition will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations are in harmony with the general purpose
and intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that they allow the renovation,
restoration and rehabilitation of a structurally sound existing building while
maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the community and protecting
established trees and landscaping.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2:  That the Subject Property, commonly known as 220 Birch Street,
Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-2 Single-Family Residential Zoning
District provided in Chapter 17.24 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17
of the Winnetka Village Code, is hereby granted a variation of 35.32 feet (53
%) from the 50-foot front setback requirement of Section 17.30.010(H) of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance to permit an existing covered porch that observes a
front setback of 14.68 feet from the Birch Street property line to be enclosed
and converted into a mudroom, all in accordance with the plans and elevations
submitted with the application for variations.
   SECTION 3: That the variation hereby granted is conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 18th day of November, 2003, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Abell, Brower, Greenough, Presser, Webster and Woodbury
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    None
