Posted: December 3, 2003
Passed and Approved: December 16, 2003
Posted: December 17, 2003
ORDINANCE NO. M-49-2003
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (530 Sunset Road)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
Lot 6 (except the East 25 feet of said lot measured on the north line thereof
and except that part lying West of a line of line 92 feet West and parallel
with the East line of said Lot 6) in block 123 in the subdivision of Blocks 18,
21, 22 and 23 in J.C. Garland’s Addition to Winnetka, being a subdivision of
the North 120 acres of the Southwest Quarter of Section 21, Township 42 North,
Range 13, East of the Third Principal Meridian, in Cook County, Illinois,
commonly known as 530 Sunset Road, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
5 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.12 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for the
following variations: (a) a variation from the corner yard setback requirements
of Section 17.30.050 to allow a two-story addition proposed for the west side
of the house on the Subject Property to observe a corner yard setback of 5.45
feet along the Wilson Avenue street frontage, rather than the required corner
yard setback of 10.88 feet, resulting in a variation of 5.43 feet (49.91%); and
(b) a variation from the side yard setback requirements of Section 17.30.060,
to allow a proposed one-story addition at the southeast corner of the house to
observe a side yard of 5.42 feet, rather than the required 6.0 feet, resulting
in a variation of 0.58 feet (9.67%); and
   WHEREAS, as originally filed, the variation application also sought a corner
yard setback variation for a proposed garage expansion to be constructed at the
rear of the Subject Property and an intensity of use of lot variation to allow
the garage expansion, a bay window addition to the front of the house and a
mudroom addition, as well as the two-story west addition, and the kitchen
addition at the southeast corner of the house; and
   WHEREAS, on November 10, 2003, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals, with six of its seven members present, conducted a public hearing on
the requested variations and, after voting on the requested variations, has
reported to the Village Council as follows: (a) the six members have
unanimously recommended that the requested front yard setback variation for the
garage addition be granted, but that the intensity of use of lot variation be
denied; (b) five of the six members have voted to recommend that the variation
for the addition to the west side of the house be denied; and (c) due to a tie
vote, the Zoning Board of Appeals has failed to make a favorable recommendation
as to the addition to the east side of the house; and
   WHEREAS, subsequent to the Zoning Board of Appeals’ hearing and vote, the
applicants modified their plans by eliminating the garage expansion, the bay
window addition, and the mudroom, and thereby eliminated the necessity for the
roofed lot coverage and corner setback variations; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and unique hardships associated
with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect
to the Subject Property in that: (a) the Subject Property is an irregularly
shaped, undersized lot located at the southeast corner of Wilson and Sunset
Roads; (b) the Subject Property is nearly triangular in shape, with a 67.0-foot
front lot line along Sunset, a 144.69-foot east lot line that is perpendicular
to Sunset, and a west lot line that is parallel to the east lot line for a
distance of 37.54 feet before angling to the southeast, parallel to Wilson, for
a distance of 127.6 feet until it meets the east lot line; (c) because the
entire west lot line fronts on Wilson Road for a total of 165.14 feet, it is
treated as a corner lot line subject to a corner yard setback, which is greater
than a standard side yard setback; (d) because of the angle of the west lot
line, the Subject Property does not have a true rear lot line, and the area
that functions as a rear yard has a triangular shape; (e) because of the nearly
triangular shape of the Subject Property, the Subject Property has triangular
buildable area; (f) because of the nearly triangular shape of the Subject
Property, the triangular buildable area and the rectangular shape of the home,
the southwest corner of the home is substantially closer to the west lot line
than the northwest corner of the home, and a portion of the west side of the
home is in a nonconforming location; (g) the combined effect of the irregular
shape of the Subject Property, the irregular shape of the buildable area, the
location of the house and the existence of a significant tree directly behind
the house severely limits the areas available for expanding the home, which was
built in 1925; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that:
(a) the existing house is relatively small, with a gross floor area of 1,781.59
square feet; (b) the nonconformities in the home cannot be cured without either
removing substantial portions of home or demolishing it and constructing a
triangular house to fit the irregular shape of the Subject Property; (c) the
proposed one-story addition to the east will square off the east building line
and allow for the reconfiguration and expansion of both the dining room and the
kitchen, providing better work space and a kitchen island with seating area in
the kitchen; (d) the proposed two-story addition to the west will allow for the
reconfiguration of the second floor to make a usable bedroom on the west side
and to provide a modest master bedroom suite; and (e) the proposed additions
will add features that are standard amenities in contemporary homes in the
Village; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because the two additions are modest in size, are consistent
with the architectural style of the existing house and maintain the home’s
existing orientation toward the Sunset Road frontage, thus preserving the
appearance of the Subject Property on the block face; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not impair an adequate supply of
light and air because of the one-story height of the east addition and because
the west addition will be opposite the railroad embankment located on the west
side of Wilson; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property as they will comply with all applicable
building and fire protection codes, the east addition will not encroach any
further into the side yard than the existing north east building wall, and the
west addition is across the street from the railroad embankment; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not diminish the taxable value of
land and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed additions will not contribute to congestion on the
public streets, as the use of the Subject Property will not change; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed additions will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village.
   WHEREAS, the requested variations are in harmony with the general purpose
and intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that they will allow the
renovation, restoration and rehabilitation of a structurally sound existing
building while maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the community
and protecting established trees and landscaping.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2: That the following variations are hereby granted to the Subject
Property, commonly known as 530 Sunset Road, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in
the R-5 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.12 of
the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code: (a) a
variation from the corner yard setback requirements of Section 17.30.050 to
allow a two-story addition to the west side of the house on the Subject
Property to observe a corner yard setback of 5.45 feet along the Wilson Avenue
street frontage, rather than the required corner yard setback of 10.88 feet,
resulting in a variation of 5.43 feet (49.91%); and (b) a variation from the
side yard setback requirements of Section 17.30.060, to allow a one-story
addition to the east side of the house to observe a side yard of 5.42 feet,
rather than the required 6.0 feet, resulting in a variation of 0.58 feet
(9.67%); all in accordance with the plans and elevations submitted with the
application, as modified by the revised site plan and drawings dated November
19, 2003.
   SECTION 3: That the variations hereby granted are conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 6th day of January, 2004, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Abell, Brower, Greenough, Presser, and Woodbury
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    Trustee Webster
   Signed:
   s/s Michael F. Duhl
    Village President
Countersigned:
s/s Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
