ORDINANCE NO. M-3-2004
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (66 Abbotsford Road)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
Lot 12 in Block 2 in Tempel’s Resubdivision of that part of the West Half of
the Northeast Quarter of Section 28, Township 42 North, Range 13, East of the
Third Principal Meridian, lying East of the Chicago & Milwaukee Electric
Railroad, and North of the Village limits of Kenilworth, in Cook County,
Illinois,
commonly known as 66 Abbotsford Road, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
5 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.16 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for the
following variations from the regulations of Chapter 17.30 of the Zoning
Ordinance: (a) a variation from the intensity of use of lot provisions in
Section 17.30.030 to allow a roofed lot coverage of 2,030.01 square feet
whereas a maximum of 1,878 square feet is permitted, a variation of 152.01
square feet (8.09%); and (b) a variation from the intensity of use of lot
provisions of Section 17.30.030, to allow an impermeable surface area of
3,908.75 square feet, whereas a maximum of 3,756 square feet is permitted, a
variation of 152.75 square feet (4.07%); all to permit the construction of a
two story addition, a one-story enclosed porch and a deck at the rear of the
existing house; and
   WHEREAS, on December 8, 2003, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variations and, by the
unanimous vote of the seven members then present, has reported to the Council
recommending that the variations requested be granted; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties associated with carrying out the
strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect to the Subject Property
in that: (a) the house on the subject property was built in 1921, prior to the
initial enactment of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance; (b) both the breakfast area
adjacent to the kitchen and the master bath directly above it are located at
the rear of the house and measure 8.0 feet by 12.20 feet; (c) the owners
propose to construct a two story addition to expand the dimensions of the
breakfast area and master bath to measure 13.0 feet by 14.67 feet, to further
extend the rear of the building another 12.2 feet to provide for the
construction of a one-story “three-season” room with dimensions of 12.2 feet by
14.67 feet, and to add a 4-foot deep raised deck across the rear of the
expanded house; (d) the two-story addition could be constructed without a
variation; (e) due to the internal configuration of the house, there is no
other place for the proposed additions, which will be in the typical location
for such rooms, in close proximity to the kitchen at the rear of the house; (f)
the one-story three-season room addition, which will function as a family room,
is modest in size; and (g) the expanded house will remain well within maximum
building size limitations ; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance because
(a) the need for the variation is triggered by the one-story addition for the
three-season room, (b) due to the existing configuration of the house, the part
of the house that functions as a dining room is at the opposite end of the
house from the kitchen, in a one-story sunroom that is separated from the
kitchen by the central stairway, entrance foyer and living room, (c) the
enclosed three-season room will function as a family room, thereby allowing the
room adjacent to the kitchen to be restored to its original dining room use,
and (d) the absence of a dining room in its typical location in immediate
proximity to the kitchen, coupled with the absence of a breakfast area and
family room in the general vicinity of the kitchen, all of which are standard
features of contemporary housing in the Village has negative impact on the
value of the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because the expanded house will remain well within the maximum
building size limitations and the proposed additions, which are at the rear of
the house, have been designed to be harmonious with the character of the
existing house and to minimize the apparent volume of the new space from the
street and adjoining properties; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not impair an adequate supply of
light and air because the proposed improvements will conform with all
applicable setback and height requirements and the expanded house will remain
below applicable gross floor area limitations; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property, as the proposed construction will comply
with all applicable building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not diminish the taxable value of
land and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed construction will not contribute to congestion on the
public streets, as the property will continue to be used for single family
residential purposes; and
   WHEREAS, a sanitary sewer line that serves several adjoining parcels is
located in the rear yard of the Subject Property, in the area of the proposed
building additions; and
   WHEREAS, the Council find that it is in the interests of the public health,
safety, comfort and general welfare for the owners of the Subject Property to
assume liability for any personal injuries or property damage resulting from
damage to or interference with the sewer line arising from the construction of
the proposed buildings additions; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed addition will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations are in harmony with the general purpose
and intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that they allow the renovation,
restoration and rehabilitation of a structurally sound existing building while
maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the community and protecting
established trees and landscaping.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2:  That the following variations are hereby granted to the Subject
Property, commonly known as 66 Abbotsford Road, and located in the R-5 Single-
Family Residential District provided in Chapter 17.16 of the Winnetka Village
Code, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code: (a) a variation from the intensity
of use of lot provisions in Section 17.30.030 to allow a roofed lot coverage of
2,030.01 square feet whereas a maximum of 1,878 square feet is permitted, a
variation of 152.01 square feet (8.09%); and (b) a variation from the intensity
of use of lot provisions of Section 17.30.030, to allow an impermeable surface
area of 3,908.75 square feet, whereas a maximum of 3,756 square feet is
permitted, a variation of 152.75 square feet (4.07%); all to permit the
construction of a two story addition, a one-story enclosed porch and a deck at
the rear of the existing house, in accordance with the plans and elevations
submitted with the application for variations.
   SECTION 3: That the variations hereby granted are subject to the following
conditions:
      A.   The proposed construction shall commence within 12 months after the
effective date of this Ordinance.
      B.   The owners of the Subject Property shall be solely responsible for,
and shall hold the Village harmless against, any and all personal injuries or
property damage resulting from damage to or interference with the municipal
sanitary sewer line on the Subject Property arising from the construction of
the proposed building additions.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 3rd day of February, 2004, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Abell, Brower, Greenough, Presser, Webster and Woodbury
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    None
   APPROVED this 3rd day of February, 2004.
   Signed:
   //s//Michael F. Duhl
   Village President
Countersigned:
//s//Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: January 20, 2004
Posted: January 21, 2004
Passed and Approved: February 3, 2004
Posted: February 4, 2004
ORDINANCE NO. M-4-2004
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (139 DeWindt Road)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
That part of Lots “F” and “A” in Daughaday’s Resubdivision of Lots 8 to 14,
both inclusive, in Daughaday Acres, being a subdivision in the Southwest ¼ of
Section 20, Township 42 North, Range 13 East of the Third Principal Meridian,
according to the plat thereof recorded April 5, 1929 as Document No. 10330520,
in Cook County, Illinois, bounded and described as follows: Commencing on the
Westerly line of said Lot “F” at a point 164 feet South of, measured at right
angles to, the North line of Lot “E” in said Daughaday’s Resubdivision; running
thence East on a line drawn parallel to and 164 feet South of, measured at
right angles to, the North line and said North line extended of said Lot “E”; a
distance of 213.25 feet to the Easterly line of said Lot “F” (being also the
Westerly line of said Lot “A”); thence North, across said Lot “A”, along a line
at right angles to said North line extended of said Lot “E”, a distance of
163.94 feet to the Northeast corner of said Lot “F”; thence along the
Northerly, Northwesterly, and Westerly line of said Lot “F” to the Point of
Beginning, situated in the Village of Winnetka, County of Cook, in the State of
Illinois,
commonly known as 139 DeWindt Road, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
2 Single Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.24 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, a variation
from the setback requirements of Section 17.30.050 of the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance to permit the installation of three air conditioner units 6.25 feet
from the west edge of a platted, but unimproved private street along the east
side of the Subject Property, whereas a minimum setback of 47 feet is required,
a variation of 40.75 feet (86.7%); and
   WHEREAS, on January 12, 2004, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variation and by the
unanimous vote of the six members then present, has reported to the Council
recommending that the requested variation be granted; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and unique hardships associated
with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect
to the Subject Property in that: (a) the Subject Property has three street
frontages, with both the north and west lot lines being formed by DeWindt Road;
(b) the east part of the lot is occupied by substantial portions of an
unimproved 25-foot wide platted private street, which requires the east setback
to be measured from the edge of the platted private street; (c) the platted,
unimproved street is not used to access any of the four properties that it
abuts, because the Subject Property, the parcel immediately to the south of the
Subject Property and the parcel immediately to the east of the Subject Property
are all accessed directly from DeWindt Road, and the parcel at the southeast
terminus of the unimproved street is a flag lot with access directly from
DeWindt Road, through the "flagpole" portion of the property, along the east
lot line of that property; (d) because of the location of the house on the
Subject Property, the interior configuration of the house, and the larger
setbacks imposed along the three street frontages of the Subject Property, the
Subject Property has a significantly reduced buildable area, making it
impossible to construct an addition to the existing residence within a
conforming location; (e) because the private street in the east portion of the
Subject Property is unimproved, the east lot line of the Subject Property
functions as a side lot line although it is considered a front or corner lot
line under the Zoning Ordinance; and (f) the Village Council has previously
recognized the unique circumstances of the Subject Property in granting setback
variations for a two-story addition pursuant to Ordinance M-4-2003; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that
(a) the north, west and south setbacks meet or exceed the requirements of the
Zoning Ordinance; (b) the three proposed air conditioner units will be set
farther back than the two story addition that is being constructed pursuant to
Ordinance M-4-2003; and (c) the proposed location for the air conditioners is
to the east side of the house, in the part of the Subject Property that
functions as a side yard and appears to be a side yard when viewed from the
street; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because the three air conditioners will be located behind the
new addition on the east side of the house, and thus will not be visible from
the street and will be obscured from view by neighbors due to the presence of
mature trees to the east; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not impair an adequate supply of light
and air because the air conditioners are of minimal height and fall within
existing shadow lines of the house itself; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property, as the installation will comply with all
applicable building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the air conditioners will not contribute to congestion on the
public streets and there is no evidence that they will otherwise impair the
public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the inhabitants of the
Village; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation is in harmony with the general purpose and
intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that it allows for the renovation,
restoration and rehabilitation of a structurally sound existing building while
maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the community and protecting
established trees and landscaping.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2: That the Subject Property, commonly known as 139 DeWindt Road,
Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-2 Single Family Residential Zoning
District provided in Chapter 17.24 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17
of the Winnetka Village Code, is hereby granted a variation from the setback
requirements of Section 17.30.050 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance to permit
the installation of three air conditioners 6.25 feet from the west edge of a
platted, but unimproved street along the east side of the Subject Property,
whereas a minimum setback of 47 feet is required, resulting in a variation of
40.75 feet (86.7%), as depicted in the plans and elevations submitted with the
application for variation.
   SECTION 3: That the variation hereby granted is conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 17th day of February, 2004, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Abell, Brower, Greenough, Presser, Webster and Woodbury
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    None
   APPROVED this 17th day of February, 2004.
   Signed:
   //s//Michael F. Duhl
   Village President
Countersigned:
//s//Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
