ORDINANCE NO. M-5-2004
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (818 Elm)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
The North 111 feet of the West 27 feet of Lot 1 in Wilson and Dale’s
Subdivision of the north half of Block 26 in Winnetka, being Charles E. Peck’s
Subdivision of the Northeast Quarter of Section 20, and the North Half of
Fractional Section 21, all in Township 42 North, Range 13, East of the Third
Principal Meridian, in Cook County, Illinois.
commonly known as 818 Elm Street, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the C-
2 Commercial Overlay Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.44 of the Winnetka
Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for a variation from
the off-street parking requirements of Section 17.44.010.K to permit the
conversion of three second floor apartments into three commercial offices
without providing the required six on-site parking spaces; and
   WHEREAS, on January 12, 2004, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variation and, by the
unanimous vote of the six members then present, has reported to the Council
recommending that the variation requested be granted; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties associated with carrying out the
strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect to the Subject Property
in that: (a) the Subject Property has existed in its current configuration
since 1941; (b) there is insufficient open space on the Subject Property to
provide off-street parking; (c) the three existing residential apartments on
the second floor of the building on the Subject Property were in use prior to
February 3, 1998, and thus are not required to provide 2¼ parking spaces per
dwelling unit pursuant to zoning regulations enacted on that date; (d) as a
result of the conversion of the three dwelling units into office space, the
Subject Property will now be required to provide six off-street parking spaces;
(e) the conversion of the three dwelling units to office space is part of a
building renovation that includes bringing the building up to code, including
fire safety, electrical and plumbing requirements; and (f) due to the small
size of the three dwelling units, it is not cost effective to make the same
renovations for continued use as residential apartments; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that:
(a) due to the small size of the three dwelling units, it is not cost effective
to make the same renovations for continued use as residential apartments; and
(b) one apartment has been vacant since September of 2003; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood and will not impair an adequate supply of light and air,
because the variation is being requested due to a change of use in the second
floor apartments, which will not change the exterior appearance of the building
and will slightly reduce the parking requirements from the general parking
requirement applicable to apartment units; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property because the requested variation pertains
to off-site parking and the renovations related o the change of use will comply
with all applicable building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not contribute to congestion on the
public streets because, as reflected in the Zoning Ordinance’s parking
requirements, the parking requirements for office space are not as great as the
parking demands for residential apartments; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the requested variation will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation is in harmony with the general purpose and
intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that it allows the renovation,
restoration and rehabilitation of a structurally sound existing building while
maintaining the existing commercial streetscape.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1: That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2: That the Subject Property, commonly known as 818 Elm Street, and
located in the C-2 Commercial Overlay Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.44
of the Winnetka Village Code, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, is hereby
granted a variation from the off-street parking requirements of Section
17.44.010.K to permit the conversion of three second floor apartments into
three commercial offices without providing the required six on-site parking
spaces, all in accordance with the plans and elevations submitted with the
application for variation.
   SECTION 3: That the variations hereby granted are conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 17th day of February, 2004, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Abell, Brower, Greenough, Presser, Webster and Woodbury
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    None
   APPROVED this 17th day of February, 2004.
   Signed:
   //s//Michael F. Duhl
   Village President
Countersigned:
//s//Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: February 3, 2004
Posted: February 4, 2004
Passed and Approved: February 17, 2004
Posted: February 18, 2004
ORDINANCE NO. M-6-2004
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (228 Poplar)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
   The south 50 feet of Lot 1 (measured on the east line) in Block 22 in J.C.
Garland’s Addition to Winnetka, a subdivision of the north 120 acres of the
Southwest ¼ of Section 21, Township 42 North, Range 13 east of the Third
Principal Meridian, according to the plat thereof recorded June 9, 1909 as
Document 4388795, in Cook County, Illinois.
commonly known as 128 Poplar Street, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
5 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.16 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for the
following variations: (a) a variation from the front yard setback requirements
of Section 17.30.050 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance to allow a front setback
of 3 feet along Wilson Street, whereas a setback of 30 feet is required, a
variation of 27 feet (90%) and (b) a variation from the side yard setback
requirements of Section 17.30.060 of the Zoning Ordinance, to allow a side yard
setback of 0.5 feet, whereas a setback of 6 feet is required, a variation of
5.5 feet (83%); said variations being requested for the purpose of replacing a
dilapidated one-car detached garage with a new two-car detached garage; and
   WHEREAS, on October 17, 2003, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variations and, by the
unanimous vote of seven members, has reported to the Council recommending that
the variations requested be granted; and
   WHEREAS, following proceedings before the Village Council in November and
December of 2003, staff recommended that the proposed new garage be moved to
the north and east, a change in plan that required a new hearing before the
Zoning Board of Appeals; and
   WHEREAS, on January 12, 2004, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the revised variation request and, by the
unanimous vote of the six members then present, has reported to the Council
recommending that the variations requested for the revised plan be granted; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and particular hardships
associated with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance
with respect to the Subject Property in that: (a) the Subject Property is a
through-lot located on the west side of Poplar Street, with its rear yard
adjoining the east side of Wilson Street; (b) the Subject Property has an
irregular shape because Poplar Street and Wilson Street are not parallel; (c)
the Subject Property is a non-conforming lot that is improved with a residence
that was built in 1919, prior to the enactment of the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance; and (d) because of the irregular lot shape, the dimensions of the
lot and the 30-foot setback required along Wilson Street, there is no
conforming location for a garage on the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that:
(a) the proposed new garage would replace a dilapidated garage that was built
in 1921 and that cannot safely accommodate a vehicle; (b) two-car garages are a
typical feature of single family residential property in the vicinity; and (c)
the proposed garage is consistent with other two-car garages on similar through
lots abutting Wilson street; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because the proposed new garage will be in the same location
on the Subject Property as the garage that it will replace, improving the
appearance of the Subject Property while maintaining the development of the
Subject Property as it has existed on the block face for over 80 years; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not impair an adequate supply of
light and air because the proposed garage will conform with the applicable
building height limitations, and will maintain a sufficient distance from other
structures on the adjoining properties; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property, as the proposed garage will comply with
all applicable building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not diminish the taxable value of
land and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed construction will not contribute to congestion on the
public streets, as the property will continue to be used for single family
residential purposes and the new garage will provide enclosed, off-street
parking for two vehicles; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed garage will otherwise impair
the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the inhabitants of
the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations are in harmony with the general purpose
and intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that they allow the replacement
of a structurally inadequate garage with a new garage that maintains the
existing scale and appearance of the community and protecting established trees
and landscaping.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2:  That the following variations are hereby granted to the Subject
Property, commonly known as 228 Poplar Street, Winnetka, Illinois, and located
in the R-5 Single-Family Residential District provided in Chapter 17.12 of the
Winnetka Village Code, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code: (a) a variation
from the front yard setback requirements of Section 17.30.050 of the Winnetka
Zoning Ordinance to allow a front setback of 3 feet along Wilson Street,
whereas a setback of 30 feet is required, a variation of 27 feet (90%) and (b)
a variation from the side yard setback requirements of Section 17.30.060 of the
Zoning Ordinance, to allow a side yard setback of 0.5 feet, whereas a setback
of 6 feet is required, a variation of 5.5 feet (83%); said variations being
requested for the purpose of replacing a dilapidated one-car detached garage
with a new two-car detached garage as amended for consideration at the Zoning
Board of Appeals on January 12, 2004.
   SECTION 3: That the variations hereby granted are conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 3rd day of February, 2004, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Abell, Brower, Greenough, Presser, Webster and Woodbury
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    None
   APPROVED this 3rd day of February, 2004.
   Signed:
   //s//Michael F. Duhl
   Village President
Countersigned:
//s/Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: November 4, 2003
Posted: November 5, 2003
Passed and Approved: February 3, 2004
