ORDINANCE NO. M-10-2004
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (1275 Scott)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
The west half of Lot 12 in Block 8 in Lloyd’s Subdivision of Blocks 1, 2, 3, 4,
5 in Taylor’s Second Addition to Taylorsport, a subdivision of part of the
Northeast ¼ of Section 18, and part of the Northwest ¼ of Section 17, also the
east 24 acres of the north 48 acres of the Northeast ¼ of Section 18, Township
42 North, Range 13 East of the Third Principal Meridian, in Cook County,
Illinois.
commonly known as 1275 Scott Avenue, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
5 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.16 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for the
following variations from the regulations of Chapter 17.30 of the Zoning
Ordinance: (a) a variation from the intensity of use of lot limitations of
Section 17.30.030, to allow a roofed lot coverage of 2,461.35 square feet,
which exceeds the permitted maximum of 1,995 square feet, resulting in a
variation of 466.35 square feet (23.37%); (b) a variation from the maximum
building size limitations of Section 17.30.040 to allow a gross floor area of
5,242.19 square feet, which exceeds the permitted maximum of 3,192 square feet,
resulting in a variation of 2,050.19 square feet (64.23%); (c) a variation from
the side yard setback requirements of Section 17.030.060, to allow a setback of
4.9 feet in the west side yard, which is less than the required minimum of 6
feet, resulting in a variation of 1.1 feet (18.53%); and (d) a variation from
the building height limitations of Section 17.30.080, to allow a building
height of 3 stories, which exceeds the permitted maximum of 2 ½ stories; all to
allow the construction of a second floor addition; and
   WHEREAS, on February 9, 2004, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variations and, by the
unanimous vote of the 5 members then present, has reported to the Council
recommending that the variations requested be granted; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and particular hardships
associated with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance
with respect to the Subject Property in that: (a) the Subject Property is a
legal, nonconforming “split lot” that is located on the north side of Scott
Street, west of Lake Avenue, with approximately the front one-third of the
property in Winnetka and the rear two-thirds of the property in Glencoe; (b)
the Subject Property is improved with a Craftsman style single family residence
and a detached, one story garage which were built in 1920, before the enactment
of the Zoning Ordinance; (c) the existing residence has an unfinished basement
and an uninhabitable attic; (d) the living space, all of which is on the first
floor, consists of a living room, dining room, two bedrooms, a single bathroom
and a small kitchen and family room; (e) the owners propose a vertical
expansion of the residence that will increase the height of the building by
6.35 feet, from 24.42 feet to 30.77 feet, and will allow four bedrooms,
including a master bedroom, on the second floor, and will, in turn, allow the
first floor to be remodeled and reconfigured by expanding the dining room,
converting a bedroom into a study/guest room, and creating a combined family
room and kitchen with adjacent bathroom, closet and mud room; (f) due to the
height of the original basement and the slope of the Subject Property from
front to back, the entire perimeter of the basement is more than 4 feet above
grade and is thus counted as a full story and is included in calculating gross
floor area; (g) because the basement is considered the first floor of the
building for zoning purposes, the expanded house will be treated as a three
story building, which exceeds the 2 ½ story maximum, although the maximum
distance from the grade of the building to the ridge of the roof will remain
below the vertical height limitations of the Zoning Ordinance; (h) the second
floor addition would add 1,359.59 square feet of gross floor area, which is
approximately 700 square feet less than the 2,051.35 square feet of gross floor
area attributed to the basement; and (i) although the requested variation in
roofed lot coverage is 466.35 square feet, the net increase in roofed lot
coverage is 192.5 square feet, and results from replacing the existing one-car
garage with a 410 square foot two-car garage; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that:
(a) all of the existing nonconformities are the result of the architectural
style of the existing house and its location on the property, all of which have
existed since it was constructed in 1920, before the enactment of the Zoning
Ordinance; (b) the proposed alterations to the house are necessary to provide
two floors of living space with a master bedroom and bath, and a family room
and kitchen that are consistent with typical amenities found in homes in the
immediate vicinity; and (c) the roofed lot coverage variation is necessary to
allow the existing, aging garage to be replaced with a garage that is
consistent with the typical size of garages in the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because: (a) the footprint of the existing house will not
change; (b) the improvements to the house and garage maintain the existing
orientation of the house and garage on the Subject Property as they have
existed since they were first constructed in 1920; (c) the proposed alterations
maintain the Craftsman style of the original house; and (d) the alterations to
the home include opening the enclosed front porch, thereby reducing the visual
bulk of the front building line and
improving the appearance of the building when viewed from the street; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not impair an adequate supply of
light and air because: (a) the total increase in building height is 6.35 feet;
(b) the finished vertical height of the expanded house will be 30.77 feet,
which is less than the applicable building height limitations; (c) the height
of the gables visible to the neighbors on the east and west sides of the
Subject Property will only be increased by 4.9 feet; and (d) and the increased
pitch of the roof will minimize the effect of shadows cast by the higher house;
and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property, as the proposed construction will comply
with all applicable building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not diminish the taxable value of
land and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed construction will not contribute to congestion on the
public streets, as the property will continue to be used for single family
residential purposes; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed construction will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations are in harmony with the general purpose
and intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that they allow the renovation,
restoration and rehabilitation of a structurally sound existing building while
maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the community and protecting
established trees and landscaping.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2:  That the following variation(s) are hereby granted to the
Subject Property, commonly known as 1275 Scott Avenue, and located in the R-
5 Single-Family Residential District provided in Chapter 17.16 of the Winnetka
Village Code, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code: (a) a variation from the
intensity of use of lot limitations of Section 17.30.030 to allow a roofed lot
coverage of 2,461.35 square feet, which exceeds the permitted maximum of
1,995square feet, resulting in a variation of 466.35 square feet (23.37%); (b)
a variation from the maximum building size limitations of Section 17.30.040, to
allow a gross floor area of 5,242.19 square feet, which exceeds the permitted
maximum of 3,192 square feet, resulting in a variation of 2,050.19 square feet
(64.23%); (c) a variation from the side yard setback requirements of Section
17.030.060, to allow a setback of 4.9 feet in the west side yard, which is less
than the required minimum of 6 feet, resulting in a variation of 1.1 feet
(18.53%); and (d) a variation from the building height limitations of Section
17.30.080 to allow a building height of 3 stories, which exceeds the permitted
maximum of 2½ stories; all to allow the construction of a second floor addition
in accordance with the plans and elevations submitted with the application for
variations.
   SECTION 3: That the variations hereby granted are conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 16th day of March, 2004, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Abell, Brower, Greenough, Presser and Webster
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    Trustee Woodbury (recused)
   APPROVED this 16th day of March, 2004.
   Signed:
   //s//Michael F. Duhl
   Village President
Countersigned:
//s//Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: March 2, 2004
Posted: March 3, 2004
Passed and Approved: March 16, 2004
Posted: March 17, 2004
ORDINANCE NO. M-11-2004
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (566 Orchard)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
The northerly half of the East 100 feet (except the West 53.75 feet thereof) of
the West 187 feet of that part of Block 15 lying east of the east line of
Walnut Street in Garland’s Addition to Winnetka, said Addition being a
subdivision of the North 120 acres of the Southwest Quarter of Section 21,
Township 42 North, Range 13 East of the Third Principal Meridian (except the
North 33 feet of said land used for street purposes) in Cook County, Illinois,
commonly known as 566 Orchard Lane, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
5 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.16 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for the
following variations: (a) a variation from the intensity of use of lot
limitations of Section 17.30.030, to allow a roofed lot coverage of 1,914.58
square feet, which exceeds the permitted maximum of 1,910.47, a variation of
4.11 square feet (0.21%); (b) a variation from the maximum building size
limitations of Section 17.30.040, to allow a gross floor area of 3,994.62
square feet, which exceeds the permitted maximum of 3,056.76 square feet,
resulting in a variation of 937.86 square feet (30.38%); (c) a variation from
the side yard setback requirements of Section 17.30.060, to allow an east side
yard setback of 6.04 feet, which is less than the required minimum of 8 feet,
resulting in a variation of 1.96 feet (24.5%); (d) a variation from the
limitations on garage door width in Section 17.30.110, to allow a single front-
facing garage door to be 16 feet wide, which exceeds the applicable limitation
of 15.26 feet, and (e) a variation from the nonconformities regulations of
Section 17.64.010, to allow for new and enlarged window and door openings in a
nonconforming wall; all to allow the construction of a two story addition at
the rear of the home and the installation of a new front-facing garage door;
and
   WHEREAS, on February 9, 2004, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variations and, by the
unanimous vote of the 5 members then present, has reported to the Council
recommending that the side yard variation and the variation for the garage door
be granted, and by the favorable vote of four of the five members then present,
has reported to the Council recommending that the remaining variations be
granted; and
   WHEREAS, the owners have modified their application to eliminate the
construction of the second floor of the proposed addition, which reduces the
scope of the variation requested by reducing the requested gross floor area
variation from 937.86 square feet to 483.14 square feet, although the resulting
modification in the footprint increases the roofed lot coverage variation from
4.11 square feet to 19.06 square feet; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties associated with carrying out the
strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect to the Subject Property
in that: (a) the Subject Property is a legal, nonconforming lot that is 46.25
feet wide and 165.23 feet deep; (b) the Subject Property is improved with a
single family residence that was constructed in 1967, before the side yard
setback requirements required one eight-foot side yard setback; (c) the side
yard variation is requested to allow the replacement and addition of windows in
the nonconforming east building wall, and to allow the roof line of the
building to be raised; (d) the single family residence on the Subject Property
has an attached garage with front-facing garage doors forming part of the front
building line; (e) the variation in garage door width will allow the existing
door to be replaced without requiring structural changes to the garage; (f) the
building addition at the rear of the house will allow the replacement of an
octagonal living room that is connected to the family room at the rear of the
house by a narrow hallway; (g) the proposed first floor plan will allow a
reconfiguration of the interior space on the first floor to provide a
rectangular living room, a dining room, a kitchen with separate breakfast room,
and a mud room and laundry area between the garage and the kitchen; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that
(a) the garage door replacement is part of exterior improvements to the
building; (b) the front-loading garage is part of the structure of the house
and neither the size nor the location of the garage door can be altered without
structural changes to both the garage and the house; (c) the changes in the
windows on the east wall are necessary to accommodate the reconfiguration of
the first floor, which will place the mud room and kitchen in typical locations
adjacent to the garage entrance; (d) the first floor addition will allow the
creation of a dining room in the area between the kitchen and living room; and
(e) the first floor addition will allow the replacement of the octagonal living
room appendage at the rear of the house, providing a more typical building
footprint and exterior, and a more typical first floor room configuration; and
   WHEREAS, the variations granted by this ordinance will not alter the
essential character of the neighborhood, because the proposed modifications to
the garage, east wall and first floor addition will increase the roof pitch and
change the exterior building materials to make them more consistent with older
homes in the area and will maintain the appearance of the front of the building
within the existing streetscape; and
   WHEREAS, the variations granted by this ordinance will not impair an
adequate supply of light and air because the proposed improvements will not
encroach further into the side yards and the building height will conform with
applicable limitations; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property, as the proposed construction will comply
with all applicable building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the variations granted by this ordinance will not diminish the
taxable value of land and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable
value of the Subject Property may be increased because of the proposed
improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the construction allowed pursuant to this ordinance will not
contribute to congestion on the public streets, as the property will continue
to be used for single family residential purposes; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed first floor addition and
window and garage door improvements will otherwise impair the public health,
safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the inhabitants of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the variations granted by this ordinance are in harmony with the
general purpose and intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that they allow
the renovation, restoration and rehabilitation of a structurally sound existing
building while maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the community
and protecting established trees and landscaping.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2:  That the following variations are hereby granted to the Subject
Property, commonly known as 566 Orchard Lane, and located in the R-5 Single-
Family Residential District provided in Chapter 17.16 of the Winnetka Village
Code, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code: (a) a variation from the intensity
of use of lot limitations of Section 17.30.030, to allow a roofed lot coverage
of 1,914.58 square feet, which exceeds the permitted maximum of 1,910.47 square
feet, a variation of 4.11 square feet (0.21%); (b) a variation from the maximum
building size limitations of Section 17.30.040, to allow a gross floor area of
3,539.9 square feet, which exceeds the permitted maximum of 3,056.76 square
feet, resulting in a variation of 483.14 square feet (15.8%); (c) a variation
from the side yard setback requirements of Section 17.30.060, to allow an east
side yard setback of 6.04 feet, which is less than the required minimum of 8
feet, resulting in a variation of 1.96 feet (24.5%); (d) a variation from the
limitations on garage door width in Section 17.30.110, to allow a single front-
facing garage door to be 16 feet wide, which exceeds applicable limitation of
15.26 feet, and (e) a variation from the nonconformities regulations of Section
17.64.010, to allow for new and enlarged window and door openings in a
nonconforming wall; all to allow the construction of a one story addition at
the rear of the home and the installation of a new front facing garage door in
accordance with the plans and elevations submitted with the application for
variations, as modified by the drawings dated March 30, 2004.
   SECTION 3: That the variations hereby granted are conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
ADOPTED this 6th day of April, 2004, pursuant to the following roll call vote:
AYES:    Trustees Abell, Brower, Greenough, Presser, Webster and Woodbury
NAYS:    None
ABSENT:    None
APPROVED this 6th day of April, 2004.
   Signed:
   //s//Michael F. Duhl
   Village President
Countersigned:
//s//Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: March 16, 2004
Posted: March 17, 2004
Passed and Approved: April 6, 2004
Posted: April 7, 2004
ORDINANCE NO. M-12-2004
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (1360 Edgewood)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
Parcel 1: Lot 2 in Brockett’s Resubdivision of part of Block 19 in Chicago
North Shore Land Company’s Subdivision in Section 8, 17 and 18, Township 42
North, Range 13, East of the Third Principal Meridian, according to the plat of
resubdivision recorded as Document Number 18639535,in Cook County, Illinois,
commonly known as 1360 Edgewood, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
5 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.16 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for the
following variations: (a) a variation from the side yard setback requirements
of Section 17.30.060, to allow a north side-yard setback of 9.35 feet, which is
less than the required minimum of 12 feet, resulting in a variation of 2.65
feet (22.08%); (b) a variation from the limitations on changes to nonconforming
structures in Section 17.64.010, to allow for the enlargement of windows in a
nonconforming wall; and (c) a variation from Section 17.30.110 to permit a
front-facing garage door to be 16 feet wide, which exceeds the permitted
maximum of 9 feet, all to allow the construction of a second floor addition on
the north side of the house, a one-story kitchen and mudroom addition at the
rear, and the replacement of windows and doors in nonconforming walls; and
   WHEREAS, on February 9, 2004, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variations and, by the
unanimous vote of the 5 members then present, has reported to the Council
recommending that the variations requested be granted; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and particular hardships
associated with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance
with respect to the Subject Property in that: (a) the Subject Property is a
flag lot located on the south side of Edgewood Lane; (b) the “flagpole” portion
of the Subject Property is 10 feet wide and 127 feet long and attaches to the
body of the Subject Property at its northwest corner; (c) the 10-foot wide
“flagpole” and a 10-foot easement along the west lot line combine with a 10-
foot easement over properties to the west, to provide access to the Subject
Property, the property to the north of the Subject Property, and to a flag lot
west of the Subject Property; (d) because of the shared access and the
orientation of the single family residence on the Subject Property toward the
shared access, the west lot line is treated as the front lot line for zoning
purposes, with the north and south lot lines forming the side lot lines and the
rear lot line being the east lot line; (e) because of the location of the
required yards, the body of the lot has an average lot width of 133.64 feet,
and a lot depth of 105.87 feet; (f) although the house on the Subject Property
was in a conforming location at the time it was constructed in 1966, amendments
since that time, including recent amendments to side yard requirements that
impose greater side yard setback requirements for lots that are more than 100
feet wide, the Subject Property now has a legally nonconforming front (west)
yard setback and north side yard setback; and (g) the side yard variation is
being requested to allow for the construction of a second floor addition above
the attached, front-loading garage located at the north end of the house, and
to allow a mud room addition at the northeast corner of the house, behind the
attached garage; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that:
(a) the requested variations are needed to make improvements to the home by
adding a second floor above the existing garage, adding a mud room behind the
garage to connect to a new, conforming breakfast room at the center rear area
of the home, to modify windows in the nonconforming east wall and to replace
the existing garage doors; (b) the nonconforming location of the house cannot
be corrected without removing more than nine feet of the existing garage, which
would render it unusable; and (c) the single, 16-foot wide garage door cannot
be replaced with two smaller garage doors because the garage is not wide enough
to accommodate two separately framed, conforming doors; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because the modifications to the existing home will not be
visible from the street and do not encroach further into either the front yard
setback or the north side-yard setback, and;
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not impair an adequate supply of
light and air because the north side-yard is opposite the rear yard of the
property to the north and the addition will not cast any shadow in the
immediate vicinity of the house and living areas of the property to the north;
and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property, as the proposed construction will comply
with all applicable building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not diminish the taxable value of
land and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed construction will not contribute to congestion on the
public streets, as the property will continue to be used for single family
residential purposes; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed addition will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations are in harmony with the general purpose
and intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that they allow the renovation,
restoration and rehabilitation of a structurally sound existing building while
maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the community and protecting
established trees and landscaping.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2:  That the following variations are hereby granted to the Subject
Property, commonly known as 1360 Edgewood, and located in the R-5 Single-Family
Residential District provided in Chapter 17.16 of the Winnetka Village Code,
Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code: (a) a variation from the side yard
setback requirements of Section 17.30.060, to allow a north side-yard setback
of 9.35 feet, which is less than the required minimum of 12 feet, resulting in
a variation of 2.65 feet (22.08%); (b) a variation from the limitations on
changes to nonconforming structures in Section 17.64.010, to allow for the
enlargement of windows in a nonconforming wall; and (c) a variation from
Section 17.30.110, to permit a front-facing garage door to be 16 feet wide,
which exceeds the permitted maximum of 9, all to allow the construction of a
second floor addition on the north side of the house, a one-story kitchen and
mudroom addition at the rear, and the replacement of windows and doors in
nonconforming walls, all in accordance with the plans and elevations submitted
with the application for variations.
   SECTION 3: That the variations hereby granted are conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 16th day of March, 2004, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Abell, Brower, Greenough, Presser, Webster and Woodbury
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    None
   APPROVED this 16th day of March, 2004.
   Signed:
   //s//Michael F. Duhl
   Village President
Countersigned:
//s//Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: March 2, 2004
Posted: March 3, 2004
Passed and Approved: March 16, 2004
Posted: March 17, 2004
ORDINANCE NO. M-13-2004
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (224 Sheridan)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
Lot 5 in White’s Resubdivision of Winnetka Villas, being a subdivision of Block
2 in Phillip Partition in Fractional Southeast ¼ of Section 21, Township 42
North, Range 13, East of the Third Principal Meridian, in Cook County,
Illinois,
commonly known as 224 Sheridan Road, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
4 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.16 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for the
following variations from Chapter 17.30 of the Zoning Ordinance: (a) a
variation from the intensity of use of lot limitations of Section 17.30.030, to
allow a roofed lot coverage of 2,169.8 square feet, which exceeds the permitted
maximum of 2,094.25 square feet, resulting in a variation of 75.55 square feet
(3.61%); (b) a variation from the maximum building size limitations of Section
17.30.040, to allow a gross floor area of 3,891.35 square feet, which exceeds
the permitted maximum of 3,350.8 square feet, resulting in a variation of
540.55 square feet (16.13%); (c) a variation from the corner setback
requirements in Section 17.30.050, to allow a corner setback of 10.95 feet,
whereas a minimum of 24.4 feet is required, resulting in a variation of 13.45
feet (55.12%); and (d) a variation from Section 17.30.110, to allow a front-
facing garage door to be 18 feet wide, whereas a maximum width of 9 feet is
permitted, all to allow for the construction of a two-story addition at the
northwest corner of the residence and for the construction of a second-floor
addition above the garage; and
   WHEREAS, on February 9, 2004, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variations and, by the
unanimous vote of the 5 members then present, has reported to the Council
recommending that the variations requested be granted; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and particular hardships
associated with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance
with respect to the Subject Property in that: (a) the Subject Property is part
of a five-lot subdivision that was developed in 1926 on the west side of
Sheridan Road north of Fuller Lane; (b) the five lots in the subdivision are
each developed with an Italian-style building, with the buildings encircling a
central courtyard and with access to the five lots being provided by a private
road that encircles the subdivision; (c) the curving private road is formed, in
part, by an easement along the south lot line of the Subject Property; (d)
because the Subject Property fronts on Sheridan Road and the private road is a
private street under the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, the Subject Property is a
corner lot that is subject to the normal setback from Sheridan Road and a 24.4
foot corner setback that is measured from the edge of the easement along the
south of the Subject Property; (e) the owners propose to expand the attached
garage located at the southwest corner of the house by removing a bay window of
the room adjacent to the kitchen, which currently serves as a family room; (f)
the owners also propose to add a second story above the existing one-story
garage and to integrate it into the existing second floor to create a master
suite; (g) the owners also propose to replace a one-story screened porch at the
interior northwest corner of the home and replace it with a two-story addition
that will replace the family room by adding a living area adjacent to the
former living room, and will create an additional bedroom, bath and laundry
area on the second floor; and (h) in addition to requiring an increased setback
along the south lot line, the easement area for the private street is also
excluded from the lot area when calculating gross floor area; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that:
(a) the requested setback variation is due to the location of the house and the
easement along the south lot line, and cannot be cured without reconstructing
the house; (b) the requested garage door variation is necessary to allow for
the expansion of the existing garage, which is accessed from the private
street; (c) the width of the proposed expanded garage is not sufficient to
allow two individual garage doors to be provided without requiring additional
structural changes to the building and reduction of the size of the kitchen;
(d) the gross floor area and lot coverage variations are related to the
creation of the master bedroom suite, addition of a first-floor family room and
relocation of a bedroom on the second floor while maintaining consistency of
design and building lines; and (e) a two-car garage, a family room and a master
suite are all typical amenities found in homes within the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because the requested variations do not affect the portions of
the building that face Sheridan Road and the interior courtyard, thus
maintaining the integrity of both the Sheridan Road block face and the five-lot
subdivision; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not impair an adequate supply of
light and air because the two-story addition is in a conforming location, the
degree of nonconformity in the south setback will be reduced due to the removal
of an existing bay window and the proposed additions will conform with building
height limitations; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property, as the proposed construction will comply
with all applicable building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not diminish the taxable value of
land and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed construction will not contribute to congestion on the
public streets, as the property will continue to be used for single family
residential purposes; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed additions will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations are in harmony with the general purpose
and intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that they allow the renovation,
restoration and rehabilitation of a structurally sound existing building while
maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the community and protecting
established trees and landscaping.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2:  That the following variations are hereby granted to the Subject
Property, commonly known as 224 Sheridan Road, and located in the R-4 Single-
Family Residential District provided in Chapter 17.16 of the Winnetka Village
Code, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, (a) a variation from the intensity
of use of lot limitations of Section 17.30.030, to allow a roofed lot coverage
of 2,169.8 square feet, which exceeds the permitted maximum of 2,094.25 square
feet, resulting in a variation of 75.55 square feet (3.61%); (b) a variation
from the maximum building size limitations of Section 17.30.040, to allow a
gross floor area of 3,891.35 square feet, which exceeds the permitted maximum
of 3,350.8 square feet, resulting in a variation of 540.55 square feet
(16.13%); (c) a variation from the corner setback requirements of Section
17.30.050, to allow a corner setback of 10.95 feet, whereas a minimum of 24.4
feet is required, resulting in a variation of 13.45 feet (55.12%); and (d) a
variation from Section 17.30.110, to allow a front-facing garage door to be 18
feet wide, whereas a maximum width of 9 feet is permitted, all to allow for the
construction of a two-story addition at the northwest corner of the residence
and for the construction of a second-floor addition above the garage, all in
accordance with the plans and elevations submitted with the application for
variations.
   SECTION 3: That the variations hereby granted are conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 16th day of March, 2004, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Abell, Brower, Presser, Webster and Woodbury
   NAYS:    Trustee Greenough
   ABSENT:    None
   APPROVED this 16th day of March, 2004.
   Signed:
   //s//Michael F. Duhl
   Village President
Countersigned:
//s//Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: March 2, 2004
Posted: March 3, 2004
Passed and Approved: March 16, 2004
Posted: March 17, 2004
ORDINANCE NO. M-14-2004
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (1117 Merrill)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
Lot 9 in Block 2 in Jared Gage’s Subdivision of part of the Northwest Quarter
fractional Section 17 and part of the East half of the Southwest Quarter of
fractional Section 8, Township 42 North, Range 13, East of the Third Principal
Meridian, in Cook County, Illinois.
commonly known as 1117 Merrill Street, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the
R-5 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.16 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for the
following variations: (a) a variation from the intensity of use of lot
limitations of Section 17.30.030, to allow a roofed lot coverage of 2,202.07
square feet, whereas a maximum of 1,875 square feet is permitted, a variation
of 327.07 square feet (17.44%); (b) a variation from the intensity of use of
lot limitations of Section 17.30.030, to permit impermeable lot coverage of
3,821.48 square feet, whereas the permitted maximum is 3,750 square feet, a
variation of 71.48 square feet (1.91%); and (c) a variation from the side yard
setback requirements of Section 17.30.060 to allow an east side yard setback of
7.28 feet, whereas a minimum of 8 feet is required, a variation of 0.72 feet
(9%), all for the purpose of constructing a 45.67 square foot addition on the
west side of the residence, constructing a 113.4 square foot addition at the
rear of the house, and constructing a second floor addition over the entire
first floor; and
   WHEREAS, on April 12, 2004, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variations and, by the
unanimous vote of the 6 members then present, has reported to the Council
recommending that the variations requested be granted; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties associated with carrying out the
strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect to the Subject Property
in that: (a) the Subject Property is a 50-foot wide lot that is improved with a
one-story house that was built in 1910, before the enactment of the original
Zoning Ordinance; (b) the Subject Property has a conforming west side yard of
6.25 feet and an east side yard of 7.28 feet, which was rendered nonconforming
by recent amendments to the Zoning Ordinance that increased side yard setbacks
by requiring one 8-foot side yard; (c) the side yard variation is required to
allow the vertical extension of the east building wall in conjunction with the
addition of the proposed second floor; (d) the Subject Property has a legal,
nonconforming roofed lot coverage that exceeds the applicable roofed lot
coverage limitations by 168 square feet; (e) the applicant proposes to add an
additional 159.07 square feet of roofed lot coverage, to allow the 14-foot, 5-
inch long dining room wall to be bumped out three feet, and to allow the
construction of a 10.25-foot by 10.83-foot addition at the rear entrance of the
house for a powder room and pantry on the first floor; (f) the proposed
addition to the dining room is on the west side of the house, where the side
yard setback is conforming, and the addition will comply with building
articulation requirements; (g) constructing the second floor addition with a
conforming side yard setback would put the second floor walls out of alignment
with the first floor walls, would require structural supports to be placed in
the first floor living areas, would require a flat roof over portions of the
east part of the first floor and would increase both construction costs and
subsequent maintenance costs while making both the first and second floors less
functional; (h) the proposed rear addition is necessary to allow the
construction of an adequately sized rear bedroom on the second floor, and to
allow for the location of the interior stairway; and (i) the applicant proposes
to reduce the amount of impermeable surface by 207.06 square feet, thereby
reducing the degree of nonconformity to 71.48 square feet; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that:
(a) the existing house is a one-story building, with two bedrooms and one and
one-half baths; (b) the existing house does not have a master bedroom suite,
and the two bedrooms, which share a common bathroom, are both too small to meet
typical contemporary living standards; (c) the kitchen is not consistent with
typical contemporary living standards, in that it is a small, square room with
a U-shaped layout, and it is not adjacent to either the dining room or the
family room; and (d) the proposed modifications to the first floor will create
a master bedroom suite, enlarge the dining room and kitchen, and create an open
floor plan on the first floor, thereby providing better access from the kitchen
area to eating areas and improving traffic flow throughout the first floor; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because the proposed additions will improve the appearance of
the house from the street, while conforming with maximum building size and
building height limitations; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not impair an adequate supply of
light and air because the proposed additions will conform with applicable
building height limitations, will have a conforming west side yard and an
articulated west building line, and the east building line will be no closer to
the adjoining property than the building as originally constructed; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property, as the proposed construction will comply
with all applicable building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not diminish the taxable value of
land and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed construction will not contribute to congestion on the
public streets, as the property will continue to be used for single family
residential purposes; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the proposed additions will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations are in harmony with the general purpose
and intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that they allow the renovation,
restoration and rehabilitation of a structurally sound existing building while
maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the community and protecting
established trees and landscaping.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1   The foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the findings
of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth herein.
   SECTION 2:   The following variations are hereby granted to the Subject
Property, commonly known as 1117 Merrill Street, and located in the R-5 Single-
Family Residential District provided in Chapter 17.16 of the Winnetka Village
Code, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code: (a) a variation from the intensity
of use of lot limitations of Section 17.30.030, to allow a roofed lot coverage
of 2,202.07 square feet, whereas a maximum of 1,875 square feet is permitted, a
variation of 327.07 square feet (17.44%); (b) a variation from the intensity of
use of lot limitations of Section 17.30.030, to permit impermeable lot coverage
of 3,821.48 square feet, whereas the permitted maximum is 3,750 square feet, a
variation of 71.48 square feet (1.91%); and (c) a variation from the side yard
setback requirements of Section 17.30.060 to allow an east side yard setback of
7.28 feet, whereas a minimum of 8 feet is required, a variation of 0.72 feet
(9%), all for the purpose of constructing a 45.67 square foot addition on the
west side of the residence, constructing a 113.4 square foot addition at the
rear of the house, and constructing a second floor addition over the entire
first floor, all in accordance with the plans and elevations submitted with the
application for variations.
   SECTION 3:   The variations hereby granted are conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4:   This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 4th day of May, 2004, pursuant to the following roll call vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Abell, Eilers, Greenough, Ritchell, Tucker and Webster
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    None
   APPROVED this 4th day of May, 2004.
   Signed:
   //s//Michael F. Duhl
   Village President
Countersigned:
//s//Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
