ORDINANCE NO. M-21-2004
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (1174 Scott)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
Lot 5 and the East 30 feet of Lot 6 in Block 11 in Chicago Northshore Land
Company’s Subdivision in Sections 17 and 18, Township 42 North, Range 13 East
of the Third Principal Meridian, in Cook County, Illinois,
commonly known as 1174 Scott Avenue, Winnetka, Illinois and located in the R-
5 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.16 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for a
variation from the 2½-story building height limitation of Section 17.30.080, to
allow the upper floor of the existing residence to be extended to the west
building line, which will increase the degree of nonconformity in the building
height, in that the proposed third floor expansion will increase the area of
the third floor, which is currently a third story because the area of that
floor with a height of seven feet or more exceeds 50% of the area of the floor
below; and
   WHEREAS, on June 14, 2004, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variations and, by the
unanimous vote of the six members then present, has reported to the Council
recommending that the requested variations be granted; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and particular hardships
associated with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance
with respect to the Subject Property in that: (a) the house was built in 1916,
before the enactment of the original Zoning Ordinance; (b) the house is a Dutch
colonial style with a gambrel roof and shed dormers, resulting in a third floor
that is treated as a full third story for zoning purposes because the area of
the third floor with a height of seven feet or more exceeds 50% of the area of
the floor below it; (c) the vertical height of the building will remain at
29.83 feet, which is less than the vertical height limitation of 31 feet; and
(d) the third floor gambrel roof line was not extended completely to the west
building line when the house was originally constructed, giving an asymmetrical
appearance to an otherwise symmetrical structure; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that:
(a) the home as it now stands has an unfinished and asymmetrical appearance;
(b) it is not possible to make the improvements proposed for the first and
second floors without extending the third floor to the west building line; (c)
the upper floor modifications are part of improvements that will allow the
second floor master bedroom to be upgraded; and (d) applying the 2½ story
limitation to the existing house would effectively preclude any improvements to
the upper two floors of the house without reconfiguring the roof and reducing
the amount of usable space on the third floor; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because (a) the proposed extension of the third floor and
dormer to the west building line will create a symmetrical, gambrel roof
thereby improving the appearance of the Dutch colonial style house, and
maintaining the appearance of the Subject Property in relation to the other
properties on the block face; (b) the gambrel roof softens the visual bulk of
the third floor; (c) the only other zoning nonconformity on the Subject
Property is not affected by the proposed addition because it is at the east
side yard, where a car port extends over the driveway; and (d) the house will
continue to meet or exceed all other applicable zoning requirements for the R-
5 Zoning District; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not impair an adequate supply of light
and air because the proposed improvements will comply with the applicable
vertical height limitations and the west side yard line will continue to be
19.72 feet, which exceeds the 12-foot minimum west side yard that is required
on the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property, as the proposed construction will comply
with all applicable building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed construction will not contribute to congestion on the
public streets, as the property will continue to be used for single family
residential purposes; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the requested variation will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation is in harmony with the general purpose and
intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that it allows the renovation and
rehabilitation of a structurally sound existing building while maintaining the
existing scale and appearance of the community and protecting established trees
and landscaping; and
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 :   The foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the findings
of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth herein.
   SECTION 2:   The following variation is hereby granted to the Subject
Property, commonly known as 1174 Scott Avenue, Winnetka, Illinois and located
in the R-5 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.16
of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code: a
variation from the 2½-story building height limitation of Section 17.30.080, to
allow the upper floor of the existing residence to be extended to the west
building line, in accordance with the plans and elevations submitted with the
application for variation, which will increase the degree of nonconformity in
the building height, in that the proposed third floor expansion will increase
the area of the third floor, which is currently a third story because the area
of that floor with a height of seven feet or more exceeds 50% of the area of
the floor below.
   SECTION 3:   The variation hereby granted is conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4:   This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 20th day of July, 2004, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Abell, Eilers, Greenough and Ritchell
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    Trustees Tucker and Webster
   APPROVED this 20th day of July, 2004.
   Signed:
   //s//Michael F. Duhl
   Village President
Countersigned:
//s/Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: July 6, 2007
Posted: July 7, 2007
Passed and Approved: July 20, 2004
