ORDINANCE NO. M-22-2004
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (1128 Mt. Pleasant)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
Lot 1 in Laurie’s Subdivision of Lots 71, 72, 73 and 74 in Alles Sunset
Subdivision of the Northeast Quarter of the Southwest Quarter of Section 20,
Township 42, Range 13, East of the Third Principal Meridian, in Cook County,
Illinois,
commonly known as 1128 Mt. Pleasant Lane, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in
the R-2 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.16 of
the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for a
variation from the corner yard setback requirements of Section 17.30.050 to
allow the west building line of a proposed new home to have a corner (front)
setback of 10.5 feet as measured to an encroaching window well and of 11.5 feet
as measured to the point on an eave 18 inches from the wall of the proposed
screened porch, whereas a setback of 28.48 feet is required, resulting in
variations of 17.98 feet (63.13%) and 16.98 feet (59.62%) respectively; and
   WHEREAS, on June 14, 2004, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variations and, by a vote
of 5 to 1, has reported to the Council recommending that the variations
requested be granted; and
   WHEREAS, after failing to obtain the requisite votes to introduce an
ordinance granting the variation as requested, the owner of the Subject
Property modified the building plans so that the proposed corner setback along
the west lot line will be 15 feet, which is the side yard setback that would be
required if the Subject Property were an interior lot; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and particular hardships
associated with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance
with respect to the Subject Property in that: (a) the Subject Property is
considered a corner lot because the dedicated, but unimproved Glendale Avenue
right of way lies adjacent to its west lot line; (b) the unimproved Glendale
Avenue right of way is part of Crow Island Woods, which is owned by the
Winnetka Park District; (c) on January 6, 1998, the Village Council enacted
Ordinance M-509-97, which vacated all of the platted, unimproved public rights
of way within the boundaries of Crow Island Woods and transferred ownership of
those vacated rights of way to the Winnetka Park District; (d) the Village did
not include the unimproved Glendale Avenue right of way in the rights of way to
be vacated by Ordinance M-509-97, because the statute then governing street
vacations the adjoining owners on each side to take ownership to the center
line of the right of way, which would have severed a portion of Crow Island
Woods and attached it to the Subject Property; (e) the Village does not plan to
open the unimproved Glendale Avenue right of way for vehicular or pedestrian
use, because it does not connect to another public right of way, it is needed
as a location for certain Village utility lines, and vacating the right of way
is not necessary for it to continue to function as part of Crow Island Woods;
(f) it is not in the public interest to vacate the Glendale Avenue right of way
and convey any portion of it to an adjoining private owner, because it would
reduce the amount of public open space in Crow Island Woods and increase the
allowable building size on the adjoining private property, which would alter
the character and scale of the neighborhood; and (g) because the Village does
not plan for the unimproved Glendale Avenue right of way to function as a
street, the Subject Property is effectively an interior lot; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that
the interests of the public in observing the full corner setback on the Subject
Property are outweighed by the negative impact on the Subject Property, because
(a) the Subject Property looks like and functions as an interior lot; and (b)
observing the full corner setback on the Subject Property would shift the bulk
of the house to the east and south and require the construction of house that
is as much as 18 feet narrower than the one proposed which, in turn, would
negatively affect the appearance of the Subject Property within the existing
neighborhood and reduce available open space in the rear yard; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because new home would be located on an existing dead-end
street and the proposed setback would be the same as that of the home currently
on the property; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not impair an adequate supply of light
and air because the proposed improvements will comply with the Zoning Ordinance
with regard to building height and gross floor area; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property, as the proposed construction will comply
with all applicable building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings throughout the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed construction will not contribute to congestion on the
public streets, as the property will continue to be used for single family
residential purposes; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the requested variation will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation is in harmony with the general purpose and
intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that it will allow the construction
of a new residence while maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the
community and protecting established trees and landscaping.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1 : The foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the findings
of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth herein.
   SECTION 2:  The following variation is hereby granted to the Subject
Property, commonly known as 1128 Mt. Pleasant Lane, and located in the R-
2 Single-Family Residential District provided in Chapter 17.16 of the Winnetka
Village Code, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code: a variation from the
corner yard setback requirements of Section 17.30.050 to allow the west
building line of a proposed new home to have a corner (front) setback of 15.0
feet, whereas a setback of 28.48 feet is required, resulting in variations of
13.48 feet (47.33%), all in accordance with the plans and elevations submitted
with the application for variations.
   SECTION 3: The variation hereby granted is conditioned upon the commencement
of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective date of this
Ordinance.
   SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 10th day of August, 2004, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES:    Trustees Abell, Eilers, Greenough and Tucker
   NAYS:    None
   ABSENT:    Trustees Ritchell and Webster
   APPROVED this 10th day of August, 2004.
   Signed:
   //s//Michael F. Duhl
   Village President
Countersigned:
//s//Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: July 20, 2004
Posted: July 21, 2004
Passed and Approved: August 10, 2004
