ORDINANCE NO. M-23-2004
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (1205 Tower)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
The East 100.00 feet of the West 200.00 feet of that part of the South 20 acres
of the West ½ of the Northwest ¼ of Section 17, Township 42 North, Range 13
East of the Third Principal Meridian bounded as follows: Beginning at a point
440.00 feet west of the east line and 33.00 feet north of the south line of
said 20 acres, thence running north parallel to the east 132.00 feet, thence
west parallel with said south line 400.00 feet, thence south parallel with the
east line 132.00 feet, thence east parallel with the south line 400.00 feet, to
the point of beginning, in Cook County, Illinois;
commonly known as 1205 Tower Road, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
3 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.16 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for the
following variation from requirements of the Lot, Space, Bulk and Yard
Regulations for Single-Family Residential Districts established by Section
17.30 of the Zoning Ordinance: a variation from the average front setback
requirement of Section 17.30.050.C to allow a new single family residence to be
constructed with a front yard setback of 40 feet, rather than the required
average front setback of 53.65 feet, resulting in a variation of 13.65 feet
(25.44%); and
   WHEREAS, on June 14, 2004, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variations and, by the vote
of 4 of the 6 members then present, has reported to the Council recommending
that the requested variation be denied; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and particular hardships
associated with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance
with respect to the Subject Property in that: (a) the minimum front setback
required in the R-3 Single Family Residential Zoning District pursuant to
Section 17.30.050.A is 40 feet and the applicant proposes a front setback of 40
feet; (b) the average front setback imposed on the Subject Property pursuant to
Section 17.30.050.C is 53.65 feet; (c) the additional 13.65 feet of front yard
setback for the Subject Property is the result of the combined impact of the
length of the block face and the depths of the lots on the block face; (d) the
Subject Property is located on a unique block face that extends along the north
side of Tower Road from Forest Glen to Gordon Terrace and contains 10 parcels
of property located in two different zoning districts; (e) the two lots at the
west end of the block face both have a lot depth of 136.75 feet and are zoned
R-5 Single Family Residential; (f) the next four lots from the west end of the
block face, which include the Subject Property, all have a lot depth of 132.00
feet and are zoned R-3 Single Family Residential; (g) the seventh, eighth and
ninth lots from the west end of the block face, commonly known as 1173, 1163
and 1141 Tower Road, have lot depths of 264 feet, 297 feet and 167 feet,
respectively, and are also zoned R-3 Single Family Residential; (h) the last
lot on the block face, at its east end, has a lot depth of 115 feet and is one
of four parcels that are located at the northwest corner of Tower Road and
Gordon Terrace and are zoned R-5 Single Family Residential; (i) the deepest
properties on the block face, 1173, 1163 and 1141 Tower Road, also have the
greatest front yard setbacks, with the front yard setbacks being 109.03 feet,
118.94 feet and 60.94 feet, respectively; (j) the remaining six parcels on the
block face (not including the Subject Property) have front setbacks that range
from 36.33 feet to 49.00 feet, with an average front setback of 41.59 feet; (k)
the average front setback on the block face, excluding only the Subject
Property and the two deepest parcels, is 44.35 feet; (l) the applicant requests
the minimum permitted front yard setback of 40 feet to maintain open space in
the rear yard of the Subject Property, because Tower Road is an arterial
street, which makes front yard activities undesirable and unsafe for children;
(m) the house proposed for the Subject Property has also been designed to
maximize the west side yard, so as to preserve a large oak tree in the
buildable portion of the lot and to reduce the amount of building bulk that is
visible from the street; (n) the strict application of the average front
setback requirement will necessitate reorienting the proposed house to preserve
rear yard open space by using more of the width of the lot, which will result
in the loss of the mature oak tree and reduce the amount of open space between
the new house and the house on the property immediately to the west; (o) the
strict application of the average front setback requirement will have a
negative impact on both the Subject Property and the other properties on the
block face, because (i) it will disrupt the existing consistency in front
setbacks, particularly at the west end of the block face, and (ii) it will
gradually increase the average front setbacks as the other properties on the
block face are redeveloped; and (p) the strict application of the average front
setback requirement to the Subject Property is not consistent with either the
stated purpose of the R-3 Single Family Residential Zoning District or the land
use and development objectives stated in Section 22.04.020.B of the Zoning
Ordinance, in that a 53.65-foot front yard setback (i) will alter the
architectural scale and character of the immediate vicinity, (ii) will increase
the visual density of the block face, as viewed from the street, (iii) will
reduce open space in the rear of the Subject Property and the property to the
west, and (iv) will not protect established trees and landscaping; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that
requiring a 53.65-foot front yard setback: (a) will reduce the amount of open
space in the rear yard, although space for rear yard activities is necessary
due to the Subject Property’s location on an arterial street; (b) will require
changing the orientation of the home, thereby reducing the side yard setbacks
and placing the new house in closer proximity to its neighbors; (c) will
present a more visually jarring line on the block face; and (d) will jeopardize
the mature, 34-inch diameter oak tree located near the west side of the Subject
Property, all without providing a measurable public benefit; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because: (a) the proposed front setback (i) will be consistent
with the front yard setbacks on most of the other properties on the block face,
especially the properties adjacent to the east and west of the Subject
Property, (ii) will allow the new house to maintain larger side yard setbacks
than are required by the Zoning Ordinance, and (iii) will provide greater
protection for the 34-inch diameter oak tree near the west side of the Subject
property; and (b) the design of the new house contains both vertical and
horizontal articulation, which serve to reduce the appearance of the building
mass; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not impair an adequate supply of light
and air because: (a) the proposed new construction will comply with the
building height, building size and intensity of use limitations of the Zoning
Ordinance to height and gross floor area; and (b) the placement of the new
house on the Subject Property will provide side yard setbacks that exceed the
setback requirements of the Zoning Ordinance; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property, as the proposed construction will comply
with all applicable building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed new construction; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed construction will not contribute to congestion on the
public streets, because the Subject Property will continue to be used for
single family residential purposes; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the requested variation will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation is in harmony with the general purpose and
intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that it will allow the construction
of a new residence that is designed and situated on the Subject Property (a) so
as to maintain the existing scale and appearance of the Subject Property and
the immediate neighborhood, and (b) so as to protect established trees and
landscaping.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1:   The foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the findings
of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth herein.
   SECTION 2:   The following variation from the Front Yard Setback Regulations
for Single-Family Residential Districts established by Section 17.30 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance is hereby granted to the Subject Property, commonly
known as 1205 Tower Road, and located in the R-3 Single-Family Residential
District provided in Chapter 17.16 of the Winnetka Village Code, Title 17 of
the Winnetka Village Code: a variation from the average front setback
requirement of Section 17.30.050.C to allow a new single family residence to be
constructed with a front yard setback of 40 feet, rather than the required
average front setback of 53.65 feet, resulting in a variation of 13.65 feet
(25.44%), all in accordance with the plans and elevations submitted with the
application for variation.
   SECTION 3:   The variation hereby granted is conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4:   This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
ADOPTED this 7th day of September, 2004, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
AYES:    Trustees Abell, Greenough, Eilers, Ritchell and Tucker
NAYS:    None
ABSENT:    Trustee Webster
APPROVED this 7th day of September, 2004.
   Signed:
   //s//Michael F. Duhl
   Village President
Countersigned:
//s//Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: August 10, 2004
Posted: August 11, 2004
Passed and Approved: September 7, 2004
Posted: September 10, 2004
ORDINANCE NO. M-24-2004
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A SPECIAL USE PERMIT TO ALLOW A HEALTH CLUB FACILITY AT
723 ELM STREET
   WHEREAS, on March 10, 2004, the owner and a tenant of the following
described real estate (the “Subject Property”):
   Lot 16 (except the east 40 feet thereof) in McGuire and Orr’s Arbor Vitae
Road Subdivision of Block 4 and that part of Block 5 lying east of the east
line of Lincoln Avenue in Winnetka, a subdivision of the Northeast ¼ of Section
20 and the North Fractional ½ of Section 21, Township 42 North, Range 13, East
of the Third Principal Meridian, in the Village of Winnetka, Cook County,
Illinois,
commonly known as 723 Elm Street, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the C-
2 General Retail Commercial Overlay Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.44
of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, filed
an application for a special use permit to allow a women’s health club facility
to be located in the basement of the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, pursuant to Section 17.44.010.B.2.e of the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, health clubs are permitted as
special uses in the C-2 overlay zoning district, subject to the conditions and
requirements set forth in Chapter 17.44 and further subject to the conditions
and requirements pertaining to special uses set forth in Chapter 17.56 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance; and
   WHEREAS, on June 14, 2004, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals opened a public hearing on the requested special use; and,
   WHEREAS, the Zoning Board of Appeals hearing continued on July 12, 2004,
and, by the unanimous vote of the six members then present, has reported to the
Council recommending that the special use permit be granted; and
   WHEREAS, on July 28, 2004, on due notice thereof, the Plan Commission
considered the requested special use and, by the unanimous vote of the eight
members then present, found the proposed special use to be consistent with the
Comprehensive Plan, Winnetka 2020, and has reported to the Council recommending
that the special use be granted, although it expressed concern about the
potential impact of the proposed use on parking; and
   WHEREAS, the record of proceedings before the Zoning Board of Appeals and
Plan Commission consists of: (a) the application for special use; (b) a survey
of the Subject Property; (c) Sidwell and Sanborn maps depicting the location of
the subject Property; (d) a “Floor/Ceiling Plan” of the portion of the Subject
Property that is the subject of the special use; (e) a June 4, 2004, parking
study prepared by Kenig, Lindgren, O’Hara, Aboona Inc., (“KLOA”); (f) a June
21, 2004, memo and attachment from KLOA, correcting and clarifying the earlier
study report; and a July 8, 2004, e-mail from the Village Engineer to the
Director of Community Development commenting on the KLOA parking study; and
   WHEREAS, the special use application proposes to construct an exercise
studio, rest rooms and related office space in the basement of the Subject
Property, for use by the lessee, “Curves for Women – Winnetka,” a franchisee of
Curves International, Inc., which provides exercise facilities exclusively for
women’s fitness; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed use will also include the retail sale of nutritional
supplements; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed use is intended for local users, as there are other
Curves locations in Highland Park, Northfield and Wilmette; and
   WHEREAS, at the hearing before the Zoning Board of Appeals, the Board heard
the testimony of one of the three partners of the applicant/franchisee, Pamela
Amatore, a registered nurse with a master’s degree who co-owns the franchise
with her son, a high school wrestling coach, and her daughter, a physical
trainer; and
   WHEREAS, the proceedings before the Zoning Board of Appeals included
questioning of the applicant and the Director of Community Development by
members of the Zoning Board of Appeals; and
   WHEREAS, the application indicates that the franchisee had contacted
neighboring businesses, who expressed concerns about noise but otherwise
supported the proposed use; and
   WHEREAS, none of the owners of the properties located within 250 feet of the
Subject Property submitted any evidence or requested an opportunity to cross-
examine witnesses at either the Zoning Board of Appeals hearing or at the Plan
Commission proceedings; and
   WHEREAS, a tenant of the property at 715 Elm appeared at the Zoning Board of
Appeals hearing and expressed concerns regarding noise and parking; and
   WHEREAS, the proceedings of the Zoning Board of Appeals and Plan Commission
conformed with all requirements of their procedural rules, the Winnetka Village
Code and applicable statutes of the State of Illinois; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property is located in the Elm Street Business District
on the north side of Elm Street between Lincoln Avenue and Arbor Vitae Road;
and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property is improved with a two-story building that
currently houses two women’s clothing stores on the first floor and offices on
the second floor and has unimproved space in the basement; and
   WHEREAS, the initial hours of operation for the proposed special use are
from 9:00 a.m. to 11:30 a.m. and 3:30 p.m. to 6:00 p.m., Monday through Friday,
with 5:00 set as the closing time on Fridays, and with the facility being
closed Saturday and Sunday and
   WHEREAS, the applicant projects that the eventual expansion of hours as the
club’s membership grows, with the morning hours to be lengthened by opening at
6:00 a.m. on Monday, Wednesday and Friday and at 8:30 a.m. on Tuesday and
Thursday, and with the afternoon and evening hours extended to 8:00 p.m. Monday
through Thursday and to 6:00 p.m. on Friday; and
   WHEREAS, the record establishes that, because the peak periods of operation
of the proposed use do not occur during peak retail shopping hours, and because
the facility will be closed entirely during the mid-day hours and on weekend,
the parking supply for the proposed use is adequate to accommodate its needs;
and
   WHEREAS, the proposed special use will neither endanger nor be detrimental
to the public health, safety, comfort, morals or general welfare, in that the
proposed use is compatible with the immediate neighborhood, and will provide a
small, state-of-the-art health club facility in the Elm Street Business
district; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed special use will neither substantially diminish nor
impair property values in the immediate vicinity, in that it will have a
minimal impact on the immediate neighborhood, and may draw new patrons to the
commercial businesses located along Elm Street, which will be beneficial to the
immediate vicinity; and
   WHEREAS, adequate measures have been taken to provide ingress and egress in
a manner that minimizes pedestrian and vehicular traffic congestion in the
public ways, in that: (a) because the facility will not have timed classes, the
proposed use will have periodic traffic throughout the day without having the
traffic and parking concentrations that would accompany a class schedule; (b)
the facility will be closed each week day over the mid-day lunch hours to the
benefit of other local businesses; and (c) the facility will be closed entirely
on weekends; and
   WHEREAS, adequate parking, utilities, access roads, drainage and other
facilities necessary for the operation of the special use already exist; and
   WHEREAS, because the proposed special use will not change the appearance of
the immediate vicinity, it is consistent with the Winnetka 2020 objective to
“ensure that Commercial, institutional, and residential development is
appropriate to the character of and minimizes the adverse impact on its
surrounding neighborhood;” and
   WHEREAS, because of its minimal intensity and the pre-existing
infrastructure, the proposed special use is consistent with the Winnetka 2020
objectives to: (a) “limit commercial, institutional and residential development
within the Village to minimize potentially adverse impacts on adjacent
residential neighborhoods and to prevent the need for significant increases in
infrastructure and other community resources,” and (b) “ensure that development
proposals minimize the potential adverse impact they might have on residential
neighborhoods, including the impact on pedestrian character, on site parking,
traffic patterns, congestion, open space, storm water management and Village
infrastructure;” and
   WHEREAS, the proposed special use is consistent with the Winnetka 2020 goal
to “provide for a wide range of office/service and retail commercial land uses
and development within the existing business districts in the Corridor;” and
   WHEREAS, because it adds a use that is complementary to the commercial
retail operations in the immediate vicinity, without using street level
commercial space, the proposed special use is consistent with the Winnetka 2020
goal to maintain the essential quality, viability and attractiveness of
Winnetka’s business districts while encouraging new economic development
consistent with the character of the Village and the individual business
districts.”
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1:   The foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the findings
of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth herein.
   SECTION 2:   Pursuant to Section 17.44.010.B.2.e and Section 17.56.010 of
the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, and subject to the conditions set forth in
Section 3 of this ordinance, a special use is hereby granted to the Subject
Property, commonly known as 723 Elm Street, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in
the C-2 General Retail Commercial Overlay Zoning District, to allow for a
women’s health club facility to operate on the Subject Property, all in
accordance with the plans submitted with the application.
   SECTION 3:   This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 7th day of September, 2004, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
   AYES: Trustees Abell, Eilers, Greenough, Ritchell and Tucker
   NAYS: None
   ABSENT: Trustee Webster
   APPROVED this 7th day of September, 2004.
   Signed:
   //s//Michael F. Duhl
   Village President
Countersigned:
//s//Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: August 17, 2004
Posted: August 18, 2004
Passed and Approved: September 7, 2004
