ORDINANCE NO. M-25-2004
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A SPECIAL USE AND VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE
ZONING ORDINANCE OF THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (Bluhm’s
Resubdivision of 701-703 Sheridan Road)
   WHEREAS, the property commonly known as 701 - 703 Sheridan Road, Winnetka,
Illinois, and located in the R-2 Single Family Residential Zoning District
provided in Chapter 17.24 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the
Winnetka Village Code (the “Subject Property”) is legally described in Exhibit
A, which is attached to and made of part of this Ordinance; and
   WHEREAS, pursuant to Section 17.04.030 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance,
Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, Parcels 1, 2 and 3 constitute a single
zoning lot, and are commonly known, collectively, as 701 Sheridan Road in the
Village of Winnetka; and
   WHEREAS, pursuant to Section 17.04.030 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance,
Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, Parcel 4 constitutes a separate zoning
lot and is commonly known as 703 Sheridan Road in the Village of Winnetka; and
   WHEREAS, the two separate zoning lots that form the Subject Property are the
subject of an application to consolidate the two zoning lots into a single,
platted lot of record; and
   WHEREAS, on October 5, 2004, the Council of the Village of Winnetka adopted
Resolution R-28-2004, which granted preliminary approval of the proposed plat
of consolidation, which is captioned “Bluhm’s Resubdivision;” and
   WHEREAS, Resolution R-28-2004 sets out the terms and conditions that must be
met for final approval of the proposed Bluhm’s Resubdivision; and
   WHEREAS, upon the approval and recording of the final plat of subdivision,
the Subject Property will be known as “Lot 1 of Bluhm’s Resubdivision, being a
Subdivision of Part of the Southwest Fractional ¼ of Section 16, Township 42
North, Range 13, East of the Third Principal Meridian, in Cook County,
Illinois;” and
   WHEREAS, the owner of the Subject Property proposes to redevelop the Subject
Property by replacing the two existing single family residences with one new,
single family residence to be constructed on that part of the Subject Property
that is now commonly known as 701 Sheridan Road; and
   WHEREAS, the owner of the Subject Property currently resides in the home on
that part of the Subject Property that is now commonly known as 703 Sheridan
Road, and proposes to remain in that home until construction of the new home is
completed; and
   WHEREAS, the owner of the Subject Property has submitted an application for
a use variation from the Zoning Ordinance's restrictions that limit the Subject
Property to a single principal use, said variation being for the purpose of
allowing the existing house to remain on the Subject Property while the new
single family residence is constructed; and
   WHEREAS, on July 12, 2004, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variation and, by the
unanimous vote of the six members then present, has reported to the Council
recommending that the variation be granted; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and unique circumstances
associated with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance
with respect to the Subject Property in that: (a) the Subject Property is
formed by the consolidation of two existing zoning lots, each of which is
improved with a single family residence; (b) a new home will be constructed in
that part of the Subject Property that is currently commonly known as 701
Sheridan Road; and (c) retaining the existing residence, which is on that part
of the Subject Property that is currently commonly known as 703 Sheridan Road,
will reduce the visual impact of the construction area by maintaining the
existing appearance of the Subject Property at the Sheridan Road street
frontage; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood or impair an adequate supply of light and air, because the
requested variation is of a temporary nature, the impact of the existing house
is already established and retaining the existing residence will reduce the
visual impact of the construction area by maintaining the existing appearance
of the Subject Property at the Sheridan Road street frontage; and
   WHEREAS, when implemented pursuant to this Ordinance and the conditions it
imposes, the requested variation will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property because it will comply with all
applicable building and fire protection codes and access for emergency vehicles
will be enhanced; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings throughout the Village, because of the temporary nature of the
variation; and
   WHEREAS, when implemented pursuant to this Ordinance and the conditions it
imposes, the requested variation will not unduly contribute to congestion on
the public streets, because the impact of the construction on the public
streets will be substantially similar to the impacts of other residential
construction sites in the immediate vicinity; and
   WHEREAS, when implemented pursuant to this Ordinance and the conditions it
imposes, the requested variation will not otherwise impair the public health,
safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the inhabitants of the Village.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1:   The foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the findings
of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth herein.
   SECTION 2:   Subject to the conditions set forth in Section 3 of this
Ordinance, a use variation is hereby granted to the Subject Property, which is
commonly known as 701-703 Sheridan Road, Winnetka, Illinois, which is the
subject of the Bluhm’s Resubdivision that was preliminarily approved on October
5, 2004, by Resolution R-28-2004, and which is located in the R-2 Single Family
Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.24 of the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, said variation being from the
Zoning Ordinance's restrictions limiting the Subject Property to a single
principal use, for the purpose of allowing the existing house to remain on the
Subject Property while a new single family residence is constructed in the
design and at the location depicted in the plans and elevations submitted with
the application for variation.
   SECTION 3: The variation hereby granted is subject to the following
conditions:
      A.   The Owner shall not be entitled to exercise any of the rights
granted by this Ordinance unless and until the final subdivision plat for
Bluhm’s Resubdivision has been approved and recorded, and no building permit
for the new house shall be issued unless and until said final subdivision plat
has been approved and recorded.
      B.   The proposed construction of the new house shall commence within 12
months after the effective date of this Ordinance.
      C.   The variation granted by this ordinance shall terminate, and the
existing home on that part of the property known as 703 Sheridan Road shall be
removed, within 45 days after the occurrence of the earliest of any of the
following events:
         1.   The issuance of a temporary certificate of occupancy for the new
principal residence.
         2.   The expiration of the building permit for the new principal
residence.
         3.   The relocation of the owner and his family to another residence.
         4.   The failure of the owner to remedy any of the following
circumstances within 48 hours after the Village issues written notice of such
circumstance or, in the case of an oral notice, within 48 hours after written
confirmation of the oral notice thereof:
            a.   a violation of the construction access restrictions of this
Ordinance;
            b.   a violation of any of the terms of the building permit or any
ordinances of the Village of Winnetka pertaining to the construction on the
Subject Property; and
            c.   the creation of a hazardous obstruction of the private road
used by the owner and his employees, contractors and agents to access Subject
Property, whether (i) by preventing the owners of the other two properties
served by the private access road from having access to and from their
respective properties, or (ii) by preventing the free and immediate access of
emergency vehicles to the Subject Property or to the other properties served by
the private access road.
      D.   The Village shall at all times retain the sole and exclusive right
to enforce its ordinances and all other applicable laws at the Subject
Property, including but not limited to Titles 12 and 15 of the Winnetka Village
Code, and nothing in this Ordinance shall be construed as granting any
exemptions from said ordinances and laws, or as granting any additional rights
to the owner, other than the variation granted herein.
      E.   No final certificate of occupancy shall be issued for the new house
unless and until the existing house is demolished and site restoration has been
completed. For purposes of this Ordinance, site restoration shall include the
removal of all debris, restoration of the grade of the property, and the
installation of the permanent driveway from the west lot line at Sheridan Road
(the “Primary Access”).
      F.   The owner shall provide the Village with an irrevocable letter of
credit to secure the demolition of the existing house and site restoration as
specified in the foregoing paragraph E. Said letter of credit shall be issued
by a bank located in the Village of Winnetka, shall be in a form acceptable to
the Village Attorney, and shall be in an amount that the Director of Community
Development determines is sufficient to cover 125% of the estimated costs of
demolition and site restoration as specified in this Ordinance. Except as
provided in the following paragraphs G and H, the letter of credit shall not be
reduced or released unless and until the existing house has been demolished and
the Director of Community Development determines that all site restoration
specified in the foregoing paragraph E has been satisfactorily completed.
      G.   If the site restoration, as defined in paragraph E of this Section
3, cannot be completed in its entirety, the Director of Community Development
shall be authorized to reduce the amount of the letter of credit, provided that
all of the following conditions are met:
         1.   the only part of the site restoration that remains to be
completed shall be the completion of the permanent Primary Access;
         2.   the Director of Community Development shall have determined that
an event or condition described in paragraph I of this Section 3 prevents the
timely completion of the permanent Primary Access;
         3.   the owner has constructed and agrees to maintain a temporary
driveway to provide the required Primary Access; and
         4.   in no event shall the balance of the letter of credit be less
than 125% of the amount that the Director of Community Development determines
is sufficient to cover the costs of completing the installation of the
permanent driveway that will provide the Primary Access to the Subject
Property.
      H.   If the home on that part of the Subject Property commonly known as
703 Sheridan Road is demolished before a temporary certificate of occupancy is
issued for the new home, and before the owner has moved any personal belongings
other than construction materials into the new home, the Director of Community
Development shall be authorized to release the letter of credit, provided that
all of the following conditions are met:
         1.   the only part of the site restoration that remains to be
completed shall be the completion of the permanent Primary Access;
         2.   the owner shall maintain a temporary driveway to provide the
required Primary Access; and
         3.   the owner shall not be entitled to any certificate of occupancy,
whether temporary or permanent, until such time as the Director of Community
Development, in the exercise of his discretion in the ordinary course of
business, determines that the site restoration specified in paragraph E of this
Section 3 has been completed and that the owner has met all other applicable
requirements for a certificate of occupancy.
      I.   The Director of Community Development shall be authorized to extend
the 45-day period for removing the house in the event that weather conditions
or such acts beyond the reasonable control of the owner as strikes, acts of God
and acts of war, prohibit its timely demolition, provided that the final
Certificate of Occupancy shall not be issued until all of the demolition and
site restoration conditions have been met and provided that the letter of
credit required by this Ordinance shall remain in full force until the final
Certificate of Occupancy is issued.
      J.   All construction materials and construction equipment shall be
stored on the Subject Property.
      K.   Except as provided in this paragraph K, all access to the
construction site on the Subject Property shall be directly from Sheridan Road
via that part of the Subject Property formerly known as 703 Sheridan Road.
Notwithstanding the foregoing, vehicles that meet all of the following
conditions may access the Subject Property from the private road and 8-foot
driveway that originally served the 701 Sheridan portion of the Subject
Property, provided the Owner has not been notified by the Village either that
the use of the access road by such vehicles has created a hazardous obstruction
or that the use of the access road has resulted in a violation of a provision
of the building permit or the Village Code that pertains to construction
access:
         1.   the vehicles must be an automobile, SUV, pick-up truck or van of
similar size, as evidenced by displaying either an automobile license plate or
an Illinois “B” truck license plate; and
         2.   the vehicle must have no more than two axles; and
         3.   the vehicle must not be pulling a trailer, another vehicle, or
any equipment of any kind.
The access provisions of this paragraph K shall remain in full force and effect
until a final certificate of occupancy has been issued, notwithstanding the
demolition of the house on that part of the Subject Property now commonly known
as 703 Sheridan Road or the reduction or release of the letter of credit
required by paragraph E of this Section 3.
      L.   In the event that the construction of the new house on that part of
the Subject Property now commonly known as 701 Sheridan Road does not proceed,
the Owner shall not be required to demolish the house on that part of the
Subject Property now commonly known as 703 Sheridan Road, provided that the
house on that part of the Subject Property now commonly known as 701 Sheridan
Road shall be demolished and the Subject Property shall be restored.
   SECTION 4:   This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
ADOPTED this 19th day of October, 2004, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
AYES:    Trustees Abell, Eilers, Greenough, Ritchell, Tucker and Webster
NAYS:    None
ABSENT:    None
APPROVED this 19th day of October, 2004.
   Signed:
   //s//Michael F. Duhl
   Village President
Countersigned:
//s//Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: September 7, 2004
Posted: September 8, 2004
Passed and Approved: October 19, 2004
Posted: October 20, 2004
