ORDINANCE NO. M-4-2005
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (879 Cherry)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
Lot 33 in Block 8 in Provident Mutual Land Association Subdivision of Blocks 7
to 12 inclusive, 28 to 33 inclusive and 54 to 59 inclusive, in Village of
Winnetka, being a subdivision in the West ½ of the Northeast ¼ of Section 20,
Township 42 North, Range 13, East of the Third Principal Meridian, in Cook
County, Illinois.
commonly known as 879 and 879½ Cherry Street, Winnetka, Illinois, and located
in the R-5 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.12
of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for a
variation of 5.5 feet (68.7%) from the side yard setback requirements of
Section 17.30.060 of the Lot, Space, Bulk and Yard Regulations for Single-
Family Residential Districts established by Section 17.30 of the Zoning
Ordinance, to allow a proposed 20-foot by 20-foot detached garage to be located
with its east wall 3.0 feet from the east lot line, and with its roof overhang
2.5 feet from the east lot line, although a minimum side yard setback of 8.0
feet is required; and
   WHEREAS, on March 14, 2005, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variations and, by the
unanimous vote of the six members then present, has reported to the Council
recommending that the requested variation be granted; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and particular hardships
associated with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance
with respect to the Subject Property in that: (a) the Subject Property is an
interior lot with a legally nonconforming lot width of 50 feet and a lot area
of 9,350 square feet, which exceeds the minimum lot size requirements for the
R-5 Zoning District; (b) the Subject Property is improved with a single family
residence that was constructed in 1907 and has a street address of 879 Cherry
Street, and with a two-story coach house that is located in the northwest
corner of the Subject Property and has a street address of 879½ Cherry Street;
(c) there is currently no garage on the property and the presence of the coach
house precludes construction of a garage in the rear portion of the Subject
Property, where it would have a side yard setback requirement of 2 feet; (d)
because a garage cannot be built in the rear quarter of the Subject Property,
constructing a garage in full conformity with the Zoning Ordinance would
require an east setback of at least 8 feet; and (e) constructing a garage with
a conforming side yard setback would make it difficult to access the western
portion of the garage, would make it more difficult to turn a vehicle around
and would place the garage closer to the center of the Subject Property,
rendering a larger part of the back yard unusable; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that
the Subject Property does not currently have a garage and a two-car garage is a
standard amenity for homes within the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because the proposed construction is compatible with the
character of existing development within the immediate neighborhood; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not impair an adequate supply of light
and air because the proposed new garage conforms to height and door width
requirements; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property, as the proposed construction will comply
with all applicable building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvement; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed construction will not contribute to congestion on the
public streets, as the property will continue to be used for single family
residential purposes; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the requested variation will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation is in harmony with the general purpose and
intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that it allows the addition of a
detached garage while maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the
community, preserving the coach house dwelling unit and protecting established
trees and landscaping.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1:   The foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the findings
of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth herein.
   SECTION 2:   The Subject Property, commonly known as 879 and 879½ Cherry
Street, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-5 Single-Family Residential
Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.12 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance,
Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, is hereby granted a variation of 5.5
feet (68.7%) from the side yard setback requirements of Section 17.30.060 of
the Lot, Space, Bulk and Yard Regulations for Single-Family Residential
Districts established by Section 17.30 of the Zoning Ordinance, to allow a
proposed 20-foot by 20-foot detached garage to be located with its east wall
3.0 feet from the east lot line, and with its roof overhang 2.5 feet from the
east lot line, although a minimum side yard setback of 8.0 feet is required,
said garage to be constructed in accordance with the plans and elevations
submitted with the application for variation.
   SECTION 3:   The variation hereby granted is conditioned upon (a) the
removal of the existing storage shed and (b) the commencement of the proposed
construction within 12 months after the effective date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4:   This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
ADOPTED this 19th day of April, 2005, pursuant to the following roll call vote:
AYES:    Trustees Abell, Eilers, Greenough, Ritchell, Tucker and Woodbury
NAYS:    None
ABSENT:    None
APPROVED this 19th day of April, 2005.
   Signed:
   //s//Michael F. Duhl
   Village President
Countersigned:
//s//Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: April 5, 2005
Posted: April 6, 2005
Passed and Approved: April 19, 2005
Posted: April 20, 2005
ORDINANCE NO. M-5-2005
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (644 - 646 Pine Lane)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
Lots 1 and 2 in the Harold Nutting Subdivision of part of the South ½ of the
Southwest ¼ of Section 17, Township 42 North, Range 13, east of the Third
Principal Meridian, in Cook County, Illinois.
commonly known as 644 and 646 Pine Lane, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the
R-2 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.24 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for the
following variations from requirements of the Lot, Space, Bulk and Yard
Regulations for Single-Family Residential Districts established by Section
17.30 of the Zoning Ordinance: (a) a variation of 4.99 feet (41.58%) from the
side yard setback requirements of Section 17.30.060 of the Zoning Ordinance to
allow a proposed screen porch to be located 7.01 feet from the north lot line,
whereas a minimum side yard setback of 12 feet is required; (b) a variation
from the limitations on changes to nonconforming structures under Section and
17.64.040 to allow the construction of the screen porch on the east side of the
coach house and to allow the construction of a one-story addition to the south
end of the coach house; and (c) a variation from the limitations on permitted
uses in Section 17.24.020, to allow the continuation of two principal buildings
on a single zoning lot; and
   WHEREAS, on April 11, 2005, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variations and, by the
unanimous vote of the 6 members then present, has reported to the Council
recommending that the variations requested be granted; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and particular hardships
associated with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance
with respect to the Subject Property in that: (a) the Subject Property is
improved with a single family residence that was constructed in 1922 and has a
street address of 644 Pine Lane; (b) the one-story coach house that is the
subject of the variation request was constructed on the Subject Property 7 or 8
years after the principal residence; (c) although coach houses are ordinarily
considered an accessory, the subject coach house is a single-family residence
with its own street address of 646 Pine Lane, and is therefore considered an
additional principal building under the Zoning Ordinance, subject to zoning
requirements for principal buildings; (d) the coach house is a legal
nonconforming structure that encroaches in the north side yard of the Subject
Property so that the proposed addition to the structure requires a variance;
(e) the coach house was previously used as rental property but with the
completion of the proposed additions, it will be used to accommodate the
owners' in-laws; and (f) the Subject Property has a lot area of 75,645 square
feet, which is more than three times the minimum lot size in the R-2 zoning
district, and the proposed expansion of the coach house will have a minimal
effect on the open space on the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that
(a) the proposed screen porch is a minimal expansion of the nonconforming
structure and is set further from the north lot line than the body of the coach
house; (b) the proposed addition is consistent with the established use of the
coach house as a single-family residence; and (c) without a zoning variation,
no change could be made to improve the coach house, which is a long-established
use on the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because the proposed additions will be in keeping with the
architecture of the primary residence on the Subject Property and the proposed
expansion of the coach house will have a minimal effect on the open space on
the Subject Property because the Subject Property has a lot area of 75,645
square feet, which is more than three times the minimum lot size in the R-
2 zoning district; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not impair an adequate supply of
light and air because (a) the proposed improvements, while still nonconforming,
will increase the side yard setback by 5.48 feet from 1.53 feet to 7.01 feet,
(b) the building addition is in a conforming locations and (c) the Subject
Property will remain well within the maximum allowable roofed lot coverage and
gross floor area limits for the R-2 Zoning District; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property, as the proposed construction will comply
with all applicable building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not diminish the taxable value of
land and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed construction will not contribute to congestion on the
public streets, as the property will continue to be used for single family
residential purposes; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the requested variations will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations are in harmony with the general purpose
and intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that they allow the renovation,
restoration and rehabilitation of a structurally sound existing building while
maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the community and protecting
established trees and landscaping.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1:   The foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the findings
of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth herein.
   SECTION 2:   The following variations from the Lot, Space, Bulk and Yard
Regulations for Single-Family Residential Districts established by Section
17.30 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance are hereby granted to the Subject
Property, commonly known as 644 and 646 Pine Lane, and located in the R-
2 Single-Family Residential District provided in Chapter 17.24 of the Winnetka
Village Code, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code: (a) a variation of 4.99
feet (41.58%) from the side yard setback requirements of Section 17.30.060 of
the Zoning Ordinance to allow a proposed screen porch to be located 7.01 feet
from the north lot line, whereas a minimum side yard setback of 12 feet is
required; (b) a variation from the limitations on changes to nonconforming
structures under Section and 17.64.040 to allow the construction of the screen
porch on the east side of the coach house and to allow the construction of a
one-story addition to the south end of the coach house; and (c) a variation
from the limitations on permitted uses in Section 17.24.020, to allow the
continuation of two principal buildings on a single zoning lot in accordance
with the plans and elevations submitted with the application for variations.
   SECTION 3:   The variations hereby granted are conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4:   This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
