ORDINANCE NO. M-6-2005
AN ORDINANCE AMENDING THE AFFORDABLE HOUSING PLAN FOR THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA
PURSUANT TO ITS HOME RULE AUTHORITY AS PROVIDED IN THE CONSTITUTION OF THE
STATE OF ILLINOIS
   WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka, Cook County, Illinois (the
"Village Council"), find that the Village of Winnetka, Cook County, Illinois
(the “Village”) is a home rule municipal corporation as provided in Article
VII, Section 6 of the 1970 Constitution of the State of Illinois, pursuant to a
referendum held at the Consolidated Election of April 5, 2005; and
   WHEREAS, the Village Council further find that, pursuant to said
constitutional home rule authority, the Village of Winnetka may exercise any
power and perform any function pertaining to its government and affairs for the
protection of the public health, safety, morals and welfare of the Village of
Winnetka and its citizens; and
   WHEREAS, the Village Council further find that the availability of
affordable housing in the Village, and the adoption of plans, ordinances,
regulations and other legislative and administrative actions to encourage and
otherwise provide for the development of affordable housing within the Village
are matters pertaining to the government and affairs of the Village of Winnetka
and its citizens that are therefore within the scope of the Village's
constitutional home rule authority; and
   WHEREAS, the Village Council further find that it is the longstanding policy
of the Village to encourage the development of affordable housing options so
that persons with moderate incomes, particularly senior citizens and young
families, can enjoy the benefits of living in the Village and so that Village
residents can enjoy the benefits of a community with housing diversity, and
that the Village has long attempted to implement said policy as opportunities
have occurred, while attempting to maintain a balance between the worthy
objectives of that housing policy and such other important Village policies as
the policy of maintaining the Village’s single-family home character and the
policy of assuring that land use development and redevelopment are compatible
with the capacity of existing infrastructure; and
   WHEREAS, the Village Council further find that the Village's policy
regarding the development of moderately priced housing was first articulated in
the 1979 Statement of Community Objectives, which was an element of the
Village's Comprehensive Plan, which contained a statement acknowledging the
importance "of the community's recognition of the need to facilitate housing
for those who contribute to [the community's] well being – public employees,
storekeepers and retirees;" and
   WHEREAS, the Village Council further find that the housing policies in the
1979 Statement of Community Objectives included the policy of encouraging and
assisting in the provision of moderate cost residences in the Village,
especially for those who already live or work in the Village, and the policy of
facilitating the provision of housing for senior citizens who live in the
Village but no longer need or can no longer afford single-family residences;
and
   WHEREAS, the Village Council further find that, in furtherance of the
policies stated in the 1979 Statement of Community Objectives, the housing
chapter of the Winnetka Comprehensive Plan included a discussion of the need to
provide alternative housing for older, long-term residents of the Village who
no longer wish to live in and maintain their single-family homes; and
   WHEREAS, the Village Council further find that the most recent statement of
the Village's housing policies is contained in the Winnetka Comprehensive Plan
of 1999, Winnetka 2020, which was formally adopted by the Winnetka Village
Council on November 16, 1999, pursuant to Ordinance M-592-99 and which serves
as the principal policy document for guiding future land development within the
Village's planning area and for making informed resource management decisions
as development occurs; and
   WHEREAS, the Village Council further find that, consistent with the
Village's prior policy statements, Winnetka 2020 identifies a community need
for more housing options for senior citizens and young families, recognizes not
only that multi-family residential buildings provide homes for older residents
and those with modest incomes, but also that rental units provide an important
element of diversity in housing options, and includes as one of the stated
goals of Winnetka 2020 the goal of ensuring that multi-family development
provides a variety of housing choices for residents of all ages; and
   WHEREAS, the Village Council further find that Winnetka 2020 also observes
that multi-family options in the Village are limited and that older couples
often find few housing alternatives in the Village at a time in their lives
when they seek more simplified living arrangements; and
   WHEREAS, the Village Council further find that the State of Illinois has
enacted the Affordable Housing, Planning and Appeal Act, Public Act 93-595,
effective January 1, 2004, as amended by Public Act 93-678, and as now or
hereafter amended (collectively, the “Act”), which provides for the designation
of particular municipalities in the State as either “exempt” or “non-exempt,”
based on the amount of housing in those municipalities determined under the Act
to be “affordable,” requires non-exempt municipalities to develop and submit to
the State on or before April 1, 2005 an “affordable housing plan,” and provides
that, beginning January 1, 2009, developers who have been denied land use
approvals by a “non-exempt” municipality may appeal such a decision to a “State
Housing Appeals Board,” in order to seek relief from the decision; and
   WHEREAS, the Village Council further find that the Illinois Housing
Development Authority has determined that the Village is a “non-exempt”
municipality under the Act; and
   WHEREAS, the Village Council further find that, on March 15, 2005, prior to
the April 5, 2005, home rule referendum pursuant to which the Village has
become a home rule municipality, the Village Council adopted resolution R-15-
2005, which adopted the Village of Winnetka Affordable Housing Plan; and
   WHEREAS, the Village Council further find that, prior to the Village's
becoming a home rule municipality pursuant to the referendum held at the
Consolidated Election on April 5, 2005, the Village's ability to implement its
policy of encouraging or otherwise providing for the development of moderately
priced housing in a manner that would serve the particular needs of the Village
was limited by the absence of adequate powers in the Village Charter of 1869
and the Illinois Municipal Code; and
   WHEREAS, the Village Council further find that the Act does not preempt home
rule authority and that there is considerable uncertainty over provisions of
the Act governing local land use decisions and possible loss of local control
over those decisions, and that the public interest of the Village and its
citizens would best be served by the Village's addressing its affordable
housing needs by exercising its home rule powers and by the Village's not
ceding its final authority over local land use decisions to a State board or
commission; and
   WHEREAS, the Village Council further find that, pursuant to the Illinois
Constitution, the Village now has broader powers as a home rule municipality to
develop and implement an affordable housing policy that better addresses the
Village's specific needs, and that the Village Council have therefore
determined that the Village of Winnetka Affordable Housing Plan (the "Initial
Plan") should be amended, as set forth in the Village of Winnetka Amended
Affordable Housing Plan (the "Amended Plan"), a copy of which is attached
hereto as Exhibit A and is incorporated by reference as if set forth fully
herein, and which the Village Council find addresses the subject of affordable
housing in the Village of Winnetka in a manner most beneficial to the Village,
any further or different requirements of the Act notwithstanding; and
   WHEREAS, the Village Council further find that adoption of the Amended Plan
will be in the Village’s best interest; and
   WHEREAS, the Village Council further find that the Village also intends that
all decisions about Winnetka’s housing needs and future development will
continue to be made at the local level and not subject to appeal to a State
board or commission; and
   WHEREAS, the Village Council further find that the Winnetka Plan Commission,
which conducted a two-year study of land use issues in the Village before
drafting Winnetka 2020, and which has the primary responsibility for reviewing
and reporting on the implementation of the goals and objectives of Winnetka
2020, is best suited to work with the Community Development Department to
continue the Village's analysis of its affordable housing needs, to monitor and
report to the Village Council on the implementation of the Village's Amended
Plan, and to make findings and recommendations to the Village Council
concerning the details of the various components of the Village's affordable
housing policies, including (i) affordability standards, (ii) which of the
broader array of land use and development tools and incentives available to the
Village as a home rule unit are best suited to the Village's particular needs,
goals and objectives, and (iii) potential amendments to the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance and other provisions of the Winnetka Village Code, so as to increase
the opportunities to successfully implement the Village's affordable housing
policies; and
   WHEREAS, the Village Council further find that, based on all the foregoing,
the Village intends this Ordinance and the adoption of the Amended Plan
provided for herein to constitute part of the Village's total, comprehensive
and exclusive regulation of the subject matter of affordable housing in the
exercise of the Village’s home rule authority, notwithstanding any provision of
the Act with which the Village’s Amended Plan may differ, and that this
Ordinance and the Amended Plan provided for herein shall supersede and replace
the Initial Plan and shall totally displace and preempt the Act and all its
provisions to the maximum extent permitted under the Village’s constitutional
home rule authority; and
   WHEREAS, the Village Council further find that, while it is the intent of
the Village to submit its Amended Plan to the State within 60 days after the
March 15, 2005, adoption of the Village's Initial Plan, in order to demonstrate
the Village’s good faith commitment to the goals of the Act and to demonstrate
the Village's compliance with the Act prior to the Village's becoming a home
rule municipality, such filing shall also give notice to the State of the
Village’s assertion of its home rule authority and shall not act as a
concession that the Village as a home rule municipality is required to comply
with the Act, but rather shall be done in furtherance of the Village’s
longstanding affordable housing policy described above and as an exercise of
the Village’s home rule authority to completely regulate this subject and
completely displace the Act, as now or hereafter amended, thereby serving both
to further the Village's policy supporting affordable housing and to assure
that the Village's decisions regarding the use and development of land located
within its corporate limits are controlled at the local level by the Village,
that such decisions are in furtherance of the particular characteristics and
needs of the community, and in conformity with all of the Village's zoning,
land use, building and development regulations, that such decisions address the
particular characteristics and needs of the community;
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1:   The foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the findings
of the Council of the Village of Winnetka as if fully set forth herein.
   SECTION 2:   The Village, through adoption of this Ordinance and the Amended
Affordable Housing Plan ("Amended Plan") attached hereto as Exhibit A and
incorporated by reference as if set forth in full herein, in combination with
the Village’s Zoning Ordinance and the provisions of the Village Code covering
subdivision of land, building and construction and other matters relating to
land use, shall collectively be the sole and exclusive laws regulating the
development of affordable housing in the Village of Winnetka.
   SECTION 3:   The Amended Plan approved and established by the passage of
this Ordinance shall supersede the Affordable Housing Plan of the Village of
Winnetka that was approved and established by the adoption of Village of
Winnetka Resolution R-15-2005 on March 15, 2005 (the "Initial Plan").
   SECTION 4:   This Ordinance and the Amended Plan shall constitute an
exercise of the home rule authority of the Village of Winnetka on the subject
matter hereof to the maximum extent permitted under the Constitution and laws
of the State of Illinois, and shall totally preempt and displace each and all
of the provisions of the above described Affordable Housing Planning and Appeal
Act, as now or hereafter amended. Without limiting the generality of the
foregoing, pursuant to this home rule Ordinance and the Amended Plan, no land
use decision of the Village of Winnetka shall be subject to appeal to a State
Housing Appeals Board described in the Act, as now or hereafter amended.
   SECTION 5:   Pursuant to the provisions of the Amended Plan approved and
established by this Ordinance, the Plan Commission of the Village of Winnetka
is hereby authorized and directed to study and develop recommendations
regarding (a) housing and demographic characteristics of the Village of
Winnetka, (b) affordable housing standards that address the Village's
particular characteristics and needs, (c) affordable housing goals that address
the Village's particular characteristics and needs, (d) techniques and
incentives to encourage and facilitate the development of affordable housing
units in the Village of Winnetka that will address the Village's particular
characteristics and needs, and (e) related amendments to the Comprehensive
Plan, the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, and other provisions of the Winnetka
Village Code, all as set forth in greater detail in the Amended Plan.
   SECTION 6:   The Plan Commission shall report back to the Village Council by
December 1, 2005, with a status report and proposed timetable for completing
the study described in the foregoing Section 5.
   SECTION 7:   The Village Clerk is hereby authorized and directed to file
with the Illinois Housing Development Authority a certified copy of this
Ordinance and the Amended Plan approved herein, in lieu of filing the Initial
Plan with the Illinois Housing Development Authority, as originally directed in
Resolution R-15-2005. The Amended Plan shall be filed with the Illinois Housing
Development Authority on or before May 14, 2005, said date being 60 days after
the adoption of the Initial Plan on March 15, 2005.
   SECTION 8:   This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
ADOPTED this 10th day of May, 2005, pursuant to the following roll call vote:
AYES:    Trustees Abell, Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell and Tucker
NAYS:    None
ABSENT:    None
APPROVED this 10th day of May, 2005.
