ORDINANCE NO. M-11-2005
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (746-748 Green Bay Road)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
Lots 41, 42 and 43 in County Clerks Division of that part of the Southeast
fractional ¼ of Section 17, Township 42 North, Range 13, east of the Third
Principal Meridian lying West of the railroad, in Cook County, Illinois.
commonly known as 746-748 Green Bay Road, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in
the B-1 Multi-Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.32 of
the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for the
following variations from the provisions of said Chapter 17.32 and of Chapter
17.64, "Nonconformities," of the Zoning Ordinance: (a) a variation from the
limitations on changes to nonconforming uses and structures under Section
17.64.040, to allow the construction of a paved parking lot and turnaround in
the area along the south side of the Subject Property, which is currently
occupied by three green houses and a frame garage, and the construction of an
outdoor sales area and gravel pathways at the rear of the Subject Property,
which is currently occupied by three greenhouses; (b) a variation of 2,257.44
square feet (12.81%) from the intensity of use of lot limitations of Section
17.30.030 to permit the paving of the new parking lot and turnaround area where
there now exists a gravel parking area; (c) a variation from the general front
setback requirements of Section 17.32.010.H to permit the paved parking area to
observe an east front setback of two feet, whereas a minimum setback of 25 feet
is required, a variation of 23 feet (92%); (d) a variation from the general
side yard setback requirements of Section 17.32.010.I, to allow the proposed
parking lot to observe a south side yard setback of two feet, whereas a minimum
of 12 feet is required, a variation of 10 feet (83.33%); and (e) variations
from the parking lot setback requirements of Section 17.32.010.P, which
requires parking lots to be located at least five feet from any lot line, a
variation of 3 feet (60%) along the east and south lot lines; and
   WHEREAS, on May 9, 2005, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of Appeals
conducted a public hearing on the requested variations and, by the unanimous
vote of all 7 of its members, has reported to the Council recommending that the
requested variations be granted; and
   WHEREAS, on April 21, 2005, the Design Review Board considered the
appearance of the proposed work and has issued favorable comment to the Village
Council, and also recommended: (a) that the applicant remove the first parking
space, adjacent to Green Bay Road, and provide landscaping in that area
instead; and (b) that the applicant add some trees and improved materials in
the adjacent parkway; and
   WHEREAS, the applicant has modified its original application at the Zoning
Board hearing by withdrawing a request to construct an 8-foot high fence; and
   WHEREAS, the applicant further modified its application in response to the
Design Review Board's recommendation by replacing the first parking space with
landscape materials and by providing for the addition of two new parkway trees,
all as indicated on the revised site plan dated April 25, 2005; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and unique circumstances
associated with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance
with respect to the Subject Property in that: (a) the Subject Property was
originally developed in 1916, before the enactment of the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance; (b) the Subject Property has been the site of Victor Hlavacek
Florist, a family-owned business in operation since 1948, and the business is
an established fixture in both the Village and the neighborhood in which the
business is located; (c) both the use and accessory buildings are considered to
be legal nonconformities; (d) the current gravel parking area is nonconforming
and substandard, and presents a safety hazard for cars trying to exit onto
Green Bay Road; and (e) there is no comparable, suitable alternative location
in the Village for the applicant's business, which generates sales tax revenues
for the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that
(a) the current parking lot is too small and does not provide safe ingress to
and egress from the Subject Property; (b) the nonconformities preclude any
improvements to the Subject Property without the granting of variations, unless
the applicant removed both the business and the nonconforming structures from
the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because (a) the proposed improvements will improve the
appearance of the Subject Property without changing the existing use of the
Subject Property, which is well-established use in the neighborhood; and (b)
the proposed improvements, as modified by the revised site plan dated April 25,
2005, will improve the streetscape by adding parkway trees in front of the
Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not impair an adequate supply of
light and air because the proposed improvements will include the removal of six
small greenhouses and one frame garage, thereby increasing the open space
between the principal building and the properties to the east and south, as
well as reducing the total roofed lot coverage by 4,600.87 square feet; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property, as the proposed construction will comply
with all applicable building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not diminish the taxable value of
land and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed construction will not contribute to congestion on the
public streets, as the proposed improvements will increase the amount of on-
site parking and will provide an area for vehicles to turn around before
exiting the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed improvements will include bringing the parking area
into full compliance with the Village, including the installation of storm
water drainage, which will mitigate the impact of the increased impermeable
surface; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the requested variations will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations are in harmony with the general purpose
and intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that the proposed improvements:
(a) protect the scale and character of the existing neighborhood from the
encroachment of excessive building bulk; (b) increase open space; (c) lessen
the hazards of storm water run-off; and (d) reduce congestion and promote
safety on the public streets by increasing the amount of off-street parking and
improving the circulation of vehicle traffic on the Subject Property.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1:   The foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the findings
of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth herein.
   SECTION 2:   The following variations from the provisions of said Chapter
17.32 "B-1 Multi-Family Residential Zoning District" and of Chapter 17.64
"Nonconformities" of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka
Village Code, are hereby granted to the Subject Property, commonly known as
746-748 Green Bay Road, and located in the B-1 Multi-Family Residential
District provided in said Chapter 17.32 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance:: (a)
a variation from the limitations on changes to nonconforming uses and
structures under Section 17.64.040, to allow the construction of a paved
parking lot and turnaround in the area along the south side of the Subject
Property, which is currently occupied by three green houses and a frame garage,
and the construction of an outdoor sales area and gravel pathways at the rear
of the Subject Property, which is currently occupied by three greenhouses; (b)
a variation of 2,257.44 square feet (12.81%) from the intensity of use of lot
limitations of Section 17.30.030 to permit the paving of the new parking lot
and turnaround area where there now exists a gravel parking area; (c) a
variation from the general front setback requirements of Section 17.32.010.H to
permit the paved parking area to observe an east front setback of two feet,
whereas a minimum setback of 25 feet is required, a variation of 23 feet (92%);
(d) a variation from the general side yard setback requirements of Section
17.32.010.I, to allow the proposed parking lot to observe a south side yard
setback of two feet, whereas a minimum of 12 feet is required, a variation of
10 feet (83.33%); and (e) variations from the parking lot setback requirements
of Section 17.32.010.P, which requires parking lots to be located at least five
feet from any lot line, a variation of 3 feet (60%) along the east and south
lot lines; all in accordance with the plans and elevations submitted with the
application for variations, as modified by the revised site plan dated April
25, 2005.
   SECTION 3:   The variations granted by this Ordinance are subject to the
following conditions:
      A.   The proposed construction shall commence within 12 months after the
effective date of this Ordinance.
      B.   All exterior lighting, signage and landscaping shall be
substantially in the form submitted with the application for variations, as
amended by the revised site plan dated April 25, 2005, and shall be subject to
review and approval by the Design Review Board, provided that the Village
Council reserves the right and authority to reverse or modify the final
decision of the Design Review Board in the event the applicant and Design
Review Board are unable to reach an agreement on any aspect of the exterior
lighting and signage.
   SECTION 4:   This Ordinance is adopted by the Council of the Village of
Winnetka in the exercise of its home rule powers pursuant to Section 6 of
Article VII of the Illinois Constitution of 1970.
   SECTION 5:   This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon its passage,
approval and posting as provided by law.
ADOPTED this 5th day of July, 2005, pursuant to the following roll call vote:
AYES:    Trustees Abell, Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell and Tucker
NAYS:    None
ABSENT:    None
APPROVED this 5th day of July, 2005.
   Signed:
   //s//Edmund C. Woodbury
   Village President
