ORDINANCE NO. M-12-2005
AN ORDINANCE ESTABLISHING 455 BIRCH STREET AS A DESIGNATED LANDMARK
   WHEREAS, the owner of the property commonly known as 455 Birch Street,
Winnetka, Illinois (the "Subject Property"), which is legally described as
follows:
Lots 9, 10 and 11 in Carlton Park Subdivision of Block 34 (except the South 80
feet of the Northeast ¼ and except the North 80 feet of the Southeast ¼) in
Winnetka, a subdivision of the Northeast ¼ of Section 20 and the fractional
North ½ of fractional Section 21, Township 42 North, Range 12 East of the Third
Principal Meridian, in the Village of Winnetka, Cook County, Illinois,
Permanent Real Estate Index Number (PIN) of 05-20-216-013
has submitted an application seeking designation of the Subject Property as a
certified Village of Winnetka landmark; and;
   WHEREAS, pursuant to Article VII, Section 6 of the Constitution of the State
of Illinois of 1970, the Village of Winnetka, a home rule municipality, has the
authority, except as limited by said Section 6 of Article VII, to exercise any
power and perform any function pertaining to the government and affairs of the
Village, including, but not limited to, the powers to regulate for the
protection of the public health, safety, morals and welfare; and
   WHEREAS, the identification, designation and preservation of buildings and
structures in the Village that are historically, culturally, and
architecturally significant, and the encouragement of the restoration and
rehabilitation of those buildings and structures, is a matter of the government
and affairs of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, Chapter 15.64 of Title 15 of the Winnetka Village Code, “Landmark
Preservation,” establishes the standards and procedures for the preservation,
protection, enhancement, rehabilitation and regulation of buildings,
structures, objects, and places of historical, cultural or architectural
importance; and
   WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka have determined that it is
in the best interests of the Village and its residents that participation in
the landmark designation process shall be limited to those owners who
voluntarily elect to participate in that process; and
   WHEREAS, pursuant to the provisions of Chapter 15.64 of the Winnetka Village
Code (the “Landmark Ordinance”) and notice duly published and sent, the
Landmark Preservation Commission conducted a public hearing on October 4, 2004,
to consider the owners' application for landmark designation of the Subject
Property; and
   WHEREAS, the Landmark Preservation Commission applied the Village’s System
for the Evaluation of Landmarks to the information received at the hearing and
made findings regarding the Subject Property, including the following:
      (1)   The architectural type is extremely rare, in that: (a) the Lola
Maverick Lloyd house was constructed in 1920 in a style that transcends three
decades of architectural style: Arts & Crafts, Art Deco, and WPA; (b) the
residence’s simple features, low roofline, exposed rafter tails and hand-
crafted ornamentation are all reflective of the Arts & Crafts style; (c) the
vertical board and batten siding, window boxes, clipped gables and strong
coloration suggest the influence of its Swedish architect;
      (2)   The method of construction is extremely rare, in that: (a) the
builder of the residence, Charles Haag, was dedicated to hand craftsmanship, as
evidenced by the clipped gables, the unusual windows, board-and-batten siding
and the homes’ colors, which are Swedish in origin; (b) the roof line is broken
by numerous dormers, clad in vertical board-and-batten siding painted brick red
with clipped gable ends; (c) the lower exterior is stuccoed and painted a
strong golden yellow with aqua trim and barn red siding above; (d) heavy shaped
braces support the open gabled front porch roof; (e) there are two square bays
projecting from the long north wall; (f) the windows are of many shapes and
styles and placed irregularly according to the interior plan, and include a
triple window on the west, three small windows on the second floor of the south
façade which are decorated with a long window box, and a window on the west
elevation that is ornamented with carved doves;
      (3)   The Subject Property is associated with a person of historical
significance as well as a master builder, in that it was designed and built by
Charles Haag, a Swedish sculptor who trained in Paris and later emigrated to
the U.S., and whose sculptures are still on display through the state;
      (4)   The Subject Property is a symbol of the neighborhood and a
conspicuous and familiar structure in the context of the entire Village,
because it is situated on the corner of Birch and Cherry Streets in a native
hardwood grove which contains a remarkable variety of trees and native
wildflowers, making this prominent setting and unusual house a familiar visual
landmark for the Village; and
      (5)   The Subject Property received the highest rating for originality
and design integrity because the home has been left virtually unchanged.
   WHEREAS, based on its findings, the Landmark Preservation Commission has
concluded that the residence located at 455 Birch is unique and meets the
criteria of the Landmark Ordinance and the Commission members present at the
October 4, 2004, meeting have unanimously recommended that it be so designated;
and
   WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka have considered the
recommendation of the Landmark Preservation Commission and have determined that
it is in the best interest of the Village and its residents to establish the
house located at 455 Birch as a designated, certified landmark.
   NOW THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain:
   SECTION 1:   That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2:   That the property located at 455 Birch Street, permanent real
estate index number 05-20-216-013, is hereby designated a certified landmark
under the Landmark Ordinance, in accordance with the findings and
recommendations of the Landmark Preservation Commission.
   SECTION 3:   This Ordinance is adopted by the Council of the Village of
Winnetka in the exercise of its home rule powers pursuant to Section 6 of
Article VII of the Illinois Constitution of 1970.
   SECTION 4:   This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon its passage,
approval and posting as provided by law.
PASSED this 19th day of July, 2005, pursuant to the following roll call vote:
