ORDINANCE NO. M-15-2005
AN ORDINANCE AUTHORIZING THE SALE OR OTHER DISPOSITION OF SURPLUS ELECTRICAL
TRANSFORMERS OWNED BY THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA
   WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka ("Village Council") have
determined that certain electrical transformers and equipment that are owned by
the Village of Winnetka and that have been removed from service in the
Village’s electric distribution system are no longer necessary or useful to the
Village of Winnetka (the "Surplus Personal Property"); and
   WHEREAS, the Surplus Personal Property is listed in Exhibit A, which is
attached to this ordinance and incorporated herein by reference; and
   WHEREAS, the Water and Electric Department has reported to the Village
Council that from time to time during the 2005-2006 fiscal year certain other
transformers will also be retired from service and will no longer be necessary
or useful to the Village of Winnetka (the “Additional Retired Transformers”);
and
   WHEREAS, the Village Council has been requested to authorize the Water and
Electric Department to dispose of the Surplus Personal Property and Additional
retired Transformers; and
   WHEREAS, the Village of Winnetka is a home rule municipality in accordance
with Article VII, Section 6 of the Constitution of the State of Illinois of
1970; and
   WHEREAS, pursuant to Article VII, Section 6 of the Constitution of the State
of Illinois of 1970, the Village of Winnetka has the authority, except as
limited by said Section 6 of Article VII, to exercise any power and perform any
function pertaining to the government and affairs of the Village, including,
but not limited to, the powers to regulate for the protection of the public
health, safety, morals and welfare; and
   WHEREAS, the Village Council find that the disposal of surplus property
owned by the Village, such as the Surplus Personal Property and Additional
Retired Transformers described in this ordinance is a matter pertaining to the
affairs of the Village and that the manner of disposal of such property
pertains to the public health, safety and general welfare of the Village and
its citizens; and
   WHEREAS, the Village Council have determined that disposal of the Surplus
Personal Property and Additional Retired Transformers as provided in this
ordinance is necessary and proper so as to avoid incurring unnecessary
additional costs and unnecessary exposure to liability related to storing or
disposing of the Surplus Personal Property and Additional Retired Transformers;
and
   WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka, in the exercise of its home
rule powers pursuant to Section 6 of Article VII of the Illinois Constitution
of 1970, have determined that it is in the best interests of the Village and
its citizens to dispose of the Surplus Personal Property and Additional Retired
Transformers in a manner consistent with the provisions of Section 11-76-4 of
the Illinois Municipal Code (65 ILCS 5/11-76-4), as more fully set forth in
this Ordinance.
   NOW THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1:   The foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the findings
of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth herein.
   SECTION 2:   The Council of the Village of Winnetka find: (a) that the
electric transformers and equipment listed in the attached Exhibit A (“Surplus
Personal Property”) are no longer necessary or useful to the Village of
Winnetka; (b) that any electrical transformers that are retired or replaced
during the 2005-2006 fiscal year and that the Director of Water and Electric,
with the approval of the Village Manager, determines cannot reasonably be
reused in the Village's electric distribution system (“Additional Retired
Transformers”) will no longer be necessary or useful to the Village; and (c)
that the best interests of the Village of Winnetka will be served by the sale
or other disposition of the Surplus Personal Property and the Additional
Retired Transformers as hereinafter provided.
   SECTION 3:   The Council of the Village of Winnetka further find that, based
on prior experience in disposing of similar items of property, the cost of
conducting a public sale of such items of property, including the costs of
advertising and publishing, and the costs of personnel needed to maintain
security and to conduct the public sale exceeds the value of such items.
   SECTION 4:   The Village Manager is hereby authorized and directed to
determine the value and dispose of the Personal Property and the Additional
Retired Transformers in the manner provided in Sections 5 and 6 of this
Ordinance.:
   SECTION 5:    The Village Manager, in the exercise of his discretion, may
dispose of transformers that contain no PCBs in one of the following ways:
      A.   by sale to the highest bidder, with or without advertising; or
      B.   in furtherance of the goals of intergovernmental cooperation set
forth in Article VII, Section 10 of the Illinois Constitution of 1970, and the
Intergovernmental Cooperation Act, 5 ILCS 220/1, et seq., by transferring title
to any Illinois municipal electric utility, with or without competitive
bidding; provided, that (1) competitive bids may be obtained with or without
advertising, and (2) the terms and conditions of any transfer of title without
competitive bidding shall be established by the Village Manager on a case by
case basis, after considering such factors as the technical needs and financial
capabilities of such municipal electric utility and the estimated value of the
transformers.
   SECTION 6:   All transformers that contain PCBs or that are PCB contaminated
shall be disposed of at the lowest cost to the Village, said cost being
determined with or without advertising; provided, that any person or entity
that disposes of or destroys any part of such transformers shall provide a
sworn statement to the Village certifying that such disposal or destruction
complies with all applicable environmental laws and regulations.
   SECTION 7:   This Ordinance is adopted by the Council of the Village of
Winnetka in the exercise of its home rule powers pursuant to Section 6 of
Article VII of the Illinois Constitution of 1970.
   SECTION 8:   This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon its passage,
approval and posting as provided by law.
PASSED this 16th day of August, 2005, pursuant to the following roll call vote:
