ORDINANCE NO. M-16-2005
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (550 Sheridan)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
That part of Block 17 in Winnetka, Illinois described as follows: beginning at
the intersection of the centerline of Sheridan Road with the centerline of Elm
Street; thence Westerly along center of Elm Street, a distance of 258.18 feet;
thence Northerly on a line parallel to centerline of Walnut Street, a distance
of 190.0 feet; thence Easterly along a line of Sheridan Road, thence
Southeasterly along centerline of Sheridan Road to place of beginning in the
Northwest ¼ of Section 21, Township 42 North, Range 13, East of the Third
Principal Meridian (except the West 63.0 feet) in the Village of Winnetka, Cook
County, Illinois.
commonly known as 550 Sheridan Road, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
4 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.16 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for the
following variations from requirements of the Lot, Space, Bulk and Yard
Regulations for Single-Family Residential Districts established by Section
17.30 of the Zoning Ordinance: (a) a variation of 10 feet (33.33%) from the 30-
foot front setback required by Section 17.30.050 to allow a south front setback
of 20 feet; (b) a variation of 7.32 feet (41.23%) from the 17.73-foot corner
setback required by Section 17.30.050 to allow an east setback of 10.42 feet;
(c) a variation from the building line articulation requirements of Section
17.30.090 to permit an unarticulated west building wall of 40 feet, whereas a
maximum permitted unarticulated length is 35 feet; and (d) a variation from the
garage door width limitations of Section 17.30.110 to allow a 14-foot wide
garage door on the north side of the attached garage, facing Sheridan Road,
which exceeds the maximum allowable garage door width of 9 feet, resulting in a
variation of 5 feet (55.55%); all of said variations being requested to allow
the existing single-family residence on the Subject Property to be improved by
constructing a new attached garage, constructing two one-story additions
between the house and garage to add a mud room, expand the existing great room,
raising the roof ridge beam 2 feet and adding three new dormers to the attic;
and
   WHEREAS, on July 11, 2005, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variations; and
   WHEREAS, at the July 11, 2005 hearing, the applicant withdrew his request
for the garage door variation, and the Zoning Board of Appeals, by the
unanimous vote of all seven members, has reported to the Council recommending
that the remaining variation requests be granted; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and particular hardships
associated with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance
with respect to the Subject Property in that: (a) the Subject Property is an
irregularly shaped lot located at the northwest corner of Elm Street and
Sheridan Road; (b) the Subject Property does not have a rear yard because,
although the west and south lot lines of the Subject Property meet at right
angles, the remaining lot line, which is formed by Sheridan Road, is the
hypotenuse of the triangular lot; (c) the Subject Property also has a legally
nonconforming lot area of 8,439.75 square feet, which is considerably smaller
than the 13,300 square foot minimum corner lot size required in the R-4 Single-
Family Residential Zoning District (d) the Subject Property is subject to a
corner setback of 17.73 feet from Sheridan Road; (e) application of setback
requirements to the Subject property results in a relatively small, triangular
shaped buildable area; (f) although the rectangular house is situated so that
its length runs parallel to the west lot line, the south end of the home and
the northeast corner of the attached garage are in nonconforming locations; and
(g) although the west mud room will provide 18 inches of articulation for the
length of that proposed addition, it is not possible to fully comply with the
articulation requirement without shifting the garage farther east, into the
nonconforming corner setback; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that:
(a) the existing garage is in a deteriorated condition, including a cracked
concrete slab and needs to be replaced; (b) the west side yard has a legal
nonconformity of approximately one-half foot; (c) it is not possible for the
garage to be constructed in a conforming location without either moving the
garage to the west, where the setback is already nonconforming, or moving the
garage to the south, which would require eliminating the existing great room,
would preclude adding the mud room, would be considered and expansion of the
nonconforming side yard setback, and would increase the nonconformity in
building articulation by creating a longer uninterrupted west building line;
(d) due to the nonconforming location of the west building line and the south
portion of the house, the proposed increase in building height, the existing
west dormer and the proposed south dormer all expand on the existing
nonconformities; (e) the proposed mud room is a standard amenity in homes in
the vicinity; (f) the proposed mudroom provides as much articulation as
possible without forcing the garage to shift farther east into the corner
setback; (g) the mud room addition to the expansion of the great room are
modest one-story additions to standard features in single family residences in
the vicinity, and could not be constructed in full conformance with the Zoning
Ordinance; (h) the attic is currently unusable, as it does not provide the
minimum required clearance at the top of the stairs; (i) the expanded height of
the attic will create usable space and improve fire safety conditions within
applicable height limitations; and (j) the additional setback nonconformity
resulting from the 2-foot height increase is minimal; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because the proposed additions will maintain the character of
the home while improving its exterior condition and maintaining its established
orientation toward the existing streets; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not impair an adequate supply of
light and air because the proposed improvements do not exceed height and gross
floor area limits; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property, as the proposed construction will comply
with all applicable building and fire protection codes and the increased height
in the attic space will bring that space into compliance with minimum height
standards for occupied space; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not diminish the taxable value of
land and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed construction will not contribute to congestion on the
public streets, as the property will continue to be used for single family
residential purposes; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the requested variations will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations are in harmony with the general purpose
and intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that they allow the renovation,
restoration and rehabilitation of a structurally sound existing building while
maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the community and protecting
established trees and landscaping.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1:   The foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the findings
of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth herein.
   SECTION 2:   The following variations from the Lot, Space, Bulk and Yard
Regulations for Single-Family Residential Districts established by Section
17.30 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code,
are hereby granted to the Subject Property, commonly known as 550 Sheridan Road
and located in the R-4 Single-Family Residential District provided in Chapter
17.16 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance: (a) a variation of 10 feet (33.33%)
from the 30-foot front setback required by Section 17.30.050 to allow a south
front setback of 20 feet; (b) a variation of 7.32 feet (41.23%) from the 17.73-
foot corner setback required by Section 17.30.050 to allow an east setback of
10.42 feet; and (c) a variation from the building line articulation
requirements of Section 17.30.090 to permit an unarticulated west building wall
of 40 feet, whereas a maximum permitted unarticulated length is 35 feet; all of
said variations being requested to allow the existing single-family residence
on the Subject Property to be improved by constructing a new attached garage,
constructing two one-story additions between the house and garage to add a mud
room, expand the existing great room, raising the roof ridge beam 2 feet and
adding three new dormers to the attic, all in accordance with the plans and
elevations submitted with the application for variations, subject to said plans
and elevations being modified to bring the garage doors into conformity with
the Zoning Ordinance.
   SECTION 3:   The variations hereby granted are conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4:   This Ordinance is adopted by the Council of the Village of
Winnetka in the exercise of its home rule powers pursuant to Section 6 of
Article VII of the Illinois Constitution of 1970.
   SECTION 5:   This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
PASSED this 16th day of August, 2005, pursuant to the following roll call vote:
AYES:    Trustees Abell, Behles, Berger, Ritchell
NAYS:    None
ABSENT: Trusetes Eilers, Tucker
APPROVED this 16th day of August, 2005.
   Signed:
   //s//Edmund C. Woodbury
   Village President
Countersigned:
//s//Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: August 2, 2005
Posted: August 3, 2005
Passed and Approved: August 16, 2005
Posted: August 17, 2005
ORDINANCE NO. M-17-2005
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (652 Lincoln)
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owners of the following described
real estate (the “Subject Property”):
That part of Lot 9 described as follows: beginning at a point at the Southwest
corner of said Lot 9 and running from there in a Northerly direction along the
Westerly line of said lot to a point 40.00 feet Southerly from the Northwest
corner of said lot (as measured along the Westerly line of said lot) thence
Easterly a distance of 10.00 feet along the line which if extended in a
straight line would intercept the Easterly line of Lot 9, 50.00 feet Southerly
from the Northeast corner of said Lot 9 (as measured along the Easterly line of
said Lot 9) thence in a Southerly direction along a line 10.00 feet Easterly
from and parallel with the Westerly line of said Lot 9 to Southerly line of
said Lot 9 an thence Westerly along the Southerly line of said Lot 9 to the
place of beginning in Webster’s Resubdivision of Lots 1 to 5 inclusive in Block
13 in Park Addition to Winnetka, also that part of Lot 9 described as follows:
commencing at a point at the Northeasterly corner of said Lot 9 and running
from there 50.00 feet in a Southeasterly direction along the Westerly line of a
Lincoln Avenue from there in a Southwesterly direction to a point on the
Southwesterly line of said Lot 9. 40.00 feet Southeasterly from the
Northwesterly corner of said lot, from there in a Northwesterly direction along
the Southwesterly line of the lot to its Northwest Corner and from there in a
Northeasterly direction along the Northwesterly line of said Lot 9 to the place
of beginning in Webster’s Resubdivision of Lots 1 to 5 inclusive in Block 13 in
Park Addition to Winnetka, all in the East ½ of the Southeast ¼ of Section 17,
Township 42, Range, 13, in Cook County, Illinois,
commonly known as 652 Lincoln Avenue, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
4 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.16 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for the
following variations from requirements of the Lot, Space, Bulk and Yard
Regulations for Single-Family Residential Districts established by Section
17.30 of the Zoning Ordinance: (a) a variation from the intensity of use of lot
requirements of Section 17.30.030 to permit a roofed lot coverage of 2,240.87
square feet, whereas a maximum of 1,732.62 square feet is allowed, a variation
of 508.25 square feet (29.33%); (b) a variation from the side yard setback
requirements of Section 17.30.060 to allow a north side yard setback of 4.75
feet, whereas a minimum setback of 6 feet is required, a variation of 1.25 feet
(20.83%); and (c) a variation from the building line articulation requirement
of Section 17.30.090 to allow the extended, 45.13-foot long north building wall
to be articulated by 0.95 feet, rather than the 1.5-foot articulation that is
required to begin within 35 feet of the front of the building; all to allow the
construction of a one-story kitchen and powder room addition and a slightly
larger deck at the rear of the existing residence; and
   WHEREAS, on July 11, 2005, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variations and, by the
unanimous vote of all 7 of its members, has reported to the Council
recommending that the variations requested be granted; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and particular hardships
associated with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance
with respect to the Subject Property in that: (a) the Subject Property is an
irregularly shaped lot that has east and west lot lines meeting the north lot
line at right angles, but a non-parallel south lot line, as well as a driveway
appendage measuring approximately 12.69 feet by 14.50 feet that extends from
the south property line to Pine Street at the southwest corner of the Subject
Property; (b) the Subject Property has a legally nonconforming lot with a lot
area of 6,930.49 square feet, which is substantially smaller than the 12,600
square foot minimum lot size required in the R-4 Single-Family Residential
Zoning District; (b) the Subject Property has a legally nonconforming north
side yard setback, because the single-family residence on the Subject Property
was built 2.55 feet from the north lot line in 1921, prior to the enactment of
the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance; (c) the existing residence has eaves that are 32
to 33 inches deep, accounting for 60 feet of roofed lot coverage; (d) it is not
possible to comply with the articulation requirement because the existing jog
in the nonconforming north wall is less than the required 1.5-foot depth, and
further articulation for the addition would disrupt the alignment of the
kitchen walls and shift the proposed eating area so that it conflicts with the
rear entrance; (e) the proposed addition and rear deck will have a north side
yard setback of 4.75 feet, which is more than 2 feet greater than the existing
side yard setback; and (f) because the nonconformities relate to the existing
house's placement on the lot, its deep eaves and its interior configuration,
they cannot be cured without removing substantial portions of the house or
building an entirely new house; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that:
(a) the variations are necessary to allow a 162.62 square foot addition that
will remove a narrow and steep interior basement stairway, add a conforming
basement stairway in the central traffic area of the house, add a first floor
powder room between the kitchen and family room, add an eating area adjacent to
the kitchen, and improve interior traffic patterns by connecting the kitchen
with the family room and living room; (b) a family room that is connected to
the kitchen, a first floor powder room and a kitchen eating area are all
standard amenities for residences throughout the Village; (c) it is not
possible to comply with the articulation requirement because the existing jog
in the nonconforming north wall is less than the required 1.5-foot depth, and
further articulation for the addition would disrupt the alignment of the
kitchen walls and shift the proposed eating area so that it conflicts with the
rear entrance; (e) because the nonconformities relate to the existing house's
placement on the lot, its deep eaves and its interior configuration, they
cannot be cured without removing substantial portions of the house or building
an entirely new house; and (f) although the proposed new deck will be slightly
larger than the existing deck, it will have a modest area of approximately
73.30 square feet; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because the proposed addition fills in an interior corner at
the rear of the house and will maintain the established appearance of the
Subject Property when viewed from the streets; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not impair an adequate supply of
light and air because the proposed addition: (a) is one-story high, (b) will be
set back further from the north lot line than the existing north wall, and (c)
will remain well within applicable height and gross floor area limitations; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property, as the proposed construction will comply
with all applicable building and fire protection codes, and will improve fire
and life safety within the house by eliminating the nonconforming basement
stairway, adding a conforming basement stairway and providing connections
between all rooms on the first floor; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not diminish the taxable value of
land and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed construction will not contribute to congestion on the
public streets, as the property will continue to be used for single family
residential purposes; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the requested variations will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations are in harmony with the general purpose
and intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that they allow the renovation,
restoration and rehabilitation of a structurally sound existing building while
maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the community and protecting
established trees and landscaping.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1:   The foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the findings
of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth herein.
   SECTION 2:   The following variations from the Lot, Space, Bulk and Yard
Regulations for Single-Family Residential Districts established by Section
17.30 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code,
are hereby granted to the Subject Property, commonly known as 652 Lincoln, and
located in the R-4 Single-Family Residential District provided in Chapter 17.16
of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance: (a) a variation from the intensity of use of
lot requirements of Section 17.30.030 to permit a roofed lot coverage of
2,240.87 square feet, whereas a maximum of 1,732.62 square feet is allowed, a
variation of 508.25 square feet (29.33%); (b) a variation from the side yard
setback requirements of Section 17.30.060 to allow a north side yard setback of
4.75 feet, whereas a minimum setback of 6 feet is required, a variation of 1.25
feet (20.83%); and (c) a variation from the building line articulation
requirement of Section 17.30.090 to allow the extended, 45.13-foot long north
building wall to be articulated by 0.95 feet, rather than the 1.5-foot
articulation that is required to begin within 35 feet of the front of the
building; all to allow the construction of a one-story kitchen and powder room
addition and a slightly larger deck at the rear of the existing residence and
all in accordance with the plans and elevations submitted with the application
for variations.
   SECTION 3:   The variations hereby granted are conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4:   This Ordinance is adopted by the Council of the Village of
Winnetka in the exercise of its home rule powers pursuant to Section 6 of
Article VII of the Illinois Constitution of 1970.
   SECTION 5:   This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
