ORDINANCE NO. M-24-2005
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA, COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (84-90 Warwick Rd. and 442 and
444 Winnetka Ave)
   WHEREAS, the Village of Winnetka is a home rule municipality in accordance
with Article VII, Section 6 of the Constitution of the State of Illinois of
1970; and
   WHEREAS, pursuant to Article VII, Section 6 of the Constitution of the State
of Illinois of 1970, the Village of Winnetka has the authority, except as
limited by said Section 6 of Article VII, to exercise any power and perform any
function pertaining to the government and affairs of the Village, including,
but not limited to, the powers (i) to regulate for the protection of the public
health, safety, morals and welfare, (ii) to license, (iii) to tax, and (iv) to
incur debt; and
   WHEREAS, the Village Council find that establishing standards for the use
and development of lands and buildings within the Village and establishing and
applying criteria for variations from those standards are matters pertaining to
the affairs of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
Lots 1, 2 and 3 in Owner’s Subdivision Of Lots 1, 2, 3, 4, and 5 in Block 3 of
Temple’s Resubdivision of that part of the West ½ of the Northeast ¼ of Section
28, Township 42 North, Range 13 East of the Third Principal Meridian lying East
of the Chicago Milwaukee Electric right-or-way and North of the village limits
of Kenilworth, in Cook County, Illinois, Book of Plats, 98, Page 9, Document
Number 4133672.
commonly known as 84-90 Warwick Rd. and 442 and 444 Winnetka Ave, Winnetka,
Illinois, and located in the B-1 Multifamily Residential Zoning District
provided in Chapter 17.32 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the
Winnetka Village Code, for a variation from the front setback requirements of
Section 17.32.010 (H) to allow a west front setback of 2 feet, whereas a
setback of 24.01 feet is required, resulting in a variation of 22.01 feet
(91.67%), in order to replace the unenclosed parking spaces serving the
Winnetka Warwick Townhomes with a new, six-car detached garage; and
   WHEREAS, on November 14, 2005, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variations and, by a vote
of six members for and one member against the request, has reported to the
Council recommending that the requested variations be granted; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and particular hardships
associated with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance
with respect to the Subject Property in that: (a) the Subject Property is
improved with a six-unit town home development, constructed in 1953, before the
B-1 Multifamily Residential zoning district was created; (b) the Subject
Property has three street frontages, with the east lot line adjoining Warwick
Road, the north lot line adjoining Winnetka Avenue, and the east lot line
adjoining Tempel Court; (c) the townhomes on the Subject Property face the
Warwick Road and Winnetka Avenue street frontages; (d) the Tempel Court
frontage serves as the rear of the Subject Property, with Tempel Court
providing access to an existing, 6-bay, paved parking area at the rear of the
townhomes; (e) Tempel Court is not a through street, but functions as an alley,
providing access to the parking spaces for the town homes and to the parking
area for the Tudor Apartment/office building located on the west side of Tempel
Court along Winnetka Avenue; (f) the proposed garage will be situated in
approximately the same location as an existing paved lot along Temple Court;
and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that:
(a) garage space is a basic amenity of homes in the Village, providing
enclosed, off-street parking and storage; (b) the townhomes do not have
basements, increasing the need for enclosed garage storage; (c) the absence of
garages has a negative impact on the value of the Warwick Townhomes; (d) it is
not possible to construct the proposed garage in a fully conforming location on
the Subject Property; and (e) constructing the proposed garage closer to the
town homes and with less of a nonconformity in the west setback would
negatively impact the value of the Subject Property by eliminating the rear
yards of the townhomes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because: (a) the proposed new parking garage will be located
in the same area as the unenclosed parking area that it will replace; (b) the
proposed garage will maintain the existing configuration of the Subject
Property; (c) the proposed garage will maintain the established uses and
traffic patterns along Tempel Court; and (c) the design of the proposed new
garage will be in keeping with the architectural character of the town home
development; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not impair an adequate supply of light
and air because the proposed new garage will conform to height limitations in
the B-1 Multi-Family residential district and will be lower than the principal
buildings in the immediate vicinity; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property, as the proposed construction will comply
with all applicable building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed new garage; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed new garage will not contribute to congestion on the
public streets, as the property will continue to be used for the same number of
vehicles currently provided for on the paved parking lot; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the requested variation will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation is in harmony with the general purpose and
intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that it allows for the provision of
a basic amenity while maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the
community and protecting established trees and landscaping.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1:   The foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the findings
of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth herein.
   SECTION 2:   The Subject Property, commonly known as 84-90 Warwick Rd. and
442 and 444 Winnetka Ave, and located in the B-1 Multifamily Residential
District provided in Chapter 17.32 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17
of the Winnetka Village Code, is hereby granted a variation from the front
setback requirements of the B-1 Multifamily Residential District regulations
established by Section 17.32.010 (H)of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, to allow
a west front setback of 2 feet, whereas a setback of 24.01 feet is required,
resulting in a variation of 22.01 feet (91.67%), in order to replace the
unenclosed parking area serving the Winnetka Warwick Townhomes with a new, six-
car detached garage in accordance with the plans and elevations submitted with
the application for variation.
   SECTION 3:   The variation hereby granted is conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4:   This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
ADOPTED this 17th day of January, 2006, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
AYES:    Trustees Abell, Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, Tucker
NAYS:    None
ABSENT:    None
APPROVED this 17th day of January, 2006.
   Signed:
   //s//Edmund C. Woodbury
   Village President
Countersigned:
//s//Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: December 20, 2005
Posted: December 21, 2005
Passed and Approved: January 17, 2006
