ORDINANCE NO. M-2-2006
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (382 Fairview)
   WHEREAS, the Village of Winnetka is a home rule municipality in accordance
with Article VII, Section 6 of the Constitution of the State of Illinois of
1970; and
   WHEREAS, pursuant to Article VII, Section 6 of the Constitution of the State
of Illinois of 1970, the Village of Winnetka has the authority, except as
limited by said Section 6 of Article VII, to exercise any power and perform any
function pertaining to the government and affairs of the Village, including,
but not limited to, the powers (i) to regulate for the protection of the public
health, safety, morals and welfare, (ii) to license, (iii) to tax, and (iv) to
incur debt; and
   WHEREAS, the Village Council find that establishing standards for the use
and development of lands and buildings within the Village and establishing and
applying criteria for variations from those standards are matters pertaining to
the affairs of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
Parcel 1) That part of Lot 5 in Graves’ Subdivision in Winnetka, a Subdivision
of that part of fractional North ½ of fractional Section 21, Township 42 North,
Range 13, East of the Third Principal Meridian, formerly know (now vacated) as
Blocks 44, 68, and 69 of the Village of Winnetka and 33.0 feet West of and
adjoining said Blocks 44 and 68 described as follows: commencing at a point in
said Lot 5, 206.85 feet East of the West line of said Lot 5 and 186.0 feet
North of the North line of Willow Road; thence East 78.0 feet to the West line
of Fairview Avenue; thence South 60.0 feet; thence East 78 feet; thence North
to the point of beginning, in Cook County, Illinois
Parcel 2) That part of said Lot 5 described as follows: commencing at a point
in said Lot 5 224.85 feet East of the West line of Said Lot 5 and 186.0 feet
North of the North line of Willow Road; thence North 71.20 feet to the West
line of Fairview Avenue; thence Southeasterly along the West line of Fairview
Avenue to a point 60.0 feet due East of the point of beginning; thence West to
the point of beginning, all in Cook County, Illinois.
commonly known as 382 Fairview Avenue, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the
R-4 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.16 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for the
following variations from requirements of the Lot, Space, Bulk and Yard
Regulations for Single Family Residential Districts established by Section
17.30 of the Zoning Ordinance: (a) a variation from the maximum building size
provisions of Section 17.30.040 to allow a gross floor area of 3,031.7 square
feet, whereas a maximum of 2,735.56 is permitted, a variation of 296.14 square
feet (10.82%); (b) variations from the side yard setback requirements of
Section 17.30.060 to allow a west side yard setback of 5.16 feet, whereas a
minimum of 7.05 feet is required, a variation of 1.89 feet (26.81%), resulting
in a sum of side yard setbacks totaling 15.38 feet, whereas the minimum total
required is 17.62 feet, a variation of 2.24 feet (12.71%); and (c) a variation
from the garage door width limitations of Section 17.30.110 to allow a garage
door width of 16 feet, whereas a maximum of 9 feet is permitted, a variation of
7 feet (77.78%), all of said variations being to allow the existing attached
garage to be converted to a family room and to allow the construction of a new,
attached two-car garage in a one-story addition ; and
   WHEREAS, the owners initially applied for variations from (a) a variation of
314.76 square feet (11.3%) from the maximum building size limitations, to allow
a gross floor area of 3,050.32 square feet; (b) a variation of 2.39 feet
(33.9%) from side yard setback requirements to allow a west side yard setback
of 4.66 feet; (c) a variation of 2.74 feet (15.55%) from the required minimum
sum of side yard setbacks to allow the combined side yard setbacks to total
14.88 feet; (d) a variation of 0.33 feet (2.27%) from the rear yard setback
requirements to allow a rear yard setback of 14.22 feet; (e) a variation of
295.92 square feet from the limitations on front yard impermeable surfaces to
allow a paved area of 1,087.36 square feet; (f) a variation of 0.14 feet (64%)
from the limitations on front-facing garage doors to allow front facing garage
doors to have a total width of 22.14 feet; and (g) a variation of 7 feet
(77.78%) from the limitations on garage door widths to allow a garage door to
be 16 feet wide; and
   WHEREAS, on October 10, 2005, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variations; and
   WHEREAS, prior to the October 10, 2005, hearing, the owners withdrew the
request for the variation from front yard impermeable surfaces; and
   WHEREAS, following the October 12, 2005, hearing, the Zoning Board of
Appeals forwarded the revised application to the Village Council for comparison
without a favorable recommendation, the applicant having received only 3 votes
in favor of the requested variations; and
   WHEREAS, prior to the time their application was presented to the Village
Council for consideration, the owners further amended their application,
reducing the number and extent of variations to those described above in the
fourth recital of this Ordinance; and
   WHEREAS, at its regular meeting on November 15, 2005, the Village Council
considered the amended application and remanded it to the Zoning Board of
Appeals for further hearing and findings; and
   WHEREAS, on December 12, 2005, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the amended application seeking the
variations described above in the fourth recital of this Ordinance and, by the
unanimous vote of the 5 members then present, has reported to the Council
recommending that the amended request for variations be granted; and
   WHEREAS, at the request of the Village Council, the owners have agreed to
modify the design of the 16-foot wide garage door so that it will appear to be
two individual doors; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and particular hardships
associated with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance
with respect to the Subject Property in that: (a) the Subject Property is
located on the west side of Fairview Avenue on the part of Fairview Avenue that
lies between Ash Street and Willow Road and runs diagonally to the northwest,
generally parallel to Sheridan Road; (b) the Subject Property is an irregularly
shaped lot, with a rectangular southern portion and a triangular northern
portion; (c) the base of the right triangle that forms the triangular northern
portion of the Subject Property is narrower than the rectangular southern
portion, resulting in a jog in the west lot line, with the west side of the
triangular portion being 17.8 feet further to the east than the west side of
the rectangular portion of the Subject Property; (d) the Subject Property is
substandard lot with a legally nonconforming lot area of 6,838.89 square feet;
(e) the Subject Property is improved with a single-family residence that was
constructed in 1922; (d) due to the irregular shape of the Subject Property and
the corresponding, irregular shape of the buildable area of the lot, the single
family residence on the Subject Property is located in the rectangular portion
of the Subject Property and has an interior northwest corner that corresponds
to the interior northwest corner caused by the offset west lot line; (e)
although nearly all of the triangular northern portion of the Subject Property
is open space, nearly all of it is located in the 30-foot front yard setback
along Fairview Avenue; (f) because of the irregular lot shape, the shape and
location of the existing home on the lot, and the interior configuration of the
existing home, there are no fully conforming locations for the proposed
improvements, the only viable locations for such improvements being at the
interior northwest corner of the home and in the Fairview Avenue front setback;
and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that:
(a) the existing first floor of the current residence does not have a family
room or a dining room, which are standard amenities in homes throughout the R-
4 Zoning District; (b) the proposed garage addition at the interior northwest
corner will allow the existing attached garage to be converted to a family room
with a powder room and a mudroom entrance, and will also allow the area
adjacent to the kitchen to be used exclusively as an eating area; (c) it is not
possible to provide a family room and a functioning garage in a conforming
location on the Subject Property unless the family room is placed at the rear
of the house in the shallow rear yard, which would substantially reduce the
size of the only part of the Subject Property suitable for outdoor living
activities; (d) the nonconformities in the size and shape of the Subject
Property cannot be corrected because of the development of the adjacent
properties; and (e) the nonconformities in the house cannot be corrected
without demolishing substantial portions of the house; the
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because (a) the proposed addition will be constructed using
matching exterior materials so that the finished façade will appear as though
it was part of the original home; (b) the design of the single, 16-foot wide
garage door will be modified to make it appear to be two individual doors; (c)
the proposed addition will provide a more uniform front building line; (d) the
proposed addition will preserve the appearance of the existing streetscape by
maintaining the open front setback area along Fairview Avenue; and (e) the
proposed garage addition will be in close proximity to the garages in the rear
yards of the two adjoining properties to the west; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not impair an adequate supply of
light and air because (a) the proposed attached garage will meet the height
limitations of the Zoning Ordinance; and (b) the proposed garage addition will
not interfere with either the residences or open spaces on the adjoining
properties, because the new garage will be located in the interior northwest
corner of the Subject Property, in close proximity to the garages in the two
adjoining properties to the west; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property, as the proposed construction will comply
with all applicable building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not diminish the taxable value of
land and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed construction will not contribute to congestion on the
public streets, as the property will continue to be used for single family
residential purposes; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the requested variations will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, when constructed in accordance with the conditions imposed by this
Ordinance, the requested variations are in harmony with the general purpose and
intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that they allow the renovation,
restoration and rehabilitation of a structurally sound existing building while
maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the community and protecting
established trees and landscaping.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1:   The foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the findings
of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth herein.
   SECTION 2:   Subject to the conditions set forth in Section 3 of this
Ordinance, the following variations from the Lot, Space, Bulk and Yard
Regulations for Single Family Residential Districts established by Section
17.30 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance are hereby granted to the Subject
Property, commonly known as 382 Fairview Avenue, and located in the R-4 Single-
Family Residential District provided in Chapter 17.16 of the Zoning Ordinance,
Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code: (a) a variation from the maximum
building size provisions of Section 17.30.040 to allow a gross floor area of
3,031.7 square feet, whereas a maximum of 2,735.56 is permitted, a variation of
296.14 square feet (10.82%); (b) variations from the side yard setback
requirements of Section 17.30.060 to allow a west side yard setback of 5.16
feet, whereas a minimum of 7.05 feet is required, a variation of 1.89 feet
(26.81%), resulting in the sum of side yard setbacks totaling 15.38 feet,
whereas the minimum total required is 17.62 feet, a variation of 2.24 feet
(12.71%); and (c) a variation from the garage door width limitations of Section
17.30.110 to allow a garage door width of 16 feet, whereas a maximum of 9 feet
is permitted, a variation of 7 feet (77.78%), all of said variations being to
allow the existing attached garage to be converted to a family room and to
allow the construction of a new, attached two-car garage in a one-story
addition all in accordance with the amended plans and elevations submitted with
the revised application for variations, dated December 20, 2005, and as further
amended to comply with the conditions set forth in the following Section 3.
   SECTION 3:   The variations granted herein are subject to the following
conditions:
      A.   The design of the 16-foot wide garage door shall be revised so that,
when constructed, the single door shall appear to be two separate doors. The
Director of Community Development or his designee shall have the sole
authority, in the exercise of his professional judgment, to determine whether
the revised door detail complies with this condition.
      B.   The proposed construction shall commence within 12 months after the
effective date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4:   This Ordinance is passed by the Council of the Village of
Winnetka in the exercise of its home rule powers pursuant to Section 6 of
Article VII of the Illinois Constitution of 1970.
   SECTION 5:   This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon its passage,
approval and posting as provided by law.
PASSED this 7th day of February, 2006, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
AYES:    Trustees Behles, Berger, Ritchell, Tucker
NAYS:    None
ABSENT:    Trustees Abell, Eilers
APPROVED this 7th day of February, 2006.
   Signed:
   //s//Edmund C. Woodbury
   Village President
Countersigned:
//s//Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: January 17, 2006
Posted: January 18, 2006
Passed and Approved: February 7, 2006
Posted: February 9, 2006
ORDINANCE NO. M-3-2006
939 Green Bay Special Use – Park District Warming Hut continued 02/07/06
ORDINANCE NO. M-4-2006
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (194 Fuller)
   WHEREAS, the Village of Winnetka is a home rule municipality in accordance
with Article VII, Section 6 of the Constitution of the State of Illinois of
1970; and
   WHEREAS, pursuant to Article VII, Section 6 of the Constitution of the State
of Illinois of 1970, the Village of Winnetka has the authority, except as
limited by said Section 6 of Article VII, to exercise any power and perform any
function pertaining to the government and affairs of the Village, including,
but not limited to, the powers (i) to regulate for the protection of the public
health, safety, morals and welfare, (ii) to license, (iii) to tax, and (iv) to
incur debt; and
   WHEREAS, the Village Council find that establishing standards for the use
and development of lands and buildings within the Village and establishing and
applying criteria for variations from those standards are matters pertaining to
the affairs of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
Parcel 1: Lot 30 (except that part described as follows: Beginning at the
Southeasterly corner of said lot running thence Northwesterly along the
Easterly line of said lot, 65 feet; thence Southerly along a line parallel with
the Southerly line of said Lot 92.43 feet more or less to the Westerly line of
said lot; thence Southeasterly along the Westerly line of said lot. 65.09 feet,
more or less, to the Southwesterly line of said lot, 95.55 feet, more or less,
to the point of beginning) in Orth’s Subdivision of part of the Fractional
Southeast ¼ of Section 21, Township 42 North, Range 13, East of the Third
Principal Meridian according to the plat thereof recorded September 24, 1921 as
Document Number 7276951, in Book 165 of Plats, Pages 34 and 35, in Cook County,
Illinois,
Parcel 2: That part of the vacated Sunset Road lying Northerly and adjoining
Lot 30 in Orth’s Subdivision, aforesaid described as follows: Beginning at the
Northeasterly corner of said Lot 30, running thence Northwesterly along the
Easterly line extended of said Lot 30 47.66 feet, more or less, to the center
of vacated Sunset Road (said point being distant 20.3 feet from the
Southeasterly corner of Lot 31 in Orth’s Subdivision aforesaid); thence
Westerly along a line making an angle with the Easterly line extended of said
Lot 30 of 86 degrees 45 minutes 30 seconds as measured from the South to West,
distance of 89.86 feet, more or less to the Westerly line extended of said Lot
30; thence Southerly along said Westerly line extended a distance of 22.5 feet,
more or less, to the Northwesterly corner of said Lot 30; thence easterly along
the Northerly line of said Lot 30 to the point of beginning.
Parcel 3: that part of Lot 29 in Remy Park Subdivision of parts of Section 21,
Township 42 North, Range 13, East of the Third Principal Meridian, according to
the Plat thereof Recorded in the Recorder’s office of Cook County, Illinois on
November 18, 1916 as Document Number 5995688 in Book 147 of Plants, page 46,
bounded and described as follows: beginning at the Southwesterly corner of said
Lot 29 and running thence Northwesterly along the Easterly line of said Lot 29
and a distance of 167.25 feet to the Northeasterly line of said Lot 29 a
distance of and a distance of 167.25 feet to the Northeasterly corner of said
Lot 29; running thence Northwesterly on and along the Northeasterly line of
said Lot 29 a distance of 10.98 feet to a point; running thence Southeasterly
in direct line to the place of beginning, (except therefrom that part thereof
lying Southerly of the Southerly line extended Westerly, of Parcel 1,
aforesaid).
Parcel 4: That part of the unsubdivided territory lying between Orth’s
Subdivision aforesaid, and the Remy Park Subdivision, aforesaid, and lying
South of the Northerly line extended of Parcel 2, aforesaid and North of the
Southerly line extended of Parcel 1, aforesaid, all in Cook County, Illinois.
commonly known as 194 Fuller Lane, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
5 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.12 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for a
variation of 480.39 square feet (17.94%) from the maximum building size
limitations of Section 17.30.040 of the Lot, Space, Bulk and Yard Regulations
for Single-Family Residential Districts established by Section 17.30 of the
Zoning Ordinance, to allow an additional 211.92 square feet of gross floor area
so a dormer can be added to the second floor at the rear of the house,
resulting in a gross floor area of 3,158.55 square feet, whereas a maximum of
2,678.16 square feet is permitted; and
   WHEREAS, on December 12, 2005, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variations and the Board
has reported to the Council unanimously recommending that the requested
variation be granted; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and particular hardships
associated with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance
with respect to the Subject Property in that: (a) the Subject Property is a
legally nonconforming substandard lot with a lot area of 6,695.4 square feet;
(b) the Subject Property is located on the west side of Fuller lane and abuts a
large lot located at the east end of Sunset Road; (c) the single family
residence on the Subject Property is a legally nonconforming structure that was
constructed in 1926; (d) the proposed addition is a dormer that will be located
at the rear of the house and will add 211.92 square feet of gross floor area;
(e) the Subject Property slopes down toward the rear of the lot, approximately
from the middle of the residence, resulting in 318 square feet of the home’s
basement area being included in the gross floor area calculations; (f) the
proposed dormer addition will not expand the footprint of the existing
building, will not be visible from the street, and will be set in from the
sides of the existing building, reducing its visibility from the properties to
the north and south; and (g) although the dormer will be visible from the
property to the west, it will merely appear to be a full second story, because
the ridge of the roof is currently approximately 29 feet above the grade at the
rear of the house, the proposed dormer will be lower than the ridge; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that
(a) the existing master bedroom has a small closet and shares a bathroom with
the other two bedrooms on the second floor; (b) the dormer will add a bathroom
and closet space at the rear of the master bedroom, thereby creating a master
suite, which is a typical amenity of homes in the vicinity, and will also allow
closet space in the adjoining bedroom to be improved; and (c) there is no
conforming location for the proposed improvements, and the existing
nonconformities in the building cannot be cured unless the residence, which is
in sound condition, is demolished and rebuilt; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because the proposed dormer addition will not expand the
footprint of the existing building, will not be visible from the street, will
be set in from the sides of the existing building, and will merely appear to be
a full second story when viewed from the rear of the Subject Property, because
the ridge of the roof is currently approximately 29 feet above the grade at the
rear of the house and the proposed dormer will be lower than the ridge
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not impair an adequate supply of light
and air because the proposed addition will not increase the existing building
height and will be set in from the sides of the existing residence; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property, as the proposed construction will comply
with all applicable building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed construction will not contribute to congestion on the
public streets, as the property will continue to be used for single family
residential purposes and the living capacity of the residence will not be
increased; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the requested variation will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation is in harmony with the general purpose and
intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that it will allow the renovation,
restoration and rehabilitation of a structurally sound existing building while
maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the community and protecting
established trees and landscaping.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1:   The foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the findings
of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth herein.
   SECTION 2:   The Subject Property, commonly known as 194 Fuller Lane and
located in the R-5 Single-Family Residential District provided in Chapter 17.12
of the Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, is hereby
granted a variation of 480.39 square feet (17.94%) from the maximum building
size requirements of Section 17.30.040 of the Lot, Space, Bulk and Yard
Regulations for Single-Family Residential Districts established by Section
17.30 of the Zoning Ordinance, to allow an additional 211.92 square feet of
gross floor area on the Subject Property, so a dormer can be added to the
second floor at the rear of the house, resulting in a gross floor area of
3,158.55 square feet, whereas a maximum of 2,678.16 square feet is permitted,
said construction to be in accordance with the plans and elevations submitted
with the application for variations.
   SECTION 3:   The variation hereby granted is conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4:   This Ordinance is passed by the Council of the Village of
Winnetka in the exercise of its home rule powers pursuant to Section 6 of
Article VII of the Illinois Constitution of 1970.
   SECTION 5:   This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon its passage,
approval and posting as provided by law.
PASSED this 21st day of February, 2006, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
AYES:    Trustees Abell, Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell and Tucker
NAYS:    None
ABSENT:    None
APPROVED this 21st day of February, 2006.
   Signed:
   //s//Edmund C. Woodbury
   Village President
Countersigned:
//s//Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: February 7, 2006
Posted: February 8, 2006
Passed and Approved: February 21, 2006
Posted: February 22, 2006
ORDINANCE NO. M-5-2006
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (1128 Mt. Pleasant)
   WHEREAS, the Village of Winnetka is a home rule municipality in accordance
with Article VII, Section 6 of the Constitution of the State of Illinois of
1970; and
   WHEREAS, pursuant to Article VII, Section 6 of the Constitution of the State
of Illinois of 1970, the Village of Winnetka has the authority, except as
limited by said Section 6 of Article VII, to exercise any power and perform any
function pertaining to the government and affairs of the Village, including,
but not limited to, the powers (i) to regulate for the protection of the public
health, safety, morals and welfare, (ii) to license, (iii) to tax, and (iv) to
incur debt; and
   WHEREAS, the Village Council find that establishing standards for the use
and development of lands and buildings within the Village and establishing and
applying criteria for variations from those standards are matters pertaining to
the affairs of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
Lot 1 in Laurie’s Subdivision of Lots 71, 72, 73 and 74 in Alles Sunset
Subdivision of the Northeast Quarter of the Southwest Quarter of Section 20,
Township 42, Range 13, East of the Third Principal Meridian, in Cook County,
Illinois,
commonly known as 1128 Mt. Pleasant Lane, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in
the R-2 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.24 of
the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for the
following variations from the garage requirements of Section 17.30.110 of the
Lot, Space, Bulk and Yard Regulations for Single Family Residential Districts
established by Section 17.30 of the Zoning Ordinance: (a) a variation from the
limitations on front-facing garages to allow a front-facing garage that is
34.38 feet in width, whereas a maximum of 22 feet is permitted, a variation of
12.38 feet (56.27%); and (b) a variation on the limitation on the width of
garage doors, to allow two garage doors, one that is 16 feet wide and the other
9 feet wide, for a combined width of 25 feet, whereas a maximum of two 9-foot
wide doors are allowed, a variation of 7 feet (38.89%); and
   WHEREAS, on December 12, 2005, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variations and, by the
unanimous vote of the Board, has reported to the Council recommending that the
requested variations be granted; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and particular hardships
associated with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance
with respect to the Subject Property in that: (a) the Subject Property is
considered a corner lot because the dedicated, but unimproved Glendale Avenue
right-of-way lies adjacent to its west lot line; (b) the unimproved Glendale
Avenue right of way is part of Crow Island Woods Park, which is owned by the
Winnetka Park District; (c) on January 6, 1998, the Village Council enacted
Ordinance M-509-97, which vacated all of the platted, unimproved public rights-
of-way within the boundaries of Crow Island Woods Park and transferred
ownership of those vacated rights of way to the Winnetka Park District; (d) the
Village did not include the unimproved Glendale Avenue right of way in the
rights-of-way to be vacated by Ordinance M-509-97, because the statute then
governing the vacation of streets required the adjoining owners on each side to
take ownership to the center line of the right of way, which would have severed
a portion of Crow Island Woods and attached it to the Subject Property; (e) the
Village does not plan to open the unimproved Glendale Avenue right-of-way for
vehicular or pedestrian use, because it does not connect to another public
right-of-way, is needed as a location for certain Village utility lines, and
vacating the right-of-way is not necessary for it to continue to function as
part of Crow Island Woods Park; (f) it is not in the public interest to vacate
the Glendale Avenue right-of-way and convey any portion of it to an adjoining
private owner, because it would reduce the amount of public open space in Crow
Island Woods and increase the allowable building size on the adjoining private
property, which would alter the character and scale of the neighborhood; and
(g) because the Village does not plan for the unimproved Glendale Avenue right-
of-way to function as a street, the Subject Property is effectively an interior
lot; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that
the interests of the public in observing the full corner setback on the Subject
Property are outweighed by the negative impact on the Subject Property,
because: (a) the Subject Property looks like and functions as an interior lot;
(b) the proposed garage will be facing the open space of Crow Island Woods
Park; and (c) strict application of the garage and garage door limitations to
the Subject Property would result in garage doors that face the established
residential neighborhood or would require placing the garage in the rear yard,
which is not consistent with the established development patterns on the block
face; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because the proposed new garage will face Crow Island Woods
Park, so that only the principal use of the Subject Property will be visible
from Mt. Pleasant Road; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not impair an adequate supply of
light and air because the proposed new garage will be on the west side of the
Subject Property, where there is no adjacent neighbor to be affected by the
structure, and the proposed new residence conforms with all other bulk
requirements of the Zoning Ordinance; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property, as the proposed construction will comply
with all applicable building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not diminish the taxable value of
land and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed construction will not contribute to congestion on the
public streets, as the property will continue to be used for single family
residential purposes, and the proposed garages will increase the off-street
parking capacity on the subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the requested variations will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations are in harmony with the general purpose
and intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that they will allow the
construction of a new residence that will maintain the existing scale and
appearance of the immediate neighborhood; and
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1:   The foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the findings
of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth herein.
   SECTION 2:   The following variations from the garage requirements of
Section 17.30.110 of the Lot, Space, Bulk and Yard Regulations for Single
Family Residential Districts established by Section 17.30 of the Winnetka
Zoning Ordinance are hereby granted to the Subject Property, commonly known as
1128 Mt. Pleasant Lane and located in the R-2 Single-Family Residential
District provided in Chapter 17.24 of the Winnetka Village Code: (a) a
variation from the limitations on front-facing garages to allow a front-facing
garage that is 34.38 feet in width, whereas a maximum of 22 feet is permitted,
a variation of 12.38 feet (56.27%); and (b) a variation on the limitation on
the width of garage doors, to allow two garage doors, one that is 16 feet wide
and the other 9 feet wide, for a combined width of 25 feet, whereas a maximum
of two 9-foot wide doors are allowed, a variation of 7 feet (38.89%); said
construction to be in accordance with the plans and elevations submitted with
the application for variations.
   SECTION 3:   The variations hereby granted are conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4:   This Ordinance is passed by the Council of the Village of
Winnetka in the exercise of its home rule powers pursuant to Section 6 of
Article VII of the Illinois Constitution of 1970.
   SECTION 5:   This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon its passage,
approval and posting as provided by law.
PASSED this 21st day of February, 2006, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
AYES:    Trustees Abell, Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell and Tucker
NAYS:    None
ABSENT:    None
APPROVED this 21st day of February, 2006.
