ORDINANCE NO. M-6-2006
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (545-L Hill)
   WHEREAS, the Village of Winnetka is a home rule municipality in accordance
with Article VII, Section 6 of the Constitution of the State of Illinois of
1970; and
   WHEREAS, pursuant to Article VII, Section 6 of the Constitution of the State
of Illinois of 1970, the Village of Winnetka has the authority, except as
limited by said Section 6 of Article VII, to exercise any power and perform any
function pertaining to the government and affairs of the Village, including,
but not limited to, the powers (i) to regulate for the protection of the public
health, safety, morals and welfare, (ii) to license, (iii) to tax, and (iv) to
incur debt; and
   WHEREAS, the Village Council find that establishing standards for the use
and development of lands and buildings within the Village and establishing and
applying criteria for variations from those standards are matters pertaining to
the affairs of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
Parcel 1: Unit Number 545’L’ in the Chimneys Condominium, as delineated on a
survey of the following described real estate: Lot 1 in the Chimneys, a
consolidation in the South ½ of the Southwest ¼ of Section 21, Township 42
North, Range 13, East of the Third Principal Meridian, which survey is attached
as Exhibit ‘A’ to the declaration of condominium recorded as Document Number
89550724, together with its undivided percentage interest in common elements in
Cook County, Illinois.
Parcel 2: The exclusive right to the use of P-17, a limited common elements as
delineated on the survey attached to the declaration aforesaid recorded as
Document 89550724 and amended by Document 89570571,
commonly known as 545-L Hill Terrace, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the B-
2 Multifamily Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.36 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for a
variation from the setback requirements of the Regulations for Multifamily
Residential Districts established by Section 17.36 of the Zoning Ordinance to
permit a front setback of 0 feet, whereas a setback of 25 feet is required, a
variation of 25 feet (100%) in order to allow the installation of an air
conditioner condenser at the south property line of the condominium unit; and
   WHEREAS, on December 12, 2005, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variations and has reported
to the Council unanimously recommending that the requested variation be
granted; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and particular hardships
associated with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance
with respect to the Subject Property in that: (a) the Subject Property is an
end-unit in a condominium building that was originally constructed in 1929 as
an apartment building; (b) the south lot line of the Subject Property adjoins
the dedicated Hill Terrace right-of-way, thereby imposing a 25-foot setback
requirement; (c) although the Hill Terrace right-of-way forms a cul-de-sac, it
is only partially improved, so as to connect Hill Terrace directly to green Bay
Road; (d) the unimproved portion of the Hill Terrace right-of-way functions as
green space for the condominium building; (e) because the Subject Property
abuts the dedicated right-of-way, a 25-foot setback is required; and (f)
placing the condenser in a conforming location would put it in the building
courtyard, in violation of the condominium association's by-laws; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that
central air conditioning is a typical component of the heating and ventilation
systems of modern homes in the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because the proposed air conditioning condenser will be
located in unimproved green space and will be screened from view by foliage and
a curb; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not impair an adequate supply of light
and air because the proposed air conditioning condenser measures only 25 inches
by 27 inches; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property, as the proposed installation of the air
conditioning condenser will comply with all applicable building and fire
protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvement; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not contribute to congestion on the
public streets, as the property will continue to be used for multifamily
residential purposes; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the requested variation will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, when implemented according to the conditions imposed by this
Ordinance, the requested variation will be in harmony with the general purpose
and intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that it allows the renovation
and improvement of a structurally sound existing building while maintaining the
existing scale and appearance of the community and protecting established trees
and landscaping.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1:   The foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the findings
of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth herein.
   SECTION 2:   Subject to the conditions set forth in Section 3 of this
Ordinance, the Subject Property, commonly known as 545-L Hill Terrace, and
located in the B-2 Multifamily Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.36 of the
Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, is hereby granted a
100% variation from the front setback requirements of Section 17.36.010.F of
the Regulations for B-2 Multifamily Residential Districts established by
Chapter 17.36 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, to permit a front setback of 0
feet, whereas a setback of 25 feet is required, to allow the installation of an
air conditioner condenser at the south property line of the Subject Property,
all in accordance with the plans and elevations submitted with the application
for variation.
   SECTION 3:   The variation granted by this Ordinance is subject to the
following conditions:
      A.   The proposed construction shall commence within 12 months after the
effective date of this Ordinance.
      B.   The variation shall be subject to the Village's right to open and
pave the entire cul-de-sac that forms the east end of the Hill Terrace right of
way. In the event that the Village determines that the full cul-de-sac should
be paved and opened for vehicular travel, the owner of the Subject Property
shall be required to relocate the air conditioner at the owner's expense.
   SECTION 4:   This Ordinance is passed by the Council of the Village of
Winnetka in the exercise of its home rule powers pursuant to Section 6 of
Article VII of the Illinois Constitution of 1970.
   SECTION 5:   This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon its passage,
approval and posting as provided by law.
PASSED this 21st day of February, 2006, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
AYES:    Trustees Abell, Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell and Tucker
NAYS:    None
ABSENT:    None
APPROVED this 21st day of February, 2006.
   Signed:
   //s//Edmund C. Woodbury
   Village President
Countersigned:
//s//Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: February 7, 2006
Posted: February 8, 2006
Passed and Approved: February 21, 2006
Posted: February 22, 2006
ORDINANCE NO. M-7-2006
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (804 Lincoln)
   WHEREAS, the Village of Winnetka is a home rule municipality in accordance
with Article VII, Section 6 of the Constitution of the State of Illinois of
1970; and
   WHEREAS, pursuant to Article VII, Section 6 of the Constitution of the State
of Illinois of 1970, the Village of Winnetka has the authority, except as
limited by said Section 6 of Article VII, to exercise any power and perform any
function pertaining to the government and affairs of the Village, including,
but not limited to, the powers (i) to regulate for the protection of the public
health, safety, morals and welfare, (ii) to license, (iii) to tax, and (iv) to
incur debt; and
   WHEREAS, the Village Council find that establishing standards for the use
and development of lands and buildings within the Village and establishing and
applying criteria for variations from those standards are matters pertaining to
the affairs of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
Lot 3 in Block 7 in Winnetka Park Bluffs, a subdivision by William H. Carnduff
of parts of Section 16, 17 and 20, Township 42 North, Range 13 East of the
Third Principal Meridian, in Cook County, Illinois,
commonly known as 804 Lincoln Avenue, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
5 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.12 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for the
following variations from requirements of the Lot, Space, Bulk and Yard
Regulations for Single Family Residential Districts established by Chapter
17.30 of the Zoning Ordinance: (a) a variation from the intensity of use of lot
provisions of Section 17.30.030 to permit a roofed lot coverage of 2,501.7
square feet, whereas a maximum of 1,875 square feet is permitted, resulting in
a variation of 626.7 square feet (33.42%); and (b) a variation from the
intensity of use of lot provisions of Section 17.30.030 to permit an
impermeable surface area of 4,229.3 square feet, whereas a maximum of 3,750
square feet is permitted, resulting in a variation of 479.3 square feet
(12.78%), said variations being for the purpose of allowing the construction of
additions to the first and second floors at the rear of the existing home, and
of allowing the existing two-car detached garage to be replaced by a new two-
car garage with an additional storage area; and
   WHEREAS, on February 13, 2006, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variations; and
   WHEREAS, although five members of the Zoning Board of Appeals were present
on February 13, 2006, Board member Joe Adams, who lives on the property
immediately to the south of the Subject Property, recused himself from the
proceedings on the requested variations; and
   WHEREAS, upon the completion of hearing, the four participating members of
the Zoning Board of Appeals voted to recommend to the Council that the
requested variations be granted; and
   WHEREAS, the applicants have agreed to reduce the degree of the northern
extension of the new garage by 4 feet; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties associated with carrying out the
strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect to the Subject Property
in that: (a) the residence is a legally non-conforming structure that was
constructed in the Arts and Crafts style in 1914, prior to the enactment of the
Zoning Ordinance in 1925 and the subsequent imposition of roofed lot coverage
and impermeable surface limitations; (b) the existing residence is
characterized by large eaves, which extend more than 18 inches from the walls
of the building and therefore account for 201 square feet of roofed lot
coverage; (c) the proposed addition will match the design of the existing
house, including the deep eaves, which will add another 90.32 square feet to
the roofed lot coverage; (d) the proposed first floor addition will add a
family room adjacent to the kitchen area of the existing residence; (e) the
proposed second floor addition will be smaller than the first floor addition
and will improve the master bathroom by expanding its size to accommodate a
bath tub and to add a walk-in closet and dressing area adjacent to the master
bedroom; (f) the proposed work on the residence will improve the appearance of
the block face because the enclosed front porch, which is currently
incorporated into the interior living space of the house, will be converted to
an open front porch, a desirable architectural feature that is encouraged in
the Village's zoning regulations; and (g) the proposed additions and new garage
will improve the functionality of the house and the Subject Property while
maintaining desirable features such as large eaves, an open front porch, a
detached garage at the rear of the property, and a consistency of design
between the house and garage; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because: (a) the proposed construction is consistent with the
architectural style of the existing structure; and (b) the new, open front
porch will enhance the character of the block face; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not impair an adequate supply of
light and air because the proposed improvements will comply with both building
setback and building height limitations; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property, as the proposed construction will comply
with all applicable building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not diminish the taxable value of
land and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed construction will not contribute to congestion on the
public streets, as the property will continue to be used for single family
residential purposes; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the requested variations will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations are in harmony with the general purpose
and intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that; (a) they allow the
renovation, restoration and rehabilitation of a structurally sound existing
building; (b) they maintain the existing scale and appearance of the community
by preserving the architectural details of the Arts and Crafts style, including
the deep eaves, and adding an open front porch to the block face; and (d) they
protect established trees and landscaping.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1:   The foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the findings
of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth herein.
   SECTION 2:   The following variations from the Lot, Space, Bulk and Yard
Regulations for Single Family Residential Districts established by Chapter
17.30 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance are hereby granted to the Subject
Property, commonly known as 804 Lincoln Avenue, and located in the R-5 Single-
Family Residential District provided in Chapter 17.12 of the Zoning Ordinance,
Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code: (a) a variation from the intensity of
use of lot provisions of Section 17.30.030 to permit a roofed lot coverage of
2,501.7 square feet, whereas a maximum of 1,875 square feet is permitted,
resulting in a variation of 626.7 square feet (33.42%); and (b) a variation
from the intensity of use of lot provisions of Section 17.30.030 to permit an
impermeable surface area of 4,229.3 square feet, whereas a maximum of 3,750
square feet is permitted, resulting in a variation of 479.3 square feet
(12.78%); said variations being for the purpose of allowing the construction of
additions on the first and second floor at the rear of the existing home, and
of allowing the existing two-car detached garage to be replaced by a new two-
car garage with an additional storage area, all in accordance with the plans
and elevations submitted with the application for variations, as modified by
reducing the extension from the north wall of the new garage from a depth of
7.5 feet to 3.5 feet.
   SECTION 3:   The variations hereby granted are conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4:   This Ordinance is passed by the Council of the Village of
Winnetka in the exercise of its home rule powers pursuant to Section 6 of
Article VII of the Illinois Constitution of 1970.
   SECTION 5:   This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon its passage,
approval and posting as provided by law.
PASSED this 18th day of April, 2006, pursuant to the following roll call vote:
AYES:    Trustees Abell, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, Tucker
NAYS:    Trustee Behles
ABSENT:    None
APPROVED this 18th day of April, 2006.
   Signed:
   //s//Edmund C. Woodbury
   Village President
Countersigned:
//s//Douglas G. Willliams
Village Clerk
Introduced: April 4, 2006
Posted: April 5, 2006
Passed and Approved: April 18, 2006
Posted: April 19, 2006
ORDINANCE NO. M-8-2006
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A SPECIAL USE PERMIT TO ALLOW A HEALTH CLUB FACILITY AT
546 LINCOLN AVENUE
   WHEREAS, the property commonly known as 546 Lincoln Avenue, Winnetka,
Illinois (the “Subject Property”), is legally described as follows:
Parcel 1:
That part of Lots 8 and 9 of Block 11 in Louis D. Webster’s Resubdivision of
Lots 4, 5 and 8 in Block 9, Block 11 (except the North 75.00 feet) Block 13 and
Lots 6,8 to 10 in Block 12, all in Park Addition to Winnetka located in the
Northeast ¼ of Section 20, Township 42 North, Range 13, East of the Third
Principal Meridian, according to the plat thereof recorded in the Recorders
Office of Cook County, Illinois on October 31, 1891 in Book 54 of Plats Page 15
as document number 1561125 described as follows to wit: Beginning at the
Northeast corner of said Lot 8; thence West along the North line of Said Lot 8,
113.66 feet; thence Southeasterly on a line parallel with the Westerly line of
said Lots 8 and 9, 18.70 feet, thence Southeasterly on a straight line 3.82
feet to a point 36.86 feet Northeasterly of measured at right angles to the
Westerly line of said Lot 8; thence Southeasterly on a line parallel to the
Westerly line of Said Lots 8 and 9, 56.98 feet; thence East parallel with the
North line of said Lot 9, 70.00 feet to the East line of said Lot 9; thence
North along the East line of said Lots 8 and 9 65.75 feet to the place of
beginning; and
Parcel 2:
An easement for the benefit of the property hereinabove described as created by
a grant from Chicago North Shore and Milwaukee Railway Company, a corporation
of Illinois, to Dorethea Landry and Ruth Anselm dated January 11, 1949 and
recorded in the Recorder’s Office of Cook County, Illinois, on January 17, 1949
as Document Number 14480032 for ingress and egress over that certain tract of
land contained in said Lots 8 and 9 of Block 11 in Louis D. Webster’s
Resubdivision aforesaid described as follows: beginning at a point in the East
line of Lot 9, 68.45 feet North of the Southeast corner of said lot; thence
West on a line parallel with the North line of said Lot 9, 70.00 feet; thence
Southeasterly on a line parallel with the Westerly line of Lot 9, 14.21 feet;
thence East on a line parallel with the North line of Lot 9, 62.38 feet to the
East line of Sid Lot 9; thence North on the East line of Lot 9, 12.00 feet to
the place of beginning; and
Parcel 3:
An easement contained in said grant recorded as Document Number 14480032 for
the benefit of the property first hereinabove described, to install, maintain
and repair a storm sewer or sewers in and upon the following tracts of land
contained in said Lots 8 and 9 of Block 11 in Louis D. Webster’s Resubdivision
aforesaid described as follows: beginning at a point in the East line of Lot 9,
68.45 feet North of the Southeast corner of said lot; thence West on a line
parallel with the North line of said Lot 9, 70.00 feet; thence Southeasterly on
a line parallel with the Westerly line of Lot 9, 14.21 feet; thence East on a
line parallel with the North line of Lot 9, 62.38 feet to the East line of said
Lot 9: thence North on the East line of Lot 9, 12.00 feet to the place of
beginning; and.
Parcel 3A:
Beginning at a point in the North line of said Lot 8, a distance of 113.66 feet
West of the Northeast corner of Lot 8; thence Southeasterly on a line parallel
with the Westerly line of said Lots 8 and 9, 18.70 feet; thence Southeasterly
on a straight line 3.62 feet to a point 36.86 feet Northeasterly of measured at
right angles to the Westerly line of said Lot 8; thence Southeasterly on a line
parallel to the Westerly line of said Lots 8 and 9, 56.98 feet; thence West
along a line parallel with North line of Lot 8, a distance of 11.43 feet to a
point 27.21 feet Northeasterly of, measured at right angles to the Westerly
line of said Lots and 9; thence Northwesterly along a line parallel to the
Westerly line of said Lots 8 and 9, 77.83 feet to a point in the North Line of
Lot 8: thence East along the North line of Lot 8, 9.51 feet to the place of
beginning; and
Parcel 4:
Commencing at a point in the East line of Lot 9, 68.45 feet North of the
Southeast corner of said lot; thence West on a line parallel with the North
line of said Lot 70.00 feet to the place of beginning of the land to be
described; thence West along the last described line 11.43 feet; thence
Southeasterly on a line parallel with the Westerly line of said lot, 14.21
feet; thence East on a line parallel with the North line of said lot 11.43
feet; thence Northwesterly on a line parallel with the Westerly line of said
Lot 11, 14.21 feet to the place of beginning, all in Cook County, Illinois; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property is located in the C-2 General Retail
Commercial Overlay Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.44 of the Winnetka
Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code; and
   WHEREAS, on January 10, 2006, the lessee of the Subject Property filed an
application for a special use permit to allow a health club facility to be
located in newly renovated space at the rear of the ground floor of the
building located on the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, pursuant to Section 17.44.010 (B)(2)(e) of the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, health clubs are permitted as
special uses in the C-2 Overlay Zoning District, subject to the conditions and
requirements set forth in Chapter 17.44 and further subject to the conditions
and requirements pertaining to special uses set forth in Chapter 17.56 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance; and
   WHEREAS, on February 13, 2006, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested special use and, by the
unanimous vote of the five members then present, has reported to the Council
recommending that the special use permit be granted; and
   WHEREAS, on January 22, 2006, on due notice thereof, the Plan Commission
considered the requested special use and, by the favorable vote of six of the
eight members then present, found the proposed special use to be consistent
with the Comprehensive Plan, Winnetka 2020, and has reported to the Council
recommending that the special use be granted; and
   WHEREAS, the record of proceedings before the Zoning Board of Appeals and
Plan Commission consists of: (a) the application for special use, including a
traffic impact analysis and a supplementary description of the business; (b)
Sidwell and Sanborn maps depicting the location of the subject Property; (c) a
scale drawing of the floor plan of the portion of the Subject Property that is
the subject of the special use; (d) a January 21, 2006, memo from the Village
Engineer to the Assistant Director of Community Development commenting on
traffic and parking issues; (e) minutes of the February 13, 2006, Zoning Board
of Appeals hearing; (f) minutes of the January 22, 2006, Plan Commission
meeting; and (g) photographs of existing studio facilities; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed health facility will be located at the northwest
corner of the first floor of the renovated building, along the north side of
the building's center hallway; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed health facility will have a total area of 1,430 square
feet and will include (a) a retail sales area for the sale of fitness apparel,
fitness equipment and dietary programs and supplements, (b) two training rooms,
(c) a cardio area, (d) an office, (e) two restrooms, including one with a
shower, and (f) a laundry closet; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed use will provide one-on-one physical training in the
two training suites and there will be no group classes; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed 45-minute training sessions will be held in the two
training suites and will be scheduled by appointment, so there will be only two
training clients on the premises at a time; and
   WHEREAS, facility will have a total of five employees, with the maximum
number of employees at the facility at any one time being the manager and two
trainers; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed hours of business will be from 6:00 a.m. to 9:00 p.m.
Monday through Friday and from 6:00 a.m. to 3:00 p.m. on Saturday, with the
busiest hours expected to be between 6:00 a.m. and 11:00 a.m. and between 4:00
p.m. and 8:00 p.m.; and
   WHEREAS, no on-site parking is available, although there is adjacent on-
street parking on Lincoln Avenue and the Subject Property is also in close
proximity to the Lincoln Avenue Parking Lot; and
   WHEREAS, after reviewing the application, the Village Engineer concluded
that a formal traffic and parking study would not be required for the proposed
special use, having concluded that the use will not increase or cause traffic
congestion in the business district, an that there is adequate parking in the
Lincoln Avenue Parking Lot; and
   WHEREAS, at the hearing before the Zoning Board of Appeals, the Board heard
the testimony of the building owner, and of the applicant, who will be a lessee
of the explained the nature of the training facilities, provided sample
photographs of the operations at other facilities run by the same company; and
   WHEREAS, the proceedings before the Zoning Board of Appeals included
questioning of the applicant and the owner by members of the Zoning Board of
Appeals; and
   WHEREAS, none of the owners of the properties located within 250 feet of the
Subject Property submitted any evidence or requested an opportunity to cross-
examine witnesses at either the Zoning Board of Appeals hearing or at the Plan
Commission proceedings; and
   WHEREAS, the proceedings of the Zoning Board of Appeals and Plan Commission
conformed with all requirements of their procedural rules, the Winnetka Village
Code and applicable statutes of the State of Illinois; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property is located in the Elm Street Business District
on the west side of Lincoln Avenue, north of Elm Street; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property is improved with a two-story building that
formerly was occupied by L&A Stationers and that has been undergoing
renovation, which is nearly complete; and
   WHEREAS, the owner of the Subject Property has not been able to find a
tenant for the full 10,100 square foot first floor, and the area proposed for
the special use is not desirable for retail use because it is at the rear of
the building and has no street frontage on Lincoln Avenue; and
   WHEREAS, the applicant estimates that the retail sales from the proposed
operation will account for approximately 15% to 20% of its revenues; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed special use will neither endanger nor be detrimental
to the public health, safety, comfort, morals or general welfare, in that the
proposed use is compatible with the immediate neighborhood, and will provide a
small health club facility with related retail sales in a space that is
otherwise not desirable for retail purposes; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed special use will neither substantially diminish nor
impair property values in the immediate vicinity, in that (a) it will enable
the owner to lease space that is not generally desirable for retail sales, (b)
it will have a minimal impact on the immediate neighborhood, and (c) may draw
new patrons to the other commercial businesses that will occupy the Subject
Property and other properties along Lincoln Avenue; and
   WHEREAS, adequate measures have been taken to provide ingress and egress in
a manner that minimizes pedestrian and vehicular traffic congestion in the
public ways, in that: (a) the facility is easily accessible from Lincoln Avenue
and the Lincoln Avenue Parking Lot; and (b) the one-on-one scheduled training
format will regulate the number and flow of persons entering and leaving the
facility for training purposes, and unscheduled visits are likely to be persons
seeking to purchase the operation's retail products; and
   WHEREAS, adequate parking, utilities, access roads, drainage and other
facilities necessary for the operation of the special use already exist; and
   WHEREAS, because the proposed special use will not change the appearance of
the immediate vicinity, it is consistent with the Winnetka 2020 objective to
“ensure that Commercial, institutional, and residential development is
appropriate to the character of and minimizes the adverse impact on its
surrounding neighborhood;” and
   WHEREAS, because of its minimal intensity and the pre-existing
infrastructure, the proposed special use is consistent with the Winnetka 2020
objectives to: (a) “limit commercial, institutional and residential development
within the Village to minimize potentially adverse impacts on adjacent
residential neighborhoods and to prevent the need for significant increases in
infrastructure and other community resources,” and (b) “ensure that development
proposals minimize the potential adverse impact they might have on residential
neighborhoods, including the impact on pedestrian character, on site parking,
traffic patterns, congestion, open space, storm water management and Village
infrastructure;” and
   WHEREAS, the proposed special use is consistent with the Winnetka 2020 goal
to “provide for a wide range of office/service and retail commercial land uses
and development within the existing business districts in the Corridor;” and
   WHEREAS, because it adds a use that is complementary to the commercial
retail operations in the immediate vicinity, without using prime commercial
space with street exposure, the proposed special use is consistent with the
Winnetka 2020 goal to maintain the essential quality, viability and
attractiveness of Winnetka’s business districts while encouraging new economic
development consistent with the character of the Village and the individual
business districts;” and
   WHEREAS, the Village of Winnetka is a home rule municipality in accordance
with Article VII, Section 6 of the Constitution of the State of Illinois of
1970; and
   WHEREAS, pursuant to Article VII, Section 6 of the Constitution of the State
of Illinois of 1970, the Village of Winnetka has the authority, except as
limited by said Section 6 of Article VII, to exercise any power and perform any
function pertaining to the government and affairs of the Village, including,
but not limited to, the powers (i) to regulate for the protection of the public
health, safety, morals and welfare, (ii) to license, (iii) to tax, and (iv) to
incur debt; and
   WHEREAS, the Village Council find that establishing standards for the use
and development of lands and buildings within the Village and the application
of those standards to specific parcels of property, including establishing and
applying standards for special uses, are matters pertaining to the affairs of
the Village.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1:   The foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the findings
of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth herein.
   SECTION 2:   Pursuant to Section 17.44.010 (B)(2)(e) and Section 17.56.010
of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, and subject to the conditions set forth in
Section 3 of this Ordinance, a special use is hereby granted to the Subject
Property, commonly known as 546 Lincoln Avenue, Winnetka, Illinois, and located
in the C-2 General Retail Commercial Overlay Zoning District, to allow for a
health club facility to operate on the Subject Property, all in accordance with
the plans submitted with the application.
   SECTION 3:   The special use granted by this Ordinance shall be subject to
the following conditions:
      A.   The proposed special use shall be confined to the space at the
northwest corner of the first floor of the Subject Property, as depicted in the
floor plan submitted with the application, and as more specifically defined in
the lease between the applicant and the owner of the Subject Property.
      B.   The proposed health club facility shall begin operation within one
year after the date of the passage of this Ordinance.
      C.   The references to the proposed hours contained in the recitals of
this Ordinance shall not be construed as a limitation on the hours of operation
of the special use.
   SECTION 4 :   The stipulations, conditions and restrictions set forth in the
foregoing Section 3 of this Ordinance may be modified or revised from time to
time by the Village Council following public notice and hearing, following the
procedures specified in Section 17.56 of the Winnetka Village Code for
processing special use applications.
   SECTION 5:   This Ordinance is passed by the Council of the Village of
Winnetka in the exercise of its home rule powers pursuant to Section 6 of
Article VII of the Illinois Constitution of 1970.
   SECTION 6:   This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon its passage,
approval and posting as provided by law.
PASSED this 18th day of April, 2006, pursuant to the following roll call vote:
