ORDINANCE NO. M-9-2006
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (251 White Oak)
   WHEREAS, the Village of Winnetka is a home rule municipality in accordance
with Article VII, Section 6 of the Constitution of the State of Illinois of
1970; and
   WHEREAS, pursuant to Article VII, Section 6 of the Constitution of the State
of Illinois of 1970, the Village of Winnetka has the authority, except as
limited by said Section 6 of Article VII, to exercise any power and perform any
function pertaining to the government and affairs of the Village, including,
but not limited to, the powers (i) to regulate for the protection of the public
health, safety, morals and welfare, (ii) to license, (iii) to tax, and (iv) to
incur debt; and
   WHEREAS, the Village Council find that establishing standards for the use
and development of lands and buildings within the Village and establishing and
applying criteria for variations from those standards are matters pertaining to
the affairs of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
The West 10 feet of the South 224 feet of Lot “O”, the East 33 feet of the West
43 feet of the South 170 feet of Lot “O” and Lot “P” (except that part thereof,
lying North of a line beginning at a point in the East line of said Lot “P”,
224 feet North of the Southeast corner thereof and running thence West at right
angles to said East line, 66.54 feet more or less, to the Westerly line of said
Lot “P”) in Resubdivision of certain lots in White Oak Subdivision in the
Northwest ¼ of the Southeast ¼ of Section 20, Township 42 North, Range 13, East
of the Third Principal Meridian, according to the plat registered in the Office
of the Registrar of Titles of Cook County, Illinois on March 14, 1940, as
Document Number 850245, in Cook County, Illinois,
commonly known as 251 White Oak Lane, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
2 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.24 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for a
variation from the front yard setback requirements of Section 17.30.050 of the
Lot, Space, Bulk and Yard Regulations for Single Family Residential Districts
established by Chapter 17.30 of the Zoning Ordinance, to allow a front yard
setback of 22.39 feet, rather than the required minimum of 48.73 square feet,
resulting in a variation of 26.34 feet (54.05%), in order to construct a one-
story addition consisting of a mudroom and attached, three-car garage, and to
allow a 6.974 square foot, triangular sliver of a proposed two-story addition
at the rear of the house to encroach up to 10-5/16 inches into the corner yard
setback; and
   WHEREAS, on April 10, 2006, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variations and, by the
unanimous vote of the 5 members then present, has reported to the Council
recommending that the requested variations be granted; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and particular hardships
associated with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance
with respect to the Subject Property in that: (a) the Subject Property is an
irregularly shaped corner lot located at the northeast corner of Sunset Road
and White Oak Lane; (b) the south lot line, which has been designated as the
front lot line, is adjacent to Sunset Road, which runs east and west; (c) the
White Oak Lane street frontage of the Subject Property is a long, interior
curving lot line that is defined by the curve of White Oak Lane, which runs in
a generally diagonal direction, from the southwest corner of the Subject
Property towards the northeast; (d) the Subject Property is improved with a
legal, nonconforming single-family residence, designed by architect Edwin Clark
and constructed in 1936, and a detached garage, constructed in 1937; (e)
although Sunset Road is designated as the front yard for zoning purposes, the
existing house is oriented toward the curving, diagonal White Oak Lane street
frontage and the detached garage is located at the north end of the lot in what
is designated as the rear of the Subject Property; (f) the Subject Property is
encumbered by a 20-foot roadway easement along the White Oak Lane lot line; (g)
as a corner lot, the Subject Property is subject to a 50-foot front yard
setback along Sunset Road and a 49-foot corner yard setback along White Oak
Lane; (h) the 48.73-foot corner yard setback is measured from the edge of the
20-foot roadway easement, effectively imposing a 68.73-foot setback requirement
along the White Oak Lane frontage of the Subject Property; (i) because of the
curving corner lot line and the enlarged corner yard setback, the buildable
area of the lot is less than 25% of the total lot area and approximately 80% of
the residence and almost all of the existing garage encroach into the required
corner yard setback; (j) the buildable area of the Subject Property is further
constrained by the irregular north and east lot lines, which are the result of
an interior corner located 54 feet south of the north lot line and 33 feet west
of the east lot line; (k) there is no fully conforming location for a garage on
the Subject Property, because the buildable area of the lot is primarily behind
the existing house, and because there are two mature trees in the northeast
portion of the buildable area; (l) the proposed new garage will reduce the
degree of nonconformity of the existing garage; (m) the proposed mudroom will
connect the new garage to the existing house; and (n) Village staff has
determined that the de minimis encroachment of the proposed two-story addition
is at the point where the addition connects to the existing house and does not
require a zoning variation; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, because:
(a) the existing three-car detached garage is in a dilapidated condition and
needs to be replaced; (b) the existing nonconformities are related to the
irregular shape of the lot and the effect of the front setback, corner setback
and roadway easement, as well as to the location of the house on the Subject
Property and the house's interior configuration; (c) the garage cannot be
reconstructed in a conforming location without requiring the removal of the two
mature trees and reducing the open space behind the house; and (d) the existing
nonconformities cannot be cured without building an entirely new house, and
building a new house in a conforming location will virtually eliminate a
functional rear yard; and (e) the proposed three-car garage will replace the
existing three-car garage, the mudroom is a typical amenity in residences
throughout the Village and will be placed in a typical location, between the
garage and the kitchen; and(
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because: (a) the proposed garage and mudroom addition will
reduce the degree of nonconformity caused by the current location of the
garage; and (b) the proposed garage and mudroom addition is consistent with the
architectural design and detail of the existing building and the appearance of
the existing building from White Oak Lane will be otherwise unchanged; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not impair an adequate supply of light
and air because the proposed building additions comply with the Zoning
Ordinance's applicable height limitations height and the resulting roofed lot
coverage and gross floor area will be well below the maximum allowed in the R-
2 Zoning District; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property, as the proposed construction will comply
with all applicable building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed construction will not contribute to congestion on the
public streets, as the property will continue to be used for single family
residential purposes; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the requested variation will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation is in harmony with the general purpose and
intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that they allow the renovation,
restoration and rehabilitation of a structurally sound existing building while
maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the community and protecting
established trees and landscaping.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1:   The foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the findings
of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth herein.
   SECTION 2:   The following variation from the Lot, Space, Bulk and Yard
Regulations for Single Family Residential Districts established by Chapter
17.30 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance is hereby granted to the Subject
Property, commonly known as 251 White Oak Lane and located in the R-2 Single-
Family Residential District provided in Chapter 17.24 of the Zoning Ordinance,
Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code: a variation from the front yard setback
requirements of Section 17.30.050 of Chapter 17.30 of the Zoning Ordinance, to
allow a front yard setback of 22.39 feet, rather than the required minimum of
48.73 square feet, resulting in a variation of 26.34 feet (54.05%), in order to
construct a one-story addition consisting of a mudroom and attached, three-car
garage, in accordance with the plans and elevations submitted with the
application for variations.
   SECTION 3:   The variation hereby granted is conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4:   This Ordinance is passed by the Council of the Village of
Winnetka in the exercise of its home rule powers pursuant to Section 6 of
Article VII of the Illinois Constitution of 1970.
   SECTION 5:   This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon its passage,
approval and posting as provided by law.
PASSED this __16th ____ day of ________May___________, 2006, pursuant to the
following roll call vote:
AYES:    Trustees Abell, Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, Tucker
NAYS:    None
ABSENT:    None
