Passed and Approved:   May 16, 2006
Posted:   May 18, 2006
FINDINGS AND CONCLUSIONS OF THE WINNETKA VILLAGE COUNCIL ON THE APPLICATION OF
JEAN WRIGHT FOR A SPECIAL USE PERMIT TO EXPAND FIRST FLOOR OFFICE SPACE AT 555-
561 CHESTNUT STREET
   The Council of the Village of Winnetka, having reviewed and considered the
records of the Zoning Board of Appeals and the Plan Commission on the pending
Application, having heard and considered the presentations made by and on
behalf of the Applicant, and being otherwise fully advised, enters the
following Findings of Fact and Conclusions:
I.   FINDINGS OF FACT
A.   Nature of Application
1.   This matter involves the application of Jean Wright and Jean Wright Real
Estate (“Applicant”), dated March 1, 2006, for a special use permit to allow
the office space at 559 Chestnut Street to expand into the rear of the retail
space at 557 Chestnut Street.
2.   The Applicant proposes to modify the commercial spaces at 557 and 559
Chestnut Street by reducing the retail space occupied by The New England Trader
at 557 Chestnut and expanding the Jean Wright Real Estate offices at 559
Chestnut into the rear of the space at 557 Chestnut.
B.   Description of Parties
3.   Jean Wright is the owner of the Subject Property and of Jean Wright Real
Estate.
4.   Jean Wright Real Estate occupies the space at 559 Chestnut Street.
5.   Ted Argiris is the proprietor of a retail apparel store, The New England
Trader, which is located at 557 Chestnut Street in space leased from Jean
Wright.
C.   Description of Subject Property
6.   The property commonly known as 555-561 Chestnut Street, Winnetka, Illinois
(the “Subject Property”), is legally described as follows:
Lots 15 and 16 according to the plat of “Dale and Others” Subdivision of Block
14, in Winnetka, a subdivision in Sections 20 and 21, Township 42 North, Range
13, East of the Third Principal Meridian, filed for record in the Recorder’s
Office of Cook County, Illinois, on February 4, 1886, as Document Number
688725, and recorded in Book 21, of Plats, Page 31.
7.   The Subject Property is located on the east side of Chestnut Street
between Elm Street and Spruce Street.
8.   The Subject Property is located within the C-2 General Retail Commercial
Zoning District
9.   The Subject Property is also located in the C-2 Retail Overlay District,
which allows certain non-retail uses to be located on the ground floor only
pursuant to a special use permit.
10.   The Subject Property is 110.24 feet wide at the Chestnut Street frontage,
53.80 feet deep at its north side and 100.40 feet deep at its south side.
11.   The east lot line of the Subject Property is 110.89 feet long.
12.   The north and east lot lines of the Subject Property abut a 16-foot wide
public alley.
13.   The Subject Property’s rear lot line and the adjoining alley run
diagonally from the northwest to the southeast, parallel to Green Bay Road,
which is the next street east of Chestnut Street.
14.   The Subject Property is improved with a brick building that is divided
into four commercial spaces, with street addresses of 555, 557, 559 and 561
Chestnut Street.
15.   Each of the four commercial spaces on the Subject Property has a 25-foot
wide street frontage on Chestnut Street and an interior width of approximately
23.3 feet.
16.   The main entrance to each of the four commercial spaces on the Subject
Property is on Chestnut Street.
17.   The space at 555 Chestnut Street is occupied by McElroy Furs, a retail
furrier.
18.   The space at 557 Chestnut Streets occupied by The New England Trader, a
retail apparel store.
19.   The space at 559 Chestnut Street is occupied by Jean Wright Real Estate,
a real estate sales and management office.
20.   The space at 561 Chestnut Street is occupied by Doyle Opticians, which is
engaged in the retail sale of eyewear products and also provides eye
examination services.
D.   Description of Immediate Vicinity
21.   The Subject Property is located in the West Elm Street Business District,
which is centered around Elm Street.
22.   The Comprehensive Plan, Winnetka 2020, identifies the West Elm Street
Business District as being part of the Village’s central business district.
23.   Chestnut Street lies one block west of Green Bay Road.
24.   Chestnut Street is the main north-south street in the West Elm Street
Business District.
25.   Chestnut Street between Elm Street and Spruce Street has the largest
concentration of on-street parking in the West Elm Street Business District,
because the east side of Chestnut Street, from Elm Street to the alley on the
north side of the Subject Property, is striped for diagonal parking spaces,
with parallel parking spaces on the opposite side of the street, and from the
alley north to Spruce Street, there is diagonal parking on the west side and
parallel parking on the east side.
26.   Jean Wright Real Estate is the only office use located at street level on
the east side of Chestnut Street between Elm Street and Spruce Street.
E.   Procedural Background and Evidentiary Record
27.   The Winnetka Zoning Board of Appeals conducted a public hearing on the
special use application on April 10, 2006.
28.   The public hearing was held pursuant to all required notices, and the
public notice of the hearing was read into the record.
29.   The special use permit application does not include a request for any
zoning variations.
30.   All witnesses were sworn.
31.   The applicant’s case was presented by: (a) Jean Wright, the owner of the
Subject Property and of Jean Wright Real Estate; (b) Ted Argiris, the owner of
The New England Trader; and (c) David Schrauth, an attorney who also
represented the Chamber of Commerce, and who provided further detail about the
application and summarized the applicant’s case in relation to the Zoning
Ordinance’s special use standards.
32.   Ms. Wright testified that Jean Wright Real Estate will use the expanded
space for additional conference rooms and storage, but will not be otherwise
expanding the business or adding realtors to occupy the space.
33.   Mr. Argiris has been a business owner in the Village since 1979.
34.   Mr. Argiris testified that he had approached Ms. Wright and requested a
reduction in his lease space so he could continue to operate his business at
557 Chestnut with a smaller inventory and less overhead.
35.   In addition to the application, the applicant submitted the following
documentary evidence:
a.   a plat of survey depicting the location of the Subject Property;
b.   scale drawings of the floor plans of 557 and 559 Chestnut Street; and
c.   space calculations from T. R. Knapp Architects, showing the floor areas of
various portions of the space at 557 and 559 Chestnut, as it currently exists
and as proposed.
36.   The ZBA record also included many letters, e-mails and memos from
citizens, with all but one favoring the grant of the special use request.
37.   The Village staff reported to the ZBA that, after reviewing the
application, the Village Engineer concluded that a formal traffic and parking
study would not be required for the proposed special use, having determined
that the use will not increase or cause traffic congestion in the business
district.
38.   Three other persons testified in support of the application: (1) Libby
Graham, 1168 Hamptondale, the owner of a small service business who had located
in another town rather than apply for a special use permit in the C-2 Overlay;
(2) Roberta Rubin, owner of the Bookstall, who explained the need for creative
solutions to save retail uses and expressed support for the application; and
(3) Elise Covey, 1503 Edgewood Lane, a long-time customer who expressed her
hope that The New England Trader  would remain in the Village.
39.   There was no opposing testimony at the Zoning Board of Appeals hearing.
40.   None of the owners of the properties located within 250 feet of the
Subject Property submitted any evidence or requested an opportunity to cross-
examine witnesses at either the Zoning Board of Appeals hearing or at the Plan
Commission proceedings.
41.   At the completion of the ZBA hearing, a motion to recommend approval of
the special use request passed unanimously, with the ZBA entering favorable
findings on all of the special use standards in Section 17.56.010 (L) of the
Zoning Ordinance.
42.   The Zoning Board of Appeals did not enter any findings on the additional
standards for special uses in the C-2 Overlay District, as set out in Section
17.44.010 (B)(2) of the Zoning Ordinance.
43.   At its regular meeting on April 26, 2006, the Winnetka Plan Commission
considered the special use permit application for consistency with the
Comprehensive Plan, Winnetka 2020.
44.   At the Plan Commission meeting, Jean Wright, Ted Argiris and David
Schrauth presented further detail about the requested special use and provided
further explanation of how the space would be reconfigured.
45.   The Plan Commission also heard comments from Elise Covey and Cheryl
Chambers, who both spoke in support of the requested special use.
46.   Cheryl Chambers, of Chambers Cross Real Estate at 567 Lincoln, stated
that her business is in a situation similar to Jean Wright Real Estate and
would like the opportunity to make the same adjustments to adjacent space to
accommodate the space needs of her office.
47.   Upon the close of the public comments on the application, Plan Commission
member Midge Powell recused herself from the proceedings.
48.   The ten remaining Plan Commission members in attendance then considered
the entire record and entered its findings, finding that the proposed special
use is consistent with some provisions of the Comprehensive Plan, but
inconsistent with others.
49.   The Plan Commission found the proposed special use to be consistent with
the following goals and objectives of the Winnetka Comprehensive Plan:
a.   to ensure that commercial, institutional, and residential development is
appropriate to the character of, and minimizes the adverse impact on, its
surrounding neighborhood;
b.   to limit commercial, institutional, and residential development within the
Village to minimize potentially adverse impacts on adjacent residential
neighborhoods and to prevent the need for significant increases in
infrastructure and other community resources;
c.   to ensure that development proposals minimize the potential adverse impact
they might have on residential neighborhoods, including the impact on
pedestrian character, on-site parking, traffic patterns, congestion, open
space, storm water management and Village infrastructure;
d.   to provide for a wide range of office/service and retail commercial land
uses and development within the existing business districts in the Green Bay
Road Corridor; and
e.   to ensure that new development in business districts does not decrease
public parking supply, particularly on-street parking that supports retail use.
50.   The Plan Commission also found that the proposed special use is not
consistent with the following goals and objectives of the Winnetka
Comprehensive Plan:
a.   to promote a strong community identity and opportunities to interact,
while building a healthy commercial tax base, and to provide a broad range of
goods and services so that Winnetka can satisfy most of their ordinary shopping
requirements in the Village, and so that non-residents will come to the Village
for specialty goods and services; and
b.   to maintain the essential quality, viability and attractiveness of
Winnetka’s districts while encouraging new economic development consistent with
the character of the Village and the individual business districts.
51.   After the Plan Commission made its findings, the ten participating
members considered a resolution that would find that the proposed special use
permit application to be consistent with the Village of Winnetka Comprehensive
Plan and that would recommend that it be granted. The motion failed, with four
of the members voting for the resolution, and six of the members voting against
it.
52.   The application was placed on the Village Council’s agenda for
consideration at the regular Council meeting on June 6, 2006.
53.   The detailed minutes, findings and conclusions of the Zoning Board of
Appeals and Plan Commission were submitted to the Council, along with the
documentary record. Those materials consist of the following:
d.   the application for special use;
e.   a plat of survey depicting the location of the subject Property;
f.   scale drawings of the floor plan of the portion of the Subject Property
that is the subject of the special use and the adjacent retail portion;
g.   minutes of the April 10, 2006, Zoning Board of Appeals hearing;
h.   copies of 41 letters and emails from area residents and Winnetka Chamber
of Commerce members in support of the special use application;
i.   minutes of the April 26, 2006, Plan Commission meeting; and
j.   a GIS photograph of the location of the Subject Property.
54.   The Village Council also heard and considered the statements of Jean
Wright, Ted Argiris and the Applicant’s attorney in support of the special use
request.
55.   The Village Council also heard public comment from several members of the
audience.
II.   CONCLUSIONS
56.   The proceedings of the Zoning Board of Appeals and Plan Commission
conformed with all requirements of their procedural rules, the Winnetka Village
Code and applicable statutes of the State of Illinois
57.   The record establishes the following facts:
a.   The New England Trader is an established business in the Village, and has
been operated by Mr. Argiris for 27 years.
b.   Mr. Argiris requested a reduction in the amount of his retail space from
Ms. Wright so he could continue his business in the same place with a smaller
inventory.
c.   The New England Trader is currently closed. Mr. Argiris sold his fall and
winter inventories and canceled orders for spring and summer inventory because
the special use application was pending.
d.   Jean Wright Real Estate will use the expanded space for additional
conference rooms and storage.
e.   The reconfiguration of the space will cost approximately $100,000 and will
be permanent.
f.   The current Jean Wright Real Estate office space at 559 Chestnut has an
area of 1,872 square feet.
g.   Jean Wright Real Estate also occupies 544 square feet at the rear of 557
Chestnut, which space consists of storage space and toilets.
h.   The New England Trader occupies 1,453 square feet at 557 Chestnut, which
consists of the retail sales space, inventory storage space and fitting rooms.
The New England Trader also has access to the toilets at the rear of 557
Chestnut.
i.    The proposed plans call for the reconfiguration of the space as follows:
(i) reducing The New England Trader’s retail sales space at the front of 557
Chestnut to 565 square feet; (ii) breaking through the wall between 557
Chestnut and 559 Chestnut to connect the current Jean Wright Real Estate space
at 559 Chestnut with an additional 982 square feet at 557 Chestnut; and (iii)
creating a 450-square foot space consisting of a corridor from the retail space
to the rear of the building. The corridor will include the toilet and will be
part of the New England Trader lease space.
j.   The display window, façade and signage for 557 Chestnut will not change,
although not as much of the interior of the space will be visible from the
street, because the depth of the space will be reduced to approximately 21.5
feet.
k.   Jean Wright has received inquiries about renting the space at 557 Chestnut
for retail use in its current configuration.
l.   There is retail space available in the immediate vicinity in a size that
would suit Mr. Argiris’s needs, but the rents are not as favorable as the rents
he currently pays.
58.   The Village Council has not received any prior requests for special use
permits for office uses on the first floor in the C-2 Overlay District in the
West Elm Street Business District.
59.   To the extent that the Plan Commission’s favorable findings pertain to
the relationship between commercial and residential uses and to the impact of
commercial uses on residential neighborhoods, the findings are irrelevant,
because the Subject Property is in the center of the C-2 Overlay District and
the closest residential neighborhoods are more than a block away and are not
affected by the Subject Property.
60.   The ZBA’s findings that the Applicant has met all of the standards for
the requested special use permit are not supported by the record. The sworn
testimony and documentary evidence are insufficient to establish that the
applicant satisfies all of the Zoning Ordinance's requirements for the granting
of a special use in the C-2 overlay district, as those requirements are set
forth in Section 17.40.010 (B)(2) and Section 17.56.010 (L) of the Winnetka
Village Code.
61.   The ZBA’s findings that the use of the Subject Property will not change
are not supported by the record, as the permanent conversion of a substantial
portion of 557 Chestnut to make it a permanent part of the office space at 559
Chestnut is itself a change in the use of the property.
62.   The permanent nature of the proposed changes would make it cost
prohibitive to restore 557 Chestnut to retail use if The New England Trader did
not reopen or did not survive in the smaller space.
63.   While the record establishes that The New England Trader may no longer be
a viable business if it continues to operate in the larger space with higher
rent and the larger inventories previously maintained, the record does not
establish that a down-sized business, with a reduced inventory to be sold at
higher prices in a smaller space, is likely to succeed.
64.   No special use permit is needed for the Applicant and The New England
Trader to negotiate a modified lease with lower rents, thereby allowing The New
England Trader to reduce its overhead and to operate with a reduced inventory
without making permanent changes to the space.
65.   The proposed change is not necessary to allow the Applicant to lease the
property, as the Applicant has received inquiries about leasing the property as
is for retail purposes 
66.   The proposed use is contrary to the trend of development in the area, in
that all street level development in the immediate vicinity of the Subject
Property within the C-2 Overlay District has been for retail uses.
67.   The proposed special use will impede the normal and orderly development
and improvement of other property in the immediate vicinity for uses permitted
by right in the C-2 Commercial Overlay Zoning District, because the proposed
expanded office space comes at the permanent expense of existing retail space.
68.   Granting the special use will encourage further erosion of the C-
2 Overlay District, as evidenced by statements in the record of another real
estate office that would like to make a similar expansion to its office space.
69.   Because Jean Wright Real Estate will not be adding any employees to
occupy the expanded office space, the ZBA’s finding that the level of traffic
may decrease slightly due to the reduction in retail space indicates that
granting the special use would have a negative impact on retail traffic in the
neighborhood, which is potentially detrimental to neighboring businesses in the
heart of the West Elm Street Business District.
70.   Although the retail portions of the proposed reconfigured space would be
located adjacent to the sidewalk, would maintain the current length of the
retail frontage, and would meet the minimum gross floor area requirement of at
least four hundred (400) square feet, as required by Section 17.44.010 (B)(2)
(a)(iv) of the Zoning Ordinance, there is no evidence in the record that the
resulting retail space would have market viability for retail use in the event
The New England Trader no longer occupies the space.
71.   Because of the permanent nature of the changes needed to connect the 557
Chestnut space to the 559 Chestnut space, because of the minimal size of the
reconfigured retail space, and because new partitions in the 557 Chestnut space
would make it easy to further expand the office space into the 557 Chestnut
space, granting the special use is likely to encourage further expansion of
similar office spaces and thereby erode the retail base of the Village’s
commercial districts.
72.   Based on all of the foregoing, the Council finds that the Applicant has
not presented evidence to establish that the proposed office expansion at the
proposed location will encourage, facilitate and enhance the continuity,
concentration, and pedestrian nature of the West Elm Street Business District
in a manner similar to that of retail uses of a comparison shopping nature.
73.   Based on all of the foregoing, the Council finds that the Applicant has
not met her burden of establishing by competent, credible evidence that the
establishment, maintenance and operation of the special use will not be
detrimental to or endanger the public health, safety, comfort, morals or
general welfare.
74.   Based on all of the foregoing, the Council finds that the Applicant has
not met her burden of establishing by competent, credible evidence that the
special use will not be substantially injurious to the use and enjoyment of
other property in the immediate vicinity for uses that are permitted by right
in the C-2 General Retail Commercial Zoning District.
75.   Based on all of the foregoing, the Council finds that the Applicant has
not met her burden of establishing by competent, credible evidence that the
proposed special use will not substantially diminish or impair property values
in the immediate vicinity.
76.   Based on all of the foregoing, the Council finds that the Applicant has
not met her burden of establishing by competent, credible evidence that
granting the special use will not impede the normal and orderly development or
improvement of other property in the immediate vicinity for uses permitted by
right in the C-2 General Retail Commercial Zoning District and the C-2 Retail
Overly District.
77.   Based on all of the foregoing, the Council finds that the proposed
special use is not consistent with the Comprehensive Plan, because the
permanent conversion of retail space to office space within the C-2 Retail
Overlay District would erode the retail nature of the core of the West Elm
Street Business District.
78.   Based on all of the foregoing, the Council finds that the Plan Commission
correctly found that the proposed use is not consistent with the Comprehensive
Plan.
FOR ALL OF THE FOREGOING REASONS, the Village Council rejects the
recommendation of the Zoning Board of Appeals and the special use permit is
accordingly denied.
   ADOPTED this 20th day of June, 2006, by the following roll call vote of the
Council of the Village of Winnetka.
   AYES:   Trustees Abell, Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell and Tucker
   NAYS:   None
   ABSENT   None
                  Signed:
                     Edmund C. Woodbury
                     Village President
Attest:
Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
ORDINANCE NO. M-11-2006
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (983 Vine)
   WHEREAS, the Village of Winnetka is a home rule municipality in accordance
with Article VII, Section 6 of the Constitution of the State of Illinois of
1970; and
   WHEREAS, pursuant to Article VII, Section 6 of the Constitution of the State
of Illinois of 1970, the Village of Winnetka has the authority, except as
limited by said Section 6 of Article VII, to exercise any power and perform any
function pertaining to the government and affairs of the Village, including,
but not limited to, the powers (i) to regulate for the protection of the public
health, safety, morals and welfare, (ii) to license, (iii) to tax, and (iv) to
incur debt; and
WHEREAS, the Village Council find that establishing standards for the use and
development of lands and buildings within the Village and establishing and
applying criteria for variations from those standards are matters pertaining to
the affairs of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
That part of Lots 4 and 5 of John Curres’ Resubdivision of Lot 3 (except the
North 20 feet thereof) in Herrick’s Resubdivision of that part of the County
clerk’s Division of the Southeast Quarter of Section 17, township 42 North,
Range 13, East of the Third Principal Meridian, lying North of the Northerly
line of Lots 22, 33 and 34 in said County Clerk’s Division described as
follows: beginning at a point on the North line of said Lot 4, said point being
15 feet East of the Northwest corner of said Lot; thence East along the North
line of said Lots 4 and 5, a distance of 35.1 feet; Thence southeasterly 141
feet to a point on the Southeasterly line of said Lot 5 which is 4.5 feet
Southwesterly from the Southeasterly corner of said Lot 5; Thence Southwesterly
along the Southeasterly line of said Lots 4 and 5; Thence Southwesterly along
the Southeasterly line of said Lots 4 and 5 to a point 22.5 feet Northeasterly
from the Southwesterly corner of said Lot 4; thence Northwesterly 165.5 feet
(164.0 feet measured) to the place of beginning, in Cook County, Illinois,
commonly known as 983 Vine Street, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the B-
1 Multifamily Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.32 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for a
variation from the restrictions on nonconforming uses in order to permit the
construction of a new entry porch on a single family residence; and
   WHEREAS, on May 8, 2006, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of Appeals
conducted a public hearing on the requested variation and, by the unanimous
vote of the 5 members then present, has reported to the Council recommending
that the requested variation be granted; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties associated with carrying out the
strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect to the Subject Property
in that: (a) the Subject Property is an interior lot located on the north side
of Vine Street, west of Green Bay Road; (b) the property immediately to the
west of the Subject Property is located in the R-5 Single-Family Residential
Zoning District; (c) the Subject Property is improved with a single-family
residence which was constructed in 1922, prior to the enactment of the Zoning
Ordinance in 1925; (d) the existing single-family residence is a legal,
nonconforming use in the B-1 Multifamily Residential District; (e) because the
house on the Subject Property is a legal, nonconforming single-family use in
the multifamily zoning district, any structural alteration or enlargement to
the house requires a zoning variation; and (f) although the proposed new porch
will add a roof over the front entrance and will enlarge the footprint of the
porch and stairs by 39.22 square feet, the proposed structural change is
minimal and does not involve either structural changes to other parts of the
house or an expansion of the established nonconforming use; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that:
(a) the existing stone porch on the front of the home is in a state of
disrepair, is unsafe, and needs to be replaced; (b) the remainder of the home
is in sound structural condition; (c) the proposed porch will provide a safer
entrance to the home without expanding the nonconforming use; (d) the proposed
construction is not significant enough to preclude the eventual redevelopment
of the Subject Property with a multifamily use; (e) the Subject Property is
approximately 55 feet wide and has a lot area of 7,153.18 square feet, making
it difficult to develop for multi-family use without combining with other
properties; and (f) the relatively recent sale of the Subject Property for
continued single-family use indicates that redevelopment of the Subject
Property for multifamily use is not a reasonable probability at this time; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because the proposed new front porch is in keeping with the
style of the existing residence; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not impair an adequate supply of light
and air because the proposed new porch roof will be the same height as the
existing flat sunroom roof on the front of the home; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property, as the proposed construction will comply
with all applicable building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed construction will not contribute to congestion on the
public streets, as use of the Subject Property will not change and no expansion
of living areas in the home is proposed; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the requested variation will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variation is in harmony with the general purpose and
intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that it allows the existing legal,
nonconforming use to continue in a safe manner, without making structural
changes that would be a practical deterrent to redevelopment of the Subject
Property with a conforming use and structure.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1:   The foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the findings
of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth herein.
   SECTION 2:   A variation from the restrictions on nonconforming uses of
nonconforming buildings established by Section 17.64.040 of the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance is hereby granted to the Subject Property, commonly known as 983
Vine, and located in the B-1 Multifamily Residential District provided in
Chapter 17.32 of the Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code,
to permit the construction of a new entry porch, in accordance with the plans
and elevations submitted with the application for variations.
   SECTION 3:   The variation hereby granted is conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4:   This Ordinance is passed by the Council of the Village of
Winnetka in the exercise of its home rule powers pursuant to Section 6 of
Article VII of the Illinois Constitution of 1970.
   SECTION 5:   This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon its passage,
approval and posting as provided by law.
PASSED this 6th day of June, 2006, pursuant to the following roll call vote:
AYES:    Trustees Abell, Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, Tucker
NAYS:    None
ABSENT:    None
APPROVED this 6th day of June, 2006.
   Signed:
   //s//Edmund C. Woodbury
   Village President
Countersigned:
//s//Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: Waived June 6, 2006
Posted:    
Passed and Approved: June 6, 2006
