ORDINANCE NO. M-12-2006
AN ORDINANCE AUTHORIZING THE DISPOSAL OF SURPLUS PERSONAL PROPERTY OF THE
VILLAGE OF WINNETKA
   WHEREAS, the Village of Winnetka owns various types of personal property,
such as office furniture, computer equipment, office equipment, heavy machinery
and tools, and electric transformers and vehicles (hereinafter referred to
collectively as “Personal Property”), which is used by the Village’s operating
departments in the day-to-day operations of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, from time to time, such Personal Property is retired from service
as it reaches the end of its useful life, or as it is replaced with newer, more
efficient products; and
   WHEREAS, from time to time, the Winnetka Police Department also comes into
possession of items of personal property that have been lost, found or stolen,
which personal property includes the bicycles described in Exhibit A, attached
to this ordinance (the “Bicycles”); and
   WHEREAS, said Bicycles have been in the possession of the Winnetka Police
Department for more than 6 months, during which time the Chief of Police of the
Winnetka Police Department has made reasonable inquiry and efforts to identify
and notify the owners or other persons entitled to possession of the Bicycles,
but has been unsuccessful in those attempts; and
   WHEREAS, pursuant to Section 3 of the Law Enforcement Disposition of
Property Act, 765 ILCS 1030/3, the Chief of Police has transferred the custody
of the Bicycles to the Village of Winnetka; and
   WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka (the “Village Council”) have
determined that Personal Property that is retired from service becomes surplus
property (“Surplus Personal Property”) because it is no longer useful to the
Village; and
   WHEREAS, the Village Council have also determined that, when property such
as the Bicycles is transferred from the Police Department to the Village
pursuant to the Law Enforcement Disposition of Property Act, that property also
becomes Surplus Personal Property, because it is not useful to the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the Village Council have also determined that storing Surplus
Personal Property is not in the best interests of the Village, because the
stored Surplus Personal Property (a) occupies space that could be used for
other Village purposes, (b) deprives the Village of the opportunity to gain
some income from the disposal of the Surplus Personal Property and (c) may
expose the Village’s employees to environmental hazards and liability,
particularly in the case of retired electric transformers that contain PCBs;
and
   WHEREAS, the Village of Winnetka is a home rule municipality in accordance
with Article VII, Section 6 of the Constitution of the State of Illinois of
1970; and
   WHEREAS, pursuant to Article VII, Section 6 of the Constitution of the State
of Illinois of 1970, the Village of Winnetka has the authority, except as
limited by said Section 6 of Article VII, to exercise any power and perform any
function pertaining to the government and affairs of the Village, including,
but not limited to, the powers to regulate for the protection of the public
health, safety, morals and welfare; and
   WHEREAS, the Village Council find (a) that the disposal of surplus Village
property, including the Surplus Personal Property described in this Ordinance,
is a matter pertaining to the affairs of the Village and (b) that the manner of
disposal of such property pertains to the public health, safety and general
welfare of the Village and its citizens; and
   WHEREAS, the Village Council have determined that disposal of the Surplus
Personal Property as provided in this Ordinance is necessary and proper to
avoid incurring unnecessary additional storage costs, unnecessary disposal
costs and unnecessary exposure to liability related to storing or disposing of
the Surplus Personal Property; and
   WHEREAS, the Police Department has requested the authority to dispose of the
Bicycles by auctioning them off on the internet; and
   WHEREAS, the Village Council has determined that it is in the best interests
of the Village to authorize the Village Manager to determine the most
appropriate methods for disposing of the Village’s Surplus Personal Property;
and
   WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka, in the exercise of its home
rule powers pursuant to Section 6 of Article VII of the Illinois Constitution
of 1970, have determined that it is in the best interests of the Village and
its citizens to dispose of the Surplus Personal Property in a manner consistent
with the provisions of Section 11-76-4 of the Illinois Municipal Code (65 ILCS
5/11-76-4), as more fully set forth in this Ordinance.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain:
   SECTION 1:   The Council of the Village of Winnetka find that the Bicycles
described in the attached Exhibits A and all other office furniture, computer
equipment, office equipment, heavy machinery and tools, and electric
transformers that will be retired from service to the Village during the
remainder of the 2006-2007 Fiscal Year (collectively, the “Surplus Personal
Property”), is not necessary or useful to the Village of Winnetka and that the
best interests of the Village of Winnetka will be served by the sale or other
disposition of such Surplus Personal Property as provided in this Ordinance.
   SECTION 2:   The Council of the Village of Winnetka further find that, based
on prior experience in disposing of similar items of property, the cost of
having the Village conduct a public sale of the Surplus Personal Property,
including the costs of advertising and publishing, and the costs of personnel
needed to maintain security and to conduct the public sale, exceeds the value
of such items.
   SECTION 3:   The Council of the Village of Winnetka further find that
selling surplus property by auctioning the property electronically over the
internet can be a cost-effective method of disposing of surplus property; and
   SECTION 4:   The Village Manager is hereby authorized and directed to
determine the value of and to dispose of the Surplus Personal Property in any
manner he deems reasonable under the circumstances, in accordance with the
following terms and conditions:
      A.   The Surplus Personal Property may be disposed of with or without
public notice.
      B.   The Surplus Personal Property may be disposed of by auction on the
internet, using the services of PropertyRoom.com, or another similar internet
site that performs such services for governmental entities.
      C.   The Surplus Personal Property may be converted into some other form
that is useful to the Village.
      D.   If any article of Surplus Personal Property is turned in or traded
in as part of the purchase price of a similar article, the purchase shall
comply with the purchasing requirements established by Chapter 4.12 of the
Winnetka Village Code, and the Purchasing Policy established pursuant thereto,
and any competitive bids taken for the purchase of the new article shall
include competitive bids for the turn-in or trade-in value.
      E.   The Surplus Personal Property may be disposed of by donating it to
another unit of local government, regardless of its location.
      F.   The Surplus Property may be donated to a not-for-profit
organization, provided the Village does not incur any costs in making such
donation.
      G.   All Surplus Personal Property that consists of electric transformers
that contain PCBs or that are contaminated by PCBs (“Surplus Transformers”)
shall be disposed of at the lowest cost to the Village, said cost being
determined with or without advertising; provided, that any person or entity
that disposes of or destroys any part of such Surplus Transformers shall
provide a sworn statement to the Village certifying that such disposal or
destruction complies with all applicable environmental laws and regulations.
      H.   Disposal of Surplus Personal Property shall be documented in a
written record that includes a statement of the property’s value, the date of
disposition and the method of disposition. The written record shall also
account for all costs incurred and revenues derived from the disposition of the
Surplus Personal Property.
   SECTION 5:   The Village Manager is hereby authorized to execute, on behalf
of the Village, such contracts and other documents as may be necessary to
effectuate the disposal of Surplus Personal Property pursuant to this
Ordinance.
   SECTION 6:   The Finance Director, subject to the direction of the Village
Manager, shall establish such internal procedures and inventory controls as may
be necessary to document the disposal of Surplus Personal Property in
accordance with this Ordinance and with applicable government finance
recordkeeping requirements.
   SECTION 7:   The authority granted by this Ordinance to dispose of Surplus
Personal Property shall apply to the Bicycles and to all Personal Property of
the Village that becomes Surplus Personal Property during the 2006-2007 Fiscal
Year.
   SECTION 8:   This Ordinance is adopted by the Council of the Village of
Winnetka in the exercise of its home rule powers pursuant to Section 6 of
Article VII of the Illinois Constitution of 1970.
   SECTION 9:   This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon its passage,
approval and posting as provided by law.
PASSED this 20th day of June, 2006, pursuant to the following roll call vote:
AYES:    Trustees Abell, Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, Tucker
NAYS:    None
ABSENT:    None
APPROVED this 20th day of June, 2006.
   Signed:
   //s//Edmund C. Woodbury
   Village President
Countersigned:
//s//Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
