ORDINANCE NO. M-17-2006
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (1258 Scott)
   WHEREAS, the Village of Winnetka is a home rule municipality in accordance
with Article VII, Section 6 of the Constitution of the State of Illinois of
1970; and
   WHEREAS, pursuant to Article VII, Section 6 of the Constitution of the State
of Illinois of 1970, the Village of Winnetka has the authority, except as
limited by said Section 6 of Article VII, to exercise any power and perform any
function pertaining to the government and affairs of the Village, including,
but not limited to, the powers (i) to regulate for the protection of the public
health, safety, morals and welfare, (ii) to license, (iii) to tax, and (iv) to
incur debt; and
   WHEREAS, the Village Council find that establishing standards for the use
and development of lands and buildings within the Village and establishing and
applying criteria for variations from those standards are matters pertaining to
the affairs of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, there has been presented to the Council of the Village of Winnetka,
Cook County, Illinois, the request of the owner of the following described real
estate (the “Subject Property”):
Lot 2 in Kulwin-Beider Resubdivision of Lots 5, 6, 7 and 8 in Block 12 in
Chicago North Shoreland Company’s Subdivision in Sections 17 and 18, Township
42 North, Range 13, East of the Third Principal Meridian, in Cook County,
Illinois,
commonly known as 1258 Scott Avenue, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the R-
5 Single-Family Residential Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.12 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, for the
following variations from requirements of the Lot, Space, Bulk and Yard
Regulations for Single Family Residential Districts established by Section
17.30 of the Zoning Ordinance: (a) a variation from the roofed lot coverage
limitations of Section 17.30.030 to allow a roofed lot coverage of 2,602.51
square feet, which exceeds the maximum allowed roofed lot coverage of 2,554.08
square feet, resulting in a variation of 48.43 square feet (1.9%); (b) a
variation from the maximum building size limitations of Section 17.30.040 to
permit a gross floor area of 4,309.66 square feet, which exceeds the maximum
allowed gross floor area of 3,858.26 square feet, resulting in a variation of
451.4 square feet (11.7%); and (c) a variation from the side yard setback
requirements of Section 17.30.060 to allow a sum of total side yard setbacks of
13.31 feet, rather than the required minimum of 16 feet, resulting in a
variation of 2.69 feet, (16.81%), all to allow the installation of a new roof
with a steeper pitch, an expansion of the roofed front porch, the addition of a
third walk-in closet for the master suite, as well as a third bathroom and
laundry area on the second floor; and
   WHEREAS, on July 10, 2006, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variations and, by the
unanimous vote of the 6 members then present, has reported to the Council
recommending that the requested roofed lot coverage and side yard setback
variations be granted; and
   WHEREAS, at its July 10, 2006, meeting, the Zoning Board of Appeals took a
separate vote on the requested gross floor area variation, and with 4 members
voting for the variation and 2 members voting against it, has reported to the
Council recommending that the requested gross floor area variation be granted;
and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties associated with carrying out the
strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect to the Subject Property
in that: (a) the single family home on the Subject Property was constructed in
1978, prior to the imposition of roofed lot coverage, gross floor area
limitations and building line articulation requirements, and prior to the
enactment of increased side yard setback requirements; (b) the single family
home on the Subject Property has a legally nonconforming roofed lot coverage of
2,627.53 square feet, and a legally nonconforming gross floor area of 4,176.37
square feet; (c) the single family home on the Subject Property also has a west
side yard of approximately 6.61 feet and an east side yard of 6.70 feet,
resulting in total side yards of 13.31 feet and a 2.69-foot legal nonconformity
in the west side yard; (d) the existing residence has a low-pitched roof that
does not drain well and is in a deteriorating condition; (e) the small front
porch is set back at the center of the home, under the north wall of the second
floor and is therefore included in the gross floor area; (f) due to the
nonconforming side yard, it is impossible to replace the roof without a
variation; (g) the proposed 3.75-foot extension of the front porch will
increase its functionality; (h) the proposed 5.75-foot extension of the second
floor’s front building line on the east side of the home will create space for
the addition of the third bathroom and will provide vertical articulation in
the front of the home without changing the building’s footprint; (i) the
proposed change in the pitch of the garage roof will create space for the
additional master suite closet without changing the building’s footprint; (j)
the proposed change in the pitch of the roof increases the portions of the
upper floor that contribute to gross floor area, but also result in the
application of the attic floor area bonus; (k) the proposed changes also
include the removal of a front bay window on the east side of the home and the
removal of a storage shed, both of which contribute to roofed lot coverage and
gross floor area; and (l) the proposed changes will result in a net reduction
of roofed lot coverage by 25.02 square feet and a net increase in gross floor
area of 133.09 square feet; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because the remodeled home will maintain its existing width
and location in relation to the neighboring properties and to the block face,
and will comply with the applicable building height and front setback
limitations; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not impair an adequate supply of
light and air because the proposed improvements will conform with height
requirements and will not encroach further into the setbacks, and because the
steeper pitch of the roof will set the higher parts of the building back from
the lot lines; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property, as the proposed construction will comply
with all applicable building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not diminish the taxable value of
land and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed construction will not contribute to congestion on the
public streets, as the property will continue to be used for single family
residential purposes; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the requested variations will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations are in harmony with the general purpose
and intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that they allow the renovation,
restoration and rehabilitation of a structurally sound existing building in a
way that improves its appearance within the neighborhood ,while maintaining the
existing scale and appearance of the community and protecting established trees
and landscaping.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1:   The foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the findings
of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth herein.
   SECTION 2:   The following variations from the Lot, Space, Bulk and Yard
Regulations for Single Family Residential Districts established by Section
17.30 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance are hereby granted to the Subject
Property, commonly known as 1258 Scott Street and located in the R-5 Single-
Family Residential District provided in Chapter 17.12 of the Zoning Ordinance,
Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code: (a) a variation from the roofed lot
coverage limitations of Section 17.30.030 to allow a roofed lot coverage of
2,602.51 square feet, which exceeds the maximum allowed roofed lot coverage of
2,554.08 square feet, resulting in a variation of 48.43 square feet (1.9%); (b)
a variation from the maximum building size limitations of Section 17.30.040 to
permit a gross floor area of 4,309.66 square feet, which exceeds the maximum
allowed gross floor area of 3,858.26 square feet, resulting in a variation of
451.4 square feet (11.7%); and (c) a variation from the side yard setback
requirements of Section 17.30.060 to allow a sum of total side yard setbacks of
13.31 feet, rather than the required minimum of 16 feet, resulting in a
variation of 2.69 feet, (16.81%), all to allow the installation of a new roof
with a steeper pitch, an expansion of the roofed front porch, the addition of a
third walk-in closet for the master suite, as well as a third bathroom and
laundry area on the second floor, all in accordance with the plans and
elevations submitted with the application for variations.
   SECTION 3:   The variations hereby granted are conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4:   This Ordinance is passed by the Council of the Village of
Winnetka in the exercise of its home rule powers pursuant to Section 6 of
Article VII of the Illinois Constitution of 1970.
   SECTION 5:   This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon its passage,
approval and posting as provided by law.
PASSED this 5th day of September, 2006, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
AYES:    Trustees Eilers, Berger, Abell and President Woodbury
NAYS:    Trustees Behles, Ritchell and Tucker
ABSENT: None
APPROVED this 5th day of September, 2006.
   Signed:
   //s//Edmund C. Woodbury
   Village President
Countersigned:
//s//Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced:    August 15, 2006   
Posted:    August 16, 2006   
Passed and Approved:    September 5, 2006   
