ORDINANCE NO. M-20-2006
AN ORDINANCE ESTABLISHING THE RESIDENCE AT 630 PINE LANE AS A DESIGNATED
LANDMARK
   WHEREAS, the Village of Winnetka desires to identify, designate and preserve
buildings and structures in the Village that are historically, culturally, and
architecturally significant and to encourage the restoration and rehabilitation
of those buildings and structures; and
   WHEREAS, pursuant to Sections 11-48.2-1, et seq. of the Illinois Municipal
Code (65 ILCS 5/11-48.2-1, et seq.), the Council of the Village of Winnetka
enacted Chapter 15.64 of Title 15 of the Winnetka Village Code, titled
“Landmark Preservation,” for the purposes of preserving, protecting, enhancing,
rehabilitating and regulating buildings, structures, objects, and places of
historical, cultural or architectural importance; and
   WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka have determined that it is
in the best interests of the Village and its residents that participation in
the landmark designation process shall be limited to those owners who
voluntarily elect to participate in that process; and
   WHEREAS, on September 18, 2005, the Owner submitted an application for the
certified landmark designation of the residence on the property located at 630
Pine Lane permanent real estate index number 05-17-312-037-0000 (“Subject
Property”); and
   WHEREAS, pursuant to the provisions of Chapter 15.64 of the Winnetka Village
Code (the “Landmark Ordinance”) and notice duly published and sent, the
Landmark Preservation Commission conducted public hearings on October 3, 2005,
and November 7, 2005, to consider the application for certified landmark
designation; and
   WHEREAS, at its October 3, 2005, meeting the Landmark Preservation
Commission expressed concerns about the size and scale of the north wing
addition to the residence, and to the materials the Owner proposed to use; and
   WHEREAS, on October 27, 2005, the Owner submitted a modified plan that
addressed the Landmark Preservation Commission’s concerns, reducing the size
and shifting the location of the proposed addition, and proposing to replace
the asphalt shingle roof with cedar shingles; and
   WHEREAS, on November 7, 2005, the Landmark Preservation Commission
considered the applicant’s modified plan and unanimously recommended that the
residence on the Subject Property be designated a Winnetka landmark; and
   WHEREAS, on November 7, 2005, the Landmark Preservation Commission also
recommended approval of the alterations, as presented to the Plan Commission in
the modified plans dated October 27, 2005; and
   WHEREAS, the Landmark Preservation Commission applied the Village’s System
for the Evaluation of Landmarks to the information received at the hearing and
made findings regarding the property at 630 Pine Lane, including the following:
      (1)   The architectural type is rare, in that (a) the Francis & Deborah
Butler house was constructed in 1922 in a Colonial Revival style; (b) the
symmetrical center entry is flanked by bay windows on either side; (c) the
front door is flanked with pilasters and sidelights, and is topped by an
elliptical fanlight; (d) the main block of the residence is a two-story, side-
gabled structure; (e) the wooden windows are double-hung sashes in an eight-
over-eight configuration, topped with “jack” arches and have wooden louvered
shutters; and (f) although there were some alterations to the residence in 1935
and 1949, there have been no changes to the house in the past 55 years except
for replacement of the roof;
      (2)   The method of construction is somewhat rare in that it is in the
typical style of Colonial Revival style homes built in the 1920’s;
      (3)   The residence is associated with a well-known architect, in that it
was designed by Edwin Clark and Chester Wolcott in 1922, and Edwin Clark
designed many prominent buildings in Chicago and the North Shore, among them
the Winnetka Village Hall, the North Shore Country Day School, the Indian Hill
Country Club, Plaza del Lago shopping center, and the Reptile and Primate
houses at Lincoln Park Zoo; and
   WHEREAS, based on its findings, the Landmark Preservation Commission has
concluded that the residence located at 630 Pine Lane is unique and meets the
criteria of the Landmark Ordinance and the Commission members present at the
November 7, 2005, meeting have unanimously recommended that it be so
designated; and
   WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka have considered the
recommendation of the Landmark Preservation Commission and have determined that
it is in the best interest of the Village and its residents to establish the
house located at 630 Pine Lane as a designated landmark, because not only is it
significant in its style and design, but it also is associated with Edwin Clark
and is part of a body of work that has significantly contributed to the
architectural character of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the Village of Winnetka is a home rule municipality in accordance
with Article VII, Section 6 of the Constitution of the State of Illinois of
1970; and
   WHEREAS, pursuant to Article VII, Section 6 of the Constitution of the State
of Illinois of 1970, the Village of Winnetka has the authority, except as
limited by said Section 6 of Article VII, to exercise any power and perform any
function pertaining to the government and affairs of the Village, including,
but not limited to, the power to regulate for the protection of the public
health, safety, morals and welfare; and
   WHEREAS, the Village Council find that establishing standards for
identifying, designating and preserving buildings and structures in the Village
that are historically, culturally and architecturally significant promotes the
welfare of the Village and is a matter pertaining to the affairs of the
Village.
   NOW THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain:
   SECTION 1:   That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth
herein.
   SECTION 2:   Subject to the terms and conditions set forth in Sections 3 and
4 of this Ordinance, the residence located on the property located at 630 Pine
Lane, permanent real estate index number 05-17-312-037-0000, is hereby
designated a certified landmark under Section 15.04.070 the Landmark
Preservation Ordinance, in accordance with the findings and recommendations of
the Landmark Preservation Commission.
   SECTION 3:   The Owner shall complete the documentation of the certified
landmark status required by Section 15.04.070 by signing a declaration of
certified status and submitting it to the Village for recording with the Cook
County Recorder of deeds within 30 days after the passage of this Ordinance.
   SECTION 4:   No permits shall be issued for any work on any portion of the
Subject Property until the Village has received the signed declaration required
pursuant to the foregoing Section 3.
   SECTION 5:   This Ordinance is passed by the Council of the Village of
Winnetka in the exercise of its home rule powers pursuant to Section 6 of
Article VII of the Illinois Constitution of 1970.
   SECTION 6:   This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon its passage,
approval and posting as provided by law.
PASSED this 7th day of November, 2006, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
