ORDINANCE NO. M-5-2007
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA, COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (1249 Scott)
WHEREAS, the property commonly known as 1249 Scott Avenue, Winnetka, Illinois,
(the “Subject Property”) is legally described as follows:
The West half of Lot 5 in Block 9 in Lloyd Subdivision of Blocks 1,2,3,4, and 5
of Taylor’s Second Addition to Taylorsport, being a subdivision of part of the
Northeast quarter of Section 18 and part of the Southwest quarter of Section
17, also the East 24 acres of the North 48 acres of the Northeast quarter of
Section 18, all in Township 42 North, Range 13 East of the Third Principal
Meridian, in Cook County, Illinois; and
WHEREAS, the Subject Property is located in the R-5 Single Family Zoning
District provided in Chapter 17.12 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17
of the Winnetka Village Code; and
WHEREAS, on January 31, 2007, the owner of the Subject Property filed an
application for the following variations from requirements of the Lot, Space,
Bulk and Yard Regulations for Single Family Residential Districts established
by Chapter 17.30 of the Zoning Ordinance: (a) a variation of 204.98 square feet
(10.08%) from the intensity of use of lot provisions of Section 17.30.030 to
allow a roofed lot coverage of 2,238.23 square feet, whereas a maximum of
2,033.25 square feet is permitted; (b) a variation of 472.74 square feet
(14.53%) from the maximum building size limitations of Section 17.30.040, to
allow a gross floor area of 3,725.94 square feet, whereas a maximum of 3,253.2
square feet is permitted; (c) a variation of 6.42 feet (16.77%) from the front
setback requirements of Section 17.50.050 to allow a front setback of 31.86
feet, whereas a minimum of 38.28 feet is required; and (d) a variation 0.08
feet (1%) from the side yard setback requirements of Section 17.30.060 to allow
the east side yard setback to measure 7.92 feet, whereas a minimum of 8 feet is
required; all of said variations being requested to permit the construction of
an attached two-car tandem garage and a one-story, covered front entrance at
the southeast corner of the existing house; and
WHEREAS, on March 12, 2007, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of Appeals
held a public hearing on the requested variations and, by the unanimous vote of
the five members then present, has reported to the Council recommending that
the requested variations be granted; and
WHEREAS, in 2006, the owners of the Subject Property were denied variations for
the proposed construction of an attached two-car garage; and
WHEREAS, the current application entirely eliminates the need for a variation
from the front yard lot coverage limitations, whereas the prior application
requested a variation of 147.77 square feet (25.73%), entirely eliminates the
front-facing garage door variation, whereas the prior application requested a
variation of 7.0 feet (77.78%), and virtually eliminates the need for the east
side yard variation, which has been reduced from a requested variation of 7.00
feet (87.5%) to 0.08 feet (1.0%), while generating a request for only minor
increases in the roofed lot coverage, gross floor area and front yard setback
variations; and
WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and particular hardships associated
with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect
to the Subject Property in that: (a) the existing single-family home was
constructed in 1956; (b) due to amendments to the Zoning Ordinance since the
house was constructed, there are several existing legal nonconformities on the
Subject Property, including both side yard setbacks; (c) the proposed garage
will replace an existing carport and storage shed currently located at the east
side of the house, adjacent to the existing entrance to the house; (d) there is
no conforming alternative for a garage, because the existing west side yard and
the rear portion of the east side yard are both less than five feet wide, which
is insufficient width to allow for the construction of a driveway and detached
garage in the rear of the Subject Property; (e) the owner proposes to construct
the new garage in the area of the existing carport, where the existing side
yard setback is greater, resulting in a de minimis side yard variation request
of 0.08 feet; (f) the enclosed front entrance is a minimal intrusion into the
front yard and is necessary because the attached garage will enclose the
existing main entrance; (g) the proposed garage and front entrance addition
eliminate the substantial portions of the variations that had been requested
and denied in 2006; and (h) replacing the carport and storage shed with the
attached garage will enclose the storage areas that are now visible from the
street, thereby improving the appearance of both the Subject Property and the
immediate neighborhood; and
WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted to
be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance because: (a)
the Subject Property does not have a garage; (b) while the existing carport
provides some shelter for a single vehicle and the storage shed provides some
enclosed storage, the open carport leaves both the vehicle and storage visible
from the street, to the detriment of the Subject Property; (c) the attached
garage cannot be constructed without adding a new front entrance to the home,
as the enclosed garage would render the current entrance invisible from the
street; and (d) the current application addresses the principal flaws of the
prior application and confirms that there is no conforming location for the
construction of a detached garage; and
WHEREAS, the requested variations will not alter the essential character of the
neighborhood because the proposed additions (a) are consistent with the
architectural style of the existing house, (b) have a minimal appearance from
the street because of the single-door access to the tandem garage, and (c)
replace an open carport and side yard storage shed with a garage, making the
appearance of the Subject Property more consistent with the appearance of the
other properties in the immediate vicinity; and
WHEREAS, the requested variations will not impair an adequate supply of light
and air because: (a) the proposed garage and front entrance are one-story
improvements that are lower than the main body of the house; (b) the tandem
garage will be constructed in the same area now occupied by a carport and
storage shed; and (c) the new tandem garage will be set further in from the
east building line and requires a variation of less than one-tenth of a foot;
and
WHEREAS, the requested variations will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property, as the proposed construction will comply
with all applicable building and fire protection codes; and
WHEREAS, the requested variations will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
WHEREAS, the proposed construction will not contribute to congestion on the
public streets, as the property will continue to be used for single family
residential purposes and will increase the amount of off-street parking on the
Subject Property; and
WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the requested variations will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village; and
WHEREAS, the Village of Winnetka is a home rule municipality in accordance with
Article VII, Section 6 of the Constitution of the State of Illinois of 1970,
with the authority, except as limited by said Section 6 of Article VII, to
exercise any power and perform any function pertaining to the government and
affairs of the Village, including, but not limited to, the powers to regulate
for the protection of the public health, safety, morals and welfare; and
WHEREAS, the Village Council find that establishing standards for the use and
development of lands and buildings within the Village and establishing and
applying criteria for variations from those standards are matters pertaining to
the affairs of the Village; and
WHEREAS, the requested variations are in harmony with the general purpose and
intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that they allow the renovation,
restoration and rehabilitation of a structurally sound existing building while
maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the community and protecting
established trees and landscaping.
NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
SECTION 1:   The foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the findings of
the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth herein.
SECTION 2:   The following variations from the Lot, Space, Bulk and Yard
Regulations for Single Family Residential Districts established by Section
17.30 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance are hereby granted to the Subject
Property, commonly known as 1249 Scott Avenue and located in the R-5 Single-
Family Residential District provided in Chapter 17.16 of the Winnetka Village
Code, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code: (a) a variation of 204.98 square
feet (10.08%) from the intensity of use of lot provisions of Section 17.30.030
to allow a roofed lot coverage of 2,238.23 square feet, whereas a maximum of
2,033.25 square feet is permitted; (b) a variation of 472.74 square feet
(14.53%) from the maximum building size limitations of Section 17.30.040, to
allow a gross floor area of 3,725.94 square feet, whereas a maximum of 3,253.2
square feet is permitted; (c) a variation of 6.42 feet (16.77%) from the front
setback requirements of Section 17.50.050 to allow a front setback of 31.86
feet, whereas a minimum of 38.28 feet is required; and (d) a variation 0.08
feet (1%) from the side yard setback requirements of Section 17.30.060 to allow
the east side yard setback to measure 7.92 feet, whereas a minimum of 8 feet is
required; all of said variations being requested to permit the construction of
an attached two-car tandem garage and a one-story, covered front entrance at
the southeast corner of the existing house, in accordance with the plans and
elevations submitted with the application for variations.
SECTION 3:   The variations hereby granted are conditioned upon the
commencement of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective
date of this Ordinance.
SECTION 4:   This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage,
approval and posting.
ADOPTED this 17th day of April, 2007, pursuant to the following roll call vote:
AYES:    Trustees Abell, Behles, Berger, Eilers, Ritchell, Tucker
NAYS:    None
ABSENT:    None
APPROVED this 17th day of April, 2007.
         Signed:
   
   //s//Edmund C. Woodbury
                  Village President
