ORDINANCE NO. M-9-2007
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA, COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (330 Willow)
WHEREAS, the Village of Winnetka is a home rule municipality in accordance with
Article VII, Section 6 of the Constitution of the State of Illinois of 1970,
pursuant to which it has the authority, except as limited by said Section 6 of
Article VII, to exercise any power and perform any function pertaining to the
government and affairs of the Village; and
WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka (“Village Council”) find that
establishing standards for the use and development of lands and buildings
within the Village and establishing and applying criteria for variations from
those standards are matters pertaining to the affairs of the Village; and
WHEREAS, the property commonly known as 330 Willow Road, Winnetka, Illinois
(the “Subject Property”), is legally described as follows:
Lots 1 and 2 (except therefrom that part of Lot 2 described as follows):
Beginning at the Northwest corner of said Lot 2 (being the Southeast corner of
Sheridan and Willow Roads) and running thence East along the North line of said
Lot 2, a distance of 348.81 feet, thence South at right angles to said North
line, 153.19 feet more or less to the South line of said Lot 2, thence West
along said South line 219.01 feet to the Southwest corner of said Lot 2 and
thence Northwesterly along the Westerly line of said Lot 2, a distance of 201
feet more or less to a point of beginning, in Block 1 in Dale’s Addition to
Winnetka, being a Subdivision of the North 7.5 chains of the Southeast
fractional ¼ of Section 21, Township 42 North, Range 13 East of the Third
Principal Meridian, in Cook County, Illinois;
Also: A strip of land Easterly of and adjoining the Easterly line of Lot 1 in
Block 1 aforesaid and extending to the shore of Lake Michigan and between the
Northerly and Southerly lines of Lot 1 extended East and Easterly to the shore
of said lake, all in Cook County, Illinois; and
WHEREAS, the Subject Property is located in the R-2 Zoning District provided in
Chapter 17.24 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka
Village Code; and
WHEREAS, on March 30, 2007, the owner of the Subject Property filed an
application for a variation from the requirements of the Lot, Space, Bulk and
Yard Regulations for Single Family Residential Districts established by Section
17.30 of the Zoning Ordinance, requesting a variation from the corner yard
setback requirements of Section 17.30.050 to allow two one-story additions to
be constructed at the northeast and northwest corners of the existing
residence, with the northeast corner of the northeast addition being located
3.83 feet from the property line that adjoins Willow Road, and the remainder of
the additions being located within the 41.02-foot corner setback line,
resulting in a variation of 37.19 feet (90.66%); and
WHEREAS, on May 7, 2007, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of Appeals
conducted a public hearing on the requested variation and, by the unanimous
vote of the six members then present, has reported to the Council recommending
that the requested variation be granted; and
WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and particular hardships associated
with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect
to the Subject Property in that: (a) the Subject Property is an irregularly
shaped lot that has its east lot line formed by the water’s edge of Lake
Michigan and its north lot line adjoining the south side of Willow Road; (b)
the Subject Property is a corner lot, because Lake Michigan is treated as a
public right of way for zoning purposes, with the 133.75-foot Lake Michigan
frontage being treated as the front lot line and the 221.19-foot Willow Road
frontage forming the corner lot line; (c) the Subject Property is subject to a
41.02-foot setback from the Willow Road corner lot line; (d) the Subject
Property is improved with a residence that was constructed in 1912, before
Willow Road was extended to Lake Michigan, and before the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance was enacted; (e) at the time the residence was constructed, the site
was a single, 82,340 square-foot lot that extended from Sheridan Road to the
water’s edge, and the residence was therefore oriented toward Sheridan Road;
(f) the residence was designed by Albert Altschuler, a well-known architect,
making the building architecturally significant; (g) in 1953, the Subject
Property was subdivided into its current 38,706 square-foot configuration; (h)
as a result of the 1953 subdivision of the Subject Property and the extension
of Willow Road to the water’s edge, the front lot line of the Subject Property
moved from Sheridan Road to the water’s edge, and the edge of the Willow Road
right-of-way became a corner lot line, creating a legally nonconforming north
corner yard setback of 7.19 feet; and (i) approximately one-third of the
residence currently sits in the defined corner side yard setback to the north
along Willow Road, making exterior alterations almost impossible without a
zoning variance; and
WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted to
be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that: (a)
the interior floor plan is not conducive to a contemporary lifestyle, as the
kitchen was designed for servants to live and work in, and was built at the
northern end of the home, away from the home’s dining and living areas; (b) the
north end of the home lacks adequate heat in the winter and adequate air
circulation in the summer; (c) the nonconformities on the Subject Property
cannot be cured without removing a substantial portion of the residence; (d)
altering the interior of the building is not reasonably possible, because the
original house was constructed with a heavy masonry exterior, plaster interior
masonry walls and structural concrete floors; (e) the proposed one-story
additions are adjacent to the kitchen and will improve the living space at that
the north end of the residence by adding a 166.68 square-foot mud room and
laundry at its northwest corner and a 147.6 square-foot playroom at its
northeast corner; (f) the proposed additions will allow for the installation of
insulation, windows and radiators to improve the comfort of the occupants; and
WHEREAS, the requested variation will not alter the essential character of the
neighborhood because the proposed addition is designed to blend with the
existing house using the same materials and architectural features of the
original house, without changing the original stained glass windows on the
front and rear facades of the house; and
WHEREAS, the requested variation will not impair an adequate supply of light
and air because: (a) the proposed additions will not be seen from any adjacent
properties; (b) the height of the additions is well below the applicable height
limitation; and (c) the resulting gross floor area, roofed lot coverage and
impermeable surface area will all be several thousand square feet under the
applicable limits; and
WHEREAS, the requested variation will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property, as the proposed construction will comply
with all applicable building and fire protection codes; and
WHEREAS, the requested variation will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
WHEREAS, the proposed construction will not contribute to congestion on the
public streets, as the property will continue to be used for single family
residential purposes; and
WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the requested variation will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village; and
WHEREAS, the requested variation is in harmony with the general purpose and
intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that they allow the renovation,
restoration and rehabilitation of a structurally sound and architecturally
significant existing building while maintaining the existing scale and
appearance of the community and protecting established trees and landscaping.
NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
SECTION 1:   The foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the findings of
the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth herein.
SECTION 2:   The following variation from the Lot, Space, Bulk and Yard
Regulations for Single Family Residential Districts established by Section
17.30 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance is hereby granted to the Subject
Property, commonly known as 330 Willow Road and located in the R-2 Single-
Family Residential District provided in Chapter 17.24 of the Winnetka Village
Code, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code: a variation from the corner yard
setback requirements of Section 17.30.050 to allow two one-story additions to
be constructed at the northeast and northwest corners of the existing
residence, with the northeast corner of the northeast addition being located
3.83 feet from the property line that adjoins Willow Road, and the remainder of
the additions being located within the 41.02-foot corner setback line,
resulting in a variation of 37.19 feet (90.66%), said construction to be in
accordance with the plans and elevations submitted with the application for
variation.
SECTION 3:   This Ordinance is passed by the Council of the Village of Winnetka
in the exercise of its home rule powers pursuant to Section 6 of Article VII of
the Illinois Constitution of 1970.
SECTION 4:   This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon its passage,
approval and posting as provided by law.
