ORDINANCE NO. M-10-2007
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA, COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (938 Cherry)
WHEREAS, the Village of Winnetka is a home rule municipality in accordance with
Article VII, Section 6 of the Constitution of the State of Illinois of 1970,
pursuant to which it has the authority, except as limited by said Section 6 of
Article VII, to exercise any power and perform any function pertaining to the
government and affairs of the Village; and
WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka (“Village Council”) find that
establishing standards for the use and development of lands and buildings
within the Village and establishing and applying criteria for variations from
those standards are matters pertaining to the affairs of the Village; and
WHEREAS, the property commonly known as 938 Cherry Street, Winnetka, Illinois
(the “Subject Property”), is legally described as follows:
The East 60 feet of Lot 14 in Block 9 in Provident Mutual Land Association
Subdivision of Blocks 7 to 12, inclusive, 28 to 33 inclusive, and 54 to 59
inclusive, in the Village of Winnetka in Section 20, Township 42 North, Range
13 East of the Third Principal Meridian, in Cook County, Illinois; and
WHEREAS, the Subject Property is located in the R-5 Zoning District provided in
Chapter 17.12 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka
Village Code; and
WHEREAS, on March 15, 2007, the owner of the Subject Property filed an
application for a variation from requirements of the Lot, Space, Bulk and Yard
Regulations for Single Family Residential Districts established by Section
17.30 of the Zoning Ordinance: (a) a variation from the maximum building size
limitations of Section 17.30.040 to allow a gross floor area of 1,773.58 square
feet, whereas a maximum of 1,202.52 square feet is permitted, resulting in a
variation of 571.06 square feet (47.49%); (b) a variation from the intensity of
use of lot limitations of Section 17.30.030 to permit a roofed lot coverage of
976.01 square feet, whereas a maximum of 751.65 square feet is permitted,
resulting in a variation of 224.36 square feet (29.85%); (c) a variation from
the intensity of use of lot limitations of Section 17.30.030 to permit an
impermeable lot coverage of 1,670.41 square feet, whereas the maximum allowed
is 1,503.3 square feet, resulting in a variation of 167.11 square feet
(11.12%); (d) a variation from the intensity of use of lot limitations of
Section 17.30.030 to allow a front yard impermeable lot coverage of 916.79
square feet, whereas a maximum of 571.14 square feet is permitted, resulting in
a variation of 345.65 square feet (60.52%); (e) a variation from the front
setback requirements of Section 17.30.050 to permit a front yard setback of
17.21 feet, whereas a setback of 31.73 feet is required, resulting in a
variation of 14.52 feet; and (f) a variation from the rear yard setback
requirements of Section 17.30.070 to permit a rear yard setback of 6.37 feet,
whereas a minimum of 10 feet is the requirement, resulting in a variation of
3.63 feet (36.3%), all of said variations being requested in order to convert
the existing legally nonconforming two-unit residential building into a
conforming single family home by constructing a 72 square-foot second floor
addition at the rear of the home, adding two bay windows to the front, adding a
new chimney on the east side of the house, removing a portion of the porch on
the east side of the building and adding a new porch on the west side; and
WHEREAS, on May 7, 2007, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of Appeals
conducted a public hearing on the requested variations, at which time the
applicants modified their proposal by withdrawing that portion of their
application proposing to add a new porch on the west side of the house; and
WHEREAS, by the favorable vote of six of the seven members then present, the
Zoning Board of Appeals has reported to the Council recommending that the
requested variations be granted, as amended; and
WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and particular hardships associated
with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect
to the Subject Property in that: (a) the Subject Property is a small, shallow
rectangular lot with a legally nonconforming lot depth of only 50 feet and a
legally nonconforming lot area of 3,006.6 square feet, which is less than half
of the required 8,400 square-foot minimum for the R-5 Zoning District; (b) the
combination of the required 31.73-foot front yard setback and the 10-foot rear
yard setback results in an unusually small buildable area that is less than 9
feet deep; (c) the Subject Property is improved with a legally nonconforming
two-unit residential building that was constructed in 1914, before the
enactment of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance; (d) the two-unit residential
building is legally nonconforming in that multi-family residences are not
permitted uses in the R-5 Single Family Zoning District; (e) the existing
residence is legally nonconforming with respect to roofed lot coverage, gross
floor area, front yard lot coverage, and front and rear yard setbacks; and
WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted to
be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that: (a)
the modifications are needed to convert the nonconforming two-flat rental
building in a single family residential district; (b) the second floor addition
to the building is necessary to convert the second floor into usable bedroom
space with adequate closets; (c) the proposed chimney and bay windows are
minimal additions that are necessary both to increase the size of the interior
living space and to make the building’s appearance more consistent with that of
single-family buildings; (d) the substandard size of the lot makes it
impossible to make any improvements to the existing building without the
granting of zoning variations; and (e) the substandard size of the Subject
Property results in a buildable area of approximately 315 square feet, making
it practically impossible to build a usable, conforming house on the Subject
Property; and
WHEREAS, the requested variations will not alter the essential character of the
neighborhood, but will result in bringing the exterior of the building more
into character with the single family nature of the neighborhood; and
WHEREAS, the requested variations will not impair an adequate supply of light
and air because the proposed bay windows face the street, and the second story
addition will not expand the footprint of the house; and
WHEREAS, the requested variations will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property, as the proposed construction will comply
with all applicable building and fire protection codes; and
WHEREAS, the requested variations will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
WHEREAS, the proposed construction will not contribute to congestion on the
public streets, as the property will continue to be used for single family
residential purposes; and
WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the requested variations will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village; and
WHEREAS, the requested variations are in harmony with the general purpose and
intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that they allow the renovation,
restoration and rehabilitation of a structurally sound existing building while
maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the community and protecting
established trees and landscaping.
NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
SECTION 1:   The foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the findings of
the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth herein.
SECTION 2:   The following variations from the Lot, Space, Bulk and Yard
Regulations for Single Family Residential Districts established by Section
17.30 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance are hereby granted to the Subject
Property, commonly known as 938 Cherry Street and located in the R-5 Single-
Family Residential District provided in Chapter 17.12 of the Winnetka Village
Code, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code: (a) a variation from the maximum
building size restrictions of Section 17.30.040 to allow a gross floor area of
1,773.58 square feet, whereas a maximum of 1,202.52 square feet is permitted,
resulting in a variation of 571.06 square feet (47.49%); (b) a variation from
the intensity of use of lot limitations of Section 17.30.030 to permit a roofed
lot coverage of 976.01 square feet, whereas a maximum of 751.65 square feet is
permitted, resulting in a variation of 224.36 square feet (29.85%); (c) a
variation from the intensity of use of lot restrictions of Section 17.30.030 to
permit impermeable lot coverage of 1,663.26 square feet, whereas the maximum
allowed is 1,503.3 square feet, resulting in a variation of 159.96.11 square
feet (10.64%); (d) a variation from the intensity of use of lot limitations of
Section 17.30.030 to allow a front yard impermeable lot coverage of 916.79
square feet, whereas a maximum of 571.14 square feet is permitted, resulting in
a variation of 345.65 square feet (60.52%); (e) a variation from the front
setback requirements of Section 17.30.050 to permit a front yard setback of
17.21 feet, whereas a setback of 31.73 feet is required, resulting in a
variation of 14.52 feet; and (f) a variation from the rear yard setback
requirements of Section 17.30.070 to permit a rear yard setback of 6.37 feet,
whereas a minimum of 10 feet is the requirement, resulting in a variation of
3.63 feet (36.3%), all of said variations being requested in order to convert
the existing legal nonconforming two-unit residential building to a single
family home and to construct a second floor addition at the rear of the home,
add two bay windows to the front, and a new chimney on the east side of the
house, in accordance with the plans and elevations submitted with the
application for variation, as modified by the deletion of the proposed porch
and deck on the west side of the property.
SECTION 3:   This Ordinance is passed by the Council of the Village of Winnetka
in the exercise of its home rule powers pursuant to Section 6 of Article VII of
the Illinois Constitution of 1970.
SECTION 4:   This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon its passage,
approval and posting as provided by law.
PASSED this 10th day of July, 2007, pursuant to the following roll call vote:
AYES:    Trustees Berger, Eilers, Poor, Ritchell
NAYS:    None
ABSENT:    Trustees Behles, Tucker
APPROVED this 10th day of July, 2007.
   Signed:
   /s/ Edmund C. Woodbury
   Village President
Countersigned:
/s/ Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
