ORDINANCE NO. M-15-2007
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A SPECIAL USE PERMIT AND VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF
THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA, COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS, AT 910
GREEN BAY ROAD
WHEREAS, the Village of Winnetka is a home rule municipality in accordance with
Article VII, Section 6 of the Constitution of the State of Illinois of 1970,
pursuant to which it has the authority, except as limited by said Section 6 of
Article VII, to exercise any power and perform any function pertaining to the
government and affairs of the Village; and
WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka (“Village Council”) find that
establishing standards for the use and development of lands and buildings
within the Village and establishing and applying criteria for variations from
those standards are matters pertaining to the affairs of the Village; and
WHEREAS, Absolute Equity Winnetka, LLC, is the owner of the following described
real estate (the “Subject Property”), which is commonly known as 910 Green Bay
Road:
Lot 10 in Block 6 in Jared Gage’s Subdivision of part of the East Half of the
Northwest Quarter; also part of the West Half of the Northwest Quarter of
Section 17; also part of the East Half of the Southwest Quarter of Fractional
Section 8, Township 42 North, Range 13, East of the Third Principal Meridian in
the Village of Winnetka, Cook County, Illinois, and
WHEREAS, the Subject Property is located in the Hubbard Woods business
district, on the west side of Green Bay Road between Tower Road and Gage
Street, in the C-2 Retail Overlay Zoning District provided for in Chapter 17.44
of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code; and
WHEREAS, the Subject Property is improved with a 50-foot wide by 60-foot deep,
1½-story building with eight parking spaces in a paved parking area behind the
building; and
WHEREAS, the Subject Property was formerly occupied by Mig and Tig, a high-end
furniture retailer, and has been vacant since that business closed; and
WHEREAS, pursuant to Section 17.44.010(B) of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance,
Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, certain uses are permitted only as
special uses in the C-2 Overlay Zoning District when they are located within
100 feet of a public street; and
WHEREAS, special uses granted pursuant to Section 17.44.010(B) are subject to
the conditions and requirements set forth in Chapter 17.44 and further subject
to the conditions and requirements pertaining to special uses set forth in
Chapter 17.56 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance; and
WHEREAS, on July 16, 2007, Absolute Equity Winnetka, LLC filed an application
for a special use permit pursuant to Section 17.56.010 of the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance, to allow a proposed second floor addition to be served by at-grade
parking, and to allow the construction of a small dwelling unit at the rear of
the first floor; and
WHEREAS, the applicant has also filed an application for the following
variations from Section 17.44.010: (a) a variation from the requirements of the
intensity of use of lot limitations of Section 17.44.010(E) to allow an
impermeable lot coverage of 6,342 square feet, whereas a maximum of 6,030
square feet is permitted, resulting in a variation of 312 square feet (5.17%);
and (b) a variation from the parking requirements of Section 17.44.010(K)(1),
to allow the construction of the proposed residential dwelling unit without the
required 2.25 parking spaces; and
WHEREAS, the special use permit and zoning variations are being requested to
allow the construction of a new second floor addition of approximately 4,500
square feet of office space with at-grade parking facilities, and to construct
a 10-foot deep addition to the first floor, which would include a 400-square
foot “work/live” loft style dwelling unit; and
WHEREAS, on July 25, 2007, on due notice thereof, the Plan Commission convened
to consider the two requested special uses; and
WHEREAS, the nine members of the Plan Commission present on July 25, 2007,
voted unanimously to recommend that the special use permit for the at-grade
parking be approved; and
WHEREAS, of the nine Plan Commission members present at the July 25, 2007,
meeting, six voted in favor of recommending that the special use permit for the
ground floor residential unit be approved, and three voted against recommending
approval; and
WHEREAS, on August 13, 2007, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of Appeals
held a public hearing to consider the special use permit and variation requests
for the proposed addition; and
WHEREAS, by the unanimous vote of the five members of the Zoning Board of
Appeals present on August 13, 2007, the Zoning Board of Appeals has recommended
to the Village Council that the requested special use permit for the at-grade
parking be granted, and that the lot coverage variation be granted; and
WHEREAS, by the favorable vote of 4 of the 5 members present on August 13,
2007, the Zoning Board of Appeals has recommended to the Village Council that
the special use permit for the ground floor residential “work/live” loft space
be granted and that the residential parking variation request be granted; and
WHEREAS, the separate proceedings before the Zoning Board of Appeals and the
Plan Commission both included questioning of the applicant by members of the
Zoning Board of Appeals and the Plan Commission; and
WHEREAS, none of the owners of the properties located within 250 feet of the
Subject Property submitted any evidence or requested an opportunity to cross-
examine witnesses at either the Zoning Board of Appeals hearing or the Plan
Commission meeting; and
WHEREAS, the record also includes testimony from neighboring third parties who
inquired about specifics of the parking and expressed concern about the
proposed building height; and
WHEREAS, the proceedings of the Zoning Board of Appeals and Plan Commission
conformed with all requirements of their procedural rules, the Winnetka Village
Code and applicable statutes of the State of Illinois; and
WHEREAS, the additional offices resulting from the construction of the second
floor will provide office space that will complement existing retail uses as
contemplated by the zoning regulations for the C-2 Retail Overlay; and
WHEREAS, the improved surface parking will meet the needs of the new second
floor office space and will not create a nonconformity for the first floor
retail space, which is not required to provide on-site parking; and
WHEREAS, the proposed first floor “work/live” loft dwelling unit is of a
minimal size and will not alter the character of either the Subject Property or
the Hubbard Woods business district; and
WHEREAS, the proposed special uses will neither endanger nor be detrimental to
the public health, safety, comfort, morals or general welfare, in that: (a)
using the existing surface parking area and adding a minimally sized first
floor dwelling unit will retain the primary first floor retail space at the
street frontage; (b) the proposed “work/live” loft dwelling unit will add a
dual purpose space consisting of office space that can double as an affordable
dwelling unit, in furtherance of the Affordable Housing Plan adopted by the
Village Council on May 10, 2005; (c) the proposed “work/live” loft dwelling
unit will not have a negative impact on the existing retail space, which will
maintain its original depth of 60 feet; and (d) the expanded, reconfigured
building will maintain the continuity of the retail block face, while providing
for compatible uses; and
WHEREAS, the proposed special uses will not either substantially diminish or
impair property values in the immediate vicinity, in that (a) the new office
space will be served by the improved surface parking area at the rear of the
building and will not impact the retail appearance of the building from the
street or create nonconformities in parking requirements for that retail use,
(b) the proposed improvements will allow an older building to be brought up to
modern retail and office space standards; and (c) the reconfigured space will
increase the number of available business spaces, which may draw new patrons to
the other commercial businesses located along Green Bay Road, all of which will
be beneficial to the immediate vicinity; and
WHEREAS, subject to the terms and conditions of this Ordinance, the proposed
special uses will not be substantially injurious to the use and enjoyment of
land in the immediate vicinity for uses permitted by right in the C-
2 Commercial Overlay Zoning District, because the proposed special uses will
facilitate the leasing of retail space at the building’s street frontage, add
complementary office space in a conforming location, and provide an affordable
housing unit in the form of a unique “work/live” loft space without affecting
the remaining uses of the building; and
WHEREAS, adequate measures have been taken to provide ingress and egress in a
manner that minimizes pedestrian and vehicular traffic congestion in the public
ways, in that access to the new building areas will comply with all applicable
access standards, and the Subject Property will continue to use the existing
streets, sidewalks and access routes; and
WHEREAS, the parking impact of the proposed first floor “work /live” dwelling
unit is negligible during normal working hours and is nonexistent during non-
business hours, when all of the on-site parking will be available for its use;
and
WHEREAS, adequate parking, utilities, access roads, drainage and other
facilities necessary for the operation of the special use already exist; and
WHEREAS, because the proposed special uses will not change the appearance of
the immediate vicinity, they are consistent with the Winnetka 2020 objective to
“ensure that Commercial, institutional, and residential development is
appropriate to the character of and minimizes the adverse impact on its
surrounding neighborhood;” and
WHEREAS, because of their minimal intensity and the pre-existing
infrastructure, the proposed special uses are consistent with the Winnetka 2020
objectives to: (a) “limit commercial, institutional and residential development
within the Village to minimize potentially adverse impacts on adjacent
residential neighborhoods and to prevent the need for significant increases in
infrastructure and other community resources,” (b) “ensure that development
proposals minimize the potential adverse impact they might have on residential
neighborhoods, including the impact on pedestrian character, on-site parking,
traffic patterns, congestion, open space, storm water management and Village
infrastructure," and (c) "ensure that new development does not decrease the
public parking supply, particularly on-street parking that supports retail
use;” and
WHEREAS, the proposed special uses are consistent with the Winnetka 2020 goal
to “provide for a wide range of office/service and retail commercial land uses
and development within the existing business districts in the Corridor;” and
WHEREAS, the addition of the “work/live” space will further the goals of the
Village’s Affordable Housing Plan; and
WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and unique hardships associated with
carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect to the
Subject Property in that: (a) the Subject Property is improved with a 1½-story
building that was constructed in 1927; (b) the impermeable surface on the
Subject Property is legally nonconforming; (c) the increased impermeable
surface is necessary to allow improvements to the parking area that will
alleviate on-street parking demands, improve accessibility for persons with
disabilities, and will provide a small green planting area at the rear of the
building; (d) removing impermeable surface at the rear of the building would
result in an unpaved parking area that would not meet Village engineering
standards; (e) because of the existing configuration of the building it is not
possible to increase the parking area without reducing the size of the retail
space; (f) the existing first floor retail space does not require parking and
the 400-square foot space could be dedicated to retail use without requiring
additional parking; and (g) the proposed dwelling unit is of a minimal size and
will have a negligible impact on parking demands; and
WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted to
be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that (a)
the building on the Subject Property was constructed before any off-street
parking requirements were imposed; (b) the nonconforming impermeable surface
cannot be corrected without removing the existing paved parking area, which
provides valuable off-street parking for the Subject Property; and
WHEREAS, the requested variation will not alter the essential character of the
neighborhood because: (a) the use of the Subject Property will remain
consistent with what is permitted under the Zoning Ordinance; (b) the
impermeable surface variation does not affect the appearance of the building
from the street; (c) the requested variation is at the rear of the building and
does not affect the appearance of the building; (d) the parking variation for
the proposed dwelling unit has no visual impact on the neighborhood; and (e)
the greatest need for parking for the dwelling unit is during non-work hours,
when all of the spaces at the rear of the building would be available for use;
and
WHEREAS, the requested variations will not impair an adequate supply of light
and air because the building’s dimensions will conform with all applicable
limitations; and
WHEREAS, the requested variations will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property because all work will conform with
applicable building and safety codes; and
WHEREAS, the requested variations will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements to the Subject
Property; and
WHEREAS, the requested variations will not contribute to congestion on the
public streets, because the impermeable surface variation does not affect
traffic and the existing parking spaces will continue to be available for the
proposed dwelling unit; and
WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the requested variations will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village.
WHEREAS, the requested variations are in harmony with the general purpose and
intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that they: (a) maintains the scale
and character of the existing commercial neighborhood; (b) protect and respect
the justifiable reliance of existing residents, business people and taxpayers
on the continuation of existing, established land use patterns; and (c)
otherwise promotes the public health, safety, comfort, morals and welfare by
making it possible for the owner to renovate the Subject Property and assure
its continuing viability in the Hubbard Woods business district.
NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
SECTION 1:   The foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the findings of
the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth herein.
SECTION 2:   That, pursuant to Sections 17.56.010 and 17.44.010 of the Winnetka
Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, a special use permit
and variations are hereby granted with respect to the Subject Property,
commonly known as 910 Green Bay Road and located in the C-2 Retail Overlay
Zoning District, to allow the construction of a second floor office space
addition that provides at-grade parking, and the expansion of the first floor
into a work/live space without providing parking, subject to the terms and
conditions hereinafter set forth.
SECTION 3:   That, subject to the terms and conditions hereinafter set forth,
the following variations from the requirements of Section 17.44.010 of Chapter
17.44 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code,
are hereby granted to the Subject Property, commonly known as 910 Green Bay
Road and located in the C-2 Retail Overlay Zoning District: (a) a variation
from the requirements of the intensity of use of lot limitations of Section
17.44.010(E) to allow an impermeable lot coverage of 6,342 square feet, whereas
a maximum of 6,030 square feet is permitted, resulting in a variation of 312
square feet (5.17%); and (b) a variation from the parking requirements of
Section 17.44.010(K)(1), to allow the construction of the proposed residential
dwelling unit without the required 2.25 parking spaces.
SECTION 4:   The variations and special use permit hereby granted are subject
to the following conditions:
A.   The proposed construction shall commence within 12 months after the
effective date of this Ordinance.
B.   The special use permit and variation granted for the proposed “work/live”
loft space shall expire if construction of that unit is not commenced within 12
months after the effective date of this Ordinance.
C.   Nothing in this Ordinance shall be deemed as granting design approval for
the proposed building improvements, which shall remain subject to final
approval by the Design Review Board.
D..   The proposed construction shall be in accordance with the plans and
elevations identified as Drawings 1.1, 2.1, 2.2, 2.3, 2.4, 3.1, 3.2 and 3.3, as
presented in the Village Council’s agenda materials (“Proposed Plans”).
E.   The 400-square foot office space proposed for the rear of the south side
of the first floor is being permitted solely on the basis of the applicant’s
representation that it will be a “work/live” loft space that will add a new
affordable housing unit to the Village. It is therefore subject to the
following conditions:
1.   The “work/live” space shall be equipped with kitchen and bath facilities,
as depicted in the Proposed Plans.
2.   The “work/live” space shall be leased solely as a “work/live loft.“
3.   The rent for the “work/live” space shall meet dwelling unit affordability
standards as established by the Village of Winnetka. In the absence of a
Village-defined standard for dwelling unit affordability, the “work/live” space
shall meet the applicable affordability standards established by the Illinois
Housing Development Authority.
4.   No more than one person shall reside in the “work/live” space.
5.   Prior to obtaining an occupancy permit for any space in the renovated
building, the owner shall incorporate the foregoing requirements of this
subsection D into a restrictive covenant, in a form acceptable to the Village,
and shall record such restrictive covenant against the Subject Property.
SECTION 4 :   The stipulations, conditions and restrictions set forth in the
foregoing Section 3 of this Ordinance may be modified or revised from time to
time by the Village Council following public notice and hearing, following the
procedures specified in Section 17.56 of the Winnetka Village Code for
processing special use applications.
SECTION 5:   This Ordinance is passed by the Council of the Village of Winnetka
in the exercise of its home rule powers pursuant to Section 6 of Article VII of
the Illinois Constitution of 1970.
SECTION 6:   This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon its passage,
approval and posting as provided by law.
PASSED this 2nd day of October, 2007, pursuant to the following roll call vote:
AYES:    Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers, Poor and Tucker
NAYS:    Trustee Ritchell
ABSENT:    None
APPROVED this 2nd day of October, 2007.
   Signed:
   //s//Edmund C. Woodbury
   Village President
Countersigned:
//s//Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
