ORDINANCE NO. M-19-2007
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA, COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (717 Locust)
WHEREAS, the Village of Winnetka is a home rule municipality in accordance with
Article VII, Section 6 of the Constitution of the State of Illinois of 1970,
pursuant to which it has the authority, except as limited by said Section 6 of
Article VII, to exercise any power and perform any function pertaining to the
government and affairs of the Village; and
WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka (“Village Council”) find that
establishing standards for the use and development of lands and buildings
within the Village and establishing and applying criteria for variations from
those standards are matters pertaining to the affairs of the Village; and
WHEREAS, the property commonly known as 717 Locust Street, Winnetka, Illinois
(the “Subject Property”), is legally described as follows:
The South 60 feet of the West 150 feet of Lot 2 in Block 1 in Nelson’s
Subdivision of Lot 61 and part of Lot 60 in County Clerk’s Division of that
part of the Southeast Quarter of Section 17, Township 42 North, Range 13 East
of the Third Principal Meridian, lying West of the right of way of the Chicago
and Northwestern Railway in the Village of Winnetka, Cook County, Illinois; and
WHEREAS, the Subject Property is located in the R-3 Single Family Zoning
District provided in Chapter 17.20 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17
of the Winnetka Village Code; and
WHEREAS, on August 8, 2007, the owner of the Subject Property filed an
application for the following variations from requirements of the Lot, Space,
Bulk and Yard Regulations for Single Family Residential Districts established
by Chapter 17.30 of the Zoning Ordinance: (a) a variation from the average
front yard lot coverage limitations of Section 17.30.030 to permit a front yard
lot coverage of 1,733.17 square feet, whereas a maximum of 1,265.94 square feet
is permitted, resulting in a variation of 467.23 square feet (36.91%); and (b)
a variation from the front yard setback requirements of Section 17.30.050.C to
allow a new single family residence to be constructed with a front yard setback
of 43.82 feet, rather than the required average front setback of 70.33 feet,
resulting in a variation of 26.51 feet (37.69%), and
WHEREAS, on September 10, 2007, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variations and, by a vote
of five of the seven members then present, has reported to the Council
recommending that the requested variations be denied; and
WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and unique circumstances associated
with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect
to the Subject Property in that: (a) the Subject Property is located on the
east side of Locust Street, on a block that extends from Westmoor Road on the
north to Pine Street on the south, a distance of approximately 1,260 feet; (b)
there are two “T” intersections on Locust Street between Westmoor Road and Pine
Street, one at Dinsmore Road, and the second at Starr Road, as a result of
which the uninterrupted east side of Locust Street is considered to have three
‘block faces” for purposes of calculating the average front yard setback; (c)
the applicable block face is 507.75 feet long and has four parcels, including
the Subject Property, with the house at the north end of the block having three
street frontages and a Locust Street front setback of 44.5 feet, the second
house from the north having a front setback of 91.5 feet and the house
immediately south of the Subject Property having a front setback of 75 feet,
resulting in an average front setback requirement of 70.33 feet; (d) the
property immediately to the north of the Subject Property is a through lot,
having a street address of 730 Walden Road, and have its functional rear yard
on the Locust Street side of the property, adjacent to the Subject Property;
(e) the through lot at 730 Walden Road is subject to front setback requirements
on both street frontages, and its 91.5-foot Locust Street front setback is
measured from the Locust Street line to the edge of the nearest building on the
property, which is a pool house located to the rear of the principal residence,
which faces Walden Road; (e) the minimum front setback in the R-3 Single Family
Residential District is 40 feet; (f) the additional 26.51 feet of front yard
setback for the Subject Property is the result of the combined impact of the
short block face and the depths of the other lots on the block face; (g) the
applicant proposes a front setback of 43.82 feet; (g) the entire block of the
east side of Locust between Westmoor Road and Pine Street contains 14 parcels,
and the front setbacks on the remaining parcels on the east side of Locust
Street from Dinsmore Road to Pine Street have front setbacks that range from
39.75 feet to 49.93 feet; (h) the strict application of the average front
setback requirement will have a negative impact on both the Subject Property
and the other properties on the block face, because it will disrupt the
existing consistency in front setbacks, and will gradually increase the average
front setbacks as the other properties on the block face are redeveloped; (i)
the strict application of the average front setback requirement to the Subject
Property is not consistent with either the stated purpose of the R-3 Single
Family Residential Zoning District or the land use and development objectives
stated in Section 1704.020.B of the Zoning Ordinance, in that a 70.33-foot
front yard setback would alter the architectural scale and character of the
immediate vicinity, and would reduce open space in the rear of the Subject
Property; (j) the front yard impermeable surface limitation was enacted to
restrict the construction of paved turnarounds and parking areas in shallow
front yards; (k) the applicant does not propose any parking or turn-around
areas in the front yard, and the requested front yard impermeable surface
results solely from the proposed location of the new house; (l) because the
Subject Property is 60 feet wide and 150 feet, requiring a 70.33-foot front
yard setback will force the entire building backward, effectively eliminating
usable open space in the rear yard, and will eliminate the possibility of
building a detached garage for the new home; (m) the lot is not sufficiently
wide to allow for the construction of a side-loading attached garage; and (n)
due to the 21-foot total side yard requirement, the maximum allowable building
width is 39 feet, and constructing an attached garage would result in either
interior dimensions of less than 20 feet for a substantial portion of the new
home, or in constructing the attached garage so that it extends from the front
of the building, resulting in a visually jarring appearance that is
inconsistent with the character of the neighborhood; and
WHEREAS, the requested variations will not alter the essential character of the
neighborhood because: (a) the proposed front setback will be consistent with
the front yard setbacks on most of the other properties on the east side of
Locust Street between Westmoor Road and Pine Street, and (b) it will enable the
new home to be constructed with a detached garage at the rear of the property,
which is the location that is preferred and encouraged by the Zoning Ordinance;
and
WHEREAS, the requested variations will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property, as the proposed construction will comply
with all applicable building and fire protection codes; and
WHEREAS, the requested variations will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed new construction; and
WHEREAS, the proposed construction will not contribute to congestion on the
public streets, as the property will continue to be used for single family
residential purposes and the new construction includes enclosed off-street
parking; and
WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the requested variations will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village; and
WHEREAS, the requested variation is in harmony with the general purpose and
intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that it will allow the construction
of a new residence that is designed and situated on the Subject Property so as
to maintain the existing scale and appearance of the Subject Property and the
immediate neighborhood.
NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
SECTION 1:   The foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the findings of
the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth herein.
SECTION 2:   The following variations from the Lot, Space, Bulk and Yard
Regulations for Single Family Residential Districts established by Section
17.30 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance are hereby granted to the Subject
Property, commonly known as 717 Locust Street and located in the R-3 Single-
Family Residential District provided in Chapter 17.20 of the Winnetka Village
Code, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code: (a) a variation from the average
front yard lot coverage limitations of Section 17.30.030 to permit a front yard
lot coverage of 1,733.17 square feet, whereas a maximum of 1,265.94 square feet
is permitted, resulting in a variation of 467.23 square feet (36.91%); and (b)
a variation from the front yard setback requirements of Section 17.30.050.C to
allow a new single family residence to be constructed with a front yard setback
of 43.82 feet, rather than the required average front setback of 70.33 feet,
resulting in a variation of 26.51 feet (37.69%), in accordance with the plans
and elevations submitted with the application for variations.
SECTION 3:   This Ordinance is passed by the Council of the Village of Winnetka
in the exercise of its home rule powers pursuant to Section 6 of Article VII of
the Illinois Constitution of 1970.
SECTION 4:   This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon its passage,
approval and posting as provided by law.
PASSED this 20th day of November, 2007, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
AYES:    Trustees Behles, Eilers, Ritchell, and Tucker
