ORDINANCE NO. M-21-2007
AN ORDINANCE DESIGNATING THE SCHMIDT-BURNHAM LOG HOUSE A LANDMARK PURSUANT TO
CHAPTER 15.64 OF THE WINNETKA VILLAGE CODE (1140 Willow)
WHEREAS, the Village of Winnetka is a home rule municipality in accordance with
Article VII, Section 6 of the Constitution of the State of Illinois of 1970,
pursuant to which it has the authority, except as limited by said Section 6 of
Article VII, to exercise any power and perform any function pertaining to the
government and affairs of the Village; and
WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka (“Village Council”) find that
the identification, designation and preservation of buildings and structures in
the Village that are historically, culturally, and architecturally significant,
and the encouragement of the restoration and rehabilitation of those buildings
and structures are matters pertaining to the affairs of the Village; and
WHEREAS, the Winnetka Historical Society has submitted an application seeking
to have a building commonly referred to as “The Schmidt Burnham Log House”
designated a Winnetka landmark; and
WHEREAS, on October 15, 2002, the Village Council adopted Ordinance M-34-2002,
which granted a special use permit to allow the Schmidt-Burnham Log House to be
moved to a wooded portion of the property commonly known as Crow Island Woods
Park, which is owned by the Winnetka Park District and leased to the Winnetka
Historical Society; and
WHEREAS,  Crow Island Woods Park is legally described as:
Block 1 (excepting therefrom the east 13.0 feet of Lots 37 to 47 inclusive),
and Blocks 2, 9 and 10 in Vernamo Subdivision of the northwest quarter of the
southwest quarter of Section 20, Township 42 North, Range 13, East of the Third
Principal Meridian, also the vacated public streets and alleys or parts thereof
in said Vernamo Subdivision, as recorded October 30, 1891, in book 54 of plats,
page 13, as document number 1560706, to-wit: all of vacated Mt. Pleasant street
from the east line of Euclid Avenue (formerly Larson St.) to the west line
extended of Glendale Avenue (formerly Bjornson St); all of vacated Berkeley
Avenue (formerly Peterson St.) from the south line extended of Willow Road to
the previously vacated Sunset Road at the south end of Block 9; the west 33
feet of vacated Glendale Avenue (excepting therefrom that portion of Glendale
Avenue lying adjacent to Lots 37 to 47 in Block 1); and all of the vacated
alleys lying within Blocks 1, 2 and 9 in the Village of Winnetka, Cook County,
Illinois,
and has a common street address of 1140 Willow Road, Winnetka, Illinois (the
“Subject Property”) and
WHEREAS, the Schmidt-Burnham Log House was originally located in Indian Hill,
and was moved to Tower Road in 1917, from where it was moved to its current
location in a wooded area of Crow Island Woods Park, 189 feet south of the
Willow Road right-of-way, approximately 162 feet from Crow Island School and 50
feet due west-of the southwest corner of the Crow Island Woods School west
playground; and
WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka has enacted Chapter 15.64 of
Title 15 of the Winnetka Village Code, titled “Landmark Preservation,” for the
purposes of preserving, protecting, enhancing, rehabilitating and regulating
buildings, structures, objects, and places of historical, cultural or
architectural importance; and
WHEREAS, pursuant to the provisions of Chapter 15.64 of the Winnetka Village
Code (the “Landmark Ordinance”) and notice duly published and sent, the
Landmark Preservation Commission conducted a public hearing on May 7, 2007, to
consider the application for certified landmark designation and unanimously
recommended that the residence on the Subject Property be designated a Winnetka
landmark; and
WHEREAS, the Landmark Preservation Commission applied the Village’s System for
the Evaluation of Landmarks to the information received at the hearing and made
findings regarding the Subject Property, including the following:
(1)   The architectural type is extremely rare in that the Schmidt Burnham Log
House was built in circa 1837, possibly as a pioneer hotel for stagecoach
service before it was a family home;
(2)   The method of construction is extremely rare in that: (a) the original
portion of the house is a three-bay center entrance with symmetrically-spaced
double hung windows and a gable roof; (b) the hand-hewn hardwood logs are
square cut with corner lap joint notching; (c) the roof beams are held together
with wooden pegs, and some of the roof beams are in their naturally rounded
forms; (d) window casements appear to be original, and hand-forged square cut
nails hold the windows together; and (e) all exterior walls and ceilings have
exposed log walls and joists respectively;
(3)   The residence is associated with a nationally well-known historical
person in that the most significant person associated with the house was Anita
Willets Burnham, who was an individualist, a progressive thinker, artist,
teacher, writer and lecturer who studied art at four of the oldest and most
prestigious art schools in the country and is internationally known for the
book she wrote of her travels abroad with her family, first published in 1933;
(4)   The Subject Property is an established visual feature in the Village,
even though it has been relocated twice, and is a prominent and historically
important symbol of the community which is currently used for educational tours
and classes in its current location in Crow Island Woods;
(5)   Since the original portion of the residence has been left virtually
untouched, the originality of the structure is excellent, and the subsequent
renovations are historically appropriate;
(6)   The era in which the home was built was “pre-1900” which gives the
Subject Property a high score for age;
(7)   The structural condition of the home is exceptional, given the recent
efforts of the Winnetka Historical Society to restore the home using
historically appropriate materials and building techniques; and
WHEREAS, pursuant to the findings entered at the May 7, 2007, meeting of the
Landmark Preservation Commission, the members then present concluded that the
residence located at 1140 Willow is unique and meets the criteria of the
Landmark Ordinance, and have accordingly unanimously voted recommended that it
be designated a Winnetka landmark; and
WHEREAS, the Schmidt-Burnham Log House is the longest continuously occupied
home in Cook County and was continuously occupied by the Burnham family from
1917 until the death of the last surviving owner, Ann Hibbard Burnham Smith, in
2001; and
WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka have considered the
recommendation of the Landmark Preservation Commission and have determined that
it is in the best interest of the Village and its residents to establish the
house located at 1140 Willow as a designated landmark, because not only is it
significant in its style and design, but it is of great historical significance
as well.
NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
SECTION 1:   The foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the findings of
the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth herein.
SECTION 2:   The Schmidt-Burnham Log House, located on the property commonly
known as Crow Island Woods Park at 1140 Willow Road, which has a permanent real
estate index numbers 05-20-302-001-0000, 05-20-303-001-0000, 05-20-309-001-0000
and 05-20-310-001-0000, is hereby designated a landmark under Section 15.04.070
the Landmark Preservation Ordinance, in accordance with the findings and
recommendations of the Landmark Preservation Commission.
SECTION 3:   This Ordinance is passed by the Council of the Village of Winnetka
in the exercise of its home rule powers pursuant to Section 6 of Article VII of
the Illinois Constitution of 1970.
SECTION 4:   This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon its passage,
approval and posting as provided by law.
PASSED this 4th day of December, 2007, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
AYES:    Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers, Poor, Tucker
NAYS:    None
