ORDINANCE NO. M-23-2007
AN ORDINANCE DESIGNATING A LANDMARK PURSUANT TO CHAPTER 15.64 OF THE WINNETKA
VILLAGE CODE (1345 Trapp Lane)
WHEREAS, the Village of Winnetka is a home rule municipality in accordance with
Article VII, Section 6 of the Constitution of the State of Illinois of 1970,
pursuant to which it has the authority, except as limited by said Section 6 of
Article VII, to exercise any power and perform any function pertaining to the
government and affairs of the Village; and
WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka (“Village Council”) find that
the identification, designation and preservation of buildings and structures in
the Village that are historically, culturally, and architecturally significant,
and the encouragement of the restoration and rehabilitation of those buildings
and structures are matters pertaining to the affairs of the Village; and
WHEREAS, the owner of the property legally described as follows:
PARCEL 1: Lot 1 in GODDARD’S SUBDIVISION of Goddard’s Resubdivision of part of
Lots 5 and 6 in Higgins estate Subdivision of the Northeast quarter of the
Southeast quarter of Section 18, Township 42 North, Range 13 East of the Third
Principal Meridian, according to the plat thereof recorded August 18, 1966 as
Document #19920221, in cook County, Illinois;
PARCEL 2: EASEMENT FOR THE BENEFIT OF PARCEL 1, as established by decree
entered June 26, 1935, and supplemental decree entered August 9, 1937, in Case
Number 34-“C”-6741, in the Circuit Court, Cook County, Illinois and as created
by Grant recorded October 2, 1952, as Document Number 15450419 and as confirmed
by Grant recorded January 16, 1953, as Document Number 15527126, Grant recorded
April 7, 1954, as Document Number 15875098, and other various instruments of
record, FOR INGRESS, EGRESS AND PUBLIC UTILITIES, over, upon and under the
following described land; That part of Lot 6 in Higgin’s Estate Subdivision of
the Northeast quarter of the Southeast quarter of Section 18, Township 42
North, Range 13 East of the Third Principal meridian, as recorded May 4, 1873
in the Recorders Office of cook County, Illinois, in Book 9 of Plats, page 61,
as Document #16404, bounded and described as follows; Beginning at a point in
the West line of Hibbard Road, 265.50 feet North of the South line of Lot 7 in
said Subdivision; thence West parallel with the South line of said Lot 7, 319
feet to the point of beginning, in Cook County, Illinois;
PARCEL 3: EASEMENT FOR THE BENEFIT OF PARCEL 1, as created by Grant from
Winnetka Trust and Savings Bank, a Corporation of Illinois, as Trustee under
Trust Agreements dated December 23, 1930 known as Trust number “A” 129, to
Robert D. Gordon, Sr., Victor M Langsett and Kathleen Langsett, his wife, John
Trapp and Carrie M. Trapp, Robert Donaldson Gordon, Jr. and George Henry Gordon
dated October 14, 1952 and recorded January 16, 1953, as Document umber
15527126 FOR PUBLIC UTILITIES, in, on and over the North 12.0 feet of the
following described land; The West 3.0 acres of those parts of Lots 5,6 and 7
in Higgin’s Estate Subdivision (taken as a tract), lying South of a line which
is 247.50 feet South of the North line of said Lot 5; thence East along a line
a distance of 247.5 feet South of and parallel with the North line of said Lot
5 to a point thereof, 524.40 feet East of said West line of Lot 6; thence South
a distance of 249.28 feet, more or les, to a point in the South line of said
Lot 7, which point is 524.40 feet East, measured along said South line, from
the West line of said Lot 7; thence West along the South line of Lot 7 to the
point of its intersection with the West line of said Lot 7; thence North 249.11
feet along the West line of said Lots 6 and 7 to the point of beginning, all in
cook County, Illinois, which is commonly known as 1345 Trapp Lane, Winnetka,
Illinois, (“Subject Property”) has submitted an application seeking designation
of the Subject Property as a certified Village of Winnetka landmark; and
WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka has enacted Chapter 15.64 of
Title 15 of the Winnetka Village Code, titled “Landmark Preservation,” for the
purposes of preserving, protecting, enhancing, rehabilitating and regulating
buildings, structures, objects, and places of historical, cultural or
architectural importance; and
WHEREAS, pursuant to the provisions of Chapter 15.64 of the Winnetka Village
Code (the “Landmark Ordinance”) and notice duly published and sent, the
Landmark Preservation Commission conducted a public hearing on August 6, 2007,
to consider the application for certified landmark designation and unanimously
recommended that the residence on the Subject Property be designated a Winnetka
landmark; and
WHEREAS, the Landmark Preservation Commission applied the Village’s System for
the Evaluation of Landmarks to the information received at the hearing and made
findings regarding the Subject Property, including the following:
(1)   The architectural type is rare in that: (a) the Robert D. Gordon House
was built in 1929 in the Colonial Revival style; (b) the home contains
symmetrical elements, such as the formal central block of the home with wings
on either side; and (c) it was the first home constructed on Trapp Lane, and is
situated at the end of the lane on a 1½ acre parcel;
(2)   The method of construction is extremely in that: (a) the residence is
noteworthy for its carefully thought-out massing and its exquisite detailing,
which is evident on both the exterior and interior of the home; (b) the main
block of the home is symmetrical, five bays wide with a center entrance and a
side-facing gable roof framed by two rectangular brick chimneys; (c) windows
are double-hung with multiple lights and flanked by paneled shutters, a formal
classical approach which is typical of Colonial Revival houses, while the
wings, balanced yet asymmetrical, are also common features; (d) the residence
has a recessed entrance door, with the entry surrounded by simple geometric
moldings and topped by a lintel with a swan’s neck pediment, broken in the
center to accommodate an urn; and (e) the home is constructed of brick with a
graduated slate roof and a rear wing of lannonstone ashlar;
(3)   The residence is associated with a nationally well-known architect, in
that: (a) the home was designed by Howard Van Doren Shaw, an architect educated
at Yale and MIT, who began his practice in Chicago in 1893 and designed a
number of houses in Chicago’s Hyde Park neighborhood, as well as throughout
Chicagoland and the North Shore; (b) the Gordon House is trimmed in the same
blue and has the same slate roof as the architect’s own country house in Lake
Forest; (c) Mr. Shaw was made a fellow of the American Institute of Architects
in 1907 and his work began to be recognized and copied by other architects of
that time period; and (d) Mr. Shaw designed commercial buildings, churches,
meeting halls and various other structures, but is best known for his country
house architecture, and is considered to be the Midwest’s foremost country
house architect of his generation;
(4)   The residence is a symbol of the neighborhood in the context of the
entire Village, because even though it is relatively hidden with an expansive
lawn and garden, it was the first house built on Trapp Lane, and could be
considered an exemplification of the neighborhood;
(5)   The residence is an excellent example of originality, since the home has
been left mostly unchanged, with only one historically sensitive and small
alteration;
(6)   The residence receives a high score for originality, and is also in
excellent structural condition: (a) because it has been left largely in its
original form, with one small back addition to the northwest corner of the
building, which is not visible from the front or sides of the home; and (b) a
painstaking restoration process has been undertaken during the past two years,
with no detail unnoticed; and
WHEREAS, pursuant to the findings entered at the August 6, 2007, meeting of the
Landmark Preservation Commission, the members then present concluded that the
residence located at 1345 Trapp Lane is unique and meets the criteria of the
Landmark Ordinance, and have accordingly unanimously voted recommended that it
be designated a Winnetka landmark; and
WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka have considered the
recommendation of the Landmark Preservation Commission and have determined that
it is in the best interest of the Village and its residents to establish the
house located at 1345 Trapp Lane as a designated landmark, because it is
significant in its style and design, and also because of its excellent
structural condition and association with a nationally significant architect;
and
NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
SECTION 1:   The foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the findings of
the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth herein.
SECTION 2:   The residence located on the property at 1345 Trapp Lane,
permanent real estate index number Lane 05-18-402-076-0000, is hereby
designated a certified landmark under Section 15.04.070 the Landmark
Preservation Ordinance, in accordance with the findings and recommendations of
the Landmark Preservation Commission.
SECTION 3:   This Ordinance is passed by the Council of the Village of Winnetka
in the exercise of its home rule powers pursuant to Section 6 of Article VII of
the Illinois Constitution of 1970.
SECTION 4:   This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon its passage,
approval and posting as provided by law.
PASSED this 4th day of December, 2007, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
AYES:    Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers, Poor, Tucker
NAYS:    None
ABSENT: Trustee Ritchell
APPROVED this 4th day of December, 2007.
   Signed:
   s/Edmund C. Woodbury
   Village President
