ORDINANCE NO. M-27-2007
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA, COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (981 Gordon Terrace)
WHEREAS, the Village of Winnetka is a home rule municipality in accordance with
Article VII, Section 6 of the Constitution of the State of Illinois of 1970,
pursuant to which it has the authority, except as limited by said Section 6 of
Article VII, to exercise any power and perform any function pertaining to the
government and affairs of the Village; and
WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka (“Village Council”) find that
establishing standards for the use and development of lands and buildings
within the Village and establishing and applying criteria for variations from
those standards are matters pertaining to the affairs of the Village; and
WHEREAS, the property commonly known as 981 Gordon Terrace, Winnetka, Illinois
(the “Subject Property”), is legally described as follows:
Lot 2 in Block 2 in Jared Gage’s Subdivision of part of the Northwest ¼ of
fractional Section 17 and part of the East ½ of the Southwest ¼ of Section 8,
Township 42 North, Range 13, East of the Third Principal Meridian, in Cook
County, Illinois,
and is located in the R-5 Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.12 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code; and
WHEREAS, on September 28, 2007, the owner of the Subject Property filed an
application for the following variations from requirements of the Lot, Space,
Bulk and Yard Regulations for Single Family Residential Districts established
by Chapter 17.30 of the Zoning Ordinance: (a) a variation from the intensity of
use of lot limitations of Section 17.30.030 to allow a front yard lot coverage
of 840.88 square feet, whereas a maximum of 481.5 square feet is permitted,
resulting in a variation of 359.38 square feet (74.64 %); (b) a variation from
the front yard setback requirements of Section 17.30.050 to permit a front yard
setback of 16.42 feet, whereas a minimum of 30 feet is required, resulting in a
variation of 13.58 feet (45.27%); (c) a variation in the side yard setback
requirements of Section 17.30.060 to allow a side yard setback of 1.47 feet,
whereas the required minimum is 9.19 feet, resulting in a variation of 7.72
feet (84%); and (d) a variation from the provisions of Section 17.64.060
pertaining to conforming uses of nonconforming buildings, all to permit the
expansion of the existing nonconforming detached garage, which is closer to the
street than the principal building; and
WHEREAS, on November 12, 2007, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variations and, by the
favorable vote of four of the seven members then present, and with two members
voting against and one member abstaining, has reported to the Council
recommending that the requested variations be granted; and
WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and particular hardships associated
with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect
to the Subject Property in that: (a) the Subject Property is an irregularly
shaped corner lot located at the southeast corner of Gordon Terrace and Scott
Avenue; (b) the north lot line, which abuts the southern edge of the Scott
Avenue parking lot, is 44 feet long, while the south lot line is 139.05 feet
long; (c) the single family residence on the Subject Property was constructed
in 1915, before the enactment of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance; (d) the Subject
Property is subject to a front yard setback of 30 feet from its north lot line
and a corner yard setback of 30 feet along the 180-foot length of its Gordon
Terrace frontage; (e) the existing one-car garage, is 12.11 feet wide and 20.20
feet long and is located in the northern part of the Subject Property; (f) the
existing garage has a legally nonconforming side yard setback of 1.04 feet from
the east lot line; (g) there is no other reasonably available location for a
garage on the Subject Property due to the orientation of the house on the
Subject Property; and the proposed garage location enhances the actual street
frontage of the Subject Property’s west front yard along Gordon Terrace; and
(h) moving the garage to the west to conform with the side yard requirements
would create a nonconformity in the corner yard and moving the garage to the
south would increase the amount of increase the size of the driveway, thereby
increasing the amount of impermeable surface in the front yard as well as on
the property as a whole; and
WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted to
be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that: (a)
placing the garage in a conforming location would require moving it closer to
the house, which would reduce the amount of rear yard space, reduce light and
air for the house, and create unusable open space to the north of the garage;
(b) placing the garage in the rear yard of the Subject Property would require
the removal of mature landscaping and create a dense building cluster at the
south end of the Subject Property, where it adjoins other single-family
properties, and would thereby reduce light and air to the Subject Property; and
(c) two-car garages are a standard amenity on lots of comparable size
throughout the Village; and
WHEREAS, the requested variations will not alter the essential character of the
neighborhood because the proposed detached two-car garage will be located in
the same part of the Subject Property as the existing garage, in the part of
the Subject Property that abuts the public parking lot and bank property; and
WHEREAS, the requested variations will not impair an adequate supply of light
and air because the proposed addition to the garage is directly adjacent to the
existing bank and public parking lot; and
WHEREAS, the requested variations will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property, as the proposed construction will comply
with all applicable building and fire protection codes; and
WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the requested variations will diminish the
taxable value of land and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable
value of the Subject Property may be increased because of the proposed
improvements; and
WHEREAS, the proposed construction will not contribute to congestion on the
public streets, as the property will continue to be used for single family
residential purposes; and
WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the requested variations will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village; and
WHEREAS, the requested variations are in harmony with the general purpose and
intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that they allow the renovation,
restoration and rehabilitation of a structurally sound existing building while
maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the community and protecting
established trees and landscaping.
NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
SECTION 1:   The foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the findings of
the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth herein.
SECTION 2:   The following variations from the Lot, Space, Bulk and Yard
Regulations for Single Family Residential Districts established by Section
17.30 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance are hereby granted to the Subject
Property, commonly known as 981 Gordon Terrace and located in the R-5 Single-
Family Residential District provided in Chapter 17.12 of the Winnetka Village
Code, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code: (a) a variation from the intensity
of use of lot limitations of Section 17.30.030 to allow a front yard lot
coverage of 840.88 square feet, whereas a maximum of 481.5 square feet is
permitted, resulting in a variation of 359.38 square feet (74.64 %); (b) a
variation from the front yard setback requirements of Section 17.30.050 to
permit a front yard setback of 16.42 feet, whereas a minimum of 30 feet is
required, resulting in a variation of 13.58 feet (45.27%); (c) a variation in
the side yard setback requirements of Section 17.30.060 to allow a side yard
setback of 1.47 feet, whereas the required minimum is 9.19 feet, resulting in a
variation of 7.72 feet (84%); and (d) a variation from provisions of Section
17.64.060 pertaining to the conforming uses of nonconforming buildings, all to
permit the expansion of the existing nonconforming detached garage, which is
closer to the street than the principal building, in accordance with the plans
and elevations submitted with the application for variations.
SECTION 3:   This Ordinance is passed by the Council of the Village of Winnetka
in the exercise of its home rule powers pursuant to Section 6 of Article VII of
the Illinois Constitution of 1970.
SECTION 4:   This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon its passage,
approval and posting as provided by law.
PASSED this 15th day of January, 2008, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
AYES:    Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers, Poor, Ritchell and Tucker
NAYS:    None
ABSENT: None
APPROVED this 15th day of January, 2008.
   Signed:
   s/Edmund C. Woodbury
   Village President
Countersigned:
s/Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: December 18, 2007
Posted: December 19, 2007
Passed and Approved: January 15, 2008
Posted: January 16, 2008
 
2008
ORDINANCE NO. M-1-2008
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A SPECIAL USE PERMIT FOR A HEALTH CLUB FACILITY AT 906-
908 GREEN BAY ROAD
WHEREAS, the Village of Winnetka is a home rule municipality in accordance with
Article VII, Section 6 of the Constitution of the State of Illinois of 1970,
pursuant to which it has the authority, except as limited by said Section 6 of
Article VII, to exercise any power and perform any function pertaining to the
government and affairs of the Village; and
WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka (“Village Council”) find that
establishing standards for the use and development of lands and buildings
within the Village and establishing and applying criteria for variations from
those standards are matters pertaining to the affairs of the Village; and
WHEREAS, North Hermitage Building Trust (the “Owner”) is the owner of the
following described real estate (the “Subject Property”), which is commonly
known as 906-908 Green Bay Road:
Lot 11 in Block 6 in Jared Gage’s Subdivision of part of the East half of the
North West quarter. Also part of the West half of the North West quarter of
Fractional Section 17, Township 42 North, Range 13, also part of the East half
of the Southwest quarter of Fractional Section 8, Township 42, North, Range 13,
East of the Third Principal Meridian, as recorded February 8, 1872 in Book 1 on
Page 25, as document number 12837, excepting the Westerly 16 feet of said Lot
11 taken for alley, in the Village of Winnetka, Cook County, Illinois; and
WHEREAS, the Subject Property is located in the Hubbard Woods business
district, on the west side of Green Bay Road between Tower Road and Gage
Street, in the C-2 Retail Overlay Zoning District provided for in Chapter 17.44
of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code; and
WHEREAS, the Subject Property is improved with a 50-foot wide by 121-foot deep,
one-story building that was constructed in 1937 and that includes an additional
upper-level mezzanine space at the rear, which adds an additional 880 square
feet of floor space; and
WHEREAS, the Subject Property was formerly occupied by Roche Bobois, a high-end
furniture retailer, and has been vacant since that business closed; and
WHEREAS, the Owner has renovated and reconfigured the space in the Subject
Property from one large retail space to two retail spaces that will extend
approximately 73 feet back from Green Bay Road, and common service space and
one space for lease at the rear of the building; and
WHEREAS, on October 10, 2007, North Hermitage Building Trust filed an
application for a special use permit to allow a health club facility, known as
Pilates Barre, to be located in the newly renovated space at the rear of the
building located on the Subject Property; and
WHEREAS, pursuant to Section 17.44.010 (B)(2)(e) of the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, health clubs are permitted as
special uses in the C-2 Overlay Zoning District, subject to the conditions and
requirements set forth in Chapter 17.44 and further subject to the conditions
and requirements pertaining to special uses set forth in Chapter 17.56 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance; and
WHEREAS, on November 28, 2007, on due notice thereof, the Plan Commission
convened to consider the requested special use, at which time the ten members
of the Plan Commission who were present voted unanimously in favor of
recommending approval of the special use permit, finding the proposed special
use to be in compliance with the Comprehensive Plan; and
WHEREAS, on December 10, 2007, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals held a public hearing to consider the special use permit and by the
unanimous vote of the five members then present, voted to recommend approval of
the request; and
WHEREAS, the proposed health club facility will occupy the rear 60 feet of the
renovated building, and will be accessed from Green Bay Road, through the
building's center hallway; and
WHEREAS, the proposed health club facility will have a total area of 2,700
square feet consisting of one large room on the north side of the ground floor,
with an adjacent mezzanine and step-down lower level on the south side; and
WHEREAS, the proposed use will provide one-on-one physical training, training
in groups of four, and exercise or dance classes in groups of approximately
eight, all of which will occur pursuant to pre-set class schedules or fixed
appointments; and
WHEREAS, the facility will have a total of three employees, with that being the
maximum number of employees at the facility at any one time; and
WHEREAS, the proposed hours of business will be from 6:00 a.m. to 7:00 p.m.
Monday through Friday and early on Saturday and Sunday, with the busiest hours
expected to be between 6:00 a.m. and 10:00 a.m.; and
WHEREAS, the proposed classes and training sessions have been scheduled so that
the maximum number of people on the premises, when training and classes occur
simultaneously, will be approximately 24 and will occur during off-peak hours;
and
WHEREAS, no on-site parking is available, although there is adjacent on-street
parking on Green Bay Road and the Subject Property is also in close proximity
to the Tower Road Parking Lot; and
WHEREAS, the separate proceedings before the Zoning Board of Appeals and the
Plan Commission both included questioning of the applicant by members of the
Zoning Board of Appeals and the Plan Commission; and
WHEREAS, none of the owners of the properties located within 250 feet of the
Subject Property submitted any evidence or requested an opportunity to cross-
examine witnesses at either the Zoning Board of Appeals hearing or the Plan
Commission meeting; and
WHEREAS, the record includes testimony from neighboring businesses supporting
the requested special use; and
WHEREAS, the proceedings of the Zoning Board of Appeals and Plan Commission
conformed with all requirements of their procedural rules, the Winnetka Village
Code and applicable statutes of the State of Illinois; and
WHEREAS, the Village Engineer has reviewed the traffic and parking study
prepared by the applicant’s engineers and has determined that the proposed
health club facility will have a negligible impact on traffic and parking; and
WHEREAS, the use of the rear building space for the proposed special use does
not irreversibly alter the nature of the space, which can be returned to retail
use in the event the health club facility use vacates the space; and
WHEREAS, the proposed special use does not alter the retail character of the
building, as the principal use of the building will remain retail, with two 73-
foot deep retail spaces having a combined area of 3,300 square feet, and each
with display window frontages on Green Bay Road; and
WHEREAS, the owner submitted two prior special use applications in 2007, the
first of which would have reduced the front retail space to a depth of 50 feet,
and the second of which would have placed an unspecified office use at the rear
of the building; and
WHEREAS, the proposed special use application is substantially different from
the prior requests, in that it provides more retail space by extending such
space deeper into the building, and provides for a specific non-retail use that
will bring more foot traffic directly into the building and past the retail
uses; and
WHEREAS, the proposed special use will neither endanger nor be detrimental to
the public health, safety, comfort, morals or general welfare, in that
situating a health club at the rear of the building will retain retail uses at
the street frontage, and will thus maintain the continuity of the retail block
face, while providing for a health club facility; and
WHEREAS, the proposed special use will not either substantially diminish or
impair property values in the immediate vicinity, in that (i) the health club
facility space will be at the rear of the building and will not impact the
retail appearance of the building from the street, (ii) the proposed
reconfiguration will allow an older building to be brought up to modern retail
standards; and (iii) the proposed special use will provide steady foot traffic
past the other retail uses in the building, and may also draw new patrons to
the other commercial businesses located along Green Bay Road, all of which will
be beneficial to the immediate vicinity; and
WHEREAS, subject to the terms and conditions of this Ordinance, the proposed
special use will not be substantially injurious to the use and enjoyment of
land in the immediate vicinity for uses permitted by right in the C-
2 Commercial Overlay Zoning District, because the proposed special use
complements the retail space at the building’s street frontage, and a health
club is a compatible use in the retail zoning districts; and
WHEREAS, adequate measures have been taken to provide ingress and egress in a
manner that minimizes pedestrian and vehicular traffic congestion in the public
ways, in that the front and rear exits of the building will be maintained, the
interior spaces will be connected by a corridor, and the reconfigured space
will continue to use the existing streets, sidewalks and access routes to the
Subject Property; and
WHEREAS, the parking impact of the proposed increase in space available for a
health club use will be negligible, and the regular turnover of patrons and
peak hours of operation may be slightly beneficial in terms of on-street
parking adjacent to the site; and
WHEREAS, adequate parking, utilities, access roads, drainage and other
facilities necessary for the operation of the special use already exist; and
WHEREAS, because the proposed special use will not change the appearance of the
immediate vicinity, and will maintain the retail appearance of the building, it
is consistent with the Winnetka 2020 objective to “ensure that Commercial,
institutional, and residential development is appropriate to the character of
and minimizes the adverse impact on its surrounding neighborhood;” and
WHEREAS, because of its minimal intensity and the pre-existing infrastructure,
the proposed special use is consistent with the Winnetka 2020 objectives to:
(a) “limit commercial, institutional and residential development within the
Village to minimize potentially adverse impacts on adjacent residential
neighborhoods and to prevent the need for significant increases in
infrastructure and other community resources;” (b) “ensure that development
proposals minimize the potential adverse impact they might have on residential
neighborhoods, including the impact on pedestrian character, on-site parking,
traffic patterns, congestion, open space, storm water management and Village
infrastructure;" and (c) "ensure that new development does not decrease the
public parking supply, particularly on-street parking that supports retail
use;” and
WHEREAS, the proposed special use is consistent with the Winnetka 2020 goal to
“provide for a wide range of office/service and retail commercial land uses and
development within the existing business districts in the Corridor.”
NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
SECTION 1:   The foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the findings of
the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth herein.
SECTION 2:   Pursuant to Section 17.44.010(B)(2) and Section 17.56.010 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, and subject to the terms and conditions hereinafter
set forth, a special use is hereby granted to the Subject Property, commonly
known as 906-908 Green Bay Road, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the C-
2 General Retail Commercial Overlay Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.44
of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, to
allow the rear of the existing building to be reconfigured for a health club
facility, in accordance with the plans and elevations presented in the Village
Council’s agenda materials.
SECTION 3:   The special use permit hereby granted is subject to the condition
that the reconfigured space at the rear of the building shall be used only for
a health club facility as enumerated in Section 17.44.010(B) of the Winnetka
Zoning Ordinance.
SECTION 4 :   The stipulations, conditions and restrictions set forth in the
foregoing Section 3 of this Ordinance may be modified or revised from time to
time by the Village Council following public notice and hearing, following the
procedures specified in Section 17.56 of the Winnetka Village Code for
processing special use applications.
SECTION 5:   This Ordinance is passed by the Council of the Village of Winnetka
in the exercise of its home rule powers pursuant to Section 6 of Article VII of
the Illinois Constitution of 1970.
SECTION 6:   This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon its passage,
approval and posting as provided by law.
PASSED this 7th day of February, 2008, pursuant to the following roll call
vote: 
