ORDINANCE NO. M-2-2008
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A SPECIAL USE PERMIT FOR A MEDICAL OFFICE ON A PORTION OF
THE GROUND FLOOR AT 564 LINCOLN AVENUE
WHEREAS, the Village of Winnetka is a home rule municipality in accordance with
Article VII, Section 6 of the Constitution of the State of Illinois of 1970,
pursuant to which it has the authority, except as limited by said Section 6 of
Article VII, to exercise any power and perform any function pertaining to the
government and affairs of the Village; and
WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka (“Village Council”) find that
establishing standards for the use and development of lands and buildings
within the Village and establishing and applying criteria for variations from
those standards are matters pertaining to the affairs of the Village; and
WHEREAS, Anitfield Finance Company, LLC is the owner of the following described
real estate (the “Subject Property”), which is commonly known as 564 Lincoln
Avenue:
Lot 1 in Cummins Winnetka Galleria Consolidation, being a subdivision of Lot 6
in Lewis D. Webster’s Resubdivision of Block 11 (except the North 75 feet
thereof), also part of Lot 11 and 12 in Block 12 in Park Addition to Winnetka
Subdivision by William H. Cairnduff, also part of Lot 15 in Block 13 in
Winnetka Park Bluffs, all in Section 20, Township 42, Range 13 East of the
Third Principal Meridian, according to the plat of said Cummins Winnetka
Galleria Consolidation recorded January 27, 1999 as Document Number 99087636,
in Cook County, Illinois; and
WHEREAS, the Subject Property is located in the East Elm business district, on
the west side of Lincoln Avenue between Elm Street and Pine Street, in the C-
2 Retail Overlay Zoning District provided for in Chapter 17.44 of the Winnetka
Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code; and
WHEREAS, the Subject Property is improved with a 2½-story, mixed use building
that was constructed in 1999 and includes first floor commercial space and
residential condominiums on the second and third floors ; and
WHEREAS, the building is designed with a shallow center courtyard along the
west side of Lincoln Avenue; and
WHEREAS, the building has retail space located along both the north and south
sides of the courtyard, with display windows along both Lincoln Avenue and the
courtyard; and
WHEREAS, the southern retail space was formerly occupied by a salon and day
spa, and has been vacant since that business closed in 2005; and
WHEREAS, pursuant to Section 17.44.010(B) of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance,
Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, certain uses are permitted only as
special uses in the C-2 Overlay Zoning District when they are located within
100 feet of a public street; and
WHEREAS, special uses granted pursuant to Section 17.44.010(B) are subject to
the conditions and requirements set forth in Chapter 17.44 and further subject
to the conditions and requirements pertaining to special uses set forth in
Chapter 17.56 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance; and
WHEREAS, on November 7, 2007, Anitfield Finance Company, LLC filed an
application for a special use permit pursuant to Section 17.44.010 (B)(2) of
the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, to allow a medical office use to be located at
the rear of the south portion of the first floor, beginning at a distance of
35.67 feet from the street; and
WHEREAS, on November 28, 2007, on due notice thereof, the Plan Commission
convened to consider the requested special use, at which time the ten members
of the Plan Commission who were present voted seven in favor and three against
to recommend approval of the special use permit, finding that the proposed
special use did comply with the Comprehensive Plan; and
WHEREAS, on December 10, 2007, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals held a public hearing to consider the special use permit and by a
unanimous vote of the five members then present, voted to recommend approval of
the request; and
WHEREAS, the applicant proposes to redevelop the Subject Property by
reconfiguring the south side of the first floor into three spaces, with one
retail space directly on Lincoln Avenue in the southeast corner of the
building, a second retail use in the front 35.67 feet of the building with
frontage on Lincoln Avenue and the courtyard, and a medical office beginning at
a distance of 35.68 feet from the street and extending 66.03 feet to the rear
building line; and
WHEREAS, each of the three spaces on the south side of the courtyard will be
served by a separate entrance, the two front retail space from entrances off
Lincoln Avenue and the medical office from an entrance at the rear of the
building courtyard, approximately 38 feet from Lincoln Ave.; and
WHEREAS, due to the relatively shallow depth of the building, all but the rear
1.7 feet of the medical office will be within 100 feet of Lincoln Avenue and
therefore requires a special use permit pursuant to Section 17.44.010(B) of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance; and
WHEREAS, all of the retail space on the north side of the courtyard is occupied
by a retail use and the applicant testified that he has obtained a retail
tenant for the front 35.67 feet of the retail space located adjacent to the
south side of the courtyard; and
WHEREAS, the separate proceedings before the Zoning Board of Appeals and the
Plan Commission both included questioning of the applicant by members of the
Zoning Board of Appeals and the Plan Commission; and
WHEREAS, none of the owners of the properties located within 250 feet of the
Subject Property submitted any evidence or requested an opportunity to cross-
examine witnesses at either the Zoning Board of Appeals hearing or the Plan
Commission meeting; and
WHEREAS, the record includes testimony from neighboring businesses supporting
the requested special use; and
WHEREAS, the proceedings of the Zoning Board of Appeals and Plan Commission
conformed with all requirements of their procedural rules, the Winnetka Village
Code and applicable statutes of the State of Illinois; and
WHEREAS, the building has sufficient depth to allow for viable, conforming
retail space in the front of the building and medical office space at the rear
of the Subject Property; and
WHEREAS, the reconfigured retail space is of a substantial enough size that it
has already attracted a retail businesses that will add to the mix of quality
retail businesses already located in the East Elm business district; and
WHEREAS, the applicant has made a reasonable effort to develop the Subject
Property as zoned without a special use, and the special use is necessary to
reconfigure the existing space to accommodate the retail tenant that would be
leasing a portion of the first floor space; and
WHEREAS, the continued vacancy of the south side of the courtyard jeopardizes
the viability of the retail business on the north side of the courtyard, which
is at the fringe of the C-2 Retail Overlay District; and
WHEREAS, the proposed special use will support the adjoining retail uses, in
that it will not reduce street exposure for retail occupancies, and it is being
requested for an established medical practice that will generate a steady
stream of foot traffic through the courtyard and past the adjoining retail
spaces throughout the business day; and
WHEREAS, the medical practice for which the special use is being sought already
exists in the neighborhood and the Village Engineer has determined that the
proposed relocation of the medical office to larger space on the Subject
Property will have a negligible impact on traffic and parking; and
WHEREAS, the applicant has defined the nature of the proposed special use with
sufficient specificity by limiting the request to a medical office use; and
WHEREAS, the proposed special use will neither endanger nor be detrimental to
the public health, safety, comfort, morals or general welfare, in that
situating a medical office at the rear of the building will retain retail uses
at the street frontage, and will thus maintain the continuity of the retail
block face, while providing for compatible office uses; and
WHEREAS, the proposed special use will not either substantially diminish or
impair property values in the immediate vicinity, in that the medical office
space will be at the rear of the building and will not impact the retail
appearance of the building from the street; and
WHEREAS, subject to the terms and conditions of this Ordinance, the proposed
special use will not be substantially injurious to the use and enjoyment of
land in the immediate vicinity for uses permitted by right in the C-
2 Commercial Overlay Zoning District, because the proposed special use will
facilitate the leasing of retail space at the building’s street frontage, and
medical office space is a compatible use in the retail zoning districts; and
WHEREAS, adequate measures have been taken to provide ingress and egress in a
manner that minimizes pedestrian and vehicular traffic congestion in the public
ways, in that the front and rear exits of the building will be maintained, and
the reconfigured space will continue to use the existing streets, sidewalks and
access routes to the Subject Property; and
WHEREAS, the parking impact of the proposed increase in space available for
medical office use will be negligible, and the regular turnover of patrons of
professional services may be slightly beneficial in terms of on-street parking
adjacent to the site; and
WHEREAS, adequate parking, utilities, access roads, drainage and other
facilities necessary for the operation of the special use already exist; and
WHEREAS, because the proposed special use will not change the appearance of the
immediate vicinity, it is consistent with the Winnetka 2020 objective to
“ensure that commercial, institutional, and residential development is
appropriate to the character of and minimizes the adverse impact on its
surrounding neighborhood;” and
WHEREAS, because of its minimal intensity and the pre-existing infrastructure,
the proposed special use is consistent with the Winnetka 2020 objectives to:
(a) “limit commercial, institutional and residential development within the
Village to minimize potentially adverse impacts on adjacent residential
neighborhoods and to prevent the need for significant increases in
infrastructure and other community resources;” (b) “ensure that development
proposals minimize the potential adverse impact they might have on residential
neighborhoods, including the impact on pedestrian character, on-site parking,
traffic patterns, congestion, open space, storm water management and Village
infrastructure;" and (c) "ensure that new development does not decrease the
public parking supply, particularly on-street parking that supports retail
use;” and
WHEREAS, the proposed special use is consistent with the Winnetka 2020 goal to
“provide for a wide range of office/service and retail commercial land uses and
development within the existing business districts in the Corridor.”
NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
SECTION 1:   The foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the findings of
the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth herein.
SECTION 2:   Pursuant to Section 17.44.010(B)(2) and Section 17.56.010 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, and subject to the terms and conditions hereinafter
set forth, a special use is hereby granted to the Subject Property, commonly
known as 564 Lincoln Avenue, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the C-2 General
Retail Commercial Overlay Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.44 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, to allow the
existing building to be reconfigured for medical office space, beginning at a
distance of 35.67 feet from the street frontage, as depicted in the plans
submitted by the applicant, in accordance with the plans and elevation
presented in the Village Council’s agenda materials.
SECTION 3:   The special use permit hereby granted is subject to the condition
that the reconfigured space at the rear of the building shall be used only for
medical offices as enumerated in Section 17.44.010(B) of the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance.
SECTION 4 :   The stipulations, conditions and restrictions set forth in the
foregoing Section 3 of this Ordinance may be modified or revised from time to
time by the Village Council following public notice and hearing, following the
procedures specified in Section 17.56 of the Winnetka Village Code for
processing special use applications.
SECTION 5:   This Ordinance is passed by the Council of the Village of Winnetka
in the exercise of its home rule powers pursuant to Section 6 of Article VII of
the Illinois Constitution of 1970.
SECTION 6:   This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon its passage,
approval and posting as provided by law.
PASSED this 7th day of February, 2008, pursuant to the following roll call
vote: 
AYES:    Trustees Behles, Berger, Eilers, Poor, Ritchell, Tucker
NAYS:    None
ABSENT:    None
APPROVED this 7th day of February, 2008.
   Signed:
   /s/Edmund C. Woodbury
   Village President
Countersigned:
/s/Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
