ORDINANCE NO. M-4-2008
AN ORDINANCE DESIGNATING A LANDMARK PURSUANT TO CHAPTER 15.64 OF THE WINNETKA
VILLAGE CODE (592 Cherry Street)
WHEREAS, the Village of Winnetka is a home rule municipality in accordance with
Article VII, Section 6 of the Constitution of the State of Illinois of 1970,
pursuant to which it has the authority, except as limited by said Section 6 of
Article VII, to exercise any power and perform any function pertaining to the
government and affairs of the Village; and
WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka (“Village Council”) find that
the identification, designation and preservation of buildings and structures in
the Village that are historically, culturally, and architecturally significant,
and the encouragement of the restoration and rehabilitation of those buildings
and structures are matters pertaining to the affairs of the Village; and
WHEREAS, the property commonly known as 592 Cherry Street, Winnetka, Illinois,
(“Subject Property”) is legally described as follows:
The North 120 feet (lying South of the South line of street North and
adjoining) of the East 93 ½ feet (lying West of the West Line of street East
and adjoining) of Block 47 of the Original Village of Winnetka, in Section 21,
Township 42 North, Range 13, East of the Third Principal Meridian, in Cook
County, Illinois;
and
WHEREAS, the owner of the Subject Property has submitted an application seeking
designation of the Subject Property as a certified Village of Winnetka
landmark; and
WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka has enacted Chapter 15.64 of
Title 15 of the Winnetka Village Code, titled “Landmark Preservation,” for the
purposes of preserving, protecting, enhancing, rehabilitating and regulating
buildings, structures, objects, and places of historical, cultural or
architectural importance; and
WHEREAS, pursuant to the provisions of Chapter 15.64 of the Winnetka Village
Code (the “Landmark Ordinance”) and notice duly published and sent, the
Landmark Preservation Commission conducted a public hearing on December 3,
2007, to consider the application for certified landmark designation and
unanimously recommended that the residence on the Subject Property be
designated a Winnetka landmark; and
WHEREAS, the Landmark Preservation Commission applied the Village’s System for
the Evaluation of Landmarks to the information received at the hearing and gave
the home, known as the Joseph R. Gay House, an overall score of 84.4, with a
rating of “Unique,” and made findings regarding the Subject Property, including
the following:
(1)   The Commission rated the architectural type, style and period of the
Joseph R. Gay House to be “Extremely Rare,” rare in that: (a) the house was
constructed in 1896, in the Queen Anne style; (b) the house is unique in its
symmetry and has a variety of shapes, colors and textures; (c) the house is
featured in Winnetka Architecture:  Where Past is Present, published by the
Winnetka Historical Museum; and
(2)   The Commission rated the method of construction to be “Extremely Rare;”
and
(3)   The Commission rated the home as being of “National Significance,”
because it is associated with Dinsmore Ely, a person of historical and national
significance in that: (a) he was a pilot in the Army in World War I who lost
his life in the war; and (b) in a letter written shortly before his death he
stated, “It is an investment, not a loss, when a man dies for his country,”
(4)   The Commission found the residence to be a symbol of the neighborhood in
the context of the entire Village, given its prominent location on the corner
of Cherry and Walnut Streets, warranting a score of 5;
(5)   The Commission found the home to be an excellent example of originality,
since very few renovations have been done to the residence and the structure
was scraped and repainted its original color, causing the Commission to rate
the design integrity of the alterations a 4, or “Good; and”
(6)   The age of the house was given a high rate, as it was constructed in
1896; and
(7)   The Commission rated the home’s alteration of surrounding properties a
score of 3 for “Minor” since most of the properties surrounding 592 Cherry are
unchanged, with the exception of a new home across the street; and
(8)   The restoration process has been diligently undertaken, and the
structural condition of the home was rated “Exceptional” with a score of 5; and
WHEREAS, pursuant to the findings entered at the December 3, 2007, meeting of
the Landmark Preservation Commission, the members then present concluded that
the residence located at 592 Cherry Street is unique and meets the criteria of
the Landmark Ordinance, and have unanimously voted to recommend that it be
designated a Winnetka landmark; and
WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka have considered the
recommendation of the Landmark Preservation Commission and have determined that
it is in the best interest of the Village and its residents to accept the
findings and recommendation of the Commission and to establish the house
located at 592 Cherry Street as a designated landmark, because it is
significant in its style and design, its excellent structural condition and its
association with a nationally prominent figure.
NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
SECTION 1 :   The foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the findings of
the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth herein
SECTION 2:   The residence located on the property at 592 Cherry Street,
permanent real estate index number 05-21-123-005 is hereby designated a
certified landmark under Section 15.64.070 the Landmark Preservation Ordinance,
in accordance with the findings and recommendations of the Landmark
Preservation Commission.
SECTION 3:   This Ordinance is passed by the Council of the Village of Winnetka
in the exercise of its home rule powers pursuant to Section 6 of Article VII of
the Illinois Constitution of 1970.
SECTION 4:   This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon its passage,
approval and posting as provided by law.
PASSED this 1st day of April, 2008, pursuant to the following roll call vote: 
