ORDINANCE NO. M-8-2008
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A SPECIAL USE PERMIT TO ALLOW THE EXPANSION A HEALTH CLUB
FACILITY AT 813 OAK STREET
WHEREAS, the Village of Winnetka is a home rule municipality in accordance with
Article VII, Section 6 of the Constitution of the State of Illinois of 1970,
pursuant to which it has the authority, except as limited by said Section 6 of
Article VII, to exercise any power and perform any function pertaining to the
government and affairs of the Village; and
WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka (“Village Council”) find that
establishing standards for the use and development of lands and buildings
within the Village and establishing and applying criteria for variations from
those standards are matters pertaining to the affairs of the Village; and
WHEREAS, the property commonly known as 813 Oak Street, Winnetka, Illinois (the
“Subject Property”), is legally described as follows:
Parcel 2: The South 53 feet of part of Block 26 in the Village of Winnetka,
lying West of the East line of Lot 7 in Oak Knoll Subdivision of said part of
Block 26 in the Northeast ¼ of the Northeast ¼ of Section 20, Township 42
North, Range 13 East of the Third Principal Meridian, in Cook County, Illinois.
Parcel 3: Lot 6 (except the North 41 feet taken for Chestnut Court) in Oak
Knoll Subdivision of that part of Block 26 in the Village of Winnetka in the
Northeast ¼ of Section 20, Township 42 North, Range 13 East of the Third
Principal Meridian, described as follows: Commencing at a point 37 feet East of
the Northeast corner of Chestnut and Oak Streets; thence North 187 feet; thence
East and parallel with the South line of said Block 150 feet; thence South 30
feet; thence East 8 feet; thence South 32 feet; thence West 8 feet; thence
South 125 feet; thence west 150 feet to the point of beginning, according to
the Plat of said Oak Knoll Subdivision recorded in the Office of the Recorder
of Cook County, Illinois in Book 119 of Plats, Page 26 as Document 4991672 all
in Cook County, Illinois; and
WHEREAS, the Subject Property is located in the C-2 General Retail Commercial
Overlay Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.44 of the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code; and
WHEREAS, on March 5, 2008, the lessee of the Subject Property filed an
application for a special use permit to allow the expansion of a health club
facility, known as “Spynergy,” which is located on the ground floor of the
building located on the Subject Property; and
WHEREAS, pursuant to Section 17.44.010 (B)(2)(e) of the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, health clubs are permitted as
special uses in the C-2 Overlay Zoning District, subject to the conditions and
requirements set forth in Chapter 17.44 and further subject to the conditions
and requirements pertaining to special uses set forth in Chapter 17.56 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance; and
WHEREAS, on April 14, 2008, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of Appeals
conducted a public hearing on the requested special use and, by the unanimous
vote of the six members then present, has reported to the Council recommending
that the special use permit be granted; and
WHEREAS, on April 23, 2008, on due notice thereof, the Plan Commission
considered the requested special use and, by the unanimous vote of the nine
members then present, found the proposed special use to be consistent with the
Comprehensive Plan, Winnetka 2020, and has reported to the Council recommending
that the special use be granted; and
WHEREAS, the record of proceedings before the Zoning Board of Appeals and Plan
Commission consists of: (a) the application for special use, including a
traffic impact analysis and a supplementary description of the business; (b)
GIS and ALTA/ACSM survey maps depicting the location of the subject Property;
(c) a scale drawing of the floor plan of the Subject Property that is the
subject of the special use; (d) a parking survey prepared by Kenig, Lindgren,
O’Hara and Aboona (KLOA); (e) a March 21, 2008 memo from the Village Engineer
to the Zoning Board of Appeals commenting on traffic and parking issues; and
(f) photographs of existing studio facilities; and
WHEREAS, the Subject Property is improved with a one-story building which
extends from Chestnut Court to Oak Street, between Chestnut Street and Green
Bay Road (Linden Avenue), and has been divided into two separate lease
premises; and
WHEREAS, the current facility occupies approximately 850 square feet at the
rear of the building on the north side of Oak Street, of which approximately
620 square feet is an exercise studio; and
WHEREAS, the applicant proposes to expand the facility to the north and east by
removing an existing demising wall and building a new wall to create a larger
space; and
WHEREAS, the proposed expanded health facility will have a total area of 1,187
square feet and will include (a) a studio space for 38 exercise bicycles, as
well as two additional back-up bicycles, (b) storage space for clients’
personal belongings, (c) a waiting area, (d) two restrooms, and (e) a laundry
closet; and
WHEREAS, the proposed expanded health club facility will provide group spinning
classes of 35 riders per class; and
WHEREAS, the existing facility operates from 6:00 a.m. to 10:30 a.m.. Monday
through Friday and from 6:00 a.m. to 8:30 a.m. on Saturday, with the busiest
hours between 8:30 a.m. and 10:30; and
WHEREAS, although no on-site parking is available, there is adjacent on-street
parking on Oak Street and the Subject Property is also in close proximity to
the Village Hall Parking Lot; and
WHEREAS, the KLOA parking survey concludes that there is adequate parking in
the area to meet the demands of the proposed expansion; and
WHEREAS, the Village Engineer has reviewed the KLOA parking survey and concurs
with KLOA’s finding that there is a surplus of available parking in the
immediate vicinity, and that in the event there is an increase in patrons, the
impact on the parking in the vicinity will be minimal; and
WHEREAS, the proceedings before the Zoning Board of Appeals included
questioning of the applicant and the owner by members of the Zoning Board of
Appeals; and
WHEREAS, none of the owners of the properties located within 250 feet of the
Subject Property submitted any evidence or requested an opportunity to cross-
examine witnesses at either the Zoning Board of Appeals hearing or at the Plan
Commission proceedings; and
WHEREAS, the proceedings of the Zoning Board of Appeals and Plan Commission
conformed with all requirements of their procedural rules, the Winnetka Village
Code and applicable statutes of the State of Illinois; and
WHEREAS, the proposed special use will neither endanger nor be detrimental to
the public health, safety, comfort, morals or general welfare, in that the
proposed use is compatible with the immediate neighborhood, and will expand a
small, existing health club facility in a space that has no windows facing the
street and is not desirable for retail purposes; and
WHEREAS, the proposed special use will neither substantially diminish nor
impair property values in the immediate vicinity, in that (a) it will have a
minimal impact on the immediate neighborhood, and (b) may draw new patrons to
the other commercial businesses located in the West Elm business district; and
WHEREAS, adequate measures have been taken to provide ingress and egress in a
manner that minimizes pedestrian and vehicular traffic congestion in the public
ways, in that: (a) the facility is easily accessible from Oak Street; and (b)
the peak operating hours occur before most retail establishments in the West
Elm business district are open for business; and
WHEREAS, adequate parking, utilities, access roads, drainage and other
facilities necessary for the operation of the special use already exist; and
WHEREAS, because the proposed special use will not change the appearance of the
immediate vicinity, it is consistent with the Winnetka 2020 objective to
“ensure that Commercial, institutional, and residential development is
appropriate to the character of and minimizes the adverse impact on its
surrounding neighborhood;” and
WHEREAS, because of its minimal intensity and the pre-existing infrastructure,
the proposed special use is consistent with the Winnetka 2020 objectives to:
(a) “limit commercial, institutional and residential development within the
Village to minimize potentially adverse impacts on adjacent residential
neighborhoods and to prevent the need for significant increases in
infrastructure and other community resources,” and (b) “ensure that development
proposals minimize the potential adverse impact they might have on residential
neighborhoods, including the impact on pedestrian character, on site parking,
traffic patterns, congestion, open space, storm water management and Village
infrastructure;” and
WHEREAS, the proposed special use is consistent with the Winnetka 2020 goal to
“provide for a wide range of office/service and retail commercial land uses and
development within the existing business districts in the Corridor;” and
WHEREAS, the proposed special use is consistent with the Winnetka 2020
objective to “ensure that new development does not decrease public parking
supply,” and;
WHEREAS, because it adds a use that is complementary to the commercial retail
operations in the immediate vicinity, without using prime commercial space with
street exposure, the proposed special use is consistent with the goals of
Winnetka 2020 to: (a) “promote a strong community identity and opportunities to
interact while building a healthy commercial tax base,” and (b) “maintain the
essential quality, viability and attractiveness of Winnetka’s business
districts while encouraging new economic development consistent with the
character of the Village and the individual business districts.”
NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
SECTION 1:  The foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the findings of
the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth herein.
SECTION 2: Subject to the terms and conditions set forth in Section 3 of this
Ordinance, and pursuant to Section 17.44.010 (B)(2)(e) and Section 17.56.010 of
the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, a special use is hereby granted to the Subject
Property, commonly known as 813 Oak Street, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in
the C-2 General Retail Commercial Overlay Zoning District, to allow for an
expansion of the existing health club facility on the Subject Property, known
as “Spynergy,” all in accordance with the plans submitted with the application.
SECTION 3: The special use granted by this ordinance is subject to the
following conditions:
A.   Nor more than 38 exercise bicycles shall be set up for use in the studio
at one time.
B.   No more than two additional exercise bicycles shall be stored on the
premises.
C.   No other exercise equipment shall be permitted unless the number of
exercise bicycles is reduced and the Director of Community Development
determines, in writing, that the change will not substantially alter the nature
of the use and will not result in more than 38 people being in any class.
D.   No equipment or other items used in the operation of the facility shall be
placed outside the building.
SECTION 4 : The stipulations, conditions and restrictions set forth in the
foregoing Section 3 of this Ordinance may be modified or revised from time to
time by the Village Council following public notice and hearing, following the
procedures specified in Section 17.56 of the Winnetka Village Code for
processing special use applications.
SECTION 5: This Ordinance is passed by the Council of the Village of Winnetka
in the exercise of its home rule powers pursuant to Section 6 of Article VII of
the Illinois Constitution of 1970.
SECTION 6: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon its passage,
approval and posting as provided by law.
PASSED this 3rd day of June, 2008, pursuant to the following roll call vote: 
AYES:    Trustees Berger, Greable, Johnson, Poor and Rintz
NAYS:    None
ABSENT:    President Woodbury
APPROVED this 3rd day of June, 2008.
   Signed:
   ss/Kenneth L. Behles
   Acting Village President
Countersigned:
ss/Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
