ORDINANCE NO. M-2-2009
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA, COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (1509 Scott)
 
   WHEREAS, the Village of Winnetka is a home rule municipality in accordance
with Article VII, Section 6 of the Constitution of the State of Illinois of
1970, pursuant to which it has the authority, except as limited by said Section
6 of Article VII, to exercise any power and perform any function pertaining to
the government and affairs of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka (“Village Council”) find
that establishing standards for the use and development of lands and buildings
within the Village and establishing and applying criteria for variations from
those standards are matters pertaining to the affairs of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the property commonly known as 1509 Scott Street, Winnetka,
Illinois (the “Subject Property”), is legally described as follows:
      Lot 16 in Block 28 in Chicago North Shore Land Company’s Subdivision in
Sections 8, 17, and 18, Township 42 North, Range 13 East of the Third Principal
Meridian, in Cook County, Illinois; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property is located in the R-5 Zoning District provided
in Chapter 17.12 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka
Village Code; and
   WHEREAS, on November 5, 2008, the owner of the Subject Property filed an
application for the following variations from the Lot, Space, Bulk and Yard
Regulations for Single Family Residential Districts established by Chapter
17.30 of the Zoning Ordinance: (a) a variation from the maximum building size
limitations of Section 17.30.040 to allow a gross floor area of 3,566.86 square
feet, whereas the maximum permitted is 3,191.2 square feet, resulting in a
variation of 375.66 square feet (11.77%); (b) a variation from the side yard
setback requirements of Section 17.30.060 to allow an east side yard setback of
5.84 feet, whereas a minimum side yard of 8 feet is required, resulting in a
variation of 2.16 feet (27%); and (c) a variation from the building line
provisions of Section 17.30.090 to allow an unarticulated west side building
wall length of 50.05 feet, whereas articulation of 18 inches is required within
35 feet of the front building line, all of said variations being requested in
order to permit the construction of a one-story addition at the front of the
existing residence, a two-story addition at the rear, and a second story over
the front-facing attached garage; and
   WHEREAS, on December 8, 2008, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variations and, by the
favorable vote of six of the seven members then present, has reported to the
Council recommending that the requested variations be granted; and
   WHEREAS, on January 20, 2009, at the request of the applicant, the Village
Council tabled the requested variation to allow the applicant the opportunity
to modify the plans and reduce the degree of variations requested; and
   WHEREAS, the original application proposed to add 709.56 square feet of
gross floor area through the construction of the following additions: (i) a
one-story addition to the living room measuring 12.08 feet by 5 feet and a
proposed front entrance addition of 5.68 feet by 2.96 feet; (ii) a two-story
addition at the rear of the residence that will consist of a 14-foot by 11-foot
breakfast room on the first floor and a master bath and walk-in closet on the
second floor that will measure 16 feet by 11 feet and 7 feet by 1.5 feet,
respectively; and (iii) a second story addition over the attached garage that
will measure 12.25 feet by 28.36 feet, and will consist of a new bedroom, a
laundry room and a bathroom and will allow the existing fourth bedroom to be
converted into an office; and
   WHEREAS, on February 9, 2009, the applicants submitted modified plans that
reduce the proposed additional gross floor area by a total of 114.22 square
feet, to 595.34 square feet, so that the proposed gross floor area is now
3,452.64 square feet, resulting in a requested gross floor area variation of
261.44 square feet (8.19%); and
   WHEREAS, the reduced size of the proposed additions has been accomplished
through the following plan modifications: (i) eliminating the second floor
laundry room and providing for a laundry area in the first floor mud room; (ii)
reducing the size of a proposed new bedroom, thereby reducing the additional
square footage by 56.11 square feet; (iii) reducing the length of the master
bath and closet additions by two feet, thereby reducing the additional square
footage by 51 square feet; (iv) reducing the overhanging master bath bay by 6
inches; and (v) removing the master closet space from the addition and
incorporating it into the existing master bedroom; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties associated with carrying out the
strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect to the Subject Property
and the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted to be
used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that: (a)
the Subject Property is a legally nonconforming undersized lot with an area of
7,978 square feet and a lot width of 50.23 feet; (b) the Subject Property is
improved with a single family home that was built in 1950 and has a legally
nonconforming unarticulated west wall of 52.05 feet and a legally nonconforming
east side yard setback of 5.84 feet; (c) the room that serves as the fourth
bedroom of the residence is substandard at 9 x 11 feet with no closet; (d) due
to the location and design of the original building, no changes can be made to
the east or west side of the building without a variation from the setback and
articulation requirements; (e) both the setbacks and the gross floor area
calculation are affected by the deep eaves; (f) a master bath, family room and
mud room are considered standard amenities in homes throughout the Village; (g)
it is not possible to construct an eating area adjacent to the kitchen without
extending the building footprint to the north, because the area immediately
east of the kitchen is a small room with a low ceiling and functions as an
entryway from the rear door and the attached garage; (h) it is not reasonably
practical to have a basement laundry room due to the nonconforming height and
width of the basement stairs , as well as the low ceiling clearance in the
basement; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because the alterations to the front of the residence are
consistent with the existing neighborhood and most of the additional bulk will
be at the rear of the house, where it will not be visible from the street; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not impair an adequate supply of
light and air because the proposed additions will conform with applicable
height limitations; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property, as the proposed construction will comply
with all applicable building and fire protection codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not diminish the taxable value of
land and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed construction will not contribute to congestion on the
public streets, as the property will continue to be used for single family
residential purposes; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the requested variations will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations are in harmony with the general purpose
and intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that they allow the renovation,
restoration and rehabilitation of a structurally sound existing building while
maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the community and protecting
established trees and landscaping.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1:  The foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the findings
of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth herein.
   SECTION 2: The Subject Property, commonly known as 1509 Scott Street and
located in the R-5 Single-Family Residential District provided in Chapter 17.12
of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code is
hereby granted the following variations from the Lot, Space, Bulk and Yard
Regulations for Single Family Residential Districts established by Chapter
17.30 of the Zoning Ordinance: (a) a variation from the maximum building size
limitations of Section 17.30.040 to allow a gross floor area of 3,452.64 square
feet, whereas the maximum permitted is 3,191.2 square feet, resulting in a
variation of 261.44 square feet (8.19%); (b) a variation from the side yard
setback requirements of Section 17.30.060 to allow an east side yard setback of
5.84 feet, whereas a minimum of 8 feet is required, resulting in a variation of
2.16 feet (27%); and (c) a variation from the building line provisions of
Section 17.30.090 to allow an unarticulated west side building wall length of
50.05 feet, whereas articulation of 18 inches is required within 35 feet of the
front building line, all of said variations being requested in order to permit
the construction of additions at the front and rear of the residence, as well
as on both the first and second floor levels, all in accordance with the plans
and elevations submitted with the application for variations, as amended by the
plans submitted on February 9, 2009.
   SECTION 3:   This Ordinance is passed by the Council of the Village of
Winnetka in the exercise of its home rule powers pursuant to Section 6 of
Article VII of the Illinois Constitution of 1970.
   SECTION 4:   This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon its passage,
approval and posting as provided by law.
PASSED this 3rd day of March, 2009, pursuant to the following roll call vote: 
AYES:    Trustees Greable, Johnson, Rintz and President Woodbury   
NAYS:    Trustees Behles, Berger, Poor   
ABSENT:    None   
APPROVED this 3rd day of March, 2009.
   Signed:
   ss/Edmund C. Woodbury   
   Village President
Countersigned:
ss/Douglas G. Williams   
Village Clerk
