ORDINANCE NO. M-3-2009
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA, COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (941 Tower)
 
   WHEREAS, the Village of Winnetka is a home rule municipality in accordance
with Article VII, Section 6 of the Constitution of the State of Illinois of
1970, pursuant to which it has the authority, except as limited by said Section
6 of Article VII, to exercise any power and perform any function pertaining to
the government and affairs of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka (“Village Council”) find
that establishing standards for the use and development of lands and buildings
within the Village and establishing and applying criteria for variations from
those standards are matters pertaining to the affairs of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the property commonly known as 941 Tower Road, Winnetka, Illinois
(the “Subject Property”), is legally described as follows:
      Lot 2 in Wild Cherry Circle, being a subdivision of that part of Lot 6,
lying West of a straight line extending Northerly from a point in the South
line of said Lot 6, 89 feet 9 inches, East of the Southwest corner thereof to a
point in the Northerly line of said Lot 6, 95 feet 3 inches Easterly from the
Northwest corner thereof, in the subdivision of Lot 2 in the subdivision of
Block 13, in Hubbard Estate Subdivision in the Northeast ¼ of the Fractional
Section 17, Township 42 North, Range 13, East of the Third Principal Meridian,
in the Village of Winnetka, County of Cook, State of Illinois; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property is located in the R-2 Zoning District provided
in Chapter 17.24 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka
Village Code; and
   WHEREAS, on August 6, 2008, the owner of the Subject Property filed an
application for the following variations from requirements of the Lot, Space,
Bulk and Yard Regulations for Single Family Residential Districts established
by Chapter 17.30 of the Zoning Ordinance: (a) a variation from the front corner
yard setback requirements of Section 17.30.050 to allow an east front corner
yard setback of 2.11 feet, whereas a minimum of 23.35 feet is required,
resulting in a variation of 21.24 feet (90.96%); and (b) a variation from the
rear yard setback provisions of Section 17.30.070 to permit a north rear yard
setback of 13.2 feet, whereas the minimum required is 16.21 feet, resulting in
a variation of 3.01 feet (18.57%), in order to construct a new single family
residence on the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, on due notice thereof, the owner’s application was heard and
considered by the Zoning Board of Appeals at its meetings on October 13, 2008;
November 10, 2008; and December 8, 2008; and
   WHEREAS, on December 8, 2008, the Zoning Board of Appeals voted on the
requested variations and, by the favorable vote of 6 of the 7 members then
present, has reported to the Council recommending that the requested variations
be granted; and
   WHEREAS, the applicant has submitted modified plans, dated March 12, 2009,
which make the following changes: (i) reduce the ridge line of the proposed new
home from 33 feet to 29 feet and 7 inches; (ii) reduce the building height from
2½ stories to 2 stories; (iii) reduce the gross floor area by 229.29 square
feet to 2,947.77 square feet; and (iv) slightly increase the east front corner
yard setback from 2.11 feet to 2.29 feet, thereby reducing the extent of the
variation request for the east front corner yard setback from 21.24 feet
(90.96%) to 21.06 feet (90.19%); and
   WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka make the following findings
after having considered all of the evidence, as well as the respective
interests of the applicant, the Objectors and the general welfare of the
Village; and
   WHEREAS, neighbors of the Subject Property, represented by legal counsel,
appeared before the Zoning Board of Appeals and the Village Council and made
extensive arguments in opposition to the requested variations; and
   WHEREAS, the neighbors who opposed the requested variations (“Objectors”)
were allowed to present extensive evidence both before the Zoning Board of
Appeals and before the Village Council, including a detailed model of the
Subject Property and a shadow study of the effect of the house as originally
proposed on the property at 939 Tower Road on December 21; and
   WHEREAS, the Objectors’ model did not depict the large residence immediately
to the west of 939 Tower and did not reflect that the property at 939 Tower is
1.5 feet to 2 feet higher than the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, the Objectors’ shadow study did not take into consideration the
effect of shadows cast by the home on the property immediately to the west; and
   WHEREAS, the applicant presented extensive evidence to establish the grounds
for the requested variations and to respond to the evidence and arguments
presented by the opponents; and
   WHEREAS, the applicant’s shadow study of the effects that the house on 941
Tower, as originally proposed, would have on the property at 939 Tower, covered
the full year and included the shadows cast by the house immediately to the
west of the house at 939 Tower; and
   WHEREAS, the Council find that the Objectors’ arguments based on the local
landmark status of 939 Tower are not well-founded, as no changes are proposed
to the house at 939 Tower; and
   WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and particular hardships
associated with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance
with respect to the Subject Property in that: (a) the Subject Property is an
irregularly shaped trapezoidal lot with a width of 77.82 feet and a substandard
lot area of 8,409 square feet, which is 16,791 square feet less than the
requirement for the R-2 Zoning District; (b) the Subject Property is bisected
by an easement that serves several properties on the north side of Tower Road
and is therefore considered a street for zoning purposes; (c) the private
street curves from the southwest corner of the Subject Property, to its
northeast corner, dividing it into two roughly triangular portions; (d) because
of the private street easement, the Subject Property is considered a corner lot
under the Zoning Ordinance and is subject to front yard setbacks from both the
east and west side of the private street, as well as from Tower Road; (e) the
strict application the Zoning Ordinance setback requirements results in a
buildable area on the west side of the private road that has no right angles
and has an area of 1,100 square feet; and (f) the current residence on the
Subject Property is situated well beyond the north rear and east corner front
yard setbacks; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted
to be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that:
(a) the current residence on the Subject Property is dilapidated, has mold, and
does not contain standard amenities found in other homes throughout the
Village; (b) due to the placement of the private road easement on the Subject
Property, a new conforming home would be triangular and would have 6-foot wide
roofs; (c) the only suitable location for a home on the Subject Property is the
area proposed by the applicant, which will allow the home to comply with the
setback required along Tower Road; and (d) no usable home can be constructed on
the Subject Property without requesting zoning relief; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not alter the essential character of
the neighborhood because the proposed new construction will be located in the
same general area of the Subject Property as the existing home and will be
designed to be in keeping with the surrounding neighborhood; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not impair an adequate supply of
light and air because: (a) the new home will be farther from the north and west
lot lines than the existing home; (b) the proposed new construction complies
with all height and bulk requirements of the Zoning Ordinance and the revised
plans have been further reduced; and (c) although the view from the house at
939 Tower will be altered by virtue of the new construction, the shadow studies
show that the proposed new home will have a minimal impact on light and air,
because it will increase the distance between the house at 941 Tower and the
house at 939 Tower, and because the primary cause of shadow on the house at 939
Tower is the house on the property immediately to the west; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property, and safety may actually increase as a
result of the proposed new construction, which will replace a dilapidated
structure and will comply with all applicable building and fire protection
codes; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations will not diminish the taxable value of
land and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed new construction; and
   WHEREAS, the proposed construction will not contribute to congestion on the
public streets, as the property will continue to be used for single family
residential purposes; and
   WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the requested variations will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the requested variations are in harmony with the general purpose
and intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance of assuring redevelopment that is
consistent with the existing character and appearance of the neighborhood and
protecting established trees and landscaping.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
   SECTION 1:   The foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the findings
of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth herein.
   SECTION 2:   The Subject Property, commonly known as 941 Tower Road, and
located in the R-2 Single-Family Residential District provided in Chapter 17.24
of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code is
hereby granted the following variations from requirements of the Lot, Space,
Bulk and Yard Regulations for Single Family Residential Districts established
by Chapter 17.30 of the Zoning Ordinance: (a) a variation from the front corner
yard setback requirements of Section 17.30.050 to allow an east front corner
yard setback of 2.29 feet, whereas a minimum of 23.35 feet is required,
resulting in a variation of 21.06 feet (90.19%); and (b) a variation from the
rear yard setback provisions of Section 17.30.070 to permit a north rear yard
setback of 13.2 feet, whereas the minimum required is 16.21 feet, resulting in
a variation of 3.01 feet (18.57%), in order to construct a new single family
residence on the Subject Property, all in accordance with the plans and
elevations submitted with the application for variations.
   SECTION 3:   This Ordinance is passed by the Council of the Village of
Winnetka in the exercise of its home rule powers pursuant to Section 6 of
Article VII of the Illinois Constitution of 1970.
   SECTION 4:   This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon its passage,
approval and posting as provided by law.
PASSED this 21st day of April, 2009, pursuant to the following roll call vote: 
