ORDINANCE NO. M-6-2009
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING PRELIMINARY APPROVAL OF AN APPLICATION FOR A PLANNED
DEVELOPMENT AT 513-515 LINCOLN AVENUE AND 718-732 ELM STREET IN THE VILLAGE OF
WINNETKA (New Trier Partners)
 
   WHEREAS, NTP-Winnetka, LLC is the owner of the properties commonly known as
511 Lincoln Avenue and 718 – 732 Elm Street–– in the ––––Village– of
––Winnetka; and
   WHEREAS, NTP-Lincoln Avenue, LLC is the owner of the properties commonly
known as 513 – 515 Lincoln Avenue–– in the ––––Village– of ––Winnetka––; and
   WHEREAS, NTP-Winnetka, LLC and NTP-Lincoln Avenue, LLC, are both wholly
owned subsidiaries of New Trier Development, LLC, an Illinois limited liability
company; and
   WHEREAS, the properties commonly known as 511 Lincoln Avenue, 513 – 515
Lincoln Avenue, and 718 – 732 Lincoln Avenue in the Village of Winnetka
(collectively, the “Subject Property”) are legally described in Exhibit A,
which is attached hereto and incorporated by reference as if fully set forth
herein; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property is located in the C-2 General Retail
Commercial District provided in Chapter 17.44 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance,
Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property is also located in the C-2 Overlay District,
which provides that certain specified non-retail commercial uses are permitted
only pursuant to a special use permit; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property has an area of 36,245 square feet and is
therefore required to be developed in accordance with the Planned Development
regulations set out in Chapter 17.58 of the Winnetka Village Code (“Planned
Development Ordinance”); and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property is located on the south side of Elm Street,
east of Lincoln Avenue, and on the east side of Lincoln Avenue, south of Elm
Street; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property includes several one-story buildings that are
or have been occupied by retail uses at street level; and
   WHEREAS, the existing buildings on the part of the Subject Property that
lies along Elm Street also have a lower level that is or has been occupied by a
mixture of retail and non-retail commercial uses; and
   WHEREAS, the part of the Subject Property on the south side of Elm Street is
directly east of the buildings occupied by Phototronics and Conney’s Pharmacy
and directly west of the building occupied by Baird & Warner Real Estate and
several other office uses; and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property also includes a two-level parking area with
ingress by way of a driveway on Lincoln Avenue, at the south end of the Subject
Property, and egress by way of a driveway that empties onto Elm Street at the
east end of the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, on April 9, 2008, at the request of New Trier Development, LLC
(“Applicant”) the Zoning Administrator and other representatives of the Village
of Winnetka (“Village”) attended a pre-application conference with the
Applicant, as provided in Section 17.58.060 of the Planned Development
Ordinance, in which the Applicant presented a conceptual plan for a four-story,
single-building planned development for the Subject Property and received
comment thereon; and
   WHEREAS, on June 5, 2008, in an open meeting, pursuant to due notice and in
accordance with Section 17.58.080(D), the Design Review Board reviewed a
conceptual plan for the development of the Subject Property, dated May 14,
2008, for consistency with the Village of Winnetka Commercial Design Guidelines
and passed a motion stating that the Design Review Board favored the design,
aesthetics and overall design concept as presented in the Application and that
the architectural character of the proposed building is in keeping with the
goals of the Design Review Board, with the understanding that such details as
material, finishes, decorative finishes, awnings, signage, fenestration,
streetscape, landscaping, lighting, brick and the like would be clarified at a
later time in the process; and
   WHEREAS, on June 13, 2008, Applicant filed an application for a planned
development pursuant to the Planned Development Ordinance, seeking preliminary
approval of a planned development for the redevelopment of the Subject Property
with a four-story, mixed use building with retail uses on the first floor,
residential condominiums on the second, third and fourth floors, and
underground parking (“Application”); and
   WHEREAS, the Application also requested the following exceptions from and
modifications to the development standards for height limitation of 45 feet set
out in Section 17.58.040(C) of the Planned Development Ordinance: (i) an
exception from and modification to the building height to allow the proposed
building to have an average height of 49.5 feet, an excess of 4.5 feet over the
standard height limitation of 45 feet; and (ii) an exception from and
modification to the building height to allow the proposed building to have a
prominently placed turret detail with a height of 62.5 feet, an excess of 17.5
feet over the standard height limitation of 45 feet; and
   WHEREAS, the Application also requested the following exceptions from and
modifications to the development standards for intensity of use of lot set out
in Section 17.58.040(E) of the Planned Development Ordinance: (i) an exception
from and modification to the intensity of use of lot provisions to allow a
floor area of 34,856.8 square feet on the first floor (96.17%), which exceeds
the standard limitation of 90%; (ii) an exception from and modification to the
intensity of use of lot provisions to allow a floor area of 27,593.3 square
feet on the second floor (76.13%), which exceeds the standard limitation of
60%; (iii) an exception from and modification to the intensity of use of lot
provisions to allow a floor area of 25,418.6 square feet on the third floor
(70.13%), which exceeds the standard limitation of 60%; and (iv) an exception
from and modification to the intensity of use of lot provisions to allow a
floor area of area of 23,573.75 square feet on the fourth floor (65.04%), which
exceeds the standard limitation of 60%; and
   WHEREAS, the Application also requested an exception from and modification
to the development standards for yards and setbacks set out in Section
17.58.040(B) of the Planned Development Ordinance, to allow the building to be
built with no setback from the south (rear) lot line, although the standard
requirement is 10 feet: and
   WHEREAS, Applicant’s initial proposed plans were circulated to the Plan
Commission, the Zoning Board of Appeals and the Design Review Board for their
review and consideration in accordance with Section 17.58.080 of the Planned
Development Ordinance; and
   WHEREAS, on June 25, 2008, pursuant to duly published notice, the Plan
Commission convened a public hearing to begin its consideration of the
Applicant’s proposed planned development for the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, the Plan Commission’s consideration included both determining
whether the proposed planned development is consistent with the Winnetka
Comprehensive Plan, Winnetka 2020, as required by Chapters 17.56 and 17.58 of
the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, and determining whether the Applicant has met
the requirements of Chapter 17.58 for the granting of certain exceptions from
and modifications to the applicable development standards; and
   WHEREAS, at the meeting on June 25, 2008, the Plan Commission adopted rules
of procedure to govern the Plan Commission’s hearing and deliberations; and
   WHEREAS, the Plan Commission continued its consideration of the proposed
development at its meetings on July 23, 2008, August 27, 2008, September 24,
2008, October 22, 2008, and November 19, 2008; and
   WHEREAS, the Applicant submitted additional exhibits at the July 23, 2008,
meeting of the Plan Commission; and
   WHEREAS, on August 11, 2008, pursuant to duly published notice, the Zoning
Board of Appeals convened a public hearing on the four-story mixed use planned
development proposed by Applicant; and
   WHEREAS, the Zoning Board of Appeals continued the public hearing to a
special meeting held on October 6, 2008; and
   WHEREAS, as the Application proceeded through the Plan Commission and Zoning
Board of Appeals, various public improvements were identified and associated
with the proposed planned development; and
   WHEREAS, at the September 24, 2008, Plan Commission meeting, the Applicant
presented a revised plan, dated September 17, 2008, (“Revised Plan”) for the
redevelopment of the Subject Property as a mixed use planned development
consisting of a single, four-story building comprising 22,300 square feet of
retail commercial space on the ground floor, three floors of residential
condominium units, one lower level with 13,000 square feet of commercial space
and a parking area, and a second lower level dedicated to parking; and
   WHEREAS, in a letter dated September 29, 2008, the Applicant outlined
changes the Revised Plan made to the proposed development and enumerated a
series of possible proposed benefits, based on the list of previously
identified benefits; and
   WHEREAS, as part of the proposed public benefits of the Revised Plan, the
Applicant agreed to set aside three residential units to meet Village of
Winnetka affordability standards, as such standards would be established by the
Council of the Village of Winnetka ( “Village Council”); and
   WHEREAS, the Applicant also submitted a revised traffic study, dated
September 17, 2008; and
   WHEREAS, the Applicant also submitted a letter from the Assistant Managing
Broker at Coldwell Banker Residential Brokerage in Winnetka, who stated his
opinion that the proposed redevelopment will satisfy an unmet demand for
persons who are downsizing from single family residences, and that the proposed
redevelopment is “strengthened” by the size of the units, the ceiling heights,
the location of the development, additional underground parking and the
provision of “state of the art” retail and commercial space; and
   WHEREAS, the Applicant also presented its Revised Plan and the foregoing
additional supporting materials to the Zoning Board of Appeals at the continued
public hearing on October 6, 2008; and
   WHEREAS, on October 15, 2008, pursuant to the request of the Plan Commission
made at the September 24, 2008, Plan Commission meeting, the Applicant
submitted three-dimensional computer-generated models of the Revised Plan; and
   WHEREAS, at the October 22, 2008, Plan Commission meeting, the Applicant
presented its three-dimensional computer-generated models of the Revised Plan,
which included elevations of the adjacent buildings along the south side of Elm
Street and the east side of Lincoln Avenue, as well as the buildings opposite
the Subject Property on the north side of Elm Street; and
   WHEREAS, the Revised Plan continued to propose a mixed use planned
development consisting of a single, four-story building comprising 22,300
square feet of retail commercial space on the ground floor, three floors of
residential condominiums, a lower level with 13,000 square feet of commercial
space and a parking area, and a second lower level parking area; and
   WHEREAS, the Revised Plan made the following changes: (i) it reduced the
building footprint on all four floors, bringing the first floor to a conforming
area of 32,516.8 square feet (89.7%); (ii) provided a 14-foot setback from the
rear (south) lot line; (iii) increased the building setback between Conney’s
Pharmacy and the east wall of the proposed new building; (iv) increased the
setback of the upper floors from the east property line; (v) removed the turret
originally proposed for the northwest corner of the building; (vi) relocated
the residential entrance by setting it back from Lincoln Avenue and locating it
within the footprint of the building, so that it is accessed from the driveway
that leads to the underground parking; (vii) increased the setback from the
south lot line by placing the driveway to the lower level along the south lot
line; (viii) reconfigured some of the proposed commercial space into smaller,
shallower units; and (ix) relocated the refuse collection area to be adjacent
to the rear of the Conney’s Pharmacy building at 736 Elm Street; and.
   WHEREAS, the Revised Plan also provided for 104 off-street parking spaces in
two below-grade parking levels, and restored three on-street parking spaces due
to the elimination of the driveway exit to Elm Street and the relocation of the
residential entrance on Lincoln Avenue; and
   WHEREAS, because of modifications made in the Revised Plan, the Applicant
modified its request for modifications to and exceptions from the Planned
Development Ordinance, so that the Applicant requested only the following
modifications and exceptions: (i) an exception from and modification to the
building height standards of Section 17.58.040(C) of the Planned Development
Ordinance to allow the proposed building to have an average height of 49.5
feet, an excess of 4.5 feet over the standard height limitation of 45 feet;
(ii) an exception from and modification to the intensity of use of lot
development standards of Section 17.58.040(E) of the Planned Development
Ordinance to allow a floor area of 26,132.6 square feet on the second floor
(72.1%), which exceeds the standard limitation of 60%; (iii) an exception from
and modification to the intensity of use of lot development standards of
Section 17.58.040(E) of the Planned Development Ordinance to allow a floor area
of 24,320.4 square feet on the third floor (67.1%), which exceeds the standard
limitation of 60%; and (iv) an exception from and modification to the intensity
of use of lot development standards of Section 17.58.040(E) of the Planned
Development Ordinance to allow a floor area of 22,722 square feet on the fourth
floor (62.69%), which exceeds the standard limitation of 60%; and
   WHEREAS, in the proceedings before the Plan Commission, the Applicant
presented sworn testimony and exhibits in support of the proposed redevelopment
as modified in the Revised Plan; and
   WHEREAS, due notice of the Plan Commission’s proceedings was given and those
entitled to receive notice were afforded the opportunity to present evidence,
cross-examine Applicant’s witnesses and otherwise participate in the
proceedings; and
   WHEREAS, the owners of Conney’s Pharmacy and Phototronics entered
appearances before the Plan Commission, as did owners of certain residential
condominiums at 711 Oak (“Condominium Owners”), which is located immediately to
the south of the Subject Property at the northeast corner of Lincoln Avenue and
Oak Street (all referred to collectively as “Interested Parties”); and
   WHEREAS, in addition to evidence and comments from the Interested Parties,
the Plan Commission also received comments from other owners and residents of
single family residential properties located within 250 feet of the Subject
Property, including residents of Arbor Vitae Road, as well as from other
residents of the Village and representatives of the architect who designed the
original Fell clothing store building, which is part of the Subject Property
and is proposed to be demolished; and
   WHEREAS, all witnesses were sworn and all evidence and testimony received by
the Plan Commission were presented under oath; and
   WHEREAS, as is the customary practice, the Village Staff assisted the Plan
Commission identifying 36 specific provisions of the Winnetka Comprehensive
Plan, Winnetka 2020 (“Comprehensive Plan”) that may be applicable to the
Revised Plan; and
   WHEREAS, the Plan Commission considered and made an individual determination
on each of the 36 Comprehensive Plan provisions, to determine the extent to
which they are relevant to the Revised Plan and, if so, whether the Revised
Plan is consistent with the specific provision; and
   WHEREAS, the Plan Commission determined that the following goals, objectives
or standards of the Comprehensive Plan do not apply to the specifics of the
Revised Plan: (i) to encourage an appropriate number of rental units compatible
with the predominantly single- family residential character of the Village; and
(ii) to consider impact fees for new replacement housing that places an
additional burden on such public services as infrastructure, schools and parks;
and
   WHEREAS, the Plan Commission determined that the Revised Plan is consistent
with the goals, objectives and recommendations of the Comprehensive Plan to
recognize the critical role of the Village's historic architecture in defining
Winnetka's unique character in public, institutional, commercial and
residential areas, and encourage its preservation; and
   WHEREAS, the Plan Commission determined that the Revised Plan is consistent
with the goals, objectives and recommendations of the Comprehensive Plan to
require the screening and buffering of off-street parking lots while
considering the safety of pedestrians and motorists; and
   WHEREAS, the Plan Commission determined that the Revised Plan is consistent
with the goals, objectives and recommendations of the Comprehensive Plan to
continue to implement and supplement the Village's tree planting program on all
public rights-of-way; and
   WHEREAS, the Plan Commission determined that the Revised Plan is consistent
with the goals, objectives and recommendations of the Comprehensive Plan to
encourage landscaping of public and private properties that complements and
enhances the mature character of existing plantings and provides a buffer as an
alternative to walls and fences; and
   WHEREAS, the Plan Commission determined that the Revised Plan is consistent
with the goals, objectives and recommendations of the Comprehensive Plan to
reduce and control noise pollution; and
   WHEREAS, the Plan Commission determined that the Revised Plan is consistent
with the goals, objectives and recommendations of the Comprehensive Plan to use
high quality materials when constructing public improvements and to enhance the
beauty of the improvements with appropriate decorative details, artwork, or
sculpture; and
   WHEREAS, the Plan Commission determined that the Revised Plan is consistent
with the goals, objectives and recommendations of the Comprehensive Plan to
ensure that multi-family development provides a variety of housing choices for
residents of all ages; and
   WHEREAS, the Plan Commission determined that the Revised Plan is consistent
with the goals, objectives and recommendations of the Comprehensive Plan to
ensure that multi-family buildings complement adjacent single-family residences
in scale and architectural style and that architectural styles complement the
historic character of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the Plan Commission determined that the Revised Plan is consistent
with the goals, objectives and recommendations of the Comprehensive Plan to
require that multi-family development be of high quality material and design,
combined with adequately screened or underground parking and substantial
landscaping; and
   WHEREAS, the Plan Commission determined that the Revised Plan is consistent
with the goals, objectives and recommendations of the Comprehensive Plan to
improve major streets, especially their intersections, to enhance traffic flow,
safety and appearance, as well as their use by pedestrians and bicyclists; and
   WHEREAS, the Plan Commission determined that the Revised Plan is consistent
with the goals, objectives and recommendations of the Comprehensive Plan to
promote alternatives to motor vehicles such as bicycling and walking; and
   WHEREAS, the Plan Commission determined that the Revised Plan is consistent
with the goals, objectives and recommendations of the Comprehensive Plan to
ensure good condition of streets, curbs and sidewalks; and
   WHEREAS, the Plan Commission determined that the Revised Plan is consistent
with the goals, objectives and recommendations of the Comprehensive Plan to
encourage the maintenance and improvement of a rapid and comfortable commuter
service to and from the existing train stations in the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the Plan Commission determined that the Revised Plan is consistent
with the goals, objectives and recommendations of the Comprehensive Plan to
provide for adequate parking in commercial areas; and
   WHEREAS, the Plan Commission determined that the Revised Plan is consistent
with the goals, objectives and recommendations of the Comprehensive Plan to
require people who work in the commercial districts to park in employee-
designated off-street or underground parking areas; and
   WHEREAS, the Plan Commission determined that the Revised Plan is consistent
with the goals, objectives and recommendations of the Comprehensive Plan to
provide adequate off-street or underground parking for Winnetka commuters; and
   WHEREAS, the Plan Commission determined that the Revised Plan is consistent
with the goals, objectives and recommendations of the Comprehensive Plan to
enhance the overall appearance and environmental quality of public rights-of-
way; and
   WHEREAS, the Plan Commission determined that the Revised Plan is consistent
with the goals, objectives and recommendations of the Comprehensive Plan to
limit commercial, institutional and residential development within the Village
to minimize potentially adverse impacts on adjacent residential neighborhoods
and to prevent the need for significant increases in such infrastructure as
streets, parking, utilities, sewers, and such other community resources as
schools, parks and recreational facilities; and
   WHEREAS, the Plan Commission determined that the Revised Plan is consistent
with the goals, objectives and recommendations of the Comprehensive Plan to
ensure that there is an appropriate transition to buffer single family
neighborhoods from commercial districts; and
   WHEREAS, the Plan Commission determined that the Revised Plan is consistent
with the goals, objectives and recommendations of the Comprehensive Plan to
minimize the potential adverse impact of commercial activity on residential
neighborhoods; and
   WHEREAS, the Plan Commission determined that the Revised Plan is consistent
with the goals, objectives and recommendations of the Comprehensive Plan to
plan for a systematic water main replacement program in conjunction with street
replacement and renovation programs; and
   WHEREAS, the Plan Commission determined that the Revised Plan is consistent
with the goals, objectives and recommendations of the Comprehensive Plan to
encourage the use of existing mass transit within the Green Bay Road corridor
and to provide expanded services if needed; and
   WHEREAS, the Plan Commission has determined that the Revised Plan is
consistent with the goals, objectives and recommendations of the Comprehensive
Plan to provide for a wide range of office, service and retail commercial land
uses and development within the existing business districts in the Green Bay
Corridor; and
   WHEREAS, the Plan Commission determined that the Revised Plan is consistent
with the goals, objectives and recommendations of the Comprehensive Plan to
promote a strong community identity and opportunities to interact while
building a healthy commercial tax base, and to provide a broad range of goods
and services, so that Winnetka residents can satisfy most of their ordinary
shopping requirements in the Village and that non-residents will come to the
Village for specialty goods and services; and
   WHEREAS, the Plan Commission determined that the Revised Plan is consistent
with the goals, objectives and recommendations of the Comprehensive Plan to
maintain the essential quality, viability and attractiveness of Winnetka's
business districts while encouraging new economic development consistent with
the character of the Village and the individual business districts; and
   WHEREAS, the Plan Commission determined that the Revised Plan is consistent
with the goals, objectives and recommendations of the Comprehensive Plan to
encourage development that is appropriate for the scale and intensity of
commercial activity and consistent with the Comprehensive Plan Map; and
   WHEREAS, the Plan Commission determined that the Revised Plan is consistent
with the goals, objectives and recommendations of the Comprehensive Plan to
minimize the number of curb cuts to help retain block face continuity in the
business districts; and
   WHEREAS, the Plan Commission determined that the Revised Plan is consistent
with the goals, objectives and recommendations of the Comprehensive Plan to
ensure that new development does not decrease public parking supply,
particularly on-street parking that supports retail uses; and
   WHEREAS, the Plan Commission determined that the Revised Plan is consistent
with the goals, objectives and recommendations of the Comprehensive Plan to
require development to be appropriate to the character of its surroundings,
with developments that interface with the surrounding neighborhood, rather than
exist as an isolated complex, and with multi-family buildings that are designed
to complement the historic character of the Village, using high quality
materials, and providing below grade parking; and
   WHEREAS, notwithstanding its favorable findings on specific goals,
objectives and recommendations of the Comprehensive Plan, the Plan Commission
determined that, due to the size of the proposed building, the Revised Plan is
not consistent with the following goals, objectives and recommendations of the
Comprehensive Plan: (i) to ensure that commercial, institutional, and
residential development is appropriate to the character of and minimizes the
adverse impact on its surrounding neighborhood; (ii) to reduce and control
light pollution, preserving property rights for natural light and controlling
the use and location of artificial lighting; (iii) to ensure that the density
of multiple family developments provide an appropriate transition to adjacent
single family neighborhoods; (iv) to maintain the 2½ story limit on commercial
buildings; and (v) to require development on the south side of Elm Street to be
compatible and architecturally harmonious with the less intense character of
this portion of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, notwithstanding its favorable findings on the specific goals,
objectives and recommendations of the Comprehensive Plan, the Plan Commission
determined that the Revised Plan, as a whole, did not meet all of the standards
for a planned development set out in Section 17.58.110 of the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance, for the following reasons: (i) the proposed building height
interferes with natural light for pedestrians on Elm Street; (ii) the height of
the proposed building is incompatible with the height of the two, shorter
buildings at the southeast corner of Elm Street and Lincoln Avenue (the “Corner
Properties”), and will cast shadows onto those two buildings; (iii) the
proposed redevelopment will impact the future development of the two Corner
Properties, in that they are not large enough to be redeveloped under the
Planned Development Ordinance and therefore can only be redeveloped to a
maximum height of 2½ stories unless a variance is obtained at some time in the
future; (iv) to the extent that the building’s proposed average ridge line
height exceeds 45 feet, it is incompatible with the building heights in the
immediate vicinity; (v) the height of the proposed building along Elm Street is
incompatible with the immediate neighborhood and, in particular, with the
adjacent buildings at the southeast corner of Elm and Lincoln; and (vi) the
mass of the proposed building along Elm Street is incompatible with the
buildings in the immediate vicinity; and
   WHEREAS, the Plan Commission also determined that the Applicant did not meet
the standards of Section 17.58.040(C) for a modification to the height
limitation of 45 feet, based on its conclusion that: (i) the proposed building
height is higher than all other buildings in the immediate neighborhood; (ii)
the proposed building height is substantially higher than the buildings at the
southeast corner of Elm Street and Lincoln Avenue; and (iii) the proposed
height of the building along Elm Street, in particular, is not consistent with
the established character of the East Elm business district; and
   WHEREAS, the Plan Commission also determined that the Applicant did not meet
the standards of Section 17.58.040(E) for an exception to the intensity of use
of lot limitations of 60% of lot area for upper floors, based on its conclusion
that: (i) the Applicant did not establish that the larger footprint proposed
for the second, third and fourth floors is necessary to provide for the three
units proposed to meet affordability standards; and (ii) the Applicant did not
establish that the exceptions are necessary to meet the stated goals and
objectives of the Planned Development Ordinance and the Comprehensive Plan; and
   WHEREAS, after making its detailed findings, the Plan Commission determined
that the Revised Plan submitted by New Trier Partners for the redevelopment of
the Subject Property did not meet the standards of the Planned Development
Ordinance for preliminary planned development approval, in that the Revised
Plan, taken as a whole, is not consistent with either the goals, objectives and
recommendations of the Winnetka Comprehensive Plan or the stated goals and
objectives of the Planned Development Ordinance; and
   WHEREAS, after making its detailed findings, the Plan Commission determined
that the exceptions sought by the Applicant in the Revised Plan did not meet
the applicable standards of Chapter 17.58 of the Winnetka Village Code; and
   WHEREAS, based on its detailed findings and determinations, the Plan
Commission recommended: (i) that the Village Council deny the application for
preliminary approval of the redevelopment of the Subject Property according to
the Revised Plan; (ii) that the Village Council deny the application for an
exception to the height limitations of Section 17.58.040(C); and (iii) that the
Village Council deny the application for an exception to the intensity of use
of lot limitations of Section 17.58.040(C) be denied; and
   WHEREAS, the Zoning Board of Appeals conducted its hearing and deliberations
on August 11, 2008, October 6, 2008, and October 13, 2008; and
   WHEREAS, the Zoning Board of Appeals conducted the hearing on the
Application in accordance with it rules of procedure; and
   WHEREAS, due notice of the Zoning Board of Appeals proceedings was given and
those entitled to receive notice were afforded the opportunity to present
evidence, cross-examine Applicant’s witnesses and otherwise participate in the
proceedings; and
   WHEREAS, at the hearing before the Zoning Board of Appeals, the Applicant
presented testimony and exhibits in support of the proposed redevelopment; and
   WHEREAS, the owners of Conney’s Pharmacy and Phototronics entered formal
appearances before the Zoning Board of Appeals, as did owners of certain
residential condominiums at 711 Oak (“Condominium Owners”), which is located
immediately to the south of the Subject Property at the northeast corner of
Lincoln Avenue and Oak Street (all referred to collectively as “Interested
Parties”); and
   WHEREAS, in addition to evidence and comments from the Interested Parties,
the Zoning Board of Appeals also received comments from other interested
persons, including owners and residents of single family residential properties
located within 250 feet of the Subject Property, including residents of Arbor
Vitae Road, from other residents of the Village and from representatives of the
architect who designed the original Fell clothing store building, which is part
of the Subject Property and is proposed to be demolished.; and
   WHEREAS, all witnesses were sworn and all evidence and testimony received by
the Zoning Board of Appeals were presented under oath; and
   WHEREAS, subject to certain terms and conditions set forth below in these
recitals (the “Zoning Board’s Conditions”), the Zoning Board of Appeals found
that the Revised Plan was consistent with the land use and development
objectives of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, particularly the following: (i)
the redevelopment proposed in the Revised Plan maintains the rational pattern
of land uses as prescribed by the Zoning Ordinance; (ii) the mixture of retail,
commercial and residential uses, along with accessory parking in the proposed
redevelopment encourage the most appropriate use of the Subject Property; (iii)
the increased setbacks, the elimination of the turret, and the use of terraces
and balconies to break up the mass of the building helps to protect the scale
and character of the existing commercial district and the adjoining residential
areas from the encroachment of incompatible uses and structures; (iv) the
increased setbacks and the stepping back of upper floors provide for adequate
natural light and air to adjoining properties; (v) the reconfigured building
access removes an unnecessary interruption of the Lincoln Avenue sidewalk and
reduces hazards to pedestrians; (vi) the modifications to the south side of the
building reduce the appearance of the mass of the building from the
condominiums to the south and help to maintain the privacy of residents of both
the Subject Property and the adjoining property; and (vii) the height of the
proposed building, as depicted in the Revised Plan is appropriate to the size
of the Subject Property and is compatible with building heights in the
immediate vicinity; and
   WHEREAS, the Zoning Board of Appeals found that average ridge line height of
49.5 feet, as depicted in the Revised Plan, is not excessive and is compatible
with the building height of the four-story multi-family building immediately to
the south along Lincoln Avenue; and
   WHEREAS, subject to the Zoning Board’s Conditions, the Zoning Board of
Appeals found that the proposed building height, as depicted in the Revised
Plan, is compatible with the building heights of the existing commercial
buildings in the vicinity of the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, subject to the Zoning Board’s Conditions, the Zoning Board of
Appeals found that the Revised Plan takes reasonable steps to protect and
respect the justifiable reliance of the adjoining property owners and
businesses, and of the Village’s taxpayers, on the continuation of existing,
established land use patterns; and
   WHEREAS, subject to the Zoning Board’s Conditions, the Zoning Board of
Appeals found that the proposed redevelopment of the Subject Property pursuant
to the Revised Plan will protect and enhance the taxable value of land and
buildings in the Village; and
   WHEREAS, subject to the Zoning Board’s Conditions, the Zoning Board of
Appeals found that the revised configuration of some of the proposed retail
spaces into smaller spaces, as depicted in the Revised Plan, is consistent with
smaller boutique retail sales; and
   WHEREAS, subject to the Zoning Board’s Conditions, the Zoning Board of
Appeals found that the revised configuration of the retail spaces into spaces
consistent with smaller boutique retail sales, as depicted in the Revised Plan,
is consistent with the following stated purposes of the C-2 General Commercial
District: (i) to provide a community commercial district that offers a wide
range of goods and services for residents of the Village and a wider market
area; (ii) to encourage retailing of comparison shopping goods and personal
services compatible with such retailing on the ground floor in order to
encourage a clustering of such uses; and (iii) to provide for a wide variety of
retail shops and to expose such shops to maximum foot traffic; and
   WHEREAS, subject to the Zoning Board’s Conditions, the Zoning Board of
Appeals found that the fence to be provided along the south lot line will
protect the residents of the condominium building to the south from the
intrusion of light from the headlights of vehicles driving up the ramp as they
exit from the lower level parking areas; and
   WHEREAS, based on all of its detailed findings, and subject to the Zoning
Board’s Conditions, the Zoning Board of Appeals found that the mixed use
building proposed by the Revised Plan is consistent with the stated intent of
the Planned Development Ordinance; and
   WHEREAS, based on all of its detailed findings, and subject to the Zoning
Board’s Conditions, the Zoning Board of Appeals found that the mixed use
building proposed by the Revised Plan will neither endanger nor be detrimental
to the public health, safety, comfort, morals or general welfare, in that the
proposed planned development, as depicted in the Revised Plan: (i) will
complement and supplement the community by providing a modern, mixed use
building that provides a combination of boutique-sized retail spaces at street
level, additional retail and commercial spaces on the lower level, three
stories of residential units, including three units that will meet Village of
Winnetka affordability standards, and new off-street parking; (ii) will replace
out-of-date and underutilized retail spaces with modern facilities; and (iii)
will expand the Village’s property tax and sales tax base; and
   WHEREAS, based on all of its detailed findings, and subject to the Zoning
Board’s Conditions, the Zoning Board of Appeals found and concluded that the
mixed use building proposed by the Revised Plan will not substantially diminish
or impair property values in the immediate vicinity and will not be
substantially injurious to the use and enjoyment of land in the immediate
vicinity for uses permitted by right in the C-2 General Retail Commercial
Zoning District, in that: (i) the proposed building will be set back from the
side and rear property lines and from the adjoining existing retail buildings,
so that shadows related to the additional building height will be confined to
the Subject Property during holiday parades on Elm Street; (ii) the electrical
transformer for the proposed development will be located in the southeast
corner of the Subject Property and will be screened from view; (iii) the
building mechanicals will be located so that they are screened from the
adjoining properties, particularly the residential property to the south; (iv)
the fence and other screening that will be located along the south property
line will protect the residents of the property to the south from the intrusion
of light from the headlights of vehicles driving up the ramp as they exit from
the lower level parking areas; and (v) no balconies or other parts of the
building will encroach into required setbacks; and
   WHEREAS, based on all of its detailed findings, and subject to the Zoning
Board’s Conditions, the Zoning Board of Appeals found and concluded that the
mixed use building proposed by the Revised Plan will not impede the normal and
orderly development and improvement of other property in the immediate vicinity
for uses permitted by right in the zoning district, in that: (i) the proposed
building will be set back from the property lines and from the adjoining
existing retail buildings; and (ii) in the event of a request from the owners
of the Conney’s Pharmacy and Phototronics properties to redevelop those sites
with a building of similar height, the owners of the Subject Property will not
object on the basis of building height; and
   WHEREAS, based on all of its detailed findings, and subject to the Zoning
Board’s Conditions, the Zoning Board of Appeals found and concluded that
adequate measures have been or will be taken to provide ingress and egress in a
manner which minimizes pedestrian and vehicular traffic congestion in the
public and private ways, in that: (i) the relocation of the residential
entrance to the south side of the building reduces interference with pedestrian
traffic on Lincoln Avenue and eliminates interference with the driveway access
to the residential condominium building to the south; and (ii) providing for
two-way traffic on the driveway access to the lower level parking area allows
the existing curb cut on Elm Street to be eliminated and provides for a
continuous expanse of sidewalk past all of the commercial buildings on the
south side of Elm Street east of Lincoln Avenue; and
   WHEREAS, based on all of its detailed findings, and subject to the Zoning
Board’s Conditions, the Zoning Board of Appeals found and concluded that
adequate parking, utilities, access roads, drainage and other facilities
necessary for the operation of the proposed development, as depicted in the
Revised Plan, either exist or will be provided, in that: (i) the Revised Plan
restores several on-street parking spaces along Elm Street, adds 104
underground parking spaces on site; (ii) the proposed redevelopment, as
depicted in the Revised Plan, contributes to the implementation of the Village
of Winnetka Streetscape Plan on Elm Street and Lincoln Avenue; and (iii) the
proposed redevelopment, as depicted in the Revised Plan, contributes to
improvements to the Village’s water distribution system through the
construction of a new, enlarged water main on Elm Street; and
   WHEREAS, based on all of its detailed findings, and subject to the Zoning
Board’s Conditions, the Zoning Board of Appeals found that the mixed use
building proposed by the Revised Plan in all other respects conforms to the
applicable zoning regulations and other applicable provisions of the Winnetka
Village Code; and
   WHEREAS, based on all of its detailed findings, and subject to the Zoning
Board’s Conditions, the Zoning Board of Appeals recommended that the final
design of the building be approved by the Village Council or by the Design
Review Board, if so delegated by the Village Council; and
   WHEREAS, based on all of its detailed findings, and subject to the Zoning
Board’s Conditions, the Zoning Board of Appeals found that exceptions sought by
the Applicant in the Revised Plan meet the applicable standards of the Planned
Development Ordinance; and
   WHEREAS, the recommendation of the Zoning Board of Appeals was subject to
the following conditions:
      (i)   that the Village Engineer, in the exercise of his professional
judgment, concur with the conclusions of the September 17, 2008 revised traffic
study submitted by the Applicant; and
      (ii)   that the redevelopment of the Subject Property be in accordance
with the Revised Plan, which was dated September 17, 2008, and was presented to
the Zoning Board of Appeals on October 6, 2008; and
      (iii)   that the Applicant continue to work in good faith with the
Village to determine the extent of Applicant’s responsibilities to construct or
contribute financially to the construction of improvements identified in the
September 17, 2008 letter that accompanied the Revised Plan; and
      (iv)   that the transformers for electrical service to the redeveloped
Subject Property be located in the southeast corner of the Subject Property,
and that they be screened from view from the street and from adjoining
properties; and
      (v)   that the Applicant expend its best efforts to locate all other
exterior mechanical equipment, including HVAC equipment, as far as possible
from view from the street and from adjoining properties and that they be
equipped with adequate sound attenuation devices to protect adjoining
properties from excessive noise; and
      (vi)   that the Applicant provide a solid fence along the length of the
south lot line, along with landscaping materials and such other visual
screening materials as may be necessary to protect the residents of the
property to the south of the Subject Property from the intrusion of light from
the headlights of vehicles exiting the lower level parking area; and
      (vii)   that the Applicant provide at least three residential units that
meet affordability standards as determined by the Village Council; and
      (viii)   that the Applicant expend its best efforts to reduce the
building height in the course of finalizing building architectural plans; and
      (ix)   that the first floor retail space be configured for smaller
commercial uses, consistent with the depiction in the Revised Plan; and
   WHEREAS, on November 20, 2008, the Design Review Board considered the
Applicant’s Revised Plan and approved the design as submitted, subject to the
following conditions: (i) that the proposed south balconies be studied further
to determine how they would be held up; (ii) that the Applicant provide an
accurate depiction of the Elm Street fenestration, including the glass and two
gables; and (iii) that the package of final building and finish materials,
including the brick, decorative finishes, awnings, signage, streetscape,
landscape and lighting be submitted to the Design Review Board for final
approval; and
   WHEREAS, pursuant to Section 17.58.080 of the Planned Development Ordinance,
the findings and recommendations of the Plan Commission, Zoning Board of
Appeals and Design Review Board were submitted to the Village Council, along
with the complete records of all three bodies, all supporting materials, and
all correspondence and written comment received at any time on the proposed
redevelopment; and
   WHEREAS, pursuant to Section 17.58.080(E) of the Planned Development
Ordinance, at least twenty percent (20%) of the owners of property located
within 250 feet of the Subject Property (“Objectors”) have submitted a signed
and acknowledged protest to the Village Council, opposing the proposed
redevelopment of the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, pursuant to Section 17.58.080(E) of the Planned Development
Ordinance, as a result of the filing of the written protest by the Objectors,
the favorable vote of four Village Trustees is required for preliminary
approval of the proposed development; and
   WHEREAS, following the Council’s review of planned development procedures at
its December 9, 2008, study session, the Village Council began its substantive
consideration of the application for preliminary approval of the proposed
planned development of the Subject Property, as depicted in the Revised Plan,
at the regular Council meeting on December 16, 2008; and
   WHEREAS, the Village Council continued its deliberations of the Application
at a January 13, 2009, special meeting that was held solely to discuss the
Application; and
   WHEREAS, in further response to comments from both the Village Council and
its advisory bodies, the Applicant has made further revisions to the proposed
development, as depicted in plans date February 4, 2009 (“2009 Amended Plan”);
and
   WHEREAS, the Village Council continued its deliberations of the Application
at its February 10, 2009, study session, which was also devoted only to the
proposed redevelopment of the Subject Property; and
   WHEREAS, at the end of the February 10, 2009 meeting, it was the sense of
the Village Council that the Application for preliminary approval should be
granted, subject to various terms and conditions, and directed the Village
Attorney to prepare an ordinance granting such approval; and
   WHEREAS, the Village Council introduced a draft of this ordinance at its
regular meeting on April 7, 2009; and
   WHEREAS, at every meeting at which the Application was considered by the
Village Council, the Village Council continued to receive comment from the
Applicant, the Objectors, other interested parties, and members of the general
public; and
   WHEREAS, in addition to comment received at public meetings, the Village
Council has continued to receive written comment from the general public and
other interested parties, both by mail and e-mail, all of which has been
included in the record before the Council; and
   WHEREAS, the evidentiary record forwarded to the Village Council by its
advisory bodies includes the record of the sworn testimony of Applicant’s
principals, architects, planner and traffic engineer, as well as financial
evidence, Applicant’s documentary exhibits and a three-dimensional scale model
of the proposed development in the context of the immediate vicinity, including
the Conney’s and Phototronics properties, the four-story condominium located at
711 Oak, and the buildings opposite the Subject Property on the north side of
Elm Street; and
   WHEREAS, the evidentiary record submitted by the interested parties and
Objectors expressed their opinions that the proposed development would have a
substantial negative impact on property values and quality of life because the
building is too tall for the site, exceeds the limitations of the Zoning
Ordinance, is too intense for the neighborhood and will obstruct the light, air
and view of the neighbors; and
   WHEREAS, the interested parties and Objectors did not offer any expert
testimony or other evidence in support of their opinions; and
   WHEREAS, the evidentiary record contains sufficient credible evidence in
support of the Application to enable the Council to make the findings and grant
the relief set forth herein; and
   WHEREAS, the 2009 Amended Plan reduces the building area from 184,478 square
feet to 167,835 square feet; and
   WHEREAS, the 2009 Amended Plan reduces the building footprint from 35,120
square feet to 26,535 square feet; and
   WHEREAS, the 2009 Amended Plan maintains several key changes that were made
in the Revised Plan, including: (i) the elimination of the turret detail; (ii)
the 14-foot setback from the south property line; (iii) the relocated and
reconfigured entrance at the south end of the building, including the
consolidation of the residential entrance drive with the driveway to the
parking ramp; (iv) the relocated refuse area; and (v) the reconfiguration of
some of the commercial space into smaller, shallower units; and
   WHEREAS, the 2009 Amended Plan reduces the overall building height by one
foot, adjusting the height to address the eastward downhill slope of the
Subject Property, making the height of the top of the parapets approximately
48.5 feet, and reducing the requested height exception and modification from
approximately four feet to approximately three feet; and
   WHEREAS, the 2009 Amended Plan also reduces the building length along Elm
Street from 170 feet to 150 feet, by providing a 10-foot side yard setback on
both the east and west sides of the Elm Street building frontage; and
   WHEREAS, the 2009 Amended Plan also reduces the size of the fourth floor
along the Elm Street frontage, so that (i) the length of the fourth floor is
125 feet; (ii) the front building line on the fourth floor is set back 20 feet
from the Elm Street property line; (iii) the east building line on the fourth
floor is set back a total of 25 feet from the east property line; and (iv) the
west building line on the fourth floor is set back approximately 20 feet from
the west property line for a distance of 52.67 feet; and
   WHEREAS, the 2009 Amended Plan also increases the building setback along
that portion of the north property line that adjoins the Conney’s Pharmacy
property to provide a north setback of 16.5 feet; and
   WHEREAS, the 2009 Amended Plan also reduces the size of the fourth floor
along Lincoln Avenue and adjacent to the Phototronics and Conney’s Pharmacy
properties, by setting the fourth floor back (i) a total of 20 feet from the
east lot line of the Conney’s Pharmacy property; (ii) approximately 12 feet
from the south lot lines of the Phototronics and Conney’s Pharmacy properties;
and (iii) approximately 15 feet from the Lincoln Avenue property line; and
   WHEREAS, the combination of the reduced height and increased setbacks along
Elm Street and Lincoln Avenue, as depicted in the 2009 Amended Plan, reduces
the visibility of the fourth floor from the street, and gives the appearance of
a three-story building when viewed from Elm Street; and
   WHEREAS, the 2009 Amended Plan pulls the building further back from the
south property line, so that the south elevation of the second, third and
fourth floors is set back slightly more than 16 feet from the south property
line, with the exception of (i) two bay window details on each residential
floor, each with a 12-foot south setback; and (ii) one balcony on each
residential floor, each with a south setback of slightly more than 10.5 feet;
and
   WHEREAS, the 2009 Amended Plan provides a more appropriate scale and
residential level of detailing than in the earlier plans, by changing the east
end of the building from a relatively unarticulated four-story wall capped with
a mansard roof, to three stories with more appropriate architectural detail,
including a lowered eave/gutter line, a slate roof, cross gables and dormers;
and
   WHEREAS, the 2009 Amended Plan also significantly improves the composition
and style of the gables facing Elm Street by reducing the amount of glazing in
the gables, using a more traditional window arrangement, and framing the window
openings in stucco, all of which is more consistent with the architectural
styles and traditions in the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the 2009 Amended Plan also addresses in a positive fashion how the
proposed building transitions to the adjacent one-story Conney’s Pharmacy and
Phototronics buildings by significantly improving the architectural detail
adjacent to Conney’s and Phototronics through the elimination of the blank wall
with no setback that was originally proposed for the upper floors and providing
a three story, architecturally detailed wall at the west end of the Elm Street
portion of the building and at the north end of the Lincoln Avenue portion of
the building, adjacent to the Conney’s Pharmacy and Phototronics properties;
and
   WHEREAS, the revised Lincoln Avenue elevation in the 2009 Amended Plan
depicts a building with a three-story, architecturally detailed wall with
windows and dormers, with the fourth floor pulled back 20 feet from the east
property line of Conney’s, whereas the original plan called for a four-story
wall with four windows; and
   WHEREAS, the perceived height of the building has been reduced by changing
several elements in the 2009 Amended Plan, in that the height of the building
along Elm Street has been modified to bring the roof’s eave and gutter line
down to 26 feet from a previous height of 37 feet, with exceptions to the
lowered height being provided by articulating the building façade with the
three gables facing Elm Street, resulting in a desired architectural rhythm and
sense of horizontal articulation, which is called for in the Commercial Design
guidelines; and
   WHEREAS,  the reduction of the upper floor footprint in the 2009 Amended
Plan to comply with the 60% intensity of use of lot restrictions not only
eliminates a requested exception, it also effectively reduces the perception of
the building’s height and reduces the visibility of the fourth floor from the
street by (i) minimizing the fourth floor footprint; and (ii) reducing the
relationship of the volume of the upper floor to the volume of the lower floors
to approximate the half-story standard in the Zoning Ordinance; and
   WHEREAS, the 2009 Amended Plan also makes the height and scale of the
proposed building consistent with the height and scale of the buildings
opposite the Subject Property on the north side of Elm Street and minimizes the
effect of building massing on natural light on Elm Street; and
   WHEREAS, the Village Council has the discretion, under Section 17.58.080(E)
of the Planned Development Ordinance, to accept, reject, or modify the
recommendations of the Zoning Board of Appeals, the Plan Commission and the
Design Review Board, or to return the application to any or all of those three
advisory bodies for further hearing and recommendation; and
   WHEREAS, the changes made to the Applicant’s proposal, as depicted in the
2009 Amended Plan, adequately address and resolve the concerns, as expressed in
the Plan Commission’s findings; and
   WHEREAS, the establishment of the planned development as depicted in the
2009 Amended Plan and subject to the terms and conditions hereinafter set forth
will satisfy the conditions recommended by the Zoning Board of Appeals; and
   WHEREAS, for the reasons stated in the findings of the Zoning Board of
Appeals, the establishment of the planned development subject to the terms and
conditions hereinafter set forth will neither endanger nor be detrimental to
the public health, safety, comfort, morals or general welfare; and
   WHEREAS, for the reasons stated in the findings of the Zoning Board of
Appeals, the establishment of the planned development subject to the terms and
conditions hereinafter set forth will not impede the normal and orderly
development or improvement of other property in the immediate vicinity for uses
permitted by right in the adjacent C-2 General Retail Commercial District and
B-2 Multi-Family Residential District, or in the other nearby residential
zoning districts; and
   WHEREAS, for the reasons stated in the findings of the Zoning Board of
Appeals, the establishment of the planned development subject to the terms and
conditions hereinafter set forth will assure that adequate measures will be
taken to provide ingress and egress in a manner that minimizes pedestrian and
vehicular traffic congestion in the public ways; and
   WHEREAS, for the reasons stated in the findings of the Zoning Board of
Appeals, the establishment of the planned development subject to the terms and
conditions hereinafter set forth adequate parking facilities necessary to the
operation of the special use exist or will be provided; and
   WHEREAS, for the reasons stated in the findings of the Zoning Board of
Appeals, the establishment of the planned development subject to the terms and
conditions hereinafter set forth will assure that adequate utilities, access
roads, drainage and other facilities necessary to the operation of the proposed
special use exist; and
   WHEREAS, when implemented in accordance with the terms and conditions set
forth in Section 4 of this Ordinance, the special use in all other respects
will conform to the applicable regulations of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance and
other Village ordinances and codes; and
   WHEREAS, the Village of Winnetka is a home rule municipality in accordance
with Article VII, Section 6 of the Constitution of the State of Illinois of
1970, pursuant to which it has the authority, except as limited by said Section
6 of Article VII, to exercise any power and perform any function pertaining to
the government and affairs of the Village; and
   WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka (“Village Council”) find
that establishing standards for the use and development of lands and buildings
within the Village and establishing and applying criteria for variations from
those standards are matters pertaining to the affairs of the Village.
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
 
   SECTION 1:   Findings. The foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated into
this section as the findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if
fully set forth herein. 
   SECTION 2:   Additional Findings. The Council of the Village of Winnetka
(“Village Council”) further find as follows:
      A.   The Village Council has jurisdiction over the subject matter of the
Application pursuant to Section 17.58.080(E) of Chapter 17.58 of the Winnetka
Village Code (the “Planned Development Ordinance”), which jurisdiction includes
the discretion to accept, reject or modify the Application or to return it to
the Plan Commission, Zoning Board of Appeals or Design Review Board for further
consideration and findings.
      B.   The changes made to the Applicant’s proposal, as depicted in the
Approved Preliminary Plans, as defined in Section 4 of this ordinance M-6-2009
(hereinafter, the “Preliminary Approval Ordinance”), do not require new public
notice or further hearing or consideration by the Plan Commission, Zoning Board
of Appeals or Design Review Board, because the Approved Preliminary Plans
reduce the size of the proposed building and reduces or eliminates the
modifications and exceptions originally requested by the Applicant.
      C.   Subject to the terms and conditions hereinafter set forth, the
proposed Development, as depicted in the Approved Preliminary Plans and as
defined in Section 3 of this Preliminary Approval Ordinance, is consistent with
the Village of Winnetka Comprehensive Plan, Winnetka 2020.
      D.   Subject to the terms and conditions hereinafter set forth, the
proposed Development, as depicted in the Approved Preliminary Plans and as
defined in Section 3 of this Preliminary Approval Ordinance, complies or will
comply with all applicable Village ordinances.
      E.   Subject to the terms and conditions hereinafter set forth, the
proposed Development, as depicted in the Approved Preliminary Plans and as
defined in Section 3 of this Preliminary Approval Ordinance, considered both as
a whole and in its component parts, is in conformity or will be in conformity
with the Village of Winnetka Design Guidelines.
      F.   The individual uses proposed or to be proposed for the Development
are uses that are permitted in the C-2 General Commercial Retail Zoning
District.
      G.   Subject to the terms and conditions hereinafter set forth, the
proposed Development as depicted in the Approved Preliminary Plans and as
defined in Section 3 of this Preliminary Approval Ordinance, when considered as
a whole, meets the standards for the granting of special use permits, as
established in Chapter 17.56 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance.
      H.   Subject to the terms and conditions hereinafter set forth, the
proposed Development as depicted in the Approved Preliminary Plans and as
defined in Section 3 of this Preliminary Approval Ordinance, is responsive to a
demonstrated need within the Village, in that:
         1.   it will replace aging and underutilized buildings with new
construction compatible in design with the East Elm Street Business District;
         2.   it will provide new retail space that can be adjusted to meet
size and layout demands of various types of retail users;
         3.   it will provide new multi-family residential space in close
proximity to the Elm Street Metra Station;
         4.   it will provide at least three new affordable housing units;
         5.   it will facilitate the future redevelopment of the Corner
Properties, as defined in Section 6.G of this Preliminary Approval Ordinance,
by providing access to potential underground parking for the two Corner
Properties in the event they are redeveloped; and
         6.   its mixture of commercial and residential uses will increase the
level of activity in the East Elm Business District by    providing new,
flexible commercial space and additional residential units within the East Elm
Business District and in direct proximity to public transportation.
      I.   Subject to the terms and conditions hereinafter set forth, the
exceptions from and modifications to the building height of the proposed
Development, as depicted in the Approved Preliminary Plans and as defined in
Section 5 of this Preliminary Approval Ordinance, meets the standards for
modification defined in Section 17.58.040(C) of the Planned Development
Ordinance, in that the Approved Preliminary Plans carefully considers the scale
and proportion of other buildings in the vicinity by placing the tallest
portions of the building adjacent to a neighboring structure along Lincoln
Avenue and setting other portions of the building back from the Elm Street and
other flanking facades of the building, so that the appearance from street
level is consistent and compatible with other buildings in the vicinity and
with the adjoining properties.
      J.   Subject to the terms and conditions hereinafter set forth, the
proposed Development, as depicted in the Approved Preliminary Plans and as
defined in Section 3 of this Preliminary Approval Ordinance, meets the general
purpose and intent of the Planned Development Ordinance, as set out in Section
17.58.020 of the Planned Development Ordinance, in that:
         1.   it provides multiple commercial spaces with more flexibility in
layout than would exist if the existing properties were redeveloped
individually;
         2.   it provides residential units that are enhanced with balconies or
decks;
         3.   it maintains and enhances the existing retail character on
Lincoln Avenue by providing entrances to commercial spaces off both Elm Street
and Lincoln Avenue;
         4.   it allows vehicular access to the entire Subject Property through
a single curb cut by allowing access to the residential drive at the vestibule
to the residential units to be accessed from the driveway to the parking ramp;
and
         5.   the vehicular access has been located on Lincoln Avenue at the
south end of the Subject Property, in the same area as the entrance to the
existing parking, where the driveway is an established presence, and where it
is at the edge of the C-2 Zoning District and therefore compatible with
pedestrian and vehicular traffic in the East Elm Business District.
      K.   Subject to the terms and conditions hereinafter set forth, the
exceptions and modifications to the building height of the proposed
Development, as depicted in the Approved Preliminary Plans and as defined in
Section 5 of this Preliminary Approval Ordinance, meets the standards for
modification defined in Section 17.58.040(C) of the Planned Development
Ordinance, in that the additional height is necessary to promote a unified site
plan that meets the objectives of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance and the
Comprehensive Plan, as set out in the preamble to this Ordinance.
      L.   Subject to the terms and conditions hereinafter set forth, the
exceptions and modifications to the building height of the proposed
Development, as depicted in the Approved Preliminary Plans and as defined in
Section 5 of the Preliminary Approval Ordinance, meets the standards for
exceptions and modifications defined in Section 17.58.040(C) of the Planned
Development Ordinance, in that the exception and modification is necessary to
meet the purpose and intent of the Planned Development Ordinance, as set out in
Section 17.58.020 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that:
         1.   it promotes progressive development and re-development of
functionally obsolete buildings in the East Elm Commercial District;
         2.   it results in a more creative building design for the Subject
Property than would be possible under the generally applicable zoning
regulations, by allowing the use of balconies and decks to enhance the
residential units, by allowing both horizontal and vertical articulation in the
building façade, and by allowing a building design that includes gables and
roof details with a pitch consistent with the building’s Tudor design and
detailing;
         3.   it achieves a more desirable physical environment by allowing
greater flexibility in building design than would be possible under the
generally applicable zoning and subdivision regulations;
         4.   it provides more efficient use of the Subject Property and a more
economic network of utilities and other facilities to serve the Subject
Property; and
         5.   it is the result of the detailed review by the Zoning Board of
Appeals, Plan Commission, Design Review Board and Village Council and an
extensive process that provided procedural protections for adjacent businesses,
adjacent residential property owners, the East Elm Business District and the
Village as a whole.
      M.   Subject to the terms and conditions hereinafter set forth, the
exceptions and modifications to the intensity of use of lot of the proposed
Development, as depicted in the Approved Preliminary Plans and as defined in
Section 5 of this Preliminary Approval Ordinance, meets the standards for
exceptions and modification defined in Section 17.58.040(E) of the Planned
Development Ordinance, in that the additional square footage makes it possible
to provide affordable units with two bedrooms or one bedroom and a den, making
the proposed units of sufficient size to be attractive to residents and
potential residents, and in particular to senior citizens and persons on fixed
incomes seeking to relocate from single family residences to a multi-family
building in a convenient location in proximity to the center of the Village and
public transportation, or to young people who want to live in a convenient
location in the Village but cannot yet afford a single family residence.
      N.   Subject to the terms and conditions hereinafter set forth, the
proposed Development, as depicted in the Approved Preliminary Plans, will
provide substantial public benefit to the Village, in that:
         1.   it provides for full funding of the streetscape improvements
along the south side of Elm Street and east side of Lincoln Avenue from the
eastern edge of the Subject Property on Elm to the southern edge of the Subject
Property on Lincoln, including in front of the two properties at the southeast
corner of Elm Street and Lincoln Avenue (the “Corner Properties”), which are
not part of the proposed Development;
         2.   it provides for full funding of the streetscape improvements
along the north side of Elm Street from Lincoln Avenue to Arbor Vitae Road;
         3.   it provides for full funding for the streetscape improvements to
the northeast and southeast corners of Elm Street and Lincoln Avenue, and to
the northwest and northeast corners of Arbor Vitae Road and Elm Street;
         4.   it provides for partial funding of the streetscape improvements
on the west side of Lincoln Avenue from Elm Street to Oak Street;
         5.   it provides for partial funding of the streetscape improvements
at the northwest and southwest corners of Elm Street and Lincoln Avenue;
         6.   it provides for partial funding for wayfinding signage at the
intersection of Green Bay Road and Elm Street;
         7.   it restores three on-street parking spaces along Elm Street and
Lincoln Avenues by reducing the number of curb cuts;
         8.   it will facilitate the future redevelopment of the Corner
Properties by providing access to potential underground parking for the two
Corner Properties in the event they are redeveloped;
         9.   it adds at least three residential units that meet affordability
standards specific to the Village of Winnetka;
         10.   it will contribute funds to the construction, in the Elm Street
right-of-way from Lincoln Avenue to Maple Avenue, of a water main with a
diameter of up to 10 inches; and
         11.   it will provide an energy efficient and environmentally
sensitive building designed to qualify for LEED or National Green Building
Standard certification.
   SECTION 3:   Preliminary Approval. Subject to the terms, conditions,
restrictions, modifications and exceptions set forth in Sections 4 through 13
of this Preliminary Approval Ordinance, the application of New Trier
Development LLC (“Developer”) for preliminary approval of a planned development
for the Subject Property, commonly known as 511 Lincoln Avenue, 513-515 Lincoln
Avenue and 718-732 Elm Street, Winnetka, Illinois, and located in the C-
2 Commercial Overlay Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.24 of the Winnetka
Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code (“Zoning Ordinance”),
as such development has been modified and amended during the preliminary
approval process (“Development”), is hereby granted.
   SECTION 4:   Development Plans: Subject to the terms and conditions set
forth in Sections 5 through 13 of this Preliminary Approval Ordinance, the
preliminary approval of the proposed Development shall be subject to the
condition that the final Development be substantially as depicted in the plans,
sections and elevations prepared by OKW Architects, captioned “Fell Site
Redevelopment” and dated February 4, 2009, provided that the final plans shall
extend the Tudor detail to the fourth floor of the building (the “Approved
Preliminary Plans”), which Approved Preliminary Plans are attached hereto as
Exhibit B and are incorporated herein by reference.
   SECTION 5:   Exceptions and Modifications. Subject to the terms and
conditions set forth in Sections 6 through 11 of this Preliminary Approval
Ordinance, the following exceptions and modifications from the provisions of
the Planned Development Ordinance are conditionally granted as part of the
preliminary approval of the Development:
      A.   Building Height. An exception from and modification to the building
height limitations of Section 17.58.040(C) of the Planned Development
Ordinance, to allow a maximum building height in excess of 45 feet. This
exception shall allow for a maximum building height, as measured pursuant to
the Zoning Ordinance, of: (i) 47.5 feet at the southeast and southwest corners
of the Development; (ii) 48.5 feet at the northwest corner of the Development;
and (iii) 51.0 feet at the northeast corner of the Development; provided, that
the top of the fourth floor parapet has an elevation of no more than 699.0’
above sea level and provided further that the peaks of the gables may extend
above the parapet, but no more than 3 feet above the top of the parapet, all as
depicted in greater detail in the Approved Preliminary Plans, and in particular
in the building sections labeled “Section A - Lincoln Avenue,” “Section B - Elm
Street,” and “Section C – South Property Line.”
      B.   Intensity of Use of Lot. An exception from and modification to the
intensity of use of lot limitations of Section 17.58.040(E) of the Planned
Development Ordinance to allow: (i) a floor area of 25,915 square feet on the
second floor of the proposed new building, which results in a lot coverage of
72.0%; and (ii) a floor area of 25,650 square feet on the third floor, which
results in a lot coverage of 71.0%, both as depicted in the Approved
Preliminary Plans.
      C.   Limitation of Approval. The foregoing exceptions and modifications
shall be approved only in the event that the Developer obtains approval of a
final development plan. In the event that the Developer fails to obtain final
plan approval as provided in this Preliminary Approval Ordinance, the
conditional approval of the foregoing exceptions and modifications shall become
null and void. Nothing in this Preliminary Approval Ordinance shall be
construed either as granting preliminary or final approval of any zoning
variance or as granting any preliminary or final zoning exception, zoning
modification or special use that was not specifically requested in the
Developer’s application for preliminary approval, as modified and amended by
the Approved Preliminary Plans.
   SECTION 6:   Conditions. The preliminary approval of the Development shall
be subject to the following conditions, all of which shall be incorporated into
the ordinance granting final approval of the Development and into the
Development Agreement required by Section 15.32.080(K) of the Winnetka Village
Code:
      A.   Streetscape Improvements. Developer shall contribute to the actual
hard costs of the construction of certain streetscape improvements by the
Village, as identified below, which streetscape improvements shall be designed
and constructed in accordance with the 2008 Village of Winnetka Master
Streetscape and Wayfinding Plan, prepared by The Lakota Group and approved by
the Village Council pursuant to Resolution R-37-2008, adopted on December 2,
2008 (“Streetscape Improvements”). All such construction shall be done by the
Village pursuant to contracts let by the Village and performed under the
direction of the Village Manager. As used in this subsection A, “actual hard
costs” shall mean the final costs incurred by or on behalf of the Village for
construction activities pursuant to contracts approved by the Village Council,
including change orders thereto, provided such change orders are related to
field conditions or other circumstances beyond the Village’s control and not to
an expansion of the Streetscape Improvements described in the following
paragraphs 1 and 2. “Actual hard costs” shall not include the cost of Village
staff time, the cost of architectural, engineering or design services, or
bidding procedure costs.
         1.   Site-Related Streetscape. Developer shall reimburse the Village
for 100% of the actual hard costs of installing the Streetscape Improvements at
the following locations, as graphically depicted on the Work Area Map that is
attached hereto as Exhibit C and is incorporated herein by reference:
            a.   The south side of Elm Street, extending from the east property
line of the Subject Property to and including the eastern edge of the Lincoln
Avenue street pavement.
            b.   The east side of Lincoln Avenue, extending from the southern
edge of the Elm Street pavement to the south property line of the Subject
Property.
            c.   The work described in the foregoing subparagraphs a and b
shall include the streetscape improvements to the southeast corner of Elm
Street and Lincoln Avenue.
         2.   East Elm Streetscape. Developer shall reimburse the Village for
Developer’s share of the actual hard costs of installing the Streetscape
Improvements at the following locations and percentages, as graphically
depicted on the Work Area Map that is attached hereto as Exhibit C and is
incorporated herein by reference:
            a.   The full amount (100%) of the actual hard costs of the
streetscape improvements on the north side of Elm Street, extending from the
eastern edge of the Lincoln Avenue street pavement to and including the
northeast corner of Arbor Vitae Road.
            b.   One-half (50%) of the actual hard costs of the streetscape
improvements to the northwest corner of Lincoln Avenue and Elm Street.
            c.   One-fourth (25%) of the actual hard costs of the streetscape
improvements along the west side of Lincoln Avenue, extending from the southern
edge of the street pavement at the southwest corner of Lincoln Avenue and Elm
Street to the northern edge of the street pavement at Oak Street, including the
streetscape improvements to the southwest corner of Elm and Lincoln and to the
northwest corner of Oak and Lincoln. The reimbursement requirement of this
paragraph shall be subject to the condition that the Village enter into a
contract for such work within two years after the issuance of the first
certificate of occupancy for the Development.
            d.   One-fourth (25%) of the actual hard costs of the installation
of wayfinding signage at the intersection of Green Bay Road and Elm Street. The
reimbursement requirement of this paragraph shall be subject to the condition
that the Village enter into a contract for such work within two years after the
issuance of the first certificate of occupancy for the Development.
         3.   Notwithstanding the forgoing, the Village, at its sole discretion
and at its sole cost, shall have and retain the discretion to enter into
contracts and proceed with the construction of the Streetscape Improvements or
to expand the extent of any streetscape to be undertaken during any given phase
of the streetscape construction, at any time; provided, that the actual
construction of such improvements immediately adjacent to the Subject Property
shall not take place before Developer has completed the exterior building
construction of the Development unless the Developer gives its written consent.
         4.   The Village shall have the right to reimbursement for all
Streetscape Improvements covered by this Section 6.A, in the percentage amounts
set forth in this Section 6.A, provided that the contracts for such Streetscape
Improvements have been advertised for bid after the date of this Preliminary
Approval Ordinance, but prior to the issuance of the final certificate of
occupancy for the Development. The Village’s failure to advertise for bid and
enter into contracts for such streetscape improvements as provided in this
subsection A shall not be grounds for withholding a certificate of occupancy
for the Development.
         5.   Developer shall reimburse the Village Developer’s share of the
above-described actual hard costs within 30 days after receiving a copy of the
contractor’s invoice from the Village, provided that reimbursement to the
Village for work completed by the Village prior to the issuance of the first
building permit for the Development shall be due and payable at the time said
building permit is issued, and no such building permit shall be issued unless
Developer has paid the outstanding reimbursement amount in full. The Village
shall not forward any invoices to Developer for reimbursement unless the
Village has first determined, in the ordinary course of business, that the
contractor has submitted all required documentation for payment.
         6. The Developer shall have no obligation to reimburse the Village for
any of the costs set forth in Section 6.A unless and until the Village has
granted final approval of the Development.
      B.   Elm Street Water Main. Developer shall contribute to the actual hard
costs of the construction of a water main in the Elm Street right-of-way, as
identified below (“Water Main Improvements”). As used in this subsection B,
“actual hard costs” shall mean the final costs incurred by or on behalf of the
Village for construction activities for the Water Main Improvements pursuant to
contracts approved by the Village Council, including change orders thereto,
provided such change orders are related to field conditions or other
circumstances beyond the Village’s control and not to an increase in the size
of the main or an expansion of the work beyond the areas designated in the
following paragraph 1. “Actual hard costs” shall not include the cost of
Village staff time, the cost of architectural, engineering or design services,
or bidding procedure costs.
         1.   The maximum length of the water main shall be the distance from
the center line of Lincoln Avenue to the center line of Maple Avenue.
         2.   The water main shall have a diameter of no more than 10 inches.
         3.   The Developer shall reimburse the Village for one-tenth (10%) of
the actual hard costs of the construction of the Water Main Improvements under
this Section 6.B.
         4.   Notwithstanding the foregoing, if the Village has not entered
into a contract for such work and commenced construction by the time Developer
begins construction on the water connection for the Development, then the
requirements of this subsection B shall not apply.
         5.   The Developer shall have no obligation to reimburse the Village
for any of the costs set forth in Section 6.A unless and until the Village has
granted final approval of the Development.
      C.   Site Utilities. Developer shall provide, at its sole cost and
expense, for the extension, installation and connection of all utility
facilities necessary to serve the Development, including without limitation:
(a) water service connections and meters, fire hydrants and standpipes,
electric service connections and individual service lines, electric meters and
transformers, sanitary sewer lines and storm sewer lines; (b) all applicable
service and connection fees; and (c) any upgrades to Village utility systems
that the Village determines is directly attributable and reasonably necessary
to provide adequate utility service to Development. Where utility service is
provided by the Village, the Village shall retain all authority and discretion,
pursuant to applicable State law and the Winnetka Village Code, to determine
the nature and extent of the utility facilities necessary to serve the
Development and to determine the methods of construction.
      D.   Use and Maintenance of the Subject Property. The Village finds and
acknowledges that Developer has purchased the Subject Property as is, for the
purpose of demolishing all existing buildings and redeveloping the Subject
Property with a new building. To assure that the public health, safety and
general welfare are protected pending such demolition and redevelopment, the
following minimum standards for the use and maintenance of the Subject Property
shall apply:
         1.   Prior to demolition, Developer shall not allow the building and
Subject Property to fall into disrepair and shall promptly make all necessary
repairs. Developer shall also maintain the building and Subject Property in a
safe and first class condition and in good repair.
         2.   As used in this subsection, “first class” shall mean:
            a.   that Developer shall use commercially reasonable efforts, as
determined by the Director of Community Development, under the supervision of
the Village Manager, to ensure: (i) that exterior enclosures (exterior walls,
windows, doors, etc.) are adequately maintained, watertight and in good
operating condition; (ii) that roofing systems are adequately maintained and
watertight; (iii) that all heating, ventilating, air conditioning, electric,
plumbing and fire- and life-safety systems serving the existing buildings and
commercial tenant spaces are maintained in good repair and remain functional at
all times; (iv) that parking areas are kept clean and well lit; and (v) that
structural systems are maintained in good repair, with any indication of
structural deterioration promptly repaired;
            b.   that Developer shall use its best efforts, as determined by
the Director of Community Development, under the supervision of the Village
Manager, to ensure: (i) that any damage from vandals, including graffiti, are
promptly removed; (ii) that any debris and accumulations of dirt are promptly
removed; (iii) that salt or similar de-icers are applied to pedestrian
walkways, vehicular driveways and parking areas to protect against the
accumulation of snow and ice; and (iv) that accumulations of snow and ice on
such areas be promptly removed; and
            c.   that Developer shall promptly replace any cracked or broken
windows within 7 days, unless otherwise approved by the Director of Community
Development.
         3.   All materials used in maintaining and repairing the Subject
Property and the existing buildings shall be of equal or better quality than
the existing materials.
         4.   Developer shall use commercially reasonable efforts, as
determined by the Director of Community Development, under the supervision of
the Village Manager, to keep the existing buildings occupied and under lease to
tenants at all times until a demolition permit is issued by the Village.
Developer shall submit quarterly reports to the Director of Community
Development regarding building occupancy and Developer’s efforts to lease the
existing buildings. Such reports shall include the names of tenants, the
lengths and termination dates of leases, and the status of leases in
negotiation, including tenants contacted during any such period, the status of
discussions with such tenants and letters of intent. Such information shall not
be subject to public disclosure unless required by law.
      E.   Taxes and Utilities. Developer shall pay all taxes and utility fees
as and when due and shall provide proof that the Subject Property is free of
utility, tax or other liens as a condition for final planned development
approval and as a condition for the issuance of any permits.
      F.   Underground Parking. As an alternative to the two levels of
underground parking depicted in the Approve Preliminary Plans, Developer shall
be permitted to construct a single level of underground parking, provided all
of the following conditions are met:
         1.   The reduction in number of parking levels shall be accomplished
by eliminating the level identified in the Approved Preliminary Plans as the
“First Lower Level Plan.”
         2.   No below-grade commercial uses shall be permitted.
         3.   The single lower level parking plan shall provide no less than 70
parking spaces, except as necessary to comply with Section 6.G of this
Preliminary Approval Ordinance.
         4.   The single lower level parking plan shall fully comply with the
following Section 6.G of this Preliminary Approval Ordinance.
The construction of a single level of parking as provided in this subsection
shall be considered part of the approval granted pursuant to this Preliminary
Approval Ordinance and shall not be considered an amendment or change to the
plans under Section 17.58.120 of the Planned Development Ordinance.
      G.   Redevelopment of Adjoining Properties. The underground parking shall
be designed and constructed so as to enable the corner properties commonly
known as 736 Elm and 740 Elm and currently occupied by Conney’s Pharmacy and
Phototronics (the “Corner Properties”), to be redeveloped with underground
parking to be accessed through the building to be constructed on the Subject
Property, as follows:
         1.   Parking Access. Whether Developer provides parking in accordance
with the Approved Preliminary Plans or in accordance with the alternative
outlined in Section 6.F, Developer, at its sole cost and expense, shall design
the upper level of underground parking to allow for the efficient flow and
operation of parking within the Subject Property and to ensure that it is
integrated with a parking plan for the Corner Properties. Such design shall
include the installation of “knock-out” panels in the foundation walls to
provide access from the Subject Property to future underground parking for the
Corner Properties. Except as provided in paragraph 3 below, Developer shall not
be entitled to recover any part of such costs and expenses from the Corner
Properties.
         2.   Covenant for Construction and Access Easements. The final plan
shall include a declaration of non-exclusive construction and access easements
for the joint benefit of the Corner Properties. The declaration shall be in a
form acceptable to the Village and shall incorporate the requirement of a
mutually agreeable access agreement. The access agreement shall include the
terms and conditions set out in the following paragraph and shall be required
as a condition for the issuance of any building permit for the redevelopment of
the Corner Properties with underground parking.
         3.   Access Agreement. The access agreement shall be negotiated in
good faith by the Developer and the owners of the Corner Properties, without
unduly burdensome or unreasonable requirements being imposed by either party,
provided that the agreement shall include at least the following provisions:
(i) non-exclusive ingress and egress for construction of underground parking on
the Corner Properties, (ii) non-exclusive permanent ingress and egress to
parking under the Corner Properties by way of the ramps and underground parking
aisles of the Subject Property, and (iii) a requirement that the owners of the
Corner Properties shall be responsible for all costs of constructing
underground parking on the Corner Properties, including the costs of breaking
through the above-described “knock-out” panels and of restoring the Subject
Property after completing such construction. In addition, said access agreement
shall include provisions regarding (i) reasonable and customary indemnification
and insurance coverage provisions to be provided by the Corner Properties to
protect and hold harmless the Developer against damage to the Subject Property
and injury to third parties arising from the Corner Properties’ exercise of
rights under the construction and access easements, (ii) allocation to the
Corner Properties on a straight-line, pro-rata basis of the costs of normal
maintenance and repair of those portions of the garage on the Subject Property
used by the Corner Properties, (iii) reasonable and customary security
provisions and regulations for the Subject Property, and (iv) payment to
Developer of either an annual license fee not to exceed a combined total of
$10,000 for both Corner Properties, which amount shall be subject to an annual
inflation increase of 2% or the CPI increase, whichever is less, calculated
from the date the declaration is recorded. In lieu of the annual license fee,
said access agreement may provide for a mutually agreeable one-time payment for
the conveyance of the construction and access easements.
         4.   Limitations.
            a.   The requirements of this Section 6.G shall not apply if the
property commonly known as 740 Elm is redeveloped independently of the other
Corner Property, in which case Developer shall be entitled to a release of the
covenants and easements described in paragraph 6.G.2, above, which shall be
prepared and recorded by Developer, at its sole cost, in a form acceptable to
the Village Attorney. The Village President and Village Clerk shall be
authorized to execute and attest to said release on behalf of the Village,
without further action by the Village Council.
            b.   Nothing in this Section 6.G shall be construed as granting to
the Corner Properties an entitlement to park on the Subject Property.
      H.   First Floor Commercial Space. The first floor shall be developed in
substantial conformity with the first floor site plan depicted in the Approved
Preliminary Plans, subject to the following conditions:
         1.   Any reconfiguration of space that is made at any time prior to
two years from the date of the issuance of the first certificate of occupancy
for commercial space and that results in a retail space in excess of 9,500
square feet shall be considered an amendment to the planned development and
shall be subject to approval by the Village Council pursuant to Section
17.58.120(C) of the Zoning Ordinance. The Developer shall be entitled to
approval of the change if the Village Council, in the sole and exclusive
exercise of its discretion, finds and determines that all of the following
conditions have been met:
            a.   that the proposed use of the reconfigured space meets a
demonstrated community need in the East and West Elm Commercial Districts; and
            b.   that the Developer has exercised due diligence in attempting
to lease the space in a conforming configuration, but has been unable to obtain
a tenant that will agree to occupy the approved space within two years after
the issuance of any certificate of occupancy for the first floor space.
         2.   Except as provided in the foregoing paragraph 1, changes in the
locations of internal demising walls and partitions shall not be considered an
amendment or change to the to the Approved Preliminary Plans or to final plans
that are otherwise in substantial conformity with the Approved Preliminary
Plans under Section 17.58.120 of the Zoning Ordinance.
         3.   Except as provided in this Section 6.H, all amendments to the
final, approved plans shall be subject to Section 17.58.120 of the Zoning
Ordinance.
      I.   Affordable Housing.
         1.   Developer shall reserve and market at least three of the
residential units as affordable housing units. Each affordable unit shall meet
the following qualifications:
            a.   The unit shall have at least two bedrooms or one bedroom and a
den.
            b.   The unit shall be priced so that mortgage, amortization,
taxes, insurance, and condominium or association fees, if any, constitute no
more than 30% of the gross annual household income for a household of the size
that may occupy the unit.
            c.   The unit shall be owned, occupied and marketed in perpetuity
only to purchasers who meet the qualifications of the following paragraph 2.
         2.   The affordable units shall be sold only to purchasers who meet
the following qualifications:
            a.   Income Qualifications. The maximum household income of the
purchaser at the time the property is purchased must not exceed 80% of the then
current Village of Winnetka median income, adjusted for household size, as
reported from time to time by the by U.S. Bureau of the Census, U.S. Department
of Housing and Urban Development or any successor thereof.
            b.   Citizenship Qualification. The purchaser must be a citizen of
the United States or a registered alien thereof.
         3.   The affordable housing units shall be designated in the plans
submitted for final planned development approval and their intended location
shall be incorporated into the final approval of the Development, subject to
relocation within the Development in the manner provided in the following
subparagraph 4.
         4.   The Village shall have no obligation to issue a permit for the
construction of the Development unless and until the plans submitted for
permits for the construction of the residential units include construction
details for the affordable housing units which shall provide for the perpetual
affordability of the subject units. Notwithstanding the foregoing, so long as
at least five residential units in the Development remain unsold, Developer
shall have the right to relocate any or all of the three affordable housing
units in the Development; provided, that such relocated affordable units (i)
are no smaller than the affordable units depicted on the approved final plans,
(ii) are otherwise in full compliance with the requirements of this Section 6.I
and (iii) are submitted for permit review and found to be in compliance with
all applicable provisions of the Building Code.
         5.   The Village shall have no obligation to issue a certificate of
occupancy for any residential unit in the Development unless and until the
Developer has recorded a declaration of covenants, conditions, and restrictions
against each affordable unit (“Declaration”). The Declaration shall provide
that each affordable unit shall be owned, occupied, marketed and conveyed in
perpetuity only to purchasers who meet the affordability standards defined in
paragraph 6.I.2, above. The Declaration shall be in a form approved by the
Village Attorney and shall be recorded as part of the recording of the
condominium declaration for the Development.
         6.   Notwithstanding the foregoing, in the event that the United
States Government or the State of Illinois enact any law that mandates any
affordable housing standards that are different from those provided herein, and
in the event that the Development or Developer do not qualify for an exemption
under such laws, then such federal or State law shall apply to the Development.
      J.   Term of Building Permit. The building permit issued for the
construction of the Development shall be in effect for a term of 30 months from
the date of issuance.
      K.   Successors, Transferees and Release of Transfers.
         1.   Binding Effect. Developer acknowledges, agrees and intends that
this Preliminary Approval Ordinance, as well as the subsequent final approval
and the Development Agreement required by this Preliminary Approval Ordinance
shall inure to the benefit of and shall be binding upon Developer and any and
all of its heirs, successors and Permitted Transferees, and the successor
owners of all or any portions of the Subject Property, except as otherwise
expressly provided in the following paragraphs 2, 3, 4 and 5.
         2.   Transfer and Transferee Defined.
            a.   For purposes of this subsection, the term “transfer” shall be
deemed to include any assignment, sale, transfer to a receiver or to a trustee
in bankruptcy, transfer in trust, or other transfer or disposition of the
Subject Property, or any beneficial interest therein, in whole or in part, by
voluntary or involuntary sale, foreclosure, merger, sale and leaseback,
consolidation, or otherwise except for Permitted Transfers and Excluded
Transfers.
            b.   For purposes of this subsection, neither the restructuring of
the Developer’s legal form of business, whether through the formation of a new
corporation, limited liability company, partnership or joint venture, nor the
addition of shareholders, members, managers, partners or joint venturers shall
be considered a transfer under this Section 6.K; provided that (i) such
restructuring does not constitute a transfer as defined in the foregoing
subparagraph a, (ii) Developer remains in majority ownership and management
control of the Subject Property and the Development, (iii) Developer does not
transfer any of its rights or responsibilities under this Preliminary Approval
Ordinance, the final approval or the Development Agreement, and (iv) Developer
remains solely responsible for the construction of the Development and
Developer’s performance under this Preliminary Approval Ordinance, the final
approval and the Development Agreement.
         3.   Excluded Transfers and Excluded Transferees Defined. The term
“transfer” shall not be deemed to include the formation of a condominium
association, or the assignment, transfer, sale or conveyance of a residential
unit upon its completion, or the mortgage of any residential or commercial
unit, unless such assignment, transfer, sale or conveyance is to the Developer
or any person or entity for the benefit of or subject to the control of the
Developer, which permitted transfers shall be referred to herein as “Excluded
Transfers”. The recipients of such Excluded Transfers shall be known as
“Excluded Transferees.”
         4.   Transferee Assumption. To assure that any potential heir,
successor, or Permitted Transferee has notice of this Preliminary Approval
Ordinance and the obligations created by it, Developer shall agree as follows:
            a.   The final plat of development and the Development Agreement
shall be recorded with the Cook County Recorder of Deeds.
            b.   Prior to the transfer of a legal or beneficial interest in all
or any portion of the Subject Property to other than an Excluded Transfer to an
Excluded Transferee, Developer shall require, the transferee of the Subject
Property to execute an enforceable written agreement stating the transferee’s
unequivocal agreement to be bound by the provisions of this Preliminary
Approval Ordinance, the final approval and the Development Agreement and, in
the event that the Development has not been completed at the time of such
transfer, to provide the Village with such assurance of the transferee’s
ability to meet such obligations as are required pursuant to this Preliminary
Approval Ordinance, the final approval and the Development Agreement, as such
qualifications are set forth in paragraph 6, below (“Transferee
Qualifications”).
            c.   Developer agrees to notify the Village in writing at least 45
days prior to the date on which Developer proposes to transfer a legal or
beneficial interest in all or any portion of the Subject Property or the
Development, other than with respect to Excluded Transfers described above.
Developer shall, at the same time, provide the Village with a fully executed
copy of the above required agreement by the transferee to be bound by the
provisions of this Preliminary Approval Ordinance, the subsequent approval
ordinance and the Development Agreement and the transferee’s proof of
Transferee Qualifications. The Village shall promptly review Developer’s
request and approve or reject the proposed transfer prior to the proposed date
of such transfer, as set out in the Developer’s notice. If the Village
determines that the proposed successor or transferee has completed the
foregoing requirements, the transfer shall be deemed a “Permitted Transfer” and
the Village Council shall adopt a resolution accepting the proposed transferee
as a “Permitted Transferee” hereunder, and releasing the liability of Developer
or other predecessor obligor to the extent of the Permitted Transferee’s
assumption of liability. For purposes of the foregoing, the consent of the
Village Council shall not be unreasonably withheld, and the Village’s decision
as to whether to so consent shall be made in good faith and shall be based
solely upon the Transferee Qualifications as defined in paragraph 6, below.
         5.   Prohibited Assignments and Transfers. It is the express intent of
the parties hereto that this Preliminary Approval Ordinance, the final approval
and the Development Agreement, and all of the rights and privileges granted
pursuant to those instruments, are for the sole and exclusive benefit of
Developer. Accordingly, notwithstanding any other provision of this Preliminary
Approval Ordinance, and except for a Permitted Transfer or an Excluded
Transfer, in the event that Developer, at any time prior to the completion of
the Development, does, or attempts to, voluntarily or involuntarily transfer
its interests in the Subject Property, in whole or in part, without the prior
consent of the Village Council as provided in the foregoing paragraph 4, all of
the rights and privileges granted Developer herein, shall, at the option of the
Village, become null and void and be of no force or effect, and the Village
shall be entitled to stop all work on the Development.
         6.   Transferee Qualifications. For purposes of this Section 6.K,
“Transferee Qualifications” shall mean and include that the proposed
transferee: (i) has the financial and economic ability of a proposed transferee
to meet Developer’s financial obligations under the Preliminary Approval
Ordinance, the final approval and the Development Agreement, (ii) demonstrates
that it has experience in completing a development reasonably comparable to the
Development; (iii) is not currently in personal bankruptcy or in a bankruptcy
for any reasonably comparable development; (iv) does not have any pending
criminal charges against it; and (v) is not in default or breach of any
development agreements to which it may be bound for any reasonably comparable
development. The information provided pursuant to this paragraph 6 shall be
considered proprietary and shall not be subject to public disclosure unless it
is a record maintained by a court or other public body and is generally
available to the public as such, or unless such disclosure is required by law.
      L.   No Third Party Beneficiaries. Nothing in this Preliminary Approval
Ordinance shall create, or shall be construed or interpreted to create, any
third party beneficiary rights.
      M.   Effective Date of Final Approval Ordinance. The ordinance granting
final approval of the development shall be effective only upon the occurrence
of all of the following events:
         1.   Passage of the final approval ordinance by the Village Council in
the manner required by law;
         2.   Publication and posting of the final approval ordinance in the
manner required by law;
         3.   Developer’s filing with the Village Clerk, within 10 days after
the passage of the final approval ordinance, for recording at Developer’s
expense in the Office of Cook County Recorder of Deeds, of an unconditional
agreement and consent, signed by the Developer and all other owners of the
Subject Property, to accept and abide by each and all of the terms, conditions,
and limitations set forth in the final approval ordinance; and
         4.   the recording of the approved final development plan with the
Cook County Recorder of Deeds.
In the event that the Developer does not file with the Village Clerk a fully
executed copy of the unconditional agreement and consent required pursuant to
paragraph 3 of this subsection 6.M, the final approval ordinance within 10 days
after the date of passage of said ordinance, then the Village Council shall
have the sole and exclusive option to adopt a resolution duly declaring this
ordinance to null, void and of no force or effect.
      N.   Certifications. From time to time, at the request of Developer and
subject to Developer’s payment of applicable fees as established in the
Village’s fee resolutions, the Village will provide certified copies of any or
all of the following: (i) this Preliminary Approval Ordinance, the final
approval ordinance and the Development Agreement, including any amendments
thereto; (ii) resolutions authorizing transfers of any part of the Development
pursuant to Section 6.K of this Preliminary Approval Ordinance; and (iii) any
outstanding notices of performance or failure to perform under the terms of
this Preliminary Approval Ordinance, the final approval ordinance and the
Development Agreement, including any amendments thereto.
   SECTION 7:   Development Agreement. The final approval of the Development
shall include the requirement that, prior to the issuance of any permits, the
Developer shall enter into a development agreement with the Village. The
Development Agreement shall contain, without limitation, all of the provisions
required by Section 15.32.080(K) of the Winnetka Village Code; provided, that
the Development Agreement shall not conflict with the terms of this Preliminary
Approval Ordinance or confer any additional modifications or exceptions not
granted in the final approval of the Development. In addition, the Development
Agreement shall include the following provisions, substantially in the form set
forth below in this Section 7:
      A.   Exterior Building Equipment.
         1.   The electric transformers for the Development shall be in the
location depicted in the Approved Preliminary Plan and shall be screened so as
to minimize their view from the street to the extent reasonably possible.
         2.   All equipment located on the rooftop of the building shall be
subject to the height limitations of Section 17.44.010(C) of the Village Code.
In addition, such equipment shall be screened and located so as to minimize its
view from the street to the extent reasonably possible.
      B.   Performance Security. The performance security for the Development
shall include the following:
         1.   Project Financing. Prior to the issuance of the demolition
permit, the Developer shall provide current proof that it has obtained and
closed on all debt, equity and self-financed portions of financing, satisfying
all conditions to funding, in amounts sufficient to fully fund the construction
and full completion of all requirements of the Development in full compliance
with the final development approval ordinance and is in full compliance with
the performance security requirements of the final approval ordinance and all
other applicable laws, rules and regulations. Developer shall have the option
to propose, in the alternative to third-party financing, that the Development
be self-financed by the Developer, provided that the Village shall not be
required to accept such proposal unless the Developer submits information and
performance security requirements that the Village determines is adequate to
demonstrate that the Developer, including its members and shareholders, have
liquid assets sufficient to fully fund the construction of all requirements of
the Development in full compliance with the final development approval
ordinance, and that such assets shall be irrevocably pledged exclusively to the
completion the Development project as approved. The amount of required
financing shall be based on the total estimated project costs to construct and
complete the work, shall be consistent with the costs used in setting permit
fees under the Village’s fee resolutions and shall include all soft costs
required for the Development. Only unencumbered tangible assets, as reported on
the audited financial statements of the Developer, including its members and
shareholders, shall be considered in determining the sufficiency of self-
financing under this paragraph. The information provided to establish the
sufficiency of self-financing under this paragraph 1 shall be considered
proprietary and shall not be subject to public disclosure unless it is a record
maintained by a court or other public body and is generally available to the
public as such, or unless such disclosure is required by law.
         2.   General Performance Security Requirements.
            a.   Streetscape Improvements and Elm Street Water Main. Prior to
the issuance of any permits, the Developer shall provide a letter of credit to
the Village in the amount of 125% of the Developer’s share of the Village
Engineer’s estimated “actual hard costs” of Streetscape Improvements, Elm
Street Water Main described in Sections 6.A and 6.B, above, as that term is
defined therein; provided, that if, at the time the performance security is
required, any portion of such work either is the subject of a contract to
perform such work or has been performed and billed to the Village, then the
amount of the letter of credit shall be 125% of the Developer’s share either of
the actual contract amounts, or of the actual amounts invoiced to the Village
for work performed under such contracts.
            b.   Site Utilities. Prior to the issuance of any permits, the
Developer shall provide a letter of credit to the Village in the amount of 125%
of the Village Engineer’s estimated cost of the Site Utilities described in
Section 6.C, above.
            c.   Site Restoration. The performance security for the Development
shall also be sufficient to secure Developer’s performance of its site
restoration obligations pursuant to Section 7.E, below, and shall be in an
amount equal to 125% of the Village Engineer’s estimate of the cost of
restoration, which shall not include the reconstruction of the existing
building. The performance security shall be in the form of cash and/or an
irrevocable letter of credit in a form provided by and acceptable to the
Village.
            d.   Form of Performance Security. All performance security shall
be in the form of cash and/or an irrevocable letter of credit in a form
provided by or acceptable to the Village. Any and all letters of credit
provided to the Village shall be issued by a bank that the Village, in the sole
exercise of its discretion and acting through its Finance Director, shall find
to have sufficient assets to perform its obligations under such letter of
credit. The terms for drawing against the performance security and for
releasing said performance security shall be set out in the development
agreement required by Section 15.32.080(K) of the Winnetka Village Code.
            3.   Maintenance Guarantee. Following the Village's release of any
percentage of the performance security, Developer shall provide a maintenance
guarantee, which shall be in effect for a period of two years from the date of
the Village’s acceptance of the improvements; provided, that such maintenance
guarantee shall be limited solely to the utility facilities that are
constructed as part of the Site Utilities and that are conveyed to the Village
pursuant to Title 13 of the Village Code. The maintenance guarantee shall be in
the form of a letter of credit or performance bond, shall be equal to 15% of
the actual costs of such utility facilities, and shall be in a form acceptable
to the Village Attorney. The specific terms and conditions for the maintenance
guarantee shall be set out in the development agreement required by Section
15.32.080(K) of the Winnetka Village Code.
      C.   Environmental Design Certification. The Development shall be
designed to meet standards in effect as of the date of this Preliminary
Approval Ordinance for Leadership in Energy and Environmental Design (LEED)
Silver Certification or National Green Building Standard (ANSI) Gold
Certification. Upon receiving proof of such qualification, Developer shall
promptly deliver such proof to the Director of Community Development. Upon
receiving proof of certification following the construction of the Subject
Property, Developer shall promptly deliver such proof to the Director of
Community Development.
      D.   Indemnification and Waiver of Liability.
         1.   Waiver of Liability for Village Review. Developer acknowledges
and agrees (i) that the Village is not, and shall not be, in any way liable for
any violations of restrictive covenants applicable to the Subject Property that
may occur, or for any damages or injuries that may be sustained, as the result
of the Village's review and approval of Developer’s applications and plans for
the Subject Property, or as a result of the issuance of any approvals, permits,
certificates, or acceptances relating to the use and development of the Subject
Property; and (ii) that the Village's review and approval of any such plans and
applications and the issuance of any such approvals, permits, certificates, or
acceptances does not, and shall not, in any way, be deemed to insure Developer,
or any of its heirs, successors, assigns, tenants, or licensees, or any third
party, against restrictive covenant violations or damage or injury of any kind
at any time.
         2.   Indemnity. Developer agrees to, and does hereby, hold harmless
and indemnify the Village, the Village Council, all elected and appointed
Village officials, officers, employees, agents, representatives, and attorneys,
from any and all claims that may, at any time, be asserted against any of such
parties in connection with (i) the Village’s defense against challenges to the
Village’s approval of the Development, whether to the preliminary or final
approval; (ii) the Village's review and approval of any of the Developer’s
plans, applications, or improvements of the Subject Property, or the issuance
of any approvals, permits, certificates, or acceptances relating to the use and
development of the Subject Property; (iii) the development, construction, and
maintenance of the Subject Property; (iv) the violation of any restrictive
covenant applicable to the Subject Property; and (v) the performance by the
Developer of its obligations under this Preliminary Approval Ordinance, the
subsequent approval ordinance, the development agreement and all related
ordinances, resolutions, or other agreements; provided, however, that the
provisions of this subsection D.2 of Section 7 shall not apply to willful,
wanton, reckless, or negligent action by the Village, the Village Council, or
any elected or appointed Village officials, officers, employees, agents,
representatives, or attorneys.
         3.   Defense Expenses. Developer shall and does hereby agree to pay,
without protest, all expenses incurred by the Village in defending itself with
regard to any and all of the claims for which the Developer is required to
provide indemnification under the foregoing paragraph 2. These expenses shall
include all out-of-pocket expenses, such as attorneys' and experts' fees, and
shall also include the reasonable value of any services rendered by any
employees of the Village, including, without limitation, the Village Attorney.
         4.   Limitations. Nothing in this Section 7.D shall be construed as
creating any duty or liability on the part of Developer for work done on
related Village projects by third parties under contract with the Village,
including the improvements performed by or on behalf of the Village under
subsections A and B of Section 6 of this Preliminary Approval Ordinance.
      E.   Site Restoration; Partial Completion . The Village shall have the
right and authority to remove any partially demolished or partially completed
building or structure on the Subject Property, to remove any improvements,
weeds or debris on the Subject Property, to fill in any incomplete or
unrestored excavations and to otherwise restore the Subject property, in the
event the Developer fails to diligently pursue all construction to completion
within the time period prescribed in the building permit or permits issued by
the Village for such construction, and if the building permit or permits are
not revived within three months after the lapse thereof, and if, after being
given due notice by the Village, Developer fails, within the time specified in
such notice, to cure such violation or to remove such partially completed
building or structure, any improvements, weeds or debris and restore the
Subject Property to its original condition. In the event of such failure by the
Developer, the Village shall have, and shall be granted, in addition to all
other rights afforded to the Village in the Development Agreement and by law,
the right, at its option, to demolish and remove said buildings, structures,
improvements, weeds, and debris from the Subject Property and to restore the
Subject Property, and the Village shall have the right to charge Developer an
amount sufficient to defray the entire cost of such work, including legal and
administrative costs. If the amount so charged is not paid by Developer within
30 days after a demand in writing by the Village for such payment, such charge,
together with interest and costs of collection, shall become a lien on the
Subject Property, and the Village shall have the right to collect such charge,
with interest and costs, and to enforce such lien in the same manner as in
mortgage foreclosure proceedings.
      F.   Failure to Comply with Conditions.  Upon failure or refusal of the
Developer, or any of its officers, employees, or agents, to comply with any or
all of the conditions, restrictions, or provisions of the final approval
ordinance or development agreement, the permits and approvals granted in the
final approval ordinance shall, at the sole discretion of the Village Council,
by ordinance duly adopted, be revoked and become null and void; provided, the
Developer shall first have been provided with at least 60 days notice of the
reasons for the revocation and an opportunity to cure, and provided that
Developer shall have an opportunity to be heard at a regular meeting of the
Village Council. In the event of revocation, the development and use of the
Subject Property shall be governed solely by the regulations of the C-2 General
Retail Commercial District, as the same may, from time to time, be amended.
Further, in the event of a revocation of the permits or approvals granted
herein, the Village Manager and Village Attorney are hereby authorized and
directed to bring any zoning enforcement action that may be appropriate under
the circumstances.
   SECTION 8:   Payment of Village Fees and Costs.  Except as otherwise
provided in the Development Agreement, Developer shall pay to the Village, as
and when due, all application, review, inspection, and permit fees, all water
and sewer general and special connection fees, tap-on fees, charges and
contributions, and all other fees, expenses, charges and contributions required
by applicable Village codes, ordinance, resolutions, rules or regulations,
including, without limitation, the fees, charges and contributions provided in
the fee schedules set by Village resolution or established by Village Code. All
payments shall be at the rates that are in effect at the time the charge is
incurred, and the Village reserves its right and discretion to establish or
increase rates from time to time, provided that the Developer shall retain the
right to challenge any new rates or charges on the grounds that they
unreasonably apply solely to the developer or the Development.
   SECTION 9:   Protection of Buildings on Adjoining Properties During
Construction. Developer shall be solely responsible for protecting the
buildings on adjoining properties from damage during Developer’s construction
of the Development and shall be solely responsible for any damage or injury to
such buildings or properties. The means and methods for providing such
protection shall be determined by the Developer, subject to review and approval
by the Village Engineer and Director of Community Development or their
designees, and shall be incorporated into the Development Agreement.
   SECTION 10:   Non-interference with Businesses. All of Developer’s
construction activities for the Development shall be performed so as to
minimize, to the extent reasonably possible, any interruption of or
interference with the business on adjacent properties, and any interference
with pedestrian and vehicular traffic, including parking, and other businesses
in the vicinity of the Subject Property.
   SECTION 11:   Effect of Preliminary Approval; Final Plan Approval.
      A.   Duration of Preliminary Approval. Except as provided in the
following subsection B, this preliminary approval shall expire 18 months from
the effective date of this Preliminary Approval Ordinance unless Developer has
initiated the filing of its application for final approval prior to that time;
provided, that in the event a third party sues the Developer and/or the Village
concerning this Preliminary Approval Ordinance, such time shall toll until the
final disposition of such litigation.
      B.   Final Plan Approval. Developer shall submit its application for
final approval of the Development as required by Chapter 17.56 and 17.58 of the
Winnetka Village Code.
         1.   Review by Plan Commission and Zoning Board of Appeals. If the
proposed final plans are in substantial conformity with the Approved
Preliminary Plans and are otherwise in compliance with all of the terms,
conditions and exceptions of this Preliminary Approval Ordinance, and if the
Developer is in full compliance with the terms, condition and exceptions of
this Preliminary Approval Ordinance, then the Plan Commission and Zoning Board
of Appeals shall enter findings as provided in Section 17.58.110 of the Zoning
Ordinance and shall recommend final approval of the planned development.
Notwithstanding the provisions of Section 17.58.100(D) of the Zoning Ordinance,
neither the Plan Commission nor the Zoning Board of Appeals shall recommend any
further conditions for development, unless (i) the proposed final plan contains
substantial changes, as defined in Section 17.58.090(A)(1) of the Zoning
Ordinance, from the Approved Preliminary Plan and such conditions are related
to and limited to such changes, or (ii) the recommendations are related to
Village staff recommendations arising from the review of engineering drawings.
         2.   Review by Design Review Board. The Design Review Board’s review
shall be limited to providing comment and recommendations to the Village
Council, as provided in Section 17.58.110(D) of the Zoning Ordinance, as to
whether the building design and other proposed exterior aspects of the
Development, including the proposed Tudor detail for the fourth floor, are in
substantial conformity with the Approved Preliminary Plans and otherwise comply
with the Village of Winnetka Commercial Design Guidelines and with the terms
and conditions of this Preliminary Approval Ordinance that pertain to building
design and materials.
         3.   Approval by Village Council. If the Village Council finds that
the proposed final plans are in substantial conformity with the Approved
Preliminary Plans and are otherwise in compliance with all of the terms,
conditions and exceptions of this Preliminary Approval Ordinance, and if the
Developer is in full compliance with the terms, condition and exceptions of
this Preliminary Approval Ordinance, then Developer shall be entitled to
receive final approval of the Development from the Village Council.
Notwithstanding the foregoing, the Village Council shall have and retain the
discretion to incorporate all of the conditions of this Preliminary Approval
Ordinance into the grant of final approval and to impose additional conditions
for final approval, provided the Village Council finds one or more of the
following circumstances exist:
            a.   the proposed final plan contains changes from the Approved
Preliminary Plan and such conditions are related to such changes;
            b.   such conditions are related to Village staff recommendations
arising from the review of the detailed architectural and engineering drawings;
            c.   such conditions are related to details of the building design
or materials that were not part of the Approved Preliminary Plan; or
            d.   such conditions are related to changes in, on or in the
immediate vicinity of the Subject Property since the adoption of this
Preliminary Approval Ordinance and are necessary to protect the public health,
safety and welfare.
      C.   Duration of Final Approval. Except as provided in the following
subsection D, the final approval of the Development shall expire 36 months from
the effective date of the final approval ordinance unless Developer has
submitted an application for building permit prior to that time.
      D.   Extension of Final Approval. Notwithstanding the foregoing, the
Village, acting through the Village Manager, may grant two consecutive one-year
extensions to the time for filing a complete building permit application,
provided the Village Manager determines the Developer has met the following
conditions:
         1.   the Developer has made good faith effort to meet the applicable
filing deadline;
         2.   the inability of the Developer to meet the applicable filing
deadline is due to circumstances beyond the Developer’s control, provided that
the financial condition of the Developer shall not be considered a condition
beyond Developer’s control; and
         3.   the Developer is in full compliance with all other applicable
development requirements, including the terms of this Preliminary Approval
Ordinance.
      E.   No Work Authorization. Nothing in this Preliminary Approval
Ordinance shall be construed as authorizing the issuance of any permits for the
demolition of the existing building or for the construction of the Development.
   SECTION 12:   Modifications and Revisions. Except as otherwise provided
hereinabove, the stipulations, conditions and restrictions set forth in the
foregoing Sections 4 through 8 of this Preliminary Approval Ordinance may be
modified or revised from time to time by the Village Council, at the
Developer’s request, pursuant to public notice and hearing, following the
procedures specified in Chapter 17.56 of the Winnetka Village Code for
processing special use applications.
   SECTION 13:   Acceptance by Developer. The preliminary approval of the
Development granted by this Preliminary Approval Ordinance shall be null and
void if, within 30 days after passage and approval of this Preliminary Approval
Ordinance, Developer does not affix its duly authorized and lawful signature to
this Preliminary Approval Ordinance as evidence of Developer’s unequivocal
acceptance of all terms, conditions and exceptions of this preliminary Approval
Ordinance.
   SECTION 14:   Home Rule Powers.  This Preliminary Approval Ordinance is
passed by the Council of the Village of Winnetka in the exercise of its home
rule powers pursuant to Section 6 of Article VII of the Illinois Constitution
of 1970.
   SECTION 15:   Effective Date. This Preliminary Approval Ordinance shall take
effect immediately upon its passage, approval and posting as provided by law.
   SECTION 16:   This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon its passage,
approval and posting as provided by law.
