ORDINANCE NO. M-12-2009
AN ORDINANCE DESIGNATING A LANDMARK PURSUANT TO CHAPTER 15.64 OF THE WINNETKA
VILLAGE CODE (660 Pine)
WHEREAS, the Village of Winnetka is a home rule municipality in accordance with
Article VII, Section 6 of the Constitution of the State of Illinois of 1970,
pursuant to which it has the authority, except as limited by said Section 6 of
Article VII, to exercise any power and perform any function pertaining to the
government and affairs of the Village; and
WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka (“Village Council”) find that
the identification, designation and preservation of buildings and structures in
the Village that are historically, culturally, and architecturally significant,
and the encouragement of the restoration and rehabilitation of those buildings
and structures are matters pertaining to the affairs of the Village; and
WHEREAS, the property commonly known as 660 Pine Street, Winnetka, Illinois
(the “Subject Property”), is legally described as follows:
The west ½ of the north ½ (except the east 65 feet thereof) of block 3 (except
such portions thereof located in streets) in Winnetka, in the northwest ¼ of
section 21, township 42 north, range 13, east of the third principal meridian,
in Cook County, Illinois; and
WHEREAS, the owner of the Subject Property has submitted an application seeking
designation of the Subject Property as a Village of Winnetka landmark; and
WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka has enacted Chapter 15.64 of
Title 15 of the Winnetka Village Code, titled “Landmark Preservation,” for the
purposes of preserving, protecting, enhancing, rehabilitating and regulating
buildings, structures, objects, and places of historical, cultural or
architectural importance; and
WHEREAS, pursuant to the provisions of Chapter 15.64 of the Winnetka Village
Code (the “Landmark Ordinance”) and notice duly published and sent, the
Landmark Preservation Commission conducted a public hearing on January 5, 2009,
to consider the application for landmark designation and unanimously
recommended that the residence on the Subject Property be designated a Winnetka
landmark; and
WHEREAS, the Landmark Preservation Commission applied the Village’s System for
the Evaluation of Landmarks to the information received at the hearing and gave
the home, known as the William Nicholls House, an overall score of 87.6, with a
rating of “unique;” and
WHEREAS, the Commission found the home to have an extremely unconventional and
unique design, that the architectural type, style and period of the William
Nicholls House is “extremely rare,” and that the architectural integrity of the
building is excellent, in that the house was constructed in 1905 by architect
William Carbys Zimmerman, but was extensively renovated in 1935 by noted
architect Andrew Rebori, changing its architectural style to one that is termed
“Depression Modern,” and that the building as it stands today remains true to
the intent of the 1935 renovations; and
WHEREAS, the Commission judged the home to be of high local significance,
because it was home to two former Village trustees, William Nicholls and
Everett “Tuck” Weaver, and also gave the home a high rating as a “symbol of the
Village as a whole;” and
WHEREAS, the Commission found the home to have extremely rare construction
methods, as brick and glass block were used expressively as both structural and
decorative elements; and
WHEREAS, the Commission gave the home the highest available rating for having
an association with a master builder in that, although the original architect
was William Carbys Zimmerman, the 1935 renovation by noted architect Andrew
Rebori was more significant, as examples of his work can be seen throughout
Chicago, notably his design of several buildings for the 1933 Century of
Progress World’s Fair and his position as co-designer of the Streets of Paris
exhibit; and
WHEREAS, pursuant to the findings entered at the January 5, 2009, meeting of
the Landmark Preservation Commission, the members then present unanimously
voted to recommend that that the William Nicholls House, located at 660 Pine
Street, be designated a Winnetka landmark, based on the Commission’s conclusion
that the overall rating of the residence is unique, and that it meets the
criteria of the Landmark Ordinance; and
WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka have considered the
recommendation of the Landmark Preservation Commission and have determined that
it is in the best interest of the Village and its residents to accept the
findings and recommendation of the Commission and to establish the William
Nicholls House, located at 660 Pine Street, as a designated landmark, because
of its unique, unconventional example of architecture, its excellent structural
condition and its association with a prominent architect.
NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
SECTION 1 :   The foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the findings of
the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth herein.
SECTION 2:   The residence located on the property at 660 Pine Street,
permanent real estate index number 05-21-102-019-0000, is hereby designated a
landmark under Section 15.64.070 the Landmark Preservation Ordinance, in
accordance with the findings and recommendations of the Landmark Preservation
Commission.
SECTION 3:   This Ordinance is passed by the Council of the Village of Winnetka
in the exercise of its home rule powers pursuant to Section 6 of Article VII of
the Illinois Constitution of 1970.
SECTION 4:   This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon its passage,
approval and posting as provided by law.
PASSED this 2nd day of June, 2009, pursuant to the following roll call vote: 
AYES:    Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian, Poor, Rintz, Spinney
NAYS:    None
ABSENT:    None
APPROVED this 2nd day of June, 2009.
   Signed:
   s/Jessica B. Tucker
   Village President
Countersigned:
s/Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
