ORDINANCE NO. M-1-2010
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA, COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (1004 Pine Tree)
WHEREAS, the Village of Winnetka is a home rule municipality in accordance with
Article VII, Section 6 of the Constitution of the State of Illinois of 1970,
pursuant to which it has the authority, except as limited by said Section 6 of
Article VII, to exercise any power and perform any function pertaining to the
government and affairs of the Village; and
WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka (“Village Council”) find that
establishing standards for the use and development of lands and buildings
within the Village and establishing and applying criteria for variations from
those standards are matters pertaining to the affairs of the Village; and
WHEREAS, the property commonly known as 1004 Pine Tree Lane, Winnetka, Illinois
(the “Subject Property”), is legally described as follows:
Lot 1 in Resubdivision of Lots 18 to 28, both inclusive, in Blietz Winnetka
Subdivision, being a Subdivision of that part of the East ½ of the Southeast ¼
of the Northwest ¼ of Section 18, Township 42 North, Range 13, East of the
Third Principal Meridian, lying North of a line lying midway between the center
line of Asbury Avenue and Edgewood Lane, extended West of Grove Street, and
that part of the West ½ of the Southeast ¼ of the Northwest ¼, lying North of
the center line of Edgewood Lane, extended West in Section 18, Township 42
North, Range 13, East of the Third Principal Meridian, in Cook County,
Illinois; and
WHEREAS, the Subject Property is located in the R-2 Zoning District provided in
Chapter 17.24 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka
Village Code; and
WHEREAS, on August 5, 2008, the Council of the Village of Winnetka (“Village
Council” passed Ordinance M-12-2008, granting the Subject Property a variation
from the Front Yard Setback requirements of Section 17.30.050, Chapter 17.30 of
the Zoning Ordinance in order to construct a new home on the Subject Property
that will have a north front yard setback of 25 feet, whereas the minimum
required is 50 feet, resulting in a variation of 25 feet (50%); and
WHEREAS, the construction authorized pursuant to Ordinance M-12-2008 did not
commence within 12 months, and the variation accordingly expired; and
WHEREAS, on November 10, 2009, the owners of the Subject Property filed a new
application for a variation from the Front Yard Setback requirements of Section
17.30.050, Chapter 17.30 of the Zoning Ordinance in order to construct a new
home on the Subject Property that will have a north front yard setback of 23.5
feet, whereas the minimum required is 50 feet, resulting in a variation of 26.5
feet (53%); and
WHEREAS, the design of the new home now proposed for the Subject Property has
been modified slightly, resulting in the new variation request’s being 1.5-foot
greater than the previously granted variation; and
WHEREAS, on December 14, 2009, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the requested variations and, by the
unanimous vote of the 6 members then present, has reported to the Council
recommending that the requested variations be granted; and
WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and particular hardships associated
with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect
to the Subject Property in that (a) the Subject Property is an irregularly-
shaped corner lot located on the west side of Pine Tree Lane at the northern
terminus of that street; (b) the northern terminus of Pine Tree Lane is a cul-
de-sac, which creates a curved lot line at the southeast corner of the Subject
Property; (c) the Subject Property’s northern lot line is 317.74 feet long and
abuts the Ivy Lane right-of-way in the Village of Glencoe; (d) as a result of
the cul-de-sac, the Subject Property’s southern lot line is 264.11 feet long;
(e) as a result of the cul-de-sac, the rear lot line, at the west end of the
Subject Property, is 94.74 feet long, while the east lot line is 66.92 feet
long; (f) because it is a corner lot, the Subject Property is subject to front
yard setbacks along both street frontages; (g) although the standard front yard
setback in the R-2 Zoning District is 50 feet, the Subject Property is subject
to an average setback requirement, resulting in a required minimum front yard
setback of 62.54 feet along the arc of the Pine Tree Lane right-of-way; (h) the
Subject Property is also subject to a 50-foot front yard setback for the full
length of the corner lot line along the Ivy Lane right-of-way; (i) the
application of all setback requirements results in a long, narrow buildable
area that is approximately 32 feet wide; (j) the applicant proposes a 23.5-foot
setback along the Ivy Lane corner lot line, which is greater than the 11.92-
foot north setback of the existing home; and (k) on June 19, 2001, the Village
Council passed ordinance M-15-2001, which granted a variance to allow a 25-foot
setback from Ivy Lane for the construction of a new home on the property
immediately to the east, which is located on the east side of the north
terminus of Pine Tree Lane and is subject to the same front and corner setbacks
as the Subject Property; and
WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted to
be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that: (a)
the combined effect of the large setbacks and the irregular shape of the
Subject Property result in an unusually long and narrow buildable area that
severely limits the size and location of what can be built on the Subject
Property; and (b) constructing a home that fully conforms with all setbacks
will result in an oddly shaped, long, narrow home that is inconsistent with
housing standards in the Village and with the house built in the corresponding
location on the property to the east; and
WHEREAS, the requested variation will neither alter the essential character of
the neighborhood nor impair an adequate supply of light and air because the
proposed new construction: (a) will be of a character and scale similar to the
other homes in the area; (b) the proposed 23.5-foot north setback will be
greater than the setback observed by the existing home; (c) the variation being
requested is located along the Ivy Lane frontage, which is not adjacent to any
other single-family properties; (d) the proposed new home will comply with the
larger, average setback requirement on the Pine Tree Lane frontage, and will
meet or exceed all other required setbacks; and (e) the setback along the Ivy
Lane frontage will be the same as the setback permitted on the adjoining
property to the east; and
WHEREAS, the requested variation will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property, as the proposed construction will comply
with all applicable building and fire protection codes; and
WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the requested variation will diminish the
taxable value of land and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable
value of the Subject Property may be increased because of the proposed
improvements; and
WHEREAS, the proposed construction will not contribute to congestion on the
public streets, as the property will continue to be used for single family
residential purposes; and
WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the requested variation will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village; and
WHEREAS, the requested variation is in harmony with the general purpose and
intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that it maintains the existing
scale and appearance of the community.
NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
SECTION 1:   The foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the findings of
the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth herein.
SECTION 2:   The following variation from the Lot, Space, Bulk and Yard
Regulations for Single Family Residential Districts established by Section
17.30 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance is hereby granted to the Subject
Property, commonly known as 1004 Pine Tree Lane and located in the R-2 Single-
Family Residential District provided in Chapter 17.24 of the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code: a variation from the Front
Yard Setback requirements of Section 17.30.050, Chapter 17.30 of the Zoning
Ordinance in order to construct a new home on the Subject Property that will
have a north front yard setback of 23.5 feet, whereas the minimum required is
50 feet, resulting in a variation of 26.5 feet (53%), in accordance with the
plans and elevations submitted with the application for variations.
SECTION 3:   The variation granted herein is conditioned upon the commencement
of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective date of this
Ordinance. 
SECTION 4:   This Ordinance is passed by the Council of the Village of Winnetka
in the exercise of its home rule powers pursuant to Section 6 of Article VII of
the Illinois Constitution of 1970.
SECTION 5:   This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon its passage,
approval and posting as provided by law.
PASSED this 4th day of February, 2010, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
AYES:    Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian, Rintz, Spinney
NAYS:    None
ABSENT:    Trustee Poor
APPROVED this 4th day of February, 2010.
   Signed:
   s/Jessica B. Tucker
   Village President
Countersigned:
s/Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: January 19, 2010
Posted: January 20, 2010
Passed and Approved: February 4, 2010
