ORDINANCE NO. M-8-2010
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA, COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (310 Woodland)
WHEREAS, the Village of Winnetka is a home rule municipality in accordance with
Article VII, Section 6 of the Constitution of the State of Illinois of 1970,
pursuant to which it has the authority, except as limited by said Section 6 of
Article VII, to exercise any power and perform any function pertaining to the
government and affairs of the Village; and
WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka (“Village Council”) find that
establishing standards for the use and development of lands and buildings
within the Village and establishing and applying criteria for variations from
those standards are matters pertaining to the affairs of the Village; and
WHEREAS, the property commonly known as 310 Woodland Avenue, Winnetka, Illinois
(the “Subject Property”), is legally described as follows:
The North Half of Lots 1, 2 and 3 in Block 8 in Lake Shore Subdivision, in the
Village of Winnetka, being in the Southeast Quarter of Section 21, Township 42
North, Range 13, East of the Third Principal Meridian, in Cook County,
Illinois; and
WHEREAS, the Subject Property is located in the R-4 Single Family Zoning
District provided in Chapter 17.16 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17
of the Winnetka Village Code; and
WHEREAS, on March 10, 2010, the owner of the Subject Property filed an
application for a variation from the garage setback requirements set out in
Section 17.30.110(E) of Chapter 17.30 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, to
permit a proposed new, detached garage to be located 0.67 feet from the south
lot line, whereas the a minimum setback of 12.22 feet is required, resulting in
a variation of 11.55 feet(94.52%); and
WHEREAS, on April 12, 2010, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of Appeals
conducted a public hearing on the requested variations and, by the unanimous
vote of the six members then present, has reported to the Council recommending
that the requested variation be granted; and
WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and particular hardships associated
with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect
to the Subject Property in that: (a) the Subject Property is improved with a
single family residence that was constructed in 1913, before the enactment of
the Zoning Ordinance; (b) the existing garage has been in the same location for
approximately 60 years and its condition has deteriorated to the point that it
can no longer be repaired; (c) the existing garage is substandard, in that it
is less than 20 feet long, which makes it impossible to close the garage door
with a typical, modern car in it, unless the car is touching the rear wall of
the garage; (d) the proposed new garage would be constructed in the same
location as the existing garage, but would be lengthened by approximately 5
feet to allow cars to be parked in it without coming in contact with the garage
door and the rear wall of the garage; (e) the garage cannot be constructed in
the rear quarter of the Subject Property because that area is obstructed by an
in-ground swimming pool that was abandoned in place and filled in with dirt;
(f) constructing the garage in the rear quarter of the yard is not reasonably
possible, because of the additional costs that would be incurred in excavating
the rear yard to remove the swimming pool structure, and in subsequently
filling in the excavated area; (g) constructing the garage in the rear quarter
of the Subject Property would also require the removal of mature trees located
behind the garage; (h) because the proposed garage cannot reasonably be
constructed in the rear quarter of the Subject Property, the garage is subject
to the same setback requirements as the principal building, including the
requirement that the sum of the setbacks on the Subject Property equal at least
11.22 feet; and (i) constructing the garage with a conforming south side yard
would require the removal of a large birch tree and other landscaping on the
north side of the garage, would interfere with the existing cedar deck at the
rear of the house, and would eliminate the open area that now exists between
the house and garage; and
WHEREAS, the Subject Property cannot yield a reasonable return if permitted to
be used only under the conditions allowed by the Zoning Ordinance, in that: (a)
requiring the new garage to be constructed in a conforming location in the rear
quarter of the Subject Property would impose a substantial financial burden on
the owners; (b) requiring the new garage to be shifted to the north to provide
a conforming south side yard would have a negative impact on the value of the
Subject Property because it would entail removing a substantial birch tree in
the rear yard, would interfere with the deck and would close off open space in
the rear yard; (c) the public benefit that would result from constructing the
garage in a conforming location on the Subject Property does not offset the
financial burdens that would be imposed on the Subject Property and its owners;
and
WHEREAS, the requested variation will not alter the essential character of the
neighborhood because the proposed new garage will be in the same location as
the one it will replace and the design and materials for the proposed garage
are consistent with the existing home; and
WHEREAS, the requested variation will not impair an adequate supply of light
and air because the proposed new garage will be of a similar size and location
as the existing garage ; and
WHEREAS, the requested variation will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property, as the proposed construction will comply
with all applicable building and fire protection codes; and
WHEREAS, the requested variation will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings throughout the Village, as the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements, while
constructing a new garage in a fully conforming location would have a negative
impact on the Subject Property, which would be likely to reduce its taxable
value; and
WHEREAS, the proposed construction will not contribute to congestion on the
public streets, as the new garage will provide two off-street parking spaces
and the property will continue to be used for single family residential
purposes; and
WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the requested variation will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village; and
WHEREAS, the requested variation is in harmony with the general purpose and
intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that it will allow the replacement
of a dilapidated accessory building while maintaining the existing scale and
appearance of the community and protecting established trees and landscaping.
NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
SECTION 1:   The foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the findings of
the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth herein.
SECTION 2:   The Subject Property, commonly known as 310 Woodland Avenue, and
located in the R-4 Single-Family Residential District provided in Chapter 17.16
of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code is
hereby granted a variation from the garage setback requirements set out in
Section 17.30.110(E) of Chapter 17.30 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, to
permit a proposed new, detached garage to be located 0.67 feet from the south
lot line, whereas the a minimum setback of 12.22 feet is required, resulting in
a variation of 11.55 feet(94.52%), in accordance with the plans and elevations
submitted with the application for variation.
SECTION 3:   This Ordinance is passed by the Council of the Village of Winnetka
in the exercise of its home rule powers pursuant to Section 6 of Article VII of
the Illinois Constitution of 1970.
SECTION 4:   This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon its passage,
approval and posting as provided by law.
PASSED this 1st day of June, 2010, pursuant to the following roll call vote: 
AYES:    Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian, Poor, Rintz, Spinney
NAYS:    None
ABSENT:    None
APPROVED this 1st day of June, 2010.
   Signed:
   s/Jessica B. Tucker
   Village President
Countersigned:
s/Elizabeth A. Rosenthal
Village Clerk
Introduced: May 18, 2010
Posted: May 19, 2010
Passed and Approved: June 1, 2010
