ORDINANCE NO. M-9-2010
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA, COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (1108 Merrill)
WHEREAS, the Village of Winnetka is a home rule municipality in accordance with
Article VII, Section 6 of the Constitution of the State of Illinois of 1970,
pursuant to which it has the authority, except as limited by said Section 6 of
Article VII, to exercise any power and perform any function pertaining to the
government and affairs of the Village; and
WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka (“Village Council”) find that
establishing standards for the use and development of lands and buildings
within the Village and establishing and applying criteria for variations from
those standards are matters pertaining to the affairs of the Village; and
WHEREAS, the property commonly known as 1108 Merrill Winnetka, Illinois (the
“Subject Property”), is legally described as follows:
Lot 5 in Block 3 in Jared Gage’s Subdivision of part of the East ½ of the
Northwest ¼ of Fractional Section 17 also part of the West ½ of the Northwest ¼
of said Fractional Section 17 also part of the East ½ of the Southwest ¼ of
Section 8, Township 42 North, Range 13, East of the Third Principal Meridian,
in Cook County, Illinois; and
WHEREAS, the Subject Property is located in the R-5 Zoning District provided in
Chapter 17.12 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka
Village Code; and
WHEREAS, on March 5, 2010, the owner of the Subject Property filed an
application for the following variations from requirements of the Lot, Space,
Bulk and Yard Regulations for Single Family Residential Districts established
by Chapter 17.30 of the Zoning Ordinance: (a) a variation from the garage
setback requirements of Section 17.30.110(E) to allow a rear yard setback of
1.32 feet, whereas a minimum of 2 feet is required, resulting in a variation of
0.68 feet (34%); and (b) a variation from the height limitations of Section
17.64.060 to permit a garage to have a height of 20.67 feet and two stories,
whereas a maximum of 15 feet and one story is permitted, resulting in a
variation of 5.67 feet (37.8%), all to allow the existing four-car garage to be
enlarged to create a second story home office space; and;
WHEREAS, on April 12, 2010, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of Appeals
conducted a public hearing on the requested variations and, by the favorable
vote of four of the six members then present, has reported to the Council
recommending that the requested variations be granted; and
WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and particular hardships associated
with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect
to the Subject Property in that: (a) the Subject Property is located on the
north side of Merrill Street and borders the C-2 General Retail Commercial
Zoning District in Hubbard Woods; (b) the Subject Property is bounded on the
east by a public alley, by a school property to the south, and single family
residences to the west; (c) the Subject Property has a legal nonconforming lot
width of 49.99 feet, which is 10 feet less than the requirement in the R-
5 Zoning District; (d) the Subject Property is improved with a single family
residence, which is believed to have been constructed before 1900, well before
the initial enactment of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, and a four-car garage
located at the rear of the lot; (e) the existing garage is located in a legally
nonconforming location, 1.32 feet from the rear lot line, and also has a
legally nonconforming height of 20.67 feet and two stories; (f) the proposed
second floor addition to the garage would add dormers to the north and south
sides of the garage, thereby vertically extending the height of the
nonconforming rear wall of the garage and expanding the degree of the height
nonconformity; (g) the rear yard of the Subject Property abuts a parking and
service area of the school located on the property to the north; (h) the garage
additions are being proposed to allow the second floor of the garage to be used
as an office, rather than adding an office addition to the existing residence;
(i) the proposed garage additions have a minimal impact on the size of the
building and its appearance as seen from the neighboring properties, while
building an addition to the existing house to provide a home office would add
significant visual bulk to the Subject Property, would increase impermeable
surfaces, would reduce the open space in the rear yard of Subject Property and
would create a visual obstruction for the property immediately to the west; and
WHEREAS, the requested variations will not alter the essential character of the
neighborhood in that: (a) the proposed addition to the garage is of minimal
size and maintains the existing configuration of buildings on the Subject
Property; and (b) the expansion to the existing nonconformities is minimal not
only in relation to the Subject Property but also in relation to the adjacent
properties and to the uses in the immediate vicinity; and
WHEREAS, the requested variations will not impair an adequate supply of light
and air because the proposed garage expansion is of a minimal size, with the
bulk of the addition facing into the Subject property itself and the height
additions being set away from any other residential properties; and
WHEREAS, the requested variations will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property, as the proposed construction will comply
with all applicable building and fire protection codes; and
WHEREAS, the requested variations will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
WHEREAS, the proposed construction will not contribute to congestion on the
public streets, as the property will continue to be used for single family
residential purposes; and
WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the requested variations will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village; and
WHEREAS, the requested variations are in harmony with the general purpose and
intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that they allow the renovation,
restoration and rehabilitation of a structurally sound existing building while
maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the community and protecting
established trees and landscaping.
NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
SECTION 1:   The foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the findings of
the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth herein.
SECTION 2:   The Subject Property, commonly known as 1108 Merrill, and located
in the R-5 Single-Family Residential District provided in Chapter 17.12 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code is hereby
granted the following variations from requirements of the Lot, Space, Bulk and
Yard Regulations for Single Family Residential Districts established by Chapter
17.30 of the Zoning Ordinance: (a) a variation from the garage setback
requirements of Section 17.30.110(E) to allow a rear yard setback of 1.32 feet,
whereas a minimum of 2 feet is required, resulting in a variation of 0.68 feet
(34%); and (b) a variation from the height limitations of Section 17.64.060 to
permit a garage to have a height of 20.67 feet and two stories, whereas a
maximum of 15 feet and one story is permitted, resulting in a variation of 5.67
feet (37.8%), all to allow the existing four-car garage to be enlarged to
create a second story home office space, in accordance with the plans and
elevations submitted with the application for variations.
SECTION 3:   This Ordinance is passed by the Council of the Village of Winnetka
in the exercise of its home rule powers pursuant to Section 6 of Article VII of
the Illinois Constitution of 1970.
SECTION 4:   This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon its passage,
approval and posting as provided by law.
PASSED this 1st day of June, 2010, pursuant to the following roll call vote: 
AYES:    Trustees Greable, Johnson, Pedian, Poor, Rintz, Spinney
NAYS:    None
ABSENT:    None
APPROVED this 1st day of June, 2010.
   Signed:
   s/Jessica B. Tucker
   Village President
Countersigned:
         
Elizabeth A. Rosenthal
Village Clerk
Introduced: May 18, 2010
Posted: May 19, 2010
Passed and Approved: June 1, 2010
