ORDINANCE NO. M-11-2010
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA, COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (606 Green Bay)
WHEREAS, the Village of Winnetka is a home rule municipality in accordance with
Article VII, Section 6 of the Constitution of the State of Illinois of 1970,
pursuant to which it has the authority, except as limited by said Section 6 of
Article VII, to exercise any power and perform any function pertaining to the
government and affairs of the Village; and
WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka (“Village Council”) find that
establishing standards for the use and development of lands and buildings
within the Village and establishing and applying criteria for variations from
those standards are matters pertaining to the affairs of the Village; and
WHEREAS, the property commonly known as 606 Green Bay Road, Winnetka, Illinois
(the “Subject Property”), is legally described as follows:
Parcel 1:   All of Lots 1, 2, 3, 4, 11, 12 and 13 in Jopson and Osgood
Subdivision of Block 6 (except the East 101st part thereof) in Charles E.
Peck’s Subdivision of the Northeast Quarter of Section 20 and the North half of
Fractional Section 21, Township 42 North, Range 13, East of the Third Principal
Meridian, also
Parcel 2:   The East 101st part (excepting therefrom that portion taken for
streets described in deed from Max K. Meyer to Village of Winnetka, dated
November 25, 1932, recorded December 16, 1932, as Document No. 11176456, as the
portion of said East 101st part lying Easterly and Southerly of a curved line,
convex to the Southeast and having a radius of 20.0 feet, beginning at a point
on the Southwesterly line of Center Street (formerly Railroad Avenue), 36.68
feet Northwesterly of the intersection of the North line of Spruce Street with
the Southwesterly line of said Center Street, and running thence Southerly and
Westerly on said curved line to a point on the North line of Spruce Street
36.68 feet West of said intersection of the North line of said Spruce Street
with the Southwesterly line of said Center Street) of Block 6 in Winnetka,
being Charles E. Peck’s Subdivision of the Northeast Quarter of Section 20, and
the North Half of Fractional Section 21, Township 42 North, Range 13, East of
the Third Principal Meridian, in Cook County, Illinois; and
WHEREAS, the Subject Property is located in the C-2 General Retail Commercial
Zoning District provided in Chapter 17.44 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance,
Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code; and
WHEREAS, on April 7, 2010, the owner of the Subject Property filed an
application for the following variations from requirements of the Lot, Space,
Bulk and Yard Regulations for Single Family Residential Districts established
by Chapter 17.30 of the Zoning Ordinance: (a) a variation from the intensity of
use of lot limitations of Section 17.44.010(E) to allow an impermeable lot
coverage of 80,657 square feet, whereas the maximum allowed is 74,366.1 square
feet, resulting in a variation of 6,290.9 (8.46%); and (b) a variation from the
front yard setback requirements of Section 17.44.010(N) to allow a south front
yard setback of 8.26 feet and an east front yard setback of 12.72 feet whereas
a maximum of 3 feet is permitted, resulting in variations of 5.26 feet
(175.33%), and 9.72 feet (324%), respectively, all being requested in order to
expand the existing one-story Grand Food Center with a one-story addition to
the east of the building at the southwest corner of Pine Street and Green Bay
Road; and
WHEREAS, on April 15, 2010, the Design Review Board reviewed the proposed plans
for expansion and, although generally making favorable comments about the
proposed design, suggested that the applicant consider reducing the building
height along the east elevation, lowering the roof height of the proposed
addition, lowering the center parapet wall, lowering the window sill heights
and reducing the scale of the south entry; and
WHEREAS, in response to the Design Review Board’s comments, the applicant
submitted revised plans on May 10, 2010; and
WHEREAS, on May 10, 2010, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of Appeals
conducted a public hearing on the revised plans and, by the unanimous vote of
the seven members then present, has reported to the Council recommending that
the requested variations be granted; and
WHEREAS, the Design Review Board considered the revised plans at its regular
meeting on May 20, 2010, after which the applicant submitted further revisions,
dated May 26, 2010; and
WHEREAS, the applicant presented the May 26 plans at a special meeting of the
Design review Board on May 27, 2010, at which time the Design Review Board
voted to recommend approval of the proposed addition, subject to the following
conditions: (i) that the columns be lowered so that they do not engage the
cornice, (ii) that there be a one-inch projection on the stone sill, and (iii)
that the applicant return to the Design Review Board after further studying the
window mullion proportions on the east elevation; and
WHEREAS, the Design Review Board also expressed a strong preference for: (i)
including four-inch caliper trees within the Green Bay Road parkway, (ii)
providing a planting strip on the west side of the Green Bay Road sidewalk
along the building, (iii) doing further study as to the optimal size for the
sidewalk at the northeast corner of the site; and (iv) placing the Grand Foods
garbage enclosure in the Village parking lot parcel that adjoins the west
property line of the Subject Property; and
WHEREAS, the Village Engineer has reviewed the Design Review Board’s comments
and comments raised at both the Design Review Board meetings and the Zoning
Board of Appeals hearing and has reported that, subject to minor modifications,
the proposed addition will not have adverse consequences on traffic or
pedestrian safety; and
WHEREAS, the proposed one-story addition to the Grand Food Center would expand
the store: (a) to provide expanded space for produce and flowers, (b) to
provide a more functional entry, and (c) to improve customer flow within the
store; and
WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties and unique circumstances associated
with carrying out the strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect
to the Subject Property in that: (a) the Subject Property is an irregularly
shaped parcel, with a nonconforming intensity of use of lot coverage; (b) the
Grand Food Center is located in a rectangular building in a trapezoidal area at
the north end of the Subject Property; (c) the Grand Food Center building has
been a grocery store since its construction in 1962, and the general layout of
the Subject Property, including the location of the buildings and parking
areas, has remained the same ever since; (d) the only location available for an
expansion of the existing store building is to the east, into a triangular
grassy area between the building and Green Bay Road; and (e) expanding the
store to the south would require the elimination of parking spaces and would
cut off the internal circulation route for delivery trucks; and
WHEREAS, there are unique circumstances associated with carrying out the strict
application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect to the Subject Property, in
that: (a) unlike the typical setback variation, the requested setback variation
is to exceed the maximum setback; (b) the maximum setback in the commercial
zoning districts assures a relatively continuous architectural “street wall” at
the sidewalk and discourages the placement of parking areas between buildings
and the adjacent to the sidewalk; (c) the Subject Property is located at the
northernmost end of the West Elm Street business district, and the Grand Food
Center is the only building between Spruce and Pine Streets that is set near
the Pine Street and Green Bay Road sidewalks; (d) all of the other buildings on
the Subject Property are located along the west property line, with the entire
east portion of the Subject Property being a paved parking area; (e) the
location and dimensions of the proposed addition provide a “street wall” at the
northernmost portion of the Subject Property, which will not affect the layout
or orientation of the remainder of the Subject Property; (f) the Grand Food
Center anchors the north end of the West Elm Street business district and
generates substantial sales tax revenues to the Village; and (g) the proposed
addition is necessary to enable the Grand Food Center to remain competitive
with other food stores in the area; and
WHEREAS, the requested variations will not alter the essential character of the
neighborhood because the proposed addition is a modest expansion of an
established existing use, with the proposed addition being visible primarily
from Green Bay Road and the Subject Property’s parking lot rather than from the
residential properties on Pine Street; and
WHEREAS, the requested variations will not impair an adequate supply of light
and air because the area is bounded by three streets and the proposed addition
is shorter and set back further than what could be constructed on the Subject
Property without a variation; and
WHEREAS, the requested variations will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property, as the proposed construction will comply
with all applicable building and fire protection codes; and
WHEREAS, the requested variations will not diminish the taxable value of land
and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable value of the Subject
Property may be increased because of the proposed improvements; and
WHEREAS, the proposed construction will not contribute to congestion on the
public streets, as the proposed addition will maintain the parking areas that
currently exist on the Subject Property and the relocation of the trash
enclosure will allow more delivery vehicles to enter and exit the Subject
Property through the parking lot rather than Pine Street; and
WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the requested variations will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village; and
WHEREAS, the requested variations are in harmony with the general purpose and
intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that: (a) they foster the goals and
objectives of the Comprehensive Plan related to maintaining the commercial
business districts; (b) they assure the development and redevelopment of the
established business area in a manner consistent with its established character
by allowing the renovation and expansion of a structurally sound existing
building while maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the Subject
property and the immediate vicinity; and (c) they protect the justifiable
reliance of taxpayers, business and the residents in the nearby properties on
the continuation of existing, established land use patterns.
NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
SECTION 1:  The foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the findings of
the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth herein.
SECTION 2: The Subject Property, commonly known as 606 Green Bay Road and
located in the C-2 General Retail Commercial Zoning District provided in
Chapter 17.44 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka
Village Code is hereby granted the following variations from requirements of
the Lot, Space, Bulk and Yard Regulations for Single Family Residential
Districts established by Chapter 17.30 of the Zoning Ordinance: (a) a variation
from the intensity of use of lot limitations of Section 17.44.010(E) to allow
an impermeable lot coverage of 80,657 square feet, whereas the maximum allowed
is 74,366.1 square feet, resulting in a variation of 6,290.9 (8.46%); and (b) a
variation from the front yard setback requirements of Section 17.44.010(N) to
allow a south front yard setback of 8.26 feet and an east front yard setback of
12.72 feet whereas a maximum of 3 feet is permitted, resulting in variations of
5.26 feet (175.33%), and 9.72 feet (324%), respectively, all being requested in
order to expand the existing one-story Grand Food Center with a one-story
addition to the east of the building at the southwest corner of Pine Street and
Green Bay Road, all in accordance with the revised plans and elevations dated
May 26, 2010, as further modified by the revised site plan dated June 10, 2010
(“Revised Site Plan”).
SECTION 3:   The variations granted herein are subject to the following
conditions:
   A.   The proposed construction shall commence within 12 months after the
effective date of this Ordinance. 
   B.   The trash enclosure located at the south end of the service drive shall
be moved to the west, onto the adjoining parking lot, in the area depicted on
the Revised Site Plan; provided, that the trash enclosure (i) shall be fully
enclosed on all four sides, as provided in subsection D, below; (ii) shall be
located so that it opens to the east for deposit and removal of trash; and
(iii) shall be located so as to minimize, to the extent reasonably possible,
the reduction of parking spaces in the public parking lot on which it will be
situated.
   C.   The fence on the west side of the Subject Property shall be replaced
with an 8-foot high stockade fence that shall extend along the length of the
adjacent public parking lot and shall fully surround the relocated trash
enclosure. The access openings for the trash enclosure shall be on its east
side and shall be made of the same 8-foot high stockade material as the rest of
the fence.
   D.   Grand Foods and the owners of the Subject Property shall enter into a
license agreement with the Village, which agreement shall define the
compensation, duration and other terms pursuant to which the Village will allow
the trash enclosure to be relocated to the Village’s parking lot. The license
agreement shall also incorporate the terms of the foregoing subsections B and
C.
   E.   Grand Foods shall make such modifications to the sidewalk configuration
at the southwest corner of Pine Street and Green Bay Road as the Village
Engineer shall determine to be necessary to comply with applicable traffic,
engineering and safety standards for adequate sight lines and pedestrian safety
at that intersection.
   F.   Grand Foods shall provide such signage and striping as the Director of
Community Development and the Village Engineer shall determine to be necessary
to comply with applicable traffic, engineering and safety standards for traffic
circulation through the parking lot and loading dock.
   G.   Grand Foods shall work with its vendors to schedule deliveries during
early morning hours before regular business hours, so that delivery vehicles
can be channeled through the parking lot and exit the Subject Property at Pine
Street, so as to minimize disruption to the adjacent residential neighborhood
and to avoid conflicts with peak traffic periods. Grand Foods shall post such
signage as the Director of Community Development may determine to be necessary
to notify delivery and service vehicles of delivery hours and routes.
   H.   The north building elevation shall be modified by using building
materials and/or other design elements to provide visual detail to the existing
wall. The revised building elevation shall be subject to Village Council
approval after review and comment by the Design Review Board.
SECTION 4: This Ordinance is passed by the Council of the Village of Winnetka
in the exercise of its home rule powers pursuant to Section 6 of Article VII of
the Illinois Constitution of 1970.
SECTION 5: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon its passage,
approval and posting as provided by law.
PASSED this 6th day of July, 2010, pursuant to the following roll call vote: 
AYES:    Trustees Poor, Pedian, Johnson, Spinney and Greable
NAYS:    None
ABSENT: Trustee Rintz
APPROVED this 6th day of July, 2010
