05-18-402-076 1345 Trapp Lane
05-18-402-046 1350 Trapp Lane
05-18-402-075 1360 Trapp Lane
05-18-402-074 1368 Trapp Lane
05-18-402-043 1370 Trapp Lane
05-18-402-042 1372 Trapp Lane
05-18-402-077 1385 Trapp Lane
 
   Winnetka Special Service Area No. 3 is to be established to provide certain
public services (the “Services”) to the Area that will supplement the services
currently or customarily provided by the Village to the Area, in particular the
installation of a new storm sewer and all appurtenances thereto, the
reconstruction and repaving of Trapp Lane including the construction of a new
cul-de-sac, and the furnishing of all necessary labor and materials in
connection therewith, and any other similar types of amenities and improvements
that shall be compatible with the proposed improved plan for the Area. The
proposed Winnetka Special Service Area No. 3 is intended to improve stormwater
drainage to the Area and to upgrade Trapp Lane to Village standards so that
Trapp Lane, which is currently a private roadway, can be dedicated to the
Village as a public right-of-way. The Services proposed to be provided in
Winnetka Special Service Area No. 3 are unique and in addition to the general
municipal services provided to the Village as a whole and will be for the
common interests and specific benefit of the Area.
   At the hearing, consideration shall also be given to borrowing an amount not
to exceed $631,895.00 (125% of the engineer’s estimate of project costs), to be
evidenced by general obligation bonds (the “Bonds”). The proceeds of the Bonds
shall be used to pay part of the costs of the proposed Winnetka Special Service
Area No. 3. The Bonds are to be retired over a period not to exceed 10 years
and are to bear interest at a rate not to exceed 5.00% per annum or the maximum
rate permitted by law. The Bonds, if issued, shall be retired by the levy of a
direct tax on all taxable real property within the Area for a maximum period of
10 years, to discharge the principal as it matures and the interest thereon,
and said tax shall be in addition to all other taxes presently levied by any
taxing district within the Area.
   All interested persons affected by the formation of the Winnetka Special
Service Area No. 3, including, but not limited to, all persons owning taxable
real property located within the Area, will be given an opportunity to be heard
regarding the formation and the boundaries of the proposed Winnetka Special
Service Area No. 3, and will be given an opportunity to file objections to the
formation of Winnetka Special Service Area No. 3, the issuance of bonds, and
the related levy of taxes affecting the Winnetka Special Service Area No. 3.
   The public hearing may be adjourned by the Village to another date without
further notice other than a motion to be entered upon the minutes of its
meeting fixing the time and place of its adjournment.
   If a petition signed by at least 51 % of the electors residing within the
Area and by at least 51% of the owners of record of the land included within
the Area is filed with the Village Clerk within 60 days following the final
adjournment of the public hearing objecting to the establishment of the
proposed Winnetka Special Service Area No. 3, the enlargement thereof, the levy
or imposition of a tax or the issuance of the Bonds for the provision of the
Services to the Area, or to a proposed increase in the tax rate, then the
proposed Winnetka Special Service Area No. 3 may not be created or enlarged,
nor the tax levied or imposed, nor the rate increased, and the Bonds may not be
issued.
Dated this 22nd day of April, 2010.
s/Douglas G. Williams
Douglas G. Williams, Village Clerk
Village of Winnetka, Cook County, Illinois
ORDINANCE NO. M-14-2010
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A VARIATION IN THE APPLICATION OF THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF
THE VILLAGE OF WINNETKA, COOK COUNTY, ILLINOIS (389 Ridge)
WHEREAS, the Village of Winnetka is a home rule municipality in accordance with
Article VII, Section 6 of the Constitution of the State of Illinois of 1970,
pursuant to which it has the authority, except as limited by said Section 6 of
Article VII, to exercise any power and perform any function pertaining to the
government and affairs of the Village; and
WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka (“Village Council”) find that
establishing standards for the use and development of lands and buildings
within the Village and establishing and applying criteria for variations from
those standards are matters pertaining to the affairs of the Village; and
WHEREAS, the property commonly known as 389 Ridge Avenue, Winnetka, Illinois
(the “Subject Property”), is legally described as follows:
Lot 9 in George H. Mayr’s Subdivision of the North 264.4 feet in Block 63, West
of Railroad, in Winnetka, being Peck’s Subdivision of the Northeast ¼ of
Section 20 and the North Fractional Half of Section 21, Township 42 North,
Range 13, East of the Third Principal Meridian, in Cook County, Illinois; and
WHEREAS, the Subject Property is located in the R-5 Zoning District provided in
Chapter 17.12 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka
Village Code; and
WHEREAS, on June 9, 2010, the owner of the Subject Property filed an
application for a variation of 3.00 feet (50.00%) from the garage setback
requirements of Section 17.30.110 of Chapter 17.30 of the Zoning Ordinance, to
permit the construction of a new two-car garage with a rear yard setback of
3.00 feet, rather than with the required minimum rear yard setback of 6.00
feet; and;
WHEREAS, on July 12, 2010, on due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of Appeals
conducted a public hearing on the requested variations and, by the unanimous
vote of the five members then present, has reported to the Council recommending
that the requested variation be granted; and
WHEREAS, the existing garage sits 2.21 feet from the rear lot line of the
Subject Property and the proposed new garage will increase the rear yard
setback to 3.00 feet; and
WHEREAS, there are practical difficulties associated with carrying out the
strict application of the Zoning Ordinance with respect to the Subject Property
in that: (a) the Subject Property is a legally nonconforming under-sized lot,
with a width of 50 feet and a lot depth of 150 feet, with a correspondingly
small buildable area; (b) the configuration of the Subject Property was
established no later than 1927, when the existing garage was constructed; (c)
the front yard on the Subject Property is deeper than on the adjacent lots and
exceeds the required minimum by more than 10 feet, which pushes the house
toward the rear of the Subject Property; (d) the rear lot line of the Subject
Property abuts a portion of the side lot line of the property to the east,
commonly known as 712 Ash Street, which makes the Subject Property subject to
the requirement that the rear yard setback for garages must equal the side yard
setback on the adjacent property; (e) the rear lot lines of the two lots
immediately to the north of the Subject Property also abut the side lot line of
712 Ash, and lie approximately opposite the house at 712 Ash, which is located
in the northern half of that property; (f) the rear of the house extends
further toward the rear lot line than do the houses on the two lots to the
north of the Subject Property; (g) the rear lot line of the Subject Property
begins at the mid-point of the side lot line of 712 Ash and extends 50 feet to
the south, so that the garage on the Subject Property lies opposite open space
on the neighboring 712 Ash property; (h) the distance between the garage and
the deck at the rear of the house on the Subject Property is approximately 13.0
feet, so that requiring a conforming rear yard setback for the proposed new
garage would reduce the space between the deck and the garage to approximately
10 feet and would shift the widest portion of the driveway closer to the house,
thereby reducing the usable open space on the Subject Property; (i) because of
the fence that lies along the rear lot line of the Subject Property, the
proposed rear yard setback will not have any measurable impact on the property
at 712 Ash; and (j) strict compliance with the rear yard setback requirements
in this instance would reduce the usable open space on the Subject Property
without providing any offsetting benefit to the adjacent properties or to the
public health, safety an welfare; and
WHEREAS, the requested variation will not alter the essential character of the
neighborhood in that: (a) the proposed garage will be located in the same area
of the Subject Property as the existing garage; (b) the proposed garage will be
of a size and design similar to the existing garage; and (c) the proposed
garage will improve the appearance of the rear of the Subject Property from
both the street and the adjacent properties; and
WHEREAS, the requested variation will not impair an adequate supply of light
and air because: (a) the side yard setback and height of the new garage will
conform with applicable zoning regulations; and (b) the new garage will have a
slightly larger rear yard setback than the existing garage; and
WHEREAS, the requested variation will not increase the hazard from fire and
other dangers to the Subject Property, as the proposed construction, including
the distances between the garage and other buildings, will comply with all
applicable building and fire protection codes; and
WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the requested variation will diminish the
taxable value of land and buildings throughout the Village, and the taxable
value of the Subject Property may be increased because of the proposed
improvement; and
WHEREAS, the proposed garage will not contribute to congestion on the public
streets, as it will increase the amount of enclosed, off-street parking on the
Subject Property; and
WHEREAS, there is no evidence that the requested variation will otherwise
impair the public health, safety, comfort, morals, and welfare of the
inhabitants of the Village; and
WHEREAS, the requested variation is in harmony with the general purpose and
intent of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, in that it will allow the replacement
of a dilapidated accessory building with a new, structurally sound building
while maintaining the existing scale and appearance of the community and
protecting established trees and landscaping.
NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
SECTION 1:   The foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the findings of
the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth herein.
SECTION 2:   The Subject Property, commonly known as 389 Ridge Road, is hereby
granted a variation of 3.00 feet (50.0%) from the garage setback requirements
of Section 17.30.110 of Chapter 17.30 of the Zoning Ordinance, to permit the
construction of a new two-car garage with a rear yard setback of 3.00 feet,
rather than with the required minimum rear yard setback of 6.00 feet, said
construction to be in accordance with the plans and elevations submitted with
the application for variation.
SECTION 3:   The variation granted herein is conditioned upon the commencement
of the proposed construction within 12 months after the effective date of this
Ordinance.
SECTION 4:   This Ordinance is passed by the Council of the Village of Winnetka
in the exercise of its home rule powers pursuant to Section 6 of Article VII of
the Illinois Constitution of 1970.
SECTION 5:   This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon its passage,
approval and posting as provided by law.
PASSED this 7th day of September, 2010, pursuant to the following roll call
vote: 
AYES:    Trustees Greable, Johnson, Rintz, Spinney
NAYS:    Trustees Pedian, Poor
ABSENT:    None
APPROVED this 7th day of September, 2010
   Signed:
   s/Jessica B. Tucker
   Village President
Countersigned:
s/Elizabeth Rosenthal
Village Clerk
