ORDINANCE NO. M-7-2018
AN ORDINANCE GRANTING A SPECIAL USE PERMIT FOR THE EXPANSION OF AN EXISTING
PARKING LOT WITHIN THE B-1 MULTIFAMILY RESIDENTIAL ZONING DISTRICT OF THE
VILLAGE
(614 Lincoln Avenue)
   WHEREAS, the Winnetka Community House ( "Applicant" ), is the record title
owner of: (i) that certain parcel of real property commonly known as 620
Lincoln Avenue, Winnetka, Illinois, at which it currently operates a community
center as an existing legal conforming special use ( “Community House Property”
); and (ii) that certain parcel of real property commonly known as 614 Lincoln
Ave, Winnetka, Illinois, and legally described in Exhibit A ( “Subject
Property” ) attached to and, by this reference, made a part of this Ordinance;
and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property is improved with: (i) an existing two-story
single family structure commonly known as the “Champion House,” which has been
used as offices for Applicant’s employees and employees of other non-profit
organizations pursuant to a special use permit granted in accordance with
Resolution R-1130-92 adopted by the Council of the Village of Winnetka
( “Village Council” ) on February 4, 1992 ( “Existing Building” ); and (ii) a
20-space parking lot ( “Parking Lot” ); and
   WHEREAS, the Applicant desires to: (i) demolish and remove the Existing
Building on the Subject Property ( “Existing Building Demolition” ); and (ii)
expand the Parking Lot on the Subject Property by six parking spaces to a total
of 26 parking spaces ( “Parking Lot Expansion” ) (collectively, the Existing
Building Demolition and the Parking Lot Expansion are the “Proposed
Improvements” ); and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property is located within the B-1 Multifamily
Residential District of the Village ( "B-1 District" ); and
   WHEREAS, pursuant to Section 17.32.010 and the table of uses set forth in
Section 17.46.010 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance ( "Zoning Ordinance" ), the
principal use of the Subject Property as a parking lot is not permitted within
the B-1 District without a special use permit; and
   WHEREAS, on November 6, 2017, the Applicant filed an application for a
demolition permit for the Existing Building Demolition in compliance with the
requirements set forth in Chapter 15.52 of the Winnetka Village Code
( “Demolition Permit” ); and
   WHEREAS, on December 4, 2017, after due notice thereof, the Landmark
Preservation Committee conducted a public hearing, and by a vote of seven in
favor and zero opposed, issued a determination and findings in favor of
approving the application for a Demolition Permit and issuing the Demolition
Permit for the Existing Building Demolition; and
   WHEREAS, on February 7, 2018, the Applicant filed an application for a
special use permit pursuant to Section 17.32.010.B.2.a and Chapter 17.56 of the
of the Zoning Ordinance to permit the Parking Lot Expansion ( "Special Use
Permit" ); and
   WHEREAS, on February 28, 2018, after due notice thereof, the Plan Commission
conducted a public hearing on the proposed Special Use Permit and, by a vote of
eleven in favor and zero opposed, recommended that the Village Council approve
the Special Use Permit, upon the conditions that: (i) the Applicant work with
their lighting consultant to consider a warmer color light for the parking lot
lighting and to consider reducing the number of light poles while maintaining a
uniform lighting level across the parking lot (“Lighting Condition”); (ii) the
expanded Parking Lot after the Parking Lot Expansion (“Expanded Parking Lot”)
is to be used by only employees of and visitors to the Winnetka Community
House; and (iii) the Council consider an expiration date for the Special Use
Permit due to the temporary nature of the Parking Lot Expansion; and
   WHEREAS, on March 6, 2018, the Applicant submitted revised lighting plans
labeled “Point-By-Point Illuminance Calculation”, provided by U.S.
Architectural & Sun Valley Lighting dated 3/6/18, eliminating one light pole
and changing the lighting to a warmer color, as requested by the Plan
Commission in its Lighting Condition; and
   WHEREAS, on March 12, 2018, after due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals ( “ZBA” ) conducted a public hearing on the proposed Special Use Permit
and, by a vote of six in favor and zero opposed, recommended that the Village
Council approve the Special Use Permit, upon the condition that the Village
Council consider an expiration date for the Special Use Permit; and
   WHEREAS, pursuant to Chapter 17.56 of the Zoning Ordinance, the ZBA heard
evidence and made certain findings in support of recommending approval of the
Special Use Permit, respectively, which findings are set forth in the ZBA
public hearing minutes; and
   WHEREAS, on March 21, 2018, after due notice thereof, the Design Review
Board ( “DRB” ) conducted a public hearing on the proposed Special Use Permit,
and by a vote of four in favor and zero opposed, found the Special Use Permit
application consistent with the Village Design Guidelines and recommended that
the Village Council approve the Special Use Permit; and
   WHEREAS, the Village Council has determined that approval of the proposed
Special Use Permit for the Parking Lot Expansion at the Subject Property
satisfies the standards for the approval of special use permits within the B-
1 District set forth in Chapter 17.56 and Section 17.32.010.B.2.a of the Zoning
Ordinance and is in the best interest of the Village and its residents and has
determined that an expiration date for the Special Use Permit is not necessary;
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka does ordain as
follows: 
   SECTION 1:   RECITALS. The foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated into
this Section 1 as the findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if
fully set forth herein. 
   SECTION 2:   SPECIAL USE PERMIT. Subject to, and contingent upon, the terms
and conditions set forth in Section 3 of this Ordinance, the Special Use Permit
is hereby granted, pursuant to Chapter 17.56 and Section 17.32.010.B.2.a of the
Zoning Ordinance and the home rule powers of the Village, to allow the Parking
Lot Expansion by the Applicant at the Subject Property within the B-1 District.
   SECTION 3:   CONDITIONS. The Special Use Permit granted by Section 2 of this
Ordinance is subject to, and contingent upon, compliance by the Applicant with
the following conditions:
   A.   Expiration of Special Use Permit. The Special Use Permit granted by
Section 2 of this Ordinance will expire on the date on which the Special Use
Permit becomes null and void for any reason set forth in this Ordinance or
Section 17.56.100 of the Zoning Ordinance.
   B.   Commencement of Operation. The Applicant must commence operation of the
Proposed Improvements no later than 12 months after the effective date of this
Ordinance.
   C.   Use of Expanded Parking Lot Restricted to Winnetka Community House
Employees and Visitors. The Expanded Parking Lot may only be used by: (i)
Applicant’s employees; (ii) Applicant’s visitors; or (iii) visitors to the
Winnetka Community House. It is the sole responsibility of the Applicant to
insure compliance with this condition. The parking of any vehicle or vehicles
by anyone not falling into one of the three categories set forth in this
Section 3.C shall be deemed a failure to comply with the requirements of this
Ordinance pursuant to Section 5 of this Ordinance.
   D.   Compliance with Regulations. The development, use, and maintenance of
the Subject Property must comply at all times with all applicable Village codes
and ordinances, as they have been or may be amended over time.
   E.   Reimbursement of Village Costs. In addition to any other costs,
payments, fees, charges, contributions, or dedications required under
applicable Village codes, ordinances, resolutions, rules, or regulations, the
Applicant must pay to the Village, promptly upon presentation of a written
demand or demands therefor, of all fees, costs, and expenses incurred or
accrued in connection with the review, negotiation, preparation, consideration,
and review of this Ordinance. Payment of all such fees, costs, and expenses for
which demand has been made shall be made by a certified or cashier's check.
Further, the Applicant must pay upon demand all costs incurred by the Village
for publications and recordings required in connection with the aforesaid
matters.
   F.   Compliance with Plans. The construction, development, use, and
maintenance of the Proposed Improvements at the Subject Property must be in
strict accordance with the following documents and plans, except for minor
changes and site work approved by the Director of Community Development or the
Director of Public Works (within their respective permitting authority) in
accordance with all applicable Village codes, ordinances, and standards:
      1.   The “Proposed Geometric and Paving Plan” prepared by Daniel Creaney
Company, consisting of one sheet, and with a latest revision date of January
22, 2018, attached to and, by this reference, made a part of this Ordinance as
Exhibit B.
      2.   The “Proposed Grading Plan” prepared by Daniel Creaney Company,
consisting of one sheet, and with a latest revision date of January 22, 2018,
attached to and, by this reference, made a part of this Ordinance as Exhibit C.
      3.   The “Notes and Details” prepared by Daniel Creaney Company,
consisting of one sheet, and with a latest revision date of January 22, 2018,
attached to and, by this reference, made a part of this Ordinance as Exhibit D.
      4.   The “Landscape Plan” prepared by Atlas/NSPS Land Title Survey and
the Applicant, consisting of one sheet, and with a latest revision date of
February 7, 2018, attached to and, by this reference, made a part of this
Ordinance as Exhibit E.
      5.   The “Point-By-Point Illuminance Calculation” prepared by U.S.
Architectural & Sun Valley Lighting, consisting of two sheets, and with a
latest revision date of March 6, 2018, attached to and, by this reference, made
a part of this Ordinance as Exhibit F.
   SECTION 4:   RECORDATION; BINDING EFFECT. A copy of this Ordinance will be
recorded with the Cook County Recorder of Deeds. This Ordinance and the
privileges, obligations, and provisions contained herein inure solely to the
benefit of, and are binding upon, the Applicant and each of its heirs,
representatives, successors, and assigns.
   SECTION 5:   FAILURE TO COMPLY. Upon the failure or refusal of the Applicant
to comply with any or all of the conditions, restrictions, or provisions of
this Ordinance, in addition to all other remedies available to the Village, the
Special Use Permit granted in Section 2 of this Ordinance will, at the sole
discretion of the Village Council, by ordinance duly adopted, be revoked and
become null and void; provided, however, that the Village Council may not so
revoke the Special Use Permit granted in Section 2 of this Ordinance unless it
first provides the Applicant with two months advance written notice of the
reasons for revocation and an opportunity to be heard at a regular meeting of
the Village Council. In the event of revocation, the development and use of the
Subject Property will be governed solely by the regulations of the applicable
zoning district and the applicable provisions of the Zoning Ordinance, as the
same may be amended from time to time. Further, in the event of such
revocation, the Village Manager and Village Attorney are hereby authorized and
directed to bring such zoning enforcement action as may be appropriate under
the circumstances.
   SECTION 6:   AMENDMENT OF SPECIAL USE PERMIT. Any amendments to the Special
Use Permit granted in Section 2 of this Ordinance or the conditions set forth
in Section 3 of this Ordinance that may be requested by the Applicant after the
effective date of this Ordinance may be granted only pursuant to the
procedures, and subject to the standards and limitations, provided in the
Zoning Ordinance.
   SECTION 7:   EFFECTIVE DATE.
   A.   This Ordinance will be effective only upon the occurrence of all of the
following events:
      1.   Passage by the Village Council in the manner required by law;
      2.   Publication in pamphlet form in the manner required by law; and
      3.   The filing by the Applicant with the Village Clerk of an
Unconditional Agreement and Consent in the form of Exhibit G attached to and,
by this reference, made a part of this Ordinance, to accept and abide by each
and all of the terms, conditions, and limitations set forth in this Ordinance
and to indemnify the Village for any claims that may arise in connection with
the approval of this Ordinance.
   B.   In the event that the Applicant does not file with the Village Clerk a
fully executed copy of the unconditional agreement and consent described in
Section 7.A.3 of this Ordinance within 60 days after the date of passage of
this Ordinance by the Village Council, the Village Council shall have the
right, in its sole discretion, to declare this Ordinance null and void and of
no force or effect.
