ORDINANCE NO. M-13-2019
AN ORDINANCE DESIGNATING 1094 FISHER CRESCENT LANE AS A LANDMARK
   WHEREAS, Edward McNabola and Katherine McNabola (collectively, "Owners" )
are the owners of the property commonly known as 1094 Fisher Crescent Lane,
Winnetka, Illinois, and legally described in Exhibit A attached to and, by this
reference, made a part of this Ordinance ( “Subject Property” ); and
   WHEREAS, the Subject Property is improved with a single family home designed
by architect Robert Seyfarth, which was constructed in 1919 after a fire
( “Willoughby Walling House” ); and
   WHEREAS, Chapter 15.64 of the Winnetka Village Code ( “Village Code” ),
titled “Landmark Preservation,” sets forth standards and procedures for
preserving, protecting, enhancing, rehabilitating, and regulating buildings,
structures, objects, and places of historical, cultural, or architectural
significance ( “Landmark Regulations” ); and
WHEREAS, pursuant to Section 15.64.040 of the Landmark Regulations, the Owners
submitted an application for the designation of the Subject Property as a
landmark to the Director of Community Development for consideration by the
Landmark Preservation Commission ( “Commission” ); and
WHEREAS, pursuant to, and in accordance with, the Landmark Regulations, a duly
noticed public hearing of the Commission to consider the Owners’ application
was held on August 5, 2019; and
WHEREAS, on August 5, 2019, the Commission made findings, which findings are
attached as Exhibit B, and evaluated the application and the Subject Property
using the Village’s System for the Evaluation of Landmarks and gave the Subject
Property an overall score of 83.6; and
WHEREAS, pursuant to the findings made by the Commission and the overall rating
of the Subject Property as unique, the Commission: (i) determined that the
Subject Property meets the criteria for the evaluation of landmarks set forth
in the Landmark Regulations; and (ii) unanimously recommended that the Village
Council designate the Subject Property as a landmark; and
WHEREAS, the Village Council has considered the recommendation of the
Commission and has determined that it is in the best interest of the Village
and its residents to accept the findings and recommendation of the Commission,
which findings are incorporated herein, and approve the designation of the
Subject Property as a landmark;
   NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
 
   SECTION 1:   RECITALS. The foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated into
this Section 1 as the findings of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if
fully set forth herein. 
   SECTION 2:   LANDMARK DESIGNATION. In accordance with, and pursuant to,
Section 15.64.040 of the Village Code, the Village Council hereby designates
the Subject Property as a locally designated landmark.
   SECTION 3:   EFFECTIVE DATE. This Ordinance shall be in full force and
effect from and after its passage, approval, and publication in the manner
provided by law.      
PASSED this 17 th day of September, 2019, pursuant to the following roll call
vote: 
AYES:   Trustees Coladarci, Cripe, Dearborn, Lanphier, and Swierk          
NAYS:   Trustee Wedner                        
ABSENT:   None                              
APPROVED this 17 th day of September, 2019.
