ORDINANCE NO. M-06-2025
 
AN ORDINANCE APPROVING A FINAL PLAT OF SUBDIVISION, GRANTING A SPECIAL USE
PERMIT, AN AMENDMENT TO A SPECIAL USE PERMIT, VARIATIONS FROM THE WINNETKA
ZONING ORDINANCE AND A CERTIFICATE OF APPROPRIATENESS FOR THE CONSTRUCTION OF
ADDITIONS TO AN EXISTING CHURCH WITHIN THE R-5 SINGLE-FAMILY RESIDENTIAL
DISTRICT
(440 Ridge Avenue and 760 Cherry Street)
 
WHEREAS, the Village of Winnetka is a home rule municipality in accordance with
Article VII, Section 6 of the Constitution of the State of Illinois of 1970;
and
   WHEREAS, Grace Presbyterian Church of the North Shore ( “Owner” ) is the
record title owner of the parcels of real property located at 440 Ridge Avenue
and 760 Cherry Street in Winnetka, Illinois, and legally described in Exhibit A
and Exhibit B, respectively, attached to and, by this reference, made a part of
this Ordinance (collectively, “Subject Property” ); and
 
WHEREAS, the Subject Property is located within the R-5 Single Family
Residential District of the Village ( “R-5 District” ); and
 
   WHEREAS, the area of the parcel located at 440 Ridge Avenue is 35,202.75
square feet, and the area of the parcel located at 760 Cherry Street is
7,506.25 square feet; and
 
   WHEREAS, the Owner desires to consolidate the two existing lots into a
single lot of record ( “Proposed Subdivision” ); and
 
   WHEREAS, the property located at 760 Cherry Street contains a two-story
single-family residence and detached garage; and
 
   WHEREAS, the property located at 440 Ridge Avenue consists of a building
( “Building” ) and a parking lot ( “Parking Lot” ); and
 
   WHEREAS, the Owner operates a church known as Grace Presbyterian Church of
the North Shore within the Building at 440 Ridge Avenue; and
 
   WHEREAS, the Owner desires to demolish the existing residence and detached
garage on 760 Cherry Street to construct additions to the Building located on
440 Ridge Avenue; and
 
   WHEREAS, pursuant to Section 16.12.010.D.10, the consolidation of any number
of lots that results in a lot that is greater than two times the minimum lot
size for the lot in question or greater than two times the minimum average lot
width for the corner lot in question is only permitted by a special use permit;
and
 
   WHEREAS, following the Proposed Subdivision, the new lot will measure
approximately 42,709 square feet, which lot size is more than two times greater
than the minimum required lot area by 24,909 square feet, and more than two
times greater than the minimum required average lot width by 30 feet; and
   WHEREAS, in 1983, in connection with the establishment of previous additions
to the Building, the Village Council approved Ordinance No. M-151-83 and
Resolution No. 793-83, granting a special use permit and intensity of use of
lot variation that allowed construction of the Parking Lot as an accessory use
to the existing church on the Subject Property in the R-5 District (“ Original
  Special Use Permit ”); and
   
   WHEREAS, the Owner proposes to construct additions and proposed improvements
to the Building including (i) a two-story addition, as well as a one-story
addition on the south side of the Building; (ii) a two-story addition on the
north side of the Building; and (iii) reconfiguration of the Parking Lot
( “Parking Lot Improvements” ) (collectively, the Proposed Additions and
Improvements ”); and
 
WHEREAS, pursuant to Section 17.56.090 of the Village’s Zoning Ordinance, as
amended (“Zoning Ordinance”) no special use may be enlarged or extended by
structural alteration of a building or other structure without amending the
special use permit; and
WHEREAS, the Owner desires to amend the Original Special Use Permit to allow
for the construction and operation of the Proposed Additions and Improvements;
and
   WHEREAS, pursuant to Section 17.30.030.A of the Zoning Ordinance, the
maximum roofed lot coverage for the Subject Property is 10,677.25 square feet;
and    
 
   WHEREAS, pursuant to Section 17.30.040 of the Zoning Ordinance, the maximum
gross floor area for the Subject Property is 11,638.07; and
 
WHEREAS, pursuant to Section 17.30.050 of the Zoning Ordinance, the minimum
front yard setback required along Ridge Avenue is 47.01 feet; and
WHEREAS, pursuant to Section 17.30.050 of the Zoning Ordinance, the minimum
corner yard setback required along Cherry Street is 30 feet; and
WHEREAS, the Owner desires to construct and operate the Proposed Additions and
Improvements in a manner that will: (i) increase the total area of roofed lot
coverage on the Subject Property from approximately 9,329 square feet to
approximately 13,374 square feet, requiring a variation of 2,696.75 square
feet; (ii) increase the gross floor area from 18,812 square feet to 25,009
square feet, requiring a variation of 13,370.93 square feet; (iii) decrease the
minimum required front yard setback along Ridge Avenue for the Parking Lot
Improvements, from 47.01 feet to 32.5 feet, requiring a variation of 14.51
feet; and (iv) decrease the minimum required corner yard setback along Cherry
Street from 30 feet to 19.5 feet, requiring a variation of 10.5 feet; and
WHEREAS, the Owner filed an application for: (i) approval of a final plat of
subdivision for the Proposed Subdivision (“Final Plat”); (ii) a special use
permit to allow the Proposed Subdivision which consolidation results in a lot
that is greater than two times the minimum required lot size by 24,909 square
feet and minimum average lot width for a corner lot by 30 feet in the R-
5 District pursuant to Chapter 17.56 of the Zoning Ordinance and Section
16.12.010.D.10 of the Village Code (“Special Use Permit”); (iii) variations
from Sections 17.30.030.A, 17.30.040, and 17.30.050 of the Zoning Ordinance to
permit the construction of the Proposed Additions and Improvements
(collectively, the “Variations” ); (iv) an amendment to the Original Special
Use Permit pursuant to Sections 17.12.020 and Chapter 17.56 of the Zoning
Ordinance to allow the construction and operation of the Proposed Additions and
Improvements on the Subject Property within the R-5 District ( “Special Use
Permit Amendment” ); and (v) a certificate of appropriateness pursuant to
Section 15.40.010 of the Winnetka Village Code (“Village Code”) (the Final
Plat, Special Use Permit, Variations, Special Use Permit Amendment, and
Certificate of Appropriateness are, collectively, the “Requested Relief” ); and
   WHEREAS, at its meetings on June 20, 2024, and September 19, 2024, after due
notice thereof, the Design Review Board of the Village met to consider a
Certificate of Appropriateness for the Proposed Additions and Improvements, and
on September 19, 2024, by a vote of five in favor and none opposed, recommended
the issuance of a Certificate of Appropriateness for the Proposed Additions and
Improvements; and
   WHEREAS, the Design Review Board conducted a subsequent meeting on April 17,
2025, after due notice thereof, due to various changes to the design of the
Proposed Additions and Improvements that were revised during the Plan
Commission’s review and, by a vote of six in favor and none opposed,
recommended the issuance of a Certificate of Appropriateness for the Proposed
Additions and Improvements; and
   WHEREAS, at its meetings on October 23, 2024, and March 13, 2025, after due
notice thereof, the Plan Commission conducted a public hearing on the proposed
Final Plat, Special Use Permit and Special Use Permit Amendment and on March
13, 2025, by a vote of four in favor and none opposed, recommended that the
Village Council approve the Final Plat, Special Use Permit and Special Use
Permit Amendment; and
   WHEREAS, on August 6, 2024, after due notice thereof, the Historic
Preservation Commission ( “HPC” ) considered the demolition application for 760
Cherry Street to demolish the existing residence and detached garage and the
HPC determined a Historical Architectural Impact Study ( “HAIS” ) was required,
and on March 3, 2025, after due notice thereof, the HPC reviewed the HAIS and,
by a vote of five in favor and none opposed, determined the existing residence
was not significant and allowed the demolition to proceed without delay; and
WHEREAS, on April 14, 2025, after due notice thereof, the Zoning Board of
Appeals conducted a public hearing on the Special Use Permit Amendment and the
Variations and, by a vote of five in favor and one opposed, recommended that
the Village Council approve the Special Use Permit Amendment and Variations;
and
WHEREAS, the Village Council has determined that it is in the best interest of
the Village to approve the Requested Relief, subject to and in strict
accordance with the terms and conditions of this Ordinance;
NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows: 
SECTION 1:   RECITALS. The foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the
findings of the Village Council, as if fully set forth herein.
   SECTION 2:   APPROVAL OF FINAL PLAT. Subject to and contingent upon the
conditions set forth in Section 7 of this Ordinance, and pursuant to Sections
16.04.030 and 16.08.010 of the Village’s Subdivision Ordinance ( “Subdivision
Ordinanc e ” ) and the Village’s home rule authority, the Village Council
hereby approves the Final Plat, prepared by R.E. Allen and Associates, LTD.,
and consisting of one sheet, with a latest revision date of December 19, 2023,
attached to and, by this reference, made a part of this Ordinance as Exhibit C.
 
   SECTION 3:   SPECIAL USE PERMIT. Subject to, and contingent upon, the terms
and conditions set forth in Section 7 of this Ordinance, the Special Use Permit
is hereby granted, pursuant to Chapter 17.56 of the Zoning Ordinance and
Section 16.12.010.D.10 of the Village Code, and the home rule powers of the
Village, to allow the Proposed Subdivision which results in a lot that is
greater than two times the minimum required lot size and minimum average lot
width for a corner lot in the R-5 District.  
 
   SECTION 4:   APPROVAL OF SPECIAL USE PERMIT AMENDMENT. Subject to, and
contingent upon, the terms and conditions set forth in Section 7 of this
Ordinance, the Amended Special Use Permit is hereby granted, pursuant to
Chapter 17.56 and Section 17.12.020 of the Zoning Ordinance and the home rule
powers of the Village, to allow the construction and operation of the Proposed
Additions and Improvements on the Subject Property within the R-5 District.
 
   SECTION 5:   APPROVAL OF VARIATIONS. Subject to, and contingent upon, the
terms, conditions, restrictions, and provisions set forth in Section 7 of this
Ordinance, the following variations are hereby granted with respect to the
Subject Property, in accordance with and pursuant to Chapter 17.60 of the
Zoning Ordinance and the home rule powers of the Village:
A.   Roofed Lot Coverage. A variation from Section 17.30.030.A of the Zoning
Ordinance to permit a total of 13,374 square feet of roofed lot coverage.
B.   Gross Floor Area. A variation from Section 17.30.040 of the Zoning
Ordinance to permit a total of 25,009 square feet gross floor area.
C.   Front Yard Setback. A variation from Section 17.30.050 of the Zoning
Ordinance to permit a front yard setback of 32.5 feet along Ridge Avenue.
D.   Corner Yard Setback. A variation from Section 17.30.050 of the Zoning
Ordinance to permit a corner yard setback of 19.5 feet along Cherry Street.
   SECTION 6:   APPROVAL OF CERTIFICATE OF APPROPRIATENESS. Subject to, and
contingent upon, the terms, conditions, restrictions, and provisions set forth
in Section 7 of this Ordinance, the Certificate of Appropriateness is granted
for the Subject Property, pursuant to Section 15.40.010 of the Village Code and
the home rule powers of the Village.
 
   SECTION 7:   CONDITIONS. The approvals granted by Sections 2, 3, 4, 5, and 6
of this Ordinance are subject to, and contingent upon, compliance by the Owner
with the following conditions:
 
A.   Compliance with Subdivision Ordinance. The approvals granted in Section 2,
3, 4, 5 and 6 of this Ordinance are subject to and conditioned upon compliance
with, and the inclusion of all of the information on the Final Plat required by
Chapter 16.08 of the Subdivision Ordinance.
 
B.   Compliance with Regulations. Except to the extent specifically provided
otherwise in this Ordinance, the development, use, and maintenance of the
Proposed Subdivision and the Subject Property must comply at all times with all
applicable Village codes and ordinances, as they have been or may be amended
over time.
 
C.   Compliance with Plans. The development, use, and maintenance of the
Proposed Additions and Improvements at the Subject Property must be in strict
accordance with the, the Plans for the Proposed Additions and Improvements
prepared by Present Future Architects and Eriksson Engineering Associates,
Ltd., consisting of 48 sheets, attached to and, by this reference made a part
of this Ordinance as Exhibit D, except for minor changes and site work approved
by the Director of Community Development or the Director of Engineering (within
their respective permitting authority) in accordance with all applicable
Village codes, ordinances, and standards.
D.   Continued Effect of Special Use Permit. The Original Special Use Permit,
as amended by this Special Use Permit Amendment, remains in full force and
effect; provided, however, that in the event of any conflict between the
Original Special Use Permit and the Special Use Permit Amendment, the
provisions of the Special Use Permit Amendment will control.
E.   Reimbursement of Village Costs. In addition to any other costs, payments,
fees, charges, contributions, or dedications required under applicable Village
codes, ordinances, Ordinances, rules, or regulations, the Owner must pay to the
Village, promptly upon presentation of a written demand or demands therefor, of
all fees, costs, and expenses incurred or accrued in connection with the
review, negotiation, preparation, consideration, and review of this Ordinance.
Payment of all such fees, costs, and expenses for which demand has been made
shall be made by a certified or cashier’s check. Further, the Owner must pay
upon demand all costs incurred by the Village for publications and recordings
required in connection with the aforesaid matters.
 
   SECTION 8:   EXECUTION OF FINAL PLAT. The Village Council hereby authorizes
and directs the Village President and the Village Clerk, upon satisfaction of
the conditions set forth in Section 7 of this Ordinance, to execute and attest,
on behalf of the Village, the Final Plat.
 
   SECTION 9:   RECORDATION OF FINAL PLAT. Upon execution of the Final Plat by
the Village President and the Village Clerk, as provided in Section 8 of this
Ordinance, and upon this Ordinance taking effect as provided in Section 14 of
this Ordinance, the Village Clerk is hereby directed to cause the Final Plat to
be recorded with the Cook County Clerk Recording Division.
SECTION 10 :   RECORDATION OF ORDINANCE; BINDING EFFECT. A copy of this
Ordinance will be recorded with the Cook County Clerk Recording Division. This
Ordinance and the privileges, obligations, and provisions contained herein
inure solely to the benefit of, and are binding upon, the Owner and each of
their heirs, representatives, successors, and assigns.
 
   SECTION 11: FAILURE TO COMPLY. Upon the failure or refusal of the Owner to
comply with any or all of the conditions, restrictions, or provisions of this
Ordinance, in addition to all other remedies available to the Village, the
approvals granted in Sections 2, 3, 4, 5 and 6 of this Ordinance will, at the
sole discretion of the Village Council, by ordinance duly adopted, be revoked
and become null and void; provided, however, that the Village Council will not
revoke the approvals granted in Sections 2, 3, 4, 5 and 6 of this Ordinance
unless it first provides the Owner with two months advance written notice of
the reasons for revocation and an opportunity to be heard at a regular meeting
of the Village Council. In the event of revocation, the development and use of
the Subject Property will be governed solely by the regulations of the
applicable zoning district and provisions of the Zoning Ordinance, as they may,
from time to time, be amended. In the event of revocation, the Village Manager
and Village Attorney are authorized and directed to bring a zoning enforcement
action as may be appropriate under the circumstances.
 
   SECTION 12: AMENDMENTS. Any amendment to this Ordinance may be granted only
pursuant to the procedures, and subject to the standards and limitations,
provided in the Zoning Ordinance for amending or granting variations.
 
   SECTION 13:      SEVERABILITY. If any provision of this Ordinance or part
thereof is held invalid by a court of competent jurisdiction, the remaining
provisions of this Ordinance shall remain in full force and effect, and shall
be interpreted, applied, and enforced so as to achieve, as near as may be, the
purpose and intent of this Ordinance to the greatest extent permitted by
applicable law.
 
   SECTION 14:   EFFECTIVE DATE.
 
   A.   This Ordinance will only be in full force and effect upon the
occurrence of all of the following events: (i) passage and approval of this
Ordinance as provided by law; and (ii) the demolition of the single-family
residence and detached garage located on the current 760 Cherry Avenue lot as
of the date of this Ordinance in accordance with all applicable provisions of
the Village Code.
 
   B.   The approvals set forth in Sections 2, 3, 4, 5, and 6 of this Ordinance
will be effective only upon: (i) the filing by the Owner with the Village Clerk
of an Unconditional Agreement and Consent in the form of Exhibit E attached to
and, by this reference, made a part of this Ordinance to accept and abide by
each and all of the terms, conditions, and limitations set forth in this
Ordinance and to indemnify the Village for any claims that may arise in
connection with the approval of this Ordinance; and (ii) the approval and
recording of the Final Plat.
 
   C.   In the event that the Owner does not file with the Village Clerk a
fully executed copy of the unconditional agreement and consent described in
Section 14.B of this Ordinance within 60 days after the date of passage of this
Ordinance by the Village Council, the Village Council shall have the right, in
its sole discretion, to declare the approvals granted in this Ordinance null
and void and of no force or effect.
 
