ORDINANCE NO. MC-217-99
AN ORDINANCE AMENDING THE DEFINITION OF SIDE YARD IN THE WINNETKA ZONING
ORDINANCE
WHEREAS, on October 16, 1998, the Council of the Village of Winnetka received
an application from a private citizen seeking text amendments to the
definitions of “Average Width of Lot” and “Side Yard” contained in Section
22.04 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Chapter 22 of the Winnetka Village
Code; and
WHEREAS, on December 15, 1998, on due notice thereof, the Council of the
Village of Winnetka, sitting as a Committee of the Whole, conducted a hearing
on the application for text amendments and subsequently, by unanimous vote,
adopted findings which denied the request for the amendment to the definition
of “Average Width of Lot” and directed the preparation of an ordinance amending
the definition of “Side Yard;” and
WHEREAS, the definition of side yard is one of several definitions of types of
yards found in the Zoning Ordinance; and
WHEREAS, the proposed amendment discloses the apparently inadvertent omission
of language which designates the side lot line as the point of reference for
measuring the side yard, and the definition has consistently been applied by
using the side lot line as that point of reference; and
WHEREAS, although the current definition of side yard is clear and
understandable when read in the context of the side yard requirements for each
of the single family residential zoning districts, and has been consistently
applied by the Village, the proposed amendment serves to further clarify the
definition; and
WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka finds that amending the
definition of side yard to include the inadvertently omitted designation of the
side lot line as the point of reference for measuring the side yard is in the
best interests of the Village and its residents, as it will provide for
consistency among the definitions of the various types of yards without
changing the manner in which any yards are calculated.
NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
SECTION 1: That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the findings
of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth herein.
SECTION 2: That the definition of “Side Yard,” in Section 22.04, “Definitions,”
of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Chapter 22 of the Winnetka Village Code, is
hereby amended to provide as follows:
Side Yard: A yard extending along a side lot line between that line and a line
drawn parallel to the side lot line at a distance therefrom equal to the
required width of such yard, but excluding any area encompassed within a front
yard or rear yard.
SECTION 3: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage, approval
and posting.
   ADOPTED this 2nd day of March, 1999, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
AYES:      Trustees Derning, Duhl, Lanphier, Lien and Meuer
NAYS:   None
ABSENT:   Trustee Hilton
Signed:
s/s Louise A. Holland
Village President
Countersigned:
s/s Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: February 16, 1999
Posted: February 17, 1999
Passed and Approved: March 2, 1999
Posted: March 3, 1999
ORDINANCE NO. MC-218-99
AN ORDINANCE AMENDING THE DEFINITION OF THE TERM RESTAURANT IN CHAPTER 35 OF
THE WINNETKA VILLAGE CODE
WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka have determined that the
definition of the term “Restaurant” in the Village’s Liquor Ordinance, Section
35.01 of the Winnetka Village Code, should be amended in order to make the
portion of the definition pertaining to kitchen equipment more consistent with
the Illinois Liquor Control Act, and to add further distinctions between
restaurants for purposes of qualifying for liquor licenses and restaurants as
defined elsewhere in the Village Code.
NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
SECTION 1: The recitals stated in the foregoing preamble to this Ordinance are
adopted by the Council of the Village of Winnetka as its findings.
SECTION 2: That the definition of "Restaurants" in Section 35.01 of Chapter 35
of the Winnetka Village Code "Liquor," be amended to read:
   Restaurant: Any public place kept, used, maintained, advertised and held out
to the public as a place where complete meals are served, and where complete
meals are actually and regularly served, such space being provided with
adequate and sanitary kitchen and dining room equipment and having employed
therein a sufficient number and kind of employees to prepare, cook and serve
suitable food for its patrons, where a host or hostess is present to seat
patrons, where patrons order from individual pre-printed menus, where orders
are taken from and food is served to patrons while they are seated at tables,
where complete meals are served using non-disposable dishes, glassware and
utensils, and at which the service of alcoholic beverages is incidental and
complementary to the service of such meals. For purposes of this chapter, none
of the following premises shall be considered to be a Restaurant hereunder: (i)
any premises containing a bar or counter at which patrons may be served meals;
(ii) any premises containing a television that has not been granted a
Television Rider pursuant to this chapter; and (iii) limited food
establishments, including, but not limited to, fast food restaurants and drive-
in restaurants as defined in the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, luncheonettes,
lounges, diners, drive-ins, or self-service or carry-out establishments.
SECTION 3: That this ordinance shall be in full force and effect from and after
its passage, approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 16th day of February, 1999, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
AYES:      Trustees Derning, Duhl, Hilton, Lanphier, Lien and Meuer
NAYS:   None
ABSENT:   None
Signed:
s/s Louise A. Holland
Village President
Countersigned:
s/s Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: February 3, 1999
Posted: February 3, 1999
Passed and Approved: February 16, 1999
Posted: February 17, 1999
ORDINANCE NO. MC-219-99
AN ORDINANCE AMENDING THE WINNETKA ZONING ORDINANCE AS IT PERTAINS TO THE C-
1 LIMITED RETAIL COMMERCIAL ZONING DISTRICT
WHEREAS, on February 3, 1998, following the receipt and consideration of
preliminary recommendation of the Plan Commission for the C-1 (Limited Retail)
and C-2 (General Retail) Commercial Zoning Districts in the Village of
Winnetka, the Council of the Village of Winnetka enacted Ordinance No. MC-200-
98, which amended the provisions of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance pertaining to
building height, intensity of use and parking in the commercial zoning
districts; and
WHEREAS, on August 18, 1998, the Council of the Village of Winnetka enacted
Ordinance No. MC-210-98, imposing a moratorium on the issuance of certain
building permits and the processing of applications from zoning relief in the
C-1 Commercial Zoning District, in order to allow sufficient time for the
completion of the portions of the Comprehensive Plan pertaining to that zoning
district; and
WHEREAS, as a result of the continuing work on the Comprehensive Plan, the
Community Development staff has proposed that the zoning and development
regulations applicable to the C-1 (Limited Retail) Commercial Zoning District
be amended to reclassify some of the uses that will be permitted by right and
some of the uses that require a special use permit, and to amend the provisions
pertaining to lot area, lot density, setback and landscaping within that zoning
district; and
WHEREAS, on February 2, 1999, on due notice thereof, and in accordance with
Section 22.19 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, the Winnetka Village Council,
sitting as a Committee of the Whole, conducted a public hearing on proposed
amendments to the regulations applicable to the properties in the C-1 Limited
Retail Commercial Zoning District; and
WHEREAS, was continued to, and further evidence was received on, February 16,
1999, and March 16, 1999; and
WHEREAS, on February 16, 1999, the Council adopted ordinance number MC-229-99,
which extended the moratorium on the issuance of certain building permits and
the processing of applications for zoning relief in the C-1 Commercial Zoning
District, in order to allow additional time to finalize the amendments to the
zoning regulations applicable to the C-1 Commercial Zoning District; and
WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka have determined that the
proposed amendments will serve to guide redevelopment of properties within the
C-1 (Limited Retail) Commercial Zoning District so as to provide commercial and
retail development to serve the needs of persons living or working within the
district and the nearby area, while preserving the character of the adjoining
single-family residential zoning district and that adoption of the proposed
amendments is in the best interests of the Village, its residents and property
owners.
NOW THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
SECTION 1:  The recitals stated in the foregoing preamble to this Ordinance are
adopted by the Council of the Village of Winnetka as its findings.
SECTION 2:  That subsection (a), “District Purpose” of Section 22.10 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, “C-1” (Limited Retail) Commercial District
Regulations, is hereby amended to provide as follows:
22.10    "C-l" (LIMITED RETAIL) COMMERCIAL DISTRICT REGULATIONS.
   (a)    District Purpose. While recognizing the presence of existing uses
within the boundaries of the "C-1" (Limited Retail) Commercial District upon
the date of adoption of this ordinance, the requirements set forth herein have
been adopted in order to provide for a neighborhood service district,
complemented by multi-family residential uses, for the purpose of retailing
convenience goods and providing personal services for the accommodation of the
basic day-to-day shopping or service needs of persons living or working within
the district and the nearby area, while preserving the character of the
adjoining single family residential zoning districts.
SECTION 3: That subsection (b), “Use,” of Section 22.10 of the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance, “C-1” (Limited Retail) Commercial District Regulations, is hereby
amended to provide as follows:
   (b)    Use. No building or premises shall be used and no building shall be
erected or altered within the "C-l" (Limited Retail) Commercial District,
unless otherwise provided for in this ordinance, except for the following uses
(separately or in combination):
      1.   Principal Use. Except as otherwise provided in this Code, the ground
floor of any building in the “C-1” (Limited Retail) Commercial District shall
be used for one or more of the commercial uses enumerated in this paragraph. No
uses involving the sale or distribution of goods or materials at wholesale
shall be permitted.
         a.    A dwelling unit located above the ground floor in a commercial
building.
         b.    Antique, gift, specialty or novelty shop;
         c.    Apparel store;
         d.    Art gallery, studio or supplies store;
         e.    Bakery, in which the preparation of goods is limited to goods
sold only on the premises;
         f.    Barber shop or beauty salon;
         g.    Book, stationery, card or office supply store;
         h.    Camera or photographic equipment or supply store;
         i.    Candy, confectionery, ice-cream or nut shop;
         j.    China, glassware, ceramic or flatware shop;
         k.    Electronics and service store;
         l.    Hardware store
         m.    Interior decorating service;
         n.    Jewelry or clock store;
         o.    Laundry and dry or steam cleaning receiving store;
         p.    Leather goods or luggage;
         q.    Office uses, including medical and dental, but excluding office
uses requiring special uses;
         r.    Paint and wallpaper shop;
         s.    Pharmacy or drug store;
         t.    Photography studio;
         u.    Record, tape and video disc store;
         v.    Restaurant, standard;
         v.    Shoe store or bootery;
         w.    Shoe or hat repair shop;
         x.    Tailor shop.
         y.    Toy store;
         z.    Retail or service businesses similar in character to the uses
listed above.
      2.    Special Use.
         As a special use, subject to the conditions and requirements set
forth herein and in section 22.13, any one of the following:
         a.    Automobile service station, provided that such station shall not
be located or maintained within 200 feet of any building used or constructed
for use in whole or in part as a church, or temple, library, community or
parish house, or public or private school or kindergarten (in measuring such
minimum distance, the portion of such distance that lies within the boundaries
of any public street shall be counted twice);
         b.    Automobile sales and show room (limited to the indoor storage of
display models);
         c.    Parking lot, provided that, in addition to the standards set
forth in section 22.13 for the granting of special use permits, the applicant
demonstrates that the parking lot will comply with all applicable requirements
of subsection (k) of this section 22.10, and provided further that no motor
vehicles, motor appliances, equipment or supplies shall be offered for sale,
repair or service, or be stored for any such purposes; (MC-200-98) (2/3/98)
         d.    Public parking garage, provided that such garage shall not be
located or maintained within 200 feet of any building used or constructed for
use in whole or in part as a church or temple, library, community or parish
house, or public or private school or kindergarten (in measuring such minimum
distance, the portion of such distance that lies within the boundaries of any
public street shall be counted twice);
         e.    Restaurant, fast food, provided that, in addition to the
standards set forth in section 22.13 for the granting of special use permits,
the applicant demonstrates that the special use will be in compliance with the
following additional standards:
            (1)   No fast food restaurant, drive-in establishment or drive-in
restaurant may be located within 300 feet (measured from property line to
property line) of another fast food restaurant, drive-in establishment or
drive-in restaurant. (MC-145-95) (6/20/95)
            (2)    Principal vehicular ingress and egress to the premises shall
be from a primary street, as defined in the Village of Winnetka Comprehensive
Plan.
            (3)    One off -street-loading berth adequate for the loading and
unloading of service vehicles and trucks (together with properly related access
to a public street or alley) shall be provided such that no maneuvering
incidental to the parking, loading or unloading of such service vehicles and
trucks shall be on or across any public right-of-way, and no loading berth
shall be located within 30 feet of the nearest point of intersection of any two
streets.
            (4)    A minimum of 30 parking spaces for each 1,000 square feet of
gross floor area, plus one parking space for each 1-1/2 employees (based upon
the largest number of employees in attendance at any time), plus one parking
space for each vehicle directly associated with the use (whether stored on
premises over night or not) shall be provided.
            (5)    The lighting of parking lots or loading areas shall cease at
or before midnight when such lots or areas are within 100 feet of the boundary
of any "A" Single-Family Residential District.
         f.    Drive-in establishments or portions thereof, provided that, in
addition to the standards set forth in section 22. 13 for the granting of
special use permits, the applicant demonstrates that the special use will be in
compliance with the additional standards set forth above in subparagraphs (1),
(2), (3) and (5) of paragraph e. (MC-145-95) (6/20/95); (MC-156-96) (3/5/96)
         g.    Convenience store
         h.    Grocery store
         i.    Bank or similar financial institution.
         j.    Offices providing real estate services.
         k.    Retail or service businesses similar in character to the uses
listed above.
         l.    More than one principal building on a lot.
      3.   Essential Public Use. Essential public use, either as a principal
use or as an accessory use. (MC-195-97) (10/14/97)
      4.   Each of the enumerated permitted uses and permitted special uses may
include accessory uses, buildings or other structures, as defined in section
22.04, located on the same lot; provided, however, that satellite receiving
dishes shall be subject to the conditions and requirements set forth in section
22.13.
      5.   The final building design, building materials, site plan and
landscape plan for any of the enumerated permitted uses and permitted special
uses shall be subject to the issuance of a final Certificate of Appropriateness
of Design by the Village Council, following the receipt of a preliminary
recommendation by the Design Review Board, pursuant to the procedures
established in Section 23.36 of the Building Code.
SECTION 4: That subsection (d), “Lot Area and Density,” of Section 22.10 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, “C-1” (Limited Retail) Commercial District
Regulations, is hereby amended to provide as follows:
   (d)   Lot Area and Density. No multiple-family dwelling or building
containing a dwelling unit shall be erected or altered to accommodate or make
provision for more than 32 dwelling units on any acre of land, nor make
provision for more than a proportional number of dwelling units on a fractional
part of any acre of land.
SECTION 5: That Section 22.10 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, “C-1” (Limited
Retail) Commercial District Regulations, is hereby amended by adding a new
subsection (n), entitled, “Set Back,”, which shall provide as follows:
   (n)    Set Back. There shall be a front yard set back of not less than one
foot nor more than three feet maximum, along all street frontages, except that
the maximum shall not apply to the portion of a courtyard building that is set
back from a front lot line to form the rear of the courtyard.
SECTION 6: That Section 22.10 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, “C-1” (Limited
Retail) Commercial District Regulations, is hereby amended by adding a new
subsection (o), entitled, “Landscaping,” which shall provide as follows:
   (o)    Landscaping. There shall be a landscaped area at least five feet deep
along the rear lot line of each zoning lot and along any other lot line that
abuts single-family residential property, in order to provide a buffer and
visual screen between the commercial property and the adjacent single-family
residential zoning district.
SECTION 7 : That Section 22.04 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, “Definitions,”
is hereby amended by adding a new term, “Courtyard,” which shall be defined as
follows:
   Courtyard: An area of open, unoccupied space, three sides of which are
formed by buildings located on the same zoning lot and the entire fourth side
of which abuts and is open and accessible to a public street.
SECTION 8: That subsection (d), “Notice of Application,” of Section 23.36 of
the Building Code, “Certificate of Appropriateness of Design,” Chapter 23 of
the Winnetka Village Code, is hereby amended to provide as follows:
   (d)    Notice of Application. Upon receiving a completed application for a
certificate of appropriateness of design, the director shall promptly transmit
such application to the design review board. If the application is subject to
final action by the Village Council, the director shall issue written notice of
the time and place of the hearing required by subsection (f), below, and
written notice of the time and place of the proceedings before the Village
Council, to all persons to whom the latest general real estate tax bills were
sent for all property situated within 250 feet of the property that is the
subject of the application. The notice shall be addressed to the aforesaid
persons in the names and at the addresses to which the latest general real
estate taxes were sent, and shall be so sent by first class mail, postage
prepaid, not less than 10 days prior to the date of such hearing or council
proceeding.
SECTION 9: That subsection (g), “Decision of Design Review Board,” of Section
23.36 of the Building Code, “Certificate of Appropriateness of Design,” Chapter
23 of the Winnetka Village Code, is hereby amended to provide as follows:
   (g)    Decision of Design Review Board. The board shall render its decision,
or issue its recommendation to the Village Council, no later than 10 days after
the completion of the hearing. The board=s decision shall include specific
factual findings on each issue considered, and may include recommendations to
the applicant or, where applicable, to the Village Council, as to changes in
the plans and specifications which, in the judgment of the board, would tend to
further the general purpose of this section.
SECTION 10: That subsection (h), “Issuance of Certificate,” of Section 23.36 of
the Building Code, “Certificate of Appropriateness of Design,” Chapter 23 of
the Winnetka Village Code, is hereby amended to provide as follows:
   (h)    Issuance of Certificate.
      1.    Time Limitations. If consideration of an application for
certificate of appropriateness of design by the board has not been initiated
within 45 days, or having been initiated, has not been concluded within 45 days
following the owner=s submission of additional evidence required by the board,
the director shall, if the application is in order and the construction
documents are in compliance with this code, approve the construction documents
and issue the required permits or, where the matter is within the final
jurisdiction of the Village Council, shall forward the complete record and
supporting documentation to the Village Council for final determination.
      2.    Vote Required. A certificate of appropriateness of design shall be
issued by the board upon a concurring vote of a majority of its members
present. If fewer than two-thirds of the members present vote to grant the
certificate of appropriateness, the certificate shall not be issued until the
time for the notice of appeal provided in subsection (j) hereof has lapsed, or
if an appeal has been taken, until a final decision on the appeal has been
reached by the village council. On matters within the final jurisdiction of the
Village Council, a recommendation shall be issued by the board upon the
concurring vote of a majority of its members present.
SECTION 11: That subsection (i), “Denial of Certificate,” of Section 23.36 of
the Building Code, “Certificate of Appropriateness of Design,” Chapter 23 of
the Winnetka Village Code, is hereby amended to provide as follows:
   (i)    Denial of Certificate.
      1.    Criteria. The board or, where applicable, the Village Council, may
deny a request for a certificate of appropriateness of design only upon
findings that external architectural features of the proposal when completed
will be: (1) so inconsistent with applicable village design guidelines and such
standards and criteria as may be adopted by the board for the review of such
external architectural features and site improvements as to be inappropriate
and incompatible with the character of the immediate neighborhood; or (2) so
inappropriate and incompatible with adopted village plans or public
improvements in the immediate neighborhood as to cause substantial depreciation
in the effectiveness and value of such plans or improvements; or (3) so
inappropriate and incompatible with the existing character of the immediate
neighborhood as to cause substantial depreciation in property values in such
neighborhood.
      2.    Vote Required. The board shall not deny or recommend denial of a
certificate of appropriateness of design except upon the concurring vote of a
majority of its members present. Upon a determination or recommendation by the
board that no certificate should be issued, the board shall so notify the
Director of the board=s findings of fact and the director shall thereupon
either deny the permits applied for if the matter is within the final
jurisdiction of the board or shall forward the recommendation to the Village
Council .
SECTION 12: That subsection (j), “Appeal to Council,” of Section 23.36 of the
Building Code, “Certificate of Appropriateness of Design,” Chapter 23 of the
Winnetka Village Code, is hereby amended to provide as follows:
   (j)    Action by Council. If a certificate of appropriateness of design is
denied by the board, or if it is granted by a concurring majority vote of fewer
than two-thirds of those board members present, the applicant or any person
affected by the board=s decision, may take an appeal to the council. The appeal
shall be requested in writing and shall be submitted to the director within 7
days of the board=s decision denying or granting the certificate.
Notwithstanding the foregoing, the Council shall consider all matters
pertaining to properties located with the C-1 (Limited Retail) Zoning District
established by the Zoning Ordinance, in which case, the record and final
recommendation of the board shall be forwarded to the Council by the director
within 7 days of the board’s recommendation. The council shall render its
decision within 30 days from the date of such written appeal or recommendation
of the board, and its decision in the matter shall be final.
SECTION 13: That Ordinance MC-220-99, adopted February 16, 1999, is hereby
repealed and the moratorium extension imposed pursuant thereto is hereby
terminated.
SECTION 14: That this ordinance shall be in full force and effect from and
after its passage, approval and posting.
   ADOPTED this 16th day of March, 1999, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
AYES:      Trustees Derning, Hilton, Lanphier, Lien and Meuer
NAYS:   None
ABSENT:   Trustee Duhl
Signed:
s/s Louise A. Holland
Village President
Countersigned:
s/s Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: February 2, 1999
Posted: February 3, 1999
Passed and Approved: March 16, 1999
Posted: March 17, 1999
ORDINANCE NO. MC-220-98
AN ORDINANCE EXTENDING A MORATORIUM ON THE ISSUANCE OF CERTAIN BUILDING PERMITS
AND THE PROCESSING OF APPLICATIONS FOR ZONING RELIEF IN THE C-1 COMMERCIAL
ZONING DISTRICT
WHEREAS, on August 18, 1998, the Council of the Village of Winnetka enacted
ordinance MC-210-98, imposing a 6- month moratorium on the processing of
applications for zoning relief, demolition permits, or building permits for new
buildings or building expansions in the C-1 (Limited Retail) Commercial Zoning
Districts; and
WHEREAS, on February 2, 1999, on due notice thereof, the Village Council,
sitting as a Committee of the Whole, conducted a public hearing on proposed
amendments to the C-1 Zoning District regulations and to consider whether the
moratorium imposed by ordinance MC-210-98 should be extended for not more than
30 days; and
WHEREAS, on February 2, 1999, the Village Council introduced for first reading
Ordinance No. MC-220-99 which amends the Zoning Ordinance as it pertains to the
C-1 Zoning District and the enactment of the proposed changes to the zoning
regulations for that district and have determined that it is in the best
interests of the health, safety and welfare of the Village to extend the
moratorium for no more than 30 days in order to allow sufficient time for
Ordinance No. MC-219-99 to be finalized and enacted.
NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain:
SECTION 1: That the foregoing preambles to this ordinance are hereby adopted by
the Council of the Village of Winnetka as its findings, as if fully set forth
herein.
SECTION 2: That the moratorium on development in the C-1 (Limited Retail)
Commercial Zoning District imposed pursuant to ordinance MC-210-98 is hereby
extended for one month, to and including March 18, 1999 and the Department of
Community Development is hereby directed not to process any applications for
zoning relief, demolition permits, or building permits for new buildings or
building expansions in the C-1 (Limited Retail) Commercial Zoning Districts of
the Village during that time.
SECTION 3: That the extension of the moratorium on development in the C-1
(Limited Retail) Commercial Zoning District imposed pursuant to Section 2 of
this ordinance shall expire as of the opening of business on March 19, 1999.
SECTION 4: That this Ordinance shall be in full force and effect from and after
its passage, approval, and posting.
   ADOPTED this 16th day of February, 1999, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
AYES:      Trustees Derning, Duhl, Hilton, Lanphier, Lien and Meuer
NAYS:   None
ABSENT:   None
Signed:
s/s Louise A. Holland
Village President
Countersigned:
s/s Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: February 2, 1999
Posted: February 3, 1999
Passed and Approved: February 16, 1999
Posted: February 17, 1999
ORDINANCE NO. MC-221-99
AN ORDINANCE AMENDING THE BULK REGULATIONS APPLICABLE TO PROPERTIES IN SINGLE-
FAMILY RESIDENTIAL ZONING DISTRICTS
WHEREAS, on February 7, 1989, after considering evidence regarding the
replacement of existing housing stock with new residences that were
significantly larger than the buildings they replaced and that were out of
scale with the surrounding neighborhood, the Council of the Village of Winnetka
enacted Ordinance No. MC-42-89, which established a maximum allowable building
size; and
WHEREAS, the maximum building size limitations established by Ordinance MC-77-
91 used a formula based on the ratio of gross floor area to lot area, known as
“floor area ratio” or “FAR”; and
WHEREAS, on November 5, 1991, after evaluating the maximum building size
limitations established in 1989, the Council of the Village of Winnetka further
reduced the maximum allowable building size when it adopted Ordinance MC-77-91;
and
WHEREAS, since that time, the Council of the Village of Winnetka has
periodically enacted further amendments to the maximum building size
regulations; and
WHEREAS, despite the existence of the maximum building size regulations and the
Council’s continuing review and modification of those regulations, the number
of building demolitions has increased and existing homes continue to be
replaced with new construction that is out of scale and not in character with
the existing neighborhood; and
WHEREAS, as a result of concerns expressed in response to a community
questionnaire circulated by the Village in the course of updating the Village’s
Comprehensive Plan, the Village Council hired Thompson Dyke & Associates, a
planning and consulting firm, to conduct a detailed study of building size in
the A-1 Single-Family Residential Zoning District; and
WHEREAS, on January 12, 1999, Thompson Dyke & Associates submitted an Analysis
of Winnetka Bulk Regulations in the A-1 District and Options to Modifying
Existing Regulations to Preserve the Traditional Character of the Village (the
“TD&A Report”); and
WHEREAS, the Village Council considered the TD&A Report at a study session on
January 12, 1999, directed the Community Development staff to publish notice
for a public hearing on proposed amendments to bulk regulations, and directed
the preparation of a preliminary draft ordinance amending the bulk regulations
in the single family zoning districts; and
WHEREAS, the TD&A Report and issues related to single-family building bulk were
further discussed by the Council at a study session held on January 26, 1999;
and
WHEREAS, on February 2, 1999, on due notice thereof and in accordance with
Section 22.19 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, sitting as a Committee of the Whole, opened a public hearing on
proposed amendments to the bulk regulations applicable to the single-family
zoning districts in the Village; and
WHEREAS, the hearing was continued from February 2, 1999, and reconvened on
March 2, April 6, and April 13, 1999, all pursuant to due notice; and
WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka further discussed issues of
bulk regulations in the single-family zoning districts at its study session on
February 23, 1999, and at its regular meeting on March 16, 1999; and
WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka find that, despite the existing
maximum building size regulations, there has been an increase in building
demolitions and new residences continue to be inconsistent with the scale and
character of the established neighborhoods in which they are located; and
WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka find that the increasing number
of requests for variations from the maximum building size regulations for
rehabilitation of housing constructed before 1989 discloses that the maximum
building size regulations have imposed a greater restriction on existing
housing, due to differences in roof form, and the configuration of both
exterior and interior walls; and
WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka find that the size of building
allowed under the existing maximum building size regulations exceeds the size
allowed in comparable communities in the immediate vicinity and tends to
further encourage demolition of existing buildings and discourage their
renovation, restoration and rehabilitation; and
WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka find that the disparate impact
of the maximum building size regulations encourages the demolition of existing
housing stock and encourages the building of new homes that are too large for
the lots on which they are located, too close to adjoining properties and out
of scale and character with the established neighborhoods in which they are
located; and
WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka find that the disparate impact
of the existing maximum building size regulations exists in all single-family
residential zoning districts; and
WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka find that the replacement of
existing homes with larger new homes results in the damage and removal of
trees, mature landscaping and topsoil, while rehabilitation of existing homes
uses less intensive construction methods; and
WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka find that the new homes
replacing existing homes are able to benefit from all available floor area and
height allowances and bonuses, including increases in building height and floor
area in return for increased side yards, which bonuses are generally
unavailable to homes constructed before there was a codified incentive to
increase side yards; and
WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka find that the new homes in the
smaller zoning districts tend to be characterized by long expanses of walls
which have no articulation except for permitted encroachments into required
side yards, and which have greater wall heights that are coupled with flattened
roof forms in order to maximize interior space while staying within height
limitations; and
WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka find that the size and shape of
the new homes result in a “looming” presence on the street, thereby changing
the street character and reducing the amount of light and air to adjoining
properties; and
WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka find that new homes are also
characterized by front-loading attached garages, which adds impermeable surface
to front yards and changes the character of the street and impacts drainage
patterns; and
WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka find that renovation and
rehabilitation of existing homes maintains existing natural grades and has less
impact on natural drainage patterns and flooding; and
WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka find that the renovation and
rehabilitation of existing homes helps to preserve the historic architectural
fabric of the Village, maintains a range of architectural styles which provides
visual interest and diversity, and provides a range of housing options and
values; and
WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka find that renovation and
rehabilitation of existing homes has less visual impact on neighboring
properties through greater preservation of landscaping and screening; and
WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka find that renovations have less
visual impact on open space, views, natural light and air than does new
construction, because interior renovations have no impact on exterior
appearance, while additions to existing buildings tend to maintain the
appearance and scale of the existing streetscape, because the typical exterior
renovation consists of an addition at the rear of the existing building and
does not exceed the existing building height and width; and
WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka find that the differences in
architectural style and interior layout between homes constructed before 1989
and homes constructed since the implementation of maximum building size
regulations in 1989 requires the use of different formulas for maximum building
size in order to encourage the renovation, restoration and rehabilitation of
structurally sound existing housing while assuring that new construction and
building additions maintain the existing scale, appearance and character of the
community; and
WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka further finds that certain
technical amendments to the Zoning Ordinance are desirable in order to codify
and clarify certain interpretations of the Zoning Ordinance.
NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
SECTION 1:  That the foregoing recitals are adopted by the Council of the
Village of Winnetka as its findings.
SECTION 2 : That Section 22.02, “Objectives,” of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance,
Chapter 22 of the Winnetka Village Code, is hereby amended to provide as
follows:
22. 02   OBJECTIVES.
   (a)    General Objectives. The general objective of this Zoning Ordinance is
to maintain the Village of Winnetka as a developed community primarily
consisting of well-maintained single family residential neighborhoods,
separately located thriving business areas that complement the residential
neighborhoods, in an area with mature trees and green space.
   (b)    Land Use and Development Objectives. It is the purpose of this Zoning
Ordinance to assure that the following land use and development objectives are
accomplished:
      1.    To foster the goals and policies of the Comprehensive Plan of the
Village of Winnetka;
      2.    To establish a rational pattern of land uses and to encourage the
most appropriate use of individual parcels of land within the Village;
      3.    To encourage and promote detached single family homes as the
principal land use in the Village;
      4.    To protect the scale and character of existing neighborhoods from
the encroachment of incompatible uses and structures having excessive bulk or
providing inadequate open space;
      5.    To prohibit uses, buildings or structures that are incompatible
with the character of the zoning district in which they are located;
      6.    To regulate and limit the height and bulk of buildings;
      7.    To establish, regulate and limit building or set back lines on or
along any street, traffic way, drive, parkway or storm or floodwater runoff
channel or basin;
      8.    To regulate and limit the intensity of the use of lot areas and the
area of open spaces within and surrounding buildings;
      9.    To encourage and enhance the preservation of natural resources,
aesthetic amenities and natural features;
      10.    To secure adequate natural light, pure air, privacy, a safe
environment and convenience of access to property;
      11.    To facilitate the most efficient use of existing and planned
public facilities and utilities;
      12.    To protect existing public facilities and utilities from being
overloaded due to excess development or development incompatible with the
capabilities of the Village’s utility systems;
      13.    To protect and enhance a pattern of interconnected streets and
roadways that is unified, integrated, safe, effective and efficient;
      14.    To reduce congestion and promote safety on streets and public ways
by limiting traffic generation through the control of land use intensity;
      15.    To avoid or lessen the hazards of flooding or stormwater
accumulation and runoff;
      16.    To ensure and facilitate the preservation of sites, areas and
structures of historical, architectural and aesthetic importance;
      17.   To assure the development and redevelopment of established
neighborhoods in a manner consistent with their established character, by
encouraging the renovation, restoration and rehabilitation of structurally
sound existing housing, by establishing standards to assure that new
construction and building additions maintain the existing scale and appearance
of the community, and by protecting established trees and landscaping;
      18.    To protect and respect the justifiable reliance of existing
residents, business people and taxpayers on the continuation of existing,
established land use patterns;
      19.    To protect and enhance the taxable value of land and buildings;
      20.    To otherwise promote the public health, safety, comfort, morals
and welfare;
      21.    To define the powers and duties of administrative officers and
bodies necessary to administer this Zoning Ordinance;
      22.    To establish procedures for the efficient and effective use of the
provisions of this Zoning Ordinance;
      23.    To establish standards for the review of applications filed
pursuant to this Zoning Ordinance; and
      24.    To prescribe penalties for the violation of the provisions of this
Zoning Ordinance.
SECTION 3: That Subsection (a) of Section 22.03, “Zoning Districts,” of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Chapter 22 of the Winnetka Village Code, is hereby
amended to provide as follows:
22.03   ZONING DISTRICTS.
   (a)    In furtherance of the principal objectives of this ordinance the
Village is hereby divided into the following 10 districts:
      R-5 SINGLE-FAMILY RESIDENTIAL DISTRICT
      R-4 SINGLE-FAMILY RESIDENTIAL DISTRICT
      R-3 SINGLE-FAMILY RESIDENTIAL DISTRICT
      R-2 SINGLE-FAMILY RESIDENTIAL DISTRICT
      R-1 SINGLE-FAMILY RESIDENTIAL DISTRICT
      B-l MULTI-FAMILY RESIDENTIAL DISTRICT
      B-2 MULTI-FAMILY RESIDENTIAL DISTRICT
      C-1 (LIMITED RETAIL) COMMERCIAL DISTRICT
      C-2 (GENERAL RETAIL) COMMERCIAL DISTRICT
      D LIGHT INDUSTRIAL DISTRICT
SECTION 4: That Section 22.04, “Definitions,” of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance,
Chapter 22 of the Winnetka Village Code, by adding a definition for a new term,
“Attic,” which shall provide as follows:
   Attic: An upper floor that: (a) is located between the eave and ridge lines
of a sloping roof; (b) has an upper floor height of 7 feet or more; and (c) has
a floor area that is no more than 1/3 of the floor area of the story, or part
thereof, lying directly below the entire upper floor in which the attic is
located.
SECTION 5: That the definition of the term, “Basement,” in Section 22.04,
“Definitions,” of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Chapter 22 of the Winnetka
Village Code, is hereby amended to provide as follows:
   Basement: A portion of a building that is located wholly or partly below the
adjoining ground level.
SECTION 6: That the definition of the term, “Buildable Area,” in Section 22.04,
“Definitions,” of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Chapter 22 of the Winnetka
Village Code, is hereby amended to provide as follows:
   Buildable Area. That portion of a zoning lot remaining after required yards
and setbacks have been provided and within which principal and accessory
buildings may, subject to all applicable requirements, be located.
SECTION 7: That Section 22.04, “Definitions,” of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance,
Chapter 22 of the Winnetka Village Code, is hereby amended by adding a
definition for a new term, “Corner Lot Line,” which shall provide as follows:
   Corner Lot Line: The longest street line of a corner lot.
SECTION 8: That Section 22.04, “Definitions,” of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance,
Chapter 22 of the Winnetka Village Code, is hereby amended by adding a
definition for a new term, “Corner Yard,” which shall provide as follows:
   Corner Yard: A yard extending along the full length of a corner lot line and
back to a line drawn parallel to the corner lot line, at a distance therefrom
equal to the depth of the required set back (including, where applicable, the
average set back, but excluding any area included within a front yard.
SECTION 9: That the definition of the term “Floor Area, Gross,” in
Section 22.04 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Chapter 22 of the Winnetka
Village Code, is hereby amended to provide as follows:
   Floor Area, Gross: The total square footage of all stories and floors of all
buildings and enclosed structures on a zoning lot, measured from the exterior
face of the outside walls, regardless of the material of which the outside
walls are made, and without deduction for such interior features as hallways,
stairs, closets, thickness of walls, columns, trusses, cross-ties, elevator
shafts and chimneys, subject to the following:
   (a)   Gross floor area shall not include the area of any part of an upper
floor that has an upper floor height of less than 7 feet.
   (b) Gross Floor Area shall include, without limitation:
      (1)    all areas of a building or structure that have exterior walls that
extend more than 3.5 feet above the floor on two or more sides of such area;
      (2)    the area of all screened or enclosed porches, regardless of
whether the screens or enclosure are permanently affixed;
      (3)    the area of any porch, whether open or enclosed, if it is on the
first floor of a building and has a ceiling formed by the floor of a porch or
any other portion of the building above it;
      (4)    the area of each floor level below a roofed porch or other
cantilevered structural feature located above the first floor level of a multi-
story building or structure; and
      (5)    the area of each floor below a roof that is supported by columns
and is located above the first floor level of a multi-story building or
structure.
SECTION 10 : That the definition of the term, “Front Lot Line,” in Section
22.04, “Definitions,” of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Chapter 22 of the
Winnetka Village Code, is hereby amended to provide as follows:
   Front Lot Line: The front street line. The shortest street line of a corner
lot shall be the front lot line. On through lots, both street lines shall be
deemed front lot lines, except where no set back requirement is applicable. For
lots abutting Lake Michigan, the water’s edge of the lake shall be deemed a
front lot line.
SECTION 11: That the definition of the term, “Front Yard,” in Section 22.04,
“Definitions,” of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Chapter 22 of the Winnetka
Village Code, is hereby amended to provide as follows:
   Front Yard: A yard other than a corner yard, extending along the full length
of a front lot line, or other street line, and back to a line drawn parallel to
that front lot line, or other street line, at a distance therefrom equal to the
depth of the required set back (including, where applicable, the average set
back).
SECTION 12 : That the definition of the term, “Half-Story,” in Section 22.04,
“Definitions,” of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Chapter 22 of the Winnetka
Village Code, is hereby amended to provide as follows:
   Half-Story: An upper floor of a building that (a) is located between the
eave and ridge lines of a sloping roof; (b) has an upper floor height of 7 feet
or more; and (c) has a floor area that is more than one-third (1/3),but no more
than one-half (1/2) of the floor area of the story or part thereof, lying
directly below the upper floor in which the half-story is located.
SECTION 13 : That “Height of Building,” in Section 22.04, “Definitions,” of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Chapter 22 of the Winnetka Village Code, is hereby
amended by changing the term to “Height, Building” and the definition of that
term is hereby amended to provide as follows:
   Height, Building: The vertical distance measured from the average natural
grade to the highest point of the building for flat roofs, to the deck line for
mansard roofs, and to the mid-point between the eaves and ridge for gable and
hip roofs. The average natural grade shall be determined by taking the average
of the highest and lowest points of the natural ground level adjoining the
buildings walls.
   For purposes of this definition, the following principles shall apply:
      (1)    The height of a chimney or chimneys shall not be included in
measuring the height of a building.
      (2)    The height of dormers shall not be included in measuring the
height of a building unless the ridge of the dormer is higher than the ridge of
the building roof.
SECTION 14: That Section 22.04, “Definitions,” of the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance, Chapter 22 of the Winnetka Village Code, by adding a definition for
a new term, “Height, Upper Floor,” which shall provide as follows:
   Height, Upper Floor: The vertical distance between (a) the lowest ceiling
joists of the floor directly below an upper floor, and (b) either the bottom of
the roof rafters above the upper floor or a point no more than 12 inches below
the top of the roof rafters, whichever is higher, notwithstanding the presence
of trusses, cross-ties or other interior building components. Notwithstanding
the foregoing, the upper floor height shall not include the vertical distance
under the roof of any dormer that is no more than 6 feet wide and is set in at
least 3.5 feet from the gable end walls, but only if the total width of all
dormers does not exceed 25% of the length of the roof on which they are
located.
SECTION 15 : That “Line of Building,” in Section 22.04, “Definitions,” of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Chapter 22 of the Winnetka Village Code, is hereby
amended by changing the term to “Building Line” and by amending the definition
to provide as follows:
   Building Line: The boundary of any side of a building or projection
therefrom at its outermost vertical plane, excluding only such parts of the
building as are located in and permitted as obstructions in one or more
required yards under section 22.15.
SECTION 16: That the definition of the term, “Lot; Zoning Lot,” in Section
22.04, “Definitions,” of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Chapter 22 of the
Winnetka Village Code, is hereby amended to provide as follows:
   Lot; Zoning Lot: A lot of record, or a combination of contiguous lots of
record under common ownership or control, used or to be used by the owner or
occupant thereof for a single principal building, except to the extent that
this ordinance expressly permits such lot or lots to be used for more than one
principal building or principal use. As used in this ordinance, the term “lot”
or “zoning lot” shall not include any part of a platted lot that lies beyond
the water’s edge of Lake Michigan or within the boundaries of any other river,
lake or stream that is under the ownership and control of the State of
Illinois.
SECTION 17 : That the definition of the term, “Lot Area,” in Section 22.04,
“Definitions,” of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Chapter 22 of the Winnetka
Village Code, is hereby amended to provide as follows:
   Lot Area: The area of any lot within its exterior lot lines, not including
any area lying within a street. As used in this ordinance, the term “lot area”
shall not include any part of a platted lot that lies beyond the water’s edge
of Lake Michigan or within the boundaries of any other river, lake or stream
that is under the ownership and control of the State of Illinois.
SECTION 18: That the definition of the term, “Maximum Depth of Lot,” in Section
22.04, “Definitions,” of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Chapter 22 of the
Winnetka Village Code, is hereby amended to provide as follows:
   Maximum Depth of Lot: The distance from the front base line of the lot to
the farthest point of the lot therefrom, measured along a line perpendicular to
the front base line, or its extension. The front base line of a lot shall be a
line passing through the 2 termini of the front lot line. On through lots
abutting Lake Michigan, the front base line shall be a line passing through the
two termini of the front lot line opposite the water’s edge, and the maximum
depth of lot shall be no greater than the distance from the front base line of
the lot to the water’s edge of Lake Michigan.
SECTION 19: That the definition of the term, “Public Street,” in Section 22.04,
“Definitions,” of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Chapter 22 of the Winnetka
Village Code, is hereby amended to provide as follows:
   Public Street: The area lying within the described limits of a right of-way
or thoroughfare dedicated for vehicular traffic (excluding an alley), whether
or not so used. For purposes of this Zoning Ordinance, Lake Michigan shall be
considered a public street.
SECTION 20: That the definition of the term, “Rear Lot Line,” in Section 22.04,
“Definitions,” of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Chapter 22 of the Winnetka
Village Code, is hereby amended to provide as follows:
   Rear Lot Line: The lot line that is most distant from and that is, or is
most nearly, parallel to the front lot line; provided, that there shall be no
rear lot line on a through lot.
SECTION 21: That the definition of the term, “Rear Yard,” in Section 22.04,
“Definitions,” of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Chapter 22 of the Winnetka
Village Code, is hereby amended to provide as follows:
   Rear Yard: A yard extending along the full length of the rear lot line
between that line and a line drawn parallel to the rear lot line at a distance
therefrom equal to the required depth of such yard. For purposes of this
ordinance, there shall be no rear yard on any through lot.
SECTION 22: That Section 22.04, “Definitions,” of the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance, Chapter 22 of the Winnetka Village Code, is hereby amended by adding
a definition for a new term, “Rehabilitation,” which shall provide as follows:
   Rehabilitation: The act or process of making possible the efficient
contemporary use of a building through repair, alterations or additions, while
preserving those portions or features that convey its historical or
architectural values and while maintaining the character of the property, its
neighborhood and environment.
SECTION 23: That the definition of the term, “Story,” in Section 22.04,
“Definitions,” of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Chapter 22 of the Winnetka
Village Code, is hereby amended to provide as follows:
   Story: That portion of a building included between the surface of any floor
and the surface of the floor next above it or, in the case of an upper floor,
between the surface of the upper floor and the bottom of the roof deck,
provided the upper floor has a floor area that is more than ½ of the floor area
of the part of the story, or part thereof, lying directly below the upper
floor.
   For purposes of this definition, the following principles shall apply:
      (1)    The floor of a story may split levels, provided that there is not
more than four feet difference in elevation between the different levels.
      (2)    Any balcony, mezzanine, partial floor or open-beamed ceiling that
does not extend horizontally to fill the perimeter of the building shall be
deemed to be a full floor or ceiling at that level.
      (3)    Any area of a building in which the distance from one floor to the
floor or roof rafters above it is more than 14 feet, and which is uninterrupted
by a balcony, mezzanine, partial floor or open-beamed ceiling, shall be deemed
to consist of one story for each 14 feet of height or fraction thereof.
      (4)    A basement that has an average height above grade of more than 4
feet, measured to the bottom of the beams of the floor above, shall be deemed
to be a full story.
SECTION 24: That the definition of the term, “Street Line,” in Section 22.04,
“Definitions,” of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Chapter 22 of the Winnetka
Village Code, is hereby amended to provide as follows:
   Street Line: The outermost limits of a public or private street. The water’s
edge of Lake Michigan shall be deemed the outermost limits of that public
street.
SECTION 25: That Section 22.04, “Definitions,” of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance
of Chapter 22 of the Winnetka Village Code, is hereby amended by deleting the
defined term, “Sub-basement.”
SECTION 26: That Section 22.04, “Definitions,” of the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance, Chapter 22 of the Winnetka Village Code, is hereby amended by adding
a definition for a new term, “Upper Floor,” which shall provide as follows:
   Upper Floor: That portion of a building that is located directly below and
adjacent to the roof. An upper floor may also be an attic, half-story or story,
depending on its height and floor area.
SECTION 27 : That Section 22.05 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, “A-1” (One-
Sixth Acre) Single-Family Residential District Regulations, Chapter 22 of the
Winnetka Village Code, is hereby amended by changing its title to “R-5 Single-
Family Residential District.”
SECTION 28: That subsection (a) “District Purpose,” of Section 22.05 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, “R-5 Single-Family Residential District
Regulations,” is hereby amended to provide as follows:
22.05   R-5 SINGLE-FAMILY RESIDENTIAL DISTRICT REGULATIONS.
   (a)    District Purpose. The requirements set forth herein, together with
other provisions set forth in the building code of the village, have been
adopted in order to provide for a single-family residential environment of
relatively intense suburban character, wherein the degree of improvements upon
an individual parcel of land is in approximate balance with the landscape area
of such parcel of land. Due to the mature character of development within this
district, such requirements are also intended to assure that future
modifications to existing structures, or the replacement of existing structures
with new structures, will not alter the scale and general character of
established neighborhoods. In this regard, the requirements set forth herein
are further intended to foster development which is compatible, in general,
with the character of existing single-family development within the immediate
neighborhood with respect to external architectural scale, landscaping and
other site improvements.
SECTION 29 : That subsection (c), “Height,” of Section 22.05 of the Winnetka
Zoning Ordinance, R-5 Single-Family Residential District Regulations, Chapter
22 of the Winnetka Village Code, is hereby amended to provide as follows:
   (c)    Height. No building shall be more than 35 feet in building height,
nor more than 2- 1/2 stories high, and no structure that is not a building
shall exceed 35 feet in height, subject to the following additional
limitations:
      1.    No public building, church or temple, or school shall exceed a
building height of up to 60 feet.
      2.    No storage shed, playhouse, walled enclosure for refuse containers
or swimming pool equipment, or any similar structure, shall exceed a building
height of 7 feet.
      3.    No other accessory building shall exceed a building height of 14
feet or one-story.
      4.    If a structure is attached to or supported by a building, its
height, together with that of the building to which it is attached or
supported, shall not exceed the building height limitation applicable to that
building.
SECTION 30: That subsection (f), “Intensity of Use of Lot,” of Section 22.05 of
the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, R-5 Single-Family Residential District
Regulations, is hereby amended to provide as follows:
   (f)   Intensity of Use of Lot. No principal building with its accessory
buildings shall occupy in excess of 25 percent of the lot area, nor shall more
than 50 percent of the lot area be covered by impermeable surfaces, including
all buildings, except that up to 275 square feet of the roofed area of a porch
that either extends along the front of the principal building or extends
continuously along the front and any part of the side of the principal
building, may be excluded from the total square footage of the roofed lot
coverage and the impermeable surfaces.
SECTION 31: That subsection (g), “Maximum Building Size,” of Section 22.05 of
the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, R-5 Single-Family Residential District
Regulations is hereby amended to provide as follows:
   (g)    Maximum Building Size. No building, other enclosed structure or
addition thereto shall be constructed on a lot unless the aggregate size of the
principal building with all accessory buildings and similar enclosed structures
does not exceed the gross floor area (GFA) and floor area ratio (FAR)
limitations established pursuant to this subsection.
      1.   Formula for Calculating Maximum Gross Floor Area (GFA). The maximum
(GFA) shall be calculated by applying the following formulas:
         a.    Except as provided in subparagraph c, below, for any lot with a
principal building that was lawfully in existence on or before February 7,
1989, the maximum GFA formula shall be:
Lot Area (“LA”)   Formula for Maximum GFA
Up to and including 9,075   .40 x LA
Over 9,075, to and including 16,000   3.630 + [(LA - 9,075) x 0.2]
Over 16,000 to and including 22,000   3,630 + [(LA - 9,075) x 0.2] + ({'{'}[
(LA-16,000)/1,000] x 0.005{'}'} x LA)
Over 22,000   3,630 + [(LA – 9,075) x 0.2] + (0.03 x LA)
         b.    For any lot with a principal building that was not lawfully in
existence on or before February 7, 1989, or that was constructed since February
7, 1989, the FAR formula shall be:
Lot Area   Formula for Maximum GFA
Up to and including 9,075   .38 x LA
Over 9,075, to and including 12,000   3.630 + [(LA - 9,075) x 0.2] – (0.02 x
LA)
Over 12,000 to and including 16,000   3.630 + [(LA - 9,075) x 0.2] – (0.02 x
LA) + ({'{'}[(LA-12,000)/1,000] x 0.005{'}'} x LA)
Over 16,000 to and including 22,000   3,630 + [(LA - 9,075) x 0.2] + ({'{'}[
(LA-16,000)/1,000] x 0.005{'}'} x LA)
Over 22,000   3,630 + [(LA – 9,075) x 0.2] + (0.03 x LA)
         c.    The FAR formula set forth in the foregoing subparagraph b shall
also be applied to any lot with a principal building that has been, or that is
proposed to be, the subject of any work that exceeds the scope of
rehabilitation and ordinary repair and maintenance, regardless of whether the
original principal building was lawfully in existence on or before February 7,
1989.
      2.    Flag Lots. The maximum building size for flag lots shall be
computed by using a lot area that excludes the “flagpole” portion of the lot,
provided that, if the flag lot was lawfully in existence on October 7, 1997,
the resulting maximum building size shall not be less than 85% of the maximum
building size computed by using a lot area that includes both the “flagpole”
and “flag” portions of the lot.
      3.    Basements. The floor area of basements shall not be included in
determining the maximum building size, except to the following extent:
         a.    The gross floor area of basements that were lawfully in
existence on or before February 7, 1989, and that have exposed exterior walls
more than 4.0 feet above grade, shall be calculated by multiplying the gross
floor area within the exterior face of the outside walls of the basement by the
ratio of the number of linear feet of the outside wall of the basement more
than 4.0 feet above grade to the total outside perimeter of the basement.
         b.    The gross floor area of basements that were not lawfully in
existence on or before February 7, 1989, or that were constructed since
February 7, 1989, and that have walls more than 3 feet above grade, shall be
calculated by multiplying the gross floor area within the exterior face of the
outside walls of the basement by the ratio of the number of linear feet of the
outside wall of the basement more than 3 feet above grade to the total outside
perimeter of the basement.
      4.    Exclusions. The following floor areas shall be excluded from the
maximum building size;
         a.    An amount of garage floor area equal to one of the following:
            (i)    The first 400 square feet of the floor area of a one-story
detached garage located in the rear quarter of the lot; or
            (ii)    The first 200 square feet of the floor area of an attached
garage located in the rear of a house, provided that no part of the garage
forms any part of the front building line or the building line exposed to a
corner lot line;
         b.    Up to 150 square feet or the equivalent of 1 percent (.01) of
the lot area, whichever is greater, of the floor area of an attic or half-
story;
         c.    Up to 64 square feet of the aggregate floor area of all bay
windows and chimneys that form part of the exterior building line;
         d.     The floor area under any dormer that is no more than 6 feet
wide and is set in at least 3.5 feet from the gable end walls, provided that
the total width of all dormers does not exceed 25% of the length of the roof on
which they area located.
         e.    The first 50 square feet of the aggregate floor area of a
detached storage shed, playhouse, walled enclosures for refuse containers or
swimming pool equipment, or similar enclosed structures, provided they are
located in the rear quarter of the lot.
      5.    Notwithstanding the foregoing, any zoning lot consisting of a
single, legal, nonconforming lot that has a lot area of less than 7,260 square
feet as the result of the acquisition or condemnation of a portion of the rear
yard of the lot for a public purpose, shall be entitled to an additional 400
square feet of gross floor area on the lot, provided that such additional floor
area permitted by this paragraph shall be in lieu of, and not in addition to,
the additional floor area permitted pursuant to subparagraph 4a of this
subsection.
      6.    Maximum FAR. Notwithstanding the allowances and exclusions
permitted in the foregoing paragraphs, under no circumstances shall the
resulting FAR for any lot exceed 0.50 (50%).
SECTION 32 : That subsection (h), “Set Back,” of Section 22.05 of the Winnetka
Zoning Ordinance, R-5 Single-Family Residential District Regulations, is hereby
amended by changing the second paragraph to read as follows:
   On corner lots, there shall be a minimum corner yard set back equal to the
set back established in the preceding paragraph, except that this regulation
shall not reduce the width of the building area as measured from the minimum
required side yard to less than 60 percent of the average width of the lot, and
shall not reduce the length of the buildable area as measured from the minimum
required rear yard to less than 50 percent of the maximum depth of the lot.
SECTION 33 : That subsection (i), “Side Yard,” of Section 22.05 of the Winnetka
Zoning Ordinance, R-5 Single Family Residential District Regulations, Chapter
22 of the Winnetka Village Code, is hereby amended to provide as follows:
   (i)    Side Yard. There shall be a side yard on each side of the lot, one
side yard having a width of not less than 6 feet and the other having a width
of not less than 8 feet, provided that the sum of the width of the two side
yards shall not be less than 20 percent of the average width of the lot.
SECTION 34 : That subsection (o), “Front-facing Garage Doors,”, of Section
22.05 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, R-5 Single-Family Residential District
Regulations is hereby amended by changing the title of the subsection to
“Garages,” and amending the text to provide as follows:
   (o)    Garages.
      1.   Front-facing Garage Doors. Garage door widths may not exceed 33
percent of the lot width or 18 feet, whichever is less, provided that no
individual door shall extend more than 9 feet when facing the front yard. (MC-
160-96) (08/16/96)
      2.   Width of Attached Garages. No attached garage with garage doors that
face a front yard or corner yard shall exceed the greater of 50 percent of the
front building line or 22 feet.
      3.   Location of Attached Garages. An attached garage shall be permitted
as the lowest story of the principal building provided that the garage doors
are not a part of the front building elevation of the principal building.
      4.   Garages on Corner Lots. Any garage located on a corner lot shall be
set back at least 20 feet from the corner lot line.
SECTION 35: That Section 22.05 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, R-5 Single-
Family Residential District Regulations, Chapter 22 of the Winnetka Village
Code, is hereby amended by adding a new subsection (q), which shall be
entitled, “Building Line Articulation,” and shall provide as follows:
   (q)    Building Line Articulation. Any side building line that is located
along the smaller side yard and is more than 40 feet in length shall be
articulated by at least 2 feet, beginning at a point no more than 30 feet from
the front building line and extending for the remainder of the length of the
building. If the distance between the side lot line and the side building line
along the smaller side yard exceeds the required side yard by two feet or more,
then the articulation may be toward either the side lot line or the interior of
the lot. In all other instances, the articulation shall be towards the interior
of the lot. No encroachments into the side yard, whether permitted or
otherwise, shall be considered articulation of the side building line for
purposes of this subsection.
SECTION 36: That Section 22.05A of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, “A-5” Single-
Family Residential District Regulations, Chapter 22 of the Winnetka Village
Code is hereby amended by changing its title to “R-4 Single-Family Residential
District.”
SECTION 37: That subsection (a) “District Purpose,” of Section 22.05A of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, R-4 Single-Family Residential District Regulations,
Chapter 22 of the Winnetka Village Code is hereby amended to provide as
follows:
22.05A   R-4 SINGLE-FAMILY RESIDENTIAL DISTRICT REGULATIONS.
   (a)    District Purpose. The requirements set forth herein, together with
other provisions set forth in the building code of the village, have been
adopted in order to provide for a single-family residential environment of
relatively intense suburban character, wherein the degree of improvements upon
an individual parcel of land is in approximate balance with the landscape area
of such parcel of land. Due to the mature character of development within this
district, such requirements are also intended to assure that future
modifications to existing structures, or the replacement of existing structures
with new structures, will not alter the scale and general character of
established neighborhoods. In this regard, the requirements set forth herein
are further intended to foster development which is compatible, in general,
with the character of existing single-family development within the immediate
neighborhood with respect to external architectural scale landscaping and other
site improvements.
SECTION 38 : That subsection (c), “Height,” of Section 22.05A of the Winnetka
Zoning Ordinance, R-4 Single-Family Residential District Regulations, Chapter
22 of the Winnetka Village Code, is hereby amended to provide as follows:
   (c)    Height. No building shall be more than 35 feet in building height,
nor more than 2- 1/2 stories high, and no structure that is not a building
shall exceed 35 feet in height, subject to the following additional
limitations:
      1.    No public building, church or temple, or school shall exceed a
building height of up to 60 feet.
      2.    No storage shed, playhouse, walled enclosure for refuse containers
or swimming pool equipment, or any similar structure, shall exceed a building
height of 7 feet.
      3.    No other accessory building shall exceed a building height of 14
feet or one-story.
      4.    If a structure is attached to or supported by a building, its
height, together with that of the building to which it is attached or
supported, shall not exceed the building height limitation applicable to that
building.
SECTION 39 : That subsection (f), “Intensity of Use of Lot,” of Section 22.05A
of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, R-4 Single-Family Residential District
Regulations, Chapter 22 of the Winnetka Village Code, is hereby amended to
provide as follows:
   (f)    Intensity of Use of Lot. No principal building with its accessory
buildings shall occupy in excess of 25 percent of the lot area, nor shall more
than 50 percent of the lot area be covered by impermeable surfaces, including
all buildings, except that up to 275 square feet of the roofed area of a porch
that either extends along the front of the principal building or extends
continuously along the front and any part of the side of the principal
building, may be excluded from the total square footage of the roofed lot
coverage and the impermeable surfaces.
SECTION 40: That subsection (g), “Maximum Building Size,” of Section 22.05A of
the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, R-4 Single-Family Residential District
Regulations, Chapter 22 of the Winnetka Village Code, is hereby amended to
provide as follows:
   (g)    Maximum Building Size. No building, other enclosed structure or
addition thereto shall be constructed on a lot unless the aggregate size of the
principal building with all accessory buildings and similar enclosed structures
does not exceed the gross floor area (GFA) and floor area ratio (FAR)
limitations established pursuant to this subsection.
      1.   Formula for Calculating Maximum Gross Floor Area (GFA). The maximum
(GFA) shall be calculated by applying the following formulas:
         a.    Except as provided in subparagraph c, below, for any lot with a
principal building that was lawfully in existence on or before February 7,
1989, the maximum GFA formula shall be:
Lot Area (“LA”)   Formula for Maximum GFA
Up to and including 9,075   .40 x LA
Over 9,075, to and including 16,000   3.630 + [(LA - 9,075) x 0.2]
Over 16,000 to and including 22,000   3,630 + [(LA - 9,075) x 0.2] + ({'{'}[
(LA-16,000)/1,000] x 0.005{'}'} x LA)
Over 22,000   3,630 + [(LA – 9,075) x 0.2] + (0.03 x LA)
         b.    For any lot with a principal building that was not lawfully in
existence on or before February 7, 1989, or that was constructed since February
7, 1989, the FAR formula shall be:
Lot Area   Formula for Maximum GFA
Up to and including 9,075   .38 x LA
Over 9,075, to and including 12,000   3.630 + [(LA - 9,075) x 0.2] – (0.02 x
LA)
Over 12,000 to and including 16,000   3.630 + [(LA - 9,075) x 0.2] – (0.02 x
LA) + ({'{'}[(LA-12,000)/1,000] x 0.005{'}'} x LA)
Over 16,000 to and including 22,000   3,630 + [(LA - 9,075) x 0.2] + ({'{'}[
(LA-16,000)/1,000] x 0.005{'}'} x LA)
Over 22,000   3,630 + [(LA – 9,075) x 0.2] + (0.03 x LA)
         c.    The FAR formula set forth in the foregoing subparagraph b shall
also be applied to any lot with a principal building that has been, or that is
proposed to be, the subject of any work that exceeds the scope of
rehabilitation and ordinary repair and maintenance, regardless of whether the
original principal building was lawfully in existence on or before February 7,
1989.
      2.    Flag Lots. The maximum building size for flag lots shall be
computed by using a lot area that excludes the “flagpole” portion of the lot,
provided that, if the flag lot was lawfully in existence on October 7, 1997,
the resulting maximum building size shall not be less than 85% of the maximum
building size computed by using a lot area that includes both the “flagpole”
and “flag” portions of the lot.
      3.    Basements. The floor area of basements shall not be included in
determining the maximum building size, except to the following extent:
         a.    The gross floor area of basements that were lawfully in
existence on or before February 7, 1989, and that have exposed exterior walls
more than 4.0 feet above grade, shall be calculated by multiplying the gross
floor area within the exterior face of the outside walls of the basement by the
ratio of the number of linear feet of the outside wall of the basement more
than 4.0 feet above grade to the total outside perimeter of the basement.
         b.    The gross floor area of basements that were not lawfully in
existence on or before February 7, 1989, or that were constructed since
February 7, 1989, and that have walls more than 3 feet above grade, shall be
calculated by multiplying the gross floor area within the exterior face of the
outside walls of the basement by the ratio of the number of linear feet of the
outside wall of the basement more than 3 feet above grade to the total outside
perimeter of the basement.
      4.    Exclusions. The following floor areas shall be excluded from the
maximum building size;
         a.    An amount of garage floor area equal to one of the following:
            (i)    The first 400 square feet of the floor area of a one-story
detached garage located in the rear quarter of the lot; or
            (ii)    The first 200 square feet of the floor area of an attached
garage located in the rear of a house, provided that no part of the garage
forms any part of the front building line or the building line exposed to a
corner lot line;
         b.    Up to 150 square feet or the equivalent of 1 percent (.01) of
the lot area, whichever is greater, of the floor area of an attic or half-
story;
         c.    Up to 64 square feet of the aggregate floor area of all bay
windows and chimneys that form part of the exterior building line;
         d.    The floor area under any dormer that is no more than 6 feet wide
and is set in at least 3.5 feet from the gable end walls, provided that the
total width of all dormers does not exceed 25% of the length of the roof on
which they area located.
         e.    The first 50 square feet of the aggregate floor area of a
detached storage shed, playhouse, walled enclosures for refuse containers or
swimming pool equipment, or similar enclosed structures, provided they are
located in the rear quarter of the lot.
      5.    Notwithstanding the foregoing, any zoning lot consisting of a
single, legal, nonconforming lot that has a lot area of less than 7,260 square
feet as the result of the acquisition or condemnation of a portion of the rear
yard of the lot for a public purpose, shall be entitled to an additional 400
square feet of gross floor area on the lot, provided that such additional floor
area permitted by this paragraph shall be in lieu of, and not in addition to,
the additional floor area permitted pursuant to subparagraph 4a of this
subsection.
      6.    Maximum FAR. Notwithstanding the allowances and exclusions
permitted in the foregoing paragraphs, under no circumstances shall the
resulting FAR for any lot exceed 0.50 (50%).
SECTION 41: That subsection (h), “Set Back,” of Section 22.05A of the Winnetka
Zoning Ordinance, R-4 Single-Family Residential District Regulations, is hereby
amended by changing the second paragraph to read as follows:
   On corner lots, there shall be a minimum corner yard set back equal to the
set back established in the preceding paragraph, except that this regulation
shall not reduce the width of the building area as measured from the minimum
required side yard to less than 60 percent of the average width of the lot, and
shall not reduce the length of the buildable area as measured from the minimum
required rear yard to less than 50 percent of the maximum depth of the lot.
SECTION 42: That subsection (i), “Side Yard,” of Section 22.05A of the Winnetka
Zoning Ordinance, R-4 Single Family Residential District Regulations, Chapter
22 of the Winnetka Village Code, is hereby amended to provide as follows:
   (i)    Side Yard. There shall be a side yard on each side of the lot, one
side yard having a width of not less than 6 feet and the other having a width
of not less than 8 feet, provided that the sum of the width of the two side
yards shall not be less than 20 percent of the average width of the lot.
SECTION 43 : That subsection (o), “Front-facing Garage Doors,”, of Section
22.05A of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, R-4 Single-Family Residential District
Regulations, Chapter 22 of the Winnetka Village Code, is hereby amended by
changing the title of the subsection to “Garages,” and amending the text to
provide as follows:
   (o)    Garages.
      1.   Front-facing Garage Doors. Garage door widths may not exceed 33
percent of the lot width or 18 feet, whichever is less, provided that no
individual door shall extend more than 9 feet when facing the front yard. (MC-
160-96) (8/16/96)
      2.   Width of Attached Garages. No attached garage with garage doors that
face a front yard or corner yard shall exceed the greater of 50 percent of the
front building line or 22 feet.
      3.   Location of Attached Garages. An attached garage shall be permitted
as the lowest story of the principal building provided that the garage doors
are not a part of the front building elevation of the principal building.
      4.   Garages on Corner Lots. Any garage located on a corner lot shall be
set back at least 20 feet from the corner lot line.
SECTION 44: That Section 22.05A of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, R-4 Single-
Family Residential District Regulations, Chapter 22 of the Winnetka Village
Code, is hereby amended by adding a new subsection (q), which shall be
entitled, “Building Line Articulation,” and shall provide as follows:
   (q)    Building Line Articulation. Any side building line that is located
along the smaller side yard and is more than 40 feet in length shall be
articulated by at least 2 feet, beginning at a point no more than 30 feet from
the front building line and extending for the remainder of the length of the
building. If the distance between the side lot line and the side building line
along the smaller side yard exceeds the required side yard by two feet or more,
then the articulation may be toward either the side lot line or the interior of
the lot. In all other instances, the articulation shall be towards the interior
of the lot. No encroachments into the side yard, whether permitted or
otherwise, shall be considered articulation of the side building line for
purposes of this subsection.
SECTION 45: That Section 22.06 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, “A-2” Single-
Family Residential District Regulations, Chapter 22 of the Winnetka Village
Code, is hereby amended by changing its title to “R-3 Single Family Residential
District.”
SECTION 46 : That subsection (a), “District Purpose,” of Section 22.06 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, R-3 Single-Family Residential District Regulations,
Chapter 22 of the Winnetka Village Code, is hereby amended to provide as
follows:
22.06   R-3 SINGLE-FAMILY RESIDENTIAL DISTRICT REGULATIONS.
   (a)    District Purpose. The requirements set forth herein, together with
other provisions set forth in the building code of the village, have been
adopted in order to provide for a single-family residential environment of a
moderately intense suburban character, wherein the degree of improvements upon
an individual parcel of land is generally subordinate to the landscape. Due to
the mature character of development within this district, such requirements are
also intended to assure that future modifications to existing structures, or
the replacement of existing structures with new structures, will not alter the
scale and general character of established neighborhoods. In this regard, the
requirements set forth herein are further intended to foster development which
is compatible, in general, with the character of existing single-family
development within the immediate neighborhood with respect to external
architectural scale, landscaping and other site improvements.
SECTION 47 : That subsection (c), “Height,” of Section 22.06 of the Winnetka
Zoning Ordinance, R-3 Single-Family Residential District Regulations, Chapter
22 of the Winnetka Village Code, is hereby amended to provide as follows:
   (c)    Height. No building shall be more than 35 feet in building height,
nor more than 2- 1/2 stories high, and no structure that is not a building
shall exceed 35 feet in height, subject to the following additional
limitations:
      1.    No public building, church or temple, or school shall exceed a
building height of up to 60 feet.
      2.    No storage shed, playhouse, walled enclosure for refuse containers
or swimming pool equipment, or any similar structure, shall exceed a building
height of 7 feet.
      3.    No other accessory building shall exceed a building height of 14
feet or one-story.
      4.    If a structure is attached to or supported by a building, its
height, together with that of the building to which it is attached or
supported, shall not exceed the building height limitation applicable to that
building.
SECTION 48: That subsection (g), “Maximum Building Size,” of Section 22.06 of
the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, R-3 Single-Family Residential District
Regulations, Chapter 22 of the Winnetka Village Code, is hereby amended to
provide as follows:
   (g)    Maximum Building Size. No building, other enclosed structure or
addition thereto shall be constructed on a lot unless the aggregate size of the
principal building with all accessory buildings and similar enclosed structures
does not exceed the gross floor area (GFA) and floor area ratio (FAR)
limitations established pursuant to this subsection.
      1.   Formula for Calculating Maximum Gross Floor Area (GFA). The maximum
(GFA) shall be calculated by applying the following formulas:
         a.    Except as provided in subparagraph c, below, for any lot with a
principal building that was lawfully in existence on or before February 7,
1989, the maximum GFA formula shall be:
Lot Area (“LA”)   Formula for Maximum GFA
Up to and including 9,075   .40 x LA
Over 9,075, to and including 16,000   3.630 + [(LA - 9,075) x 0.2]
Over 16,000 to and including 22,000   3,630 + [(LA - 9,075) x 0.2] + ({'{'}[
(LA-16,000)/1,000] x 0.005{'}'} x LA)
Over 22,000   3,630 + [(LA – 9,075) x 0.2] + (0.03 x LA)
         b.    For any lot with a principal building that was not lawfully in
existence on or before February 7, 1989, or that was constructed since February
7, 1989, the FAR formula shall be:
Lot Area   Formula for Maximum GFA
Up to and including 9,075   .38 x LA
Over 9,075, to and including 12,000   3.630 + [(LA - 9,075) x 0.2] – (0.02 x
LA)
Over 12,000 to and including 16,000   3.630 + [(LA - 9,075) x 0.2] – (0.02 x
LA) + ({'{'}[(LA-12,000)/1,000] x 0.005{'}'} x LA)
Over 16,000 to and including 22,000   3,630 + [(LA - 9,075) x 0.2] + ({'{'}[
(LA-16,000)/1,000] x 0.005{'}'} x LA)
Over 22,000   3,630 + [(LA – 9,075) x 0.2] + (0.03 x LA)
         c.    The FAR formula set forth in the foregoing subparagraph b shall
also be applied to any lot with a principal building that has been, or that is
proposed to be. the subject of any work that exceeds the scope of
rehabilitation and ordinary repair and maintenance, regardless of whether the
original principal building was lawfully in existence on or before February 7,
1989.
      2.    Flag Lots. The maximum building size for flag lots shall be
computed by using a lot area that excludes the “flagpole” portion of the lot,
provided that, if the flag lot was lawfully in existence on October 7, 1997,
the resulting maximum building size shall not be less than 85% of the maximum
building size computed by using a lot area that includes both the “flagpole”
and “flag” portions of the lot.
      3.    Basements. The floor area of basements shall not be included in
determining the maximum building size, except to the following extent:
         a.    The gross floor area of basements that were lawfully in
existence on or before February 7, 1989, and that have exposed exterior walls
more than 4.0 feet above grade, shall be calculated by multiplying the gross
floor area within the exterior face of the outside walls of the basement by the
ratio of the number of linear feet of the outside wall of the basement more
than 4.0 feet above grade to the total outside perimeter of the basement.
         b.    The gross floor area of basements that were not lawfully in
existence on or before February 7, 1989, or that were constructed since
February 7, 1989, and that have walls more than 3 feet above grade, shall be
calculated by multiplying the gross floor area within the exterior face of the
outside walls of the basement by the ratio of the number of linear feet of the
outside wall of the basement more than 3 feet above grade to the total outside
perimeter of the basement.
      4.    Exclusions. The following floor areas shall be excluded from the
maximum building size;
         a.   An amount of garage floor area equal to one of the following:
            (i)    The first 400 square feet of the floor area of a one-story
detached garage located in the rear quarter of the lot; or
            (ii)    The first 200 square feet of the floor area of an attached
garage located in the rear of a house, provided that no part of the garage
forms any part of the front building line or the building line exposed to a
corner lot line;
         b.    Up to 150 square feet or the equivalent of 1 percent (.01) of
the lot area, whichever is greater, of the floor area of an attic or half-
story;
         c.    Up to 64 square feet of the aggregate floor area of all bay
windows and chimneys that form part of the exterior building line;
         d.   The floor area under any dormer that is no more than 6 feet wide
and is set in at least 3.5 feet from the gable end walls, provided that the
total width of all dormers does not exceed 25% of the length of the roof on
which they area located.
         e.   The first 50 square feet of the aggregate floor area of a
detached storage shed, playhouse, walled enclosures for refuse containers or
swimming pool equipment, or similar enclosed structures, provided they are
located in the rear quarter of the lot.
      5.    Notwithstanding the foregoing, any zoning lot consisting of a
single, legal, nonconforming lot that has a lot area of less than 7,260 square
feet as the result of the acquisition or condemnation of a portion of the rear
yard of the lot for a public purpose, shall be entitled to an additional 400
square feet of gross floor area on the lot, provided that such additional floor
area permitted by this paragraph shall be in lieu of, and not in addition to,
the additional floor area permitted pursuant to subparagraph 4a of this
subsection.
      6.    Maximum FAR. Notwithstanding the allowances and exclusions
permitted in the foregoing paragraphs, under no circumstances shall the
resulting FAR for any lot exceed 0.50 (50%).
SECTION 49: That subsection (h), “Set Back,” of Section 22.06 of the Winnetka
Zoning Ordinance, R-3 Single-Family Residential District Regulations, is hereby
amended by changing the second paragraph to read as follows:
   On corner lots, there shall be a minimum corner setback equal to the set
back established in the preceding paragraph, except that this regulation shall
not reduce the width of the building area as measured from the minimum required
side yard to less than 60 percent of the average width of the lot, and shall
not reduce the length of the buildable area as measured from the minimum
required rear yard to less than 50 percent of the maximum depth of the lot.
SECTION 50: That subsection (i), “Side Yard,” of Section 22.06 of the Winnetka
Zoning Ordinance, R-3 Single Family Residential District Regulations, Chapter
22 of the Winnetka Village Code, is hereby amended to provide as follows:
   (i)    Side Yard. There shall be a side yard on each side of the lot, one
side yard having a width of not less than 6 feet and the other having a width
of not less than 8 feet, provided that the sum of the width of the two side
yards shall not be less than 25 percent of the average width of the lot.
SECTION 51: That Section 22.06 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, R-3 Single-
Family Residential District Regulations, Chapter 22 of the Winnetka Village
Code, is hereby amended by adding a new subsection (p) “Garages,” which shall
provide as follows:
   (p)    Garages.
      1.   Front-facing Garage Doors. Garage door widths may not exceed 33
percent of the lot width or 18 feet, whichever is less, provided that no
individual door shall extend more than 9 feet when facing the front yard. (MC-
160-96) (08/16/96)
      2.   Width of Attached Garages. No attached garage with garage doors that
face a front yard or corner yard shall exceed the greater of 50 percent of the
front building line or 22 feet.
      3.   Location of Attached Garages. An attached garage shall be permitted
as the lowest story of the principal building provided that no the garage doors
are not a part of the front building elevation of the principal building.
      4.   Garages on Corner Lots. Any garage located on a corner lot shall be
set back at least 20 feet from the corner lot line.
SECTION 52: That Section 22.07 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, “A-3” Single-
Family Residential District Regulations, Chapter 22 of the Winnetka Village
Code, is hereby amended by changing its title to “R-2 Single Family Residential
District.”
