Posted: October 4, 2000
ORDINANCE NO. MC-6-2000
AN ORDINANCE AMENDING CHAPTER 15.52 OF THE WINNETKA VILLAGE CODE AS IT PERTAINS
TO DEMOLITION PERMITS
WHEREAS, the Winnetka Village Code requires that any application to demolish a
complete building must include either a detailed site restoration schedule and
site restoration plan, or a complete building permit application; and
WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka have determined that requiring
an applicant to incur the cost of a complete set of plans for a new building in
order to demolish an existing building has the unintended consequence of
reducing the effectiveness of the advisory review procedure of Chapter 15.52,
which is intended to encourage the preservation of architecturally or
historically significant structures by evaluating alternatives to demolition.
NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
SECTION 1: That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the findings
of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth herein.
SECTION 2: That Section 15.52.010 of Chapter 15.52 "Demolition Permits," of
Title 15, "Buildings and Construction," of the Winnetka Village Code, be
amended to provide as follows:
Section 15.52.010   Demolition permit.
   A.   Permit Required. A demolition permit shall be required before
proceeding with any of the following work:
      1.   The demolition of fifty (50) percent or more of the floor area of
any single-family or two-family dwelling;
      2.   The demolition of any interior spaces or exterior walls of any
commercial, institutional, multifamily or mixed use buildings; and
      3.   The removal of any accessory structure.
   B.   Permit Application Requirements. No application for a demolition permit
will be accepted for review unless it is complete. Application for a demolition
permit shall be made by the owner on forms provided by the Director and shall
be accompanied by all applicable fees and deposits and by the following items:
      1.   A legible property survey, prepared and sealed by a surveyor,
licensed by the State of Illinois. The survey shall include the legal
description of the property, and shall accurately depict lot boundaries and
current improvements.
      2.   A tree survey that accurately depicts the location, size and species
of the trees, the location of underground utilities and the proposed locations
for protective fencing.
      3.   A demolition schedule.
      4.   A list of the addresses of all properties located within two hundred
fifty (250) feet of the subject property.
      5.   Such other information and documentation as the Director may require
for review of the application, including, but not limited to, a site
restoration plan and schedules.
   C.   Permits From Other Authorities Required. Prior to the approval of any
demolition permit, the owner shall provide the Director with proof that any
permits required for the requested demolition by the county, state, or other
regulating authorities have been approved.
   D.   Notice of Application. Upon receiving a completed application for a
demolition permit, the Director shall promptly cause the following to be done:
      1.   Notify the occupant of each address on the list provided by the
applicant. The notice shall include a copy of the demolition permit
application, the demolition schedule, the construction or site restoration
schedules, and the location and times at which the application and any related
construction documents or site restoration plans may be examined. The time
period for such examination shall not exceed thirty (30) days from the date of
receipt of the application. Failure of any occupant to receive the notification
required under this paragraph shall not invalidate, impair or otherwise affect
any demolition permit that is subsequently approved and released.
      2.   Notify the Chairperson of the Landmark Preservation Commission to
determine if the building or structure which is the subject of the application
has been designated a landmark.
      3.   Determine if the permit application is subject to an advisory review
by the Building Review Committee pursuant to subsection H of this section.
      4.   Notify the Building Review Committee of the demolition permit
application.
   E.   Application Review. No later than thirty (30) days after the date a
completed application is accepted for filing, the Building Review Committee
shall consider the application, including conducting an advisory review, if
applicable, and shall recommend that the Director take one of the two following
actions:
      1.   That the Director approve and issue the demolition permit, if the
application is otherwise in accordance with this code; or
      2.   That the Director delay approval and issuance of the demolition
permit for a period of time not to exceed ninety (90) days.
   F.   Delay of Approval and Issuance. The Director may delay the approval of
a demolition permit for a period of time not to exceed ninety (90) days under
the following circumstances:
      1.   If two or more building permits for primary structures have been
approved for properties, for which work is continuing, on either side of the
right-of-way block face along which the subject property is located;
      2.   If the Director determines that a delay is necessary to prevent
undue congestion and noise impacts within the neighborhood;
      3.   If the Building Review Committee has requested a delay in accordance
with the advisory review procedures; or
      4.   If the subject property is a landmark designated pursuant to Chapter
15.64 of this code and the Landmark Preservation Commission has not completed
its determination as to whether certificate of appropriateness should be
issued.
   G.   Lifting of Delay; Issuance of Permit. The delay shall be lifted and the
permit approved and issued, if the Director determines that the circumstances
on which the delay was based on longer exist. Notwithstanding the foregoing, no
permit for the demolition of a principal building shall be released to the
applicant unless the Village has received, reviewed and approved either a
complete site restoration plan and schedule, or a complete building permit
application for a new principal building.
   H.   Criteria for Advisory Review. A demolition permit application shall be
subject to advisory review by the Building Review Committee if the demolition
affects an external architectural feature and the structure meets one of the
following criteria: (1) the structure is at least fifty (50) years old; (2) the
structure was selected for inclusion in the most recent Illinois Historic
Structures Survey, conducted under the auspices of the Illinois Department of
Conservation, and subsequent updates or Village surveys; or (3) the structure
is listed on the National Register of Historic Places, the Illinois Register of
Historic Places, and/or has been designated a landmark pursuant to Chapter
15.64 of this code.
   I.   Advisory Review Procedure.
      1.   The Building Review Committee shall review and attempt to preserve a
structure according to the following criteria: (a) the structure exhibits a
high quality of architectural design without regard to the time built or
historic associations; (b) the structure exhibits a high quality of
architectural design that is not the result of a change or a series of changes
in the original structure; (c) the structure exemplifies an architectural
style, construction technique or building type once common in the Village; and
(d) the structure exhibits an unusual, distinctive or eccentric design or
construction technique which contributes to the architectural interest or its
environs as an accent or counterpart.
      2.   The Committee's recommendations to the owner shall identify and
evaluate the structure's historical or architectural significance, propose
preservation alternatives and relevant planning considerations based on such
evaluation, encourage interest in and understanding of preservation in the
whole of the Village as it may be applicable to the demolition permit request
under review, and to encourage and provide means of communication and exchange
of views between the demolition permit applicants and the occupants of
properties within two hundred fifty (250) feet of the subject property.
      3.   If the Building Review Committee determines that the proposed
demolition appears to affect adversely the purposes and goals of this section,
the Committee shall request a conference with the owner and the review period
shall be extended to a maximum of ninety (90) days from acceptance of the
completed application for filing. Any delay by the applicant in complying with
such request shall be added to the time allowed in this section for comment by
the Committee. The Committee shall determine its recommendations and transmit
them in writing, to the applicant, and attempt to work out a mutually
satisfactory solution. A copy of the Committee's recommendations shall be
forwarded to the Village Council. (Prior code § 23.35)
SECTION 3: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage, approval
and posting.
   ADOPTED this 3rd day of October, 2000, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
AYES:      Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Lien, Powell, Presser and Woodbury
NAYS:   None
ABSENT:   None
Signed:
s/s Louise A. Holland
Village President
Countersigned:
s/s Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: September 19, 2000
Posted: September 20, 2000
Passed and Approved: October 3, 2000
Posted: October 4, 2000
ORDINANCE NO. MC-7-2000(tabled)
ORDINANCE NO. MC-8-2000
AN ORDINANCE AMENDING VARIOUS PROVISIONS OF THE WINNETKA ZONING ORDINANCE,
TITLE 17 OF THE WINNETKA VILLAGE CODE, THE SUBDIVISION CODE, TITLE 16 OF
WINNETKA VILLAGE CODE, AND THE TREE PRESERVATION CODE, CHAPTER 15.28 OF TITLE
15 OF THE WINNETKA VILLAGE CODE, ALL AS THEY PERTAIN TO AVERAGE LOT WIDTH,
DIMENSIONS OF FLAG LOTS AND REQUIRED YARDS AND SETBACKS
WHEREAS, in December 1998, the Village Council considered a request to amend
the Zoning Ordinance by redefining the term “average width of lot” and changing
the method for measuring lot width; and
WHEREAS, following a public hearing on that request, the Village Council found
that an amendment was not warranted, because there was no way to measure the
extent to which the proposed change would create nonconformities in irregularly
shaped lots, and there were no variation requests that indicated that the
existing definition created any problems definition when applied to individual
parcels of property; and
WHEREAS, based on comments received in the course of considering recent
requests for side yard variations, the Village Council directed staff to
reexamine the issue to determine whether applying the definition of “average
width of lot” to the properties in question triggered the need for the
variations; and
WHEREAS, after analyzing both the recent variations requests and the 1998
request to amend the Zoning Ordinance, the staff concluded that need for the
variations came not from the definition of “average width of lot” but from the
way it is calculated when the configuration of a lot results in having a lot
line other than the street line being designated as the front lot line; and
WHEREAS, upon receiving the report from Village staff, the Village Council
directed that proposed amendatory language be prepared and that the matter be
set for public hearing in the manner provided in the Zoning Ordinance; and
WHEREAS, on October 7, 2000, on due notice thereof, the Village Council held a
public hearing on proposed amendments to various Zoning Ordinance definitions
pertaining to the orientation and measurement of dimensions on zoning lots; and
WHEREAS, at the same hearing, the Council also considered other Zoning
Ordinance amendments proposed by staff, said amendments being to other
definitions and to the application of the proposed amendments to the specific
zoning regulations, all of which proposed amendments were within the scope of
the notice of hearing; and
WHEREAS, upon the completion of the hearing, the Village Council found that the
proposed amendments were warranted and proceeded to introduce this Ordinance;
and
WHEREAS, the Village Council find that the amendments set forth in this
Ordinance are consistent with General Residential Objective 1 of the goals and
objectives pertaining to Village Character and Appearance in Residential Areas,
as set forth in Section 2.4 of Winnetka 2020, the Winnetka Comprehensive Plan;
and
WHEREAS, the Village Council finds that the amendments set forth in this
Ordinance are consistent with Single-Family Residence Objectives 1 through 4 of
the goals and objectives pertaining to Village Character and Appearance in
Residential Areas, as set forth in Section 2.4 of Winnetka 2020, the Winnetka
Comprehensive Plan; and
WHEREAS, the Village Council further find that the amendments set forth in this
Ordinance are consistent with the recommendations on land use, development and
Village character stated in Sections 4.3, 4.3.1, 4.3.2 and all of Section 4.4
of Chapter IV of Winnetka 2020 Comprehensive Plan; and
WHEREAS, the Village Council further find that the amendments set forth in this
Ordinance further the purposes of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance and will
encourage development within the various single family residential zoning
districts that is consistent with the stated character of the individual
districts; and
WHEREAS, the Village Council further finds that flag lots detract from the
pedestrian orientation of residential neighborhoods by stacking one home behind
another, reducing the visibility and accessibility of the rear residence from
the street, while adding to density at the rear of the adjoining lots, which is
the primary location for open space on single-family residential lots; and
WHEREAS, in light of the foregoing, the Village Council find that the
amendments set forth in this Ordinance are in the best interests of the health,
safety and general welfare of the Village of Winnetka and its citizens.
NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
SECTION 1: That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the findings
of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth herein.
SECTION 2 : That the definition of “average width of lot” in Section 17.04.030,
entitled "Definitions," of Chapter 17.04 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance,
Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, is hereby amended by changing the term
to “average lot width” and amending the definition of that term to provide as
follows:
   "Average lot width” means the quotient resulting from the division of the
lot area in square feet by the lot depth in feet.
SECTION 3 : That the term “depth of lot” in Section 17.04.030, entitled
"Definitions," of Chapter 17.04 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of
the Winnetka Village Code, is hereby amended by changing the term to “lot
depth” and amending the definition of that term to provide as follows:
    “Lot depth” means the distance from the front base line of the lot to the
farthest point of the lot from the front base line, measured along a line
perpendicular to the front base line, or its extension. For flag lots, the
access corridor (“flagpole”) shall not be included in measuring the lot depth.
SECTION 4: That the definition of “corner yard” in Section 17.04.030, entitled
"Definitions," of Chapter 17.04 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of
the Winnetka Village Code is hereby amended as follows:
   "Corner yard" means a yard extending along the full length of a corner lot
line and back to a line drawn parallel to the corner lot line, at a distance
equal to the distance between the corner lot line and the nearest point of the
building including, where applicable, the average front setback, but excluding
any area included within a front yard.
SECTION 5: That Section 17.04.030, entitled "Definitions," of Chapter 17.04 of
the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, is hereby
amended by adding a definition for the term “front base line,” which shall
provide as follows:
   “Front base line” means a line passing through the two termini of the front
lot line. On through lots abutting Lake Michigan, the front base line shall be
a line passing through the two termini of the front lot line opposite the
water's edge, and the lot depth shall be no greater than the distance from the
front base line of the lot to the water's edge of Lake Michigan.
SECTION 6: That the definition of “front lot line” in Section 17.04.030,
entitled "Definitions," of Chapter 17.04 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance,
Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code is hereby amended as follows:
   "Front lot line" means the front street line. If the front street line is
less than fifty (50) feet long and if the lot is at least seventy-five (75)
feet wide at its widest point, then the front lot line shall be the lot line
from which the front setback is measured, as determined by the Director. The
shortest street line of a corner lot shall be the "front lot line." On through
lots, both street lines shall be deemed "front lot lines," except where no
setback requirement is applicable. For lots abutting Lake Michigan, the water's
edge of the lake shall be deemed a "front lot line."
SECTION 7: That the definition of “front yard” in Section 17.04.030, entitled
"Definitions," of Chapter 17.04 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of
the Winnetka Village Code is hereby amended as follows:
   "Front yard" means a yard, other than a corner yard, extending along the
full length of a front lot line, or other street line, and back to a line drawn
parallel to that front lot line, or other street line, at a distance equal to
the distance between the front lot line, or other street line, and the nearest
point of the building.
SECTION 8: That the definition of “lot area” in Section 17.04.030, entitled
"Definitions," of Chapter 17.04 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of
the Winnetka Village Code is hereby amended as follows:
   "Lot area" means the area of any lot within its exterior lot lines, not
including any area lying within a street. As used in this title, "lot area"
shall not include any part of a platted lot that lies beyond the water's edge
of Lake Michigan or within the boundaries of any other river, lake or stream
that is under the ownership and control of the state of Illinois. For flag
lots, the access corridor (“flagpole”) shall not be included in calculating the
lot area.
SECTION 9: That the definition of “maximum depth of lot” in Section 17.04.030,
entitled "Definitions," of Chapter 17.04 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance,
Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code is hereby repealed.
SECTION 10: That the definition of “rear yard” in Section 17.04.030, entitled
"Definitions," of Chapter 17.04 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of
the Winnetka Village Code is hereby amended as follows:
   "Rear yard" means a yard extending along the full length of the rear lot
line between that line and a line drawn parallel to the rear lot line at a
distance equal to the distance between the rear lot line and the nearest point
of the building. For purposes of this title, there shall be no rear yard on any
through lot.
SECTION 11: That the definition of “set back” in Section 17.04.030, entitled
"Definitions," of Chapter 17.04 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of
the Winnetka Village Code is hereby amended as follows:
   ”Setback” means the minimum required horizontal distance between any lot
line and a line drawn parallel to that lot line at a distance equal to the
minimum required yard.
SECTION 12: That the definition of “side yard” in Section 17.04.030, entitled
"Definitions," of Chapter 17.04 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of
the Winnetka Village Code is hereby amended as follows:
   "Side yard" means a yard extending along a side lot line between that line
and a line drawn parallel to the side lot line at a distance equal to the
distance between the side lot line and the nearest point of the building, but
excluding any area lying within a front setback or rear yard.
SECTION 13: That Subsection (E)4 of Section 15.28.010, entitled “Tree
enhancement and preservation,” of Chapter 15.28, “Tree Preservation Code,” of
Title 15 of the Winnetka Village Code, is hereby amended to provide as follows:
      4.   Consistent with the various yard setback requirements of the Zoning
Ordinance, all buildings and other structures shall be located on a lot or
parcel of land to minimize tree damage and/or removal.
SECTION 14 : That Subsection (O) of Section 16.08.020, “Specifications of final
land subdivision plans,” of Chapter 16.08, “Procedure,” of Title 16,
“Subdivision,” of the Winnetka Village Code, is hereby amended to provide as
follows:
   O.   It shall show any building or setback lines which are more restrictive
than those provided by the Zoning Ordinance of the Village with all necessary
dimensions, together with any private restrictions and trusteeships and the
period of their existence. Should such restrictions and trusteeships be of such
length as to make the lettering of the same on the plan impracticable, they may
be contained in a separate instrument if reference is made to such instrument
on the plan.
SECTION 15: That Subsection (B)7 of Section 17.04.020, “Objectives,” Chapter
17.04, “Introductory Provisions and Definitions,” Title 17, “Zoning,” of the
Winnetka Village Code, is hereby amended to provide as follows:
      7.   To establish, regulate and limit building or setback lines on or
along any street, traffic way, drive, parkway or storm or floodwater runoff
channel or basin;
SECTION 16: That Subsection (D) of Section 16.12.010, “Lots,” of Chapter 16.12,
“Design Standards,” of Title 16 of the Winnetka Village Code, “Subdivisions,”
is hereby amended to provide as follows:
   D.   Lots.
      1.   All lots created by any plan for land subdivision shall comply with
all standards of the Zoning Ordinance, except as provided in this chapter.
      2.   All side lines of lots shall be approximately perpendicular to
street lines, or radial to curved street lines unless, in the opinion of the
Plan Commission, a variation of the requirement would enhance public safety or
convenience.
      3.   All lots created by any plan for land subdivision shall meet the lot
area requirements of the Zoning Ordinance. All lot area calculations shall
exclude all easements for ingress and egress, all public or private streets,
all public or private alleys, and the access corridor, or “flagpole,” of any
flag lot.
      4.   Where a lot is already improved with buildings or structures, the
plan shall show whether the dimensions and locations of such improvements
comply with the use, intensity of use of lot (including impermeable surface
requirements), setback, side yard, rear yard and other bulk requirements of the
Zoning Ordinance then in effect. If a prior legal nonconformity, or a
previously granted variation, with respect to any such requirements exists, the
Plan Commission shall determine whether such nonconformity or previously
granted variation, in the context of the proposed subdivision, would result in
a material increased adverse impact upon the public health, safety or welfare.
If such a determination is made, the Plan Commission may deny the plan for land
subdivision. No plan for land subdivision which itself creates a departure from
such requirements shall be approved by the Plan Commission until such time as a
variation for such departure is granted by the Zoning Board of Appeals or the
Village Council, as the case may be, in accordance with the Zoning Ordinance.
      5.   At intersections with major streets, acute angle intersections and
other places where, in the opinion of the Plan Commission, safety or
convenience of traffic movement would be enhanced, corners shall be cut back by
joining the intersecting street lines through circular arcs having adequate
radii.
      6.   No plan for any land subdivision shall be approved if it results in
the creation of one or more lots having side lot lines abutting rear lot lines.
      7.   All lots created by any plan for land subdivision shall meet the lot
depth requirements of the Zoning Ordinance. Notwithstanding the foregoing, the
access corridor, or “flagpole,” shall be excluded when measuring the lot depth
of a flag lot.
      8.   All lots created by any plan for land subdivision shall meet the lot
width requirements of the Zoning Ordinance. Notwithstanding the foregoing, all
new flag lots shall meet the minimum lot width requirements of the Zoning
Ordinance, both as measured by excluding the access corridor, or “flagpole,”
and as measured by including the access corridor or “flagpole.”
      9.   All lots created by any plan for land subdivision shall meet all
yard and setback requirements of the Zoning Ordinance. Notwithstanding the
foregoing, for any flag lot that has a front street line that is less than
fifty (50) feet long and that is at least seventy-five (75) feet wide at its
widest point, all yards and setbacks shall be measured based both on using the
actual front street line as the front lot line, and on using as the front lot
line the lot line from which the Director determines the front setback shall be
measured for development purposes.
SECTION 17: That Subsection (E), “Width of Lot,” of Section 17.12.010 of
Chapter 17.12, “R-5 Single-Family Residential District,” of Title 17 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, “Zoning,” is hereby amended to provide as follows:
   E.   Lot Width. No building shall be erected upon any interior lot that has
an average lot width of less than sixty (60) feet, a front street line of less
than twenty (20) feet long or a lot width of less than sixty (60) feet measured
along a horizontal line between points on the side lot lines at a distance of
forty-five (45) feet from the front street line. No building shall be erected
upon any corner lot that has an average lot width of less than seventy (70)
feet.
SECTION 18: That Subsection (H), “Set Back,” of Section 17.12.010, of Chapter
17.12, “R-5 Single-Family Residential District,” of Title 17 of the Winnetka
Village Code, “Zoning,” is hereby amended to provide as follows:
   H.   Front Setback. There shall be a minimum front yard setback of not less
than thirty (30) feet. On block faces where there are three or more principal
buildings and where a setback of more than thirty (30) feet has been maintained
by fifty (50) percent or more the principal buildings on that block face, each
additional building shall maintain a setback of not less than the average
setback of such principal buildings as calculated at the time of the
construction of each such additional building; provided, however, that the
average setback requirement shall not necessitate a setback of more than sixty
(60) feet or (provided the thirty (30) foot minimum requirement is met) more
than fifteen (15) feet greater than that maintained by an existing principal
building on an immediately adjoining lot.
   On corner lots, there shall be a minimum corner yard setback equal to the
setback established in the preceding paragraph, except that this regulation
shall not reduce the width of the buildable area, as measured from the minimum
required side yard, to less than sixty (60) percent of the average lot width,
and shall not reduce the length of the buildable area, as measured from the
minimum required rear yard, to less than fifty (50) percent of the lot depth.
   Where a lot has three street frontages and where a side or rear yard line
does not exist from which to measure the width or depth of the buildable area,
the buildable area shall be situated on the lot so that equal setbacks shall be
required from the opposite street lines.
   In lieu of measuring the required setback from the front street line, if the
front street line of a lot extends less than fifty (50) feet along the street
line and if the lot is at least seventy-five (75) feet wide at its widest
point, the Director shall designate the front yard for zoning purposes and
shall establish the lot line from which the required setback shall be measured
to the nearest line of a building so as to conform as closely as practicable to
the intent and purposes of this title to require uniform front yards, uniform
setbacks and appropriate spacing between buildings.
   A church or temple, or any enlargement or extension of such church or
temple, that is located on a corner lot and that otherwise complies with the
provisions of this title, may be allowed a setback of not less than twenty (20)
feet on any, but not more than one, of its street frontages; provided that, no
part of any such building that extends in front of the setback line otherwise
required by this title shall be less than one hundred (100) feet from the point
of intersection of the lot line from which the reduced setback is allowed and
the lot line that forms the boundary between such lot and the lot next
adjoining, and that the area of the portion of any such building that extends
in front of the setback line otherwise required by this title not be more than
fifteen (15) percent of the total area of any such building.
   An accessory building may be placed nearer the street line than the
principal building only on a through lot. Provided that the setback provision
for each street shall be observed.
SECTION 19: That Subsection (J), “Rear Yard,” of Section 17.12.010 of Chapter
17.12, “R-5 Single-Family Residential District,” of Title 17 of the Winnetka
Village Code, “Zoning,” is hereby amended to provide as follows:
   J.   Rear Yard Setback. There shall be a rear yard setback having a depth of
not less than fifteen (15) percent of the lot depth; provided that, such rear
setback shall not be less than ten (10) feet and need not exceed twenty-five
(25) feet in depth.
   If a lot forms a point at the rear, or if the rear lot line extended forms
an angle of more than forty-five (45) degrees with the front lot line extended,
the rear lot line and the rear yard setback shall be established for zoning
purposes by the Director so as to conform as closely as practicable to the
intent and purposes of this title to require uniform rear yards and appropriate
spacing between buildings.
   Whenever the boundary line of the rear yard of any lot is also the side line
of a required side yard on an adjoining lot, no building nor any part of such
building shall be built in the rear yard closer to the side line of the
adjoining lot than the width of the required side yard on the adjoining lot.
SECTION 20: That Subsection (E), “Width of Lot,” of Section 17.16.010 of
Chapter 17.16, “R-4 Single-Family Residential District,” of Title 17 of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, “Zoning,” is hereby amended to provide as follows:
   E.   Lot Width. No building shall be erected upon any interior lot that has
an average width of less than sixty (60) feet, a front street line of less than
twenty (20) feet long or a width of less than sixty (60) feet measured along a
horizontal line between points on the side lot lines at a distance of forty-
five (45) feet from the front street line. No building shall be erected upon
any corner lot that has an average lot width of less than seventy (70) feet.
SECTION 21: That Subsection (H), “Set Back,” of Section 17.16.010 of Chapter
17.16, “R-4 Single-Family Residential District,” of Title 17 of the Winnetka
Village Code, “Zoning,” is hereby amended to provide as follows:
   H.   Front Setback. There shall be a minimum front yard setback of not less
than thirty (30) feet. On block faces where there are three or more principal
buildings and where a setback of more than thirty (30) feet has been maintained
by fifty (50) percent or more of the principal buildings on that block face,
each additional building shall maintain a setback of not less than the average
setback of such principal buildings as calculated at the time of the
construction of each such additional building; provided, however, that the
average setback requirement shall not necessitate a setback of more than sixty
(60) feet or (provided the thirty (30) foot minimum requirement is met) more
than fifteen (15) feet greater than that maintained by an existing principal
building on an immediately adjoining lot.
   On corner lots, there shall be a minimum corner yard setback equal to the
setback established in the preceding paragraph, except that this regulation
shall not reduce the width of the buildable area, as measured from the minimum
required side yard, to less than sixty (60) percent of the average lot width,
and shall not reduce the length of the buildable area, as measured from the
minimum required rear yard, to less than fifty (50) percent of the lot depth.
   Where a lot has three street frontages and where a side or rear yard line
does not exist from which to measure the width or depth of the buildable area,
the buildable area shall be situated on the lot so that equal setbacks shall be
required from the opposite street lines.
   In lieu of measuring the required setback from the front street line, if the
front street line of a lot extends less than fifty (50) feet along the street
line and if the lot is at least seventy-five (75) feet wide at its widest
point, the Director shall designate the front yard for zoning purposes and
shall establish the lot line from which the required setback will be measured
so as to conform as closely as practicable to the intent and purposes of this
title to require uniform front yards, uniform setbacks and appropriate spacing
between buildings.
   A church or temple, or any enlargement or extension of such church or
temple, that is located on a corner lot and that otherwise complies with the
provisions of this title, may be allowed a setback of not less than twenty (20)
feet on any, but not more than one, of its street frontages; provided that, no
part of any such building that extends in front of the setback line otherwise
required by this title shall be less than one hundred (100) feet from the point
of intersection of the lot line from which the reduced setback is allowed and
the lot line that forms the boundary between such lot and the lot next
adjoining, and that the area of the portion of any such building that extends
in front of the setback line otherwise required by this title not be more than
fifteen (15) percent of the total area of any such building.
   An accessory building may be placed nearer the street line than the
principal building only on a through lot, provided that the setback provision
for each street shall be observed.
SECTION 22: That Subsection (J), “Rear Yard,” of Section 17.16.010 of Chapter
17.16, “R-4 Single-Family Residential District,” of Title 17 of the Winnetka
Village Code, “Zoning,” is hereby amended to provide as follows:
   J.   Rear Yard Setbacks. There shall be a rear yard setback having a depth
of not less than fifteen (15) percent of the maximum lot depth; provided, such
rear yard setback shall not be less than ten (10) feet and need not exceed
twenty-five (25) feet in depth.
   If a lot forms a point at the rear, or if the rear lot line extended forms
an angle of more than forty-five (45) degrees with the front lot line extended,
the rear lot line and the rear yard setback shall be established for zoning
purposes by the Director so as to conform as closely as practicable to the
intent and purposes of this title to require uniform rear yards and appropriate
spacing between buildings.
   Whenever the boundary line of the rear yard of any lot is also the side line
of a required side yard on an adjoining lot, no building nor any part of such
building shall be built in the rear yard closer to the side line of the
adjoining lot than the width of the required side yard on the adjoining lot.
SECTION 23: That Subsection (E), “Width of Lot,” of Section 17.20.010 of
