ORDINANCE NO. MC-1-2001
AN ORDINANCE AMENDING SECTION 2.04.020 OF THE WINNETKA VILLAGE CODE AS IT
PERTAINS TO THE SCHEDULE OF REGULAR MEETINGS OF THE VILLAGE COUNCIL
WHEREAS, on June 21, 1994, the Winnetka Village Council passed ordinance MC-
127-94, which amended the schedule for Village Council meetings to require
regular meetings to be held on the following Thursday if the first Tuesday of
the month coincides with the date on which a general election is scheduled
pursuant to the provisions of the Illinois Election Code; and
WHEREAS, the Village Council has determined that, despite public notice of such
departures from the regularly scheduled Tuesday Council meetings, the more
changes there are in the meeting schedule, the more likely it becomes that the
public will be confused as to when meetings are scheduled; and
WHEREAS, the Village Council has also determined that, the more changes there
are in the regular meeting schedule, the more likely it is that public
attendance and participation in the meetings will be reduced; and
WHEREAS, the Village Council has determined that the schedule of Council
meetings should be set so that there are as few departures from the regular
Tuesday meeting schedule as possible.
NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
SECTION 1: The foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the findings of
the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth herein.
SECTION 2:  Subsection (A) of Section 2.04.020, “Meetings,” of Chapter 2.04 of
the Winnetka Village Code, “Village Council,” is hereby amended to provide as
follows:
   A.   Time and Day of Meetings. The Council shall hold its regular meetings
on the first and third Tuesday of each month at seven-thirty p.m.; provided,
that if any such Tuesday falls either on the first day of January or on the
fourth day of July, then the Council shall hold its regular meeting at the same
hour on the immediately following Thursday.
SECTION 3: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage, approval
and posting.
   ADOPTED this 16th day of January, 2001, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
AYES:      Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Lien, Powell, Presser and Woodbury
NAYS:   None
ABSENT:   None
Signed:
s/s Louise A. Holland
Village President
Countersigned:
s/s Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: January 2, 2001
Posted: January 3, 2001
Passed and Approved: January 16, 2001
Posted: January 17, 2001
ORDINANCE NO. MC-2-2001 (tabled)
ORDINANCE NO. MC-3-2001
AN ORDINANCE AMENDING CHAPTER 5.08 OF THE WINNETKA VILLAGE CODE TO ESTABLISH
STANDARDS AND A NEW CLASSIFICATION OF LICENSES FOR THE SALE OF BEER AND WINE AT
SIDEWALK RESTAURANTS
WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka has received a request to allow
the holder of a class A-1 liquor license to sell alcoholic beverages as part of
meal service at its sidewalk restaurant; and
WHEREAS, the Village Council, in its capacity as Liquor Advisory Board, has
considered the request and has determined that the limited sale of alcoholic
beverages as part of meal service at sidewalk restaurants can have a beneficial
impact on the economic viability of restaurants and nearby businesses located
in the Village’s business districts, provided sufficient standards are
established; and
WHEREAS, the Village Council has determined that the sale of alcoholic
beverages at sidewalk restaurants should be restricted to restaurants that are
the subject of Class A or Class A-1 liquor licenses, provided they have also
been permitted by the Village Council to serve food on public sidewalks at the
sidewalk restaurant; and
WHEREAS, the Village Council has determined that the sale of alcoholic
beverages at sidewalk restaurants should be limited to beer and wine; and
WHEREAS, the Village Council has determined that, in order to assure the public
safety and welfare, the sale of alcoholic beverages at sidewalk restaurants
should be subject to other conditions pertaining to hours of service, the
condition of the premises, control over service at the sidewalk restaurant, and
protection of the Village from liability for damage or injury caused from the
use of the public sidewalk; and
WHEREAS, the Village Council has further determined that licenses for the sale
of liquor at sidewalk restaurants shall be issued only for certain months of
the year, and that the licensee shall not be entitled to renewal unless the
Village Council, in the sole exercise of its discretion, determines that the
applicant has complied with all applicable provisions of the Village Code and
that the issuance of a new license for sidewalk service will not harm the
public health, safety and welfare.
NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
SECTION 1: The foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the findings of
the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth herein.
SECTION 2:  Section 5.08.100, “Classification and schedule of fees,” of Chapter
5.08 of the Winnetka Village Code, “Alcoholic Beverages,” is hereby amended by
adding a new Subsection J, which shall be entitled “Sidewalk Restaurant Rider”
and shall provide as follows:
   J.   Sidewalk restaurant rider license, which shall authorize Class A or
Class A-1 licensees, upon issuance of the sidewalk restaurant rider license, to
sell beer or wine at retail to customers seated at tables at a permitted
sidewalk restaurant located on the public sidewalk adjacent to the premises for
which the Class-A or Class A-1 license was issued, subject to the following
conditions:
      1.   The sale of the beer or wine must be incidental and complementary to
the sale and service of a complete meal for consumption only at a table in the
area defined in the license.
      2.   It is unlawful for any holder of a sidewalk restaurant rider license
to render a bill for the sale of wine or beer that does not include a charge
for a complete meal.
      3.   Service at the sidewalk restaurant shall cease no later than the
hour specified in Section 5.08.250.A of this Chapter unless the Village Council
specifies an earlier time in an ordinance adopted at the time it authorizes the
license.
      4.    The area for service shall be contiguous to the premises for which
the Class A or Class A-1 license is issued, shall be defined in the application
and specified in the license, and shall be separated from the pedestrian areas
of the public sidewalk by fencing, planters or such other device as may be
specified by the Local Liquor Commissioner in the license.
      5.   The licensee shall indemnify and hold the Village, its officers and
employees from any and all costs arising from claims for personal injury or
property damage resulting in any way from the licensee’s use of the public way,
whether the claim, injury or damages arise from an incident on the licensed
premises or on the adjacent portion of the public way that remains open for
public use.
      6.   The licensee shall maintain dram shop insurance in an amount
specified by statute or ordinance, or by rule of the State Liquor Control
Commission or the Local Liquor Commissioner, but in no event shall the amount
of dram shop insurance be less than $1,000,000.
      7.   The licensee shall maintain general liability insurance coverage of
at least $2 million, with excess liability coverage of at least an additional
$2 million, with the Village named as additional insured. The certificate of
insurance shall be in a form acceptable to the Village.
      8.   The licensed premises shall be supervised at all times by an
employee of the restaurant who is at least 21 years old.
      9.    The term of any sidewalk restaurant rider license shall begin no
earlier than April 1 of any year and shall end no later than November 30 of the
same year, except that no service shall be allowed under any such license when
weather conditions necessitate the removal of snow or other debris from the
public sidewalks.
      10.   Every sidewalk restaurant rider license issued pursuant to this
subsection shall expire on December 1 of the year it is issued and shall not be
subject to renewal. Any Class A or Class A-1 licensee who operates a permitted
sidewalk restaurant may apply for a new sidewalk restaurant rider license when
renewing the licensee’s Class A or Class A-1 license, provided that the
application for the sidewalk restaurant rider license shall be de novo each
year, and being granted a sidewalk restaurant rider license in any year shall
not be deemed to create a right or expectation of renewal or reissuance of the
sidewalk restaurant rider license for the following or any subsequent year.
      11.   Any licensee who violates any provision of a sidewalk restaurant
rider may be disqualified from receiving a sidewalk restaurant rider for any
location in the Village for a period of up to 5 years.
      12.   The Local Liquor Commissioner, in the exercise of his or her
discretion, shall have the authority to impose such other conditions for the
issuance of a sidewalk restaurant rider license as he or she may deem
reasonably necessary.
      13.   The annual fee for the sidewalk restaurant rider license shall be
as set from time to time by resolution of the Village Council. (Prior code §
35.10)
SECTION 3:  Section 5.08.130, “Numbers of License,” of Chapter 5.08 of the
Winnetka Village Code, “Alcoholic Beverages,” is hereby amended to provide as
follows:
Section 5.08.130   Numbers Of Licenses
   The maximum number of licenses to be issued in each class shall be as
follows, provided, however, that the number in any class shall be reduced by
one for each such license that expires or that is revoked, surrendered or not
renewed:
A. Class A                   4
B. Class A-1                 4
C. Class B                   2
D. Special Class C           Unlimited
E. Class D                   0
F. Class D-1                 0
G. Television Rider          1
I. Packaged Meal Rider       2
J. Sidewalk Restaurant Rider 1
 
SECTION 4:  Section 5.08.200, “Sales restricted to authorized premises,” of
Chapter 5.08 of the Winnetka Village Code, “Alcoholic Beverages,” is hereby
amended to provide as follows:
Section 5.08.200   Sales restricted to authorized premises--Change of location-
-Alteration of premises.
   A.   The sale of alcoholic liquor shall be permitted only in or from the
premises described in the license, in the manner authorized in the license and
in compliance with the terms of the license. The premises shall not include any
public way or public property, unless specific authorization to use the public
way or public property is granted through the issuance of a sidewalk restaurant
rider license pursuant to subsection J of Section 5.08.100 of this chapter.
   B.   The location of the premises for which a license is issued may not be
changed unless the Local Liquor Control Commissioner issues a written permit
authorizing such change. No change of location shall be permitted unless the
proposed new location is a permissible site for the retail sale of alcoholic
liquor under the laws of the state, this code, or other ordinances of the
Village.
   C.   A written permit issued by the Local Liquor Control Commissioner shall
be required before the licensee undertakes any work on or alterations to the
premises for which the license is issued. (Prior code § 35.20)
SECTION 5:  Section 5.08.250, “Hours of business,” of Chapter 5.08 of the
Winnetka Village Code, “Alcoholic Beverages,” is hereby amended to provide as
follows:
Section 5.08.250   Hours of business.
   A.   A Class A or a Class A-1 licensee is permitted to sell or offer for
sale alcoholic liquor for consumption on the premises in the Village only
between the hours of 11:00 a.m. and 11:00 p.m. on the same day. A Class A or a
Class A-1 licensee who holds a sidewalk restaurant rider license is permitted
to sell or offer for sale beer or wine for consumption at tables at a permitted
sidewalk restaurant between the hours of 11:00 a.m. and 9:00 p.m. Sundays
through Thursdays and 11:00 a.m. and 10:00 p.m. on Fridays and Saturdays.
   B.   A Class B licensee is permitted to sell or offer for sale at retail
alcoholic liquor in its original package for consumption off the premises only
between the hours of 8:00 a.m. and 10:00 p.m. on the same day.
   C.   A Special Class C licensee is permitted to sell or offer for sale at
retail alcoholic liquor as provided in this chapter only as follows:
      1.   Between the hours of 11:00 a.m. and 12:00 midnight on the same day
if the alcoholic liquor is sold and consumed indoors in a fixed, permanent
structure; or
      2.   Between the hours of 11:00 a.m. and 10:00 p.m. the same day if the
alcoholic liquor is sold or consumed outdoors, or in a tent, booth, concession
stand, or other such temporary or movable structure, and then only if the sale
or consumption of alcoholic liquor is incidental and complementary to the sale
and consumption of other foods.
   D.   Regardless of the license classification held, a licensee is permitted
to sell or offer liquor for sale according to the terms of such license seven
days a week, including Sunday. (Prior code § 35.25)
SECTION 6: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage, approval
and posting.
   ADOPTED this 1st day of May, 2001, pursuant to the following roll call vote:
AYES:      Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser and Woodbury
NAYS:   None
ABSENT:   None
Signed:
s/s Michael F. Duhl
Village President
Countersigned:
s/s Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: April 17, 2001
Posted: April 18, 2001
Passed and Approved: May 1, 2001
Posted: May 2, 2001
ORDINANCE NO. MC-4-2001
AN ORDINANCE AMENDING CHAPTER 5.08 OF THE WINNETKA VILLAGE CODE TO ESTABLISH
STANDARDS AND A NEW CLASSIFICATION OF LICENSES FOR THE SALE OF ALCOHOLIC
BEVERAGES BY SPECIALTY RESTAURANTS FOR CONSUMPTION ON THE PREMISES
WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka has received a request to allow
the holder of a Class E liquor license to sell alcoholic beverages for
consumption on the premises of the licensee’s limited food products store, as
part of the service of full meals on the premises; and
WHEREAS, the Village Council, in its capacity as Liquor Advisory Board, has
considered the request and has determined that the sale of alcoholic beverages
in conjunction with full meal service for consumption on the premises of a
limited food products store can have a beneficial impact on the economic
viability of both the limited food product store and nearby restaurants and
businesses located in the Village’s business districts, provided sufficient
standards are established; and
WHEREAS, the Village Council has determined that, because of the unique nature
of the meal service and configuration of the premises, a separate restaurant
and license classification should be established for the sale of alcoholic
beverages for consumption on the premises of limited food products stores in
conjunction with the service of complete meals that such restaurants should be
defined as specialty restaurants, and that specific criteria for such
restaurants should be established; and
WHEREAS, the Village Council has determined that the sale of alcoholic
beverages for consumption on the premises of a specialty restaurant may include
the same type of service of alcoholic beverages as is allowed under Class A and
Class A-1 liquor licenses; and
WHEREAS, the Village Council has determined that a specialty restaurant may
operate and serve alcoholic beverages for consumption on the same premises as a
limited food products store, provided sufficient conditions are established
pertaining to hours of service, the adequacy of the premises for seating
customers for the service of a full meal, the type of meal service offered, and
the ability of the licensee to control the number of customers seated for such
service.
NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
SECTION 1: The foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the findings of
the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth herein.
SECTION 2:  Section 5.08.010, “Definitions,” of Chapter 5.08 of the Winnetka
Village Code, “Alcoholic Beverages,” is hereby amended by adding a definition
for the term, “specialty restaurant,” which shall provide as follows:
   "Specialty Restaurant" means any public place kept, used, maintained,
advertised and held out to the public as a place where complete meals are
served, and where complete meals are actually and regularly served, such space
being provided with adequate and sanitary kitchen and dining room equipment and
having employed in such space a sufficient number and kind of employees to
prepare, cook and serve suitable food for its patrons, where a host or hostess
is present to seat patrons, where all patrons are served the same prix fixe,
multi-course meal at a scheduled seating, where admission to the scheduled
seating is by reservation only, where food is served to patrons while they are
seated at tables or at a counter, where the meals are served using
nondisposable dishes, glassware and utensils, and at which the service of
alcoholic beverages is incidental and complementary to the service of such
meals. For purposes of this chapter, none of the following premises shall be
considered to be a specialty restaurant under this definition: (1) any premises
containing a bar or counter used for serving liquor to customers located in a
waiting area; (2) any premises containing a television; and (3) limited food
establishments, including, but not limited to, fast food restaurants and drive-
in restaurants as defined in the Winnetka zoning ordinance, luncheonettes,
lounges, diners, drive-ins, or self-service or carry-out establishments.
SECTION 3: Subsection J of Section 5.08.070, “Application for license,” of
Chapter 5.08 of the Winnetka Village Code, “Alcoholic Beverages,” is hereby
amended to provide as follows:
   J.   Proof, in the form of a certificate of insurance or insurance policy,
that the applicant has obtained dram shop liability insurance in the maximum
limits; provided that, if an application for such insurance is submitted with
an application for a Class A, Class A-1 or Class A-2 retailer's license, then
the approval of the application by the Local Liquor Control Commissioner shall
not become effective until the applicant presents proof in the form of a
certificate of insurance, a copy of the policy or a valid binder, that such
insurance has gone into effect; every Class A, Class A-1, A-2, television
rider, packaged meal rider, Class B and Class E license issued under the
provisions of this chapter shall terminate on the last day of March next
following its issuance. (Prior code § 35.09)
SECTION 4: Section 5.08.090, “Termination date,” of Chapter 5.08 of the
Winnetka Village Code, “Alcoholic Beverages,” is hereby amended to provide as
follows:
Section 5.08.090   Termination date.
   A.   Every Class A, Class A-1, Class A-2, television rider, packaged meal
rider, Class B, Class D, Class D-1 and Class E license issued under the
provisions of this chapter shall terminate on the last day of March next
following its issuance. (Prior code § 35.09)
   B.   Every Class C license issued under the provisions of this chapter shall
terminate on the date specified in the license.
   C.   Every sidewalk restaurant rider shall terminate on the last day of
November in the year the license is issued.
SECTION 5: Section 5.08.100, “Classification and schedule of fees,” of Chapter
5.08 of the Winnetka Village Code, “Alcoholic Beverages,” is hereby amended by
changing its title to “Classification of licenses,” and amending the text to
add a new Class A-2 liquor license and to make other technical amendments, with
the amended Section 5.08.100 to provide as follows:
Section 5.08.100   Classification and schedule of fees.
   A.   Class A Licenses. Class A licenses authorize the retail sale of
alcoholic liquor by restaurants, but only when such sale is incidental and
complementary to the sale and service of complete meals for consumption only on
the premises where sold.
   B.   Class A-1 Licenses. Class A-1 licenses authorize the retail sale of
alcoholic liquor by restaurants, but only when such sale is incidental and
complementary to the sale and service of complete meals served in multiple
courses for consumption only on the premises where sold, which incidental and
complementary sale may include service of liquor at a counter or bar, subject
to the following conditions:
      1.   The counter or bar shall be restricted to patrons who have been
seated by the restaurant's host or hostess only as an accommodation while the
table at which the patrons will be seated for their meal is being prepared;
      2.   No meals shall be served at the bar;
      3.   There shall be no television in any area of the restaurant,
including waiting, lounge and bar areas, in which patrons are served;
      4.   Service at the counter or bar shall cease no later than one hour
before closing or one hour before the time specified by ordinance for ceasing
liquor service, whichever is earlier; and
      5.   The percentage of the total space available to patrons that is
allocated to any lounge or waiting area in which a bar, counter or shelf or any
substitute for such bar, counter or shelf will be located, shall not exceed ten
(10) percent of the total space of the premises accessible to patrons.
   C.   Class A-2 Licenses. Class A-2 licenses authorize the retail sale of
alcoholic liquor by specialty restaurants to customers seated at tables or a
counter, subject to the following conditions:
      1.   The sale of liquor must be incidental and complementary to the sale
and service of a complete meal for consumption only at a table in the area
defined in the license.
      2.   It is unlawful for any holder of a Class A-2 license to render a
bill for the sale of liquor that does not include a charge for a complete meal.
      3.   The meal shall be offered at no more than two seatings per night,
with the seatings to be set at specific times the first of which shall be no
earlier than 6:00 p.m.
      4.   The menu shall consist of a fixed menu, prix fixe meal offered in
courses.
      5   Customers for full meal service shall be admitted to the specialty
restaurant by reservation only and the number of customers on the premises for
each seating shall be limited to the number of seats available at tables or a
counter located on the premises.
   D.   Class B Licenses. Class B licenses authorize the sale of package liquor
in a full-service grocery store. A Class B license shall authorize the licensee
to use no more than ten (10) percent of the total floor space of the full-
service grocery store for the display and sale of alcoholic liquor in the
original package
   E.   Special Class C Licenses. Special Class C licenses authorize the retail
sale of alcoholic liquor by civic, fraternal, service or charitable not-for-
pecuniary-profit organizations, at picnics, outings, festivals, theater nights
or other such similar special occasions for consumption on the premises or
within the area specifically designated for such license. No more than seven
such licenses shall be issued to any one licensee within any calendar year. The
license shall specify the date(s) and hours for the authorized sale of
alcoholic liquors under this section;
   F.   Class D Licenses. Class D licenses authorize the retail sale of wine in
the original package for consumption off the premises where sold and where
delivery is made exclusively through the mail or similar package delivery
service.
   G.   Class D-1 Licenses. Class D-1 licenses authorize the sale of wine at
wholesale in the original package by an importer/distributor to a Class D
licensee as provided in this chapter.
   H.   Television Rider License. Television rider license authorizes Class A
or Class A-1 licensees to place a single television on the licensed premises in
an area where patrons are served, subject to the following conditions: (1) the
screen of the television may have a diagonal measurement of no more than thirty
(30) inches; (2) the television screen may not be visible from any bar, counter
or waiting area; (3) no signs on the premises shall advertise the presence of
the television; (4) no promotions or advertisements for the restaurant shall
include reference to the presence of the television; and (5) such other
conditions or limitations as may be deemed necessary and appropriate by the
Local Liquor Control Commissioner, either in the general exercise of his or her
rulemaking powers, or as a specific condition or limitation related to the
nature of the restaurant operated by the licensee;
   I.   Class E Licenses. Class E licenses authorize the retail sale of wine
only, by a limited food products store, subject to all of the following
conditions:
      1.   The wine must be sold in its original package, for consumption only
off the premises where sold, and not for consumption on the premises where
sold.
      2.   The sale of the wine must be incidental and complementary to the
sale of food for consumption off the premises. It is unlawful for any holder of
a Class E license to render a bill for sale of wine in its original package
which does not include a charge for food to be consumed off the premises.
      3.   All wine sold under a Class E license shall be paid for and
delivered to the purchaser only on the premises of the limited food products
store operated by the licensee.
      4.   No more than ten (10) percent of the floor space of the limited food
products store used by the licensee for the display and sale of merchandise may
be used for the display and sale of wine. The remainder of such floor space
shall be for the display and sale of other merchandise.
      5.   A limited food products store may be operated in the same premises
as a specialty restaurant, provided the food sales and display area is separate
from the meal service area.
   J.   Packaged Meal Rider License. Packaged meal rider licenses authorize the
retail sale, by a Class A or Class A-1 licensee, of beer and wine in the
original package, for consumption only off the premises where sold, and not for
consumption on the premises, subject to the following conditions:
      1.   Such sale of wine or beer shall be incidental and complementary to
the sale of a complete meal prepared on the licensed premises for consumption
off the licensed premises.
      2.   It is unlawful for any holder of a packaged meal rider license to
render a bill for the sale of wine or beer in its original package which does
not include a charge for a complete meal.
      3.   All wine and beer sold under a packaged meal rider license shall be
paid for and delivered to the purchaser only on the premises of the restaurant
operated by the licensee.
      4.   There shall be no display of wine or beer offered for sale under a
packaged meal rider license, except to the extent that wine or beer is
displayed as part of the normal operations of the restaurant for which the
Class A or Class A-1 license was issued.
(Prior code § 35.10)
   K.   Sidewalk Restaurant Rider License. Sidewalk restaurant rider licenses
authorize Class A, Class A-1 or Class A-2 licensees to sell beer or wine at
retail to customers seated at tables at a permitted sidewalk restaurant located
on the public sidewalk adjacent to the premises for which the Class-A ,Class A-
1 or Class A-2 license was issued, subject to the following conditions:
      1.   The sale of the beer or wine must be incidental and complementary to
the sale and service of a complete meal for consumption only at a table in the
area defined in the license.
      2.   It is unlawful for any holder of a sidewalk restaurant rider license
to render a bill for the sale of wine or beer that does not include a charge
for a complete meal.
      3.   Service at the sidewalk restaurant shall cease no later than the
hour specified in Section 5.08.250.A of this Chapter unless the Village Council
specifies an earlier time in an ordinance adopted at the time it authorizes the
license.
      4.   The area for service shall be contiguous to the premises for which
the Class A, Class A-1 or Class A-2 license is issued, shall be defined in the
application and specified in the license, and shall be separated from the
pedestrian areas of the public sidewalk by fencing, planters or such other
device as may be specified by the Local Liquor Commissioner in the license.
      5.   The licensee shall indemnify and hold harmless the Village, its
officers and employees from any and all costs arising from claims for personal
injury or property damage resulting in any way from the licensee’s use of the
public way, whether the claim, injury or damages arise from an incident on the
licensed premises or on the adjacent portion of the public way that remains
open for public use.
      6.   The licensee shall maintain dram shop insurance in an amount
specified by statute or ordinance, or by rule of the State Liquor Control
Commission or the Local Liquor Commissioner, but in no event shall the amount
of dram shop insurance be less than $1,000,000.
      7.   The licensee shall maintain general liability insurance coverage of
at least $2 million, with excess liability coverage of at least an additional
$2 million, with the Village named as additional insured. The certificate of
insurance shall be in a form acceptable to the Village.
      8.   The licensed premises shall be supervised at all times by an
employee of the restaurant who is at least 21 years old.
      9.   The term of any sidewalk restaurant rider license shall begin no
earlier than April 1 of any year and shall end no later than November 30 of the
same year, except that no service shall be allowed under any such license when
weather conditions necessitate the removal of snow or other debris from the
public sidewalks.
      10.   Every sidewalk restaurant rider license issued pursuant to this
subsection shall expire on December 1 of the year it is issued and shall not be
subject to renewal. Any Class A, Class A-1 or Class A-2 licensee who operates a
permitted sidewalk restaurant may apply for a new sidewalk restaurant rider
license to which the rider is attached, provided that the application for the
sidewalk restaurant rider license shall be de novo each year, and being granted
a sidewalk restaurant rider license in any year shall not be deemed to create a
right or expectation of renewal or reissuance of the sidewalk restaurant rider
license for the following or any subsequent year.
      11.   Any licensee who violates any provision of a sidewalk restaurant
rider may be disqualified from receiving a sidewalk restaurant rider for any
location in the Village for a period of up to 5 years.
      12.   The Local Liquor Commissioner, in the exercise of his or her
discretion, shall have the authority to impose such other conditions for the
issuance of a sidewalk restaurant rider license as he or she may deem
reasonably necessary.
(Prior code § 35.10) (MC-3-2001, Amended, 05/01/2001, Subsection J added)
SECTION 6:  Section 5.08.110, “Proration of Fees,” of Chapter 5.08 of the
Winnetka Village Code, “Alcoholic Beverages,” is hereby amended to provide as
follows:
Section 5.08.110 License fees
   A.   Fees Set by Council. The annual fee for all licenses issued under the
provisions of this chapter shall be set from time to by resolution of the
Village Council.
   B.   Proration of Fees. If a Class A, Class A-1, Class A-2, television
rider, packaged meal rider, Class B, Class D, Class D-1 or Class E license is
issued after April 1, the license fee shall be reduced in proportion to the
number of full calendar months that expired in the license year prior to the
issuance of the license. (Prior code § 35.11)
SECTION 7:  Section 5.08.120, “Payment and disposition of fees,” of Chapter
5.08 of the Winnetka Village Code, “Alcoholic Beverages,” is hereby amended to
provide as follows:
Section 5.08.120 Payment and disposition of fees.
   All fees for Class A, Class A-1, Class A-2, television rider, packaged meal
rider, Class B, Class D, Class D-1 or Class E licenses issued under this
chapter shall be paid to the Village on or before April 1st of the year for
which such license is to be issued. In the event the license applied for is
denied, the fee shall be returned to the applicant. If the license is granted,
then the fee shall be deposited in the general corporate purposes fund or in
such other fund as shall have been designated by the Village Council by motion,
resolution or ordinance. (Prior code § 35.12)
SECTION 8:  Section 5.08.130, “Numbers of License,” of Chapter 5.08 of the
Winnetka Village Code, “Alcoholic Beverages,” is hereby amended to provide as
follows:
Section 5.08.130   Numbers Of Licenses
   The maximum number of licenses to be issued in each class shall be as
follows, provided, however, that the number in any class shall be reduced by
one for each such license that expires or that is revoked, surrendered or not
renewed:
A. Class A                   4
B. Class A-1                 4
C. Class A-2                 1
D. Class B                   2
E. Special Class C           Unlimited
F. Class D                   0
G. Class D-1                 0
H. Class E                   2
I. Television Rider          1
J. Packaged Meal Rider       2
K. Sidewalk Restaurant Rider 2
 
SECTION 9:  Section 5.08.160, “License Renewals,” of Chapter 5.08 of the
Winnetka Village Code, “Alcoholic Beverages,” is hereby amended to provide as
follows:
Section 5.08.160   License Renewals.
   A.   Any Class A, Class A-1, Class A-2, television rider, packaged meal
rider, Class B, Class D, Class D-1 or Class E licensee may renew his or her
license upon its expiration; provided, he or she is then eligible for an
original application to receive a license and the premises for which such
license renewal is sought are suitable for such purpose. Such license renewal
shall not be construed as a vested right and nothing in this chapter shall
prevent the Village President and Trustees from decreasing the number of
licenses that may be issued within the Village. All applications for the
renewal of a liquor license shall include a statement describing all work on or
alterations to the licensed premises during the term of the current license.
(Prior code § 35.16)
   B.   Sidewalk restaurant rider licenses are not subject to renewal and each
application for a sidewalk restaurant rider license shall be considered
de novo, regardless of whether the applicant has previously held such a
license.
SECTION 10:  Section 5.08.250, “Hours of business,” of Chapter 5.08 of the
Winnetka Village Code, “Alcoholic Beverages,” is hereby amended to provide as
follows:
Section 5.08.250   Hours of business.
   A.   The sale of alcoholic liquor by a restaurant or specialty restaurant
under a Class A, Class A-1 or Class A-2 license shall be permitted only between
the hours of 11:00 a.m. and 11:00 p.m. each day of the week, unless the license
specifically limits the days or hours of service..
   B.   The sale of beer or wine under a sidewalk restaurant rider license
shall be permitted only between the hours of 11:00 a.m. and 9:00 p.m. Sundays
through Thursdays and 11:00 a.m. and 10:00 p.m. on Fridays and Saturdays.
   C.   The sale of alcoholic liquor by a full-service grocery store under a
Class B license shall be permitted only between the hours of 8:00 a.m. and 10:
00 p.m. each day of the week.
   D.   The sale of alcoholic liquor by a civic, fraternal, service or
charitable not-for-pecuniary-profit organization under a Special Class C
licensee shall be permitted only as follows:
      1.   Between the hours of 11:00 a.m. and 12:00 midnight of each day or
days specified in the license if the alcoholic liquor is sold and consumed
indoors in a fixed, permanent structure; or
      2.   Between the hours of 11:00 a.m. and 10:00 p.m. of each day or days
specified in the license if the alcoholic liquor is sold or consumed outdoors,
or in a tent, booth, concession stand, or other such temporary or movable
structure, and then only if the sale or consumption of alcoholic liquor is
incidental and complementary to the sale and consumption of other foods.
   E.   The sale of wine by a limited food products store under a Class E
license shall be permitted only during the regular business hours of such
store.
(Prior code § 35.25)
SECTION 11: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage, approval
and posting.
   ADOPTED this 5th day of June, 2001, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
AYES:      Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser and Woodbury
NAYS:   None
ABSENT:   None
