ORDINANCE NO. MC-10-2001
AN ORDINANCE AMENDING THE ZONING ORDINANCE OF THE WINNETKA VILLAGE CODE AS IT
PERTAINS TO NONESSENTIAL PUBLIC USES
WHEREAS, the term nonessential public use was added to the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance when the terms “essential public service” and “essential public use”
were added to the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance by Ordinance MC-195-97, to reflect
the division of public uses into essential and nonessential public use
categories; and
WHEREAS, the purpose of the change in terms was to establish, by law, the
Village’s policy of permitting essential public uses, which are defined as
certain services provided by the Village from land or buildings that the
Village owns and operates, without requiring zoning procedures; and
WHEREAS, prior to the addition of “essential public service” and “essential
public use” to the Zoning Ordinance, Section 17.56. 010(B) (formerly Section
22.13.(b)) contained the terms “uses affected with the public interest” and
“public use;” and
WHEREAS, prior to the addition of the terms “essential public service” and
“essential public use” to the Zoning Ordinance, the terms “uses affected with
the public interest” and “public use;” had been applied without limiting
ownership of such uses to public bodies; and
WHEREAS. the amendment to Section 17.56.010(B) and the addition of the defined
terms “essential public service” and “essential public use” pursuant to
Ordinance MC-195-97 were intended only to assure that certain services provided
by the Village from land or buildings owned and operated by the Village would
be permitted in all zoning districts without requiring the Village to go
through the special use process; and
WHEREAS, in the course of considering the application of the Winnetka
Historical Society for a special use permit, which was the first special use
application under the nonessential public use provision since it was added to
the Zoning Ordinance, the Council heard and considered argument from objectors
who maintained that a nonessential public use must be owned by a public body;
and
WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka have determined that reference
to nonessential public uses, without further definition, creates an ambiguity
in Section 17.56.010(B), because the term “public” is sometimes used in the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance to refer to ownership by a public body, although it
is more frequently used to refer to the general public; and
WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka desires to clarify the Special
Use provisions pertaining to nonessential public uses, as found in Section
17.56.010 of the Winnetka Village Code, by clarifying that, as used in the term
“nonessential public uses,” the word “public” refers to the general public and
that nonessential public uses may include uses that are not owned by a public
body; and
WHEREAS, on November 6, 2001, pursuant to notice published in accordance with
applicable statutory and ordinance provisions, the Council of the Village of
Winnetka, sitting as a Committee of the Whole, conducted a public hearing on
the proposed amendments to the Zoning Ordinance.
NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
SECTION 1: That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the findings
of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth herein.
SECTION 2: The definitions in Section 17.04.030 of the Winnetka Zoning
Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, is hereby amended by adding a
new defined term, “non-essential public use,” which shall be defined as
follows:
   “Nonessential public use” means a use affected with the public interest,
regardless of ownership, except that the term nonessential public use shall not
include essential public uses.
SECTION 3: Subsection B, Nonessential Public Uses, of Section 17.56.010 of
Chapter 17.56 of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Special Uses, is hereby amended
to provide as follows:
   B. Nonessential Public Uses. Nonessential public uses, as determined by the
Village Council, may be permitted as special uses in all districts within the
Village, subject to complying with the procedures and standards of this Section
17.56.010.
SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage, approval
and posting.
   ADOPTED this 20th day of November, 2001, pursuant to the following roll call
vote:
