ORDINANCE NO. MC-5-2002
AN ORDINANCE AMENDING SECTION 8.20.030 OF THE WINNETKA VILLAGE CODE AS IT
PERTAINS TO NOXIOUS WEEDS
WHEREAS, Section 8.20.030 of the Winnetka Village Code requires property owners
to destroy noxious weeds before they reach a seed-bearing stage and to prevent
noxious weeds from perpetuating themselves; and
WHEREAS, the list of noxious weeds in Subsection B of Section 8.20.030 includes
common milkweed; and
WHEREAS, the common milkweed is the habitat plant for the Monarch butterfly,
which is the state insect of the State of Illinois; and
WHEREAS, pursuant to Section 4 of the Illinois Noxious Weed Law (505 ILCS 100/
4), the Director of Agriculture of the State of Illinois, the Dean of the
College of Agriculture of the University of Illinois and the Director of the
Agriculture Experiment Station at the University of Illinois have determined
what weeds are noxious for the purpose of the Illinois Noxious Weed Law (505
ILCS 100/1, et seq.) and have published that list as part of the rules and
regulations of the Illinois Department of Agriculture (8 Ill. Adm. Code
§220.60); and
WHEREAS, the Department of Agriculture’s rules and regulations do not include
common milkweed as one of the noxious weeds subject to the State’s noxious weed
control program; and
WHEREAS, the Forestry Commission of the Village of Winnetka has recommended
that common milkweed no longer be considered a noxious weed; and
WHEREAS, the Forestry Commission of the Village of Winnetka has also
recommended that garlic mustard be added to the list of noxious weeds in the
Winnetka Village Code; and
WHEREAS, the Forestry Commission of the Village of Winnetka has further
recommended that property owners be required to remove ragweed, poison ivy, and
garlic mustard.
WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka have determined that common
milkweed should no longer be prohibited within the Village of Winnetka, so that
the natural habitat of the Monarch butterfly can be maintained; and
WHEREAS, the Council of the Village of Winnetka have further determined that
garlic mustard be considered a noxious weed in the Village of Winnetka and that
property owners be required to remove ragweed, poison ivy and garlic mustard
growing on their properties; and
NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
SECTION 1: That the foregoing recitals are hereby incorporated as the findings
of the Council of the Village of Winnetka, as if fully set forth herein.
SECTION 2: That Subsection B, “Noxious Weeds,” of Section 8.20.030 in Chapter
8.20 of the Winnetka Village Code, be amended as follows:
   B.   Noxious Weeds.
      1.   The following plants shall be considered noxious weeds within the
Village of Winnetka: burdock, giant ragweed, common ragweed, thistle,
cocklebur, jimson, blue vervain, wild carrot, poison ivy, wild mustard, garlic
mustard, rough pigweed, lambsquarter, wild lettuce, curled dock, all varieties
of smartweeds, poison hemlock, wild hemp, and all other weeds of a like kind.
      2.   It is the duty of all owners of property in the Village on which any
giant ragweed, common ragweed, poison ivy or garlic mustard is growing, to
remove such plant from their property.
      3.   It is the duty of all owners of property in the Village on which
burdock, thistle, cocklebur, jimson, blue vervain, wild carrot, wild mustard,
rough pigweed, lambsquarter, wild lettuce, curled dock, all varieties of
smartweeds, poison hemlock, wild hemp, or any other weed of a like kind is
growing, to destroy such weed before it reaches a seed-bearing stage and to
prevent such weed from perpetuating itself.
(Prior code § 10.05)
SECTION 4: This Ordinance shall take effect immediately upon passage, approval
and posting.
   ADOPTED this 7th day of May, 2002, pursuant to the following roll call vote:
AYES:      Trustees Aquilino, Brower, Greenough, Powell, Presser and Woodbury
NAYS:   None
ABSENT:   None
Signed:
s/s Michael F. Duhl
Village President
Countersigned:
s/s Douglas G. Williams
Village Clerk
Introduced: April 16, 2002
Posted: April 17, 2002
Passed and Approved: May 7, 2002
Posted: May 8, 2002
ORDINANCE NO. MC-6-2002
AN ORDINANCE AMENDING THE BULK REGULATIONS APPLICABLE TO PROPERTIES IN SINGLE-
FAMILY RESIDENTIAL ZONING DISTRICTS
WHEREAS, at the direction of the Council of the Village of Winnetka (the
“Village Council”), the Village staff has conducted an analysis of residential
gross floor area; and
WHEREAS, as part of its analysis, staff reported that there are several
variables in residential construction, such as the height between floors, roof
pitch and the placement of a building on a lot, that affect actual or perceived
exterior bulk of a building and its resulting impact on the surrounding
neighborhood; and
WHEREAS, following further discussion and study, the Village Council directed
the preparation of a draft ordinance aimed at reducing building height,
controlling the size of houses on oversized lots, limiting garage heights,
establishing garage setbacks and providing greater incentives for front
porches; and
WHEREAS, the Village Council further directed that a public hearing be held on
the ordinance at the time it is presented for introduction; and
WHEREAS, on due notice thereof, the Village Council held a public hearing on
May 7, 2002, to consider a draft ordinance that would amend the Zoning
Ordinance as directed by the Village Council; and
WHEREAS, the draft ordinance considered at the public hearing reflected further
staff recommendations pertaining to garage height and the restructuring of the
Zoning Ordinance to consolidate all bulk regulations pertaining to the single-
family residential zoning districts in a single chapter of the Zoning
Ordinance; and
WHEREAS, the Council find that the current building height standards in the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance allow taller homes than are permitted in
corresponding zoning districts in similar communities, and that the height
limitations in those communities do not appear to have a negative impact on
redevelopment; and
WHEREAS, the Council further find that, under the bulk regulations applicable
to single-family residential properties, it is possible to construct a single-
family residence that has a conforming gross floor area but that has a
significantly larger appearance from the exterior than other residences with
comparable gross floor areas, by maximizing the location of the attic floor,
the height of the basement and the height of the first and second stories; and
WHEREAS, the Council further find that it is necessary, and in the interests of
the general welfare of the citizens of the Village of Winnetka to amend the
Zoning Ordinance to lower the overall building height, to change the points of
reference for measuring building height, to limit the height of the basement,
to establish a fixed height for the attic floor in all districts, and to
provide for different overall building heights in the different zoning
districts, in order to assure consistency of the scale of buildings within each
single-family residential zoning district and to maintain the character of each
single-family residential zoning district; and
WHEREAS, the Council further find that it is necessary and in the interests of
the general welfare of the citizens of the Village of Winnetka to amend the
roofed lot coverage allowance for open front porches to include side porches
and front porches that extend across only a portion of the front of the
building, so as to further encourage the use of open porches as a means of
maintaining the appearance of existing streetscapes and providing visual
interest to the fronts and sides of residential buildings; and
WHEREAS, the Council further find that the structural amendments that the staff
has proposed for the Zoning Ordinance are necessary and appropriate in that
they will help to make the Zoning Ordinance easier to understand and
administer.
NOW, THEREFORE, the Council of the Village of Winnetka do ordain as follows:
SECTION 1:  That the foregoing recitals are adopted by the Council of the
Village of Winnetka as its findings.
SECTION 2 : That Section 17.04.030, “Definitions,” of Chapter 17.04,
“Introductory Provisions and Definitions,” of the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance,
Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, is hereby amended by adding the
following definitions:
   Height, Structure. "Structure height" means the vertical distance measured
from the lowest natural grade adjacent to the structure to the highest point of
the structure.
   Pre-FAR Building. “Pre-FAR Building” means a single-family dwelling that was
lawfully in existence before February 7, 1989.
   Post-FAR Building. “Post-FAR Building” means a single-family dwelling that
was not lawfully in existence before February 7, 1989, or that was constructed
since 1989.
SECTION 3 : That the first definition of “Building Area” in Section 17.04.030,
“Definitions,” of Chapter 17.04, “Introductory Provisions and Definitions,” of
the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, is hereby
amended to provide as follows:
   "Buildable area" means that portion of a zoning lot remaining after required
yards and setbacks have been provided and within which principal and accessory
buildings may, subject to all applicable requirements, be located.
SECTION 4 : That the definition of “Building Line” in Section 17.04.030,
“Definitions,” of Chapter 17.04, “Introductory Provisions and Definitions,” of
the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, is hereby
amended to provide as follows:
   "Building line" means the boundary of any side of a building or projection
from such building at its outermost vertical plane, excluding only such parts
of the building as are located in and permitted as obstructions in one or more
required yards under Chapters 17.32, 17.36, 17.40, 17.44.
SECTION 5 : That the definition of “Building Height” in Section 17.04.030,
“Definitions,” of Chapter 17.04, “Introductory Provisions and Definitions,” of
the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, is hereby
amended to provide as follows:
   Height, Building. "Building height" means the vertical distance measured
from the top of the finished first floor to the highest point of the building.
   For purposes of this definition, the following principles shall apply:
      1.   The height of a chimney or chimneys shall not be included in
measuring the height of a building.
      2.   The height of dormers shall not be included in measuring the height
of a building unless the ridge of the dormer is higher than the ridge of the
building roof.
      3.   The height of a cupola, tower or other similar roof feature or
appurtenance other than a chimney shall be included in measuring the height of
a building.
SECTION 6 : That the definition of “Height, Upper Floor,” in Section 17.04.030,
“Definitions,” of Chapter 17.04, “Introductory Provisions and Definitions,” of
the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, is hereby
amended to provide as follows:
   Height, Upper Floor. "Upper floor height" means the vertical distance
between (1) the actual upper floor level, or the maximum vertical distance
above the first floor established pursuant to Section 17.30.080.D of this Code,
whichever is lower; and (2) either the bottom of the roof rafters above the
upper floor or a point no more than twelve (12) inches below the top of the
roof rafters, whichever is higher, notwithstanding the presence of trusses,
cross-ties or other interior building components. Notwithstanding the
foregoing, the upper floor height shall not include the vertical distance under
the roof of any dormer that is no more than six feet wide and is set at least
three and one-half feet from the gable end walls, but only if the total width
of all dormers does not exceed twenty-five (25) percent of the length of the
roof on which they are located.
SECTION 7 : That the definition of “Inner Court” in Section 17.04.030,
“Definitions,” of Chapter 17.04, “Introductory Provisions and Definitions,” of
the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, is hereby
amended to provide as follows:
   "Inner court" means an open space unoccupied and unobstructed from its
lowest point to the sky, except as otherwise permitted in Chapter 17.32, 17.36,
17.40 or 17.44, surrounded on all sides by walls or by walls and a lot line.
SECTION 8 : That the definition of “Outer Court” in Section 17.04.030,
“Definitions,” of Chapter 17.04, “Introductory Provisions and Definitions,” of
the Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, is hereby
amended to provide as follows:
   "Outer court" means an open space unoccupied and unobstructed from its
lowest level to the sky, except as otherwise permitted in Chapter 17.32, 17.36,
17.40 or 17.44, surrounded on three sides by walls.
SECTION 9 : That the definition of “Yard” in Section 17.04.030, “Definitions,”
of Chapter 17.04, “Introductory Provisions and Definitions,” of the Winnetka
Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, is hereby amended to
provide as follows:
   "Yard" means a required open space, on the same lot with a building or other
structure, unoccupied and unobstructed from its lowest level upward, except as
otherwise permitted in Chapter 17.32, 17.36, 17.40 or 17.44.
SECTION 10: That Chapter 17.12, “R-5 Single-Family Residential District” of the
Winnetka Zoning Ordinance, Title 17 of the Winnetka Village Code, is hereby
amended to provide as follows:
Chapter 17.12
R-5 SINGLE-FAMILY RESIDENTIAL DISTRICT
Sections:
   17.12.010   R-5 Single-Family Residential District Purpose.
   17.12.020   Permitted Uses
Section 17.12.010   R-5 Single-Family Residential District Purpose.
   The requirements set forth in this chapter, together with other provisions
set forth in the Building Code of the Village, have been adopted in order to
provide for a single-family residential environment of relatively intense
suburban character, where the degree of improvements upon an individual parcel
of land is in approximate balance with the landscape area of such parcel of
land. Due to the mature character of development within this district, such
requirements are also intended to assure that future modifications to existing
structures, or the replacement of existing structures with new structures, will
not alter the scale and general character of established neighborhoods. In this
regard, the requirements set forth in this chapter are further intended to
foster development which is compatible, in general, with the character of
existing single-family development within the immediate neighborhood with
respect to external architectural scale, landscaping and other site
improvements.
Section 17.12.020   Permitted Uses.
   Unless otherwise provided in this title, no building or premises shall be
used and no building shall be erected or altered within the R-5 Single-Family
Residential District, except for the following uses:
   A.   Principal Use. Single-family detached dwelling (to be occupied by only
a single family as defined in Section 17.04.030).
   B.   Accessory Buildings and Uses. In addition to other considerations and
requirements set forth in this title, buildings and uses accessory to a single-
family detached dwelling shall be subject to the following conditions and
requirements:
      1.   A permitted accessory use and the use of an accessory building shall
not include the conduct of any business other than a home occupation as defined
in Section 17.04.030.
      2.   A permitted accessory use shall not include the parking or storage
of commercial trucks, commercial truck-trailers or commercial buses except in
an enclosed garage building.
      3.   A permitted accessory use shall not include outdoor parking or
storage of trailers or boats except in a rear yard.
      4.   A permitted accessory use and the use of an accessory building may
include private garages with a total capacity of not more than four vehicles,
in which may be housed not more than one vehicle not the property of the owner
or occupants of the premises, and not more than one commercial vehicle which
shall not exceed eighteen (18) feet in overall length, eight feet in overall
height, and shall not be designed or constructed to carry a load greater than
one and one-half tons, net.
      5.   Tennis courts and swimming pools shall not be located within a
required side or front yard.
      6.   Tree houses shall not be located within a required side or front
yard.
      7.   Air conditioning units shall not be located within a required yard.
      8.   Air conditioning units may be located closer to the street line than
the principal building, provided the Director determines that they are properly
screened.
      9.   Satellite receiving dishes having a surface area greater than eight
feet, solar panels not originally incorporated into the design of a building,
windmills and other energy recovery or generating devices shall be subject to
the conditions and requirements set forth in Chapter 17.56.
      10.   No accessory building shall be erected or altered to contain living
quarters, except an existing accessory building containing living quarters for
a full-time household employee or employees of the family occupying the
principal building. Use of an accessory building or a building originally
erected as an accessory building, existing and containing occupied living
quarters but not occupied by a full-time household employee of the family
occupying the principal building shall be considered and treated as a legal
nonconformity under Chapter 17.64. Uses permitted in this paragraph may include
occupancy by additional persons entitled to occupy a single-family dwelling if
the living quarters contain not less than three hundred fifty (350) square feet
of interior floor area for the first two persons residing in such single-family
dwelling and one hundred fifty (150) square feet of additional interior floor
area for each person residing in such single-family dwelling; provided,
however, that no occupancy otherwise permitted under this paragraph shall be
allowed or continued unless the owner of the principal building shall comply
with the registration requirements of Section 17.72.030; provided further that,
the number of persons entitled to occupy such living quarters shall not exceed
seven;
   C.   Special Uses. As a special use, subject to the conditions and
requirements set forth in Chapter 17.56, any one of the following:
      1.   Church or temple,
      2.   Public school, elementary and high, or private school having a
curriculum equivalent to a public elementary school, public high school or
public institution of higher learning,
      3.   Library,
      4.   Pre-school or pre-kindergarten in a nonresidential structure
existing on August 19, 1997; provided that, the pre-school or pre-kindergarten
program is licensed by the Department of Children and Family Services, pursuant
to rules and regulations adopted pursuant to the Child Care Act of 1969, as a
part-time day care center for children who are less than school aged; and
provided further that, the pre-school or pre-kindergarten program is accredited
as a pre-school or pre-kindergarten by the National Academy of Early Childhood
Programs;
   D.   Essential Public Use. An essential public use shall be permitted either
as a principal use or as an accessory use.
(Ord. MC-221-99 §§ 27--35, 1999; Ord. MC-216-99 §§ 2--4, 1999; Ord. MC-199-97 §
2, 1998; Ord. MC-195-97 § 4, 1997; Ord. MC-194-97 § 3, 1997; Ord. MC-180-97 §
4, 1997; prior code § 22.05)
(MC-8-2000, Amended, 11/21/2000, Lot width, Front Setback, and Rear Setback
amended; MC-5-2000, Amended, 10/03/2000)
